('anki-card-1758690370075-32', 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Chronic alcoholism adversely affects the utilization of these fat-soluble vitamins: *', 'vitamins A & D', 'Vitamin A degradation: Chronic alcohol consumption induces the cytochrome P450 enzyme system (specifically CYP2E1) in the liver, which heavily accelerates the breakdown and depletion of retinol (Vitamin A) stores. Additionally, alcohol directly competes with Vitamin A for the metabolic enzymes alcohol dehydrogenase (ADH) and aldehyde dehydrogenase (ALDH).

Vitamin D malabsorption & impaired activation: Alcohol interferes with the liver''s ability to hydrolyze cholecalciferol into its active form. It also disrupts bile secretion and impairs the mucosal lining of the small intestine, leading to significant fat malabsorption that prevents fat-soluble vitamins like Vitamin D from being absorbed properly.

While chronic alcoholism can impact all fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), vitamins A & D are the most clinically prominent and severely affected in terms of metabolic utilization, hepatic storage depletion, and target organ complications (e.g., night blindness and alcoholic bone disease/osteopenia).', '["vitamins A & E","vitamins D & E","vitamins D & K","vitamins A & D"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 4, 'The liver is the primary storage organ for Vitamin A and the critical site where Vitamin D undergoes its initial hydroxylation step (converting into 25-hydroxyvitamin D). Chronic alcohol consumption overburdens and damages liver cells, which severely disrupts the body''s ability to chemically activate, store, and utilize these two specific vitamins.', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-11T14:46:35.268Z","interval":13,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:46:35.268Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.013Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:46:35.472Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758691292898-80', 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'One glucose molecule will yield how many pyruvate products? *', '2', 'During the first stage of cellular respiration (glycolysis), a single glucose molecule—which is a 6-carbon sugar (C₆H₁₂O₆)—is physically split and oxidized in the cytoplasm of the cell.

This biochemical breakdown yields exactly two molecules of pyruvate (also known as pyruvic acid), which are 3-carbon structures (C₃H₄O₃).', '["1","2","3","4"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 5, 'One molecule of glucose yields exactly two molecules of pyruvate through the enzymatic pathway of glycolysis, which splits the 6-carbon hexose skeleton into two 3-carbon triose units.', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-25T14:46:10.913Z","interval":27,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":5}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:46:10.913Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.013Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:46:11.113Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758691229569-76', 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Considered inorganic nutrients: *', 'minerals & water', 'In chemistry and nutrition, inorganic nutrients are substances that do not contain carbon-to-carbon or carbon-to-hydrogen bonds. They are not produced by living organisms through organic synthesis.

Elements like calcium, iron, and zinc (minerals), along with water (H₂O), are pure inorganic compounds essential for structural and chemical processes in the body.

• Vitamins, Proteins, Fats, and Carbohydrates are all organic compounds. They are complex structures containing carbon atoms bound to hydrogen and oxygen, synthesized by plants or animals.', '["minerals & water","proteins & fats","vitamins & proteins","vitamins & minerals"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T16:57:10.718Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T16:57:10.718Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.013Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T16:57:10.895Z"'::jsonb),
