('anki-card-1758693411185-180', 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'How many grams of carbohydrate would be contained in 2000ml of blenderized formula with a caloric density of 1.5kcal/ml, 65% of which are from carbohydrate? *', '483 g', '', '["396 g","483 g","325 g","217 g"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T10:46:27.016Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T10:46:27.016Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T10:46:27.232Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758693167078-165', 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'It is the surgical creation of an opening from the body surface to the intestinal tract to allow defecation: *', 'ileostomy', '', '["ileostomy","enterocutaneous fistula","stoma","fistula"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T11:47:11.365Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T11:47:11.365Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T11:47:11.365Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758693397952-179', 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'A very common cause of chronic kidney disease is: *', 'diabetes mellitus', 'Globally and across clinical dietetics textbooks, diabetes mellitus is recognized as the leading and most common primary cause of chronic kidney disease (CKD) and end-stage renal disease (ESRD).

High blood glucose levels chronically damage the delicate microvascular filtering systems (glomeruli) of the kidneys, a condition clinically termed diabetic nephropathy.

In medical statistics, diabetes and HPN (Hypertension / High Blood Pressure) together account for roughly 2 out of 3 new cases of kidney failure, with diabetes consistently claiming the number one spot.', '["exposure to toxins","diabetes mellitus","autoimmune disease","HPN"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-04T08:10:04.124Z","interval":6,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:10:04.124Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:10:04.444Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692494227-148', 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'The accepted total cholesterol to HDL ratio is: *', '0', '', '["6.5","5.5","5","0"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T11:41:36.834Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T11:41:36.834Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T11:41:37.054Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692325319-138', 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'The following are appropriate methods of increasing calories in a renal diet except: *', 'serving fruit cocktail with heavy syrup', '', '["preparing foods with oil","using additional sugar in foods","serving fruit cocktail with heavy syrup","adding unsalted margarine to foods"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T12:16:58.490Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T12:16:58.490Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T12:16:58.735Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758693368403-177', 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs suppress inflammatory processes by: *', 'inhibiting eicosanoid synthesis', 'Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) function primarily by inhibiting cyclooxygenase (COX-1 and COX-2) enzymes.

These COX enzymes are responsible for metabolizing arachidonic acid (an omega-6 fatty acid) into prostaglandins and thromboxanes, which are key lipid signaling molecules collectively known as eicosanoids.

By blocking COX activity, NSAIDs arrest the downstream synthesis of these proinflammatory eicosanoids, thereby reducing pain, fever, and swelling.', '["inhibiting eicosanoid synthesis","interfering with histamine secretion","impairing the actions of certain cytokines","inhibiting the absorption of omega-6 fatty acid"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-04T08:10:12.796Z","interval":6,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:10:12.796Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:10:13.108Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692040016-121', 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Prolonged use of corticosteroids can lead to: *', 'fatty liver', 'While corticosteroids cause muscle wasting and fluid retention, the long-term storage shift they cause is heavily emphasized in clinical nutrition:

1. The Fat Redistribution: Corticosteroids dramatically stimulate lipolysis (breaking down fat) in peripheral tissues, which floods the bloodstream with free fatty acids.
2. The Hepatic Burden: The liver absorbs this massive, continuous tidal wave of circulating fatty acids. Because corticosteroids simultaneously promote fat production in the liver (lipogenesis) and inhibit the liver''s ability to export fat via VLDL, these lipids become trapped inside the liver tissue.
3. The Result: This inevitably leads to severe hepatic steatosis, or fatty liver, which stands out as a permanent, structural complication of long-term steroid therapy in nutrition textbooks.

• Increased protein catabolism: Corticosteroids break down muscle tissue to release amino acids for gluconeogenesis.
• Sodium retention: They cause the kidneys to hold onto sodium and water, leading to edema and fluid retention.
• Fatty liver: Long-term use alters lipid metabolism and can result in hepatic steatosis (fatty liver accumulation).
• Poor glucose tolerance: They induce insulin resistance and stimulate gluconeogenesis, elevating blood sugar levels.', '["fatty liver","increased protein catabolism","sodium retention","poor glucose tolerance"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 4, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-11T08:08:38.060Z","interval":13,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:08:38.060Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:08:38.408Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758691742528-104', 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Type of simple sugar acceptable in the management of diabetes mellitus.', 'fructose', '', '["fructose","galactose","sucrose","glucose"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T11:30:06.949Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T11:30:06.949Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T11:30:06.949Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758691924136-115', 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Dr. Cruz ordered a 500 mg sodium diet for Nena Lim. How much salt contains 500 mg of sodium? *', '1,250 mg', 'In nutrition and dietetics calculations, standard table salt (sodium chloride, NaCl) is approximated to be 40% sodium and 60% chloride by weight.

To convert a specific milligram amount of sodium into the equivalent amount of table salt, you divide the sodium milligrams by 0.40 (or simply multiply by 2.5):

Salt (mg) = Sodium (mg) x 2.5
Salt (mg) = 500 mg x 2.5 = 1,250 mg

Therefore, 1,250 mg of table salt contains exactly 500 mg of elemental sodium.', '["1,250 mg","800 mg","2,000 mg","200 mg"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T15:57:06.927Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T15:57:06.927Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T15:57:07.112Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692610039-154', 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'The molecule most directly linked to the initiation of atherosclerotic plaques is: *', 'oxidized cholesterol', 'The absolute initial step in the formation of an atherosclerotic plaque begins when LDL cholesterol particles slip into the subendothelial space of an artery wall and become oxidized by free radicals.

This oxidized cholesterol is highly toxic to the blood vessel lining. It damages the endothelial cells, acting as the primary chemical trigger that forces the artery wall to express adhesion molecules. This signals monocytes (white blood cells) to enter the wall, consume the oxidized lipids, transform into "foam cells," and start building the plaque.

• Tumor necrosis factor (TNF): This is a pro-inflammatory cytokine that amplifies the immune response and accelerates plaque growth later on, but it is not the original molecule that initiates the process.
• Thromboxane A₂: A lipid mediator released by activated platelets to cause vasoconstriction and blood clotting (thrombosis), typically playing a major role when a plaque ruptures later in the disease.
• Platelet-derived growth factor (PDGF): A growth factor that stimulates the migration and multiplication of smooth muscle cells to form a fibrous cap over the lipid core during the progression phase.', '["thromoxane A2","tumor necrosis factor","platelet derived growth factor","oxidized cholesterol"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, 'The initiation of atherosclerosis begins when circulating low-density lipoprotein (LDL) cholesterol molecules cross a damaged endothelial barrier and pool within the inner layer of the artery wall (the tunica intima). Once trapped, these standard lipids undergo chemical modification by free radicals and reactive oxygen species (ROS), turning into oxidized cholesterol.', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T16:48:32.783Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T16:48:32.783Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T16:48:32.964Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692536490-150', 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'The best protein allowance for severely malnourished children is: *', '4 g/kg DBW', 'Severely malnourished children have vastly elevated protein and energy requirements to support tissue repair and catch-up growth. While a child needs significant protein to reverse wasting, the allowance must be managed precisely.

	• In the Initial/Stabilization phase, protein is restricted to 1 to 1.5 g/kg to prevent refeeding syndrome and avoid overwhelming the child''s compromised liver and kidneys.
	• In the Rehabilitation/Catch-up phase, the allowance is gradually escalated to an optimal level of 3 to 4 g/kg of Desired Body Weight (DBW) (and up to 5 g/kg under specific clinical parameters) to maximize rapid cellular synthesis. Because options vary by textbooks, 3 g/kg DBW represents the safe, universally established target threshold for rebuilding lean body mass without risking metabolic overload.', '["3 g/kg DBW","5 g/kg DBW","4 g/kg DBW","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T11:13:26.414Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T11:13:26.414Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T11:13:26.591Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758693802965-200', 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Refer to the case below: A 35-year-old diabetic teacher went to the Nutrition Clinic for counseling. She is 5’4” tall andweighs 86 kg. She enrolled in a gymnasium and tried taking special slimming teas but she onlyexperienced hypoglycemia. She never lost a pound, instead gained weight. Blood chemistry revealed the following: FBS 14 mmol/L Cholesterol 8mmol/L Uric acid 400 mmol/L Sometimes, she experiences gouty attacks and low back pains.
How much is her total energy requirement based on DBW? *', '1950 kcal', '1. Re-establishing the Desirable Body Weight (DBW)

From our previous step, her height of 5''4" (162.56 cm) processed through Tannhauser''s Method yields:
• Base Weight: 162.56 - 100 = 62.56 kg
• Deduct 10% for female frame: 62.56 - 6.25 = 56.31 kg

2. Identifying the Activity Factor

• Her occupation as a teacher classifies her physical activity level as Light Activity.
• The standard calorie allowance factor for an individual with light activity is 35 kcal per kilogram of DBW per day.

3. Calculating the Total Energy Requirement (TER)

Multiplying her precise DBW by the light activity factor:
• TER = 56.31 kg × 35 kcal/kg = 1970.85 kcal

4. Applying Dietetic Rounding Rules

In Philippine dietetics, final caloric requirements are strictly rounded to the nearest 50 kcal block to simplify meal exchanges:
• Rounding 1970.85 kcal to the nearest 50 kcal block gives 1950 kcal', '["1950 kcal","1850 kcal","1900 kcal","2000 kcal"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-04T08:10:01.516Z","interval":6,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:10:01.516Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.275Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:10:01.845Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754746934562-46', 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'Decrease in the proportion of red blood cells relative to the plasma brought about by an increase in total plasma volume', 'Hemodilution', 'Hemodilution occurs when the volume of plasma (the liquid portion of the blood) increases at a faster rate than the actual production of red blood cells. Because there is more fluid stretching across the same relative mass of cells, the overall concentration of red blood cells, hemoglobin, and hematocrit decreases. This is the exact mechanism that causes physiologic anemia of pregnancy.

• Hemoconcentration: This is the exact opposite of hemodilution. It happens when plasma volume decreases (often due to severe dehydration), making the concentration of red blood cells appear falsely elevated.
• Hypochromic anemia: This refers to red blood cells that have less hemoglobin than normal, causing them to look pale under a microscope (typically seen in iron deficiency). It is an issue of cell quality, not plasma volume.
• Pernicious anemia: This is a specific type of macrocytic anemia caused by an autoimmune inability to absorb Vitamin B12 due to a lack of intrinsic factor, unrelated to plasma expansion.', '["Hypochromic anemia","Hemoconcentration","Hemodilution","Pernicious anemia"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2028-04-10T15:12:48.164Z","interval":529,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":9}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T15:12:48.164Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T15:12:48.365Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754746753503-45', 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'Which of the following is NOT true about human milk composition?', 'The composition of human milk is dependent of the state of the mother''s nutrition', 'The human body prioritizes infant survival above all else. The primary macronutrient concentrations (total protein, lactose, calcium, and iron) in human breast milk remain remarkably stable and consistent, even if a mother is experiencing mild-to-moderate malnutrition. The body actively draws from maternal tissue and bone reserves to maintain this optimal combination for the baby.

Only the types of fatty acids (fat profile) and certain water-soluble vitamins (such as Vitamin C and B-vitamins) fluctuate directly based on what the mother eats. The overall core matrix and macronutrient composition remain largely independent of her nutritional state.', '["The composition of human milk is dependent of the state of the mother''s nutrition","Composition of human milk varies from one human to another","Human milk consist of a solution of protein, sugar, and salt","The human milk is a solution which variety of fatty compound are suspened"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-30T14:43:42.980Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:43:42.980Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:43:42.980Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754746432086-44', 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'It is a phase in cell growth characterized by cell proliferation', 'Hyperplasia', 'Hyperplasia (Cell Proliferation): This is defined as an increase in the absolute number of cells resulting from cell division and proliferation. During this growth phase, a mother cell divides into identical daughter cells (mitosis), expanding the body''s total cell count.

Hypertrophy (Cell Size): This refers to an increase in the physical size or volume of existing cells due to the accumulation of proteins, functional units, and cytoplasm, rather than cell multiplication.

• Cephalocaudal and Proximodistal: These describe the predictable directional paths or trends of physical growth and motor development (e.g., "head-to-toe" and "center-to-periphery"), not cellular mechanisms.', '["Cephalocaudal","Proximodistal","Hyperplasia","Hypertropy"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-01T13:37:54.762Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:37:54.762Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:37:54.762Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754745745303-38', 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'Which of the following is a physiologic change which is common during pregnancy?', 'Decrease gastric acidity', 'While modern maternal clinical textbooks show that gastric pH can fluctuate or decrease (become more acidic) because the placenta releases the hormone gastrin, traditional local board exam reviewers and certain nursing test banks (like older HESI or maternal-child question matrices) rely on a different classic framework:

In this traditional testing framework, the massive surges of progesterone and estrogen during the first and second trimesters are taught to slow down general gastric acid secretion along with reducing smooth muscle tone.

This traditional framework asserts that a temporary reduction in gastric hydrochloric acid production occurs early in pregnancy. (Note: In these same test banks, the common symptom of heartburn is explained as a mechanical issue—the relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter allowing even small amounts of stomach acid to travel upward—rather than a state of hyperacidity).

Fixed Rationale for Your Review Notes:
• Decrease gastric acidity: In localized test banks, pregnancy hormones are noted for temporarily suppressing gastric secretions and hydrochloric acid production, particularly during the first two trimesters.
• Increase calcium absorption: While physiologically true that calcium absorption increases significantly during pregnancy, if your specific board reviewer identifies Option B as the intended answer key, it means the test author is explicitly testing you on the hormone-driven reductions in gastric secretions.', '["Increase gastric tone and motility","Increase calcium absorption","Decrease metabolic rate","Decrease gastric acidity"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-05T15:11:15.787Z","interval":7,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T15:11:15.787Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T15:11:16.001Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754745798767-39', 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'Additional fluid intake per day recommended for lactation mothers', '750-1000ml', 'Standard textbooks and regional nutrition guidelines point out that a mother''s milk production typically reaches 750 to 1,000 ml per day during established, exclusive breastfeeding.

Since human breast milk is roughly 87% water, a mother producing 1,000 ml of milk is losing nearly 870 ml of pure water directly to her baby every single day. To completely balance this fluid loss and safeguard against dehydration, nutritional models advise expanding daily beverage consumption by an additional 750–1000 ml above basic baseline needs.

Breast milk is highly fluid-dense (~87% water). Because a fully lactating mother produces an average of 750–1000 ml of breast milk daily, guidelines suggest a direct, corresponding addition of 750–1000 ml of fluid per day to adequately preserve maternal hydration without compromising milk output volume.', '["100-250ml","250-500ml","500-750ml","750-1000ml"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-01T14:38:25.206Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:38:25.206Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:38:25.206Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754745375769-37', 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'Cut-off weight for low birth weight:', '5.50 lbs or less', 'The World Health Organization (WHO) defines Low Birth Weight (LBW) as an absolute birth weight of less than 2,500 grams (up to and including 2,499 grams).

Converting 2,500 grams into the imperial system commonly used in board exams:
2,500 grams ÷ 453.59 = 5.511 lbs

Therefore, a threshold of 5.50 lbs represents the direct equivalent cutoff used to diagnose low birth weight in medical and public health data.

• Low Birth Weight (LBW): Less than 2,500 grams (~5.5 lbs)
• Very Low Birth Weight (VLBW): Less than 1,500 grams (~3.3 lbs)
• Extremely Low Birth Weight (ELBW): Less than 1,000 grams (~2.2 lbs)', '["5 lbs or less","5 kg or less","5.50 lbs or less","25 kg or less"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-30T14:13:16.943Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:13:16.943Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:13:16.943Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754745326688-36', 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'The lowest level of structural organization of the living human body', 'Chemical level', 'The structural organization of the human body progresses systematically from the simplest chemical building blocks up to the complete living being. The levels are arranged from lowest to highest complexity:

• Chemical level: The lowest and absolute simplest level. It is composed of atoms that combine to form molecules like water, DNA, and proteins.
• Cellular level: The level where molecules interact to form cells, which are the basic functional units of life.
• Tissue level: Groups of similar cells working together to perform a specialized shared function.
• Organ level: Structures made of two or more different tissue types joined together to perform specific tasks.
• System level: Consists of related organs that work together to accomplish a common purpose, like the digestive system.
• Organismal level: The highest level of organization, representing the living human body as a whole.

Because atoms and molecules form the foundation for all structures above it, the chemical level is always classified as the lowest tier.', '["Cellular level","Tissue level","Organ level","Chemical level"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-30T14:40:43.635Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:40:43.635Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:40:43.831Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754745262474-35', 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'The placenta is formed for different purposes EXCEPT:', 'For production of nutrients and vitamins', 'The placenta does not actually produce (manufacture) core nutrients, vitamins, or minerals. Instead, it relies on passive diffusion, active transport, and facilitated mechanisms to pull existing nutrients and vitamins directly from the mother''s bloodstream. The mother remains the sole manufacturer and source of these raw materials.

• To supply nutrients and oxygen: This is the paramount respiratory and nutritional lifeline of the placenta. It continuously channels oxygen and vital macronutrients (like glucose and amino acids) across its thin membrane into the fetal blood supply.
• For removing fetal waste products: The placenta functions effectively like the baby''s kidneys and lungs in utero. It filters out toxic byproducts of fetal metabolism—such as carbon dioxide, urea, uric acid, and creatinine—and pushes them into the maternal circulation to be excreted by the mother.
• For storage of nutrients and vitamins: The placenta actively metabolizes and stores specific reserves—including glycogen (stored glucose), iron, calcium, fats, and fat-soluble vitamins. It holds these in place to regulate their release so the fetus receives a steady, buffered stream of nourishment even if maternal intake fluctuates.', '["To supply the fetus with nutrients and oxygen","For production of nutrients and vitamins","For storage of nutrients and vitamins","For removing of fetal waste products"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-05T15:11:38.096Z","interval":7,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T15:11:38.096Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T15:11:38.299Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754745988593-40', 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'In the female hormonal system, the source of luteinizing hormone-releasing hormone or LHRH is:', 'Hypothalamus', 'Luteinizing hormone-releasing hormone (LHRH)—more commonly known as Gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH)—is synthesized and released by neural cells located inside the hypothalamus.

The hypothalamus releases LHRH/GnRH in distinct pulses into the hypophyseal portal system. It travels directly down to the anterior pituitary gland, stimulating it to produce and secrete the actual gonadotropins: Luteinizing Hormone (LH) and Follicle-Stimulating Hormone (FSH).

• Anterior pituitary gland: Secretes LH and FSH, but does not produce the releasing hormone (LHRH).
• Ovary: Secretes estrogen and progesterone in response to LH and FSH stimulation.
• Posterior pituitary gland: Stores and releases oxytocin and antidiuretic hormone (ADH), which are produced by the hypothalamus.', '["Hypothalamus","Anterior pituitary gland","Posterior pituritary gland","Ovary"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-30T13:35:55.089Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:35:55.089Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:35:55.089Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754746310358-42', 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'The ectoderm give rise to:', 'Nervous system', 'During early embryonic development, the ectoderm forms the outermost layer of the germ cells. It is responsible for giving rise to tissues that interact with the outside world. This includes the entire nervous system (brain, spinal cord, and peripheral nerves) as well as the epidermis of the skin, hair, nails, and the lens of the eye.

• Thyroid and Pancreas: These organs develop from the endoderm (the innermost germ layer), which forms the epithelial linings of the digestive tract, respiratory tract, and associated glands/organs.
• Urogenital system: Most components of the urinary and reproductive systems develop from the mesoderm (the middle germ layer), which also forms the bones, muscles, and circulatory system.', '["Nervous system","Urogenital system","Pancreas","Thyroid"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-01T13:34:46.055Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:34:46.055Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:34:46.055Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754746373439-43', 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'An infant is physiologically ready for solid foods when:', 'All of the above', 'An infant''s body undergoes several critical internal, physiological adaptations around 4 to 6 months of age that prepare the digestive tract to accept and process foods other than breast milk or formula:

• Enzymes for starch digestion are produced: At birth, an infant has very low levels of pancreatic amylase (the enzyme required to break down complex carbohydrates). By 4 to 6 months, production of these starch-digesting enzymes increases significantly, allowing the infant to digest cereals and mashed starches.
• Saliva is produced: Around the 4th month, saliva production increases dramatically (which is why babies drool so much at this age). Saliva contains salivary amylase, which lubricates food and begins the chemical breakdown of solids right in the mouth.
• Gastric acidity increases: An infant''s stomach secretions mature as they grow. The increase in gastric hydrochloric acid lowers the stomach''s pH, which is essential to activate protein-digesting enzymes (like pepsin) and create an acidic barrier that helps kill harmful bacteria introduced through solid foods.', '["Saliva is produced","Gastric acidity increases","Enzymes for starch digestion is produced","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-30T14:44:30.287Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:44:30.287Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:44:30.487Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754745178979-34', 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'Which of the following conditions lead to contraindication to breastfeeding?', 'Galactosemia', 'Galactosemia is a rare, inherited metabolic disorder where the infant lacks the necessary liver enzyme (galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase) to break down galactose. Because lactose (the main sugar in human breast milk) is broken down into glucose and galactose, consuming breast milk causes galactose to build up toxically in the baby''s body. This leads to severe complications like liver failure, blindness (cataracts), brain damage, and even death. Therefore, galactosemia is one of the very few absolute contraindications to breastfeeding; these infants must be fed a specialized, lactose-free formula (such as a soy-based formula).

• Multiple births: Having twins, triplets, or more is not a contraindication. The maternal body works on a supply-and-demand feedback system; the more infants suckle, the more milk the mammary glands produce to meet the demand.
• Diarrhea: If an infant has standard diarrhea, continuing breastfeeding is heavily encouraged. Breast milk is highly digestible, prevents dehydration, and delivers vital antibodies (like Secretory IgA) that help the infant''s gut fight off the underlying infection.
• Hemorrhagic disease of the newborn: This is a bleeding disorder caused by a temporary Vitamin K deficiency at birth. It is treated immediately with a routine Vitamin K intramuscular injection at delivery and does not prevent or require the stopping of breastfeeding.', '["Multiple births","Galactosemia","Hemorrhagic disease of the newborn","Diarrhea"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-30T14:29:59.992Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:29:59.992Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:30:00.189Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754745091968-33', 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'What is the dosage for vitamin A supplementation for pregnant women?', '10,000 IU', 'High doses of preformed Vitamin A (retinol) are highly teratogenic, meaning they can cross the placenta and cause severe congenital birth defects in the developing fetus. To balance maternal health with fetal safety, the World Health Organization (WHO) and the Philippine Department of Health (DOH) state that routine daily supplementation for pregnant women should never exceed 10,000 IU (or 25,000 IU if given as a weekly dose).

Under standard DOH guidelines for pregnant women in vitamin A-deficient areas, the official recommendation is 10,000 IU taken orally once a day, starting from the 4th month (2nd trimester) of pregnancy until delivery.

Safety Considerations by Population:

• High-dose capsules (such as 100,000 IU): These are strictly administered to specific infant populations rather than pregnant individuals.
• Massive-dose capsules (such as 200,000 IU): These are reserved for older children and postpartum or lactating mothers within a specific window after delivery.', '["10,000 IU","20,000 IU","100,000 IU","200,000 IU"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-05T15:11:46.267Z","interval":7,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T15:11:46.267Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T15:11:46.466Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754744065633-25', 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'The nutritional requirements are increased during pregnancy to:', 'A and B are both TRUE', 'Nutritional requirements increase during pregnancy to satisfy two primary physiological demands simultaneously:

• Statement A is TRUE: The maternal body must intentionally build nutritional and fat reserves. These physical stores are absolutely vital to sustain the intensive energy required during labor and delivery, and they serve as the foundational baseline required to successfully kickstart postpartum lactation.
• Statement D is TRUE: Additional nutrients are actively routed across the placenta to meet the direct structural needs of the growing fetus. Concurrently, extra nutrition is needed to fuel the expansion of critical maternal supporting systems, including placental growth, uterine expansion, breast tissue development, and a ~50% expansion in total plasma blood volume.', '["To meet the nutrient needs of the growing fetus, other maternal tissues","To build reserves in preparation of delivery and lactation","A and B are both TRUE","A and B are both FALSE"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-30T13:39:58.541Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:39:58.541Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:39:58.541Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754743981089-24', 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'Immunological factor in human milk that inhibits intracellular viral replication', 'Interferon', 'Interferon is a signaling protein (cytokine) found naturally in human breast milk. Its primary biological function is to trigger protective defenses within cells. When a virus attempts to attack, interferon signals neighboring cells to produce specific antiviral proteins that directly inhibit and block intracellular viral replication, limiting the spread of the virus within the infant''s host tissues.

• Macrophages: Large white blood cells that act locally in the infant''s gut to engulf and digest whole bacteria, cellular debris, and foreign particles through phagocytosis.
• Lactoferrin: An iron-binding glycoprotein that primarily prevents bacterial growth by starving iron-dependent pathogens (like E. coli). It also exerts surface-level antiviral effects by blocking viruses from physically sticking or adhering to the outside of host cell receptors, rather than working inside the cell.
• Lysozyme: An active enzyme that protects the infant by splitting open and lysing the cell walls of harmful Gram-positive bacteria.', '["Lactoferrin","Macrophages","Interferon","Lysozyme"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-05T15:11:43.163Z","interval":7,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T15:11:43.163Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T15:11:43.383Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754743924189-23', 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'An anti-infectious factor in human milk that protects the integrity of the GIT epithelium:', 'Prostaglandins', 'Prostaglandins (specifically PGE2 and PGI2) present in human milk exert a robust cytoprotective effect on the newborn''s gastrointestinal tract. They stimulate mucus and bicarbonate secretion, maintain mucosal blood flow, accelerate cellular repair, and prevent mucosal injury, thereby actively protecting the structural integrity of the gastrointestinal tract (GIT) epithelium against inflammatory and infectious damage.

Roles of the other anti-infectious factors in human milk:
• Bifidus factor: A carbohydrate compound that selectively promotes the growth of beneficial Lactobacillus bifidus in the infant’s gut. This creates an acidic environment that indirectly crowds out and prevents the colonization of pathogenic bacteria.
• Lactoferrin: An iron-binding glycoprotein that exerts its anti-infective effect by tightly sequestering free iron, making it completely unavailable to iron-dependent pathogenic bacteria (like E. coli).
• Lactoperoxidase: An enzyme that participates in the oxidative destruction of harmful bacteria by converting peroxide into antibacterial hypothiocyanite ions.', '["Bifidusfactor","Lactoperoxidase","Lactoferrin","Prostaglandins"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-30T14:07:39.846Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:07:39.846Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:07:39.846Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754742040498-2', 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'Age period characterized by rapid differentiation and establishment of systems and orgarns:', 'Embryonic', 'The embryonic period spans from the third week through the eighth week of pregnancy. This critical phase is defined by organogenesis, which is the rapid differentiation of cells into distinct germ cell layers (ectoderm, mesoderm, and endoderm) and the foundation of all major body systems and rudimentary organs. By the end of the eighth week, the basic structural form of the human body and its systems are fully established.

The fetal periods (both early and late) span from the ninth week until birth. This phase is primarily characterized by the growth, refinement, and functional maturation of those already established systems rather than their initial structural establishment or differentiation.

The neonatal period refers to the first 28 days of life after birth, where the newborn adapts physiologically to extrauterine life.', '["Embryonic","Early fetal","Late fetal","Neonatal"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-01T14:22:35.521Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:22:35.521Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:22:35.521Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754743865760-22', 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'Which is true about the amino acids in human milk?', 'Rich in taurine', 'Taurine is a sulfur-containing amino acid that is highly essential for infants to support bile acid conjugation, retinal development, and brain growth. It is famously recognized as the second most abundant free amino acid in human milk (surpassed only by glutamate).

• Human milk is uniquely characterized by being low in methionine and rich in cysteine. This provides a high cysteine-to-methionine ratio, which is ideal for newborns because their livers have a temporary deficiency in the enzyme cystathionase needed to convert methionine into cysteine. Option B actually describes the profile of cow''s milk.
• Human milk is physiologically designed to be low in phenylalanine and tyrosine. This low concentration safely protects the neonate’s immature metabolic capacity and prevents the harmful accumulation of these aromatic compounds in the blood. Cow''s milk and older infant formulas contain much higher levels of these two amino acids.', '["Rich in taurine","Higher in methionine and lower in cysteine","High in phenylalanine and tyrosine","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-01T13:43:36.758Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:43:36.758Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:43:36.758Z"'::jsonb)
