('anki-card-1756783888769-42', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'The following are metabolic effects of fever, EXCEPT:', 'Decreases BMR', '', '["Increased tissue catabolism","Increased loss of body water","Increased electrolyte loss","Decreases BMR"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:26:31.985Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:26:31.985Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:26:32.218Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756783837417-41', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'It is the phase of stress response characterized by failure of adaption leading to death.', 'Exhaustion phase', 'The Three Stages of Hans Selye''s General Adaptation Syndrome (GAS)
• Exhaustion Phase: This is the final stage of the stress response. If the stressor is severe, intense, or prolonged over a long period, the body''s energy reserves and compensatory resources become completely depleted. This failure of adaptation leads to immune suppression, systemic organ damage, and eventually death if left unchecked.

The Other Phases:
	• Alarm Reaction (Acute Phase): The initial "fight-or-flight" response where the sympathetic nervous system is triggered, releasing adrenaline and cortisol.
	• Resistance Phase (Adaptive Phase): The body attempts to adapt to the ongoing stressor, working to stabilize its physiological functions and return to a state of homeostasis.
	• Convalescence Phase: This refers to the recovery period after an illness or injury has passed, rather than a failure of stress management.', '["Acute phase","Adaptive phase","Convaslescence phase","Exhaustion phase"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:22:30.534Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:22:30.534Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:22:30.741Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756782702775-23', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Important components of nutritional assessment include:
I. Gathering data
II. Comparing to relevant standards
III. Identifying possible problem area', 'All of the above', '', '["I and II only","I and III only","II and III only","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:14:37.623Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:14:37.623Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:14:37.843Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756782632896-22', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'All of the following increases BMR, EXCEPT:', 'Sleeping', 'Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR) represents the minimum energy required to keep vital functions (like breathing and circulation) active. During sleep, your muscle activity drops, your heart rate slows down, and your body enters its lowest metabolic state, causing your energy expenditure to drop below awake basal levels.', '["Thyroxine","Adrenaline","Stress hormone","Sleeping"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T08:23:43.455Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:23:43.455Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:23:43.676Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756782599114-21', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'A classification of anemia to describe the hemoglobin content:', 'Hypochromic anemia', 'Anemias are classified morphologically using two main criteria: cell size and hemoglobin content (color).
• Hemoglobin Content (Color): The term chromic refers to color, which is determined by the concentration of hemoglobin inside the red blood cell (RBC).
	✓ Hypochromic means the cells have less hemoglobin than normal, making them look pale under a microscope.
	✓ Normochromic means the hemoglobin concentration is normal.
• Cell Size: The term cytic refers to the physical size of the RBC. Macrocytic, Normocytic, and Microcytic describe whether the cells are larger than normal, normal-sized, or smaller than normal. They do not classify hemoglobin content itself.', '["Macrocytic anemia","Normocytic anemia","Hypochromic anemia","Microcytic anemia"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:33:21.222Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:33:21.222Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:33:21.222Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756782543017-20', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'May interact with Warfarin:', 'Vit K', '• Warfarin is an anticoagulant that works by blocking the enzyme responsible for recycling Vitamin K. Since Vitamin K is essential for the liver to synthesize blood-clotting factors (Factors II, VII, IX, and X), a sudden increase or decrease in dietary Vitamin K directly alters how well Warfarin works.
• Consuming high amounts of Vitamin K (like green leafy vegetables) will reverse or reduce the blood-thinning effect of Warfarin, increasing the risk of blood clots. Conversely, dropping Vitamin K intake can dangerousy increase the risk of bleeding.', '["Vit A","Vit D","Vit E","Vit K"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:32:47.914Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:32:47.914Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:32:47.914Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756781934881-10', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Reducing the kcalorie intake by 1000 per day will result to a weekly weight loss of:', '2 lbs', '', '["1 lb","2 lbs","5 lbs","7 lbs"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-09T08:32:10.022Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:32:10.022Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:32:10.260Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756781869883-9', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'The diameter of a feeding tube is measured in what units?', 'French unit', 'The external diameter of feeding tubes (as well as urinary catheters) is measured using the French scale (abbreviated as Fr or F).
• One French unit is equal to exactly 1/3 of a millimeter (0.33 mm). Therefore, a larger French number means a thicker tube (e.g., a 12 Fr tube has an outer diameter of 4 mm).', '["French unit","Diameter unit","Nasogastric tube","Tube circumference unit"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:32:22.031Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:32:22.031Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:32:22.031Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756781821922-8', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'A food challenge in which neither the patient nor physician knows the identity of the test food component, which is usually disguised as a powder in a food known to be tolerated or in a gelatin capsule.', 'Double-blind, placebo-controlled food challenge (DBPCFC)', '', '["Double-blind, placebo-controlled food challenge (DBPCFC)","Single blind food challenge","Open food challenge","Food elimination"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T07:40:56.719Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T07:40:56.719Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T07:40:56.960Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756781713239-7', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Alcohol induced cirrhosis:', 'Laennec''s cirrhosis', 'Laennec''s cirrhosis (historically named after the French physician René Laennec) is the specific medical term traditionally used to classify alcohol-induced liver cirrhosis. It develops over many years of chronic, heavy alcohol exposure, moving through phases of fatty liver infiltration (steatosis) and alcoholic hepatitis before replacing functional liver architecture with fine, uniform fibrous nodules and scar tissue.

	• Biliary cirrhosis stems from the destruction or long-term chronic obstruction of the bile ducts within the liver.
	• Obstructive cirrhosis is a mechanical description of biliary pathway blockages rather than a formal classification for alcohol abuse.
	• Cardiac cirrhosis is triggered by chronic, severe right-sided heart failure causing structural liver congestion.', '["Cardiac cirrhosis","Laennec''s cirrhosis","Biliary cirrhosis","Obstructive cirrhosis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:14:14.473Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:14:14.473Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:14:14.687Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756781666126-6', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Dietary management for fatty liver:', 'High protein, high kcal for underweight', 'Underweight patients with hepatic steatosis or steatohepatitis (such as alcoholic liver disease or advanced cirrhosis with severe muscle wasting) experience accelerated hepatic gluconeogenesis, glycogen depletion, and protein catabolism. A high-protein diet (1.2 to 1.5 g/kg/day) provides essential amino acids required for hepatic export protein synthesis (albumin, clotting factors), decreases skeletal muscle proteolysis, and prevents hepatic encephalopathy by maintaining visceral nitrogen balance. High kilocalories (35 to 40 kcal/kg/day) ensure positive energy balance, shifting metabolism away from endogenous fat mobilization and skeletal muscle breakdown while sparing dietary protein for tissue synthesis rather than oxidation.', '["High protein, high kcal for underweight","Low protein, low kcal for obese","High carbohydrates","IVF"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:31:36.441Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:31:36.441Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:31:36.441Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756781615612-5', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Amount of calorie to reduce per day to effect weight loss of 1.5 lbs per week:', '750 kcal', '• 500 kcal: This deficit results in a weight loss of 1 lb per week.
• 1,000 kcal: This deficit results in a weight loss of 2 lbs per week.', '["750 kcal","500 kcal","1000 kcal","100 kcal"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T07:59:42.775Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T07:59:42.775Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T07:59:43.000Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756781455168-4', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'A classification of infection that last for weeks, months or years.', 'Chronic', '', '["Acute","Chronic","Recurrent","Intermittent"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T07:39:13.814Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T07:39:13.814Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T07:39:13.814Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756781411321-3', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Dietary recommendation for patient with liver cirrhosis:', 'High kcal, high protein, high carbohydrate, low fat', 'Medical nutrition therapy (MNT) for stable liver cirrhosis focus heavily on combatting severe tissue wasting (malnutrition) while protecting remaining hepatic function:

• High Kilocalorie (kcal) & High Carbohydrate: Patients with cirrhosis suffer from profound muscle wasting and rapid depletion of glycogen stores. A high-calorie, carbohydrate-rich diet prevents the breakdown of muscle tissue for energy.
• High Protein: Historically, protein was restricted out of fear it would cause hepatic encephalopathy. Modern clinical guidelines heavily contradict this; cirrhosis patients require an aggressive high-protein intake (typically 1.2–1.5 g/kg/day) to promote cellular repair, maintain lean body mass, and correct hypoalbuminemia. (Protein is only restricted or modified to branched-chain amino acids if the patient exhibits severe, refractory hepatic encephalopathy).
• Low Fat: Fat absorption is frequently compromised due to impaired bile synthesis or secretion. Restricting dietary fat minimizes unpleasant digestive symptoms like steatorrhea, abdominal bloating, and nausea.', '["High kcal, low protein, high carbohydrate, low fat","High kcal, high protein, high carbohydrate, low fat","High kcal, high protein, high carbohydrate, low fiber","High kcal, low protein, high carbohydrate, high fiber"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:31:09.134Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:31:09.134Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:31:09.134Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756783686944-38', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Indication/s when nutritional support is generally required among patients.', 'All of the above', '', '["10 days of prolonged partial starvation","Illness longer than 10 days","Significant/severe weight loss","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T08:15:34.083Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:15:34.083Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:15:34.320Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756781314144-2', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'A broad term that includes drug-nutrient interactions and the effect of a medication on nutritional status:', 'Food-drug interaction', '', '["Pharmaconutrition","Pharmacodynamics","Pharmacokinetics","Food-drug interaction"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:15:23.135Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:15:23.135Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:15:23.369Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756781245630-1', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Dietary management for hepatitis:', 'High kcal, high protein, high carbohydrate, low fat', 'Medical nutrition therapy (MNT) for acute and chronic hepatitis focuses on supporting liver cell regeneration, preventing tissue catabolism, and easing digestive stress:
• High Kilocalorie (kcal) & High Carbohydrate: The liver''s ability to store glycogen and maintain blood glucose levels is impaired during inflammation. A high-calorie, carbohydrate-rich diet supplies the necessary energy, preserves muscle mass, and keeps glycogen stores replenished.
• High Protein: Protein provides the necessary amino acids needed to heal, repair, and regenerate damaged liver parenchyma. (Note: Protein is only restricted if the patient develops advanced hepatic encephalopathy where the liver can no longer clear ammonia).
• Low Fat: Because the inflamed liver struggles to produce and flow bile normally, fat digestion and metabolism are severely compromised. Restricting dietary fat minimizes symptoms like nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, and steatorrhea (fatty stools).
• Why Fluids Are Not Restricted: Fluid restriction is not an intervention for hepatitis. It is typically reserved for downstream complications of advanced liver disease, such as ascites or severe fluid retention in end-stage cirrhosis.', '["High kcal, high protein, high carbohydrate, low fat","High kcal, low protein, high carbohydrate, low fat","High kcal, low protein, high carbohydrate, fluid restricted","High kcal, high protein, high carbohydrate, fluid restricted"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:01:30.154Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:01:30.154Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:01:30.154Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756781167524-0', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Classification obesity characterized by increase in the number of fat cells:', 'Hyperplastic', '• Hyperplastic Obesity: This is characterized by an increase in the total number of fat cells (adipocytes). It typically develops during childhood and adolescence when body growth occurs, making long-term weight management more challenging because the absolute number of cells does not decrease with weight loss.
• Hypertrophic Obesity: This is characterized by an increase in the size of the existing fat cells. This pattern is more common in adult-onset obesity, where the cells expand to store excess lipids.
• Android & Gynoid: These describe the anatomical distribution of fat rather than cellular characteristics. Android refers to "apple-shaped" abdominal fat distribution (common in males), while Gynoid refers to "pear-shaped" hip and thigh fat distribution (common in females).', '["Hyperplastic","Android","Hypertropic","Gynoid"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T08:07:45.065Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:07:45.065Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:07:45.274Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756784302449-48', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'It refers to the provision of nutrients both orally and via tube directly into the GIT.', 'Enteral nutrition', 'Enteral nutrition is the broader term used when nutrients are delivered directly into the gastrointestinal tract (GIT). This includes both standard oral food intake (consuming regular foods or oral supplements) and tube feeding (delivering liquid formula via nasogastric, gastrostomy, or jejunostomy tubes).

	• Parenteral nutrition bypasses the GI tract entirely, delivering nutrients directly into a central or peripheral vein.
	• Nutrition intervention is a broad step in the Nutrition Care Process that encompasses any planned action to resolve a nutrition-related problem (e.g., diet education, counseling, or supplement provision).
	• Refeeding syndrome is a dangerous metabolic complication that can occur when restarting nutrition in severely malnourished individuals.', '["Nutrition intervention","Enteral nutrition","Parental nutrition","Refeeding syndrom"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T08:57:49.896Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:57:49.896Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:57:50.135Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756784244635-47', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'A term used to describe the surgical removal of the gallbladder, inflammation of the gallbladder.', 'Cholecystectomy, Cholecystitis', '• The Root: Cholecyst- refers to the gallbladder.
• Surgical Removal: The suffix -ectomy means surgical excision or removal. Therefore, cholecystectomy is the removal of the gallbladder.
• Inflammation: The suffix -itis means inflammation. Therefore, cholecystitis is inflammation of the gallbladder.

Why the Other Options Do Not Fit
• Hemorrhoidectomy is the removal of hemorrhoids; cholelithiasis is the presence of gallstones.
• Jejunoileostomy is a surgical opening between the jejunum and ileum; cholecystolithiasis specifically means stones inside the gallbladder.
• Colostomy is a surgical opening into the colon; choledocholithiasis means stones inside the common bile duct.', '["Colostomy, Choledocholithiasis","Jejunoileostomy, Cholecystolithiasis","Cholecystectomy, Cholecystitis","Hermorrhoidectomy,Cholelithiasis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T08:27:18.374Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:27:18.374Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:27:18.603Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756784157654-46', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Which of the following biochemical measures of nutritonal status is most likely associated with over nutrition?', 'Serum LDL', 'Serum LDL (low-density lipoprotein or "bad" cholesterol) reflects fat and lipid metabolism. Elevated levels are commonly driven by a chronic overnutrition of saturated fats, trans fats, and excess calories, making it a key biochemical indicator for overnutrition and cardiovascular risk.

The Other Options (Malnutrition/Under-nutrition):
	• Nitrogen balance (B) measures protein intake versus excretion and is typically used to monitor protein catabolism or adequacy.
	• Hemoglobin and hematocrit (C) screen for anemias caused by dietary deficiencies (like iron, folate, or B12).
	• Serum albumin (D) is a standard marker used to evaluate protein-calorie malnutrition or visceral protein depletion.', '["Hemoglobin and hematocrit","Nitrogen balance","Serum albumin","Serum LDL"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T07:52:35.653Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T07:52:35.653Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T07:52:35.846Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756784100025-45', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'An anemia pertaining to anemia induced by easy damage of the RBCs?', 'Hemolytic anemia', 'Hemolytic anemia occurs when red blood cells (RBCs) are destroyed or broken down faster than the bone marrow can produce new ones. This premature destruction (hemolysis) can be caused by intrinsic defects inside the RBCs that make them weak, or external factors like autoimmune attacks, toxins, and infections that damage the cell membrane.

	• Microcytic anemia describes RBCs that are abnormally small in size, most commonly due to iron deficiency.
	• Hemorrhagic anemia is caused by excessive external or internal blood loss (bleeding), not cellular damage.
	• Pernicious anemia is a specific type of vitamin B12 deficiency anemia caused by a lack of intrinsic factor needed for absorption.', '["Microcytic anemia","Pernicious anemia","Hemorrhagic anemia","Hemolytic anemia"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:32:09.237Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:32:09.237Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:32:09.237Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756784012729-44', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Nutrition management for burns:
I. High calorie
II. High protein
III. Sodium controlled
IV. Fluid controlled', 'All of the above', '• I. High Calorie: Burn injuries trigger a massive hypermetabolic and catabolic state. Energy expenditure can double depending on the severity, requiring a high-calorie intake (predominantly from carbohydrates) to prevent significant weight and muscle tissue loss.
• II. High Protein: Large amounts of nitrogen and protein are lost through burn wounds. Aggressive high-protein delivery (often 1.5–2.0 g/kg/day or more) is essential to promote tissue repair, wound healing, and maintain lean mass.
• III. Sodium Controlled & IV. Fluid Controlled: While early-stage burn shock resuscitation demands large amounts of fluids and sodium, the downstream nutritional management phase shifts toward precision. In this phase, fluids and sodium are meticulously monitored, calculated, and controlled to prevent fluid overload, pulmonary edema, and severe electrolyte imbalances (like hypernatremia) while maintaining hemodynamic stability.', '["I and II only","I, II, III only","I, II, IV only","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:39:40.272Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:39:40.272Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:39:40.501Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756783368627-34', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Distinct parts of nutrition diagnostic statement:
I. problem
II. etiology
III. signs and symptoms
IV. Evidence', 'I, II, and III only', '', '["I and III only","I, II, and III only","I, II, and IV only","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T08:19:40.177Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:19:40.177Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:19:40.429Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756783940432-43', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Which body component is directly proportional to basal metabolic rate?', 'Lean body mass', 'Lean body mass (LBM), which includes skeletal muscle, organs, and bone, consists of highly metabolic tissue. Every kilogram of LBM requires a significant amount of oxygen and energy just to maintain itself at rest. Because of this, it is the single most important body composition component directly proportional to your Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR)—as lean mass increases, BMR increases.', '["Body fat","Extracellular volume","Lean body mass","Total body mass"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:16:40.989Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:16:40.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:16:41.225Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756782746857-24', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'The following are the domain of nutrition intervention, EXCEPT:', 'None of the above', 'The 4 Official Domains of Nutrition Intervention:
• Food and/or Nutrient Delivery (ND): Customized food, snacks, enteral/parenteral nutrition, supplements, or feeding assistance.
• Nutrition Education (E): Formal process to instruct or train a patient in a nutrition-related skill or to provide knowledge.
• Nutrition Counseling (C): A supportive process to guide collaborative goal-setting and behavior change.
• Coordination of Nutrition Care (RC): Consulting with or referring to other healthcare providers, institutions, or agencies.', '["Food and nutrient delivery","Nutrition education","Coordination of nutrition care","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:35:18.309Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:35:18.309Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:35:18.309Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756783783107-40', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Diseases within the small intestine generally result in malabsorption because of the impaired function of the organ.', 'True', 'The small intestine is the primary organ responsible for the chemical digestion and absorption of nutrients (such as carbohydrates, proteins, fats, vitamins, and minerals). It features specialized structural adaptations, namely villi and microvilli, which create a massive surface area for nutrient uptake.

When a disease affects the small intestine (such as Celiac disease, Crohn''s disease, or Tropical sprue), it damages this delicate mucosal lining, blunts the villi, and causes widespread inflammation. This impaired structural function directly prevents the organ from absorbing nutrients effectively, leading to clinical malabsorption, nutrient deficiencies, weight loss, and chronic diarrhea.', '["True","False","Maybe","Depends"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T08:50:20.443Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:50:20.443Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:50:20.672Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756783737259-39', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Foods allowed in tyramine-restricted diet:', 'Some type of beans', 'A tyramine-restricted diet is commonly prescribed to individuals taking Monoamine Oxidase Inhibitors (MAOIs) to prevent a life-threatening hypertensive crisis. Tyramine builds up naturally in foods that are aged, fermented, cured, or smoked.

Some type of beans: While broad beans (like fava beans) contain high amounts of L-dopa/tyramine and must be strictly avoided, standard fresh or canned legumes (such as standard green beans, chickpeas, and lentils) are entirely safe and allowed on this diet.', '["Gouda cheese","Smoked ham","Some type of beans","A and B only"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:34:23.173Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:34:23.173Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:34:23.173Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756783507571-36', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Weight loss target for obese adults:
I. 10% weight loss of total body weight over 6 months
II. Dietary changes include an energy deficit of 500-1000 kcal/day
III. TER of 2000 kcal per day', 'I and II only', 'Clinical guidelines for adult weight and obesity management establish specific, realistic milestones to ensure safety and long-term compliance:
• I. 10% weight loss of total body weight over 6 months: Major health organizations (such as the National Institutes of Health and the National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute) define an initial target of reducing baseline body weight by 10% over a 6-month timeframe as the clinical standard. This pace is highly realistic and yields massive improvements in blood pressure, blood glucose, and lipid profiles.
• II. Dietary changes include an energy deficit of 500-1000 kcal/day: To achieve a steady, sustainable weight loss of 1 to 2 pounds per week, the standard prescription is to introduce a daily caloric deficit of 500 to 1,000 kcal below the patient''s Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE).', '["I only","II only","I and II only","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:08:47.984Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:08:47.984Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:08:48.228Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756783422741-35', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'An adverse reaction to a food or food additive that does not involve the immune system.', 'Food intolerances', '', '["Food allergy","Food intolerances","Antigen","Food sensitivity"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T08:16:50.831Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:16:50.831Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:16:51.053Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756783288031-33', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Iatrogenic Malnutrition:', 'Physician-induced malnutrition', '', '["Physician-induced malnutrition","Cachexia","SAM","Anorexia"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T08:28:53.054Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:28:53.054Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:28:53.300Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756783255498-32', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Dietary recommendation for colostomy:', 'Low fiber', 'Immediately following surgery and during the initial recovery phase (typically the first 6 to 8 weeks), patients are placed on a strict low-fiber diet. This recommendation focuses on protecting the intestinal tract as it heals:

• Preventing Stoma Blockages: High-fiber foods (like whole grains, nuts, seeds, and raw fibrous vegetables) do not break down easily. They can form a physical mass that risks completely blocking the narrow stoma opening.
• Reducing GI Distress: A low-fiber, bland diet minimizes intestinal swelling, limits excessive stoma output, and controls gas or severe cramping while the bowel normalizes.', '["Low fiber","High fiber","Vit B12 supplementation","Increased carbohydrate"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T08:41:47.003Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:41:47.003Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:41:47.217Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756783206452-31', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Which of the following is/are true about evaluating nutrient intake of individuals using EAR and RDA?
I. If the intake is greater than RDA, there is a high level of confidence that intake is adequate
II. If the intake is between the EAR and the RDA, the intake probably needs to be improved', 'Both statement are correct', '• Statement I is correct: The Recommended Dietary Allowance (RDA) is calculated to meet the nutritional needs of nearly all (97% to 98%) healthy individuals in a specific life-stage and gender group. Therefore, if an individual''s chronic intake is monitored and found to be greater than the RDA, clinicians have a high level of confidence that their intake is entirely adequate.
• Statement II is correct: The Estimated Average Requirement (EAR) is the intake level estimated to meet the needs of only 50% of healthy individuals. If an individual''s intake falls between the EAR and the RDA, there is high statistical uncertainty regarding its adequacy, and the probability of deficiency is anywhere from 3% to 50%. Because it does not meet the safety margin of the individual''s RDA target, the intake probably needs to be improved or increased.', '["Only I is correct","Only II is correct","Both statement are correct","Both statement are incorrect"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:32:35.773Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:32:35.773Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:32:35.773Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756783081174-30', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Which of the following is not a nutrient essential in blood formation?', 'Vit A', 'While Vitamin A supports immune health, cellular growth, and vision, it is not categorized as a primary, direct building block or regulator for hematopoiesis (blood cell production).

Why the Other Options are Essential
• Protein: Red blood cells are heavily made of proteins. Globin (the protein portion of hemoglobin) requires a constant supply of dietary amino acids to synthesize new red blood cells.
• Folic acid: This B-vitamin (Vitamin B9) is absolutely vital for DNA synthesis and normal cell division in the bone marrow. A deficiency directly causes macrocytic (megaloblastic) anemia.
• Vit C: Vitamin C acts as a critical enhancer for iron absorption in the intestines. Since iron is the absolute core component needed to make hemoglobin, Vitamin C plays an essential indirect role in healthy blood formation.', '["Vit A","Vit C","Protein","Folic acid"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:33:49.428Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:33:49.428Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:33:49.428Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756783040676-29', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Which of the following is common cause of peptic ulcer?', 'Both a and b', 'A peptic ulcer is an open sore or lesion that develops on the inner lining of the stomach (gastric ulcer) or the upper portion of the small intestine (duodenal ulcer). It occurs when the digestive tract''s protective mucous layer drops or when gastric acid production increases, allowing corrosive stomach juices to burn and erode the deeper underlying sub-mucosal tissue layers.

Together, H. pylori infection and NSAID use account for nearly all cases of peptic ulcer disease:
• H. pylori: This bacterium colonizes the protective mucous layer of the stomach and duodenum. It triggers chronic localized inflammation, weakening the lining and allowing corrosive stomach acid to eat away at the tissue.
• Long-term use of NSAIDs: Non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (like ibuprofen, naproxen, and aspirin) block the COX-1 enzyme. Because this enzyme is responsible for producing the protective prostaglandins that maintain the stomach''s mucosal barrier, blocking it leaves the lining vulnerable to acid erosion.', '["H. pylori","Long term use of non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs","Both a and b","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T08:15:16.530Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:15:16.530Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:15:16.530Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756782979746-28', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Dextrose in PN provides this much calorie per gram.', '3 . 4 kcal/gram', 'In Parenteral Nutrition (PN), carbohydrates are delivered as dextrose monohydrate rather than pure anhydrous glucose. The attached water molecule adds weight but no calories, reducing its energy density from the standard dietary carbohydrate value of 4 kcal/g down to 3.4 kcal/gram.

Amino Acids (Protein)
• Energy Value: 4.0 kcal/gram
Intravenous Lipid Emulsions (IVLE / Fats)
• 10% Lipid Emulsion: Provides 1.1 kcal/mL
• 20% Lipid Emulsion: Provides 2.0 kcal/mL
• 30% Lipid Emulsion: Provides 3.0 kcal/mL (typically reserved for pharmacy compounding use only)', '["4 kcal/gram","2 kcal/gram","3 kcal/gram","3 . 4 kcal/gram"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:39:18.777Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:39:18.777Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:39:19.063Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756782917265-27', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'These are the first line of immune system defense.', 'Skin and mucous membranes', 'The Three Lines of Defense
• First Line (Anatomical Barriers): Skin and mucous membranes form the primary physical and chemical shield of the innate immune system. They block pathogens mechanically from entering the body and secrete protective fluids (like sweat, tears, and gastric acid).
• Second Line (Nonspecific Internal Defenses): If pathogens breach the physical barriers, granulocytes and macrophages, along with inflammatory responses and fever, act as the internal backup to destroy the invaders non-specifically.
• Third Line (Adaptive Immunity): T lymphocytes and antibodies represent the highly targeted, specific adaptive immune system, which takes longer to activate but creates immunological memory.', '["Skin and mucous membranes","GIT secretions","T lymphocytes and antibodies","Granulocytes and macrophages"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:39:45.614Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:39:45.614Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:39:45.844Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756782856569-26', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'It is the gold standard in measuring energy requirements by measuring oxygen consumption and carbon dioxide production.', 'Indirect calorimetry', '', '["Bomb calorimeter","Respiration chamber","Indirect calorimetry","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T07:45:12.237Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T07:45:12.237Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T07:45:12.498Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756782799204-25', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', '___ is the application of nutritional principles for enhacing athletic performance.', 'Sports nutrition', '', '["Sports science","Sports nutrition","Athletic nutrition","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T07:50:50.062Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T07:50:50.062Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T07:50:50.261Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756782176217-14', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Zinc is given to burned patients to:', 'Promote wound healing', 'Major burns cause extensive exudative loss of trace minerals through the open wounds. Zinc plays an indispensable role in every stage of tissue repair, including cellular proliferation, collagen synthesis, and re-epithelialization.', '["Prevent diarrhea","Promote wound healing","To supply oxidative enzymes","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T08:16:31.514Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:16:31.514Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:16:31.752Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756782237664-15', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'An eating disorder that is characterized by an individual that is extremely thin but still claims to "feel fat"?', 'Anorexia nervosa', '', '["Bulimia nervosa","Anorexia nervosa","Binging type","Restricting type"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T08:20:55.924Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:20:55.924Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:20:56.175Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756782298544-16', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Which of the following is TRUE about basal metabolic rate?', 'Fever increases BMR', 'When the body develops a fever, its internal temperature rises, which accelerates enzymatic and cellular activities to help fight off infection. As a general clinical rule, BMR increases by approximately 7% for every 1°F rise in body temperature.', '["Fever increases BMR","Pregnancy decreases BMR","Fasting increases BMR","Females have higher BMR than males"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:26:03.193Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:26:03.193Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:26:03.417Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756782377124-17', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) is the constant regurgitation of acidic gastric contents into the lower part of the esophagus with symptoms including frequent and severe heartburn, dysphagia, and excessive belching.', 'True', 'Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) occurs when the lower esophageal sphincter (LES) weakens or relaxes inappropriately. This structural failure allows acidic gastric juices, and sometimes bile, to flow backward (regurgitate) chronically into the esophagus.

Because the lining of the esophagus lacks the protective mucus barrier found in the stomach, this repeated exposure to harsh gastric acid causes intense irritation, inflammation, and injury. This pathophysiology directly manifests as the classic clinical triad:
• Severe, frequent heartburn (pyrosis)
• Dysphagia (difficulty swallowing due to inflammation or strictures)
• Excessive belching (eructation)', '["True","False","Maybe","Depends"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T08:55:32.966Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:55:32.966Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:55:33.192Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756782450322-18', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Compute for the osmolality of 210 ml of 5% amino acids.', '262 mOsm', '1. Find the total grams of amino acids: A 5% solution means there are 5 grams of solute in every 100 mL of fluid.
Total grams =\frac{5\text{\ g}}{100\text{\ mL}}\times 210\text{\ mL}=\mathbf{10.5}\text{\ grams\ of\ amino\ acids}\)
2. Apply the osmolarity factor for amino acids: In clinical nutrition and parenteral calculations, 1 gram of amino acids contributes approximately 10 mOsm to the solution.
3. Calculate the final osmolarity:
\(10.5\text{\ grams}\times 10\text{\ mOsm/g}=\mathbf{105}\text{\ mOsm}\)', '["262 mOsm","300 mOsm","290 mOsm","105 mOsm"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T08:19:24.613Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:19:24.613Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:19:24.613Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756783592031-37', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'What is the total fluid volume for blenderized feeding of 1900 kcal, 1.5:1 dilution?', '1267 ml', 'Total volume (ml) = 1900 kcal/1.5/ml = 1267ml', '["1267 ml","1900 ml","950 ml","2850 ml"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:02:37.308Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:02:37.308Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:02:37.525Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756782138754-13', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Also called portal systematic encephalopathy:', 'Hepatic coma', 'Portal-systemic encephalopathy (PSE) and hepatic encephalopathy are a spectrum of central nervous system dysfunctions caused by liver failure or shunting. Historically and in clinical question banks, hepatic coma (or coma hepaticum) is used interchangeably to describe its severe, final stage.', '["Obstructive cirrhosis","Portal hypertension","Hepatic coma","Pancreatitis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T07:55:10.990Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T07:55:10.990Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T07:55:11.221Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756782081923-12', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'The following are indications of parenteral nutrition, except:', 'Hemodynamically unstable patients', 'Hemodynamically unstable patients (those suffering from active shock, severe hypotension, or critical fluid/electrolyte imbalances) are a strict contraindication for parenteral nutrition, not an indication. Prior to initiating any form of nutritional support, the patient must be stabilized with fluid resuscitation and vasopressors. Attempting to infuse hyperosmolar PN into an unstable patient can worsen metabolic stress, increase the risk of severe refeeding complications, and destabilize hemodynamics further.', '["Severe diarrhea of  >500ml/day stool, or malabsorption","Hemodynamically unstable patients","Intractable vomiting","Small bowel or colon obstruction"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T08:20:47.724Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:20:47.724Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:20:47.941Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756781996177-11', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'A type of cirrhosis caused by obstruction of the bile ducts:', 'Biliary cirrhosis', 'Biliary cirrhosis develops when there is prolonged inflammation, scarring, or permanent mechanical obstruction within the bile duct system. When these ducts are blocked, bile cannot empty normally into the small intestine and instead backs up into the liver.

• Laennec''s cirrhosis: Also known as portal or alcoholic cirrhosis, this most common form of liver disease is explicitly caused by chronic, long-term alcohol abuse.
• Obstructive cirrhosis: While it sounds structurally logical, "obstructive" is a description of the mechanism rather than the formal, standard medical classification for this type of hepatic pathology.
• Cardiac cirrhosis: This rare form of liver scarring stems from severe, long-term right-sided heart failure that creates a chronic backpressure of venous blood (congestive hepatopathy) in the liver.', '["Cardiac cirrhosis","Laennec''s cirrhosis","Biliary cirrhosis","Obstructive cirrhosis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T08:31:47.876Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:31:47.876Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:31:48.120Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756782513801-19', 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'It is the disruption of the internal balance that lies beyond healthy and normal functioning:', 'Pathologic stress', '• Physiologic stress is a healthy, normal response of the body to daily changes or stressors (like exercise or cold weather) aimed at restoring homeostasis.
• When a stressor is too intense or prolonged, it pushes the body''s systems past their normal limits. This maladaptive state results in damage or a failure to maintain balance, turning into pathologic stress (which can eventually manifest as disease or tissue injury).', '["Stress","Physiologic stress","Pathologic stress","Hormonal stress"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:34:05.879Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:34:05.879Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:34:05.879Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788373146879-6', 'anki-deck-1788372990381-1790433139833-43li1', '"Unintentional weight loss related to hypermetabolism secondary to cancer as evidenced by 10% weight loss in 6 months". Which diagnosis domain is this?', 'Clinical', 'Clinical domain covers weight changes, biochemical values, physical findings influenced by disease or condition.', '["Clinical","Intake","None of the above","Behavioral-Environmental"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T10:46:10.942Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:46:10.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.026Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:46:11.249Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788373171785-7', 'anki-deck-1788372990381-1790433139833-43li1', 'Which of the following is NOT a correct PES statement?', 'Hypertension related to age as evidenced by 160/100 mmHg', 'Age is not a modifiable nutrition-related etiology. PES statements must link to nutrition problems the dietitian can address.', '["Hypertension related to age as evidenced by 160/100 mmHg","Inadequate oral intake related to poor appetite as evidenced by","Altered nutrition-related laboratory values related to uncontrolled diabetes as evidenced by FBS 300 mg/dL","Impaired nutrient utilization related to pancreatic insufficiency as evidenced by steatorrhea"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T10:45:19.540Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:45:19.540Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.026Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:45:19.858Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788373123708-5', 'anki-deck-1788372990381-1790433139833-43li1', 'A 28-year-old pregnant woman reports nausea and vomiting, with', 'At risk for malnutrition', 'Reduced intake plus physical findings of muscle wasting indicate she is at risk and needs close monitoring/intervention.', '["At risk for malnutrition","Adequate intake, no problem","Option C","Normal pregnancy symptoms only"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T10:45:39.857Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:45:39.857Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.026Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:45:40.175Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788373220308-9', 'anki-deck-1788372990381-1790433139833-43li1', 'Which of the following is NOT a category of outcomes monitored in the NCP?', 'Nutrition diagnosis statement', 'The nutrition diagnosis is not an outcome—it is a problem statement.', '["Nutrition diagnosis statement","Food/nutrition-related history","Anthropometric measurements","Biochemical data & medical tests"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T10:46:01.522Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:46:01.522Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.026Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:46:01.856Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788373059775-2', 'anki-deck-1788372990381-1790433139833-43li1', 'Which of the following is a nutrition diagnosis domain in NCPT?', 'Clinical, Behavioral-Environmental, Intake', 'Nutrition diagnoses fall into three domains: Intake (NI), Clinical (NC), and Behavioral-Environmental (NB).', '["Clinical, Behavioral-Environmental, Intake","Food/Nutrition-Related History, Anthropometric Measurements, Nutrition-Focused Physical Findings","Assessment, Diagnosis, Intervention","Anthropometrics, Biochemical, Clinical"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T10:45:33.740Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:45:33.740Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.026Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:45:34.059Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788373080763-3', 'anki-deck-1788372990381-1790433139833-43li1', 'Which of the following BEST describes Nutrition Assessment according to the Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics?', 'Collecting, analyzing, and interpreting data to determine a nutrition-related problem', 'Nutrition Assessment is the systematic process of obtaining and interpreting data to identify nutrition problems, forming the basis for the nutrition diagnosis.', '["Collecting, analyzing, and interpreting data to determine a nutrition-related problem","Option B","Planning and documenting diet prescriptions for the patient","Providing individualized counseling sessions"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T10:45:52.579Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:45:52.579Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.026Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:45:52.905Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788373197171-8', 'anki-deck-1788372990381-1790433139833-43li1', 'What is the best nutrition diagnosis?', 'Impaired nutrient utilization related to liver dysfunction as evidenced by steatorrhea', 'Cirrhosis affects nutrient metabolism/absorption.', '["Impaired nutrient utilization related to liver dysfunction as evidenced by steatorrhea","Inadequate energy intake related to poor appetite as evidenced by weight loss","Inadequate oral intake related to ascites as evidenced by early satiety","Option D"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T10:46:06.229Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:46:06.229Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.026Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:46:06.552Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788373017762-0', 'anki-deck-1788372990381-1790433139833-43li1', 'What is the main objective of DOH AO 2019-0033?', 'To establish uniform implementation of the Nutrition Care Process (NCP) in hospitals', 'The AO ensures standardized, evidence-based nutrition care through the NCP in all hospital settings.', '["To establish uniform implementation of the Nutrition Care Process (NCP) in hospitals","To standardize medical documentation across all hospitals","Option C","To replace the role of dietitians with nutrition nurses"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T10:46:16.516Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:46:16.516Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.026Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:46:16.841Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788373038968-1', 'anki-deck-1788372990381-1790433139833-43li1', 'What are the four core steps of the NCP?', 'Assessment, diagnosis, intervention, and monitoring & evaluation', 'These four steps make up the standardized NCP model recognized by DOH and the Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics.', '["Assessment, diagnosis, intervention, and monitoring & evaluation","Screening, assessment, evaluation, and referral","Planning, implementation, documentation, and discharge","Option D"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T10:45:45.592Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:45:45.592Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.026Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:45:45.915Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788373102252-4', 'anki-deck-1788372990381-1790433139833-43li1', 'During nutrition assessment, you observe muscle wasting, temporal wasting, and poor skin turgor. These findings fall under which category?', 'Nutrition-focused physical findings', 'A nutrition-focused physical exam identifies physical signs of malnutrition such as muscle loss, fat depletion, and micronutrient deficiencies.', '["Nutrition-focused physical findings","Food and nutrition-related history","Biochemical data","Anthropometrics"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T10:45:27.007Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:45:27.007Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.026Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:45:27.326Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788374176173-1', 'anki-deck-1788374097792-1790433136794-o55bb', 'A barangay achieved high attendance during nutrition education sessions. However, household dietary practices remained largely unchanged. Which conclusion is appropriate?', 'Knowledge alone does not guarantee behavior change; environmental, economic, social, and cultural factors should also be addressed.', '', '["Nutrition education should be discontinued.","Knowledge alone does not guarantee behavior change; environmental, economic, social, and cultural factors should also be addressed.","Supplemental feeding should replace nutrition education.","Attendance is the best measure of program effectiveness. e. Families should be required to attend additional lectures."]'::jsonb, '["Public Health & Community"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-01T15:14:04.524Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T15:14:04.524Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.069Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T15:14:04.725Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788374203522-2', 'anki-deck-1788374097792-1790433136794-o55bb', 'Which activity best represents the Nutritionist-Dietitian functioning at the population level?', 'Developing a city-wide strategy to improve exclusive breastfeeding rates.', '', '["Counseling an individual with diabetes regarding meal planning.","Developing a city-wide strategy to improve exclusive breastfeeding rates.","Preparing a therapeutic diet for a hospitalized patient.","Conducting an individualized nutrition assessment."]'::jsonb, '["Public Health & Community"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-01T15:13:27.776Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T15:13:27.776Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.069Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T15:13:27.972Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788374229259-3', 'anki-deck-1788374097792-1790433136794-o55bb', 'Which activity best demonstrates continuous quality improvement in a community nutrition program?', 'Regularly monitoring program indicators, identifying gaps, implementing corrective actions, and reassessing performance', 'Continuous Quality Improvement (CQI) is an ongoing, systematic process. It relies on data collection, analyzing processes, testing changes, and evaluating outcomes to make a service better. Option C directly outlines this full, cyclical loop.

Other options describe simple administrative, process-oriented tasks or resource acquisitions. While useful, they only represent single outputs or compliance measures; they do not inherently drive a continuous loop of evaluation and performance optimization.', '["Regularly monitoring program indicators, identifying gaps, implementing corrective actions, and reassessing performance","Preparing accomplishment reports on time","Increasing the number of nutrition meetings conducted","Purchasing additional anthropometric equipment annually"]'::jsonb, '["Public Health & Community"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-01-22T15:13:35.493Z","interval":85,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":6}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T15:13:35.493Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.069Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T15:13:35.690Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788374256211-4', 'anki-deck-1788374097792-1790433136794-o55bb', 'Which practice best reflects effective partnership management?', 'Clearly defining stakeholder roles, responsibilities, communication mechanisms, and shared objectives before implementation.', '', '["Sharing program information only after implementation.","Clearly defining stakeholder roles, responsibilities, communication mechanisms, and shared objectives before implementation.","Allowing each partner to establish separate monitoring systems.","Limiting meetings to year-end accomplishment reporting."]'::jsonb, '["Public Health & Community"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-01T15:13:01.014Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T15:13:01.014Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.069Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T15:13:01.212Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788374277919-5', 'anki-deck-1788374097792-1790433136794-o55bb', 'An evaluation showed that mothers understand the importance of exclusive breastfeeding but discontinue breastfeeding early because they return to work soon after giving birth. Which intervention is most appropriate?', 'Address workplace support, family support, and breastfeeding counseling alongside education.', 'The evaluation explicitly shows that the problem is not a lack of knowledge (the mothers already understand the importance). The actual barrier is structural and environmental (returning to work). Therefore, providing more education or simply testing them again will completely fail to solve the problem.

To support working mothers, a successful public health program must move beyond individual education and address the environment. This includes advocating for workplace support (such as lactation breaks and expression rooms), family backing, and practical counseling to manage milk storage while working.', '["Address workplace support, family support, and breastfeeding counseling alongside education.","Require mothers to attend more seminars.","Distribute additional pamphlets.","Increase post-test scores as the primary objective."]'::jsonb, '["Public Health & Community"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-01-22T15:13:16.365Z","interval":85,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":6}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T15:13:16.365Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.069Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T15:13:16.566Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788374298356-6', 'anki-deck-1788374097792-1790433136794-o55bb', 'Which outcome best demonstrates that a nutrition education program has been effective?', 'Improved adoption of recommended nutrition practices among participants', '', '["High attendance during the seminar","Improved adoption of recommended nutrition practices among participants","Distribution of all educational materials","Completion of the activity within budget"]'::jsonb, '["Public Health & Community"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-01T15:13:22.180Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T15:13:22.180Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.069Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T15:13:22.382Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788374319862-7', 'anki-deck-1788374097792-1790433136794-o55bb', 'A municipality has limited funds for nutrition education. Which strategy is most likely to maximize community reach while maintaining consistent messages?', 'Train Barangay Nutrition Scholars and community volunteers to deliver standardized nutrition education in their communities.', '', '["Train Barangay Nutrition Scholars and community volunteers to deliver standardized nutrition education in their communities.","Conduct repeated seminars exclusively at the Municipal Hall.","Distribute nutrition manuals only.","Limit nutrition education to health professionals."]'::jsonb, '["Public Health & Community"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-01T15:13:59.632Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T15:13:59.632Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.069Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T15:13:59.833Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788374340368-8', 'anki-deck-1788374097792-1790433136794-o55bb', 'Six months after completing a community nutrition education campaign, what is the most appropriate indicator of program success?', 'Percentage of participants demonstrating sustained positive nutrition behaviors related to the campaign objectives', '', '["Number of posters displayed","Percentage of participants demonstrating sustained positive nutrition behaviors related to the campaign objectives","Number of orientation sessions conducted","Amount of educational materials distributed e. Number of facilitators trained"]'::jsonb, '["Public Health & Community"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-01T15:13:49.371Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T15:13:49.371Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.069Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T15:13:49.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788374368303-9', 'anki-deck-1788374097792-1790433136794-o55bb', 'Following six months of nutrition education, caregivers demonstrated improved knowledge but household dietary diversity showed minimal improvement. Which explanation is most appropriate?', 'Knowledge alone is insufficient; economic, environmental, cultural, and household factors also influence food choices.', 'In public health and community nutrition, a critical distinction is always made between outputs (gaining knowledge) and outcomes (actual behavioral change). A caregiver can perfectly understand the value of a diverse diet, but if the household faces economic barriers (poverty), environmental constraints (lack of market access to fresh produce), or deep-seated cultural preferences, they will not be able to translate that knowledge into practice.', '["Knowledge alone is insufficient; economic, environmental, cultural, and household factors also influence food choices.","Nutrition education should be discontinued.","Participants should be required to attend more lectures.","The educational materials should contain more technical information."]'::jsonb, '["Public Health & Community"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-05-12T15:13:32.466Z","interval":195,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":7}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T15:13:32.466Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.069Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T15:13:32.659Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788374153014-0', 'anki-deck-1788374097792-1790433136794-o55bb', 'A municipality reported the following nutrition profile:
Stunting: 27%Wasting: 3%Childhood overweight: 9%Adult obesity: 31%Exclusive breastfeeding: 38%Access to safely managed drinking water: 58%The Municipal Mayor proposes allocating almost the entire nutrition budget to supplemental feeding because "children simply need more food." As the Municipal Nutrition Action Officer, what is the most appropriate recommendation?', 'Recommend a comprehensive package that strengthens First 1,000 Days interventions, breastfeeding promotion, WASH, dietary improvement, and food security while targeting supplemental feeding to nutritionally at-risk children.', '', '["Approve the proposal because inadequate food intake is the primary cause of stunting.","Recommend a comprehensive package that strengthens First 1,000 Days interventions, breastfeeding promotion, WASH, dietary improvement, and food security while targeting supplemental feeding to nutritionally at-risk children.","Increase the duration of supplemental feeding from 120 to 180 days.","Allocate most of the budget to obesity prevention because obesity affects more people. e. Divide the budget equally among all nutrition problems."]'::jsonb, '["Public Health & Community"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-01T15:13:12.558Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T15:13:12.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.069Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T15:13:12.756Z"'::jsonb),
