('anki-card-1757642882217-83', 'anki-deck-1783609193199-1790433139825-f6c4y', 'A measure of egg quality that relates egg weight with the height of its albumin:', 'Haugh unit', '', '["Haugh unit","Grade A","Candling score","Brix"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.234Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788372902904-7', 'anki-deck-1788372725999-1790433136743-6obos', 'A patient taking warfarin asks whether they should completely avoid green leafy vegetables. What is the best nutrition advice?', 'Maintain a consistent vitamin K intake', 'Warfarin competitively inhibits the enzyme vitamin K epoxide reductase complex 1 (VKORC1), blocking the reduction of vitamin K epoxide to vitamin K hydroquinone. Vitamin K hydroquinone is an obligate cofactor for gamma-glutamyl carboxylase, the enzyme that adds carboxyl groups to glutamic acid residues on hepatic clotting factors II (prothrombin), VII, IX, and X, as well as regulatory proteins C and S. Dosing of warfarin is empirically titrated to achieve a therapeutic target INR (typically 2.0 to 3.0) based on the patient''s habitual dietary intake of phylloquinone (vitamin K1, predominantly found in green leafy vegetables). Maintaining consistent daily intake prevents fluctuations in hepatic substrate availability; drastic reductions increase INR to supratherapeutic levels (elevating bleeding risk), whereas sudden spikes bypass VKORC1 inhibition and precipitate subtherapeutic INR (elevating thromboembolic risk).', '["Avoid all vitamin K-containing foods","Increase vitamin K intake substantially","Maintain a consistent vitamin K intake","Eliminate all dietary fat"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-26T15:44:56.889Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":1.7,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-25T15:44:56.889Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.508Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-25T15:44:57.421Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788372752831-0', 'anki-deck-1788372725999-1790433136743-6obos', 'Which nutrient provides the greatest amount of energy per gram?', 'Fat', '', '["Carbohydrate","Protein","Fat","Alcohol"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-26T15:45:27.186Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":1.7,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-25T15:45:27.186Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.508Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-25T15:45:27.491Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788372819464-3', 'anki-deck-1788372725999-1790433136743-6obos', 'What is the primary significance of the Krebs cycle in energy metabolism?', 'It produces NADH and FADH₂ for oxidative phosphorylation', 'During one turn of the Krebs cycle, acetyl-CoA is condensed with oxaloacetate to form citrate, which undergoes a series of decarboxylation and oxidation-reduction reactions catalyzed by enzymes such as isocitrate dehydrogenase and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase. These reactions reduce NAD⁺ and FAD into NADH and FADH₂. These reduced coenzymes then donate their high-energy electrons to the mitochondrial electron transport chain, establishing a proton gradient that powers oxidative phosphorylation via ATP synthase to generate the vast majority of cellular ATP.', '["It produces the majority of ATP directly","It produces NADH and FADH₂ for oxidative phosphorylation","It converts glucose directly into lactate","It produces bile acids"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-26T15:46:53.580Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":1.7,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-25T15:46:53.580Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.508Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-25T15:46:54.260Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788372856294-5', 'anki-deck-1788372725999-1790433136743-6obos', 'Long-chain dietary fatty acids are primarily transported from the intestine initially through the:', 'Lymphatic system', '❌ Portal vein: Transports water-soluble nutrients and short- to medium-chain fatty acids (SCFAs and MCFAs) directly to the liver.
❌ Renal circulation: Directs blood flow to kidneys for filtration and waste excretion; plays no role in initial lipid absorption.
❌ Hepatic artery: Delivers oxygenated arterial blood from systemic circulation to the liver; not an initial transport route from the gut.', '["Lymphatic system","Portal vein","Renal circulation","Hepatic artery"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-26T15:46:26.704Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":1.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-25T15:46:26.704Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.508Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-25T15:46:26.983Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788372772737-1', 'anki-deck-1788372725999-1790433136743-6obos', 'Which GI hormone primarily stimulates pancreatic bicarbonate secretion?', 'Secretin', '❌ Gastrin: Secreted by gastric G cells; primarily stimulates gastric acid (HCl) and intrinsic factor secretion by parietal cells.
❌ CCK: Cholecystokinin (CCK) stimulates gallbladder contraction and pancreatic enzyme secretion, not primary bicarbonate release.
❌ Motilin: Stimulates migrating motor complexes (MMCs) in the interdigestive state to promote GI motility and gastric emptying.', '["Secretin","Gastrin","Motilin","CCK"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-26T15:45:21.819Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":1.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-25T15:45:21.819Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.508Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-25T15:45:22.119Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788372875718-6', 'anki-deck-1788372725999-1790433136743-6obos', 'A patient receiving long-term isoniazid develops peripheral neuropathy. Which nutrient deficiency should be suspected?', 'Vitamin B6', 'Isoniazid is a hydrazine derivative that is structurally similar to pyridoxine. It causes vitamin B6 deficiency through two primary mechanisms: (1) it chemically binds to pyridoxal and pyridoxal-5''-phosphate (PLP) to form inactive isoniazid-pyridoxal hydrazone complexes that are rapidly excreted in the urine, and (2) it competitively inhibits pyridoxal kinase, the enzyme responsible for phosphorylating pyridoxine into bioactive PLP. Because PLP is an obligate coenzyme for glutamic acid decarboxylase (which converts glutamate to GABA) and for enzymes involved in sphingolipid and myelin sheath synthesis, severe depletion produces peripheral axonal degeneration and neuropathy. Prophylactic administration of pyridoxine (25 to 50 mg daily) is routinely indicated for high-risk patients receiving INH.

Isoniazid forms an inactive hydrazone complex with pyridoxal phosphate (PLP) and accelerates its urinary excretion, leading to secondary pyridoxine deficiency and peripheral neuropathy.
❌ Vitamin B1: Thiamine deficiency causes beriberi (wet/dry) or Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome, characteristically tied to alcohol use or poor intake.
❌ Vitamin B12: Cobalamin deficiency causes subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord (SCD) and megaloblastic anemia, typically due to lack of intrinsic factor or ileal disease.
❌ Vitamin C: Ascorbic acid deficiency results in scurvy (petechiae, bleeding gums, impaired collagen synthesis).', '["Vitamin B6","Vitamin B1","Vitamin C","Vitamin B12"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-26T15:46:42.657Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":1.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-25T15:46:42.657Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.508Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-25T15:46:42.977Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788372794460-2', 'anki-deck-1788372725999-1790433136743-6obos', 'Where does glycolysis occur?', 'Cytoplasm', '❌ Mitochondrial matrix: Site of the Krebs (Citric Acid) cycle, pyruvate decarboxylation, and beta-oxidation.
❌ Inner mitochondrial membrane: Site of the electron transport chain (ETC) and ATP synthase for oxidative phosphorylation.
❌ Nucleus: Site of DNA replication and transcription into RNA; contains no glycolytic enzymes.', '["Cytoplasm","Mitochondrial matrix","Inner mitochondrial membrane","Nucleus"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-26T15:45:34.554Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":1.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-25T15:45:34.554Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.508Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-25T15:45:35.170Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788372923211-8', 'anki-deck-1788372725999-1790433136743-6obos', 'Which combination represents the essential fatty acids?', 'Linoleic acid and α-linolenic acid', '❌ EPA and DHA: Conditionally essential / semi-essential omega-3s that can be synthesized endogenously from α-linolenic acid (ALA), though conversion efficiency is limited.
❌ Arachidonic acid and EPA: Arachidonic acid is conditionally essential (synthesized from linoleic acid), and EPA is derived from ALA; neither are primary essential precursors.
❌ Oleic acid and palmitic acid: Oleic acid (omega-9 MUFA) and palmitic acid (SFA) are fully synthesized endogenously via acetyl-CoA and Δ9 desaturase.', '["Linoleic acid and α-linolenic acid","EPA and DHA","Oleic acid and palmitic acid","Arachidonic acid and EPA"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-26T15:45:07.664Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":1.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-25T15:45:07.664Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.508Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-25T15:45:08.059Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788372836388-4', 'anki-deck-1788372725999-1790433136743-6obos', 'Which transporter is primarily responsible for intestinal absorption of fructose?', 'GLUT5', '❌ SGLT1: Sodium-glucose cotransporter 1; actively transports glucose and galactose across the apical membrane, not fructose.
❌ GLUT2: Basolateral transporter that exports glucose, galactose, and fructose out of enterocytes into portal circulation (can translocate apically only during high-sugar surges).
❌ GLUT4: Insulin-dependent glucose transporter found primarily in skeletal muscle and adipose tissue; absent from the intestinal brush border.', '["GLUT5","SGLT1","GLUT2","GLUT4"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-26T15:46:09.325Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":1.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-25T15:46:09.325Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.508Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-25T15:46:09.673Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788372940368-9', 'anki-deck-1788372725999-1790433136743-6obos', 'During prolonged fasting, which metabolic process becomes increasingly important as insulin decreases and glucagon increases?', 'Ketogenesis', 'During prolonged fasting, your body''s stored liver glycogen is completely depleted (usually within 24 hours). As insulin levels fall dramatically and glucagon rises, adipose tissue breaks down fat into free fatty acids.

The liver takes up these fatty acids and undergoes a process called ketogenesis to produce ketone bodies (acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate). These ketone bodies cross the blood-brain barrier and serve as a vital alternative fuel source for the brain and muscles, effectively preserving muscle mass by minimizing the need for protein breakdown.

❌ Glycogenesis: Glycogen synthesis is an anabolic process stimulated by insulin in the well-fed state; inhibited during fasting.
❌ Lipogenesis: De novo fatty acid synthesis requires high insulin and excess energy; shut off when glucagon levels rise.
❌ Protein synthesis: Anabolic tissue building promoted by insulin and amino acid availability; replaced by proteolysis/autophagy in prolonged starvation.', '["Ketogenesis","Glycogenesis","Lipogenesis","Protein synthesis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T08:08:47.387Z","interval":9,"easeFactor":1.3,"repetitions":6}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:08:47.387Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.508Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:08:47.718Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756787842411-12', 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'Diet management for a child with Maple Syrup Urine Disease:', 'Low leucine, isoleucine, valine', 'Maple Syrup Urine Disease (MSUD) is an inherited metabolic disorder caused by a deficiency in the enzyme complex needed to break down protein building blocks. Specifically, the body cannot process the three branched-chain amino acids (BCAAs): leucine, isoleucine, and valine. Without proper breakdown, these amino acids and their toxic byproducts accumulate in the blood and urine, giving the urine a sweet odor reminiscent of maple syrup. If left unmanaged, the buildup becomes highly neurotoxic, causing brain damage, seizures, or coma.', '["Low isoleucine, valine, 100 mg b1 per day","Low leucine, isoleucine, valine","Low leucine, valine, protein","Low tyrosine, phenylalanine"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T10:51:53.997Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:51:53.997Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:51:54.082Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756787361232-3', 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'Dietary management for person with epilepsy:', 'Ketogenic diet', 'A ketogenic diet is a specialized, high-fat, very low-carbohydrate diet medically used to manage epilepsy, especially in patients with drug-resistant or refractory seizures. The severe reduction of carbohydrates forces the body to burn fat for fuel, generating ketone bodies that act as a stabilizing, anticonvulsant energy source for the brain.', '["Regular diet","High carohydrate","Low fat","Ketogenic diet"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T10:49:51.105Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:49:51.105Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:49:51.158Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756787404314-4', 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'Cushing''s disease is characterized by:', 'All of the above', 'Cushing''s disease is a specific type of Cushing''s syndrome caused by a benign tumor (adenoma) in the pituitary gland. This tumor secretes excessive amounts of Adrenocorticotropic Hormone (ACTH), which constantly overstimulates the adrenal glands to produce massive amounts of cortisol (the body''s primary stress hormone).

• Menstrual irregularities: Chronic high cortisol levels disrupt the normal pulsatile release of gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH). This often leads to irregular periods or a complete absence of menstruation (amenorrhea) in women.
• Wasting: Cortisol is a highly catabolic hormone. Prolonged exposure causes muscle wasting and weakness, particularly visible in the thin arms and legs of patients (proximal muscle wasting).
• Low calcium levels: High levels of glucocorticoids significantly decrease intestinal absorption of calcium and increase its excretion through the kidneys. This contributes heavily to the bone loss, weakening, and osteoporosis seen in Cushing''s patients.', '["Low calcium levels","Wasting","Menstrual irregularities","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T10:50:16.460Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:50:16.460Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:50:16.546Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756787469329-5', 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'The following diseases are included in the newborn screening panel EXCEPT:', 'Congenital hyperthyroidism', '• Newborn screening panels test for congenital hypothyroidism (an underactive thyroid gland that lacks sufficient hormone).
• Congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH) is a standard, universally included hormone disorder in newborn screening panels to prevent life-threatening adrenal crises.
• Maple syrup urine disease (MSUD) is a classic inborn error of amino acid metabolism that is globally included in primary newborn screening panels.', '["Maple syrup urine disease","Congenital hyperthyroidism","Congenital adrenal hyperplasia","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-08T10:43:19.445Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:43:19.445Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:43:19.534Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756787505159-6', 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'Phytochemicals found in hot peppers that modulates blood clotting.', 'Capsaicin', 'Capsaicin is the active, spicy chemical compound unique to chili and hot peppers. Beyond giving peppers their heat, capsaicin directly modulates blood clotting by acting as a natural blood thinner. It works by inhibiting platelet aggregation (preventing blood platelets from sticking together to form clots) and enhancing fibrinolytic activity (the body''s process of breaking down clots).

• Curcumin is the powerful anti-inflammatory phytochemical found in turmeric, not hot peppers.
•  Tannins are astringent polyphenols widespread in tea, coffee, and wine that bind to proteins and can inhibit iron absorption.
• Flavonoids are a very broad, massive class of plant pigments found in almost all fruits and vegetables.', '["Capsaicin","Tannins","Flavonoids","Curcumin"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T10:49:57.701Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:49:57.701Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:49:57.785Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756787571157-7', 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'What is the segment of gluten protein associated with an inappropriate immune response in Celiac disease?', 'Gliadin', 'Gluten is composed of two primary proteins: gliadin and glutenin. Gliadin is the specific, alcohol-soluble fraction that contains the toxic amino acid sequences (such as the 33-mer peptide) that the immune system mistakenly recognizes as a threat in celiac disease, triggering the destructive autoimmune response.

• Avenin is the specific storage protein found in oats. While some celiac patients are sensitive to it, it is distinct from gluten proteins.
• Zein is the primary storage protein found in corn (maize) and is entirely safe for individuals with celiac disease.
• Glutenin is the other half of the gluten protein pair responsible for dough elasticity. While it is part of gluten, it is not the primary initiator of the toxic autoimmune reaction compared to gliadin.', '["Glutenin","Gliadin","Zein","Avenin"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T10:50:46.182Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:50:46.182Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:50:46.282Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756788377451-22', 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'A high-fat, low-carbohydrate diet given given to patients to help control seizures.', 'Ketogenic diet', 'A ketogenic (keto) diet is a therapeutic, high-fat, adequate-protein, and very low-carbohydrate diet medically used to manage drug-resistant epilepsy, especially in children. By severely limiting carbohydrates, it forces the body to burn fat for fuel instead of glucose. This metabolic shift produces ketone bodies (acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate), which cross the blood-brain barrier and exert an anticonvulsant, seizure-stabilizing effect on the brain.', '["Ketogenic diet","Ketoacidosis","Ketoanalogues","Acid ash diet"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T10:51:27.029Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:51:27.029Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:51:27.119Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756788266106-20', 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'The following are dietary management for Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome, EXCEPT:', 'High energy and protein tube feeding', 'Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome (WKS) is a severe neurological disorder caused by a profound deficiency of thiamine (Vitamin B1), most commonly seen in chronic alcohol use disorder. Thiamine is vital for glucose metabolism in the brain. Because body stores of thiamine are limited, a lack of this vitamin leads to cellular brain damage, manifesting as acute confusion, vision abnormalities, and severe memory loss.

• Why C is the EXCEPT option (Incorrect management): Initiating immediate high-energy, heavy carbohydrate tube feeding or glucose loads before correcting a thiamine deficiency is incredibly dangerous. Introducing massive calories/glucose rapidly consumes the body''s tiny remaining stores of thiamine to process those nutrients. This can trigger massive lactic acidosis and cause a sudden, catastrophic worsening of the neurological syndrome.', '["Thiamine supplementation","Adequare hydration","Avoidance of alcohol","High energy and protein tube feeding"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-08T10:50:06.182Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:50:06.182Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:50:06.266Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756788502745-24', 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'A neurodegenerative disease characterized by slow and decreased movements, muscular rigidity, resting tremor, posture instability, and decreased dopamine transmission to basal ganglia:', 'Parkinson''s disease', 'Parkinson''s disease is a neurodegenerative disorder caused by the loss of dopamine-producing neurons in the brain. This loss disrupts signals to the basal ganglia, which normally controls smooth, coordinated muscle function.

The classic four cardinal motor signs of this disease: bradykinesia (slow movements), muscular rigidity (stiffness), resting tremors, and postural instability (poor balance)—all directly caused by the drop in dopamine.

• Multiple sclerosis is an autoimmune attack on the myelin sheath of nerves, causing numbness, tingling, and vision issues, not a dopamine deficit.
• Cerebral palsy is a non-progressive movement disorder caused by brain damage that occurs around the time of birth.
• Epilepsy is characterized by sudden, abnormal bursts of electrical activity in the brain that trigger seizures.', '["Multiple sclerosis","Parkinson''s disease","Cerebral palsy","Epilepsy"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T10:44:51.553Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:44:51.553Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:44:51.553Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756787722383-10', 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'The following are hormones that regulate energy metabolism EXCEPT:', 'Calcitonin', 'Calcitonin is a hormone produced by the parafollicular cells (C-cells) of the thyroid gland. Its primary and specific function is to regulate calcium and phosphate balance in the body by lowering blood calcium levels (inhibiting bone-resorbing osteoclasts and promoting bone building). It does not play a direct role in macronutrient breakdown, cellular respiration, or overall energy metabolism.

• Thyroxine (T4) is the primary thyroid hormone responsible for establishing and regulating the body''s basal metabolic rate (BMR), dictating how fast cells utilize oxygen and burn energy.
• Norepinephrine acts as a stress hormone and neurotransmitter that activates the sympathetic nervous system. It rapidly mobilizes stored energy fuels by accelerating fat breakdown (lipolysis) and glycogen conversion into glucose for immediate energy use.
• Insulin is a major anabolic hormone produced by the pancreas. It heavily governs energy metabolism by directing cells to absorb glucose from the blood to either store it as glycogen or fat, or burn it for immediate energy.', '["Thyroxine","Norepinephrine","Calcitonin","Insulin"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T10:52:13.635Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:52:13.635Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:52:13.722Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756788418400-23', 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'Dietary management for fasting hypoglycemia:', 'high carbohydrates', 'Fasting hypoglycemia happens when the body''s baseline sugar stores run dry during long stretches without food. In medical nutrition therapy, the ultimate defense against fasting crashes is a high-carbohydrate diet (typically accounting for 60% to 65% of total daily calories). This ensures liver glycogen stores are completely saturated and continuously available to buffer blood sugar levels during a fast.', '["complex carbohydrates","small frequent meals","high carbohydrates","fiber"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T10:42:32.688Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:42:32.688Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:42:32.778Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756787992303-15', 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'In bone formation, the production of parathyroid hormone is:', 'Decreased', 'During active bone formation, osteoblasts take calcium ions out of the blood to build and mineralize the new bone matrix. As a consequence, blood calcium levels drop. In response to this drop in serum calcium, the body acts to restore homeostasis by increasing parathyroid hormone production to trigger bone resorption. Conversely, when calcium is abundant and actively filling the bones, the production of parathyroid hormone is decreased (suppressed) via negative feedback to prevent further bone breakdown.

*An increase in parathyroid hormone production primarily drives bone resorption (breakdown) because it signals osteoclasts to dissolve bone and release calcium back into the blood.', '["Increased","Decreased","Uncertain","No effect"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-08T10:51:21.492Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:51:21.492Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:51:21.581Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756787957421-14', 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'It is a syndrome characterized by weight loss, anorexia, asthenia, and anemia.', 'Cachexia', 'Cachexia (specifically cancer cachexia or wasting syndrome) is a complex metabolic syndrome defined precisely by involuntary weight loss, anorexia (loss of appetite), asthenia (severe physical weakness/loss of strength), and anemia. Unlike simple starvation, it is driven by systemic inflammation that actively breaks down skeletal muscle and fat tissue.', '["Malnutrition","Cachexia","Anorexia","Mucositis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T10:50:21.978Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:50:21.978Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:50:22.062Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756787907658-13', 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'Nutrition recommendation for protein for patients with Chronic Renal failure:', '0.6 to 0.8 g/kg BW', 'For adults with advanced Chronic Renal Failure (CRF) who are stable and not yet on dialysis, medical guidelines (such as KDOQI clinical guidelines) recommend a low-protein diet to help manage the condition. Restricting dietary protein helps reduce the accumulation of toxic nitrogenous waste products (uremic toxins), minimizes kidney workload, and helps delay the progression toward end-stage renal disease.', '["Greater than or equal to 1.0 g/kg BW","0.6 to 0.8 g/kg BW","2 g/kg BW","0.8 to 1.0 g/kg BW"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-08T10:51:33.753Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:51:33.753Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:51:33.840Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756787614121-8', 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'Dietary management for Wilson''s disease:', 'Copper-restricted diet', 'Wilson''s disease is a rare genetic disorder that prevents the body from properly excreting excess copper through the liver and bile. Because the body cannot get rid of it, copper builds up to toxic levels and deposits directly into vital organs—primarily the liver, brain, and the corneas of the eyes (causing characteristic copper-colored rings called Kayser-Fleischer rings). Left untreated, this toxic accumulation can lead to severe liver failure, neurological damage, and psychiatric symptoms.

The definitive dietary management for Wilson''s disease is a strict copper-restricted diet. Patients must strictly limit their intake of copper-rich foods—such as organ meats, shellfish, mushrooms, nuts, chocolate, and dried beans. This dietary restriction is used alongside medical therapies (like copper-chelating agents or zinc supplements) to prevent further organ toxicity.', '["Copper-restricted diet","Fe-restricted diet","High K diet","Gluten-restricted diet"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T10:45:12.002Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:45:12.002Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:45:12.002Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756787681251-9', 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'A gluten-free diet excludes:', 'Wheat, barley, rye, oats', '', '["Wheat, corn, oats","Barley, soybeans, oats","Wheat, barley, rye, oats","Wheat, barley, rice, oats"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T10:45:54.639Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:45:54.639Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:45:54.639Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756787769214-11', 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'Bone cells with an active role in releasing calcium into the blood.', 'Osteocytes', 'Osteocytes derive from osteoblasts that become trapped in the newly formed mineralized matrix. While historically viewed as passive bystanders, they form an extensive interconnected network via canaliculi that detects mechanical loading and orchestrates mineral homeostasis. Through the expression of parathyroid hormone (PTH) receptors, osteocytes respond to hypocalcemia by upregulating RANKL expression and actively participating in osteocytic osteolysis—a process where they secrete matrix-degrading enzymes (such as MMPs and cathepsins) to resorb surrounding perilacunar bone and mobilize calcium directly into the extracellular fluid and bloodstream.', '["Osteoclast","Osteocytes","Ostegenic cells","Osteoblasts"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T10:42:56.226Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:42:56.226Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:42:56.313Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756787234090-0', 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'All of the following are attributes of functional foods, EXCEPT:', 'High calories', 'Functional foods are explicitly designed or naturally chosen to provide health benefits beyond basic nutrition (like raw caloric energy). They target specific physiological advantages (such as disease prevention) rather than loading the body with empty or high calories.', '["High fiber","Low cholesterol","Reduced caffeine","High calories"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-08T10:42:18.956Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:42:18.956Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:42:19.042Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756788030617-16', 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'Dietary management for a person with Addison''s disease:', 'All of the above', 'Addison''s disease (primary adrenal insufficiency) occurs when the adrenal glands are damaged and cannot produce enough cortisol and aldosterone. Aldosterone is the hormone responsible for holding onto sodium and flushing out potassium. Without it, the body dangerously dumps sodium and retains potassium, while a lack of cortisol slows down metabolism and depletes energy reserves.

Because of this hormonal deficit, the dietary management must directly counteract these imbalances:

• High Na (Sodium): Due to the lack of aldosterone, the kidneys constantly excrete sodium into the urine, causing hyponatremia (low blood sodium). Patients need a high-sodium diet to replace these heavy losses and maintain stable blood pressure.
• Low K (Potassium): Without aldosterone to flush it out, potassium builds up dangerously in the blood (hyperkalemia). Patients must follow a low-potassium diet to prevent cardiac arrhythmias.
• High kcal (Calories): The lack of cortisol causes severe fatigue, muscle wasting, and frequent hypoglycemia (low blood sugar). A high-calorie, nutrient-dense diet with frequent small meals helps sustain energy levels and prevent dangerous blood sugar drops.', '["High Na","Low K","High kcal","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-08T10:49:46.770Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:49:46.770Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:49:46.884Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756788086065-17', 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'Bone cells responsible for the resorption of bone matrix and release of calcium and phosphate from the bone:', 'Osteoclasts', 'Osteoclasts are large, multinucleated cells that function as the "demolition crew" of the skeletal system. They secrete acids and specialized enzymes (like collagenase) to dissolve the mineralized bone matrix. This biological breakdown—called bone resorption—directly liberates and releases locked-up calcium and phosphate ions back into the bloodstream.', '["Osteogenic cells","Osteocytes","Osteoblast","Osteoclasts"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T10:52:32.185Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:52:32.185Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:52:32.273Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756788135145-18', 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'Energy requirement for patients with Chronic Renal Failure:', '35 kcal/kg BW', 'Traditional and classic clinical board guidelines (such as the standard National Kidney Foundation KDOQI Guidelines) heavily establish 35 kcal/kg/day as the baseline calorie target for adults with chronic renal failure. This high energy requirement is necessary to ensure the body does not break down its own muscle tissue for energy (preventing protein-energy wasting), allowing the strictly restricted dietary protein to be used efficiently for tissue repair.

• 30 kcal/kg BW: This is the adjusted baseline commonly allocated specifically for elderly patients (typically 60 years or older) with CRF because they have a lower metabolic rate and a more sedentary lifestyle.', '["25 kcal/kg BW","30 kcal/kg BW","35 kcal/kg BW","40 kcal/kg BW"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-08T10:51:00.966Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:51:00.966Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:51:01.056Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756788200827-19', 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'To be avoided in the diet management of celiac disease:', 'all of the above', 'Gluten is a protein composite containing prolamins (gliadin in wheat, secalin in rye, hordein in barley, and avenin in some oats) that trigger an immune-mediated enteropathy in genetically susceptible individuals expressing HLA-DQ2 or HLA-DQ8 molecules. Gastric and pancreatic enzymes cannot fully break down proline- and glutamine-rich prolamin peptides, allowing them to cross the lamina propria where tissue transglutaminase (tTG) deamidates them, provoking a destructive CD4+ T-helper cell response, crypt hyperplasia, and villous atrophy in the small intestine. Furthermore, commercial soy sauce traditionally utilizes wheat as a primary fermentation ingredient, making it a major source of hidden gluten that must also be avoided unless explicitly labeled gluten-free.', '["rye, oats, wheat, barley","high fat, rye, oats, wheat, barley","soy sauce, rye, oats, wheat, barley","all of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-08T10:46:26.691Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:46:26.691Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:46:26.691Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756788327186-21', 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'Which of the following is/are carcinogens?
I. salt-cured processed foods
II. tobacco
III. coffee and tea', 'I and II only', '• Tobacco is a well-established Group 1 carcinogen (known to cause cancer in humans).
• Salt-cured processed foods, such as Chinese-style salted fish and processed meats, are also classified as Group 1 carcinogens by the World Health Organization (WHO) due to the presence of nitrates and other cancer-causing agents formed during processing.
x Coffee and tea are not classified as carcinogens. In fact, moderate consumption of coffee and tea is often linked to a reduced risk of certain types of cancer due to their high antioxidant content.', '["I only","I and II only","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-08T10:52:04.334Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:52:04.334Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:52:04.411Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756787316135-2', 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', '___ is a common feature in cancer patients.', 'Weight loss', 'Unintentional weight loss is one of the most prominent, universal, and classic hallmarks of cancer. It occurs in up to 60–80% of patients with advanced stages or specific tumor types (such as lung or upper gastrointestinal cancers). It is driven by cancer cachexia—a metabolic syndrome where tumor-induced inflammation breaks down muscle and fat tissue.', '["Taste/smell alteration","Dysphagia","Weight loss","Early satiety"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T10:51:39.524Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:51:39.524Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:51:39.611Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756787280005-1', 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'Phytochemicals that is found in turmeric that inhibits enzymes that activates carcinogens', 'Curcumin', '', '["Tannins","Flavonoids","Capsaicin","Curcumin"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T10:43:02.868Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:43:02.868Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:43:02.954Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754960709833-9', 'anki-deck-1783608714172-1790433142615-7boq2', 'What year was the National Nutrition Council created?', '1974', '', '["1947","1959","1955","1974"]'::jsonb, '["Public Health & Community"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T10:42:33.117Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T10:42:33.117Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.564Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T10:42:33.117Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754960820811-11', 'anki-deck-1783608714172-1790433142615-7boq2', 'Which attribute/s describes a recommendable PHN practitioner?', 'All of the above', '', '["Pleasing personality","Good human relations","Respect for person","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Public Health & Community"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T10:45:59.746Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T10:45:59.746Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.564Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T10:45:59.746Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754960669818-8', 'anki-deck-1783608714172-1790433142615-7boq2', 'A function of public health nutritionist which focuses on assisting the community to solve their diet and nutrition related problems.', 'Program implementer', '', '["Program planner","Program organizer","Program implementer","Program administrator"]'::jsonb, '["Public Health & Community"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-05T10:46:59.181Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T10:46:59.181Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.564Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T10:46:59.181Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754959627468-0', 'anki-deck-1783608714172-1790433142615-7boq2', 'It is a process of enabling people to increase control over and improve their own health.', 'Health Promotion', '', '["Nutrition Education","Nutrition Policy","Health Promotion","Community Organizing"]'::jsonb, '["Public Health & Community"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T10:43:58.595Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T10:43:58.595Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.564Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T10:43:58.595Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754962858548-25', 'anki-deck-1783608714172-1790433142615-7boq2', 'What is the law that institutionalized the National Nutrition Council?', 'PD 491', '', '["PD 491","PD 941","RA 491","RA 941"]'::jsonb, '["Public Health & Community"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T10:43:18.289Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T10:43:18.289Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.564Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T10:43:18.289Z"'::jsonb),
