('anki-card-1758785990059-196', 'anki-deck-1783609268127-1790433136748-a3r8j', 'Which of the following is associated with salmonella? *', 'raw chicken', '', '["hamburger","raw chicken","ground beef","steamed vegetables"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.715Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786001558-197', 'anki-deck-1783609268127-1790433136748-a3r8j', 'Foods remain unsafe when held in the temperature danger zone for more than ____: *', '4 hours', '', '["1 hour","2 hours","3 hours","4 hours"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.715Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786011208-198', 'anki-deck-1783609268127-1790433136748-a3r8j', 'Qualitative and quantitative research methods can be used in foodservice studies. Incidence reports, surveys, and interviews are some of the commonly-used research tools. *', 'Both statements are correct.', '', '["Only the second statement is correct.","Both statements are incorrect.","Only the first statement is correct.","Both statements are correct."]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.715Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788372625366-56', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'Which artificial sweetener is a methyl ester of a dipeptide composed of aspartic acid and phenylalanine?', 'Aspartame', 'Aspartame is 160 to 220 times sweeter than sucrose that is noncariogenic and has limited glycemic response.', '["Aspartame","Sucralose","Saccharin","Acesulfame potassium"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-04T15:13:50.933Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-27T15:13:50.933Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-27T15:13:51.029Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756370050416-19', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'What is the fate of excess intake of dietary protein?', 'After absorption, the extra amino acids will be rapidly degraded', '', '["After absorption, the liver will store the extra amino acids","After absorption, the extra amino acids will be rapidly degraded","Digestion will be decreased by 30-60% resulting in less absorption","After absorption, extra protein will by synthesized and stored for use when protein intake returns to normal"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-01-14T14:05:11.856Z","interval":80,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:05:11.856Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:05:11.856Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788372300280-51', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'Which of the following is an example of soluble dietary fiber?', 'Pectin', 'Pectin is a structural polysaccharide found in the cell walls of fruits and vegetables (such as apples, citrus fruits, and carrots). It is highly water-soluble and absorbs water in the gastrointestinal tract to form a viscous, gel-like substance. This gelation slows down digestion, delays gastric emptying, stabilizes blood sugar levels, and binds to bile acids to help lower LDL cholesterol.

❌ Cellulose: Insoluble structural plant polysaccharide; adds fecal bulk without dissolving or gelling.
❌ Lignin: Insoluble, non-carbohydrate complex polymer (polyphenolic); remains completely intact throughout the GI tract.
❌ Chitin: Insoluble structural polysaccharide found in fungal walls and arthropod exoskeletons; non-gel-forming.', '["Pectin","Cellulose","Chitin","Lignin"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-04T15:15:36.076Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-27T15:15:36.076Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-27T15:15:36.175Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756370426762-26', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'The interior of the intestinal tract is called __', 'Lumen', 'The actual open, interior space inside a tubular structure like the intestines where food and fluids pass.

🔴 Ileum: The final section of the small intestine.
🔴 Mucosa: The innermost tissue layer that surrounds the lumen.
🔴 Lining: A general term for the membrane covering the internal surface.', '["Lumen","Ileum","Mucosa","Lining"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-01-15T06:17:41.327Z","interval":80,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-27T06:17:41.327Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-27T06:17:41.422Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756370385317-25', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'A measure of protein quality which is determined as the percentage of nitrogen in the food and feces of subjects on a diet: containing a test protein.', 'Protein digestibility', 'Protein digestibility (specifically apparent or true digestibility) measures how well a protein source can be broken down and absorbed by the digestive tract.

It is calculated by finding the percentage of nitrogen consumed in the food that does not appear in the feces, revealing how much of that test protein was actually taken up into the body:

Protein Digestibility (%) = [(Nitrogen in Food - Nitrogen in Feces) / Nitrogen in Food] x 100

• Amino acid score: A chemical/laboratory measure that compares the amino acid profile of a test protein against a standard reference protein; it does not utilize living subjects or fecal analysis.
• Protein efficiency ratio (PER): A measure of protein quality based on the weight gain of growing animal subjects divided by their intake of a particular food protein during a specific period.
• Nitrogen balance: Measures the total influx of nitrogen versus total efflux. While it accounts for feces, it relies heavily on measuring urinary nitrogen to determine whether the body is retaining or losing overall protein mass.', '["Amino acid score","Protein efficiency ratio","Protein digestibility","Nitrogen balance"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2034-02-07T14:47:15.262Z","interval":2658,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":9}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:47:15.262Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:47:15.482Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756370311035-24', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'Fats and carbohydrates are called protein sparing due to which of the following factors?', 'Their presence reduces the need of utilizing proteins for energy', '', '["They comprise the major portions of diets","The body does not need protein if fats and carbohydrates are present","Their presence reduces the need of utilizing proteins for energy","They prevent storage of proteins"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-12-30T14:06:53.027Z","interval":65,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:06:53.027Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:06:53.027Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756370238121-23', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'A major component of gastric juice:', 'HCI', 'Hydrochloric acid secretion is responsible for the strong acidic condition of the stomach. With such an acidic environment, ingested proteins are denaturized thus, facilitating their digestion.', '["HCI","HCI","Amylase","Hormones"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-01-14T14:05:17.002Z","interval":80,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:05:17.002Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:05:17.002Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756370211555-22', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'Hormone which stimulates the contraction of gallbladder to secrete bile:', 'Cholecystokinin', '🔴 a. Lipase: Lipase is not a hormone; it is a digestive enzyme primarily produced by the pancreas that works alongside bile to chemically break down lipids (fats) into fatty acids and glycerol.
🔴 b. Insulin: This is a metabolic hormone secreted by the beta cells of the pancreas. Its primary role is to regulate blood glucose levels by helping cells absorb glucose.
🔴 d. Cholecalciferol: This is the chemical name for Vitamin D3.', '["Cholecystokinin","Lipase","Insulin","Cholecalciferol"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-01-04T14:06:36.637Z","interval":70,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:06:36.637Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:06:36.637Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756370171373-21', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'Energy content of monosacharrides:', '4kcal/gram', '', '["2kcal/gram","4kcal/gram","6kcal/gram","9kcal/gram"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-12-30T08:07:26.267Z","interval":65,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T08:07:26.267Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T08:07:26.365Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756370126892-20', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'As essential fatty acid derived from plant oils such as corn oil:', 'Linoleic acid', 'Corn oil is an excellent source of essential fatty acids, which typically exceed 60% of its total composition, with the main contribution of linoleic acid (C18:2).', '["Linoleic acid","Palmitic acid","Linolenic acid","Arachidonic acid"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-01-09T14:08:58.957Z","interval":75,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:08:58.957Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:08:58.957Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756369946967-18', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'A protein derivative that has been widely acclaimed to be non-toxic as a sugar substitute:', 'Aspartame', '', '["Fructose","Dextrose","Galactose","Aspartame"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-12-28T14:09:46.073Z","interval":63,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:09:46.073Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:09:46.073Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756369897553-17', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'The era when the essentiality of vitamins where established.', 'Biological era', 'Biological Era (1900 – 1955) focused on the “discovery and understanding of vitamins and their role in health.”

• a. Naturalistic Era: People believed food contained a single universal nutrient and relied on philosophy rather than chemical science to explain health.
• b. Chemical-Analytical Era: Scientists used early chemistry to discover and measure macronutrients (carbohydrates, proteins, and fats) but did not yet know vitamins existed.
• d. Cellular-Molecular Era: Modern researchers use advanced biotechnology to study how nutrients interact with human genes, DNA, and microscopic cellular pathways.', '["Biological era","Naturalistic","Chemical-Analytical","Cellular-Molecular Era"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-12-30T14:11:13.999Z","interval":65,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:11:13.999Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:11:13.999Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756369854523-16', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'Limiting amino acid in rice:', 'Lysine', 'Lysine, the main limiting essential amino acid in cereals, restricts the absorption and utilization of other amino acids and proteins, which leads to nutrient deficiency.', '["Lysine","Tryptophan","Methionine","Tyrosine"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2033-10-22T08:09:52.990Z","interval":2550,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":8}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:09:52.990Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:09:53.305Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756369780309-15', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'The protein splitting enzymes of the pancreas:', 'Carboxypeptidase and trypsin', 'Both are enzymes produced by the pancreas that break down proteins.

• Pancreatic lipase breaks down fats, not proteins.
• Pancreatic amylase breaks down carbohydrates (starches), not proteins.', '["Carboxypeptidase and trypsin","Carboxypeptidase and pancreatic lipase","Pancreatic lipase and pancreatic amylase","Carboxypeptidase and pancreatic amylase"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-12-30T14:12:06.470Z","interval":65,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":5}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:12:06.470Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:12:06.564Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756369716013-14', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'Which is not considered as a complimentary protein meal?', 'Bread with jam', 'Complementary proteins combine two foods with different missing amino acids to make a complete protein. Bread with jam only provides carbohydrates and sugar. Jam contains no protein, so it cannot complete or complement the protein in bread.

🔴 a. Rice and mongo: grain (rice) and a legume (mung beans) 
🔴 b. Macaroni with cheese: wheat pasta with cheese
🔴 d. Pizza with salami: grain crust with meat and cheese', '["Bread with jam","Rice and Mongo","Macaroni with cheese","Bread with jam"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-12-30T14:07:14.440Z","interval":65,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:07:14.440Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:07:14.440Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788372560281-54', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'Sugar alcohols are produced primarily by the:', 'Reduction of sugars', 'Sugar alcohols (also called polyols) are formed when the reactive carbonyl group—specifically the aldehyde or ketone group—of a sugar molecule is reduced to a hydroxyl group.

During this reduction process, hydrogen is added to the sugar molecule. This converts the open-chain form of a sugar into a non-reducing polyol that lacks any functional carbonyl groups.

Examples of Sugar Reductions:

• Glucose reduces to form Sorbitol (D-glucitol)
• Mannose reduces to form Mannitol
• Xylose reduces to form Xylitol
• Galactose reduces to form Galactitol', '["Oxidation of fatty acids","Reduction of sugars","Hydrolysis of proteins","Oxidation of amino acids"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-04T15:14:50.039Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-27T15:14:50.039Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-27T15:14:50.137Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788372591830-55', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'Which of the following is an example of complementary plant proteins?', 'Rice and mungo', 'Complementary proteins are two or more incomplete plant-based protein sources that, when eaten together, balance out each other''s missing amino acids to create a complete protein profile containing all nine essential amino acids.

The classic formula for this is pairing a grain with a legume:
• Rice (Grain): Is low in the essential amino acid lysine but high in methionine.
• Mungo / Mung Bean (Legume): Is high in lysine but low in methionine.

When you consume them together (a staple combination in Filipino and various Asian cultures), they perfectly offset each other''s deficits.', '["Rice and fish","Chicken and egg","Rice and mungo","Milk and cheese"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-04T15:17:59.073Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-27T15:17:59.073Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-27T15:17:59.170Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756369233283-7', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'Key metabolic carbohydrate:', 'Glucose', 'Glucose, also called blood sugar is the body’s major fuel to provide energy. It attaches to other sugars to form disaccharides and complex carbohydrates.', '["Glucose","Galactose","Fructose","Sucrose"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-12-30T14:10:27.039Z","interval":65,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:10:27.039Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:10:27.134Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756369020058-2', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'Sugar alcohols are made up of:', 'Hydrogenated dextrins', 'Industrially, many bulk sugar alcohols (polyols) used in sugar-free products are produced via the hydrogenation (adding hydrogen molecules under pressure) of corn starch derivatives.

When corn starch is broken down, it creates a mixture of short-chain glucose polymers called dextrins. When these dextrins are completely hydrogenated, the aldehyde groups on the sugars are reduced to alcohol groups, forming a sweet mixture of sugar alcohols known collectively as hydrogenated dextrins or hydrogenated starch hydrolysates.', '["Glucose polymers","Hydrogenated dextrins","Galactose and glucose","Glucose and fructose"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2034-02-07T14:46:46.389Z","interval":2658,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":8}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:46:46.389Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:46:46.595Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756368971497-1', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'It manufacturers enzymes to digest all energy yielding nutrients and release sodium bicarbonate to neutralize chyme that enters the small intestine.', 'Pancreas', 'GI hormones stimulate the pancreas to release sodium bicarbonate, an alkali which neutralizes the acidic chyme, to neutralize hydrochloric acid to pH 5-8.', '["Pancreas","Liver","Kidney","Gallbladder"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-31T08:07:22.254Z","interval":5,"easeFactor":2.35,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T08:07:22.254Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T08:07:22.349Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756368874793-0', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'A complete or high-quality protein can be described as:', 'Containing essential amino acids in proper amounts and proportions', 'In nutrition and dietetics board exams, a complete or high-quality protein is defined by its ability to supply all nine essential amino acids that the human body cannot synthesize on its own.

Beyond just presence, these amino acids must be provided in proper amounts and biological proportions that match human tissue requirements. This allows the body to seamlessly synthesize its own structural and metabolic proteins without encountering a "limiting amino acid." Classic examples include animal sources (meat, fish, poultry, eggs, and dairy) as well as whole soy.', '["Providing some essential amino acids only","Combining essential and non-essential amino acids","Capable of continuous storage","Containing essential amino acids in proper amounts and proportions"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2035-03-06T14:47:05.657Z","interval":3050,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":8}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:47:05.657Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:47:05.861Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756369662180-13', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'All of the following slows or prolongs gastric emptying except:', 'Motilin', 'Motilin is released during the fasting state to stimulate the migrating motor complex (MMC), which “accelerate gastric emptying.”

🔴 Cholecystokinin (CCK): Released in response to fats and proteins in the duodenum, which “slows down the emptying of the stomach.”
🔴 Glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1): Acts as an incretin hormone that also “slows gastric emptying in healthy subjects, obese subjects, type 2 diabetic patients, and critically ill subjects.”', '["Motilin","Cholecystokinin","GLP-1","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2035-05-20T14:46:20.873Z","interval":3125,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":8}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:46:20.873Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:46:21.079Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756369612332-12', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'These are purely ketogenic amino acids:', 'Leucine and Lysine', 'Leucine and Lysine are purely ketogenic amino acids that can be catabolized to form ketone bodies and yield acetyl CoA or acetoacetate.', '["Leucine and Lysine","Leucine and Lysine","Cysteine and Arginine","Leucine and Alanine"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-01-02T14:09:19.586Z","interval":68,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:09:19.586Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:09:19.586Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756369508870-11', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'Nutrients are absorbed through:
I. Simple diffusion
II. Active transport
III. Facilitated diffusion
IV. Pinocytosis', 'All of the above', 'Simple diffusion - Water and small lipids cross into intestinal cells freely;
Facilitated diffusion - water soluble vitamins need a specific carrier to transport them from one side of the cell membrane to the other;
Active transport - glucose and amino acids move against a concentration gradient, which requires energy;
Pinocytosis, a form of endocytosis, in which material enters a cell through its membrane and is incorporated in vesicles for digestion.', '["All of the above","I and III only","I, II, and III","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-01-09T14:05:04.438Z","interval":75,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:05:04.438Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:05:04.438Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756369437607-10', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'Oat bran, legumes, guar, pectins in fruits and vegetables are example of what?', 'Insoluble fibers', '', '["Soluble fibers","Insoluble fibers","Crude fiber","Non-essential amino acids"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-01-04T14:09:13.502Z","interval":70,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:09:13.502Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:09:13.502Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756369357728-9', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'A class of carbohydrates that is also called functional foods:', 'Oligosaccharides', 'Functional foods are foods or food components that offer health benefits beyond basic nutrition (like simple energy provision).

Certain non-digestible oligosaccharides—such as Fructo-oligosaccharides (FOS) and Galacto-oligosaccharides (GOS)—act directly as prebiotics.

Instead of being broken down into simple sugars for immediate energy in the upper digestive tract, they pass into the large intestine where they feed and promote the growth of beneficial gut bacteria (Probiotics). This enhances gut health, microflora balance, and immune function, classifying them firmly as a class of carbohydrates used as functional foods.', '["Sucrose","Oligosaccharides","Fructose","Glucose"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2029-10-28T08:09:31.216Z","interval":1095,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":7}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:09:31.216Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:09:31.548Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756369313480-8', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'A measure of protein quality wherein the content of essential amino acids in food is divided by the amino acid requirements for each group.', 'Amino acid score', 'The amino acid score (also known as the chemical score) is a laboratory-based method used to evaluate protein quality. This measurement of the content of essential amino acids contribute to the nutrition value of a protein.

It is determined mathematically by taking the amount of each essential amino acid in a food protein and dividing it by the corresponding amino acid requirement of a standard reference profile (typically tailored to specific age groups like infants or preschool children):

Amino Acid Score = (mg of essential amino acid in 1 g of test protein / mg of essential amino acid in 1 g of reference protein) x 100

The lowest score among the essential amino acids identifies the limiting amino acid, which determines the overall quality of that specific protein source.

• Protein digestibility: A measure of the percentage of nitrogen in food that is absorbed by the digestive tract based on the difference between dietary intake and fecal excretion.
• Biologic value (BV): A measure of protein quality that calculates the percentage of absorbed nitrogen that is actually retained by the body for growth and maintenance. It requires tracking both fecal and urinary nitrogen in living subjects.
• Protein efficiency ratio (PER): A biological assay that evaluates protein quality by tracking the physical weight gain of growing animal subjects divided by their specific protein intake over a test period.', '["Amino acid score","Protein efficiency ratio","Biologic value","Protein digestibility"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 4, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-14T14:46:43.504Z","interval":16,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:46:43.504Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:46:43.708Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756371392250-46', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'Protein splitting enzyme secreted by the gastric glands and the stomach.', 'Pepsin', 'Pepsin is the main protein-digesting enzyme in the stomach. It is secreted by the gastric glands in an inactive form called pepsinogen. Pepsin cleaves proteins to smaller polypeptides and some free amino acids.', '["Pepsin","Rennin","Gastric lipase","Trypsin"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2034-04-28T08:09:40.815Z","interval":2738,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":8}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:09:40.815Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:09:41.137Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756371441848-47', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'Fatty acids present in animal fats are said to be:', 'Saturated fatty acids', 'In animals, the carbon atoms in the fatty acids are saturated with hydrogen atoms. This allows them to lie straight, and the molecules will pack neatly into a solid arrangement.', '["Saturated fatty acids","Containing hundreds of double bonds","Linoleic acid","Unsaturated fatty acids"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2029-09-26T08:08:04.949Z","interval":1063,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":7}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:08:04.949Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:08:05.271Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756371479365-48', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'The transport form of lipid which is associated with low cholesterol level is:', 'HDL', 'High-Density Lipoprotein (HDL) is famously referred to as "good cholesterol." It acts as a specialized scavenger vessel in the bloodstream.

Its primary physiological function is to gather excess, circulating cholesterol from peripheral tissues and blood vessel walls and transport it back to the liver. The liver then processes, breaks down, and safe-clears this lipid burden out of the body.

Because HDL acts directly to clear away excess fats, higher circulating levels of functional HDL are structurally associated with lower overall peripheral tissue cholesterol levels and a reduced risk of plaque build-up (atherosclerosis).

• LDL (Low-Density Lipoprotein): Acts as the primary transporter that delivers cholesterol to the cells and arteries. High levels of LDL are strongly associated with high peripheral cholesterol and heart disease risk.
• VLDL (Very Low-Density Lipoprotein): Primarily carries newly made triglycerides from the liver out to body tissues.
• IDL (Intermediate-Density Lipoprotein): A short-lived transition state formed as VLDL drops off its triglycerides and degrades into LDL.', '["VLDL","LDL","HDL","IDL"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2034-02-07T08:08:29.859Z","interval":2658,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":8}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:08:29.859Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:08:30.182Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756371543409-49', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'Obtained from food because the body cannot synthesize it in sufficient quantities to meet physiological needs.', 'Essential nutrients', '', '["Nutrients","Food","Essential nutrients","Non-essential nutrients"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-12-30T14:03:41.240Z","interval":65,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:03:41.240Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:03:41.240Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788372240959-50', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'Which of the following is an essential fatty acid?', 'Linoleic acid', 'Linoleic acid is an omega-6 polyunsaturated fatty acid. It is classified as "essential" because the human body lacks the desaturase enzymes required to insert a chemical double bond beyond the carbon-9 position of a fatty acid chain. Because your body cannot synthesize it from scratch, it must be consumed directly through your diet (such as from vegetable oils, nuts, and seeds).

❌ Palmitic acid: Saturated 16-carbon fatty acid synthesized endogenously by fatty acid synthase.
❌ Stearic acid: Saturated 18-carbon fatty acid readily produced in the body from palmitic acid elongation.
❌ Oleic acid: Monounsaturated omega-9 fatty acid synthesized endogenously via Δ9 desaturase.', '["Linoleic acid","Palmitic acid","Stearic acid","Oleic acid"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-04T15:17:09.130Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-27T15:17:09.130Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-27T15:17:09.222Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788372355219-52', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'Which carbohydrate-related compound is important in hepatic conjugation reactions that help detoxify certain substances?', 'Glucuronic acid', 'Glucuronic acid (a sugar acid derived from glucose oxidation) is the essential component in glucuronidation, one of the most important Phase II detoxification pathways in the liver (hepatic system).

During this process:
1. An enzyme transfers glucuronic acid onto toxic, hydrophobic (fat-soluble) substances like bilirubin, steroid hormones, and various drugs/toxins.
2. This attachment significantly increases the molecule''s water solubility.
3. The newly conjugated, non-toxic compound can then be safely excreted from the body via bile or urine.', '["Ribose","Glucuronic acid","Lactose","Maltose"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-04T15:16:29.553Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-27T15:16:29.553Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-27T15:16:29.646Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756371351636-45', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'The one animal polysaccharide that is not present in foods but is generally stored in the liver and muscle:', 'Glycogen', 'Glycogen is the storage form of carbohydrates in animals.', '["Glycogen","Lignin","Polymer","Lecithin"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-01-09T14:07:04.790Z","interval":75,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":5}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:07:04.790Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:07:04.790Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756371306506-44', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'The wavelike action causing relaxation and contraction of the muscles of the GI tract.', 'Peristalsis', '🔴 Digestion: The process of breaking down food into small nutrients.
🔴 Segmentation: Back-and-forth mixing of food in the intestine.
🔴 Excretion: The removal of waste products from the body.', '["Peristalsis","Digestion","Segmentation","Excretion"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-01-14T14:07:40.144Z","interval":80,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:07:40.144Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:07:40.144Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756371255890-43', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'An era in the field of Nutrition characterized by food taboos, magical power and medicinal value of food.', 'Naturalistic', '', '["Naturalistic","Chemical-analytical","Biological","Cellular-molecular"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-01-07T14:12:21.985Z","interval":73,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:12:21.985Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:12:22.078Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756371203559-42', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'All of these are essential amino acids for adults EXCEPT:
I. Arginine
II. Glutamine
III. Lysine
IV. Leucine', 'I and II', '🟢 Arginine (I) is conditionally essential. Adults can make enough of it on their own, though growing children need it in their diet.
🟢 Glutamine (II) is a non-essential amino acid. Your body creates it in sufficient amounts under normal conditions.', '["I and II","II only","III only","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2030-01-16T08:08:44.220Z","interval":1175,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":7}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:08:44.220Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:08:44.541Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756371134828-41', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'Carbohydrate derivative which serves as a detoxifying agent in the liver:', 'Heparin', '❌ Ribose is a 5-carbon sugar used for RNA and ATP; has no detoxifying role.
❌ Pentose is a broad chemical class of 5-carbon monosaccharides; not a specific liver derivative.
❌ Maltose is a simple glucose disaccharide; broken down solely for energy.', '["Heparin","Ribose","Maltose","Pentose"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-01-12T14:05:35.980Z","interval":78,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:05:35.980Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:05:35.980Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756371062016-40', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'The breakdown of triglycerides into fatty acids and glycerol:', 'Lipolysis', '*breakdown - hydrolysis

Lipolysis is also known as TAG degradation.', '["Lipolysis","Glycolysis","Lipogenesis","Beta oxidation"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-01-07T14:06:46.506Z","interval":73,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:06:46.506Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:06:46.506Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756371019402-39', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'Which molecule provides the immediate source of energy for muscle contraction?', 'ATP', 'Adenosine triphosphate (ATP) is the immediate source of energy used to power muscle contractions.

Inside muscle fibers, ATP binds directly to myosin heads. The subsequent hydrolysis of ATP into ADP (adenosine diphosphate) and an inorganic phosphate molecule (Pi) releases immediate chemical energy. This energy powers the myosin "power stroke," sliding the muscle filaments past each other to cause a physical contraction. ATP is also required to disconnect the cross-bridges and pump calcium back into storage.

• Lactic acid: A metabolic byproduct created during anaerobic glycolysis. It is not an energy source for contractions and can contribute to muscle fatigue if it accumulates heavily.
• Glycogen: The storage form of carbohydrates inside liver and muscle cells. While it acts as a premier reservoir of energy, it must first undergo glycogenolysis and glycolysis to generate ATP molecules before the muscles can utilize it.
• Fructose: A simple monosaccharide (fruit sugar) that must travel to the liver to be metabolized and fed into central energy pathways to generate ATP; it cannot be directly used by myosin heads.', '["Lactic acid","ATP","Glycogen","Fructose"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2033-08-03T14:46:28.011Z","interval":2470,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":9}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:46:28.011Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:46:28.217Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756370975587-38', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'The complex formed when bile salts combine with lipid material to facilitate digestion.', 'Micelle', 'Micelles are aggregated lipids formed from bile salts to help the transport and absorption of fat-soluble vitamins and digestive products of lipids in the human body.', '["Micelle","Chylomicron","Micelle","HDL"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-01-07T14:10:58.997Z","interval":73,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:10:58.997Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:10:58.997Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756370930288-37', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'The following are physiological actions of dietary fiber except:', 'Improves mineral absorption', 'Dietary fiber—particularly when paired with food components like phytates, oxalates, and tannins found in high-fiber plant foods—actually binds to essential minerals (such as iron, zinc, calcium, and magnesium) in the intestinal tract. This binding action makes the minerals insoluble and decreases or hinders their absorption rather than improving it.', '["Increases bile acid excretion","Improves glucose tolerance","Decreases stool transit time","Improves mineral absorption"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-12-05T14:46:14.678Z","interval":37,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":5}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:46:14.678Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:46:14.888Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756370867741-36', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'It prevents the reflux of small intestine contents to the stomach:', 'Pyloric sphincter', 'Acts as a barrier to prevent the backward flow (reflux) of small intestine con tents—which contain bile and pancreatic enzymes—back into the stomach.

❌ a. Upper esophageal sphincter: Controls the passage of food into the feeding tube while preventing air from entering the gastrointestinal tract.
❌ c. Lower esophageal sphincter: Regulates food entering the stomach while specifically preventing stomach acid and contents from refluxing upward into the esophagus.
❌ d. Cardiac sphincter: This is simply another name for the lower esophageal sphincter.', '["Pyloric sphincter","Upper esophageal sphincter","Pyloric sphincter","Cardiac sphincter"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-12-30T08:05:57.079Z","interval":65,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":5}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T08:05:57.079Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T08:05:57.185Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756370811360-35', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'It stores bile until needed:', 'Gallbladder', 'The gallbladder acts like a small storage pouch or holding tank. It collects and concentrates bile between meals.

When you eat (especially foods high in fat), hormones trigger the gallbladder to contract, squeezing the stored bile through the bile ducts and into the small intestine to break down fats.', '["Liver","Pancreas","Gallbladder","Stomach"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2030-03-02T08:08:21.456Z","interval":1220,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":7}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:08:21.456Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:08:21.764Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756370785980-34', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'An important function of bile in digestion:', 'Fat emulsification', 'Bile salts are emulsifying agents; they disperse fats and form water-soluble micelles, which solubilize the products of fat digestion.', '["Fat emulsification","Fat hydrolysis","Fat emulsification","Fat absorption"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-01-04T14:07:49.698Z","interval":70,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:07:49.698Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:07:49.698Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756370732781-33', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'The following are Behavioral and Human Arts with established relation with the field of Nutrition except:', 'Chemistry', '', '["Language","History","Chemistry","Sociology"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-01-03T06:17:15.574Z","interval":68,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-27T06:17:15.574Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-27T06:17:15.665Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756370682937-32', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'Enzyme responsible for the breakdown of cooked starch to dextrin:', 'Amylase', 'Amylase produced by the pancreas enters the small intestine to assist in digestion by hydrolyzing complex carbohydrates.', '["Amylase","Lipase","Chymotrypsin","Trypsin"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-01-02T08:05:34.272Z","interval":68,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T08:05:34.272Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T08:05:34.370Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788372517885-53', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'Which class of carbohydrates includes prebiotic compounds such as fructooligosaccharides?', 'Oligosaccharides', 'Oligosaccharides of various origins have been used as pharmacological supplements, food ingredients, in processed food to aid weight control, to regulation of glucose control for diabetic patients and reducing serum lipid levels in hyperlipidemics and other some acute and chronic diseases.', '["Oligosaccharides","Monosaccharides","Oligosaccharides","Disaccharides"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-04T15:14:01.497Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-27T15:14:01.497Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-27T15:14:01.592Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756370643251-31', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'The form in which lipids are transported in the blood after digestion:', 'Chylomicrons', 'Chylomicrons has the highest lipid content, lowest density, composed of TGs, synthesized in the intestinal wall and acted as transport TG in the blood and after a meal; cleared form the blood through the action of lipoprotein lipase.

🔴 Micelles: Tiny droplets that help move fatty acids to the intestinal wall, but they do not travel in the blood.
🔴 Bile salts: Chemicals made by the liver that break large fat drops into smaller ones during digestion.
🔴 Fatty acids: The individual building blocks of fats, which are reassembled into triglycerides inside cells before entering chylomicrons.', '["Chylomicrons","Micelles","Fatty acids","Bile salts"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-01-08T06:16:24.305Z","interval":73,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-27T06:16:24.305Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-27T06:16:24.402Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756369060943-3', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'Manufacturers bile for fat emulsification.', 'Liver', 'Production: The liver makes bile every day.
Storage: The gallbladder only stores and concentrates the bile until your body needs it.', '["Liver","Pancreas","Gallbladder","Stomach"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2034-02-07T14:47:19.852Z","interval":2658,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":9}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:47:19.852Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:47:20.047Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756370606671-30', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'An impairment of health resulting from a dietary, excess, or imbalance of nutrients.', 'Malnutrition', '', '["Undernutrition","Malnutrition","Overnutrition","Nutrient deficiency"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-01-09T14:03:51.738Z","interval":75,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:03:51.738Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:03:51.738Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756370556405-29', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'Disaccharides is an example of:', 'Simplex carbohydrates', '', '["Simplex carbohydrates","Starch","Glycogen","Fiber"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-01-04T14:11:20.530Z","interval":70,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:11:20.530Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:11:20.530Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756370527659-28', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'Which is a function of the saliva?
I. Lubricating dry foods
II. Allow chemoreceptors to active taste sensations', 'All of the above', '', '["I only","II only","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-01-12T08:05:38.866Z","interval":78,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T08:05:38.866Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T08:05:38.964Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756370475760-27', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'A contemporary topic in Nutrition dealing with the nutritional problems arising as a result of medical treatment.', 'Latrogenic malnutrition', '', '["Sport nutrition","Food labeling","Space exploration","Latrogenic malnutrition"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-01-12T14:04:37.121Z","interval":78,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:04:37.121Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:04:37.121Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756369203653-6', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'Transport form of lipid in blood which is associated with high cholesterol levels:', 'LDL', '❌ VLDL (Very-Low-Density Lipoprotein): Transports mainly endogenous triglycerides (not primarily cholesterol) from the liver to adipose and muscle tissue.
❌ IDL (Intermediate-Density Lipoprotein): Short-lived transitional remnant formed during the degradation of VLDL into LDL; not the primary clinical marker for elevated blood cholesterol.', '["LDL","VLDL","HDL","IDL"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2029-07-13T08:09:28.852Z","interval":988,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":7}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:09:28.852Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:09:29.188Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756369158853-5', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'The difference between nitrogen intake and nitrogen excretion:', 'Nitrogen balance', 'At nitrogen equilibrium: N intake = N output; at positive nitrogen balance: N intake > N output; at negative nitrogen balance: N output > N intake.', '["Nitrogen balance","Protein digestibility","Amino acid score","Biologic value"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-01-14T14:12:27.035Z","interval":80,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:12:27.035Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:12:27.132Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756369113122-4', 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'What is the most common, simplest fatty in foods?', 'Stearic and acetic acid', '🟢 Stearic acid is one of the most common long-chain saturated fatty acids found in everyday foods, appearing abundantly in both animal fats (like beef and butter) and plant fats (like cocoa butter).

🟢 Acetic acid is the simplest possible fatty acid because it contains only two carbon atoms (C2), making it the shortest and smallest carboxylic acid classified in this group.', '["Stearic and acetic acid","Acetic and stearic acid","Butyric and acetic acid","Butyric and stearic acid"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-01-07T14:06:25.869Z","interval":73,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:06:25.869Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T14:06:25.869Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756969348926-25', 'anki-deck-1783610504561-1790433132438-14b68', 'Which of the following are included in the nutrition care guidelines for reducing gastroesophageal reflux disease?', 'All of the above', 'Nutrition management of GERD focuses on reducing factors that can worsen reflux symptoms. Large and high-fat meals can delay gastric emptying and may promote reflux. Alcoholic beverages may aggravate symptoms in some individuals, while acidic or highly spiced foods may worsen discomfort, particularly when esophageal irritation is present. Dietary triggers are individualized, so foods that consistently provoke symptoms should be identified and limited.', '["Avoid large, high fat meals","Avoid acidic and highly spiced foods when inflammation exists","Avoid alcoholic beverages","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T11:15:48.752Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T11:15:48.752Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.748Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T11:15:48.752Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756971623603-62', 'anki-deck-1783610504561-1790433132438-14b68', 'Energy requirement for patients on pre-dialysis.', '35 kcal/kg BW', 'For adults with chronic kidney disease (CKD) who are not yet on dialysis, an energy intake of approximately 30–35 kcal/kg body weight/day has traditionally been recommended, with the exact target individualized according to age, physical activity, body size, nutritional status, and metabolic needs. Thus, among the choices, 35 kcal/kg BW is the best answer.', '["25 kcal/kg BW","30 kcal/kg BW","35 kcal/kg BW","40 kca/kg BW"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T10:48:38.683Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T10:48:38.683Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.748Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T10:48:38.683Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756971670550-63', 'anki-deck-1783610504561-1790433132438-14b68', 'The following are characteristics of hypothyroidism EXCEPT:', 'none of the above', 'Cold intolerance, bradycardia, and constipation are all classic manifestations of hypothyroidism. Reduced thyroid hormone slows metabolic activity and affects multiple body systems. Patients may also experience fatigue, weight gain, dry skin, and slowed mental and physical activity.', '["cold intolerance","bradycardia","constipation","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T11:08:54.987Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T11:08:54.987Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.748Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T11:08:54.987Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756971724482-64', 'anki-deck-1783610504561-1790433132438-14b68', 'Patients under MAOI medication should be put in what type of restricted diet?', 'Tyramine-restricted', 'Monoamine oxidase (MAO) normally helps metabolize tyramine in the gastrointestinal tract. MAO inhibition can allow more tyramine to enter the systemic circulation, where it promotes the release of norepinephrine. Excessive tyramine intake while taking traditional, nonselective MAOIs can therefore cause a potentially severe hypertensive reaction, characterized by a rapid increase in blood pressure and associated headache and other symptoms.', '["Tyramine-restricted","Phenylalanine-restricted","BCAA-restricted","Tyrosine-restricted"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T11:00:42.874Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T11:00:42.874Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.748Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T11:00:42.874Z"'::jsonb)
