('anki-card-1754882303319-18', 'anki-deck-1783608681588-1790433140276-5f3w7', 'This term refers to the child  who is "not up to expectations in general health"', 'Failure to thrive', '', '["Low birth weight","Small for gestational age","Failure to thrive","Premature infant"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-08-12T20:00:00.000Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.763Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756790423519-17', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Refers to the increased vascularity of papillary epithelium manifested by soreness of the tongue, atrophy and flattening.', 'Glossitis', '', '["Glossitis","Gingivitis","Periodontitis","Tonsilitis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T13:49:07.693Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:49:07.693Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:49:07.693Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756796000256-90', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Refers to the disruption of blood supply to the brain due to the blockage or rupture of the blood vessels.', 'Cerebrovascular accident', '', '["Hypertension","Coronary heart disease","Atherosclerosis","Cerebrovascular accident"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T14:05:23.569Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T14:05:23.569Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T14:05:23.898Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756795961443-89', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'The gradual thickening of the arteries of the walls due to the information of plaques.', 'Atherosclerosis', 'Atherosclerosis (derived from the Greek words athere meaning gruel or paste, and sclerosis meaning hardness) is the precise medical term for the gradual thickening, narrowing, and hardening of the arterial walls due to the formation of fibrofatty plaques (atheromas).

It begins when the inner lining of an artery (the endothelium) is damaged by factors like high blood pressure or smoking. This triggers a chronic inflammatory response where low-density lipoproteins (LDL) accumulate, become oxidized, and are swallowed by immune cells to form "foam cells." Over time, this mixture of cholesterol, cellular waste, and scar tissue calcifies into a rigid plaque that restricts blood flow.

• Hypertension: High blood pressure. While it causes chronic mechanical stress that damages vessel walls and drastically accelerates plaque formation, it is a hemodynamic measurement rather than the physical plaque architecture itself.
• Cerebrovascular accident (CVA): A stroke. This is a severe, acute clinical outcome or event that happens when a blood vessel supplying the brain either ruptures or becomes completely blocked by a piece of plaque that broke free.
• Coronary heart disease (CHD): A specific structural disease category that refers to the narrowing of the blood vessels feeding the heart muscle. While CHD is almost exclusively caused by atherosclerosis, atherosclerosis is the overarching universal process that can happen in arteries anywhere in the body (such as the brain, legs, or kidneys) [0.1].', '["Hypertension","Coronary heart disease","Atherosclerosis","Cerebrovascular accident"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-31T15:23:22.093Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.35,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T15:23:22.093Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T15:23:22.278Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756795863181-88', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Refers to the failure of the left ventricle to pump effectively; characterized by dyspnea/orthopnea, pulmonary edema, and fatigability.', 'Congestive Heart Failure', 'Congestive Heart Failure (CHF) happens when the heart muscle—specifically the left ventricle—weakens or stiffens and fails to pump blood out to the body effectively.

Because the left ventricle cannot clear the blood it receives, fluid backs up into the lungs. This creates the classic clinical symptoms listed in the prompt:
	• Dyspnea/Orthopnea: Severe shortness of breath, especially when lying down flat (orthopnea), forcing patients to sleep propped up on pillows.
	• Pulmonary Edema: Fluid pooling directly inside the air sacs of the lungs.
	• Fatigability: Overwhelming fatigue because organs and working muscles are starved of oxygenated blood.

• Coronary Artery Disease: The narrowing or blockage of the arteries that supply blood directly to the heart muscle, usually caused by plaque buildup. While it is a leading cause of heart failure, it is the structural artery block rather than the pump failure itself.
• Myocardial Infarction: A heart attack. This is an acute, sudden event where blood flow to a section of the heart muscle is completely cut off, causing tissue death.
• Achlorhydria: A completely unrelated gastrointestinal condition characterized by the total absence of hydrochloric acid in the stomach juices.', '["Myocardial Infarction","Congestive Heart Failure","Coronary Artery Disease","Clinical option 4"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-31T15:48:35.440Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.35,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T15:48:35.440Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T15:48:35.638Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756795807767-87', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Refers to heart attack, death of a cardiac cell/tissue due to inadequate blood supply.', 'Myocardial Infarction', '', '["Myocardial Infarction","Congestive Heart Failure","Coronary Artery Disease"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T14:04:50.701Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T14:04:50.701Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T14:04:51.413Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756795676739-86', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'The elevation of plasma lipids, somtimes called hyperlipoproteinemia', 'Hyperlipidemia', '', '["Hyperlipidemia","Fatty liver","Hemorrhoids","Endocrine-related obesities"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T14:04:38.253Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T14:04:38.253Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T14:04:38.665Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756795550179-85', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Streptokinase are drugs that are able to dissolve blood clots, which may be blocking coronary arteries.', 'Tissue plasminogen activator', 'The question refers to streptokinase, which belongs to a class of medications called thrombolytic agents or "clot busters".

Both streptokinase and tissue plasminogen activator (tPA) function by converting plasminogen into plasmin. Plasmin is the primary enzyme responsible for breaking down fibrin networks, effectively dissolving acute blood clots that block the coronary arteries during a myocardial infarction (heart attack).

• Digitalis: A cardiac glycoside that increases the force of heart contractions (positive inotrope) and slows the heart rate, primarily used for heart failure and atrial fibrillation.
• Nitroglycerin: A powerful vasodilator that relaxes and widens blood vessels to improve blood flow and relieve chest pain (angina), but it cannot dissolve an existing clot.
• Calcium-channel blockers: Anti-hypertensive and anti-anginal medications that relax blood vessels and lower the heart''s workload by disrupting calcium movement into muscle cells.', '["Calcium-channel blockers","Digitalis","Nitroglycerin","Tissue plasminogen activator"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-31T16:37:32.882Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.35,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T16:37:32.882Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T16:37:33.058Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756795513247-84', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Refers to drug that dilate coronary blood vessels, thus increasing the flow of oxygenated blood to myocardium; used to relieve or prevent angina pectoris.', 'Nitroglycerin', 'Nitroglycerin is a potent vasodilator. Once administered, it is converted into nitric oxide, which relaxes the smooth muscle walls of blood vessels. This action dilates the coronary arteries, allowing a rapid surge of oxygenated blood to reach the oxygen-deprived heart muscle (myocardium).

By widening systemic veins and arteries, it decreases the workload on the heart and reduces preload, making it the classic, first-line drug of choice used to immediately relieve or prevent acute attacks of angina pectoris (chest pain).

• Calcium-channel blockers: While these medications also help manage angina and hypertension by relaxing blood vessels and lowering heart rate, they work by blocking calcium ions from entering muscle cells. They are typically used for long-term maintenance rather than serving as the definitive, rapid-relief anti-anginal agent.
• Tissue plasminogen activator (tPA): A powerful "clot-busting" thrombolytic drug used in emergencies to dissolve blood clots during an active ischemic stroke or myocardial infarction (heart attack). It does not function as a simple coronary vasodilator for angina.
• Digitalis (Digoxin): A cardiac glycoside used to treat heart failure and certain arrhythmias. It works by increasing the strength or force of heart contractions and slowing the heart rate, rather than directly dilating the coronary vessels to treat angina.', '["Calcium-channel blockers","Digitalis","Nitroglycerin","Tissue plasminogen activator"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-04T08:10:32.809Z","interval":6,"easeFactor":2.15,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:10:32.809Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:10:33.126Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756792021442-43', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'When there is weakened movement; lazy bowel', 'Atonic', 'In clinical nutrition and gastrointestinal frameworks (specifically within Medical Nutrition Therapy (MNT) for GI disorders), atonic constipation is explicitly defined as a condition characterized by weakened movement or a "lazy bowel".

This occurs when the intestinal walls lose their normal muscular tone (atony) and elasticity, leading to weak peristaltic contractions. Because the rhythmic squeeze is diminished, waste moves too slowly through the large intestine, resulting in slow-transit constipation.

In dietetics, atonic/lazy bowel is traditionally treated by increasing dietary fiber and fluid intake alongside light physical exercise to naturally stimulate peristalsis and restore tone.

• Spastic: Refers to hypertonicity where the intestinal muscles contract too much or undergo spasms, leading to narrow, small, or ribbon-like stools (often associated with Irritable Bowel Syndrome or IBS). It is managed with a low-residue/high-soluble fiber approach rather than aggressive roughage.
• Jejunoileostomy: A surgical procedure creating a bypass between the jejunum and the ileum, historically used for severe obesity or treating specific intestinal conditions.
• Cholecystectomy: The surgical removal of the gallbladder.', '["Atonic","Spastic","Clinical option 3","Clinical option 4"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-31T16:51:51.728Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.35,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T16:51:51.728Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T16:51:51.913Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756789341872-2', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Due to an imbalance of energy intake and expenditure.', 'Simple', '• Also known as exogenous or primary obesity, simple obesity is caused directly by an imbalance between energy intake (eating too many calories) and energy expenditure (not burning enough calories through physical activity). It is the most common form of weight gain and is not caused by an underlying medical condition, endocrine disorder, or genetic mutation.

• Genetic: This refers to obesity caused primarily by hereditary factors or specific genetic syndromes (like Prader-Willi syndrome), rather than just a simple lifestyle energy imbalance.
• Gynoid & Android: These terms describe fat distribution patterns rather than the root cause of the weight gain. Android refers to "apple-shaped" abdominal fat accumulation (common in men), while Gynoid refers to "pear-shaped" hip and thigh fat accumulation (common in women).', '["Android","Gynoid","Simple","Genetic"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-31T16:26:50.510Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.35,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T16:26:50.510Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T16:26:50.691Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756789315656-1', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Lower body fat distribution, pear shaped; known as gynecoid.', 'Gynoid', '', '["Android","Gynoid","Simple","Genetic"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T14:03:15.841Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T14:03:15.841Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T14:03:15.841Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756789284657-0', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Cental body fat distribution, apple-shaped.', 'Android', '', '["Android","Gynoid","Simple","Genetic"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T14:03:10.128Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T14:03:10.128Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T14:03:10.128Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756795462001-83', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Drugs that slow the heart rate and increase strength of cardiac contraction; used in treatment of congestive heart failure (CHF) and arrhythmias. Digoxin is an example.', 'Digitalis', 'Digitalis works by reversibly inhibiting the sodium-potassium ATPase pump in myocardial cells. This inhibition directly causes:
	• Increased Contractile Strength (Positive Inotropy): It triggers a rise in intracellular calcium, which heavily strengthens the force of cardiac contractions, helping an enlarged, failing heart pump blood efficiently.
	• Slower Heart Rate (Negative Chronotropy): It increases vagal tone and slows electrical conduction through the atrioventricular (AV) node. This safely slows down a rapid heart rate, making it highly effective for treating chronic atrial fibrillation and congestive heart failure (CHF).

• Tissue plasminogen activator (tPA): A thrombolytic "clot-busting" agent used in emergencies to dissolve blood clots during an acute ischemic stroke or heart attack; it has no effect on heartbeat strength or rate.
• Nitroglycerin: A potent vasodilator used to relieve angina pectoris (chest pain) by widening the coronary arteries to improve blood flow, rather than changing the mechanical strength of myocardial contractions.
• Calcium-channel blockers: These medications relax blood vessels and lower blood pressure. While they can slow down the heart rate, they actually decrease the strength of heart contractions (negative inotrope), which is the exact opposite of what is described.', '["Calcium-channel blockers","Digitalis","Nitroglycerin","Tissue plasminogen activator"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-04T08:08:56.288Z","interval":6,"easeFactor":2.15,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:08:56.288Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:08:56.601Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756795367789-82', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Refers to drugs that block the flow of calcium into cardiac muscle cells, thus reducing heart contraction. It may also lower BP.', 'Calcium-channel blockers', '', '["Anticoagulants","Beta-adrenergic","Calcium-channel blockers","Digitalis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T14:02:41.303Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T14:02:41.303Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T14:02:41.303Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756794779417-76', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'The surgical removal of gallbladder.', 'Cholecystectomy', '', '["Tonsillectomy","Cholecystectomy","Hemorrhoidectomy","Ileorectal anastomosis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T14:02:17.075Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T14:02:17.075Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T14:02:17.075Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756794763567-75', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Refers to the surgical removal of tonsils.', 'Tonsillectomy', '', '["Tonsillectomy","Cholecystectomy","Hemorrhoidectomy","Ileorectal anastomosis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T14:02:09.042Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T14:02:09.042Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T14:02:09.042Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756794699403-74', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'A short segment of the jejunum is joined to the terminal ileum; effectively 90% of the small bowel.', 'Jejunoileostomy', '', '["Colostomy","Ileostomy","Jejunoileostomy","Tonsillectomy"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T14:02:05.485Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T14:02:05.485Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T14:02:05.485Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756794656155-73', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Refers to a surgical procedure that creates an opening from the ileum to the surface of the abdominal wall to permit drainage of the contents of the small intestine.', 'Ileostomy', '', '["Colostomy","Ileostomy","Jejunoileostomy","Tonsillectomy"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T14:01:58.208Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T14:01:58.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T14:01:58.208Z"'::jsonb),
