('anki-card-1756793208066-62', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Refers to the elevated ammonia in the blood.', 'Hyperammonemia', '', '["Ascites","Esophageal varices","Collateral formation","Hyperammonemia"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T13:59:22.996Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:59:22.996Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:59:22.996Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756793187917-61', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Refers to blood vessels that develop when the blood flow to the liver is obstructed.', 'Collateral formation', 'When normal blood flow through the liver is obstructed (such as from scarring in liver cirrhosis), pressure spikes within the portal vein.

To adapt to this high pressure, the body automatically creates or opens up alternative, secondary blood vessels. This development of bypass networks to route blood around the blockage and back toward the heart is called collateral formation or collateral circulation.

• Esophageal varices: These are the actual enlarged, swollen veins that develop in the lower esophagus as a direct result of collateral formation. While they are vessels, "collateral formation" is the precise term for the overall process of developing these bypass tracts.
• Ascites: The accumulation of fluid within the peritoneal cavity of the abdomen, caused by high portal pressure and a lack of blood proteins.
• Hyperammonemia: A dangerous biochemical buildup of ammonia in the blood, which happens when the liver is too damaged to convert ammonia into urea.', '["Ascites","Esophageal varices","Collateral formation","Hyperammonemia"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-31T12:15:17.788Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.35,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T12:15:17.788Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T12:15:17.982Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756793108937-60', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Pertain to the distended blood vessels due to obstruction.', 'Esophageal varices', 'Esophageal varices are clinically defined as abnormally distended, dilated, or swollen blood vessels (varicose veins) located in the mucosal lining of the lower esophagus.

When blood flow through the portal vein is obstructed (most commonly due to liver cirrhosis), it forces blood to back up into smaller, fragile secondary veins. Under this intense pressure, these small blood vessels stretch and balloon outward, creating the physical distensions known as varices, which are highly prone to rupturing and causing life-threatening bleeding.

• Portal hypertension: This is the underlying hemodynamic state or condition of high blood pressure within the portal venous system, rather than the name of the distended blood vessels themselves.
• Ascites: The pathological accumulation of free fluid within the peritoneal cavity of the abdomen, caused by high vascular pressure leaking fluids.
• Collateral formation: The broad anatomical process or mechanism of opening alternative bypass networks around a blockage, rather than the name of the specific swollen vessels that result from it.', '["Portal hypertension","Ascites","Esophageal varices","Collateral formation"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-31T14:15:39.636Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.35,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T14:15:39.636Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T14:15:39.825Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756792918147-57', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Also called portal systematic encephalopathy, liver malfunction characterized by confusion, apathy and neurological dysfunction.', 'Hepatic coma', 'In traditional nutrition, diet therapy, and nursing board exams, Hepatic coma is used interchangeably with Portal-Systemic Encephalopathy (PSE) and Hepatic Encephalopathy.

This condition represents a complex neuropsychiatric syndrome caused by severe liver malfunction. When the liver fails to detoxify blood from the digestive tract, toxic compounds—principally ammonia—bypass the liver, cross the blood-brain barrier, and accumulate in brain tissue. This triggers progressive neurotoxicity characterized by confusion, apathy, personality changes, slurred speech, and involuntary tremors (asterixis), which can ultimately progress to a deep coma.', '["Fatty liver","Hepatitis","Cirrhosis","Hepatic coma"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-31T14:46:07.858Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.35,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T14:46:07.858Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T14:46:08.056Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756792872665-56', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'The chronic inflammation of the liver replaced with fibrous tissues.', 'Cirrhosis', '', '["Fatty liver","Hepatitis","Cirrhosis","Hepatic coma"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T13:58:04.583Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:58:04.583Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:58:04.583Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756792848900-55', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'The inflammation of the liver caused by alcohol, infection, parasites, drugs, and some supplements.', 'Hepatitis', '', '["Fatty liver","Hepatitis","Cirrhosis","Hepatic coma"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T13:57:53.810Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:57:53.810Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:57:53.810Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756792807702-54', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Refers to the failure of the liver to metabolize fat due to inflammation, hepatic injury or poor fat transport; insulin resistance.', 'Fatty liver', 'Fatty liver (clinically known as steatosis or metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease/MASLD) is the pathologically defined condition where the liver fails to properly metabolize and export lipids. This results in an abnormal accumulation of triglycerides inside more than 5% of the liver cells (hepatocytes).

The prompt describes the classic metabolic pathways that cause this buildup:
	• Insulin resistance: When cells become resistant to insulin, adipose tissue aggressively breaks down stored fats, flooding the liver with free fatty acids.
	• Hepatic injury or poor fat transport: If the liver cells are damaged, they cannot synthesize the essential apoproteins needed to package these lipids into VLDL particles for export. This traps the fat inside the liver tissue, leading directly to a fatty liver.

• Hepatitis: This refers strictly to the inflammation of the liver tissue (often caused by viruses or toxins). While prolonged fatty liver can progress into inflammatory steatohepatitis (NASH/MASH), fatty liver is the specific metabolic stage where fat accumulates.
• Hepatic coma: An end-stage neuropsychiatric complication (portal-systemic encephalopathy) where toxic ammonia builds up in the brain, causing confusion and unconsciousness, completely distinct from early fat transport failures.
• Cirrhosis: The final, irreversible stage of chronic liver disease characterized by extensive fibrosis (scarring) and the destruction of normal liver architecture, rather than the initial accumulation of fat.', '["Fatty liver","Hepatitis","Cirrhosis","Hepatic coma"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-26T14:46:59.879Z","interval":28,"easeFactor":2.15,"repetitions":5}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:46:59.879Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:47:00.091Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756792648977-53', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Pertains to the proliferation microorganisms because of reduction of gastric secretions and other protective mechanisms.', 'Bacterial overgrowth', 'Gastric acid (hydrochloric acid) is one of the body''s primary chemical defense barriers. It sterilizes swallowed food and keeps the upper gastrointestinal tract relatively free of microbes.

When gastric secretions are reduced (hypochlorhydria or achlorhydria)—often due to aging, chronic gastritis, or the long-term use of acid-reducing medications like proton pump inhibitors—this chemical shield fails. Oral and colonic microbes penetrate the proximal gut and multiply unchecked, resulting in bacterial overgrowth (such as Small Intestinal Bacterial Overgrowth, or SIBO). This causes microvilli damage, nutrient malabsorption, and gas production.

• Irritable bowel syndrome (IBS): A functional gastrointestinal disorder marked by abdominal pain, cramping, bloating, and altered bowel habits, but it is defined by visceral hypersensitivity and motility issues rather than being caused by a drop in stomach acid.
• Hemorrhoids: Swollen, inflamed veins located around the anus or lower rectum, completely unrelated to gastric juices or microbial populations.
• Appendicitis: An acute inflammation of the appendix, typically caused by a physical blockage (such as a fecalith) trapping mucus inside the pouch, not by an upper-stomach acid reduction.', '["Appendicitis","Hemorrhoids","Irritable bowel syndrome","Bacterial overgrowth"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 4, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-11T14:47:03.033Z","interval":13,"easeFactor":2.15,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:47:03.033Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:47:03.241Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756792608180-52', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'An intestinal disorder characterized by abdominal discomfort, cramping, diarrhea or constipation.', 'Irritable bowl syndrome', '', '["Appendicitis","Hemorrhoids","Irritable bowl syndrome","Bacterial overgrowth"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T13:56:59.142Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:56:59.142Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:56:59.142Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756792553731-51', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Refers to the ruptures in the hemmorrhoidal veins. This may cause rectal bleeding, may be caused by pregnancy and constipation.', 'Hemorrhoids', '', '["Appendicitis","Hemorrhoids","Irritable bowl syndrome","Bacterial overgrowth"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T13:56:42.110Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:56:42.110Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:56:42.110Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756792415755-50', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Refers to the inflammation of the appendix characterized by severe abdominal pain and tenderness in the right lower quadrant.', 'Appendicitis', '', '["Ulcerative colitis","Crohn''s disease","Appendicitis","Hemorrhoids"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T13:56:34.924Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:56:34.924Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:56:34.924Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756792376024-49', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Refers to the crack-like ulcers accompany inflammation commonly affects the ileum and colon.', 'Crohn''s disease', 'In medical and clinical nutrition board exams, the distinct phrase "crack-like ulcers accompanying inflammation" is the hallmark description of the linear, deep mucosal fissures that form in Crohn''s disease. Because Crohn''s causes transmural inflammation (penetrating all layers of the intestinal wall), these ulcers slice deeply into the tissue, creating a classic macroscopic "cobblestone" appearance.

Crohn''s disease can affect any part of the gastrointestinal tract from the mouth to the anus, but it most commonly affects the ileum (the terminal small intestine) and the colon (large intestine).

• Ulcerative colitis: While it is also an inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) that causes ulceration, it causes shallow, continuous sores confined strictly to the innermost mucosal lining of the colon and rectum, rather than deep, crack-like structural fissures.
• Appendicitis: An acute inflammation of the appendix pouch, presenting with sharp pain and swelling rather than widespread chronic linear ulcerations across the ileum and colon.
• Hemorrhoids: Swollen, distended veins in the lower rectum and anus, completely unrelated to intestinal track wall inflammation or mucosal ulcers.', '["Ulcerative colitis","Crohn''s disease","Appendicitis","Hemorrhoids"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 4, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-11T08:08:27.016Z","interval":13,"easeFactor":2.15,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:08:27.016Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:08:27.343Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756793060858-58', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Increased blood pressure due to obstructed blood flow to the liver.', 'Portal hypertension', 'The portal vein carries blood from the digestive organs to the liver. When structural damage (like cirrhosis or scarring) blocks or obstructs normal blood flow through the liver, pressure builds backward into the portal venous system. This localized increase in blood pressure is clinically defined as portal hypertension.

Complications caused by portal hypertension:

• Collateral formation: The development of alternative, bypass blood vessels that the body creates to divert blood away from the high-pressure portal system.
• Esophageal varices: Enlarged, fragile veins in the lower esophagus that balloon outward because of the backed-up pressure from portal hypertension. These are highly prone to rupturing and causing life-threatening bleeding.
• Ascites: The accumulation of fluid in the peritoneal cavity of the abdomen, caused by a combination of high portal pressure leaking fluid and low blood albumin levels.', '["Portal hypertension","Ascites","Esophageal varices","Collateral formation"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-31T11:19:37.171Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.35,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T11:19:37.171Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T11:19:37.348Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756792343824-48', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Pertains to the chronic inflammation and ulceration of the large intestines'' mucosa characterized by rectal bleeding, fever, malnutrition, and diarrhea with pain and spasm.', 'Ulcerative colitis', 'Ulcerative colitis is an inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) that is strictly limited to the large intestine (colon and rectum).

It affects only the innermost lining, which is the mucosa and submucosa.

Because it originates in the rectum and spreads continuously upward through the colon, it is uniquely characterized by frequent bouts of bloody diarrhea (rectal bleeding), abdominal pain and spasm (tenesmus), fever, and severe malnutrition due to chronic nutrient and fluid loss.

• Appendicitis: An acute inflammation of the appendix, typically presenting with sharp, sudden pain in the lower right abdomen and fever, rather than chronic mucosal ulceration and ongoing rectal bleeding.
• Hemorrhoids: Swollen, inflamed veins in the rectum and anus that can cause bright red rectal bleeding and localized pain, but they do not cause chronic mucosal large intestinal inflammation, fever, or malnutrition.
• Crohn''s disease: While also an inflammatory bowel disease that causes diarrhea, pain, and malnutrition, Crohn''s disease is characterized by transmural inflammation (affecting all layers of the bowel wall) and can occur anywhere along the entire gastrointestinal tract (from mouth to anus) in a patchy, "skip-lesion" pattern, rather than being restricted to the large intestine''s mucosa.', '["Ulcerative colitis","Crohn''s disease","Appendicitis","Hemorrhoids"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-31T13:07:40.430Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.35,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T13:07:40.430Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T13:07:40.623Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756792163683-47', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Pertaines to the presence of inflamed diverticula.', 'Diverticulitis', '', '["Diverticula","Diverticulosis","Diverticulitis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T13:55:52.561Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:55:52.561Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:55:52.561Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756792144986-46', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Refers to the presence of many pouches or diverticula in the large intestine.', 'Diverticulosis', '', '["Diverticula","Diverticulosis","Diverticulitis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T13:55:46.945Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:55:46.945Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:55:46.945Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756792099320-45', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Are sacs or pouches that develop on weakened areas of the intestinal wall.', 'Diverticula', 'Diverticula (singular: diverticulum) is the specific anatomical term for the sacs or pouches themselves. They form when the inner layers of the intestinal wall push outward through weakened spots in the surrounding muscular layer, most commonly in the colon (large intestine).

• Diverticulosis: This is the clinical condition of having these pouches present in the intestines. It describes the overall asymptomatic state of the disease rather than the individual physical pouches themselves.
• Diverticulitis: This refers specifically to the medical complication where these pouches become inflamed or infected, leading to symptoms like severe abdominal pain, fever, and nausea.
• Peptic ulcer: An open sore or lesion that develops on the protective lining of the stomach or duodenum, completely unrelated to structural pouches forming on the intestinal wall.', '["Diverticula","Diverticulosis","Diverticulitis","Clinical option 4"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-31T14:25:36.627Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.35,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T14:25:36.627Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T14:25:36.815Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756792046423-44', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'When there are small ribbon-like stools due to increased tonicity.', 'Spastic', '', '["Atonic","Spastic"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T13:55:28.194Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:55:28.194Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:55:28.194Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756791987292-42', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'The retention of feces in the colon due to decreased gastric emptying time.', 'Constipation', '', '["Diarrhea","Constipation","Diverticulosis","Diverticula"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T13:55:12.361Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:55:12.361Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:55:12.361Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756791873398-40', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Refers to the congenital malformation of the lymphatic system causing losses of protein,  diarrhea, and steatorrhea due to malabsorption.', 'Lymphangiectasia', 'Intestinal lymphangiectasia is a rare protein-losing enteropathy characterized by the abnormal dilation or congenital malformation of the small intestine''s lymphatic vessels.

Because normal lymphatic drainage is blocked or impaired, protein-rich lymph leaks directly into the intestinal cavity. This obstruction causes severe malabsorption of dietary fats, leading to steatorrhea (fatty stools), diarrhea, and significant losses of plasma proteins like albumin.

In clinical dietetics, this condition is traditionally managed with a low-fat diet supplemented with Medium-Chain Triglycerides (MCTs). MCTs bypass the damaged lymphatic system and are absorbed directly into the portal vein, reducing the pressure and leakage within the lymph vessels.

• Tropical sprue: An acquired infectious or bacterial overgrowth syndrome found in tropical regions that primarily causes malabsorption of Vitamin B₁₂ and folate.
• Enteritis: A general medical term indicating inflammation of the small intestine, which can be caused by infections, radiation, or allergies.
• Celiac disease: An autoimmune disorder triggered by eating gluten that damages the mucosal villi of the small intestine.', '["Celiac disease","Tropical sprue","Enteritis","Lymphangiectasia"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-31T16:36:36.115Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.35,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T16:36:36.115Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T16:36:36.294Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756791605960-36', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'A condition of discomfort in the digestive tract that can be physical or psychological in origin; known as indigestion.', 'Dyspepsia', 'Dyspepsia is the formal medical term for indigestion. It describes a broad state of functional discomfort centered in the upper digestive tract.

Dyspepsia is highly unique because it can stem from physical/organic causes (such as peptic ulcers, gastritis, or medication side effects) or entirely psychological origins (such as chronic emotional stress, anxiety, or depression that alters gut motility via the brain-gut axis). Its hallmark symptoms include upper abdominal pain, early fullness during meals, uncomfortable bloating, and nausea.

• Hiatal hernia: A structural, physical defect where the upper portion of the stomach pushes upward through the diaphragm into the chest cavity.
• Gastric atony: A specific physiological condition characterized by a severe loss of muscular tone in the stomach wall, leading to delayed gastric emptying (gastroparesis).
• Gastrectasia: The severe pathological stretching or distension of the stomach walls, caused by a physical, mechanical obstruction at the pylorus.', '["Hiatal hernia","Gastric atony","Gastrectasia","Dyspepsia"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-31T15:43:25.027Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.35,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T15:43:25.027Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T15:43:25.216Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756791551745-35', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Refers to the stenosis of the pylorus which prevents complete emptying, fermentation of contents and dilatation of the stomach caused by ulcer or scar formation, adhesions or cancer.', 'Gastrectasia', 'Gastrectasia (derived from the Greek words gaster meaning stomach, and ektasis meaning stretching or extension) is the medical term explicitly defined as the pathological dilatation or distension of the stomach.

When a patient develops a structural obstruction like pyloric stenosis (narrowing of the stomach''s exit valve) due to chronic ulcers, scar tissue, adhesions, or stomach cancer, food cannot pass normally into the small intestine. This trapped food undergoes stagnation and bacterial fermentation, building up massive amounts of gas and organic acids. The resulting pressure forces the muscular walls of the stomach to over-stretch, leading directly to the profound clinical presentation of gastrectasia.

• Gastric atony: This refers to a total loss of normal muscular tone and contractions (peristalsis) in the stomach wall, typically caused by neurological damage (like diabetic gastroparesis) rather than a physical structural stenosis at the pylorus.
• Dyspepsia: The broad, generic clinical term for indigestion or an upset stomach. While it shares symptoms like bloating or nausea, it is a symptomatic classification rather than a severe structural dilatation defect.
• Hiatal hernia: A structural defect where a portion of the upper stomach pushes upward through the diaphragm into the chest cavity, unrelated to pyloric obstruction or internal fermentation loops.', '["Hiatal hernia","Gastric atony","Gastrectasia","Dyspepsia"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-04T08:09:17.573Z","interval":6,"easeFactor":2.15,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:09:17.573Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:09:17.890Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756791494383-34', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'The lack of gastric tone, delayed emptying time caused by other diseases and emotional disturbance.', 'Gastric atony', 'Gastric atony (clinically known today as gastroparesis) translates literally to a complete lack of muscle tone or strength (a- meaning without, -tony meaning tone) in the stomach wall.

Because the stomach muscles lose their normal tone, they cannot generate the coordinated rhythmic waves (peristalsis) needed to crush and move food along. This leads directly to a severely delayed gastric emptying time in the complete absence of a physical blockage.

This loss of neuro-muscular tone is highly documented as an indirect complication caused by other diseases (most famously nervous system damage from long-standing diabetes or major surgeries) as well as acute emotional disturbances or high stress, which trigger the sympathetic nervous system to aggressively shut down normal gut motility.

• Dyspepsia: The broad, medical term for general indigestion or an upset stomach. While delayed emptying causes dyspeptic symptoms (like bloating and early fullness), dyspepsia is a descriptive symptom classification rather than the underlying muscular tone condition.
• Gastrectasia: The pathological dilatation or extreme stretching of the stomach walls caused by a physical, mechanical blockage trapping food at the exit valve (such as pyloric stenosis from a tumor or severe scar tissue formation).
• Hiatal hernia: A structural anatomical defect where a portion of the upper stomach physically slides or pushes upward through the opening in the diaphragm into the chest cavity.', '["Hiatal hernia","Gastric atony","Gastrectasia","Dyspepsia"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-31T14:30:48.953Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.35,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T14:30:48.953Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T14:30:49.157Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756791398855-33', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Refers to the protrusion of the stomach into the chest through the esophageal hiatus of the diaphragm.', 'Hiatal hernia', 'A hiatal hernia occurs when the upper part of the stomach pushes upward through the esophageal hiatus (the small opening in the diaphragm where the esophagus passes through) and enters the chest cavity.

This structural displacement frequently leads to gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD), heartburn, and chest discomfort because it disrupts the normal anti-reflux barrier.

• Tropical sprue: A chronic diarrheal illness found in tropical regions that causes malabsorption in the small intestine.
• Celiac disease: An autoimmune reaction to eating gluten that damages the lining of the small intestine.
• Dyspepsia: A general medical term for indigestion or upper abdominal discomfort, not a physical structural protrusion.', '["Hiatal hernia","Dyspepsia","Celiac disease","Tropical sprue"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-31T16:21:24.638Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.35,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T16:21:24.638Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T16:21:24.820Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756791129067-32', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Gastric secretory disturbances: No free or combined HCI', 'Achylia gastrica', 'When stomach acid is secreted, some of it remains unattached (free HCl), while some of it binds to proteins or organic matter in the stomach (combined HCl).

In a patient classified with Achlorhydria under this specific grading system, they fail to produce free HCl, but some minimal traces of bound/combined acid may still be detected.

Achylia Gastrica represents a complete shutdown of the secretory glands, meaning absolutely zero free or combined HCl can be found.

• Achlorhydria: Defined strictly as the absence of free hydrochloric acid only.
• Achylia Gastrica: Defined explicitly as the absence of both free and combined hydrochloric acid (alongside the absence of gastric enzymes like pepsin).
• Hypochlorhydria: This indicates low or deficient stomach acid, but some free or combined HCl is still actively produced.
• Hyperchlorhydria: This is the opposite condition—an excessive or elevated amount of gastric acid production.', '["Hyperchlorhydria","Hypochlorhydria","Achlorhydria","Achylia gastrica"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-04T08:08:18.696Z","interval":6,"easeFactor":2.15,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:08:18.696Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:08:19.013Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756791825384-39', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Refers to the fibrosis of the bowel wall due to radiation exposure characterized by diarrhea and malabsorption.', 'Enteritis', 'Radiation enteritis is a functional and structural injury to the small or large intestines caused by receiving radiotherapy (typically for pelvic or abdominal cancers).

While the acute phase involves immediate mucosal inflammation, the chronic or delayed phase is pathologically characterized by progressive submucosal intestinal wall fibrosis and vascular sclerosis. This thickens and stiffens the bowel wall, directly disrupting its ability to absorb nutrients and pump waste properly—leading to chronic diarrhea and severe malabsorption.

Note on Exam Keys: Because standard nutrition and diet therapy board exams use multiple-choice pools derived from older foundational references, this condition is sometimes grouped under its generic localized term (Enteritis) or a textbook typo entry, but clinically and contextually, it represents radiation enteropathy/enteritis.

• Tropical sprue: A chronic diarrheal illness characterized by malabsorption and small bowel structural changes, but it is endemic to tropical regions and caused by a persistent bacterial infection, completely unrelated to radiation exposure.
• Lymphangiectasis: A rare gastrointestinal disorder characterized by pathologically dilated intestinal lymphatic vessels, which blocks lymph drainage and causes protein-losing enteropathy.
• Celiac disease: An autoimmune digestive disorder where consuming gluten (a protein found in wheat, rye, and barley) triggers immune-mediated damage to the villi of the small intestine, not caused by radiation therapy.', '["Celiac disease","Tropical sprue","Enteritis","Lymphangiectasis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 4, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-11T08:09:35.019Z","interval":13,"easeFactor":2.15,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:09:35.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:09:35.344Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756791109902-31', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Gastric secretory disturbances: No free HCI', 'Achlorhydria', '', '["Hyperchlorhydria","Hypochlorhydria","Achlorhydria","Achylia gastrica"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T13:51:55.684Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:51:55.684Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:51:55.684Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756791094651-30', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Gastric secretory disturbances: Low secretion of gastric juices.', 'Hypochlorhydria', '', '["Hyperchlorhydria","Hypochlorhydria","Achlorhydria","Achylia gastrica"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T13:51:48.647Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:51:48.647Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:51:48.647Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756791001074-29', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Pertains to the overflow of gastric juices.', 'Gastrosucchorrhea', 'In medical nutrition and traditional gastrointestinal pathology frameworks, gastrosucchorrhea (historically known as Reichmann''s disease) is explicitly defined as the excessive secretion or overflow of gastric juices.

Under these textbook guidelines, it is traditionally managed with a high-fat diet because fats trigger the release of enterogastrones (like cholecystokinin), which naturally slow down stomach emptying and inhibit gastric acid secretions.

• Gastritis: Refers strictly to the inflammation or irritation of the stomach lining.
• Peptic ulcer: Refers to open sores or lesions that develop on the lining of the stomach or duodenum.
• Hyperchlorhydria: Refers specifically to an excess concentration of hydrochloric acid in the gastric juice, but not necessarily the overall excessive overflow/volume of total secretions.', '["Peptic ulcer","Gastritis","Hyperchlorhydria","Gastrosucchorrhea"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 4, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-11T08:09:47.854Z","interval":13,"easeFactor":2.15,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:09:47.854Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:09:48.179Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756790948193-28', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Refers to the high secretion of gastric juice.', 'Hyperchlorhydria', 'Hyperchlorhydria is the medical term for the abnormally high or excessive secretion of gastric juice and hydrochloric acid (HCl) by the parietal cells in the stomach lining.

This excess of highly acidic gastric juice lowers the stomach pH significantly. In clinical nutrition and dietetics, identifying hyperchlorhydria is crucial because this constant overproduction of digestive juices can irritate the stomach lining, leading directly to conditions like gastritis, acid reflux, or peptic ulcers if left unmanaged.

• Peptic ulcer: A physical sore or lesion that forms on the lining of the stomach or duodenum. While it is frequently caused by long-term hyperchlorhydria, it is the structural damage itself rather than the secretion process.
• Gastrosucchorrhea: An older, highly specific term for continuous gastric juice flow even when fasting, but it is not the standard clinical benchmark term used on board exams.
• Gastritis: The broad medical term for the inflammation of the stomach wall lining, which can be triggered by excess acid, infection, or alcohol use.', '["Peptic ulcer","Gastritis","Hyperchlorhydria","Gastrosucchorrhea"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-31T12:13:49.131Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.35,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T12:13:49.131Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T12:13:49.323Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756790921027-27', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Refers to the inflammation of the gastric mucosa; characterized by vomiting, heartburn, diarrhea and abdominal distention.', 'Gastritis', '', '["Peptic ulcer","Gastritis","Hyperchlorhydria","Gastrosucchorrhea"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T13:51:11.231Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:51:11.231Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:51:11.231Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756790867962-26', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Refers to the removal of stomach', 'Gastrectomy', '', '["Gastrectomy","Android","Irritable bowl syndrome","Gastricsec Jaundice"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T13:51:02.082Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:51:02.082Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:51:02.082Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756790844497-25', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Refers to an eroded lesion in the gastric mucosa or duodenum.', 'Peptic ulcer', 'A peptic ulcer is an open sore or eroded lesion that develops when aggressive digestive juices (hydrochloric acid and pepsin) break through the tissue barriers and eat away at the protective inner lining.

It is the standard umbrella medical term that encompasses both:
	• Gastric ulcers: Ulcerations that form inside the mucosal lining of the stomach.
	• Duodenal ulcers: Ulcerations that form in the upper portion of the small intestine (the duodenum).

• Gastritis: This refers to the broad, generalized inflammation of the stomach lining. While prolonged erosive gastritis can eventually break down the mucosa and lead to an ulcer, gastritis itself describes tissue inflammation rather than a deep, well-defined open lesion or sore.
• Gastrosucchorrhea: An older, specialized clinical term for the continuous, excessive secretion of gastric juices even when a person is completely fasting.
• Hyperchlorhydria: As reinforced by your earlier answer key, this refers strictly to the high concentration or hypersecretion of gastric acid by the parietal cells, which functions as a biochemical cause of ulcers rather than being the physical eroded lesion itself.', '["Peptic ulcer","Gastritis","Hyperchlorhydria","Gastrosucchorrhea"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-31T14:34:31.896Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.35,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T14:34:31.896Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T14:34:32.092Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756790731002-24', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Refers to the structure of the esophagus caused by fibrotic tissues, increased pressure or herniation from adjacent organs, and abnormal growth of tissues.', 'Esophageal obstruction', 'An esophageal obstruction describes any condition that physically narrows, compresses, or blocks the lumen of the esophagus, hindering the passage of food and liquids into the stomach.

The prompt describes the specific mechanical pathways that lead to an obstruction:
	• Fibrotic tissues: Chronic irritation (like severe acid reflux) causes the mucosal lining to scar and stiffen into a tight ring, known as a peptic stricture.
	• Increased pressure or herniation: Conditions such as a large hiatal hernia or vascular anomalies push against the esophagus from the outside, creating external compression.
	• Abnormal growth of tissues: Malignant tumors (esophageal cancer) or benign polyps physically occupy space inside the tube, closing off the pathway.', '["Esophagitis","Dysphagia","Achalasia","Esophageal obstruction"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 4, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-11T14:47:25.925Z","interval":13,"easeFactor":2.15,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:47:25.925Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:47:26.133Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756791780435-38', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Refers to the disease characterized by malabsorption, diarrhea and intestinal disorders.', 'Tropical sprue', '', '["Celiac disease","Tropical sprue","Enteritis","Lymphangiectasis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T13:54:29.315Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:54:29.315Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:54:29.315Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756791748221-37', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'The disease characterized by atrophy of the intestines due to lack of peptidase to digest the gliadin fraction of gluten; known as gluten-sensitive enteropathy.', 'Celiac disease', '', '["Celiac disease","Tropical sprue","Enteritis","Lymphangiectasis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T13:53:58.122Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:53:58.122Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:53:58.122Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756790681823-23', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Pertains to the failure of the LES to relax and open during swallowing.', 'Achalasia', 'Achalasia is a specific esophageal motility disorder characterized by the selective loss of inhibitory neurons in the myenteric plexus. This neurological loss leads directly to the failure of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES) to relax and open completely when a person swallows.

Along with a tightly closed LES, the smooth muscle body of the esophagus fails to produce normal waves of contractions (aperistalsis). This combination traps food and liquids in the food pipe, causing it to dilate over time.

• Esophagitis: An inflammation or irritation of the mucosal lining of the esophagus, often caused by acid reflux (GERD), infections, or allergies, rather than a failure of the sphincter muscle to relax.
• Esophageal obstruction: A general term for a physical blockage in the food pipe, which can be caused by structural issues like strictures, webs, rings, or tumors.
• Dysphagia: The broad clinical term for difficulty swallowing. While dysphagia is the primary symptom that a person with achalasia experiences, it is a symptom name rather than the specific muscular condition described.', '["Esophagitis","Dysphagia","Achalasia","Esophageal obstruction"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-26T14:47:29.086Z","interval":28,"easeFactor":2.15,"repetitions":5}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:47:29.086Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:47:29.290Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756790652565-22', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Refers to the difficulty in swallowing.', 'Dysphagia', '', '["Esophagitis","Dysphagia","Achalasia","Esophageal obstruction"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T13:50:02.144Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:50:02.144Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:50:02.144Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756790629573-21', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Refers to the acute or chronic inflammation of the esophageal wall.', 'Esophagitis', '', '["Esophagitis","Dysphagia","Achalasia","Esophageal obstruction"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T13:49:55.327Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:49:55.327Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:49:55.327Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756790524329-20', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Refers to the inflammation of the throat.', 'Tonsilitis', '', '["Glossitis","Gingivitis","Periodontitis","Tonsilitis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T13:49:42.140Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:49:42.140Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:49:42.140Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756790506396-19', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Refers to the inflammation of the supporting structure of the teeth with a resultant loss of alveolar bone.', 'Periodontitis', 'Periodontitis is a chronic inflammatory disease that directly affects the periodontium—the tissues, ligaments, and supporting structures that surround and anchor the teeth.

Unlike milder gum diseases, periodontitis involves the progressive destruction and loss of the alveolar bone surrounding the teeth. If left untreated, this structural breakdown causes the teeth to become loose and eventually fall out.

• Tonsillitis: Refers to inflammation of the tonsils at the back of the throat.
• Gingivitis: This is the early phase of gum inflammation. While the gums are red and swollen, the inflammation remains confined to the soft tissue and does not cause alveolar bone loss or attachment loss.
• Glossitis: Refers strictly to inflammation or swelling of the tongue.', '["Glossitis","Gingivitis","Periodontitis","Tonsilitis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-31T16:56:05.850Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.35,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T16:56:05.850Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T16:56:06.029Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756790464665-18', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Pertains to the inflammation of the gums caused by plaque, Vit C or niacin deficiency.', 'Gingivitis', '', '["Glossitis","Gingivitis","Periodontitis","Tonsilitis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T13:49:13.093Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:49:13.093Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:49:13.093Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756790257593-16', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Refers to the inflammation of the oral mucosa due to niacin, tryptophan, folic acid, or vit B12 deficiency.', 'Stomatitis', 'Stomatitis is the broad medical term for any inflammation or swelling of the oral mucosa (the soft mucous membrane lining inside the mouth, including the cheeks, gums, inside of the lips, and palate).

Rapidly dividing epithelial cells lining the mouth depend heavily on continuous coenzyme energy networks to replicate. Deficiencies in vitamins like niacin (and its amino acid precursor tryptophan), folic acid, or vitamin B12 disrupt cellular synthesis, causing the protective lining to break down. This leads directly to generalized redness, painful open sores, and burning swelling inside the mouth.

• Angular stomatitis: This is a localized subcategory of inflammation that occurs strictly at the angles (corners) of the mouth, rather than representing a widespread inflammation of the entire oral mucosa. It is classically tied to riboflavin (B2) and iron deficits.
• Cheilosis: This refers specifically to the structural drying, cracking, and splitting of the skin of the lips themselves, rather than targeting the interior mucous membranes lining the oral cavity.
• Cleft lip/cleft palate: A congenital structural birth defect where the tissues of the lip or upper palate fail to fuse properly during embryonic development, unrelated to an acquired adult vitamin-deficiency inflammation.', '["Cleft lip/cleft palate","Cheilosis","Angular stomatitis","Stomatitis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 4, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-11T14:46:56.696Z","interval":13,"easeFactor":2.15,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:46:56.696Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:46:56.894Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756793083055-59', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Refers to the accumulation of fluids in the abdominal cavity.', 'Ascites', '', '["Portal hypertension","Ascites","Esophageal varices","Collateral formation"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T13:58:59.807Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:58:59.807Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:58:59.807Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756790215412-15', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Pertains to lip lesion characterized by fissures of the corners of the lips.', 'Angular stomatitis', '', '["Cleft lip/cleft palate","Cheilosis","Angular stomatitis","Stomatitis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T13:48:35.671Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:48:35.671Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:48:35.671Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756790192413-14', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Refers to the angular fissuring of mouth.', 'Cheilosis', '', '["Cleft lip/cleft palate","Cheilosis","Angular stomatitis","Stomatitis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T13:48:24.219Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:48:24.219Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:48:24.219Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756790174796-13', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Refers to congenital deformities in the lip or palate that prevents adequate closure.', 'Cleft lip/cleft palate', '', '["Cleft lip/cleft palate","Cheilosis","Angular stomatitis","Stomatitis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T13:48:17.980Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:48:17.980Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:48:17.980Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756789951674-12', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'An approach to weight loss to effectively limit food intake by reducing the size of the stomach.', 'Surgery', '', '["Fad diet","Weight cycling","Dangerous drugs","Surgery"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T13:48:11.711Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:48:11.711Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:48:11.711Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756789913359-11', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Like phenylpropanolamine, ephedra, and triiodothyroacetic acid.', 'Dangerous drugs', 'In the context of regulatory laws and professional practice boards, substances like phenylpropanolamine (PPA), ephedra, and triiodothyroacetic acid (TRIAC) are explicitly categorized under Dangerous drugs or banned/restricted pharmaceutical agents due to their severe, life-threatening toxicities.

Rather than being treated as simple lifestyle components or mild dietary trends, health ministries and food and drug administrations classify these compounds strictly as high-hazard substances because of their devastating systemic impacts:

	• Phenylpropanolamine (PPA): Legally restricted and pulled from commercial availability globally due to its confirmed risk of causing hemorrhagic strokes and severe cardiovascular damage.
	• Ephedra: An unregulated herbal amphetamine-like stimulant explicitly banned by the FDA and international drug enforcement units following documented cases of arrhythmias, seizures, and sudden cardiac death.
	• Triiodothyroacetic acid (TRIAC): A highly potent thyroid hormone metabolite classified as a dangerous prescription-only or banned hormone derivative due to its risk of inducing thyrotoxicosis and metabolic shock.', '["Fad diet","Weight cycling","Dangerous drugs","Surgery"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-31T12:20:13.616Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.35,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T12:20:13.616Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T12:20:13.807Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756789869844-10', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Lipoprotein lipase is activated causing more weight gain after weight loss.', 'Weight cycling', 'Lipoprotein lipase (LPL) is the rate-limiting enzyme responsible for the uptake and storage of triglycerides into fat cells.

When an individual loses weight, the body views the loss of fat tissue as a threat to survival. In response, it increases adipose tissue LPL activity to aggressively pull fat from the bloodstream back into storage.

Because LPL activity skyrockets and remains elevated after weight reduction, it actively drives the body to regain the lost weight. This metabolic pull leads directly to weight cycling (commonly known as "yo-yo dieting"), where an individual falls into a repetitive pattern of losing weight only to rapidly regain it.

• Dangerous drugs: Refers to hazardous chemical compounds or banned stimulants (like ephedra or PPA) used to abnormally accelerate metabolism, not the body''s natural enzymatic response to fat loss.
• Fad diet: Represents a short-term, unscientific dietary pattern (like the cabbage soup diet). While fad diets frequently trigger weight cycling because they are unsustainable, the actual physiological activation of LPL is an enzymatic survival mechanism rather than a diet type.
• Surgery: Refers to anatomical weight-loss interventions such as gastric bypass or sleeve gastrectomy.', '["Fad diet","Weight cycling","Dangerous drugs","Surgery"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 4, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-11T08:08:40.479Z","interval":13,"easeFactor":2.15,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:08:40.479Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:08:40.799Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756789838647-9', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Espouse exaggerated or false theories of weight loss and advise consumers to follow inadequate diets.', 'Fad diet', '', '["Fad diet","Weight cycling","Dangerous drugs","Surgery"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T13:47:09.238Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:47:09.238Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:47:09.238Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756789625925-8', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Permit only heat loss; no formation of adenosine, triphosphate; increase basal metabolic rate; resist weight gain.', 'Uncoupling proteins', 'In standard cellular respiration, the mitochondria create an electrochemical proton gradient across their inner membrane, which powers ATP synthase to generate adenosine triphosphate (ATP). Uncoupling proteins (UCPs) act as specialized proton channels that short-circuit this system. They allow protons to leak back across the membrane, completely uncoupling the proton motive force from ATP synthesis.

Because the energy gradient is bypassed, no ATP is formed. Instead, 100% of the energy from food oxidation is dissipated strictly as heat loss. This massive cellular energy drain forces the body to burn through excess calories, which drastically increases the basal metabolic rate and naturally helps the organism resist weight gain.

• Brown fat: Brown adipose tissue (BAT) is the specific tissue that contains a massive abundance of mitochondria packed with these uncoupling proteins. While brown fat is the site where this process happens, Uncoupling proteins are the exact molecular entities responsible for the chemical mechanism described in the prompt.
• White fat: The primary energy-storing adipose tissue. It stores excess calories as large single droplets of triglycerides for later use rather than burning them off as heat.
• Leptin: A hormone secreted by fat cells that signals the brain to suppress appetite and increase baseline metabolism, but it does not act as a mitochondrial membrane proton uncoupler.', '["Leptin","Uncoupling proteins","Brown fat","White fat"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-04T08:10:28.672Z","interval":6,"easeFactor":2.15,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:10:28.672Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:10:28.998Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756789517879-6', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Due to Cushing syndrome, hypothyroidism, pregnancy.', 'Endocrine-related obesities', '', '["Genetic","Hypertrophic","Hyperplastic","Endocrine-related obesities"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T13:45:53.480Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:45:53.480Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:45:53.480Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756789486330-5', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Childhood onset obesity, increase in number of fat cells.', 'Hyperplastic', '', '["Genetic","Hypertrophic","Hyperplastic","Endocrine-related obesities"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T13:45:35.624Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:45:35.624Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:45:35.624Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756789457431-4', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Adult onset obesity; increase in size of fat cells.', 'Hypertrophic', '', '["Genetic","Hypertrophic","Hyperplastic","Endocrine-related obesities"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T13:45:28.607Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:45:28.607Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:45:28.607Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756789427332-3', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Ob gene that codes for leptin; the 40% chance of being obese when one parent is obese is responsible for obesities in many cases.', 'Genetic', '', '["Genetic","Hypertrophic","Hyperplastic","Endocrine-related obesities"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T13:45:10.116Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:45:10.116Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T13:45:10.116Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756795317108-81', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Blockers blocks norepinephrine receptors in heart muscle and thus reduce the strength of the heart beat and may correct certain arrhythmias.', 'Beta-adrenergic', 'Beta-adrenergic blockers (commonly known as Beta-blockers, such as metoprolol or atenolol) work by directly binding to and blocking beta-1 adrenergic receptors on heart muscle cells.

By blocking the binding of stress hormones like norepinephrine and epinephrine, these medications decrease the force and strength of the heartbeat (negative inotropic effect) and lower the heart rate. This reduces the heart''s overall workload and oxygen demand while stabilizing electrical pathways to correct certain arrhythmias (irregular heartbeats).

• Anticoagulants: Medications used to prevent blood clot formation, typically by disrupting the coagulation cascade. They have no effect on norepinephrine receptors or heartbeat strength.
• Digitalis: A cardiac glycoside that does the opposite for strength—it increases the strength of the heartbeat (positive inotrope) while slowing the rate down, primarily used in heart failure management.
• Calcium-channel blockers: These medications block calcium ions from entering heart and blood vessel muscle cells. While they can treat arrhythmias and lower blood pressure, they target calcium channels rather than norepinephrine receptors.', '["Anticoagulants","Beta-adrenergic","Calcium-channel blockers","Digitalis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-04T08:08:33.743Z","interval":6,"easeFactor":2.15,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:08:33.743Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:08:34.066Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756795259244-80', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Prevents the tendency to form blood clots in patient with CAD, valve damage. Example is aspirin.', 'Anticoagulants', 'In generalized board exam questions, Anticoagulants (and antithrombotic families) are standard classifications for medications designed to prevent the tendency to form blood clots inside blood vessels and heart chambers.

They are heavily utilized in patients with coronary artery disease (CAD) to keep plaque rupture from causing a complete vascular blockage (heart attack), and in patients with valve damage or mechanical valves where turbulent blood flow puts them at extreme risk for clot formation.

Note on Pharmacology: While pharmacology textbooks distinguish between antiplatelet agents (like aspirin, which keeps platelets from clumping) and anticoagulants (like warfarin or heparin, which target coagulation cascade factors), standard board exam keys often use Anticoagulants as the macro-level matching category for clot-preventing medications when antiplatelets are not listed as a standalone choice.

• Digitalis: A cardiac glycoside that increases the force of heart contractions and slows heart rate; used primarily to treat heart failure and atrial fibrillation, not to directly prevent clotting.
• Beta-adrenergic (Beta-blockers): Medications that block epinephrine to lower heart rate and blood pressure, reducing the heart''s overall workload and oxygen demand.
• Calcium-channel blockers: Medications that relax and widen blood vessels by blocking calcium entry into muscle cells, which helps treat hypertension and stable angina rather than halting clot synthesis.', '["Anticoagulants","Beta-adrenergic","Calcium-channel blockers","Digitalis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-31T13:11:50.468Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.35,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T13:11:50.468Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T13:11:50.664Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756795127167-79', 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Assessment criteria for classification of BP for adults:

Normal:
Prehypertensive:
