('anki-card-1758787377796-40', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Ketone bodies can be used as fuel by all the following tissues except: *', 'red blood cells', '', '["the brain","skeletal muscles","the heart","red blood cells"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T04:54:41.270Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T04:54:41.270Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T04:54:41.270Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787333331-39', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Urea synthesis takes place primarily in the: *', 'liver', '', '["skeletal muscle","liver","kidneys","small intestines"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, 'The liver makes urea, while the kidneys filter and excrete it.', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-30T07:04:12.523Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-29T07:04:12.523Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-29T07:04:12.523Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787319799-38', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Tests for an elderly vegetarian patient indicate pernicious and megaloblastic anemia resulting from abnormally large “macrocytic” red blood cells that undergo rapid hemolysis. These results are most probably related to a vitamin deficiency in: *', 'folacin and cobalamin', 'Megaloblastic anemia (abnormally large, macrocytic red blood cells) is caused by a defect in DNA synthesis during red blood cell development. This specific type of anemia is caused by a deficiency in either folacin (folic acid/vitamin B9) or cobalamin (vitamin B12).

The patient is an elderly vegetarian. Vitamin B12 (cobalamin) is found almost exclusively in animal-derived foods. Because of this, long-term vegetarians are at an exceptionally high risk of developing a cobalamin deficiency.', '["ascorbic acid","riboflavin and niacin","folacin and cobalamin","pantothenic acid"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-16T01:24:14.354Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T01:24:14.354Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T01:24:14.429Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787950935-91', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Body water is lost mainly through: *', 'urine', '', '["feces","urine","insensible perspiration","lungs"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-03T06:22:26.870Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:22:26.870Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:22:26.870Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787930364-89', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'It protects folate from oxidative destruction: *', 'vitamin C', 'Folate (Vitamin B9) is a highly unstable water-soluble molecule that is extremely susceptible to degradation via heat, UV light, and environmental oxygen. Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) acts as a highly effective water-soluble antioxidant and oxygen-scavenger.

In food matrixes, the digestive tract, and blood plasma, Vitamin C shields folate from breaking down by neutralizing free radicals and reducing the primary products of folate oxidation (such as quinonoid dihydrofolate) back into their active, usable forms. Because of this symbiotic protective relationship, a deficiency in Vitamin C often accelerates the depletion and urinary excretion of oxidized folate derivatives.', '["vitamin A","vitamin B₂","vitamin C","vitamin E"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:39:07.060Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:39:07.060Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:39:07.234Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787922271-88', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'It competes with calcium for absorption: *', 'Mg', 'Magnesium (Mg) and calcium (Ca) share a common transport and homeostatic regulatory system within the gastrointestinal tract. When consumed in large or highly concentrated supplemental doses, magnesium and calcium directly compete with each other for absorption pathways across the small intestinal mucosa.

• Sodium & Potassium: These monovalent extracellular and intracellular cations are absorbed through distinct, independent active transport and electrochemical channels that do not interfere with or compete against divalent minerals like calcium.
• Phosphorus: While phosphorus (P) interacts with calcium dynamically, it does not directly compete for the same absorption binding sites. Instead, an excessive amount of phosphorus binds chemically with calcium within the intestinal lumen to form an insoluble complex (calcium phosphate), which physically reduces the overall bioavailability of both minerals rather than competing for transport carriers.', '["Mg","P","K","Na"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:50:34.400Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:50:34.400Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:50:34.400Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786946947-25', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'During anaerobic glycolysis, there is a net production of lactate and ____. *', 'ATP', 'During anaerobic glycolysis, a single molecule of glucose is broken down in the cytosol without using oxygen. This pathway results in the net production of two distinct products:
1. Lactate (2 molecules)
2. ATP (a net yield of 2 molecules)

• NADH: While NADH is temporarily produced halfway through glycolysis, the cell immediately uses up that NADH to convert pyruvate into lactate. This means there is zero net production of NADH by the end of the anaerobic process.
• FADH₂: This electron carrier is only generated inside the mitochondria during the Krebs cycle (aerobic respiration), not during glycolysis.
• NAD⁺: Because NADH is continuously recycled back into NAD⁺ to keep glycolysis running, NAD⁺ is constantly consumed and regenerated, meaning it does not experience a net increase.', '["NAD+","NADH","ATP","FADH2"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-16T02:59:16.599Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:59:16.599Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:59:16.675Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786838387-18', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The enzyme pepsin is not denatured in the stomach despite the very acidic environment due to the presence of hydrochloricacid. What could account for this? *', 'Pepsin is secreted in its precursor form, pepsinogen.', '', '["Pepsin is the only protein-enzyme that functions best in an acidic environment.","Pepsin is able to resist denaturation by increasing the pH within the stomach, making it less acidic.","Pepsin is also digested along with other proteins in the stomach.","Pepsin is secreted in its precursor form, pepsinogen."]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-14T01:25:34.102Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T01:25:34.102Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T01:25:34.174Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786507019-0', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The most abundant element in the human body accounting for some 63% of body mass. *', 'oxygen', '', '["carbon","hydrogen","oxygen","nitrogen"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T07:05:10.885Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T07:05:10.885Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T07:05:10.885Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788041865-99', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The percentage of nitrogen absorbed from dietary protein by the body is known as: *', 'biological value', 'Biological Value (BV) measures protein quality by calculating the amount of nitrogen retained for tissue growth and maintenance divided by the total nitrogen absorbed from the diet. The formula is:

BV = (Nitrogen Retained / Nitrogen Absorbed) x 100

Because it evaluates the percentage of nitrogen utilized after it has already been successfully absorbed across the intestinal wall, it tracks how perfectly a protein''s amino acid profile matches human tissue needs.

	• Net Protein Utilization - NPU: This measures the percentage of nitrogen retained out of the total nitrogen ingested (intake) rather than absorbed. It combines both digestibility and biological value into one score.
	• Protein Efficiency Ratio - PER: This measures the weight gain of a growing subject per gram of protein consumed, typically tested on laboratory rats.
	• Amino Acid Score: This is a chemical score calculated in a lab by comparing a food’s essential amino acid levels against a standard reference protein profile, completely independent of biological absorption measurements.', '["net protein utilization","biological value","protein efficiency ratio","amino acid score"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:57:29.847Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:57:29.847Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:57:30.022Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788033199-98', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Both soluble, more fermentable fiber and insoluble, less fermentable fiber have this common action in the body: *', 'they increase satiety or feeling of fullness', 'Both types of dietary fiber contribute to satiety (the feeling of being full after eating) through different physical mechanisms:
• Soluble Fiber: Absorbs water to form a thick, gel-like substance. This significantly slows down stomach emptying (gastric emptying), keeping you physically full for a longer duration.
• Insoluble Fiber: Does not dissolve in water. Instead, it acts as structural "roughage" that adds physical bulk and weight to the food matrix inside your digestive tract. This bulk physically stretches the stomach and intestinal walls, triggering nerve receptors that signal to your brain that you are full.', '["they lower blood cholesterol","they slow down the absorption of glucose","they speed up transit time through intestines","they increase satiety or feeling of fullness"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T14:10:25.077Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:10:25.077Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:10:25.154Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788023532-97', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Peak bone mass refers to the highest bone density achieved by an individual which usually occurs at the age of ____. *', '30', '• During childhood and adolescence, the body builds new bone faster than it breaks down old bone, causing bone mass to increase rapidly.
• Most people reach their peak bone mass—the maximum strength, density, and thickness their bones will ever achieve—by their late 20s or around age 30.
• After age 30, the remodeling balance shifts. The body begins to resorb (break down) bone slightly faster than it replaces it, leading to a gradual, lifelong loss of bone density.', '["20","30","40","50"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T13:42:07.334Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T13:42:07.334Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T13:42:07.407Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788003213-95', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The Krebs cycle is oxygen-requiring and takes place in the: *', 'mitochondria', 'The Krebs cycle (also known as the citric acid cycle or TCA cycle) takes place exclusively within the matrix of the mitochondria. This is because all the specific enzymes, coenzymes, and transport proteins required for the cycle are housed inside the mitochondrial membranes.

Although the chemical steps of the Krebs cycle do not directly use oxygen gas (O₂), the cycle is considered aerobic (oxygen-requiring). It cannot run without oxygen because it relies on the electron transport chain (which requires oxygen as the final electron acceptor) to regenerate its essential starting coenzymes, NAD⁺ and FAD.', '["liver","mitochondria","muscle cells","nucleus"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T15:28:33.277Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:28:33.277Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:28:33.367Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787985533-94', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'It is the largest single contributor to the body’s energy needs accounting for 60%-80% of its total need for calories. *', 'basal metabolism', 'Basal metabolism, or Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR), represents the baseline energy expended by the body at complete rest in a post-absorptive state. It is dedicated purely to maintaining life-sustaining vegetative functions—such as cellular respiration, heartbeat, brain activity, and body temperature regulation. In textbook nutrition frameworks, it serves as the largest single contributor to Total Energy Expenditure (TEE), typically accounting for 60% to 80% of daily calorie needs depending on an individual''s lean body mass.

• Thermic Effect of Food: This component represents the temporary increase in energy expenditure associated with digesting, absorbing, and storing nutrients from food. It accounts for only about 5% to 10% of total energy requirements.
• Adaptive Thermogenesis: This is the change in energy expenditure in response to environmental fluctuations (like extreme cold stress) or chronic overfeeding/underfeeding. It represents an auxiliary survival adjustment and constitutes a minor, highly variable percentage of daily needs.
• Physical Activity: This comprises the energy expended during voluntary muscle movement and exercise. While it is the most variable and changeable component of a person''s daily energy expenditure, it typically contributes only 15% to 30% of total calories in most normal-living adults.', '["physical activity","thermic effect of food","basal metabolism","adaptive thermogenesis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:31:04.112Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:31:04.112Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:31:04.285Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787972752-93', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'These glucogenic amino acids can be converted to α-keto acid, a carbohydrate former, except for: *', 'leucine', 'Leucine and lysine are the only two amino acids in the human body that are strictly purely ketogenic. They are degraded directly into acetyl-CoA or acetoacetate, which are precursors for ketone bodies and fatty acids, and cannot be used to synthesize carbohydrates via gluconeogenesis.

• Cysteine: This is a purely glucogenic amino acid. It is converted into the α-keto acid pyruvate through a multi-step oxidative pathway, making it an effective carbohydrate former.
• Glycine: This is a purely glucogenic amino acid. It enters carbohydrate-forming pathways by being converted into serine, which is then deaminated directly into pyruvate (an α-keto acid).
• Methionine: This is a purely glucogenic amino acid. It is metabolized through a pathway that converts its carbon skeleton into succinyl-CoA, a citric acid cycle intermediate that ultimately yields oxaloacetate to fuel glucose production.', '["glycine","leucine","cysteine","methionine"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:28:42.124Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:28:42.124Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:28:42.300Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787962367-92', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Due to its many roles in amino acid metabolism, its dietary needs are proportional to protein intake. *', 'B₆', '• Vitamin B_{6}\] is the direct precursor to pyridoxal phosphate (PLP). This vital coenzyme is required for almost all pathways involving amino acid metabolism, including transamination, deamination, and decarboxylation. Proportional to Protein: Because its primary biological purpose is processing and utilizing incoming amino acids, your body''s dietary requirement for Vitamin \[B_{6}\] scales up or down in direct proportion to the amount of protein you consume.', '["B₆","B₂","B₁","B₃"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T07:02:24.688Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T07:02:24.688Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T07:02:24.688Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787942252-90', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Which of the following statements about ascorbic acid is incorrect? *', 'Its requirement varies with the caloric intake.', 'Ascorbic acid (Vitamin C) functions as a water-soluble antioxidant and an essential enzymatic cofactor (e.g., for collagen synthesis). Its daily physiological requirements do not change based on how many calories you eat. In contrast, the requirements for B-complex vitamins like Thiamine (B1), Riboflavin (B2), and Niacin (B3) are directly tied to caloric and carbohydrate intake because they serve as direct coenzymes in energy metabolism pathways.

• Metabolism to Oxalate: This statement is correct. Ascorbic acid breaks down non-enzymatically in the body to form oxalate. This oxalate can bind with calcium in the urinary tract to form insoluble calcium oxalate salts, which are the main components of the most common type of kidney stones.
• Oxidation by Metal Ions: This statement is correct. Ascorbic acid is a strong reducing agent that is easily degraded and readily oxidized in the presence of heat, light, and divalent metal ions like copper (Cu²⁺) and iron (Fe²⁺).
• Iron Absorption Facilitation: This statement is correct. Vitamin C significantly enhances the absorption of non-heme iron in the gut. It does this by reducing dietary ferric iron (Fe³⁺) into its highly soluble ferrous state (Fe²⁺), making it much easier for the intestinal mucosa to absorb.', '["It facilitates the absorption of dietary iron.","It is metabolized in part to oxalate which can form an insoluble salt with calcium.","It is readily oxidized by divalent metal ions.","Its requirement varies with the caloric intake."]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:50:16.228Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:50:16.228Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:50:16.228Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787904138-86', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'All are needed for the conversion of tryptophan to niacin except: *', 'Zn', 'The synthesis of niacin (Vitamin B₃) from the essential amino acid tryptophan occurs via the kynurenine pathway in the liver. This multi-step conversion is highly nutrient-dependent and strictly requires three cofactors:

• Vitamin B₆ (Pyridoxine): Active pyridoxal 5′-phosphate (PLP) is the direct coenzyme required by kynureninase, an essential enzyme in the pathway.
• Vitamin B₂ (Riboflavin): Active FAD is required as a cofactor for kynurenine 3-monooxygenase, and riboflavin is also needed to synthesize active B₆.
• Iron (Fe): Iron is a critical component of the initial rate-limiting enzyme, tryptophan 2,3-dioxygenase.', '["B₂","B₆","Fe","Zn"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T15:14:56.158Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:14:56.158Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:14:56.249Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787893172-85', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Two vitamins whose requirements are based on an individual’s energy intake. *', 'B₁ & B₂', 'Thiamine (B₁): Serves as a mandatory coenzyme for carbohydrate metabolism and the processing of pyruvate. Standard guidelines traditionally recommend 0.5 mg of thiamine per 1,000 kcal consumed.

Riboflavin (B₂): Serves as a vital electron carrier (FAD) in mitochondrial biological oxidation and the citric acid cycle. Standard requirements are classically set at 0.5 mg of riboflavin per 1,000 kcal.

Niacin (B₃): Serves as a core electron carrier (NAD/NADH) in glycolysis and fatty acid oxidation. Standard requirements are similarly calculated as 6.6 mg of Niacin Equivalents (NE) per 1,000 kcal.', '["B₁ & B₂","B₁ & B₆","B₁ & B₃","B₁ & B₁₂"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T14:06:51.926Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:06:51.926Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:06:51.990Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787879456-84', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Minerals that are effective antioxidants except: *', 'Cr', 'Chromium (Cr) is a trace mineral primarily known for its role in carbohydrate, lipid, and protein metabolism by enhancing the action of insulin. It does not function directly or serve as a traditional cofactor for major endogenous antioxidant enzyme systems.

• Selenium: This mineral is a well-established and highly effective antioxidant. It forms an essential structural component of the active site of the enzyme glutathione peroxidase, which neutralizes harmful hydrogen peroxide and lipid hydroperoxides into harmless water and alcohols.
• Iron: This mineral plays a vital role in antioxidant defense mechanisms. Iron (Fe) serves as an essential cofactor for the enzyme catalase, an incredibly rapid antioxidant enzyme that converts toxic hydrogen peroxide molecules into water and oxygen within cells.
• Copper: This mineral is a critical component of the antioxidant defense loop. Copper (Cu) acts alongside zinc as a mandatory structural cofactor for cytosolic superoxide dismutase (CuZn-SOD). This enzyme acts as the first line of defense by transforming highly reactive superoxide free radicals into less destructive hydrogen peroxide.', '["Cu","Se","Fe","Cr"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:52:53.714Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:52:53.714Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:52:53.714Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787869806-83', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Vitamins that function in normal blood formation except: *', 'biotin', 'Biotin (Vitamin B₇) serves as a vital coenzyme for carboxylase enzymes involved in lipid synthesis, glucose production (gluconeogenesis), and amino acid catabolism. It does not play a direct, primary role in hematopoiesis (blood cell formation) or hemoglobin synthesis.

• Pyridoxine: This vitamin is essential for normal blood formation. Pyridoxine (Vitamin B₆), in its active form as pyridoxal phosphate (PLP), acts as a mandatory coenzyme for the enzyme ALA synthase. This is the enzyme that initiates the very first step of heme synthesis inside the mitochondria. A deficiency in B₆ directly leads to microcytic, hypochromic sideroblastic anemia.
• Cobalamin: This vitamin is crucial for red blood cell development. Cobalamin (Vitamin B₁₂) is a required cofactor alongside folate for DNA synthesis in rapidly dividing hematopoietic stem cells. Without it, bone marrow cells cannot mature normally, resulting in macrocytic or megaloblastic anemia.
• Folate: This vitamin is foundational for hematopoiesis. Folate (Vitamin B₉) is required for the synthesis of purines and thymidylate, which are essential building blocks for DNA replication. When folate is missing, erythroblasts in the bone marrow cannot divide smoothly, leading to aborted maturation and megaloblastic anemia.', '["cobalamin","pyridoxine","biotin","folate"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:36:15.070Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:36:15.070Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:36:15.243Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787837925-80', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Eating foods containing vitamin C in the same meal can increase the absorption of which mineral? *', 'iron', 'Dietary iron exists in two forms: heme iron (from animal tissues) and non-heme iron (from plant foods). Non-heme iron is inherently difficult for the human body to absorb because it is typically found in its oxidized ferric state (Fe³⁺).

Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) acts as a powerful reducing agent. When consumed in the same meal, it reduces ferric iron (Fe³⁺) into its highly soluble ferrous state (Fe²⁺). It also binds with the iron to form a stable chelate complex that prevents it from binding to inhibitors like phytates or tannins. This significantly boosts iron absorption across the intestinal lining.', '["iron","calcium","magnesium","zinc"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-14T03:09:19.508Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T03:09:19.508Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T03:09:19.585Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787813924-78', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The mineral needed to keep the heart beat steady and plays a major role in maintaining fluid and electrolyte balance. *', 'potassium', 'Potassium is the primary intracellular cation (positively charged ion) in the body. It functions closely with the heart''s electrical conduction system to stabilize resting membrane potentials. Proper levels are absolutely essential to maintain a regular cardiac rhythm and contractility. Severe deviations (either hypokalemia or hyperkalemia) can trigger lethal cardiac arrhythmias.

Potassium works in tandem with sodium via the cellular sodium-potassium pump. While sodium regulates fluid outside the cells, potassium regulates fluid volumes inside the cells, preserving structural cell integrity and overall fluid homeostasis.

	• Sodium: Primarily acts as the major regulator of extracellular fluid volume and blood pressure. While an imbalance stresses the heart indirectly, it does not directly regulate heartbeat steadiness like potassium does.
	• Calcium: Triggers the physical contraction of muscles (including the heart), but it is not the primary ion responsible for maintaining fluid balance across cellular compartments.
	• Magnesium: Acts as a crucial stabilizer for enzymatic functions and works closely with potassium.', '["calcium","magnesium","potassium","sodium"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:51:29.958Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:51:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:51:29.958Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787804394-77', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A slice of apple pie supplies 250 kcals with 3 grams of fiber; an apple provides 80 kcals and the same 3 grams of fiber. This is an example: *', 'nutrient density', '', '["calorie control","variety","nutrient density","diet diversity"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, 'Nutrient density refers to a measure of the nutrients a food provides relative to the energy (calories) it delivers. • In this example, both items provide the exact same amount of the nutrient (3 grams of fiber). However, the whole apple delivers that nutrient for only 80 kcals, whereas the slice of pie delivers it with an extra 250 kcals due to added fats and sugars.
• Because the whole apple gives you more nutritional value per calorie, it is considered more nutrient-dense.', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T04:48:40.134Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T04:48:40.134Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T04:48:40.134Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787793360-76', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Which of the following oils is lowest in saturated fat? *', 'canola oil', '', '["corn oil","canola oil","coconut oil","olive oil"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T07:38:15.592Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T07:38:15.592Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T07:38:15.807Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787785294-75', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Inactive precursors of enzymes: *', 'zymogens', '• A) Zymogens / D) Proenzymes: Inactive proteins that must undergo a chemical change (like clipping off a piece of the protein chain) to become active enzymes.
• B) Apoenzymes: The pure protein part of an enzyme that is currently inactive because it is missing its non-protein helper (cofactor or coenzyme).
• C) Coenzymes: Organic helper molecules (often made from B-vitamins) that bind to enzymes to help them perform chemical reactions.', '["proenzymes","zymogens","apoenzymes","coenzymes"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T06:11:02.195Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:11:02.195Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:11:02.195Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787777141-74', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'During the fasting stage, one of the following occurs: *', 'glycogen breakdown', 'Glycogen breakdown is the very first defense mechanism. Your pancreas secretes glucagon, which signals the liver to smash its stored glycogen chains back into free glucose to maintain normal blood sugar levels.', '["glycogen breakdown","protein breakdown","fat synthesis","glucose synthesis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T14:09:54.239Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":1.9,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:09:54.239Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:09:54.317Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787767011-73', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A measure of the nutrients a food provides in relation to the energy it provides. *', 'nutrient density', 'This is the literal scientific definition of nutrient density. It measures the concentration of beneficial nutrients (such as vitamins, minerals, complex carbohydrates, lean protein, and healthy fats) in a food relative to the total calories (energy content) it delivers. For example, fresh spinach has high nutrient density because it provides massive amounts of vitamins for very few calories.

• Energy density measures the number of calories (energy) per gram of food weight. High energy-dense foods (like potato chips or butter) provide a high amount of calories in a small, lightweight serving size.
• The glycemic index ranks carbohydrates based on how quickly they raise blood sugar levels,.
• Diet diversity refers to eating a wide variety of different food groups to ensure balanced nutrient intake.', '["glycemic index","nutrient density","diet diversity","energy density"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:53:10.498Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:53:10.498Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:53:10.498Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787754462-72', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The hormone secreted by duodenal cells that stimulates the pancreas to release pancreatic juice. *', 'secretin', 'Secretin, a hormone, is secreted by the "S cells" of the duodenum in response to the acidic pH of the incoming gastric contents (chyme). It travels through the bloodstream to the pancreas and stimulates the release of a pancreatic juice rich in bicarbonate. The bicarbonate acts as a buffer to neutralize the stomach acid, protecting the intestinal lining and creating the optimal alkaline environment for digestive enzymes.

• Cholecystokinin / CCK: Also secreted by the duodenum (specifically by "I cells"), CCK is triggered by the presence of fats and proteins. While it also acts on the pancreas, its primary role is to stimulate the pancreas to release digestive enzymes (like trypsin and pancreatic lipase) and signal the gallbladder to contract and release bile.
• Gastrin: This hormone is secreted by "G cells" in the stomach (not the duodenum) and functions to stimulate the gastric glands to secrete hydrochloric acid (HCl) and pepsinogen.
• Chyme: This is not a hormone at all. It is the semi-fluid mass of partly digested food expelled by the stomach into the duodenum.', '["gastrin","chyme","cholecystokinin","secretin"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:52:26.349Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:52:26.349Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:52:26.349Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787746928-71', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Fat digestion occurs in the: *', 'small intestines', 'The small intestine is the primary site for both the chemical digestion and absorption of dietary fats. This process relies on two critical substances released into the duodenum:
	• Bile Salts: Secreted by the liver and stored in the gallbladder, bile salts act as a detergent to emulsify large fat globules into tiny droplets. This mechanical action dramatically increases the surface area available for enzymes.
	• Pancreatic Lipase: The principal fat-digesting enzyme, which is secreted by the pancreas, breaks down these small emulsified fat droplets into free fatty acids and monoglycerides for absorption by enterocytes.

• The Stomach: While a small amount of lipid digestion begins here via gastric lipase, it is highly limited because the stomach lacks bile salts to emulsify fats efficiently.
• The Large Intestine: The colon is primarily responsible for absorbing remaining water, synthesizing certain vitamins through gut microbiota, and forming feces. No functional fat digestion takes place here.', '["stomach","small intestines","large intestines","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:09:29.602Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:09:29.602Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:09:29.910Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787675615-64', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Glossitis is caused by a deficiency of: *', 'B complex vitamins', 'Glossitis—which is inflammation of the tongue causing it to swell, turn smooth, and lose its tiny bumps (papillae)—is a classic clinical hallmark of B-complex vitamin deficiencies. It is most frequently tied to a lack of Vitamin B₁₂ (cobalamin), Vitamin B₉ (folate), Vitamin B₆ (pyridoxine), Vitamin B₃ (niacin), and Vitamin B₂ (riboflavin).

Cells lining the mouth and tongue have a very rapid turnover rate. Because B vitamins act as essential coenzymes for cellular energy production and DNA synthesis, a deficiency rapidly impairs the body''s ability to regenerate these tongue cells, leading to inflammation and atrophy.', '["vitamin A","B complex vitamins","vitamin C","vitamin D"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:51:58.089Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:51:58.089Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:51:58.089Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787664333-63', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'As BMR increases, there is an increased need for: *', 'thiamin, riboflavin, niacin', 'As Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR) increases, the body burns through carbohydrates, fats, and proteins at a much faster rate to produce ATP.

Thiamin (B₁), riboflavin (B₂), and niacin (B₃) function strictly as the essential coenzymes required to break down these macronutrients during cellular respiration:
	• Thiamin is a cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase to kickstart aerobic metabolism.
	• Riboflavin forms FAD/FADH₂, which acts as an electron carrier in the Krebs cycle and Electron Transport Chain.
	• Niacin forms NAD/NADH, the primary electron carrier across glycolysis and mitochondrial pathways.

Because their metabolic utilization is directly tied to total energy expenditure, your physical requirement for these three specific B vitamins scales upward as your BMR increases.

• Vitamins A, C, D, E, and K perform localized or systemic cellular tasks (such as antioxidant defenses, bone mineral regulation, immune signaling, and blood clotting factors) that operate independently of immediate carbohydrate and lipid calorie-burning metrics.', '["vitamins A & D","vitamin C","vitamins E & K","thiamin, riboflavin, niacin"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '• These B-vitamins act as essential energy coenzymes.
• Higher BMR = more energy burned = higher B-vitamin requirement.', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T14:05:01.168Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:05:01.168Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:05:01.168Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787654015-62', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The building block of carbohydrate is: *', 'glucose', '', '["sucrose","fructose","glucose","galactose"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T06:10:09.845Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:10:09.845Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:10:09.845Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787738963-70', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Fatty acids formed by hydrogenation of unsaturated fats, converting them from liquid oils to semisolid fats. *', 'trans-fatty acids', '• Industrially, liquid vegetable oils (which are rich in unsaturated fats) have hydrogen gas bubbled through them in the presence of a catalyst. This is done to make them more solid, spreadable, and shelf-stable.
• If the fat is partially hydrogenated rather than fully saturated, a chemical side reaction occurs. The remaining natural carbon-to-carbon double bonds flip from their native "cis" configuration into an unnatural, straight "trans" configuration. This chemical restructuring creates trans-fatty acids (trans fats).', '["saturated fatty acids","unsaturated fatty acids","trans-fatty acids","short-chain fatty acids"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-03T06:46:25.561Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:46:25.561Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:46:25.561Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787645050-61', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A secretion of the pancreas that neutralizes the acidic fluid from the stomach: *', 'bicarbonate', 'The pancreas secretes large amounts of sodium bicarbonate into the duodenum. This alkaline fluid directly neutralizes the highly acidic chyme coming from the stomach. This neutralization process protects the delicate intestinal lining and creates the optimal alkaline environment necessary for digestive enzymes to function.

• Digestive Enzymes: While the pancreas secretes critical digestive enzymes (like amylase, lipase, and trypsin), their function is to chemically break down carbohydrates, fats, and proteins rather than directly buffering or neutralizing stomach acid.
• Mucus: Mucus is secreted by goblet cells along the gastrointestinal tract to lubricate food passage and provide a physical structural coating against acid, but it does not act as a primary chemical neutralizing agent.
• Bile: This fluid is produced and secreted by the liver (and stored in the gallbladder), not the pancreas. Its primary function is the mechanical emulsification of fats.', '["bile","bicarbonate","mucus","digestive enzymes"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:57:23.966Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:57:23.966Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:57:24.141Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787635967-60', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'These form the accessory organs of digestion except for the _____: *', 'small intestine', 'The digestive system is split into two primary divisions: the gastrointestinal (GI) tract and the accessory organs. The small intestine is a fundamental component of the GI tract itself—the continuous muscular tube through which food physically passes, undergoes chemical digestion, and gets absorbed.

• Liver: This is a true accessory organ of digestion. Food does not pass through it, but it facilitates chemical digestion by continuously manufacturing and exporting bile into the duodenum to emulsify fats.
• Gallbladder: This is a true accessory organ of digestion. It serves as a secondary storage and concentration sac for the bile produced by the liver, squeezing it into the small intestine when fats enter the tract.
• Pancreas: This is a true accessory organ of digestion. Its exocrine cells synthesize and secrete pancreatic juice containing essential digestive enzymes and bicarbonate ions straight into the duodenum to neutralize acidity and digest macromolecules.', '["small intestine","liver","gall bladder","pancreas"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:54:22.937Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:54:22.937Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:54:23.109Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787627218-59', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Water-soluble vitamins are absorbed in the small intestine except for ____ which is absorbed in the stomach. *', 'B₁₂', 'While the final absorption of Vitamin B₁₂ actually occurs in the terminal ileum (small intestine), its entire absorption process begins critically in the stomach. The stomach produces hydrochloric acid to unbind B₁₂ from dietary proteins and secretes Intrinsic Factor (IF), a mandatory transport protein. Because B₁₂ requires this extensive gastric phase, many simplified medical board questions uniquely tie its absorption context to the stomach.', '["B₁","B₃","B₆","B₁₂"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-14T02:55:22.838Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:55:22.838Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:55:22.913Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787594503-56', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'These are simple proteins that give amino acids when hydrolyzed except for ___: *', 'metalloproteins', '• Metalloproteins are classified as conjugated (or compound) proteins, not simple proteins. Upon hydrolysis, they yield both amino acids and a non-protein prosthetic group, which in this case is a metal ion (such as iron, zinc, or copper).', '["albumin","globin","metalloproteins","collagen"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T06:46:32.829Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:46:32.829Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:46:32.829Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787586686-55', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Considered semi-essential or conditionally essential amino acids except for: *', 'alanine', 'Alanine is a strictly non-essential (dispensable) amino acid. The body can always synthesize it in adequate amounts directly from pyruvate via transamination reactions, even during periods of severe stress, illness, or rapid growth. It never crosses into the semi-essential category.

• Tyrosine: This is a classic conditionally essential amino acid. It is synthesized directly from the essential amino acid phenylalanine. In clinical conditions like Phenylketonuria (PKU) where the converting enzyme is deficient, or during severe liver disease, tyrosine cannot be produced by the body and must be supplied exogenously through the diet.
• Cysteine: This is a conditionally essential amino acid. It requires methionine as a sulfur donor for its synthesis. In premature infants or patients with severe metabolic stress or cirrhosis, the enzymatic pathways are immature or impaired, making it an essential dietary requirement.
• Serine: This is recognized under advanced physiological frameworks (such as the MedlinePlus Medical Reference) as a conditionally essential amino acid. Under extreme hypermetabolic states or specific clinical pathologies, its endogenous production cannot completely keep up with cellular demands.', '["alanine","tyrosine","cysteine","serine"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:54:18.138Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:54:18.138Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:54:18.316Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787578405-54', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The side chain attached to the carbon atom of a protein molecule which determines the characteristics and functions of each amino acid is: *', 'the radical group', 'All 20 standard amino acids share an identical foundational backbone consisting of a central alpha-carbon, an amino group, an acid (carboxyl) group, and a hydrogen atom. The radical group (R-group) is the only component that changes from one amino acid to the next. Its specific chemical layout determines whether the amino acid behaves as hydrophobic, hydrophilic, acidic, basic, or polar.', '["the amino group","hydrogen","the acid group","the radical group"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T06:05:31.256Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:05:31.256Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:05:31.256Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787567318-53', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Except for ____, these essential branched-chain amino acids are used by skeletal muscles for energy during prolonged endurance exercise: *', 'tryptophan', 'The three essential branched-chain amino acids are leucine, isoleucine, and valine. They are unique because unlike most other amino acids that are metabolized primarily in the liver, BCAAs skip the liver and are oxidized directly within skeletal muscle tissue to provide energy during prolonged endurance training.

During prolonged endurance exercise, as muscle glycogen stores deplete, muscle cells increasingly burn leucine, isoleucine, and valine for fuel. This drops blood BCAA levels, allowing more tryptophan to cross the blood-brain barrier, which actually contributes to central nervous system fatigue.

Tryptophan is an aromatic amino acid, not a branched-chain amino acid. While it is essential, it is not metabolized directly by skeletal muscle for energy during exercise.', '["isoleucine","leucine","tryptophan","valine"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T15:41:30.604Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:41:30.604Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:41:30.693Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787559305-52', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Iron absorption is increased by the following nutrients except: *', 'Zn', 'Zinc acts as a powerful inhibitor of iron absorption, rather than an enhancer. High doses of supplemental zinc compete directly with iron for the same membrane transporter pathways—specifically the DMT1 (Divalent Metal Transporter 1) channel—in the brush border cells of the small intestine. Consuming a large amount of zinc blocks this pathway, significantly reducing the absorption rate of non-heme iron.', '["Zn","vitamin C","Cu","vitamin A"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T16:07:29.138Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T16:07:29.138Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T16:07:29.220Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787520556-48', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'All are dietary carbohydrates except: *', 'glycogen', 'Dietary Fibers & Plant Carbs: Pectin, Starch, and Mucilages are carbohydrates synthesized by plants. Pectin and mucilages are classified as soluble dietary fibers, while starch is the primary digestible storage carbohydrate in plants.

Glycogen is the complex storage carbohydrate synthesized and found inside animal tissue (specifically liver and muscle cells).', '["mucilages","glycogen","starch","pectin"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T14:08:14.155Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:08:14.155Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:08:14.226Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787488707-47', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Lipoprotein made primarily by liver cells to transport lipids to various tissues in the body: *', 'VLDL', 'VLDL (Very Low-Density Lipoprotein) is synthesized and secreted primarily by liver cells. Its main job is to transport newly created endogenous triglycerides and cholesterol from the liver out to various peripheral tissues in the body.

	• Chylomicrons: These are made by intestinal cells (enterocytes), not liver cells. They transport dietary (exogenous) lipids absorbed from food.
	• HDL (High-Density Lipoprotein): Its main job is reverse cholesterol transport, meaning it picks up excess cholesterol from various tissues and returns it back to the liver.
	• LDL (Low-Density Lipoprotein): While it does deliver cholesterol to various tissues, it is not made directly by liver cells. Instead, LDL is formed in the bloodstream as VLDL shrinks and loses its triglycerides.', '["HDL","LDL","VLDL","chylomicron"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '• Very Low-Density Lipoproteins (VLDL) are synthesized and secreted primarily by liver cells (hepatocytes) to transport endogenously created lipids.
• Primary Function: They travel through the bloodstream to deliver triglycerides and fatty acids to various peripheral tissues—such as adipose tissue for storage and muscle tissue for energy.', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-14T02:52:28.682Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":1.9,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:52:28.682Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:52:28.757Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787616019-58', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A calorie-free food contains: *', '<5 calories per serving', 'According to official food labeling laws (such as FDA and international food regulations), a manufacturer can legally label a food item as "calorie-free" or "zero calories" if it contains fewer than 5 calories per reference amount customarily consumed (serving size).

Because the body expends a small amount of energy just to digest food, these negligible amounts are rounded down to zero on nutritional facts panels.', '["<5 calories per serving","no calories","<10 calories per serving","<15 calories per serving"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T15:18:26.341Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:18:26.341Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:18:26.430Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787461879-45', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The term “glycemic effect of food” includes the following meanings except: *', 'how long blood glucose is elevated after a meal', 'The glycemic effect (often measured as the glycemic index or glycemic load) specifically evaluates the intensity, speed, and recovery of the blood sugar spike following food consumption.

While a glycemic curve tracks how high blood glucose rises, how fast it gets absorbed, and how rapidly the body secretes insulin to bring it back down to baseline, it does not look at prolonged, sustained elevation as a trait of the food itself. A long-lasting elevation of blood sugar is typically a reflection of a patient''s underlying pathology (such as insulin resistance or diabetes), rather than an intrinsic characteristic of the food''s glycemic effect.', '["how quickly blood glucose returns to normal","how high blood glucose rises after a meal","how quickly glucose is absorbed after eating","how long blood glucose is elevated after a meal"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T14:09:34.318Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:09:34.318Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:09:34.397Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787442475-44', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The conversion of 1 mol of pyruvate to 3 mol of CO2 via pyruvate dehydrogenase and the citric acid cycle yields ____mol of NADH, ____ mol of FADH2, and ____ mol of ATP or GTP. *', '4; 1; 1', 'To calculate the total yield, we must look at two distinct cellular reactions: the Pyruvate Dehydrogenase (PDH) complex (the transition step) and the Citric Acid (Krebs) Cycle. 1. The Pyruvate Dehydrogenase (PDH) Complex (Transition Step): 1 Pyruvate (3 carbons) is converted into 1 Acetyl-CoA (2 carbons), releasing the first molecule of \[\text{CO}_{2}\].Yield: 1 NADH 2. The Citric Acid Cycle: 1 Acetyl-CoA enters the cycle and is fully oxidized, releasing the remaining 2 molecules of \[\text{CO}_{2}\].Yield: 3 NADH, 1 \[\text{FADH}_{2}\], and 1 ATP (or GTP) via substrate-level phosphorylation. Total Cumulative Yield:Adding the yields of both steps together: NADH: \(1\text{ (from PDH)} + 3\text{ (from Citric Acid Cycle)} =\) 4 mol\[\text{FADH}_{2}\]: \(0\text{ (from PDH)} + 1\text{ (from Citric Acid Cycle)} =\) 1 molATP/GTP: \(0\text{ (from PDH)} + 1\text{ (from Citric Acid Cycle)} =\) 1 mol', '["3; 2; 0","4; 2; 1","4; 1; 1","3; 1; 1"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T06:31:02.885Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:31:02.885Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:31:02.885Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787419743-43', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'All of the following compounds can serve as the starting material for the synthesis of glucose via gluconeogenesis except: *', 'oleic acid', 'Oleic acid is a fatty acid (specifically a 18-carbon monounsaturated fatty acid). In humans and other mammals, even-chain fatty acids are broken down via beta-oxidation into acetyl-CoA.

Acetyl-CoA cannot be used to synthesize glucose because the reaction that converts pyruvate to acetyl-CoA (catalyzed by the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex) is completely irreversible. Additionally, for every two carbons that enter the citric acid cycle as acetyl-CoA, two carbons are lost as CO2, meaning there is no net synthesis of oxaloacetate to feed into gluconeogenesis.', '["alanine","oleic acid","aspartic acid","glycerol"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:51:43.044Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:51:43.044Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:51:43.044Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787409895-42', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'When a muscle contracts aerobically, less lactic acid is formed than when it contracts anaerobically because under aerobic conditions ______: *', 'the lactic acid generated is rapidly converted back to glucose.', 'During physical exertion, any lactic acid (or more accurately, lactate) that is produced diffuses out of the skeletal muscle cells into the bloodstream and is transported to the liver. In the hepatic Cori cycle, hepatocytes take up this lactate, oxidize it back to pyruvate via lactate dehydrogenase, and subsequently convert it into glucose via gluconeogenesis, which is then released back into the bloodstream to support active muscle tissue.', '["muscles cannot convert glucose 6-phosphate to pyruvate.","most of the pyruvate generated is oxidized.","the lactic acid generated is rapidly converted back to glucose.","the major source of energy is the pentose pathway."]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T14:10:35.698Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:10:35.698Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:10:35.770Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787392461-41', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A key enzyme in carbohydrate metabolism found in the liver but not in muscles is: *', 'glucokinase', 'Glucokinase (also known as Hexokinase IV) is an irreversible enzyme found almost exclusively in the liver parenchymal cells and pancreatic beta cells. It is notably absent from skeletal muscle tissue.', '["phosphorylase","glucokinase","hexokinase","pyruvate dehydrogenase"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T13:24:40.566Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T13:24:40.566Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T13:24:40.566Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787310533-37', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Laboratory tests for a 45-year-old male alcoholic patient show an accumulation of lactate and pyruvate in body fluids, suggesting a block in the conversion of pyruvate to acetyl CoA. He appears confused and shows a slight weakness of eye movement. This patient would most probably be treated with injections of: *', 'thiamin', 'The combination of chronic alcoholism, confusion, and weakness of eye movement (ophthalmoplegia) is a classic presentation of Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome, a severe neurological complication caused by thiamin (Vitamin B1) deficiency.

Thiamin pyrophosphate (TPP) is an essential cofactor for the pyruvate dehydrogenase (PDH) complex, which converts pyruvate into acetyl CoA. When thiamin is deficient, this complex cannot function.

Because pyruvate cannot be converted into acetyl CoA, it shunts into alternative pathways. This leads to the accumulation of both pyruvate and lactate (via lactic acid fermentation) in body fluids.', '["thiamin","niacin","pantothenic acid","riboflavin"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-14T01:02:51.583Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T01:02:51.583Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T01:02:51.659Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787045928-33', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The B vitamin needed in the synthesis of niacin from tryptophan: *', 'pyridoxine', 'The body can manufacture niacin (Vitamin B₃) endogenously from the essential amino acid tryptophan.

Pyridoxine (Vitamin B₆) serves as an essential cofactor for the enzyme kynureninase. A deficiency in Vitamin B₆ halts this step, preventing tryptophan from converting into niacin and potentially leading to niacin-deficiency symptoms (like pellagra).

Riboflavin (Vitamin B₂) is also involved earlier in the pathway (for the enzyme kynurenine 3-monooxygenase), but kynureninase (B₆) is considered the most clinically significant bottleneck.', '["thiamin","pyridoxine","niacin","riboflavin"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, 'The liver can synthesize niacin (Vitamin B3) endogenously from the essential amino acid tryptophan through a complex multi-step metabolic route known as the kynurenine pathway. A critical rate-limiting enzyme in this conversion is kynureninase. This enzyme is strictly dependent on its active coenzyme cofactor, pyridoxal 5''-phosphate (PLP), which is the biologically active form of pyridoxine (Vitamin B6).', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T14:11:05.598Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:11:05.598Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:11:05.699Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787036945-32', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'This mineral works closely with insulin to regulate the release of energy from glucose: *', 'chromium', 'Chromium (specifically in its trivalent form, Cr³⁺) is an essential trace mineral that acts as an "insulin amplifier". It binds with other compounds in the body to form Glucose Tolerance Factor (GTF).

GTF stabilizes insulin and enhances its ability to dock onto insulin receptors on the surface of your cells. This binding clears glucose from the bloodstream and pulls it directly into the cells, where it can enter metabolic pathways (like glycolysis and the Krebs cycle) to be converted into usable cellular energy (ATP).

• Copper: Primarily required for iron metabolism, red blood cell synthesis, and cross-linking collagen.
• Iodine: Crucial for synthesizing thyroid hormones, which regulate overall baseline metabolic rate, but does not bind with insulin.
• Selenium: Functions as a vital component of antioxidant enzymes like glutathione peroxidase to neutralize free radicals.', '["iodine","selenium","copper","chromium"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T15:50:45.321Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:50:45.321Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:50:45.410Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787002144-30', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Digestion and absorption of carbohydrate involves all the following except: *', 'an acid stable amylase which acts in the stomach', 'The statement regarding ''an acid stable amylase which acts in the stomach'' is false, making it the correct choice for an ''EXCEPT'' question. Unlike lipid digestion—which utilizes acid-stable lingual and gastric lipases in the stomach—there is no gastric amylase secreted by humans, nor is salivary alpha-amylase acid-stable. Salivary amylase becomes inactivated when mixed with gastric juice at a pH below 4.0, halting enzymatic carbohydrate hydrolysis until pancreatic secretions neutralize the chyme in the small intestine.', '["salivary alpha amylase","disaccharidases in the intestines","an acid stable amylase which acts in the stomach","pancreatic alpha amylase"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T06:23:08.635Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:23:08.635Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:23:08.635Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786968964-27', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The respiratory quotient (RQ) is defined as: *', '(liters CO2 produced) / (liters O2 consumed)', '', '["(nitrogen intake) / (nitrogen excreted)","(caloric intake) / (fat free body weight)","(liters CO2 produced) / (liters O2 consumed)","(BMR + DIT + activity) / (fat free body weight)"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, 'Carbohydrates: \(\text{RQ} = 1.0\) (Because the amount of \[\text{O}_{2}\] required to burn a sugar equals the amount of \[\text{CO}_{2}\] produced).Proteins: \(\text{RQ} \approx 0.8\)Fats/Lipids: \(\text{RQ} \approx 0.7\) (Because fats are highly reduced molecules that require a much larger volume of oxygen to fully break down).Mixed Diet: A standard resting individual on a balanced diet usually has an average baseline \(\text{RQ} \approx 0.82\). ', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T07:10:25.560Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T07:10:25.560Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T07:10:25.560Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786957898-26', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'In the digestive tract, disaccharides are hydrolyzed to monosaccharides mainly by enzymes located in the: *', 'brush border of the small intestine', '', '["gastric secretions","brush border of the small intestine","lumen of the small intestine","lumen of the large intestine"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T01:03:03.478Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T01:03:03.478Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T01:03:03.558Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786887585-22', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'An example of a monounsaturated fatty acid is: *', 'oleic acid', '', '["linoleic acid","linolenic acid","palmitic acid","oleic acid"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '• Oleic acid (D) is a monounsaturated fatty acid (MUFA). It contains exactly one double bond in its hydrocarbon chain and is famously the primary component of olive oil.
• Linolenic acid (A) and Linoleic acid (C) are polyunsaturated fatty acids (PUFAs) because they contain multiple double bonds.
• Palmitic acid (B) is a saturated fatty acid with no double bonds.', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-16T01:04:57.366Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T01:04:57.366Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T01:04:57.440Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786865434-21', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Why is a saturated fat called “saturated”? *', 'The fatty acid carbon chain is saturated with hydrogen.', 'In a saturated fatty acid chain, there are only single bonds between the neighboring carbon atoms. Because carbon atoms must form four bonds, having only single bonds means each carbon atom can bind to the maximum possible number of hydrogen atoms.

In contrast, an unsaturated fat contains one or more carbon-to-carbon double bonds (C = C). These double bonds reduce the open spaces available for hydrogen, leaving the molecule "unsaturated".', '["The fat is saturated with water.","The fatty acid carbon chain is saturated with carbon.","The fatty acid carbon chain is saturated with hydrogen.","The fatty acid carbon chains can have more water added."]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T13:26:09.482Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T13:26:09.482Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T13:26:09.553Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787913371-87', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The following nutrients interact with vitamin D metabolism. *', 'both a & b', '', '["Ca & P","vitamin K","both a & b","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T07:39:47.580Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T07:39:47.580Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T07:39:47.740Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786856454-20', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Ceruloplasmin is a type of: *', 'metalloprotein', 'Ceruloplasmin is a type of metalloprotein (specifically a copper-binding metalloprotein) and it is also a glycoprotein.', '["glycoprotein","phosphoprotein","metalloprotein","lipoprotein"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T13:25:14.339Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T13:25:14.339Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T13:25:14.409Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786847671-19', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Ammonia is a nitrogenous waste product of protein metabolism that is harmful especially in high concentrations in the human body. Urea cycle is a means to detoxify and to prevent the accumulation of ammonia in the body by converting it to urea which is eventually excreted in the urine. How many moles of nitrogen enter the urea cycle in every turn? *', '2', 'A single molecule of urea has the chemical formula CO(NH₂)₂. As you can see from its structure, every single molecule of urea contains exactly two atoms of nitrogen. Therefore, for every complete turn of the urea cycle that creates one molecule of urea, exactly 2 moles of nitrogen must enter the cycle.

• Molar Mass: 60.06 g/mol
• Atom Count: A single molecule contains 1 Carbon, 1 Oxygen, 2 Nitrogens, and 4 Hydrogens (8 total atoms).', '["4","1","2","3"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T14:08:30.178Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:08:30.178Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:08:30.255Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786819421-17', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The building blocks of proteins are amino acids. What reaction allows several amino acids to join to form a protein? *', 'condensation reaction', '', '["hydrolysis","condensation reaction","evaporation reaction","hydrogenation"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T06:09:58.933Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:09:58.933Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:09:58.933Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786810137-16', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Which of the following is a branched-chain amino acid? *', 'isoleucine', 'There are exactly three branched-chain amino acids (BCAAs) among the standard amino acids: leucine, isoleucine, and valine. They are named for their unique, branching aliphatic hydrocarbon side-chain molecular structure.', '["alanine","lysine","isoleucine","glutamine"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T15:42:24.018Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:42:24.018Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:42:24.110Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786802137-15', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'This amino acid can form a special covalent bond called a disulfide bond. *', 'cysteine', 'Cysteine contains a highly reactive sulfhydryl group (-SH) (also known as a thiol group) on its side chain. When two cysteine residues come close to each other within a protein structure, their sulfhydryl groups can undergo an oxidation reaction. This reaction joins the two sulfur atoms together, forming a strong covalent disulfide bond (S-S), which plays a critical role in stabilizing a protein''s three-dimensional structure.

• Methionine: While methionine is the only other standard amino acid that contains sulfur, its sulfur atom is bound to a methyl group (a thioether group, -C-S-C-). Because it lacks a free, reactive -SH group, methionine cannot form disulfide bonds.
• Lysine and Tyrosine: Neither of these amino acids contains sulfur in their side chains. Lysine contains an amino group, and tyrosine contains a phenolic hydroxyl group.', '["tyrosine","methionine","cysteine","lysine"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T15:13:56.119Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:13:56.119Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:13:56.208Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786567783-6', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Glycolysis occurs in the: *', 'cytoplasm', 'Glycolysis is the universal metabolic pathway that breaks down one molecule of glucose into two molecules of pyruvate. It takes place entirely within the cytoplasm (specifically the cytosol) of the cell.

This location is specific because all ten of the soluble enzymes required to catalyze the step-by-step breakdown of glucose are located freely within the cytoplasmic fluid.

• Mitochondrial matrix: This is the internal fluid space of the mitochondria where the Krebs cycle (TCA cycle) and pyruvate oxidation happen.
• Mitochondrial membrane: The inner mitochondrial membrane houses the proteins for the electron transport chain and oxidative phosphorylation.', '["mitochondrial matrix","mitochondrial membrane","cytoplasm","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T15:54:42.005Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:54:42.005Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:54:42.094Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786559650-5', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The following statements are true regarding polysaccharides except: *', 'Amylose is made up of continuous, branched chains.', 'Amylose is actually a linear, unbranched chain of glucose units linked together by a-1,4-glycosidic bonds. Because it is unbranched, it naturally twists into a helical/spiral shape.', '["They are polymers of glucose.","Amylose is made up of continuous, branched chains.","Amylopectin has α-1,6 bonds.","Glycogen is similar to amylopectin but is more highly branched"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-16T02:47:44.442Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:47:44.442Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:47:44.517Z"'::jsonb)
