('anki-card-1756360720690-115', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'How many mg of potassium is contained in 40 mEq?', '1560 mg K', 'For potassium (K⁺), the conversion between mEq and mg is: mg = mEq * (atomic weight ÷ valence) 

Potassium has an atomic weight of approximately 39 mg/mmol and a valence of +1. 

Therefore: 40 mEq * (39mg ÷ 1) = 1560 mg K 

Thus, 40 mEq of potassium = 1,560 mg K. This conversion is clinically important because electrolytes may be prescribed or reported in mEq, while nutrition labels, laboratory references, and calculations may use mg.', '["1500 mg K","1560 mg K","2000 mg K","1864 mg K"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:29:22.943Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:29:22.943Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:29:22.943Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361348931-141', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Kidneys control pH through the following processes except:', 'Formation of carbonic acid', 'In the context of the body''s acid-base regulatory systems, the respiratory system (lungs) is the primary organ responsible for manipulating carbonic acid levels. The lungs control pH by exhaling or retaining carbon dioxide (CO₂), which directly dictates the formation and breakdown of volatile carbonic acid (H₂CO₃) in the blood.

The kidneys, on the other hand, handle non-volatile (fixed) acids. Their primary toolset for pH control relies on the active biochemical processes listed in the other options:
• Resorption of sodium ions: In the renal tubules, sodium reabsorption is directly coupled to acid excretion via Na⁺/H⁺ antiporters. The kidney actively reabsorbs sodium back into the bloodstream in exchange for pumping hydrogen ions out.
• Synthesis of ammonia: The renal cells produce ammonia (NH₃) to bind with free hydrogen ions in the urine, trapping them safely as ammonium (NH₄⁺) for excretion.
• Secretion of hydrogen ions: The kidneys physically pump H⁺ ions out of the plasma into the tubular fluid to directly eliminate acid from the body.', '["Formation of carbonic acid","Secretion Of hydrogen ions","Synthesis of ammonia","Resorption of sodium ions"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T11:32:06.203Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:32:06.203Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:32:06.203Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361061342-130', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'All the foods in the list are allowed among patients with celiac disease:', 'Sour cream, dried beans, millet, plain gelatin', 'Patients diagnosed with celiac disease must strictly follow a lifelong gluten-free diet to prevent an autoimmune reaction that damages the villi of the small intestine. This means avoiding the proteins found in wheat, barley, and rye.

• Sour cream: A plain dairy product that is naturally gluten-free.
• Dried beans: Legumes that contain no gluten proteins.
• Millet: A naturally gluten-free cereal grain that serves as a safe alternative to wheat.
• Plain gelatin: A pure animal collagen product containing no grain fillers or gluten.

Why the Other Options contain forbidden foods:
• Option A is incorrect because: Graham crackers are made from wheat flour, and Ovaltine contains malt extract derived from barley. Both will trigger a celiac flare-up. Vienna sausage also frequently uses wheat flour as a binder.
• Option B is incorrect because: Breaded fish is coated in wheat-based breadcrumbs. Additionally, textured vegetable protein (TVP) can sometimes be cross-contaminated or derived from wheat gluten.', '["Textured vegetable protein, plain yogurt, breaded fish, popcorn","Sour cream, dried beans, millet, plain gelatin","Tapioca starch, ovaltine, graham, Vienna sausage","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:32:32.214Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:32:32.214Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:32:32.214Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362596950-178', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'A deficiency of UDP-glucose:galactose-1-phosphate uridylyltransferase (GALT) will lead to an accumulation of:', 'Galactose', 'UDP-glucose:galactose-1-phosphate uridylyltransferase (GALT) is a key enzyme in the Leloir pathway, which metabolizes galactose into glucose-derived metabolites. GALT normally converts galactose-1-phosphate + UDP-glucose → UDP-galactose + glucose-1-phosphate. 

When GALT is deficient, as occurs in classic galactosemia, galactose-1-phosphate accumulates in tissues, while galactose is also elevated in the blood. Some excess galactose is converted to galactitol, which contributes to complications such as cataracts.', '["Glucose","Galactose","Glycogen","UDP"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:36:06.678Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:36:06.678Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:36:06.678Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362418324-168', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Electron-carrier molecules that are used in the electron-transport chain to generate additional ATP are:', 'NADH and FADH2', 'NADH and FADH₂ are reduced electron carriers generated during several stages of cellular respiration, including glycolysis, pyruvate oxidation, and the TCA cycle. They carry high-energy electrons to the electron transport chain (ETC) located in the inner mitochondrial membrane. As electrons move through the ETC, the released energy is used to pump protons across the membrane, creating a proton gradient that drives ATP synthesis through ATP synthase. 

NADH donates electrons to Complex I, whereas FADH₂ donates electrons through Complex II. Because FADH₂ enters the ETC downstream of Complex I, it generally results in the production of less ATP than NADH. Modern estimates are approximately 2.5 ATP per NADH and 1.5 ATP per FADH₂, although the exact ATP yield can vary depending on cellular conditions and the pathway used to transfer cytosolic reducing equivalents into the mitochondria.', '["Glucose and ketone bodies","Acetyl-CoA and amino acids","NADH and FADH2","Pyruvic acid and lactic acid"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:36:19.454Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:36:19.454Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:36:19.454Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361313665-139', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Vitamin/s known to be at low levels in patients following gluten-free diets', 'All of the above', 'Traditional gluten-containing wheat flour products are heavily fortified and enriched by law with crucial micronutrients, including thiamine, riboflavin, niacin, iron, and folic acid (folate). In contrast, commercially available gluten-free substitute products are frequently manufactured using refined starches (such as tapioca, corn, or rice starch) that are not routinely fortified, leading to chronic dietary deficits.

• Folate (Vitamin B9): Folate deficiency is exceptionally common among individuals adhering to a gluten-free diet (GFD) due to the absence of enriched wheat products, coupled with a typically low intake of alternative whole grains.
• Vitamin B6 and Vitamin B12: Multiple nutritional monitoring studies and clinical reviews indicate that patients on a strict GFD for extended periods show suboptimal plasma concentrations of Vitamin B6 and Vitamin B12. In patients with celiac disease, initial B12 deficiency stems from mucosal malabsorption in the ileum, which can persist if the diet is structurally restrictive or lacking in variety.', '["Folate","Vit B6, Vit B12","Vit B12, folate","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:37:11.480Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:37:11.480Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:37:11.480Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362542518-175', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Using the Tannhauser’s method, a 5’6” person has an ideal body weight of:', '61 kg', 'Step-by-Step Calculation

Tannhauser''s Method (widely used in the Philippines to estimate Desirable Body Weight or DBW) utilizes the following formula:
DBW (kg) = (Height in cm - 100) - [10% x (Height in cm - 100)]

1. Convert height to centimeters:
• 5 feet 6 inches = 66 inches
• 66 inches x 2.54 cm/inch = 167.64 cm

2. Calculate initial DBW baseline:
• 167.64 - 100 = 67.64 kg

3. Deduct 10% for the Filipino/Asian frame adjustment:
• 10% of 67.64 = 6.764 kg
• 67.64 kg - 6.764 kg = 60.876 kg

Rounding 60.876 kg to the nearest whole number gives 61 kg.', '["58 kg","72 kg","61 kg","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T14:43:18.419Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T14:43:18.419Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T14:43:18.602Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362557616-176', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'The desirable body weight of a 4-year-old girl is:', '16 kg', 'For children 1–6 years of age, a commonly taught formula for estimating expected or desirable body weight is Weight (kg) = (age in years × 2) + 8. 

For a 4-year-old child, the calculation is therefore (4 × 2) + 8 = 16 kg. Thus, the answer is 16 kg. This is a quick estimation formula commonly used in nutrition education and examination settings.', '["12 kg","14.5 kg","16 kg","20 kg"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T14:54:14.841Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T14:54:14.841Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T14:54:15.017Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362954916-199', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'The immediate goal of dietary management in acute diarrhea is:', 'prevent dehydration', 'In acute diarrhea, the rapid and massive loss of water and critical electrolytes (like sodium, potassium, and bicarbonate) through the stool pose an immediate risk of hypovolemic shock and cardiovascular collapse, particularly in vulnerable populations.

While preventing nutritional deficiencies and restoring long-term nutritional status are essential milestones during the subsequent convalescent phase, they cannot be addressed if the patient is acutely unstable. Therefore, the absolute primary, immediate goal of clinical dietary management is maintaining fluid volume and preventing or correcting dehydration through the immediate administration of oral rehydration solutions (ORS).', '["restore nutritional status","prevent dehydration","prevent deficiency","withhold food"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-23T08:32:58.016Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T08:32:58.016Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T08:32:58.016Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360473855-102', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Fats and carbohydrates are called “protein sparing” due to which of the following factors?', 'Their presence reduces the need of utilizing proteins for energy', 'Fats and carbohydrates provide adequate energy substrates (ATP) for cellular metabolism, preventing amino acids from being deaminated and used for fuel, thereby sparing dietary and somatic proteins for tissue maintenance and repair.', '["They comprise the major portions of diets","The body does not need protein if fats and carbohydrates are present","Their presence reduces the need of utilizing proteins for energy","They prevent storage of proteins"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:35:45.277Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:35:45.277Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:35:45.277Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360507135-104', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'The weight loss and wasting that often occur in COPD can be caused by:', 'all of the above', 'Weight loss and muscle wasting are common in advanced COPD and may result from several interacting factors. 
• Reduced food intake may occur because of dyspnea, fatigue, early satiety, or difficulty eating while breathing. 
• Increased metabolic rate can occur because the work of breathing is increased, raising energy expenditure. 
• Reduced exercise tolerance decreases physical activity and contributes to loss of muscle mass and strength. 

These factors can create a cycle of poor intake → weight loss → muscle loss → reduced respiratory muscle strength → greater difficulty breathing. Therefore, nutrition management aims to provide adequate energy and protein while avoiding overfeeding.', '["reduced food intake","increased metabolic rate","reduced exercise tolerance","all of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T13:54:53.778Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T13:54:53.778Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T13:54:53.778Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360533917-105', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Nutrition therapy for a person with respiratory failure includes:', 'careful attention to providing enough, but not too much energy', 'In patients with acute or chronic respiratory failure, providing too much total energy (overfeeding) is dangerous. Excess calories—especially from carbohydrates but also from fats and proteins—accelerate lipogenesis, which drives up carbon dioxide (CO₂) production. Since the patient''s compromised respiratory system cannot handle the increased respiratory workload needed to blow off this extra CO₂, overfeeding can trigger hypercapnia and make it extremely difficult to wean them off a mechanical ventilator.

At the same time, underfeeding must be strictly avoided. If a patient does not receive enough calories, their body will enter a catabolic state, breaking down skeletal muscle—including the diaphragm and intercostal respiratory muscles—which worsens muscle weakness and leads to respiratory muscle failure. Therefore, nutrition therapy must be balanced to provide enough energy to meet metabolic needs without exceeding them.', '["careful attention to providing enough, but not too much energy","a generous fluid intake to facilitate mucus clearance","a high fat intake to prevent weight loss and wasting","a high carbohydrate intake to increase carbon dioxide production"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-23T09:11:23.428Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T09:11:23.428Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T09:11:23.428Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361258901-137', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Non-digestible food ingredients that encourage the growth of beneficial bacteria:', 'Prebiotic', 'Prebiotics are substrates that are selectively utilized by host microorganisms and provide a health benefit. Many are non-digestible carbohydrates, such as certain oligosaccharides and inulin-type fructans, that reach the colon and are fermented by beneficial microorganisms. This fermentation can produce short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs), which contribute to intestinal and metabolic health. Thus, the description in the question corresponds to a prebiotic, not a probiotic. 

For NDLE purposes, distinguish the three terms clearly: 

Prebiotics are substrates that beneficial microorganisms use; 
Probiotics are live microorganisms that provide a health benefit when administered in adequate amounts; and 
Microbiota refers to the community of microorganisms living in a particular environment, such as the gastrointestinal tract.', '["Probiotic","Prebiotic","Microbiota","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T13:30:16.611Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T13:30:16.611Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T13:30:16.786Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360824736-120', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which of the following statements is FALSE?', 'None of the above', 'All three statements are considered true based on commonly used Philippine food-composition references for purine content. Purines are naturally occurring compounds found in many foods. They are metabolized to uric acid, making dietary purine intake particularly relevant in the nutritional management of gout and hyperuricemia. 

• Tamban (sardines) is a fish with relatively high purine content. 
• Dried beans also contain purines, but their purine content may be lower than that of certain high-purine fish such as sardines. 
• Chicken organ meats, including gizzard and intestine, contain substantial amounts of purines. 
• Pork liver is a high-purine organ meat and has a higher purine content than pork blood in the referenced food data.', '["Tamban has higher purine content as compared to dried beans.","Chicken gizzard has about the same purine content of that of the chicken intestine.","Pork liver has higher purine content as compared to pork blood","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:15:28.921Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:15:28.921Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:15:28.921Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360739276-116', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'What should be done when a known diabetic patient is undergoing hypoglycemia?', 'Give 1 tbsp sugar, then wait for 15 minutes', 'For a conscious patient with hypoglycemia who can safely swallow, the recommended immediate treatment is the 15-15 rule: provide approximately 15 g of fast-acting carbohydrate, then recheck blood glucose after 15 minutes. If blood glucose remains low, repeat the treatment. One tablespoon of table sugar provides approximately 12–15 g of carbohydrate, making it the best answer among the choices.', '["Give 1 tbsp sugar, then wait for 15 minutes","Give a cup of water to rehydrate","Inject an insulin short","Provide 1 pc candy"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T15:32:05.537Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T15:32:05.537Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T15:32:05.537Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360806038-119', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'What is the BMI of a 5''4" individual with an actual weight of 60 kg?', '22.7  kg/m²', 'BMI is calculated using: Weight in kg ÷ Height in m² 

First, convert 5''4" to meters: 5’4” = 64 inches = 1.6256 meters 

Then calculate BMI: 60 kg ÷ (1.6256)² = 60 ÷ 2.6426 = ~22.7 kg/m²', '["22  kg/m²","22.7  kg/m²","23  kg/m²","25  kg/m²"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-23T09:31:43.215Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T09:31:43.215Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T09:31:43.215Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360756642-117', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Inherent Na in the diet is 950 mg. The Diet Rx states that your patient is allowed 90 mEq/day. How much is the salt is to be added to meet the Diet Rx?', '2.8 grams', 'Step 1: Convert the Diet Rx from mEq to milligrams (mg) of Sodium
	• Rule: 1 mEq of Sodium (Na) = 23 mg
	• Calculation: 90 mEq x 23 mg/mEq = 2,070 mg of total allowed Sodium

Step 2: Calculate the additional Sodium needed
	• Subtract the inherent sodium already in the food from the total allowed sodium.
	• Calculation: 2,070 mg (Total Allowed) - 950 mg (Inherent) = 1,120 mg of additional Sodium needed

Step 3: Convert the additional Sodium to grams of Table Salt (NaCl)
	• Rule: Table salt is 40% sodium (0.40). To find the amount of salt, divide the sodium milligrams by 0.40.
	• Calculation: 1,120 mg / 0.40 = 2,800 mg of salt
	• Convert to grams: 2,800 mg / 1,000 = 2.8 grams of salt', '["2.8 grams","2.0 grams","4.3 grams","5.0 grams"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T11:38:56.482Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:38:56.482Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:38:56.482Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360878484-122', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Diet prescribed for patients with calcium oxalate stones', 'Acid-ash diet', 'Historically, an acid-ash diet was prescribed for calcium oxalate kidney stones. The traditional rationale was that acidification of the urine was thought to help maintain the solubility of calcium salts and reduce the formation of calcium-based stones. In contrast, an alkaline-ash diet was traditionally used for uric acid and cystine stones, for which increasing urinary pH promotes greater solubility.', '["Alkaline-ash diet","Acid-ash diet","High fiber diet","Mediterranean Diet"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T15:36:45.381Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T15:36:45.381Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T15:36:45.381Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360894100-123', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'A patient is prescribed a diet containing 1,500 mg of sodium. How many mEq of sodium are contained in the diet?', '65 mEq Na', 'To convert milligrams of sodium to milliequivalents (mEq), use: mEq = (mg * valence) ÷ atomic weight 

Sodium (Na⁺) has: 
• Atomic weight ≈ 23 
• Valence = 1 

Therefore: mEq Na = (1500 * 1) ÷ 23 = 65.2 mEq 

Thus, the calculated value is approximately 65 mEq.', '["55 mEq Na","60 mEq Na","65 mEq Na","70 mEq Na"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-23T06:53:41.619Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T06:53:41.619Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T06:53:41.619Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360915749-124', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'A low calcium diet contains no more than _____ mg calcium', '600 mg', 'In traditional therapeutic diet planning, a low-calcium diet is generally prescribed to provide no more than 600 mg calcium per day. This is a therapeutic restriction, not the recommended daily calcium intake for healthy individuals. 

Calcium is essential for bone and tooth mineralization, muscle contraction, nerve transmission, and blood clotting, so restricting calcium should be done only when clinically indicated. For NDLE purposes, when the question asks for the traditional definition of a low-calcium diet, remember ≤600 mg/day.', '["500 mg","600 mg","700 mg","800 mg"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-23T09:33:00.351Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T09:33:00.351Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T09:33:00.351Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360933631-125', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Diet prescribed for patients with cystine stone', 'Alkaline-ash diet', 'An alkaline-ash diet is the traditional dietary prescription for patients with cystine stones. Cystinuria is an inherited disorder in which excessive amounts of cystine are excreted in the urine. Because cystine has poor solubility in acidic urine, it is more likely to precipitate and form stones. Increasing urinary pH makes cystine more soluble and therefore helps reduce cystine crystallization. This principle remains clinically relevant. 

Current management of cystinuria emphasizes high fluid intake to dilute urinary cystine and urinary alkalinization, commonly with potassium citrate, to maintain an appropriately alkaline urine. Dietary sodium restriction is also important because high sodium intake can increase urinary cystine excretion.', '["Alkaline-ash diet","Acid-ash diet","High fiber diet","Mediterranean Diet"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T14:05:52.860Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T14:05:52.860Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T14:05:52.860Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360963266-126', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which of the following statements is true?', 'Creatinine is directly affected by dietary protein intake', 'While serum creatinine is primarily a reflection of endogenous muscle mass, it can be significantly and directly affected by dietary protein intake, specifically through the consumption of cooked meat.

During the cooking process, the intense heat converts a portion of the creatine naturally present in animal muscle tissue into creatinine. When a patient consumes a cooked-meat meal, this pre-formed creatinine is rapidly absorbed across the gastrointestinal tract and enters the bloodstream.

This causes a temporary, artificial spike in serum creatinine levels independently of the patient''s actual kidney function. This dietary variable is clinically critical to recognize during nutrition and renal assessments, as a recent high-protein meat meal can skew laboratory results and lead to a falsely lower estimated Glomerular Filtration Rate (eGFR).', '["BUN can be used as the sole indicator of nutritional status","Cereals are acid-forming foods","Creatinine is directly affected by dietary protein intake","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-23T09:36:10.035Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T09:36:10.035Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T09:36:10.035Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361117906-132', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Statements describing the management of celiac disease:', 'The management of celiac disease is a life-long gluten-free diet.', 'The cornerstone of celiac disease management is a strict, lifelong gluten-free diet. Gluten exposure triggers an immune-mediated response that can cause intestinal mucosal injury even when the patient does not experience obvious gastrointestinal symptoms. Therefore, the absence of symptoms does not mean that celiac disease has disappeared or that gluten can safely be reintroduced. Strict adherence to a gluten-free diet promotes intestinal healing, improves nutritional status, and reduces the risk of complications associated with ongoing gluten exposure.', '["The management of celiac disease is a life-long gluten-free diet.","Small quantities of gluten may be allowed when celiac disease no longer manifests.","Enough scientific evidence exists for what constitutes a safe amount of gluten intake.","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:36:24.209Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:36:24.209Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:36:24.209Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361001180-127', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which of the following statements is true?', 'All of the above', 'All three statements describe principles used in therapeutic diet planning for renal conditions. 

Creatinine clearance is an indicator of renal filtration, and protein prescription in renal disease is adjusted according to the degree of renal impairment. As renal function declines, protein intake may need to be controlled to reduce nitrogenous waste production. Therefore, within the context of the diet-therapy principle tested here, better/higher creatinine clearance permits a less restricted protein intake, whereas greater renal impairment generally requires closer protein management. 

For urinary stone management, oatmeal can be included in an oxalate-controlled diet because it is not among the foods classically considered very high in oxalate. 

The statement regarding fats reflects the older diet-therapy teaching that high fat intake may promote urinary urate retention, particularly in the context of uric acid metabolism and stone formation.', '["The higher the creatinine clearance, the greater the level of protein allowed.","Oatmeal is allowed in oxalate-controlled diet","Fats favor the retention of urates","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T14:16:05.193Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T14:16:05.193Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T14:16:05.193Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361021277-128', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'This diet is known as the cholesterol- and saturated fat restricted diet', 'TLC diet', 'TLC stands for Therapeutic Lifestyle Changes. It is a clinically designed medical nutrition therapy created by the National Institutes of Health (NIH) specifically to lower high blood cholesterol and reduce the risk of heart disease. Its primary guidelines mandate restricting saturated fat to less than 7% of total daily calories and limiting dietary cholesterol to less than 200 mg per day.

	• Atkin''s diet & Ketogenic Diet: These are low-carbohydrate, high-fat diets. They encourage high consumption of fats (including saturated fats) and proteins, making them the opposite of a fat-restricted eating plan.
	• DASH diet: While DASH (Dietary Approaches to Stop Hypertension) is also a heart-healthy diet low in saturated fat, its definitive, hallmark feature is sodium restriction aimed at lowering blood pressure. The TLC diet is the classic textbook answer explicitly labeled as the cholesterol- and saturated fat-restricted diet.', '["Ketogenic Diet","DASH diet","TLC diet","Atkin''s diet"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T15:07:14.734Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T15:07:14.734Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T15:07:14.734Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361038961-129', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which among the following has the highest sodium content?', 'Carrots', 'Among the choices, carrots have the highest sodium content based on the food-composition data underlying this question. Although all four are fresh vegetables and are generally considered low-sodium foods, their naturally occurring sodium concentrations are not identical. This is an important distinction in nutrition assessment and diet therapy: foods can be categorized as generally low in sodium while still having measurable differences in their sodium content. 

For NDLE purposes, this question is primarily testing knowledge of the relative sodium content of common vegetables, rather than asking which food is considered “high sodium” clinically. In a sodium-restricted diet, carrots, cabbage, sayote, and pake may all be used, with the major dietary sodium reduction usually coming from limiting salt, processed foods, canned products, seasoning mixes, sauces, and other sodium-containing condiments.', '["Carrots","Cabbage","Sayote","Pake"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-23T09:37:42.727Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T09:37:42.727Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T09:37:42.727Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361099908-131', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'All the foods in the list are not allowed among patients with celiac disease:', 'Distilled white wine, vodka, vanilla ice cream in cone, crema de fruta', '• Vanilla ice cream in a cone: Regular ice cream cones are made from wheat flour, which contains gluten and is highly toxic to individuals with celiac disease.
• Crema de fruta: This traditional Filipino dessert is natively constructed using layers of sponge cake (made with standard wheat flour) or graham crackers, both of which contain gluten and are strictly forbidden.

Because a person with celiac disease cannot consume wheat-based cones or traditional sponge cake/graham desserts, they cannot safely eat everything presented in this selection.

• Other choices: These lists contain naturally gluten-free foods (like corn, maple syrup, wine, fresh vegetables, tofu, and lactose-free milk) which are generally allowed on a celiac diet. Therefore, they do not fulfill the criteria of a "not allowed" list.', '["Fresh vegetables washed in water with baking soda, margarine, tofu, Lactaid","Distilled white wine, vodka, vanilla ice cream in cone, crema de fruta","Corn and maple syrup, fruit ices, sorbet, wine","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-23T09:35:01.934Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T09:35:01.934Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T09:35:01.934Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361194919-134', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Pregnancy-induced hypertension is not:', 'a complication of diabetes', 'Diabetes mellitus (both preexisting type 1/type 2 diabetes and gestational diabetes) is a major risk factor that predisposes a woman to developing pregnancy-induced hypertension (PIH). However, PIH itself is a specific vascular disorder that arises directly from placental dysfunction and endothelial stress, not a medical complication caused by diabetes.

	• Characterized by edema: PIH is classically identified in board exam frameworks by a hallmark triad of symptoms: hypertension, generalized or pitting edema, and proteinuria.
	• A complication of pregnancy: PIH is, by definition, a major medical complication that specifically occurs during pregnancy (typically developing after the 20th week of gestation).
	• Characterized by hypertension: Elevated blood pressure (systolic BP equal to or greater than 140 mmHg or diastolic BP equal to or greater than 90 mmHg) is the primary diagnostic feature of the condition.', '["a complication of diabetes","a complication of pregnancy","characterized by hypertension","characterized by edema"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-23T08:55:18.558Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T08:55:18.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T08:55:18.875Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361233728-136', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Azotemia is detected through the measurement of:', 'Blood urea nitrogen', 'Azotemia is a medical condition characterized by an abnormally high concentration of nitrogenous waste compounds (such as blood urea nitrogen (BUN) and creatinine) in the blood.

It occurs when the kidneys are damaged or poorly perfused, leaving them unable to filter and excrete these metabolic waste products efficiently. It is a hallmark diagnostic feature of acute kidney injury (AKI) and chronic kidney disease (CKD).

• Uric acid in urine: While uric acid is a nitrogenous waste, tracking its presence in urine does not define azotemia. Elevated blood uric acid is known as hyperuricemia, which is linked to gout or kidney stones.
• Blood glucose: This measures blood sugar levels and is used to monitor metabolism, insulin function, and conditions like diabetes mellitus.
• Serum albumin: This measures the primary protein in blood plasma to evaluate a patient''s liver function and overall visceral protein/nutritional status.', '["Blood urea nitrogen","Uric acid in urine","Serum albumin","Blood glucose"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-23T07:30:02.713Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T07:30:02.713Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T07:30:02.713Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362581783-177', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'A diabetic patient with HbA1c test result of 10% is indicative of _____ glucose control.', 'Poor', 'An HbA1c of 10% indicates poor glycemic control and reflects substantially elevated average blood glucose over approximately the preceding 2–3 months. HbA1c measures the percentage of hemoglobin that has glucose attached to it (glycated hemoglobin). The higher the average blood glucose concentration, the greater the proportion of glycated hemoglobin. An HbA1c of 10% corresponds to an estimated average glucose (eAG) of approximately 240 mg/dL (13.3 mmol/L). 

For most nonpregnant adults with diabetes, the commonly recommended treatment goal is an HbA1c of <7%, although targets should be individualized according to age, comorbidities, hypoglycemia risk, life expectancy, and patient preferences.', '["Good","Fair","Moderate","Poor"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T14:12:25.252Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T14:12:25.252Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T14:12:25.252Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361212436-135', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'The addition of 15 grams salt in the diet increases sodium content by:', '6 grams', 'Table salt is chemically known as sodium chloride (NaCl). By molecular weight, pure table salt is composed of approximately 40% sodium and 60% chloride.

To determine how much sodium is present in a given mass of table salt, you multiply the total mass by 40% (0.40):
15 grams of salt x 0.40 = 6 grams of sodium', '["3,000 mg","2 grams","6 grams","8 grams"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:35:36.596Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:35:36.596Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:35:36.596Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361280516-138', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Why is saturated fat called "saturated"?', 'The fatty acid carbon chains are saturated with hydrogen', 'Saturated fatty acids contain only single bonds between adjacent carbon atoms, meaning each carbon atom is fully bonded ("saturated") with hydrogen atoms.', '["The fat is saturated with water","The fatty acid carbon chains are saturated with hydrogen","The fatty acid chains can have more water added","They saturate the body fat when eaten"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T11:40:28.941Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:40:28.941Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:40:28.941Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361387878-143', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Factors that improve LDL to HDL ratio except:', 'Higher intakes of saturated fatty acids', 'Consuming higher amounts of saturated fatty acids raises both low-density lipoprotein (LDL) and high-density lipoprotein (HDL) cholesterol. However, it drives a much larger increase in LDL ("bad") cholesterol compared to HDL. This selectively worsens the overall LDL-to-HDL ratio, increasing cardiovascular risk.

• Moderate R-OH (Alcohol) Consumption: In medical terminology, "R-OH" stands for alcohol. Scientific literature consistently shows that light-to-moderate alcohol intake (such as one drink a day for women or two for men) can moderately elevate HDL cholesterol levels, which helps optimize the blood lipid ratio.
• Antioxidants: Dietary antioxidants (found in abundance in colorful fruits, vegetables, and whole grains) shield circulating LDL particles from undergoing oxidative modification. Because oxidized LDL is what triggers plaque accumulation in artery walls, antioxidants maintain healthy vascular transport and protect the functional efficiency of lipid handling.
• Weight Control: Dropping excess body weight and avoiding abdominal obesity are highly effective ways to manage dyslipidemia. Achieving a healthy weight directly reduces total triglycerides and lowers LDL cholesterol levels while boosting cardioprotective HDL.', '["Weight control","Antioxidants","Moderate R-OH consumption","Higher intakes of saturated fatty acids"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-23T07:13:44.004Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T07:13:44.004Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T07:13:44.311Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361404260-144', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Sources of trans fatty acid except:', 'Sardines', 'Trans fatty acids can occur naturally in small amounts in milk and butter because ruminant animals produce naturally occurring trans fats through microbial fermentation in the rumen. Trans fats can also be present in commercial baked products such as cookies, particularly when partially hydrogenated oils are used, although their use has been substantially reduced or eliminated in many countries.

Sardines, on the other hand, are oily fish and are primarily valued as sources of omega-3 polyunsaturated fatty acids, particularly EPA and DHA. Although naturally occurring trans fatty acids can be detected in various foods in very small amounts, sardines are not considered a meaningful dietary source of trans fat.', '["Cookies","Milk","Butter","Sardines"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T15:32:44.297Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T15:32:44.297Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T15:32:44.297Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361467974-146', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Allowed amount of purine in a purine-restricted diet:', '150 mg', 'A purine-restricted diet is designed to reduce dietary purine exposure in patients with gout, hyperuricemia, or uric acid–related conditions. Traditional diet manuals commonly identify foods containing 150 mg or more of purine per serving as foods to be restricted or avoided. Thus, 150 mg is an important threshold associated with purine-restricted diet teaching.', '["150 mg","250 mg","300 mg","0 mg"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T14:26:21.841Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T14:26:21.841Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T14:26:21.841Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362240252-161', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Nutritional management when a patient experiences vomiting', 'Withhold food until vomiting subsides', 'During active vomiting, the immediate nutritional priority is to allow the gastrointestinal tract to rest while preventing dehydration. Food is generally withheld temporarily until vomiting subsides, after which oral intake can be gradually resumed as tolerated. Once the patient can tolerate fluids, small, frequent amounts of fluid are preferred rather than large volumes at one time. 

Oral rehydration solutions may be particularly useful when fluid and electrolyte losses are significant. After vomiting has stopped, food should be reintroduced gradually, usually beginning with small portions and advancing according to tolerance. Large amounts of fluid with meals can worsen fullness and nausea, while hot, strongly scented, greasy, or spicy foods may be poorly tolerated. Cold or room-temperature foods may sometimes be better tolerated because they have less odor.', '["Liberal fluid intake during meals","Withhold food until vomiting subsides","All of the above","Serve hot soup"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-23T09:33:05.464Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T09:33:05.464Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T09:33:05.464Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361482939-147', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Inactive precursors of enzymes:', 'Zymogens', 'A zymogen (also frequently referred to as a proenzyme) is a biologically inactive precursor of an enzyme. It requires an organic chemical alteration—most commonly a specific biochemical cleavage event where a piece of its blocking peptide chain is sliced off—to expose its active catalytic site and transform into a functional, active enzyme.

In human nutrition and digestion, zymogens are absolutely critical for structural self-preservation. For example, the pancreas synthesizes and secretes protein-digesting enzymes in their inactive zymogen forms (such as trypsinogen, chymotrypsinogen, and procarboxypeptidase). This ensures that these powerful proteolytic enzymes do not accidentally digest and destroy the cells of the pancreas or the pancreatic duct themselves. They are only activated once they safely reach the lumen of the small intestine.

• Hormones: Hormones are chemical messengers synthesized by endocrine glands that travel through the bloodstream to regulate distant target organs and physiological activities. They are not enzymes or enzyme precursors.
• Nucleic acid : These are large biopolymers (like DNA and RNA) responsible for carrying and translating genetic information within cells.
• Holoenzymes: A holoenzyme is a fully active, structurally complete enzyme that is combined with its necessary coenzyme, cofactor, or prosthetic group. It is the opposite of an inactive precursor.', '["Nucleic acid","Zymogens","Hormones","Holoenzymes"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-23T09:25:02.636Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T09:25:02.636Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T09:25:02.636Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361500255-148', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Vitamin E maintains prothrombin level in the blood plasma. Vitamin K is an anticoagulant.', 'Both statements are incorrect.', 'Statement 1 is incorrect. Vitamin K, not vitamin E, is essential for the synthesis of several blood-clotting proteins, including prothrombin (factor II), in the liver. Vitamin K functions as a cofactor for the γ-carboxylation of specific glutamate residues on vitamin K–dependent clotting factors, allowing them to participate effectively in coagulation. Vitamin E, in contrast, is primarily recognized for its antioxidant function and does not maintain plasma prothrombin levels as its physiological role. 

Statement 2 is also incorrect. Vitamin K is not an anticoagulant; it supports normal blood coagulation. Vitamin K deficiency can impair clotting-factor activation and increase bleeding tendency. In fact, warfarin, an anticoagulant medication, works partly by inhibiting vitamin K recycling, thereby reducing the activity of vitamin K–dependent clotting factors.', '["Only statement 1 is correct.","Only statement 2 is correct.","Both statements are correct.","Both statements are incorrect."]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T15:00:49.872Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T15:00:49.872Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T15:00:49.872Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361640799-153', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Role of villi and microvilli in the small intestine is to:', 'Increase the surface area for nutrient absorption', 'The villi and microvilli are specialized structures lining the small intestine that greatly increase the surface area available for nutrient absorption. Villi are finger-like projections of the intestinal mucosa, while microvilli are microscopic projections of the apical membrane of enterocytes that form the brush border. Together, they maximize contact between digested nutrients and absorptive epithelial cells, making the small intestine highly efficient at nutrient absorption. 

The villi also contain blood capillaries and a central lymphatic vessel called a lacteal. Water-soluble nutrients such as glucose and amino acids generally enter the blood capillaries, whereas most dietary lipids are incorporated into chylomicrons and enter the lymphatic system through the lacteals. This structural organization is important in understanding why the small intestine is the principal site of nutrient absorption.', '["Produce all the enzymes needed for digestion","Slow down the movement of food","Increase the surface area for nutrient absorption","Secrete water in the chyme"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T15:01:26.519Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T15:01:26.519Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T15:01:26.519Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362376044-166', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'The enzyme sucrase acts on sucrose to produce:', 'Glucose and fructose', 'Sucrose is a disaccharide composed of one molecule of glucose and one molecule of fructose. The brush-border enzyme sucrase hydrolyzes the glycosidic bond in sucrose, releasing glucose and fructose as the final monosaccharide products. These monosaccharides can then be absorbed by the small intestine through specific transport mechanisms. 

Sucrase is part of the intestinal sucrase-isomaltase complex located on the brush border of enterocytes. The resulting glucose and fructose are absorbed primarily in the small intestine and enter the portal circulation, with glucose and fructose using different transport proteins. This illustrates the importance of brush-border enzymes in completing carbohydrate digestion before absorption.', '["Glucose and galactose","Glucose","Glucose and fructose","Maltose"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:30:06.375Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:30:06.375Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:30:06.375Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361536704-150', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Nutrients that leave the GIT via the lymphatic system:', 'Fats and fat-soluble vitamins', 'During digestion in the small intestine, long-chain fatty acids and fat-soluble vitamins (Vitamins A, D, E, and K) are packed into spherical lipid clusters called micelles. Once absorbed into the intestinal lining cells (enterocytes), they are re-assembled into even larger lipoprotein spheres called chylomicrogens. Because these structures are too bulky to fit through the tiny pores (fenestrations) of standard blood capillaries, they cannot enter the bloodstream directly.

Instead, these large lipid packages slip into lacteals—specialized, wide-pored lymphatic capillaries located at the center of each intestinal villus. The fluid (called chyle) travels slowly through the lymphatic system, completely bypassing the liver at first, before emptying directly into the main bloodstream via the thoracic duct near the heart.

• Water, Proteins, Minerals, and Water-Soluble Vitamins: These nutrients are water-soluble and split into small, single molecules (like glucose, amino acids, and free ions). Because they are small enough to pass easily through the pores of regular capillaries, they exit the GI tract straight into the portal blood circulation, traveling directly to the liver for immediate processing.', '["Proteins and minerals","Water and minerals","All vitamins and minerals","Fats and fat-soluble vitamins"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-23T08:43:30.056Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T08:43:30.056Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T08:43:30.056Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362201000-159', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'All contain carbohydrates except:', 'Lean meat', 'Carbohydrates are found in a wide range of foods, particularly plant foods and certain dairy products. Wheat bread contains starch, while ripe fruits contain naturally occurring sugars such as glucose and fructose. Pasteurized milk also contains carbohydrate in the form of lactose, the naturally occurring sugar in milk. Pasteurization does not remove or substantially eliminate the lactose in milk. 

Lean meat, in contrast, contains primarily protein and fat and provides essentially no carbohydrate. Although very small amounts of carbohydrate-related compounds may be present in animal tissues, meat is generally classified as a negligible-carbohydrate food for nutrition and dietary planning purposes.', '["Wheat bread","Ripe fruit","Lean meat","Pasteurized milk"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T13:21:06.750Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T13:21:06.750Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T13:21:06.750Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362689728-183', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'In conditions characterized by hypochlorhydria or achlorhydria, a suitable diet is one that is:', 'easy to digest or consists of predigested protein like protein hydrolysates', 'Hypochlorhydria refers to reduced gastric acid secretion, while achlorhydria refers to an absence or near-absence of hydrochloric acid (HCl) in the stomach. Gastric acid contributes to the denaturation of dietary proteins and facilitates the conversion of pepsinogen to pepsin, which begins protein digestion in the stomach. When gastric acid is markedly reduced or absent, protein digestion in the stomach may be impaired. 

Therefore, traditionally, a diet emphasizing easily digested foods and, when clinically indicated, predigested protein such as protein hydrolysates may be recommended. However, the important modern clinical point is that hypochlorhydria or achlorhydria does not automatically require a low-protein diet or routine use of protein hydrolysates. Protein digestion continues in the small intestine through pancreatic and brush-border enzymes. Management should focus on the underlying cause and the patient''s nutritional status.', '["low in fiber","low in protein","easy to digest or consists of predigested protein like protein hydrolysates","acid-ash"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:09:49.649Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:09:49.649Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:09:49.649Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361564987-151', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'A Recommended Dietary Allowance (RDA) represents the:', 'Average amount of a nutrient considered adequate to meet the nutrient needs of all healthy people', 'The Recommended Dietary Allowance (RDA) is the average daily nutrient intake level that is sufficient to meet the nutrient requirements of nearly all (97–98%) healthy individuals in a particular age, sex, and life-stage group. It is primarily used as a goal for planning nutritionally adequate diets for individuals. Importantly, the RDA is not literally enough for 100% of people, because individual nutrient requirements vary. 

The RDA is derived from the Estimated Average Requirement (EAR). When the requirement distribution is approximately normal, the RDA is generally set at the EAR plus two standard deviations, thereby covering the requirements of approximately 97–98% of healthy individuals. In clinical and community nutrition practice, RDAs are useful for planning diets, while the EAR is generally more appropriate for assessing the adequacy of nutrient intakes in groups.', '["Highest amount of a nutrient that appears safe for most healthy people","Average amount of a nutrient that will maintain a specific biochemical or physiological function in half of the population","Average amount of a nutrient considered adequate to meet the nutrient needs of all healthy people","Lowest amount of a nutrient considered adequate to meet the nutrient needs of all healthy people"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:29:38.495Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:29:38.495Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:29:38.495Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361707730-155', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Positive nitrogen balance can be observed during:', 'Pregnancy', 'Nitrogen balance reflects the relationship between nitrogen intake, primarily from dietary protein, and nitrogen losses, mainly through urinary nitrogen excretion. Positive nitrogen balance occurs when nitrogen intake exceeds nitrogen losses, indicating that the body is retaining protein and synthesizing new tissue. This occurs during periods of growth and tissue building, including pregnancy, childhood, adolescence, and recovery from severe nutritional depletion. 

During pregnancy, positive nitrogen balance supports the synthesis of maternal tissues and the growth of the fetus, placenta, and maternal blood volume and other tissues. In contrast, fever, surgery, and shock are generally associated with physiological stress that increases protein catabolism and nitrogen losses, resulting in negative nitrogen balance, particularly when the stress response is significant or prolonged.', '["Fever","Surgery","Shock","Pregnancy"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T14:44:57.639Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T14:44:57.639Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T14:44:57.825Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361722945-156', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Vessels where the exchange of oxygen, nutrients and waste products take place.', 'Capillaries', 'Capillaries are the smallest and most numerous blood vessels in the human body, serving as the functional "transfer stations" connecting arterioles to venules.

Unlike thick, muscular arteries or larger veins, the walls of capillaries are only one cell layer thick, composed entirely of a single layer of endothelial cells. This extremely thin barrier allows oxygen, glucose, amino acids, and vital hormones to readily pass from the bloodstream out into the surrounding body tissues via passive diffusion.

At the exact same time, cellular metabolic waste products (like carbon dioxide and urea) pass in the opposite direction—from the tissue cells back into the capillary lumen—so they can be safely transported away for filtration and excretion.

• Veins: Veins have relatively thick walls with one-way valves designed exclusively to carry deoxygenated blood under low pressure back up toward the heart. Their thick structural layers prevent any direct transfer of oxygen or nutrients.
• Arteries: Arteries have highly elastic, thick, muscular walls built to withstand the high-pressure surges of oxygenated blood being pumped directly away from the heart. Their dense structure completely prevents the diffusion of gases or nutrients.', '["Veins","Arteries","Capillaries","Veins and capillaries"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-23T08:15:09.946Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T08:15:09.946Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T08:15:10.260Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362183952-158', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'End products of the anaerobic phase of glucose metabolism', 'Pyruvate and lactic acid', 'The anaerobic phase of glucose metabolism refers primarily to glycolysis, which occurs in the cytoplasm and does not require oxygen directly. Glycolysis converts one molecule of glucose into two molecules of pyruvate, producing a net gain of 2 ATP and 2 NADH. When oxygen availability is limited or when glycolytic flux exceeds mitochondrial oxidative capacity, pyruvate can be reduced to lactate by lactate dehydrogenase. This regenerates NAD⁺, allowing glycolysis to continue producing ATP.', '["Pyruvate and lactic acid","Acetyl CoA","Carbon dioxide and water","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T13:58:53.529Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T13:58:53.529Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T13:58:53.529Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361689247-154', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'A reduced blood albumin level leads to:', 'Edema', 'Albumin is the primary protein found in blood plasma. Its main physiological job is to generate and maintain colloid osmotic pressure (also called oncotic pressure) inside the blood vessels. This pressure acts like a magnet that holds water inside the bloodstream.

When blood albumin levels drop significantly (hypoalbuminemia)—which is common in liver failure, kidney disease (nephrotic syndrome), or severe malnutrition—the oncotic pressure drops with it. Without enough albumin to hold the water inside, fluid leaks out of the capillaries and into the surrounding interstitial tissues.

This abnormal collection of fluid in the tissue spaces causes noticeable swelling, known clinically as edema.

• Alkalosis: Alkalosis refers to an abnormally high, basic blood pH (above 7.45). Albumin does act as a minor blood buffer, but a low level of albumin does not systematically push the entire body into a state of metabolic or respiratory alkalosis. Blood pH is primarily managed by your lungs (carbon dioxide) and kidneys (bicarbonate).
• Dehydration: Dehydration means a total body depletion of water and fluids, usually caused by poor intake, sweating, or vomiting. In hypoalbuminemia, the patient is not lacking water—they actually have plenty of fluid, but it is just trapped in the wrong place (leaking out into the tissues rather than staying inside the blood vessels).
• Acidosis: Acidosis refers to an abnormally low, acidic blood pH (below 7.35). Because albumin molecules carry a slight negative charge and normally bind to a tiny amount of hydrogen ions, a severe loss of albumin would theoretically cause a very minor drop in unmeasured anions, but it never causes clinical metabolic or respiratory acidosis.', '["Dehydration","Edema","Acidosis","Alkalosis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:14:53.613Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:14:53.613Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:14:53.613Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362218484-160', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'In the WHO-BMI cut off point, a BMI of 35.99 is classified as:', 'Obese Class II', 'According to the WHO International BMI Classification: Underweight <18.5, Normal 18.5–24.9, Overweight/Pre-obese 25.0–29.9, Obese Class I 30.0–34.9, Obese Class II 35.0–39.9, and Obese Class III ≥40.0 kg/m².', '["Overweight","Pre-obese","Obese Class I","Obese Class II"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T13:40:18.928Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T13:40:18.928Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T13:40:18.928Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362310496-163', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Consistent carbohydrate diet means:', 'The amount of carbohydrate consumed and the timing of meals and snacks are consistent', 'A consistent carbohydrate diet is a meal-planning approach commonly used in the management of diabetes mellitus. Rather than eliminating carbohydrates, the goal is to maintain a relatively consistent amount of carbohydrate at meals and snacks from day to day, while keeping meal timing regular. This helps minimize fluctuations in blood glucose levels and improves the effectiveness of diabetes medications, particularly insulin and insulin secretagogues. The emphasis is on consistency, not on consuming a fixed amount of carbohydrates for every individual.', '["The type and amount of carbohydrate in the diet must be consistent as well as the timing of meals and snacks","The amount of carbohydrate consumed and the timing of meals and snacks are consistent","In case of hypoglycemia, 15 grams of carbohydrates are given every 15 minutes to achieve consistent blood sugar level","45 to 75 grams of carbohydrates are given at each meal to maintain normal blood sugar level"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-23T09:35:19.325Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T09:35:19.325Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T09:35:19.325Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362330163-164', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'During glycolysis and TCA, glucose is first broken down into _____, then _____, and finally
_____for use in the body.', 'pyruvate, acetyl CoA, energy', 'During glucose oxidation, glycolysis first converts glucose into pyruvate in the cytoplasm. Each glucose molecule produces two molecules of pyruvate. 

Under aerobic conditions, pyruvate enters the mitochondria and undergoes oxidative decarboxylation, producing acetyl-CoA. Acetyl-CoA then enters the tricarboxylic acid (TCA) cycle, where its carbon atoms are progressively oxidized and energy is captured primarily in the form of NADH and FADH₂. The reduced electron carriers generated during glycolysis, pyruvate oxidation, and the TCA cycle subsequently donate electrons to the electron transport chain, driving oxidative phosphorylation and the production of ATP. 

Thus, the simplified sequence tested by this question is glucose → pyruvate → acetyl-CoA → energy (ATP).', '["pyruvate, acetyl CoA, energy","amino acids, acetyl CoA, energy","acetyl CoA, pyruvate, energy","glycerol, pyruvate, energy"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T13:40:56.783Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T13:40:56.783Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T13:40:56.783Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362355761-165', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'What is the fate of excess intake of dietary protein?', 'After absorption, the extra amino acids will be rapidly degraded', 'Unlike carbohydrates and fats, the body does not have a specialized storage depot for excess amino acids or dietary protein. After dietary protein is digested and absorbed, amino acids enter the body''s amino acid pool and are used for protein synthesis and other metabolic functions. When amino acid availability exceeds the body''s immediate needs for protein synthesis and other required compounds, the excess amino acids are deaminated. Their nitrogen is converted primarily to urea in the liver and excreted in the urine, while their carbon skeletons can be used for energy or converted into glucose, ketone bodies, or other metabolites. 

Therefore, consuming more protein than needed does not result in the body storing the extra protein as protein for later use. The body continually regulates amino acid metabolism according to physiological needs. Excess energy from protein can ultimately contribute to energy storage as body fat if overall energy intake exceeds expenditure, but this occurs after amino acid catabolism, not through storage of excess dietary protein as a protein reserve.', '["After absorption, the liver will store the extra amino acids","After absorption, the extra amino acids will be rapidly degraded","Digestion will be decreased by 30-60% resulting in less absorption","After absorption, extra proteins will be synthesized and stored for use when protein intake returns to normal"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T13:28:11.099Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T13:28:11.099Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T13:28:11.099Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362393075-167', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Essential amino acids except:', 'Glutamine', 'Histidine, leucine, and lysine are essential amino acids, meaning they cannot be synthesized by the human body in sufficient amounts to meet physiological needs and therefore must be obtained from the diet. Histidine is particularly important for growth and tissue repair, leucine is a branched-chain amino acid that plays an important role in stimulating muscle protein synthesis, and lysine is involved in protein synthesis and several other metabolic functions. 

Glutamine is classified as a nonessential amino acid under normal physiological conditions because the body can synthesize it from glutamate and ammonia. However, during severe illness, trauma, burns, or other metabolic stress, glutamine requirements may increase substantially and endogenous synthesis may not meet demand. It is therefore often described as conditionally essential in certain clinical situations.', '["Histidine","Leucine","Glutamine","Lysine"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T13:32:51.917Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T13:32:51.917Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T13:32:51.917Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362467607-171', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which of the following contain the least amount of dietary fiber?', 'Well-milled rice', 'Well-milled rice contains the least dietary fiber among the choices because the milling process removes the outer layers of the rice grain, including much of the bran and germ, where dietary fiber is concentrated. As a result, well-milled or highly polished white rice contains substantially less fiber than less-refined grains and many plant foods. This makes well-milled rice a low-fiber carbohydrate source. 

In comparison, sinigwelas (Java plum), yellow camote (sweet potato), and lakatan (banana) are plant foods that retain their cellular structures and therefore provide dietary fiber. Their exact fiber content varies with variety, maturity, preparation, and serving size, but they generally provide more dietary fiber than well-milled rice. For NDLE purposes, the key concept is that refining and milling grains removes fiber-rich outer layers.', '["Sinigwelas","Yellow camote","Lakatan","Well-milled rice"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-23T09:15:23.214Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T09:15:23.214Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T09:15:23.214Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362525687-174', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Sodium equivalent of the low sodium diet?', 'Less than 2g sodium/day', 'A low-sodium diet generally limits sodium intake to approximately 2,000 mg/day or less, particularly in clinical nutrition for conditions such as hypertension, heart failure, and certain renal or hepatic disorders. Sodium restriction reduces sodium and water retention and may help control blood pressure and fluid balance. Importantly, the exact sodium prescription should be individualized according to the patient''s diagnosis, clinical status, and current guidelines.', '["Less than 2g sodium/day","1 to 3g sodium/day","2g sodium/day","No added salt"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T14:47:13.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T14:47:13.431Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T10:26:40.397Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362452206-170', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'A 20% intravenous fat emulsion provides approximately how many kilocalories per 100 mL?', '200', 'A 20% intravenous lipid emulsion (ILE) contains approximately 20 g of lipid per 100 mL and is conventionally estimated to provide 2 kcal/mL, or approximately 200 kcal per 100 mL. This is the value commonly used when calculating the energy contribution of IV lipid emulsions in parenteral nutrition. 

A 10% ILE provides approximately 1.1 kcal/mL, while a 20% ILE provides approximately 2 kcal/mL. The distinction is important because the caloric value assigned to IV lipid emulsions is not calculated simply by multiplying grams of lipid by 9 kcal/g, as would commonly be done for dietary fat. 

Commercial IV lipid emulsions contain triglycerides along with emulsifying agents and other components, and their labeled caloric contribution is based on the formulation. For PN calculations, therefore, use the manufacturer/standard clinical kcal/mL value for the specific lipid emulsion.', '["110","200","180","20"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:37:16.128Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:37:16.128Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:37:16.128Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362436457-169', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'An essential fatty acid derived from plant oils such as corn oil:', 'Linoleic acid', 'Linoleic acid (18:2 n-6) is an essential omega-6 polyunsaturated fatty acid abundant in vegetable seed oils (corn, sunflower, safflower) that cannot be synthesized de novo by humans.', '["Linoleic acid","Linolenic acid","Arachidonic acid","Palmitic acid"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T15:38:22.915Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T15:38:22.915Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T15:38:23.082Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362489204-172', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'The following inhibit the absorption of iron EXCEPT:', 'Fructose', 'Iron absorption is influenced by both the chemical form of iron and components of the meal. Nonheme iron, the major form found in plant foods and fortified foods, is particularly affected by dietary factors. 

Coffee and tea can substantially reduce nonheme iron absorption because they contain polyphenolic compounds that bind iron and reduce its bioavailability. Soy products may also reduce nonheme iron absorption because of compounds such as phytate, although the magnitude of the effect depends on the food and overall dietary pattern. 

Fructose, in contrast, does not inhibit iron absorption and may actually enhance nonheme iron absorption under certain conditions. It has been associated with improved iron absorption, although its effect is less prominent than that of vitamin C.', '["Fructose","Soy products (if major dietary protein is from soy)","Coffee","Tea"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:37:36.680Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:37:36.680Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:37:36.680Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362641915-180', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'To increase appetite, which of the following is/are recommended?
I. Relaxed eating
II. Small frequent feedings
III. Eating favorite food', 'All of the above', 'All three strategies can help improve appetite and food intake, particularly in patients experiencing poor appetite. Relaxed eating can make meals more pleasant and reduce anxiety or stress associated with eating. Creating an appealing mealtime environment and avoiding pressure to eat may encourage intake. Small, frequent meals are useful because a large meal may be overwhelming for someone with a poor appetite; smaller portions are often better tolerated and can help increase total daily intake. Offering favorite or preferred foods is another practical strategy because food preference and palatability strongly influence willingness to eat. 

In clinical nutrition, these approaches are commonly incorporated into individualized nutrition care, especially when the immediate goal is to increase energy and nutrient intake. However, favorite foods should still be selected with consideration for the patient''s medical condition and dietary requirements.', '["II only","II and III only","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T13:23:18.765Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T13:23:18.765Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T13:23:18.765Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362719810-185', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'If a person wants to improve his/her fiber intake, which of the following food would be best?', 'Saluyot', 'Saluyot (jute leaves) is the best choice among the options because leafy vegetables such as saluyot provide a relatively high amount of dietary fiber compared with the other vegetables listed. Saluyot is also a good source of several micronutrients and is commonly consumed as a vegetable in the Philippines. Increasing intake of fiber-rich vegetables can contribute to improved bowel function and overall diet quality. 

Radish, cucumber, and tomato also contain dietary fiber, but their fiber contribution per comparable edible portion is generally lower than that of saluyot. For practical nutrition counseling, however, the overall dietary pattern matters: a person should obtain fiber from a variety of vegetables, fruits, whole grains, legumes, nuts, and seeds, rather than relying on one food alone.', '["Radish","Saluyot","Cucumber","Tomato"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T13:22:44.121Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T13:22:44.121Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T13:22:44.121Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362706261-184', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Foot care is an important aspect of diabetes education of patients to avoid:', 'wounds on the feet which may become gangrenous', 'Proper diabetic foot care is essential because diabetes can cause peripheral neuropathy and peripheral arterial disease, both of which increase the risk of foot injury and delayed wound healing. Neuropathy may reduce protective sensation, so a patient may not notice a blister, cut, burn, pressure injury, or poorly fitting footwear. Reduced blood flow can further impair tissue healing and increase the risk of infection. 

Regular foot inspection, appropriate footwear, foot hygiene, and prompt attention to injuries help prevent minor problems from progressing to serious ulcers and, in severe cases, gangrene and amputation.', '["microvascular complications","edema of the lower extremities","numbness of the lower extremities","wounds on the feet which may become gangrenous"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:14:47.403Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:14:47.403Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:14:47.403Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362754910-187', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Elimination diets are used in:', 'Allergy', 'Elimination diets systematically eliminate suspected offending foods/allergens from the diet to observe symptom resolution, serving as a primary diagnostic and therapeutic tool for food allergies and intolerances.', '["Cancer","Allergy","skin disease","gout"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T11:29:41.837Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:29:41.837Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:29:41.837Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362658815-181', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Generally, men have higher BMR/kg than women because:', 'they have more lean tissues/unit weight', 'Men generally possess a greater percentage of lean body mass (fat-free skeletal muscle and organ tissue) per unit of body weight compared to women, which is the primary determinant of higher Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR).', '["they are more active","they have more body fat","they have more lean tissues/unit weight","they are heavier"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:37:21.308Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:37:21.308Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:37:21.308Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362788975-189', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'A morbidly obese patient underwent gastric bypass. What classical side effect can the patient experience as a result of the surgery?', 'Dumping syndrome', 'Dumping syndrome is a classic complication of gastric bypass surgery. Because the stomach''s normal reservoir function is altered and food can pass relatively rapidly into the small intestine, particularly after meals high in simple carbohydrates, patients may experience rapid gastric emptying. This can produce early symptoms such as abdominal cramping, bloating, nausea, diarrhea, dizziness, flushing, and tachycardia shortly after eating. The rapid delivery of carbohydrate can also cause a subsequent exaggerated insulin response. 

Dumping syndrome may also have a late phase, typically occurring 1–3 hours after eating, characterized by symptoms of hypoglycemia such as weakness, sweating, tremors, palpitations, and confusion. Dietary management commonly includes small, frequent meals; limiting rapidly absorbed carbohydrates; emphasizing protein and complex carbohydrates; and separating fluids from meals when appropriate. Although nutritional deficiencies and undernutrition can also occur after bariatric surgery, dumping syndrome is the classical side effect specifically associated with altered gastric anatomy and rapid gastric emptying.', '["Undernutrition","Gastrostomy","Dumping syndrome","GERD"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T14:27:13.503Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T14:27:13.503Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T14:27:13.503Z"'::jsonb)
