a. b. 
c. 
d.', '<7% of total calories, 200 mg cholesterol/day', '', '["<7% of total calories, 200 mg cholesterol/day","150","mitochondria","25 kcal/KDBW"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T06:43:42.810Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:43:42.810Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:43:42.810Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788946590-173', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Tube feedings can be discontinued gradually when: *', 'the client is able to eat foods or drink formula in sufficient amounts', 'Discontinuing a tube feeding (enteral nutrition) is a step-by-step process. Clinicians typically look for the patient to meet at least two-thirds (60% to 66%) of their daily caloric and fluid needs via oral intake (eating solid food or drinking nutrition shakes) before completely pulling or stopping the tube feeding.', '["the client is able to eat foods or drink formula in sufficient amounts","as soon as the medical condition resolves","planning for discharge begins","the client experiences hunger"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T15:39:24.298Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:39:24.298Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:39:24.387Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788532694-145', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The primary purpose of nutrition screening is to: *', 'identify clients who need complete nutrition assessments', '', '["define nutritional status","identify clients who need complete nutrition assessments","determine nutrient needs","provide a basis for nutrition care plan"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-16T02:52:33.066Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:52:33.066Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:52:33.141Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788188142-113', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Which has the highest osmolality? *', 'cola', '', '["sherbet","cola","milk","buko water"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, 'Osmolality measures the concentration of dissolved particles (solutes) per kilogram of fluid. The higher the amount of dissolved simple sugars and electrolytes in a liquid, the higher its osmolality. Standard carbonated colas possess a very high sugar content and high dissolved gas/electrolyte components, leading to a significantly high hypertonic osmolality ranging anywhere from 492 to 811 mOsm/kg.', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-30T07:08:39.340Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-29T07:08:39.340Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-29T07:08:39.416Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788151059-109', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A patient has adapted to starvation because: *', 'urinary N excretion is low', 'During the initial days of fasting, the body breaks down muscle tissue (protein catabolism) to extract amino acids for gluconeogenesis. This results in a high amount of nitrogen being excreted in the urine as urea.

As starvation becomes prolonged, the body undergoes a "protein-sparing" metabolic adaptation. The brain and other vital organs switch their primary fuel source from glucose to ketone bodies derived from fat stores.

Because the body no longer needs to aggressively break down muscle protein to make glucose, urinary nitrogen (N) excretion drops significantly, serving as the classic clinical marker that a patient has successfully adapted to starvation.', '["urinary N excretion is low","his weight loss is associated with sepsis","he has low blood ketones","temperature is 103F rectally"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-16T01:29:57.563Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T01:29:57.563Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T01:29:57.639Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788160293-110', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A non-insulin dependent diabetic who is hypertensive, obese, and non-ambulatory will benefit from which of the following recommendations? *', 'low calorie, no sugar, no fat, no salt', '', '["very low-calorie diet, low salt","very low fat, no salt, high fiber","total low calorie, low Na, high fiber","low calorie, no sugar, no fat, no salt"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-03T07:00:48.495Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T07:00:48.495Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T07:00:48.495Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788179592-112', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Which is not considered a complementary protein meal? *', 'bread with jam', 'The goal of complementary proteins is to pair foods containing incomplete proteins so that they compensate for each other''s lacking essential amino acids, ultimately providing a complete amino acid profile.

While bread (a grain) contains a small amount of incomplete protein, jam contains virtually zero protein (it is almost entirely simple sugars and carbohydrates). Because jam has no amino acids to contribute, it cannot complement the protein in the bread.', '["rice & monggo","macaroni with cheese","bread with jam","pizza with salami"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:06:37.699Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:06:37.699Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:06:38.016Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788196391-114', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Cirrhotic liver disease will promote a fluid and electrolyte imbalance because: *', 'Na is retained and holds fluid', '', '["excess fluids are lost in the bile","Na is retained and holds fluid","K is excreted in the urine","blood ammonia levels become elevated"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T05:09:59.638Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T05:09:59.638Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T05:09:59.638Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788207125-115', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Amino acid supplements taken in excess can lead to: *', 'loss of urinary Ca', 'When amino acid supplements or proteins are consumed in large excess, the body metabolizes them, breaking down sulfur-containing amino acids (like cysteine and methionine). This creates an acidic load (sulfate ions and hydrogen ions) in the bloodstream.

To neutralize and excrete this mild metabolic acidosis, the kidneys alter how they handle minerals. The higher acid load and increased glomerular filtration rate (GFR) directly impair the renal tubules from reabsorbing calcium, causing the body to spill out and lose high levels of calcium in the urine (hypercalciuria).', '["K retention","loss of urinary Ca","loss of Fe","vitamin deficiencies"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T15:53:27.653Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:53:27.653Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:53:27.749Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788228571-117', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A patient who was morbidly obese underwent stomach resection. What possible side effect can she experience as a result of the surgery? *', 'vitamin B12 malabsorption', '', '["difficulty in chewing","vitamin B12 malabsorption","diabetes mellitus","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T05:03:06.619Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T05:03:06.619Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T05:03:06.619Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788241723-118', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A 15-year old insulin dependent diabetic girl is admitted in the hospital due to hyperglycemia. An appropriate nutrition education plan should begin with: *', 'a review of the relationship between blood glucose levels and intake of simple sugars', 'When a pediatric patient is acutely admitted for a crisis like hyperglycemia, nutrition education must begin with simple, high-yield, and immediate cause-and-effect relationships. Reviewing how simple sugars directly spike blood glucose levels provides the teen with immediate, understandable insight into her current clinical crisis.', '["a printed material that discusses the pathophysiology of diabetes","a detailed explanation of the exchange list and how to use it in meal planning","a review of the relationship between blood glucose levels and intake of simple sugars","a quiz to evaluate the patient’s knowledge of diet and diabetes"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:54:13.044Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:54:13.044Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:54:13.219Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788250154-119', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Foods which may be included in a full liquid diet: *', 'gelatin', '', '["gelatin","soft cooked egg","all of the above","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T05:21:45.944Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T05:21:45.944Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T05:21:45.944Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788267005-121', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'How much carbohydrates would be contained in a 2000 ml tube feeding formula with a calorie density of 1.5 kcal/ml, 63% of which are from carbohydrates? *', '470g', 'First, calculate the total calories in the formula: 2000 ml multiplied by 1.5 kcal/ml equals 3000 total kcal.

Next, find the calories coming from carbohydrates: 3000 total kcal multiplied by 63 percent (0.63) equals 1890 kcal from carbohydrates.

Finally, convert the carbohydrate calories into grams (since 1 gram of carbohydrate provides 4 kcal): 1890 kcal divided by 4 kcal per gram equals 472.5 grams.', '["315g","470g","483g","396g"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-16T02:44:49.799Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:44:49.799Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:44:49.873Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788279690-122', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A gradual transition from an IV feeding to an oral diet is primarily designed to: *', 'ensure that nutrient needs will continue to be met', 'The primary goal of a gradual transition (often called a "transitional feed") from intravenous (IV) nutrition to an oral diet is to ensure nutritional adequacy and patient safety. When a patient has been on IV nutrition, their gastrointestinal tract may have reduced capacity to absorb nutrients right away. 

By slowly tapering off the IV fluids while introducing oral foods, clinicians can track oral intake, verify that the gut is tolerating and absorbing the food properly, and ensure that the patient does not experience nutritional deficits during the switch.', '["improve the appetite","prevent hypoglycemia","ensure that nutrient needs will continue to be met","prevent apprehension about eating"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-16T02:46:29.133Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:46:29.133Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:46:29.207Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788297587-124', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'All of the following can be delivered intravenously except: *', 'hydrolyzed enteral formulas', 'Enteral formulas—including hydrolyzed or elemental formulas—are strictly designed to be processed through the gastrointestinal tract via oral ingestion or a feeding tube (such as an NG tube, G-tube, or J-tube).

Even though these formulas are "pre-digested" into smaller parts for patients with impaired gut absorption, they contain intact particles, fiber residues, and non-sterile compounds that are highly dangerous if introduced directly into the bloodstream. Delivering an enteral formula intravenously can cause catastrophic blood clots, septic shock, or fatal embolisms.', '["hydrolyzed enteral formulas","carbohydrate","amino acids","dextrose"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T14:01:32.456Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:01:32.456Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:01:32.532Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788305903-125', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A gluten-free diet excludes: *', 'wheat, barley, rye, and oats', 'A strict gluten-free diet permanently eliminates grains containing the protein gluten (and its toxic prolamin fractions: gliadin in wheat, secalin in rye, and hordein in barley). These cause severe small intestinal mucosal damage in individuals with celiac disease or gluten intolerance.

In classic nursing, nutrition, and board exam question banks (such as the Philippine Food Exchange List frameworks), oats are historically grouped alongside wheat, barley, and rye as prohibited foods. This is due to a high risk of cross-contamination during harvesting and processing, as well as the presence of avenin (a protein in oats similar to gluten that some sensitive individuals react to).', '["wheat, corn, and oats","barely, soybeans, oats, and corn","wheat, barley, rye, and oats","wheat, barley, rice, and oats"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T11:18:13.204Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T11:18:13.204Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T11:18:13.518Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788322617-126', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A soft diet for a patient with indigestion would include the following except: *', 'use foods that are chopped or pureed', 'A standard gastrointestinal soft diet (often called a bland or low-residue diet) is modified in chemical composition and digestibility rather than mechanical texture. It aims to ease abdominal distress, bloating, and irritation caused by indigestion (dyspepsia).

Mechanically altering foods by chopping, grinding, or pureeing is a requirement of a Mechanical Soft Diet or Pureed Diet, which is prescribed for patients with chewing or swallowing difficulties (such as poor dentition, dysphagia, or mouth surgery). A patient suffering from basic indigestion can chew normally; therefore, chopping or pureeing food is completely unnecessary.', '["limit highly seasoned foods","limit foods that produce gas","use foods that are chopped or pureed","limit high-fiber foods"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:38:51.312Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:38:51.312Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:38:51.491Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788870543-165', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Cholesterol level in food considered safe for daily consumption. *', '300mg', 'For many years, traditional clinical nutrition guidelines and board manuals (such as the American Heart Association and national dietary guidelines) established a strict upper limit of 300 mg of dietary cholesterol per day for healthy adults to reduce the risk of cardiovascular disease.

While modern guidelines have shifted to focusing more broadly on limiting saturated fats, the classic dietary limit taught and tested in academic question banks for a "safe daily consumption level" remains 300 mg.', '["300mg","450mg","400mg","475mg"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-14T02:53:18.286Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:53:18.286Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:53:18.360Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788170544-111', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A 45-year-old male with cancer of the upper GI tract is admitted for operation. Due to his 15% weight loss, your first recommendation would be to: *', 'feed him at least 10 days preoperatively', 'A 15% unintentional weight loss in a patient with upper GI tract cancer signifies severe malnutrition. Proceeding directly to major abdominal surgery in a severely malnourished patient drastically increases the risk of postoperative complications, including poor wound healing, anastomotic leaks, infections, and death.

Clinical guidelines dictate that patients with severe nutritional risk should receive 7 to 14 days of preoperative nutritional support (ideally via the enteral route, or parenteral if the gut is obstructed) to improve metabolic status and surgical outcomes, even if it means deliberately delaying the operation.', '["rush him to the OR","initiate TPN prior to surgery","advise surgeon to plan feeding jejunostomy at operation","feed him at least 10 days preoperatively"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T13:58:43.591Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T13:58:43.591Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T13:58:43.591Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788332085-127', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Standard liquid diets provide all of the following except: *', 'a diet that is nutritionally complete', '', '["a test of a person’s ability to tolerate oral foods","a diet that is nutritionally complete","a diet that can be eaten with minimal effort","a source of fluid and electrolytes"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-16T00:59:25.499Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T00:59:25.499Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T00:59:25.560Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788344702-128', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Bland diets eliminate all of the following foods except: *', 'foods that delay gastric emptying', 'The core purpose of a bland diet is to reduce chemical and mechanical irritation to the gastrointestinal tract, decrease stomach acid secretion, and give the digestive system a rest.

Foods that delay gastric emptying (such as high-fat foods) are actually eliminated from a bland diet. High-fat items sit in the stomach longer, which stimulates prolonged gastric acid production and increases the risk of acid reflux. Therefore, a bland diet specifically incorporates easily digestible, low-fat carbohydrates and lean proteins that clear the stomach quickly rather than delaying emptying.', '["foods that cause indigestion","foods that irritate the lining of the esophagus and stomach","foods that significantly increase gastric acid production","foods that delay gastric emptying"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:50:04.597Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:50:04.597Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:50:04.597Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788352733-129', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Sudden tissue death caused by blockages of vessels that feed the heart muscle: *', 'heart attack', 'The literal medical definition of myocardial infarction is tissue death (infarction) of the heart muscle (myocardio) caused by a lack of oxygen (ischemia). This occurs abruptly when a coronary vessel—one of the arteries feeding oxygen-rich blood to the heart itself—becomes completely blocked by a blood clot or ruptured plaque.

• Stroke: This is sudden tissue damage or death, but it occurs in the brain, caused by a blockage or rupture of blood vessels feeding brain tissue.
• Transient Ischemic Attack (TIA): Often called a "mini-stroke," this involves a temporary blockage of blood vessels feeding the brain. Because the clot breaks apart quickly, it does not cause permanent tissue death.', '["stroke","transient ischemic attack","heart attack","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:53:01.815Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:53:01.815Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:53:01.815Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788360417-130', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Using the NPC method, compute for the CHO and fat of a diet prescription with 2000kcal, P55g. *', 'C265g, F80g', 'Step-by-Step Breakdown using the Non-Protein Calorie (NPC) Method

To calculate the carbohydrate (CHO) and fat allocation for this diet prescription, we follow the standard percentage distribution of non-protein calories (using a standard 60% CHO and 40% Fat split for the remaining energy):

1. Calculate Protein Calories:
	• Protein allowance = 55g
	• Energy value of protein = 4 kcal/g
	• Protein Calories: 55g * 4 kcal = 220 kcal

2. Calculate Total Non-Protein Calories (NPC):
	• Total Energy Requirement (TER) = 2000 kcal
	• NPC = Total Calories - Protein Calories
	• NPC = 2000 kcal - 220 kcal = 1780 kcal

3. Compute Carbohydrate (CHO) Grams (60% of NPC):
	• CHO Calories = 1780 kcal * 0.60 = 1068 kcal
	• Energy value of carbohydrates = 4 kcal/g
	• CHO Grams = 1068 kcal / 4 = 267g
	• Rounding off to the nearest 5 grams = 265g

4. Compute Fat Grams (40% of NPC):
	• Fat Calories = 1780 kcal * 0.40 = 712 kcal
	• Energy value of fat = 9 kcal/g
	• Fat Grams = 712 kcal / 9 = 79.1g
	• Rounding off to the nearest 5 grams = 80g', '["C265g, F80g","C250g, F110g","C300g, F90g","C350g, F65g"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T11:59:28.891Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T11:59:28.891Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T11:59:29.202Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788382553-132', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Dr. Cruz ordered an additional 500mg Na in the diet of Maria Pecson. How much salt should be added to her food? *', '1250mg', 'Table salt (sodium chloride) is composed of approximately 40% sodium and 60% chloride.

To find the amount of salt needed to provide a specific amount of pure sodium, you divide the milligram amount of sodium by 0.40:

500 mg of sodium divided by 0.40 = 1250 mg of salt.', '["1500mg","500mg","1000mg","1250mg"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-16T02:43:07.498Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:43:07.498Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:43:07.572Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788394064-133', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Characteristics of Type 1 diabetes: *', 'It is associated with ketosis and acidosis.', '• In Type 1 diabetes, the body''s immune system destroys pancreatic beta cells, leaving the body with an absolute lack of insulin.
• Because the body completely lacks the insulin required to pull glucose into cells for fuel, cells are starved for energy. The body compensates by rapidly breaking down fats into free fatty acids. A byproduct of this accelerated fat metabolism is the accumulation of acidic ketone bodies in the blood, which drops the blood''s pH and leads to diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA).', '["It is associated with ketosis and acidosis.","It is the predominant form of diabetes.","It frequently goes undiagnosed.","Insulin secretion is ineffective in preventing hyperglycemia."]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T07:03:28.114Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T07:03:28.114Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T07:03:28.114Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788404230-134', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A simple IV solution is most appropriate for: *', 'people who are well-nourished and are expected to eat in a few days', 'A simple IV solution (such as standard Normal Saline or D5W) contains only water, basic electrolytes, and sometimes a minimal amount of dextrose for basic cellular energy. It provides zero protein, zero fat, and very few calories.

Because it lacks any real nutritional substance, a simple IV is only meant for short-term hydration and electrolyte maintenance in patients who already have good nutritional reserves and will return to eating solid food very quickly.

Meanwhile, patients who are malnourished, have high metabolic nutrient demands, or cannot eat for prolonged periods require complex Parenteral Nutrition (TPN/PPN). TPN is an advanced, customized chemical cocktail delivering concentrated amino acids, lipids, vitamins, and minerals directly into the bloodstream to keep a starving patient alive.', '["people who are well-nourished and are expected to eat in a few days","people who have high nutrient needs","people who cannot eat for long periods of time","people who are malnourished"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '• Simple intravenous solutions (such as 0.9% Normal Saline or D5W) are designed primarily for short-term fluid maintenance, rehydration, and electrolyte balance.
• Nutritional Limitations: Simple IV crystalloid solutions do not provide adequate calories, amino acids, or complex nutrients. A person can safely rely on them for only a few days if they enter the hospital well-nourished, as the body can temporarily lean on its own nutritional reserves until oral intake resumes.', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T14:02:34.443Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:02:34.443Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:02:34.516Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788421998-136', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The best food to give when a patient has a blood sugar of 60mg. *', '1 tbsp sugar', 'A blood sugar level of 60 mg/dL indicates hypoglycemia (low blood sugar), which is defined as any value under 70 mg/dL. To properly treat this, clinical guidelines recommend the "Rule of 15": consume 15 grams of fast-acting, simple carbohydrates, wait 15 minutes, and recheck the blood sugar.

• 1/3 cup fruit juice: A 1/3 cup serving is only about 10 grams, which under-treats an adult.
• 5 glucose tablets: Standard glucose tablets usually contain 4 grams of carbohydrates each. Taking 5 tablets (20 grams) over-treats a mild low, which can cause a sharp rebound spike.
• 1 tsp honey: One teaspoon is only 5 grams.', '["1 tbsp sugar","1 tsp honey","1/3 cup fruit juice","5 glucose tablets"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-14T01:18:53.685Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T01:18:53.685Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T01:18:53.759Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788434881-137', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A renal diet intentionally restricts all of the following except: *', 'calcium', '', '["calcium","phosphorus","fluid","protein"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-16T01:28:55.992Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T01:28:55.992Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T01:28:55.992Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788442062-138', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'An adult with a usual body weight of 56kg will have fluid needs of about: *', '2000ml', 'Step-by-Step Calculation:

To calculate standard adult daily fluid maintenance needs, clinicians use the standard weight-based guideline of 35 ml per kg of body weight for healthy adults:

1. Multiply the individual''s usual body weight by the fluid factor:
56 kg x 35 ml/kg = 1960 ml

2. Match to the closest multiple choice selection:
1960 ml rounds directly up to 2000 ml.', '["2000ml","1500ml","2500ml","3000ml"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:53:27.675Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:53:27.675Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:53:27.675Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788481962-141', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Refer to the following information: A 25-year old female office clerk consulted at an out-patient clinic for dizziness and headache. She is 5’4” and weighs 70 kg. Her BP is 150/80 mmHg. This is her first consultation.
What is her DBW? *', '56.3 kg', '', '["56.3 kg","62.6 kg","163 lbs.","138 lbs."]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T06:19:51.687Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:19:51.687Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:19:51.687Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788499561-142', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Refer to the following information: A 25-year old female office clerk consulted at an out-patient clinic for dizziness and headache. She is 5’4” and weighs 70 kg. Her BP is 150/80 mmHg. This is her first consultation.
What is her TER per day? *', '1700 kcal', '', '["1400 kcal","1550 kcal","1700 kcal","2100 kcal"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T06:17:15.035Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:17:15.035Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:17:15.035Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788515243-143', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Refer to the following information: A 25-year old female office clerk consulted at an out-patient clinic for dizziness and headache. She is 5’4” and weighs 70 kg. Her BP is 150/80 mmHg. This is her first consultation.
Which of the following foods would you recommend? *', 'rice, pan de sal, camote tops, fish', 'The patient’s blood pressure is 150/80 mmHg, which classifies as Stage 2 Hypertension (specifically elevated systolic pressure). A primary nutritional intervention for managing high blood pressure is a low-sodium, heart-healthy diet rich in potassium and fiber (similar to the DASH diet approach).
	• Fresh Fish: Provides a lean, high-quality source of protein that is naturally low in sodium and rich in heart-healthy omega-3 fatty acids.
	• Camote Tops (Sweet Potato Leaves): A local powerhouse vegetable that is highly concentrated in potassium. Potassium actively works to lower blood pressure by helping the kidneys excrete excess sodium and relaxing blood vessel walls.
	• Rice & Pan de Sal: Serve as staple carbohydrates. While traditional pan de sal contains a small amount of salt, the meal as a whole is overwhelmingly comprised of fresh, unprocessed items.

The remaining options contain heavily processed, canned, or cured foods (ham, corned beef, pork and beans) that are loaded with hidden sodium, which can acutely worsen hypertension by causing fluid retention and increasing blood vessel pressure:', '["pan de sal, rice, cottage cheese, corned beef, ampalaya","rice, pan de sal, camote tops, fish","pork and beans, carrots, banana, rice","milk, ham, eggs, rice"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T11:11:10.701Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T11:11:10.701Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T11:11:11.007Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758789013354-179', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Nutrition problems associated with fat malabsorption syndromes include the following except: *', 'oxalate kidney stones', '', '["essential fatty acid deficiencies","severe weight loss and PEM","bone diseases","oxalate kidney stones"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-03T06:34:26.527Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:34:26.527Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:34:26.527Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788543791-146', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The nutritionist-dietitian must closely monitor the client with nephrotic syndrome for deficiencies of which vitamin? *', 'D', 'Nephrotic syndrome is characterized by heavy proteinuria (>3.5 g/day), which leads to the urinary excretion of low-molecular-weight plasma proteins, including the 58-kDa vitamin D-binding protein (DBP). Because the majority of circulating 25-hydroxyvitamin D [25(OH)D] and 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D [1,25(OH)2D] are bound to DBP, their continuous loss in the urine depletes total body stores. Furthermore, impaired renal parenchyma and chronic inflammation decrease the activity of 1alpha-hydroxylase, reducing the conversion of 25(OH)D to its active hormonal form, 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D, thereby disrupting calcium homeostasis and secondary bone mineralization.', '["A","B₆","D","K"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T14:09:27.907Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:09:27.907Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:09:27.980Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788551991-147', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Aside from table salt, a low sodium diet limits the use of: *', 'processed foods', 'Aside from table salt, processed and ultra-processed foods are the single largest source of sodium in the modern diet. Manufacturers add massive amounts of sodium to packaged meals, canned soups, cured meats, and snacks for preservation and flavor enhancement. A low-sodium diet strictly mandates limiting these items.', '["fried foods","processed foods","dialyzed milk","all of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T13:37:27.436Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T13:37:27.436Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T13:37:27.512Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788559841-148', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A ketogenic diet is characterized as: *', 'high fat, low CHO', 'A classic ketogenic diet is structured to shift the body''s primary metabolic fuel source from glucose to fat. To induce this state of ketosis, the diet must dramatically restrict carbohydrates (CHO) and replace those calories with dietary fat.

The standard ketogenic diet typically derives approximately 70% to 80% of daily calories from fat, 15% to 20% from protein, and only 5% to 10% from carbohydrates.', '["high fat, low CHO","low fat, high CHO","high protein, low CHO","high protein, low fat"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T15:31:26.182Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:31:26.182Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:31:26.272Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788568541-149', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Characterized by low grade fever, body tissue wasting, and dry cough. *', 'tuberculosis', 'This infectious disease is caused by the bacterium Mycobacterium tuberculosis. The classic symptoms of pulmonary TB include a lingering low-grade fever (often spiking in the afternoon or evening), dry cough (which may start dry before becoming productive or producing blood), and severe body tissue wasting (traditionally referred to as "consumption" due to profound, unintended weight loss and muscle atrophy).

	• COPD: Characterized primarily by a chronic productive cough (with mucus), wheezing, and progressive shortness of breath, rather than a presenting low-grade fever.
	• AIDS: While it causes wasting and fever, it is a complex systemic syndrome of immune failure that manifests through a wide variety of opportunistic infections rather than a localized dry cough complex.
	• Cholecystitis: This is an acute or chronic inflammation of the gallbladder that presents with sharp upper abdominal pain, nausea, and potentially a high fever, rather than chronic respiratory symptoms or progressive body wasting.', '["COPD","AIDS","cholecystitis","tuberculosis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T13:39:31.454Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T13:39:31.454Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T13:39:31.526Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788596673-152', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Desensitization is a treatment for: *', 'allergy', '', '["ulcerative colitis","psoriasis","acne","allergy"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T04:56:25.160Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T04:56:25.160Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T04:56:25.160Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788606289-153', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'All measures are allowed to increase the energy intake of a renal patient except: *', 'serving fruit cocktail with heavy syrup', '', '["adding unsalted margarine","using additional sugar in foods","serving fruit cocktail with heavy syrup","preparing foods with oil"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-30T07:02:16.090Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-29T07:02:16.090Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-29T07:02:16.090Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788616456-154', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Severely malnourished children must be rehabilitated at the shortest time possible. Choose the diet for a malnourished child with diarrhea who has just been admitted in the hospital. *', 'regular foods plus a liquid preparation', 'In severe acute malnutrition (SAM) with diarrhea, mucosal atrophy, brush border enzyme depletion (lactase, sucrase), and impaired sodium-glucose cotransporter (SGLT-1) function demand cautious, low-osmolarity oral rehydration and specialized starter formulas (F-75 transitioning rapidly to F-100) combined with soft, regular foods to restore enterocyte integrity, provide adequate macronutrients for catch-up growth, and prevent villous atrophy without worsening osmotic diarrhea.', '["liquid diet high in calories and protein","BRAT","regular foods plus a liquid preparation","regular diet consisting of foods liked and disliked"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:51:04.544Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:51:04.544Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:51:04.544Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788624621-155', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Diet for calcium oxalate urinary calculi. *', 'alkaline ash diet', 'Calcium oxalate stones are the most common type of kidney stones. They tend to form more easily in acidic urine. An alkaline ash diet increases the pH of the urine (making it less acidic), which helps keep calcium oxalate and uric acid crystals dissolved so they can be passed safely. This diet focuses on eating foods like vegetables, fruits, and legumes, while reducing acid-forming foods like meats, cheeses, and grains.', '["acid ash diet","alkaline ash diet","neutral diet","low calcium diet"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T14:11:23.963Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":1.9,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:11:23.963Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:11:24.045Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758789088184-186', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Diet recommendations for clients suffering from severe stress: *', 'energy-modified, high protein diet', '', '["energy-modified, high protein diet","high calories, low protein","high carbohydrate, high fat","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-03T06:44:43.075Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:44:43.075Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:44:43.075Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788632653-156', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'It is given when nutrient requirements are greatly increased and the GIT is not functional. *', 'TPN', 'When a patient''s gastrointestinal tract (GIT) is non-functional, blocked, or requires complete clinical rest, enteral options like a gastrostomy or a nasogastric tube (NGT) cannot be used safely. Bypassing the gut entirely becomes mandatory.

While basic "intravenous feeding" usually refers to peripheral lines giving short-term hydration or dilute nutrients (like PPN), TPN is a highly concentrated, hyperosmolar formula packed with complete macronutrients, vitamins, and minerals. Because it is designed to meet massive metabolic demands (such as in severe burns, sepsis, or major trauma), it must be safely administered through a large central vein.', '["NGT","intravenous feeding","gastrostomy","TPN"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T15:59:06.128Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:59:06.128Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:59:06.217Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788649670-158', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The fat content of a 2,000 kcal diet providing 30% of calories from fat. *', '67g', 'Step-by-Step Calculation:

1. Find total calories from fat: Multiply the total calories by the fat percentage.
2,000 kcal x 0.30 = 600 kcal from fat

2. Convert calories to grams: Fat provides 9 calories per gram (9 kcal/g). Divide the fat calories by 9.
600 kcal / 9 kcal/g = 66.67g

When rounded to the nearest whole number, 66.67g gives you 67g.', '["60g","67g","77g","80g"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T15:19:44.862Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:19:44.862Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:19:44.952Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788658988-159', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Epidemiologic studies have shown that colon cancer is more frequent in populations consuming: *', 'a highly refined, low fiber diet', '', '["a highly refined, low fiber diet","a high protein diet","a high cholesterol diet","a low calcium diet"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T06:46:47.901Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:46:47.901Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:46:47.901Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788797095-162', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Refer to the following information. Based on the FEL, the diet of a burn patient contains the following meal pattern for lunch: 90g fried chicken; 160g rice; 45g sauteed squash; 250g fresh milk; and 103g ripe mango.
How much is the fat content of the meal? *', '33 g', 'According to the Philippine Food Exchange List (FEL), each food item is converted into exchange units to calculate its specific macronutrient content. Here is the breakdown for each item in the lunch pattern:

1. 160g Rice (2 Rice Exchanges):
• 1 exchange of cooked rice = 80g
• 160g = 2 exchanges
• Fat content = 0g

2. 250g Fresh Milk (1 Whole Milk Exchange):
• 1 exchange of whole milk = 250g
• Fat content = 10g

3. 103g Ripe Mango (1 Fruit Exchange):
• 1 exchange of ripe mango = 1 slice/portion (approx. 100g)
• Fat content = 0g

4. 45g Sauteed Squash (1 Vegetable Exchange + 1 Fat Exchange):
• 1 exchange of cooked squash = 45g (0g fat)
• Since it is sauteed, the FEL rules require adding 1 fat exchange for the cooking oil.
• 1 fat exchange = 5g

5. 90g Fried Chicken (3 Medium-Fat Meat Exchanges):
• 1 exchange of cooked meat = 30g
• 90g = 3 exchanges
• Chicken with skin or fried chicken is classified under Medium-Fat Meat, which contains 6g of fat per exchange.
• 3 exchanges x 6g = 18g

Final Fat Summation:

• Rice: 0g
• Fresh Milk: 10g
• Ripe Mango: 0g
• Sauteed Squash (Oil): 5g
• Fried Chicken: 18g
• Total Fat Content: 10g + 5g + 18g = 33g', '["27 g","30 g","33 g","35 g"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T15:35:50.422Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:35:50.422Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:35:50.511Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788827111-163', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Refer to the following information. Based on the FEL, the diet of a burn patient contains the following meal pattern for lunch: 90g fried chicken; 160g rice; 45g sauteed squash; 250g fresh milk; and 103g ripe mango.
How many kilocalories does the meal contain? *', '730 kcal', 'Step-by-Step Breakdown using the Food Exchange List (FEL)

To find the total energy value of this meal pattern, we break down each food item into its exact base exchanges and adjust for the preparation methods:

1. 160g Rice (Cooked Rice):
	• Exchange value: 1 exchange of cooked rice is equal to 80g (providing 100 kcal).
	• 160g / 80g = 2 Rice Exchanges.
	• Energy Contribution: 2 * 100 kcal = 200 kcal.

2. 250g Fresh Milk (Whole Milk):
	• Exchange value: 1 glass or 1 cup of whole fresh milk is equal to 250g (providing 170 kcal).
	• 250g / 250g = 1 Whole Milk Exchange.
	• Energy Contribution: 1 * 170 kcal = 170 kcal.

3. 103g Ripe Mango:
	• Exchange value: 1 exchange of ripe mango is equal to 103g gross weight (providing 40 kcal).
	• 103g / 103g = 1 Fruit Exchange.
	• Energy Contribution: 1 * 40 kcal = 40 kcal.

4. 45g Sauteed Squash (Vegetable B + Sauteing Fat):
	• Exchange value: 1 cooked vegetable exchange is equal to 1/2 cup or 45g (providing 16 kcal).
	• Preparation Modifier: Because the squash is sauteed, it adds 1 Fat Exchange (providing 45 kcal) for the cooking oil.
	• Energy Contribution: 16 kcal + 45 kcal = 61 kcal.

5. 90g Fried Chicken (Low-Fat Meat + Frying Fats):
	• Exchange value: Chicken is classified as a Low-Fat Meat, where 1 exchange equals 30g cooked meat (providing 41 kcal). Therefore, 90g / 30g = 3 Low-Fat Meat Exchanges.
	• Preparation Modifier: Because it is fried, each of the 3 meat exchanges absorbs approximately 1 Fat Exchange (3 * 45 kcal = 135 kcal).
	• Energy Contribution: (3 * 41 kcal) + 135 kcal = 258 kcal.

Total Energy Summation
Total Calorie Count = 200 kcal + 170 kcal + 40 kcal + 61 kcal + 258 kcal = 729 kcal

Rounding off to the nearest multiple of five as standard practice in FEL board examination questions, 729 kcal yields exactly 730 kcal.', '["650 kcal","700 kcal","730 kcal","775 kcal"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T11:17:02.909Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T11:17:02.909Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T11:17:03.223Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788878827-166', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Diet management for a person with esophageal reflux. *', 'low fat, low cholesterol', 'Dietary fat stimulates the release of cholecystokinin (CCK) from the duodenal mucosa. CCK induces relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES), increasing transient LES relaxations (TLESRs) and facilitating the retrograde flow of acidic gastric contents into the esophagus. Furthermore, high-fat meals delay gastric emptying, prolonging the period of high gastric volume and pressure that promotes reflux episodes. Restricting dietary fat directly attenuates postprandial CCK surges, preserves basal LES tone, and accelerates gastric transit.', '["liquid diet","high calorie, high protein","regular diet with small frequent meals","low fat, low cholesterol"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:50:51.102Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:50:51.102Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:50:51.102Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788886959-167', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A specific indication of enteral nutrition: *', 'upper gastrointestinal bleed', '', '["anorexia","inadequate intake","upper gastrointestinal bleed","intractable diarrhea"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T06:53:42.565Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:53:42.565Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:53:42.565Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758789097450-187', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A type of COPD in which the lungs lose their elasticity and the client has difficulty breathing. *', 'emphysema', 'Emphysema is a major form of Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD) characterized by the permanent destruction of the alveolar walls and pulmonary capillaries. This architectural destruction leads to a severe loss of lung elasticity. Without elastic recoil, the small airways collapse during exhalation, trapping air inside the lungs (hyperinflation) and making it deeply difficult for the patient to breathe.

• Bronchitis: Chronic bronchitis is characterized by chronic inflammation, swelling, and narrowing of the lining of the bronchial airways. Its primary presentation is a persistent, productive cough driven by severe mucus hypersecretion rather than a structural loss of lung tissue elasticity.', '["emphysema","bronchitis","both a & b","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:50:10.757Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:50:10.757Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:50:10.757Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788896092-168', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Malnutrition due to esophageal tumor is primarily related to: *', 'inadequate intake', 'The defining hallmark of an esophageal tumor is dysphagia (progressive difficulty swallowing) caused by the physical growth of the tumor narrowing the lumen of the esophagus.

While cancer cachexia can induce a catabolic state or alter systemic metabolism over time, the immediate and primary driver of severe protein-calorie malnutrition in these patients at the time of diagnosis is the simple, mechanical inability to physically swallow and consume enough food. This results in massive, structural inadequate food and fluid intake.', '["decreased absorption","inadequate intake","catabolic state","increased energy needs"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T14:10:12.680Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:10:12.680Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:10:12.754Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788906159-169', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Patients taking medicines with MAO inhibitors should be advised this diet: *', 'tyramine-restricted', 'Monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAOIs) are a class of medications used primarily as antidepressants. They work by blocking the enzyme monoamine oxidase, which normally breaks down neurotransmitters as well as tyramine (an amino acid byproduct found in aged and fermented foods).

If a patient taking an MAOI consumes high-tyramine foods, the excess tyramine cannot be broken down. This causes a massive release of norepinephrine, leading to severe vasoconstriction and a dangerous blood pressure spike known as a hypertensive crisis. Therefore, these patients must strictly follow a tyramine-restricted diet.

Aged cheeses (like cheddar, blue, or swiss), cured or smoked meats (like salami and ham), soy sauce, tofu, fermented items (like sauerkraut), tap beer, and red wine.', '["protein-restricted","fat-restricted","tyramine-restricted","phenylalanine-restricted"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:06:20.710Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:06:20.710Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:06:21.022Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788927757-171', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Exogenous sources of cholesterol include: *', 'dairy and glandular only', '• Exogenous cholesterol refers to cholesterol that comes from outside the body through the food we eat.
• The Golden Rule of Cholesterol: Cholesterol is only found in animal-based foods. Plants do not synthesize cholesterol.', '["dairy products","glandular organs","nuts","dairy and glandular only"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-03T06:56:47.109Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:56:47.109Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:56:47.109Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788967688-174', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A gradual transition from an IV feeding to an oral diet is primarily designed to?: *', 'ensure that nutrient needs will continue to be met', 'Transitional feeding is carefully structured to manage a patient''s progression from intravenous (parenteral) nutrition to eating by mouth. The foremost priority is ensuring that the patient doesn''t suffer from a drop in required calories, protein, fluids, or micronutrients while they gradually re-learn to chew, swallow, or digest regular food.', '["improve the appetite","prevent hypoglycemia","ensure that nutrient needs will continue to be met","prevent apprehension about eating"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-03T06:17:24.375Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:17:24.375Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:17:24.375Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788975988-175', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The intravenous lipid emulsion provided two or three times a week in TPN formulas serve primarily as a source of: *', 'essential fatty acid', '', '["concentrated energy","essential fatty acid","fat-soluble vitamins","vitamin C"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-14T01:03:16.204Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T01:03:16.204Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T01:03:16.280Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788996155-177', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Clients undergoing colostomies and ileostomies may temporarily receive a low-fiber, bland diet to: *', 'help the stoma heal', 'After colostomy or ileostomy surgery, the bowel and the newly formed stoma are swollen, inflamed, and healing from surgical trauma. A low-fiber, bland diet minimizes irritation to the intestinal lining, reducing the workload on the gut so it can heal efficiently.

High-fiber foods leave a large amount of indigestible residue (bulk) in the digestive tract. Because the internal lumen of a new stoma is narrowed due to post-operative swelling, a high-fiber intake poses a strict risk of causing a painful mechanical bowel blockage.', '["increase intestinal motility","help the stoma heal","prevent malabsorption","prevent mineral deficiencies"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T15:29:56.909Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:29:56.909Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:29:56.997Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758789004420-178', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Diet therapy for chronic pancreatitis may include all of the following except: *', 'a fat-restricted diet', 'International guidelines (such as ESPEN clinical guidelines) no longer recommend routine, strict dietary fat restriction for chronic pancreatitis. Because these patients suffer from severe malabsorption and weight loss, restricting fat overly constricts their caloric intake and worsens malnutrition. Instead, a normal, balanced physiological diet is prescribed in tandem with proper enzyme dosing. Fat restriction is only used as a temporary, short-term measure if painful fatty stools (steatorrhea) cannot be managed by medications.', '["a fat-restricted diet","enzyme replacements","small meals","a diet to control blood glucose"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T07:28:50.407Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T07:28:50.407Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T07:28:50.407Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758789021870-180', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The following may affect the nutrient needs of people with inflammatory bowel disease except: *', 'dumping syndrome', 'Dumping syndrome is a complication that occurs after gastric surgeries (like a partial or total gastrectomy) where food moves too rapidly from the stomach into the small intestine. It does not directly result from or impact the nutrient needs of Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD).

	• Medications: Common IBD drugs like corticosteroids lower calcium and vitamin D absorption, while others like sulfasalazine impair folate absorption, altering daily nutrient requirements.
	• Steatorrhea: Excessive fat in the stool (common in Crohn''s disease due to bile acid malabsorption) causes a severe loss of fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K) and calories, increasing nutritional requirements.
	• Fistulas: These abnormal tissue tunnels can leak digestive fluids, causing high-volume losses of protein, electrolytes, and fluids, which drastically spike a patient''s nutrient needs.', '["steatorrhea","medications","dumping syndrome","fistulas"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, 'Dumping Syndrome: This is a complication that occurs after gastric (stomach) surgeries—such as a gastric bypass or gastrectomy—where food moves too rapidly from the stomach into the small intestine. It is not an intrinsic feature or complication of Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD).', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T14:08:19.882Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:08:19.882Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:08:19.956Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758789038303-181', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Ms. Padilla, 30 years old, has several family members who have had cancer. She asks the nurse how she can reduce her risk of cancer. What would you suggest? *', 'Reduce her use of smoked, salt-cured, and nitrate-cured foods.', '', '["Limit her saturated fat intake to 30% .","Reduce her intake of sugars.","Reduce her consumption of unpeeled fruits and vegetables.","Reduce her use of smoked, salt-cured, and nitrate-cured foods."]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T05:10:50.558Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T05:10:50.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T05:10:50.558Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758789046035-182', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Mr. Santos has lung cancer which has metastasized to his spine. Based on this information, it will be essential for the nutritionist-dietitian to monitor him for: *', 'hypercalcemia', 'When a primary tumor (such as lung cancer) metastasizes to the bone/spine, it initiates osteolytic bone destruction. The invading tumor cells stimulate specialized bone cells (osteoclasts) to break down the surrounding bone tissue.

As the bone structure is aggressively broken down and reabsorbed, massive amounts of calcium are dissolved and released directly into the bloodstream. This rapid influx overwhelms the kidneys'' ability to excrete it, leading to a dangerous oncologic emergency known as Hypercalcemia of Malignancy (HCM).

Monitoring calcium levels is critical because hypercalcemia can lead to severe confusion, muscle weakness, kidney stones, and lethal cardiac arrhythmias. While dietary calcium restriction is generally not the primary treatment for HCM (as the excess calcium is coming from the bones, not food), the dietitian must aggressively monitor hydration status, fluid balance, and electrolyte trends to support clinical management.', '["hypokalemia","hyponatremia","hypercalcemia","hyperchloremia"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T15:22:25.839Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:22:25.839Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:22:25.924Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758789056669-183', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Diet order for irritable bowel syndrome: *', 'all of the above', 'Irritable Bowel Syndrome (IBS) is a functional gastrointestinal disorder where dietary management focuses on controlling symptoms like cramping, bloating, gas, diarrhea, and constipation. Standard clinical diet orders traditionally incorporate all three modifications:

• High-fiber: Gradually increasing dietary fiber (specifically soluble fiber like psyllium, oats, and flaxseeds) is a primary recommendation. It adds healthy bulk to stools to relieve constipation (IBS-C) while simultaneously helping to solidify loose stools in diarrhea-predominant IBS (IBS-D).
• Avoid highly seasoned foods: Spicy and heavily seasoned foods contain compounds (like capsaicin) that irritate the gut lining and accelerate GI transit. Eliminating them helps reduce abdominal pain, cramping, and sudden bowel urgency.
• Fat-restricted: High-fat, greasy, and deep-fried foods trigger an exaggerated gastrocolic reflex, which causes rapid, painful colonic contractions. Restricting dietary fat intake stabilizes gut motility and prevents immediate post-meal cramping and diarrhea.', '["avoid highly seasoned foods","high-fiber","fat-restricted","all of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T14:08:59.614Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:08:59.614Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:08:59.690Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758789070217-184', 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A person who has done well for 3 years following a gastrectomy should be alert to signs of: *', 'vitamin B₁₂ deficiency', 'The human liver stores a substantial reserve of Vitamin B₁₂ (cobalamin), which typically lasts 3 to 5 years. Once these internal stores are fully exhausted, symptoms of deficiency begin to appear.

A gastrectomy removes the parietal cells of the stomach, which are responsible for secreting intrinsic factor. Intrinsic factor is a protein absolutely required for Vitamin B₁₂ absorption in the ileum. Without it, the body cannot absorb dietary Vitamin B₁₂, eventually causing macrocytic/pernicious anemia and neurological issues.', '["iron deficiency anemia","blind loop syndrome","vitamin B₁₂ deficiency","dumping syndrome"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-16T02:56:40.815Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:56:40.815Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:56:40.893Z"'::jsonb)
