('anki-card-1788424793307-40', 'anki-deck-1788422977034-1791992590890-w1g2h', 'Which sensory test would be most appropriate when the objective is to determine whether panelists can distinguish between two samples based on a specified sensory attribute?', 'Paired comparison', 'Panelists are presented with two samples and asked to compare them with respect to a particular characteristic, such as sweetness, saltiness, tenderness, or intensity of flavor.', '["Paired comparison","Option B","Hedonic scaling","Rank-order"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-06T13:27:28.652Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:27:28.652Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T15:43:12.925Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:27:28.652Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788424750385-39', 'anki-deck-1788422977034-1791992590890-w1g2h', 'The recommended temperature for dry storage is approximately:', '60-70°F', 'About 60–70°F is an appropriate general range for dry storage under controlled conditions.

• 29–33°F is within the range of freezing or near-freezing storage rather than typical dry storage.
• 34–40°F is a refrigerated-storage range.
• 0–10°F is a freezing-storage range.', '["60-70°F","29-33°F","34-40°F","0-10°F"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-06T13:28:14.295Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:28:14.295Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T15:43:12.925Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:28:14.295Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788424706022-38', 'anki-deck-1788422977034-1791992590890-w1g2h', 'Which microorganism is associated with lactic acid fermentation?', 'Leuconostoc mesenteroides', 'Leuconostoc mesenteroides is a lactic acid bacterium associated with vegetable fermentations.

• Acetobacter aceti: associated with acetic acid production from ethanol.
• Saccharomyces cerevisiae: yeast associated with alcoholic fermentation.
• Saccharomyces carlsbergensis: yeast associated with lager brewing.', '["Leuconostoc mesenteroides","Saccharomyces carlsbergensis","Acetobacter aceti","Saccharomyces cerevisiae"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-06T13:28:22.196Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:28:22.196Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T15:43:12.925Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:28:22.196Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788424647805-37', 'anki-deck-1788422977034-1791992590890-w1g2h', 'Soft pickles may result from:', 'Weak brine or vinegar and exposure to air', 'Pickles may become soft when the brine or vinegar solution is too weak and/or when the pickles are exposed to air. Exposure to air can contribute to undesirable microbial activity and softening.', '["Weak brine or vinegar and exposure to air","Too much vinegar","Strong vinegar concentration alone","Excessive sugar concentration alone"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-06T13:27:53.808Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:27:53.808Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T15:43:12.925Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:27:53.808Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788424613183-36', 'anki-deck-1788422977034-1791992590890-w1g2h', 'In pickling, sugar primarily contributes:', 'Sweetness and flavor balance', 'Sugar contributes sweetness and helps balance the sourness and other flavors of pickled foods

• Sugar alone does not guarantee crispness.
• The primary preservation effects in many pickles are associated with acidity and other controlled conditions rather than sugar alone.
• Sugar does not by itself cause fermentation; fermentation depends on microorganisms and suitable conditions.', '["Sweetness and flavor balance","Fermentation","Crispness by itself","The main antimicrobial action"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-06T13:30:14.210Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:30:14.210Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T15:43:12.925Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:30:14.210Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788424575034-35', 'anki-deck-1788422977034-1791992590890-w1g2h', 'A vodka-based cocktail made with tomato juice, lemon juice, and hot pepper sauce is:', 'Bloody Mary', '<img alt="Bloody Mary Recipe" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," width="201">b. Bloody Mary<img alt="Manhattan" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," width="147">A. Manhattan is a whiskey-based cocktail traditionally made with vermouth and bitters. <img alt="Gimlet" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," width="200">C. A Gimlet is traditionally made with gin or vodka and lime cordial or lime juice, not tomato juice.<img alt="Black Russian" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," width="198">D. A Black Russian is vodka and coffee liqueur.', '["Bloody Mary","Manhattan","Gimlet","Black Russian"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-06T13:28:10.691Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:28:10.691Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T15:43:12.925Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:28:10.691Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788424431218-34', 'anki-deck-1788422977034-1791992590890-w1g2h', 'Which of the following is traditionally served cold?


<img alt="Chef John''s Gazpacho" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," width="200">a. Gazpacho
<img alt="JB''s Cheese Soufflé - Soufflé au Fromage" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," width="200">b. Soufflé
<img alt="Simple French Onion Soup" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," width="201">c. French onion soup
<img alt="Chicken Consomme" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," width="175">d. Consommé', 'Gazpacho', 'Gazpacho is a cold soup traditionally served chilled.', '["Gazpacho","Soufflé","French onion soup","Consommé"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-05-12T14:48:34.100Z","interval":195,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":7}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:48:34.100Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T15:43:12.925Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:48:34.305Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788424330423-33', 'anki-deck-1788422977034-1791992590890-w1g2h', 'Apan-apan is traditionally prepared using:', 'Kangkong', 'Apan-apan is a popular Ilonggo vegetable dish from Western Visayas, Philippines. It is a flavorful, savory, and tangy variation of adobong kangkong made with water spinach (kangkong), sautéed garlic, onions, vinegar, soy sauce, and fermented shrimp paste (guinamos or bagoong alamang), often topped with crispy fried pork (tulapho).<img alt="Apan-Apan Recipe" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," width="289">', '["Kangkong","Eggplant","Fish","Malunggay"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-06T13:27:10.476Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:27:10.476Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T15:43:12.925Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:27:10.476Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788424250880-32', 'anki-deck-1788422977034-1791992590890-w1g2h', 'Kneading bread dough is primarily done to:', 'Develop gluten', 'Kneading develops the gluten network that provides structure and elasticity to bread dough.', '["Develop gluten","Option B","Caramelize starch","Prevent fermentation"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-06T13:30:47.616Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:30:47.616Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T15:43:12.925Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:30:47.616Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788424225708-31', 'anki-deck-1788422977034-1791992590890-w1g2h', 'A common mixing method used to incorporate fat into flour for pie crust is:', 'Cutting in', 'Cutting in distributes solid fat through flour to create the desired pastry texture.

• Creaming is commonly used to incorporate fat and sugar, especially in cakes and cookies.
• Folding is used to gently incorporate ingredients while preserving air or delicate structure.
• Kneading develops gluten and is not the standard method for incorporating fat into pie-crust flour.', '["Cutting in","Creaming","Folding","Kneading"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-06T13:30:00.141Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:30:00.141Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T15:43:12.925Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:30:00.141Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788424187010-30', 'anki-deck-1788422977034-1791992590890-w1g2h', 'At higher altitudes, the boiling point of water:', 'Decreases', 'At higher altitudes, atmospheric pressure is lower because there is less air pressing down on the Earth’s surface. A liquid boils when its vapor pressure becomes equal to the surrounding atmospheric pressure.

Therefore: Higher altitude → lower atmospheric pressure → liquid reaches its boiling point at a lower temperature → lower boiling point', '["Decreases","Increases","Cannot be determined","Remains exactly the same"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-06T13:28:44.690Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:28:44.690Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T15:43:12.925Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:28:44.690Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788424144218-29', 'anki-deck-1788422977034-1791992590890-w1g2h', 'The most suitable wall material for institutional kitchen is:', 'Ceramic Tile', 'Ceramic Tiles are Durable, smooth, and easy to clean.

• Quarry tile is for floors. 
• Fiberglass is not durable.
• Stainless steel is for counters.', '["Ceramic Tile","Stainless steel","Quarry Tile","Fiberglass"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-05-12T14:48:47.052Z","interval":195,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":7}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:48:47.052Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T15:43:12.925Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:48:47.254Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788424086529-27', 'anki-deck-1788422977034-1791992590890-w1g2h', 'Which of the following is a non-perishable food?', 'Dried beans', 'Dried beans are considered non-perishable foods because their low moisture content allows them to be stored for long periods at room temperature without rapid spoilage, provided they are kept in proper dry-storage conditions.

• Potatoes: semi-perishable; 
• Fresh milk and fresh meat: perishable', '["Dried beans","Potatoes","Fresh milk","Fresh meat"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-06T13:28:33.192Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:28:33.192Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T15:43:12.925Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:28:33.192Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788426167715-57', 'anki-deck-1788422977034-1791992590890-w1g2h', 'Which statement best describes ergonomics in foodservice facility design?', 'Designing workstations to fit workers and promote safe, efficient performance', 'Ergonomics applies human capabilities and limitations to workplace design to improve safety, comfort, and efficiency.', '["Designing workstations to fit workers and promote safe, efficient performance","Option B","Designing menus according to customer preferences","Designing storage systems according to food cost"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-06T13:27:03.661Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:27:03.661Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T15:43:12.925Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:27:03.661Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788424028907-26', 'anki-deck-1788422977034-1791992590890-w1g2h', 'Which of the following is a chemical leavening agent?', 'Baking soda', 'Baking soda is a fine white chemical salt called sodium bicarbonate (NaHCO3). It acts as a base that reacts with acids to create carbon dioxide gas. This reaction makes baked goods rise.

• Yeast is a biological leavening agent.
• Whipped egg white can provide physical aeration rather than chemical leavening.
• Gluten provides structure and gas retention but is not itself a leavening agent.', '["Baking soda","Gluten","Yeast","Egg white"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-06T13:28:27.971Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:28:27.971Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T15:43:12.925Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:28:27.971Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788423990077-25', 'anki-deck-1788422977034-1791992590890-w1g2h', 'Which of the following is a microorganism commonly used in bread production?', 'Yeast', 'Yeast, particularly Saccharomyces cerevisiae, is a microorganism commonly used in bread production. It ferments sugars in the dough and produces carbon dioxide (CO₂). The CO₂ becomes trapped within the gluten network, causing the dough to rise and giving bread its characteristic volume and texture.', '["Yeast","Bacteria","Mold","Protozoa"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-06T13:31:10.326Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:31:10.326Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T15:43:12.925Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:31:10.534Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788423289524-6', 'anki-deck-1788422977034-1791992590890-w1g2h', 'Which type of cooking method is generally most appropriate for a tender cut of meat?', 'Dry-heat cooking', 'Dry-heat methods are generally appropriate for tender cuts because they do not require prolonged moist cooking.

Tender cuts of meat come from muscles that do the least work (such as the loin or rib areas) and contain very little tough connective tissue (collagen).

Because these cuts are already naturally tender, they do not require prolonged cooking to break down tough fibers. Dry-heat cooking methods—such as grilling, broiling, roasting, sautéing, or pan-frying—cook the meat quickly at high temperatures, preserving its natural juices and preventing it from becoming overcooked and dry.', '["Dry-heat cooking","Stewing","Braising","Moist cooking"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-06T13:28:48.280Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:28:48.280Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T15:43:12.925Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:28:48.280Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788423256707-5', 'anki-deck-1788422977034-1791992590890-w1g2h', 'Which enzyme is associated with the tenderizing activity of figs?', 'Ficain', 'Ficain is a proteolytic enzyme found in figs and can contribute to meat tenderization.

• Papain is associated with papaya.
• Bromelain is associated with pineapple.
• Lactase hydrolyzes lactose and is not the fig enzyme used for meat tenderizing.', '["Ficain","Papain","Bromelain","Lactase"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-06T13:29:56.562Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:29:56.562Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T15:43:12.925Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:29:56.562Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788423222657-4', 'anki-deck-1788422977034-1791992590890-w1g2h', 'During dry heating of starch, the formation of shorter-chain products is known as formation of:', 'Dextrins', 'Dextrins are shorter-chain products formed when starch is subjected to dry heat.

• Gluten is a protein complex associated with wheat dough, not a starch breakdown product.
• Casein is the principal protein of milk.
• Pectin is a structural polysaccharide found in plant tissues.', '["Dextrins","Pectin","Gluten","Casein"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-06T13:27:07.456Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:27:07.456Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T15:43:12.925Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:27:07.456Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788423190457-3', 'anki-deck-1788422977034-1791992590890-w1g2h', 'A triglyceride is composed primarily of:', 'One glycerol molecule and three fatty acids', 'One glycerol plus three fatty acids describes the basic structure of a triglyceride.

• A triglyceride has a glycerol backbone esterified with three fatty acids.
• Triglycerides are lipids, not compounds made from amino acids and glycerol.
• Glucose is not the backbone of a triglyceride.', '["One glycerol molecule and three fatty acids","Three glycerol molecules and one fatty acid","One glucose molecule and three fatty acids","Three amino acids and one glycerol molecule"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-06T13:28:40.603Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:28:40.603Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T15:43:12.925Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:28:40.603Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788425368534-47', 'anki-deck-1788422977034-1791992590890-w1g2h', 'Dinuguan of Mindanao with pork intestines, lungs, liver, banana heart, and olasiman', 'Sampayna', '<img alt="Sampayna (Dinuguan from Northern Mindanao)” – FoodEverMore" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," width="203">b. Sampayna<img alt="Pipian Rojo Recipe (Mexican Red Pipian Sauce)" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," width="200">a. Pipián: historic, rich seed-based sauce/stew originating from Mesoamerica and Mexican cuisine, which also evolved into a distinct PH regional chicken stew<img alt="TINUTO is our local laing variant. It is made from fresh young gabi leaves,  coconut cream, grated salted fish meat, lemon grass and chilies. 📸 Ana  Katherine Reyes" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," width="200">c. Tinuto: coconut-based dish from Quezon province and Marinduque, similar to Bicolano laing<img alt="Nilaneg - Ang Sarap" src="data:image/jpeg;base64," width="202">d. Nilanega: Filipino soup dish from Batanes and the Cagayan Valley region, made with broiled fresh fish and pork liver simmered in a savory bagoong broth along with local vegetables', '["Sampayna","Pipian","Tinuto","Nilaneg"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-06T13:29:07.002Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:29:07.002Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T15:43:12.925Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:29:07.002Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788423147596-2', 'anki-deck-1788422977034-1791992590890-w1g2h', 'All are biological contaminants except:', 'Pesticide residue', 'Biological contaminants are hazards that come from living organisms or their products:
• Paralytic shellfish poisoning — Biological. It is caused by toxins produced by certain marine microorganisms, particularly dinoflagellates.
• Hepatitis A virus — Biological. Viruses are biological contaminants and can be transmitted through contaminated food or water.
• Aflatoxin from molds — Biological. Aflatoxins are toxins produced by certain molds, particularly Aspergillus species.
• Pesticide residue — Chemical, not biological. Pesticides are chemical substances that can remain on or in food.

Remember:
• Biological = bacteria, viruses, parasites, molds, and their toxins
• Chemical = pesticides, cleaning chemicals, heavy metals, etc.', '["Pesticide residue","Paralytic shellfish poisoning","Hepatitis A virus","Aflatoxin from molds"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-06T13:28:06.405Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:28:06.405Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T15:43:12.925Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:28:06.405Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788423125908-1', 'anki-deck-1788422977034-1791992590890-w1g2h', 'Which type of amino acid must be obtained from the diet because the body cannot synthesize it in sufficient amounts?', 'Essential amino acid', 'Essential amino acids cannot be synthesized by the body in adequate amounts and therefore must be supplied by the diet.

Remember the Essential AAs: PVTTIMHALL Phenylalanine Valine Threonine Tryptophan Isoleucine Methionine Histidine Leucine Lysine

• Non-essential amino acids can generally be synthesized by the body.
• Structural amino acid is not the nutritional classification described in the question.
• Neutral amino acid describes a chemical property rather than the nutritional requirement being tested.', '["Essential amino acid","Neutral amino acid","Non-essential amino acid","Structural amino acid"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-06T13:28:52.471Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:28:52.471Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T15:43:12.925Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:28:52.471Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788423009882-0', 'anki-deck-1788422977034-1791992590890-w1g2h', 'Lactose is composed of:', 'Glucose and galactose', 'Lactose is a disaccharide composed of glucose and galactose.

• Glucose and fructose form sucrose, not lactose.
• Fructose and galactose do not form lactose.
• Glucose and sucrose are not the two monosaccharides that make up lactose.', '["Glucose and galactose","Glucose and fructose","Glucose and sucrose","Fructose and galactose"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-06T13:26:52.280Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:26:52.280Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T15:43:12.925Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:26:52.280Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788426324208-58', 'anki-deck-1788422977034-1791992590890-w1g2h', '<img src="data:image/jpeg;base64,">A diagram shows a server approaching a seated guest with a beverage. According to conventional service, from which side should the beverage generally be served?', 'Guest''s right', 'In conventional table service, beverages are generally served from the guest’s right side. The server should approach carefully without reaching across the guest and place the beverage on the table from the right. 

• Guest’s left: generally associated with serving certain foods, particularly when food is presented from a platter.', '["Guest''s right","Guest''s left","Directly from the back","Directly from the front"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-06T13:28:36.209Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:28:36.209Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T15:43:12.925Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:28:36.209Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788424120644-28', 'anki-deck-1788422977034-1791992590890-w1g2h', 'A cooked mixture of about equal weights of flour and fat used to thicken soups, sauces, and gravies.', 'Roux', 'A roux is the culinary term for a thickener made by cooking equal parts by weight of fat (usually butter) and flour.

It serves as the foundational thickening agent for classic French mother sauces (like Béchamel and Velouté) as well as various soups, stews, and gravies.

• Sherry is a wine.
• White wash is flour + water, not flour + fat.
• Custard is simple base of milk/cream, eggs or egg yolks and sugar.', '["Roux","Custard","Sherry","White wash"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-06T13:29:20.178Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:29:20.178Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T15:43:12.925Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:29:20.178Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792143191130-u5v7m', 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'Based on WHO cut-off points, a BMI of 27 is considered to be which of the following?', 'Overweight', 'WHO Standard Adult BMI Reference Ranges

The international classification breaks down weight status according to these specific metric boundaries:
• Underweight: BMI less than 18.5
• Normal weight: BMI of 18.5 to 24.9
• Overweight: BMI of 25.0 to 29.9 (A BMI of 27 falls directly in this bracket)
• Obese: BMI of 30.0 or higher', '["Overweight","Underweight","Normal weight","Obese"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, 'Community and Public Health Nutrition', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T10:33:32.319Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:33:32.319Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T09:33:11.631Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:33:32.319Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792143343281-3lpg5', 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'Which dietary assessment method determines nutrient intake by asking how often certain foods are consumed?', 'Food frequency', 'By definition, a Food Frequency Questionnaire (FFQ) asks a participant to report how often (the rate or frequency, such as daily, weekly, or monthly) they consume specific items from a pre-defined checklist of foods over a designated timeframe.

• Diet history: This is an in-depth retrospective interview method that focuses on mapping out overall long-term habitual eating patterns, meal frequencies, cooking behaviors, and general food habits, rather than solely ticking off the frequencies of a structured food item checklist.
• 24-hour recall: This method asks the participant to remember and describe all food and beverages consumed over the exact, immediate 24 hours prior to the interview. It looks at a single-day snapshot of consumption rather than the general frequency of specific items over time.', '["Food frequency","Diet history","24-hour recall","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, 'Community and Public Health Nutrition', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T10:33:49.298Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:33:49.298Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T09:35:44.101Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:33:49.298Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792143476380-7whhk', 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'Wasting in children is defined as _________?', 'Weight-for-height that is more than 2 Z-scores below the reference', 'In pediatric anthropometry and public health frameworks managed by the World Health Organization (WHO), childhood malnutrition indicators are divided into distinct categories based on standard deviations (Z-scores) relative to a healthy reference population:

• Wasting (Acute Malnutrition): This measures body mass in relation to body length. It is defined as a weight-for-height (or weight-for-length) value that sits more than 2 standard deviations below (-2 Z-scores) the WHO Child Growth Standards. It signifies rapid, severe weight loss or a failure to gain weight, typically caused by recent starvation, severe illness, or infection.
• Stunting (Chronic Malnutrition): This measures linear growth retardation and is defined as a height-for-age value more than 2 Z-scores below the reference. It reflects long-term, ongoing nutritional deprivation.
• Underweight: This is a composite metric combining acute and chronic factors, defined as a weight-for-age value more than 2 Z-scores below the reference.', '["Weight-for-height that is more than 2 Z-scores below the reference","Height-for-age that is more than 2 Z-scores below the reference","Weight-for-age that is more than 2 Z-scores below the reference","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, 'Community and Public Health Nutrition', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T10:34:35.302Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:34:35.302Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T09:37:57.031Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:34:35.302Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792143640290-s4x09', 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'Abdominal obesity in females is defined as a waist–hip ratio greater than:', '0.85', 'According to the World Health Organization (WHO) diagnostic guidelines, abdominal obesity (also known as central or android obesity) is measured using specific cutoff thresholds for the waist–hip ratio (WHR):

• Females: Abdominal obesity is defined as a waist–hip ratio greater than 0.85.
• Males: Abdominal obesity is defined as a waist–hip ratio greater than 0.90.

Any values exceeding these points indicate a high concentration of visceral fat around the abdominal organs, which is heavily associated with an elevated risk of developing type 2 diabetes, hypertension, and cardiovascular diseases.', '["0.85","0.70","0.95","1"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, 'Community and Public Health Nutrition', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T10:34:43.228Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:34:43.228Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T09:40:41.593Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:34:43.228Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792143832799-3zjc5', 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'A function of public health nutritionist which focuses on assisting the community to solve their diet and nutrition related problems.', 'Program Implementer', 'According to standard professional public health nutrition curriculum tracking guides (like those from UPLB and the FNDRC mock boards), the roles are explicitly partitioned by these exact action fragments:

1. Program Implementer: The core action of this role explicitly states that the nutritionist "guides or assists the people in solving their diet and nutrition-related problems" directly in the field. It is an on-the-ground execution role focused on helping the community change behaviors and overcome immediate barriers.
2. Program Administrator: This function is academically defined as focusing on financial management, budgeting, and organizing the personnel who are involved in the nutrition program implementation.
3. Program Planner: Focuses on organizing program planning activities, assisting the community in identifying and prioritizing their problems, and designing the specific course of action to be taken.
4. Program Organizer / Assessor: Focuses on initiating the development of the organization or community network to clear the path for future interventions.', '["Program Implementer","Program Planner","Program Organizer","Program Administrator"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, 'Community and Public Health Nutrition', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T10:34:52.469Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:34:52.469Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T09:43:54.483Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:34:52.469Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792143900814-2647g', 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', '“Hidden hunger” refers to:', 'Micronutrient deficiency', '"Hidden hunger" is a specific public health term used to describe a chronic lack of essential vitamins and minerals (micronutrients)—such as iron, iodine, vitamin A, and zinc—in a person''s diet.

Unlike classic undernutrition (like marasmus or wasting), where a person visibly looks emaciated due to a severe shortage of total calories or protein, hidden hunger often goes unnoticed. An individual may consume enough food or calories to feel full or maintain a normal body weight, but their body is still suffering from invisible, internal nutrient starvation that damages their immune system, cognitive development, and overall physical health.', '["Micronutrient deficiency","Calorie deficiency","Protein deficiency","Conditioned malnutrition"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, 'Community and Public Health Nutrition', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T10:34:57.611Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:34:57.611Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T09:45:01.579Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:34:57.611Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792144030281-4epjh', 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'With reference to the UNICEF framework on the causes of undernutrition, unhealthy environment is categorized as:', 'Underlying cause', 'Under the globally recognized UNICEF Conceptual Framework on the Causes of Malnutrition, the factors that lead to undernutrition are structurally categorized into three distinct, hierarchical levels of causation:

1. Immediate Causes (Individual level): These are the direct physical factors that affect the person''s body—specifically inadequate dietary intake and diseases.
2. Underlying Causes (Household/Community level): These are the social, economic, and environmental factors that lead to those immediate causes. An unhealthy environment (such as a lack of safe water, poor sanitation, and inadequate hygiene) falls squarely in this category, alongside a lack of household food security and inadequate caregiving practices.
3. Basic Causes (Societal level): These are the systemic or structural root problems within a society, such as political instability, poverty, poor governance, and lack of resources.', '["Underlying cause","Immediate cause","Potential cause","Basic cause"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, 'Community and Public Health Nutrition', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T10:35:19.712Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:35:19.712Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T09:47:10.965Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:35:19.712Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792144140131-fgx12', 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'Which is true about the physical growth of an infant?', 'The infant’s length changes more slowly than the weight', 'An infant''s birth weight typically doubles by about 4 to 6 months, not two months. It does triple by one year, but the first half of the statement is biologically inaccurate for normal infant development.

Weight changes much faster and more dynamically than height (linear growth). During the first year, an infant''s weight triples (a 200% increase), whereas their length increases by roughly 50% (from an average of 20 inches at birth to about 30 inches at one year). Linear growth is a more gradual, steady biological process compared to rapid mass accumulation.', '["The infant’s length changes more slowly than the weight","Infants’ birth weight doubles by about two months and triples by one year","A and B are both TRUE","A and B are both FALSE"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, 'Community and Public Health Nutrition', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T10:35:38.689Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:35:38.689Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T09:49:00.806Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:35:38.689Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792144266554-0lcij', 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'Babies with low birth weight:', 'Have high possibility of neonatal death compared with non-low birth weight infants', 'To understand why only this option is correct, let''s break down the clinical parameters of both statements:

• While it is true that low birth weight (LBW) infants have an increased susceptibility to infection and difficulty regulating their body temperature, they do not have mature kidneys. On the contrary, their organs are highly underdeveloped and immature, often leading to a reduced number of nephrons at birth and an increased risk of chronic kidney disease later in life. Because of the word "mature", Statement A is factually incorrect.
• Epidemiological statistics show that low birth weight is a leading driver of newborn mortality. LBW infants have a significantly higher possibility of neonatal death—ranging from 20 to 24 times more likely to die shortly after birth compared to infants born with a normal weight.', '["Have high possibility of neonatal death compared with non-low birth weight infants","Have an increased susceptibility to infection, difficulty regulating body temperature and mature kidneys","A and B are both TRUE","A and B are both FALSE"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, 'Community and Public Health Nutrition', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T10:38:09.740Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:38:09.740Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T09:51:07.277Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:38:09.922Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792144352906-h51rg', 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'What is the recommended ratio of whole milk and water when preparing milk for an infant below three months of age?', '2:1', 'When preparing traditional, home-modified animal milk for an infant below three months of age, the standard pediatric and public health recommendation is a 2:1 ratio (2 parts whole cow''s milk or carabao''s milk to 1 part water), usually paired with a specific amount of sugar (around 5%).

Whole, undiluted animal milk contains an exceptionally high concentration of protein and minerals (such as sodium and phosphorus). A young infant''s immature kidneys cannot handle this heavy solute load, which can lead to severe dehydration.

Diluting the milk with 1 part water lowers the renal solute load to a safer level for infants under 3 months. After the child passes 3 months of age, their renal function matures slightly, and the ratio is typically advanced to 3:1 before moving to whole, undiluted milk later in infancy if commercial formula or breast milk is absolutely unavailable.', '["2:1","1:2","1:3","3:1"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, 'Community and Public Health Nutrition', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T10:36:06.659Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:36:06.659Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T09:52:33.856Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:36:06.659Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792144437027-op8jv', 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'The effortless expulsion of gastric contents caused by GI reflux in babies', 'Regurgitation', 'Regurgitation is clinically defined as the effortless, passive expulsion of swallowed food or gastric contents from the stomach back into the esophagus and mouth. In infants, this is primarily driven by an immature lower esophageal sphincter—a common, normal condition known as gastroesophageal reflux (GER) that usually resolves on its own as the baby grows.

• Colic: This refers to periods of intense, frequent, and unexplained crying in an otherwise healthy infant, often thought to be linked to gas or abdominal discomfort, rather than the physical ejection of stomach contents.
• Diarrhea: This describes loose, watery stools passing through the lower gastrointestinal tract, not an upward reflux into the mouth.
• Spitting-up-food: While this is the casual, everyday term parents use for this exact behavior, Regurgitation is the primary scientific and medical terminology used in public health exams to describe the involuntary mechanism itself.', '["Regurgitation","Colic","Spitting-up-food","Diarrhea"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, 'Community and Public Health Nutrition', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T10:36:18.477Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:36:18.477Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T09:53:58.190Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:36:18.477Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792144517142-ps1h1', 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'An infant is physiologically ready for solid foods when:', 'All of the above', 'An infant becomes developmentally and physiologically ready to transition to solid foods (complementary feeding) around 6 months of age, marked by several key internal changes:
• Saliva is produced: Around 3 to 6 months, an infant’s salivary glands mature, significantly increasing saliva production. Saliva contains lubricants and enzymes that help moisten and break down solid textures.

• Gastric acidity increases: A newborn''s stomach has a relatively high pH (less acidic). As the infant approaches 6 months, gastric acid production increases, lowering the stomach pH to adult-like levels. This is necessary to properly digest complex proteins from solid foods and kill harmful foodborne pathogens.
• Enzymes for starch digestion are produced: During early infancy, levels of pancreatic amylase (the enzyme needed to digest complex starches) are very low. By 6 months of age, the secretion of these digestive enzymes matures significantly, allowing the infant''s GI tract to safely process and absorb starches from rice, cereals, and mashed root crops.', '["All of the above","Saliva is produced","Gastric acidity increases","Enzymes for starch digestion is produced"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, 'Community and Public Health Nutrition', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T10:36:22.229Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:36:22.229Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T09:55:17.868Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:36:22.229Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792145015213-bzxs1', 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'Which is true about the basal metabolic of the elderly?', 'A and B are both FALSE', 'The basal metabolic rate (BMR) does decline with age, but it happens because of a decrease in the functional capacity of cells and a prominent loss of functioning lean muscle tissue (sarcopenia). It does not decline because cell capacity increases.

The decline in baseline metabolic needs is typically accompanied by an increase in body fat (adipose tissue) and a redistribution of fat toward the abdomen, rather than a decrease in body fat.', '["A and B are both FALSE","It declines with age because of the increase in the functional capacity of cells","The declined metabolic needs is accompanied by a decrease in body fat","A and B are both TRUE"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, 'Community and Public Health Nutrition', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T10:36:29.110Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:36:29.110Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:03:36.002Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:36:29.110Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792145072979-404ox', 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'Psychological related factors resulting to malnutrition in elderly except:', 'Immobility', 'Immobility is a physical/biological factor: It refers to the physical inability to move around easily, which causes malnutrition by making it difficult for an elderly person to shop for groceries, lift heavy cooking utensils, or prepare meals independently.

The other options are psychological/social factors: Living alone (which leads to loneliness and isolation), depression, and anxiety are all mental, emotional, or psychological states that directly destroy an elderly person''s appetite, interest in food, and motivation to eat (a condition often called the "anorexia of aging").', '["Immobility","Living alone","Depression","Anxiety"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, 'Community and Public Health Nutrition', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T10:36:35.677Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:36:35.677Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:04:36.137Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:36:35.677Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792145133410-lknqx', 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'Hyposmia is also know as:', 'Lowered sense of smell', 'Hyposmia is the official clinical term for a partially reduced or lowered ability to smell and detect odors. It is very common in older adults due to natural aging, as well as clinical conditions like chronic sinusitis, viral infections, or neurodegenerative changes.

• Decreased taste sensitivity: This is known as hypogeusia (whereas a complete loss of taste is ageusia).
• Loss of appetite: This is known clinically as anorexia (such as the anorexia of aging).
• Lowered thirst sensation: This is known as hypodipsia.', '["Lowered sense of smell","Decreased taste sensitivity","Loss of appetite","Lowered thirst sensation"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, 'Community and Public Health Nutrition', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T10:36:48.366Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:36:48.366Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:05:34.069Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:36:48.366Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792145308872-7k558', 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'Which is true about menopause among females:', 'A and B are both TRUE', 'Menopause is the permanent cessation of menstrual periods resulting from the natural biological aging of the female reproductive system:

• Statement A is TRUE: As a woman transitions into menopause, her ovaries dramatically reduce their secretion of the key female sex hormones, leading to severely decreased levels of estrogen and progesterone. This sharp hormonal decline is responsible for classic menopausal symptoms like hot flashes and night sweats.
• Statement B is TRUE: The root biological cause of this hormone drop is the natural depletion of the ovarian reserve. A woman is born with a finite number of primordial follicles. Over her reproductive lifespan, as more follicles undergo ovulation or atresia (shrinkage/cell death), the ovaries end up with a significantly lower number of viable ovarian follicles. Consequently, fewer follicles develop completely to ovulate and transform into a corpus luteum (the temporary structure responsible for secreting progesterone).', '["A and B are both TRUE","A result of decreased levels of estrogen and progesterone","A result of less number of ovarian follicles and corpora lutea","A and B are both FALSE"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, 'Community and Public Health Nutrition', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T10:36:59.927Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:36:59.927Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:08:29.336Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:36:59.927Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792145374332-vvkhi', 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'The breast milk that women produce in the first few days after delivery', 'Colostrum', 'Colostrum is the distinct, specialized form of breast milk produced by the mammary glands in late pregnancy and the first few days after delivery (typically days 1 to 4). It is a thick, yellowish fluid that is uniquely adapted to a newborn''s immediate needs:

It is highly concentrated with leukocytes and protective antibodies—especially Immunoglobulin A (IgA)—acting as the infant''s first natural vaccine.

It contains high levels of epidermal growth factors to help mature the infant''s gut lining, and acts as a mild laxative to assist the baby in passing meconium (the first stool).

• Mature milk: This thinner, bluish-white milk transitions in around day 10 to 15 postpartum, supplying the stable balance of water, carbohydrates, lipids, and proteins needed for long-term growth.
• Foremilk: This is the milk available at the beginning of a single feeding session, which is watery and high in lactose to satisfy the infant''s thirst.
• Hindmilk: This is the creamier milk released toward the end of a single feeding session, which is dense in fat and calories to promote satiety and weight gain.', '["Colostrum","Mature milk","Foremilk","Hindmilk"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, 'Community and Public Health Nutrition', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T10:37:12.562Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:37:12.562Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:09:34.927Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:37:12.562Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792145503463-fnle0', 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'A multi-level tool for promoting and sustaining risk-reducing behavior change in individuals and communities by distributing tailored specific messages through a variety of channels.', 'Behavior Change Communication', 'Behavior Change Communication (BCC) is a strategic, multi-level framework designed to foster and maintain healthy, risk-reducing lifestyle habits within populations.

It systematically delivers tailored, highly specific messages designed to resonate with particular audiences. Rather than relying on a single approach, BCC distributes these messages across a variety of communication channels—including interpersonal counseling, group discussions, community media, and mass marketing campaigns.

While Nutrition Education is a component of this process, BCC focuses more broadly on the psychological and social drivers of action to shift actual behavior, moving beyond simply providing raw academic facts.', '["Behavior Change Communication","Nutrition Education","Learning Strategies","Health Promotion"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, 'Community and Public Health Nutrition', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T10:37:17.906Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:37:17.906Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:11:44.166Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:37:17.906Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792145652252-xfzat', 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'Which is an example of interpersonal communication?', 'Nutrition counseling', 'Interpersonal communication refers to a face-to-face, interactive exchange of information between two or more people. In this context, nutrition counseling is the perfect example because it involves a direct, personalized, two-way dialogue between a healthcare professional (or nutrition worker) and an individual client or family. This layout allows for active listening, immediate feedback, and tailored advice.', '["Nutrition counseling","Mother''s class","Airing of nutrition messages in TV","Distribution of nutrition handouts"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, 'Community and Public Health Nutrition', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T10:37:22.233Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:37:22.233Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:14:12.722Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:37:22.233Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792145907870-itdps', 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'Who is the chair of the National Nutrition Council Governing Board?', 'Department of Health', 'By law, under Presidential Decree No. 491 (The Nutrition Act of the Philippines) and subsequent executive updates, the Secretary of the Department of Health (DOH) serves as the official Chairperson of the National Nutrition Council (NNC) Governing Board.

The NNC is the country''s highest policy-making and coordinating body on nutrition. Its leadership structure consists of:
• Chairperson: Secretary of the Department of Health (DOH)
• Vice-Chairs:
	1. Secretary of the Department of Agriculture (DA)
	2. Secretary of the Department of the Interior and Local Government (DILG)

The board also includes the heads of several other departments (such as DepEd, DSWD, DTI, DOST, and DOLE) alongside representatives from the private sector to ensure a multi-sectoral approach to addressing malnutrition.', '["Department of Health","Department of Agriculture","Department of Education","Department of Interior and Local Government"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, 'Community and Public Health Nutrition', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T10:37:31.656Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:37:31.656Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:18:28.393Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:37:31.656Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792145946968-1bk4n', 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'Which law specifies rules and regulations which contain standard procedures to be followed for rooming-in and breastfeeding in all private and government health institutions?', 'RA 7600', '', '["RA 7600","RA 8172","RA 8976","RA 10028"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, 'Community and Public Health Nutrition', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T10:37:36.568Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:37:36.568Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:19:08.347Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:37:36.568Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792145970153-bvwoz', 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'Which law requires private enterprises as well as government agencies, including government-owned and controlled corporations, to put up lactation stations?', 'RA 10028', '', '["RA 10028","RA 7600","RA 8172","RA 8976"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, 'Community and Public Health Nutrition', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T10:37:41.505Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:37:41.505Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:19:30.850Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:37:41.505Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792145996585-1rs6n', 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'What executive order regulates the marketing of infant milk formula, other milk products, foods and beverages, as well as feeding bottles and teats?', 'EO 51', '', '["EO 51","EO 41","EO 61","EO 71"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, 'Community and Public Health Nutrition', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T10:37:45.300Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:37:45.300Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:19:57.360Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:37:45.300Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792146059233-gfgva', 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'What law strengthens the Barangay Nutrition Program by providing for a Barangay Nutrition Scholar in every barangay to assist in the coordination and implementation of nutrition programs, projects, and activities and the barangay level?', 'PD 1569', 'Signed on June 11, 1978, Presidential Decree No. 1569 is titled "Strengthening the Barangay Nutrition Program by providing for a Barangay Nutrition Scholar (BNS) in every barangay, providing funds therefor, and for other purposes."

It officially established the BNS program across the Philippines, mandating that every barangay should have a trained local volunteer (the BNS) responsible for:
• Monitoring the nutritional status of children through routine weighing (Operation Timbang Plus)
• Linking malnourished children and families to health and nutrition services
• Coordinating nutrition programs, projects, and activities directly at the grass-roots barangay level', '["PD 1569","PD 1596","PD 1659","PD 1695"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, 'Community and Public Health Nutrition', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T10:38:01.908Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:38:01.908Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:20:59.881Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:38:02.089Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792663303603-gy2x0', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Drinking coffee or tea together with an iron-rich meal primarily decreases the absorption of which type of iron?', 'Non-heme iron', 'Coffee and tea contain polyphenols and tannins that form insoluble complexes with non-heme iron, substantially reducing its intestinal absorption. The inhibitory effect is greatest when these beverages are consumed with or shortly after meals. Heme iron, derived from animal foods, is absorbed through a different mechanism and is much less affected by dietary inhibitors. Vitamin C can counteract this inhibition by reducing ferric iron (Fe³⁺) to the more absorbable ferrous form (Fe²⁺).', '["Non-heme iron","Heme iron","Ferritin-bound iron","Both heme and non-heme iron equally"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, 'Nutritional Biochemistry and Clinical Dietetics', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-23T10:28:32.918Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T10:28:32.918Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T10:01:45.146Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T10:28:33.103Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792663393777-ahua3', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which lipoprotein is primarily responsible for transporting dietary triglycerides from the intestine to peripheral tissues?', 'Chylomicrons', 'Chylomicrons are the largest and least dense lipoproteins. They are synthesized in the intestinal mucosal cells after dietary fat absorption and transport exogenous triglycerides, cholesterol, fat-soluble vitamins, and phospholipids through the lymphatic system before entering the bloodstream. Lipoprotein lipase (LPL), activated by apolipoprotein C-II, hydrolyzes triglycerides in chylomicrons, releasing free fatty acids for uptake by adipose tissue and skeletal muscle. The remaining chylomicron remnants are taken up by the liver.', '["Chylomicrons","HDL","LDL","VLDL"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, 'Nutritional Biochemistry and Clinical Dietetics', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-23T10:28:43.839Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T10:28:43.839Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T10:03:15.390Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T10:28:44.022Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792663446718-613b8', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which enzyme is responsible for hydrolyzing triglycerides in circulating chylomicrons and VLDL?', 'Lipoprotein lipase', 'Lipoprotein lipase (LPL) is an extracellular enzyme attached to the capillary endothelium of adipose tissue, skeletal muscle, and cardiac muscle. It hydrolyzes triglycerides contained in circulating chylomicrons and VLDL into free fatty acids and glycerol. 

The released fatty acids are either: 
• Stored as triglycerides in adipose tissue, or 
• Oxidized by muscle tissue for energy. 

LPL activity is stimulated by apolipoprotein C-II, making Apo C-II an essential cofactor for normal triglyceride metabolism.', '["Lipoprotein lipase","Hormone-sensitive lipase","Pancreatic lipase","Hepatic lipase"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, 'Nutritional Biochemistry and Clinical Dietetics', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:29:59.665Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:29:59.665Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T10:04:08.299Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:29:59.665Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792663543407-vbzop', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which of the following hormones primarily stimulates glycogen breakdown in the liver during fasting?', 'Glucagon', 'Glucagon is a peptide hormone secreted by the pancreatic α-cells in response to declining blood glucose concentrations. Its primary role is to maintain euglycemia during the post-absorptive state by stimulating hepatic glucose production. 

The major metabolic actions of glucagon include: 
• Glycogenolysis – breakdown of liver glycogen into glucose.
• Gluconeogenesis – synthesis of glucose from non-carbohydrate precursors such as lactate, glycerol, and glucogenic amino acids. 
• Lipolysis – indirectly promotes mobilization of fatty acids from adipose tissue. 
• Ketogenesis – enhances ketone body formation during prolonged fasting. It is important to note that skeletal muscle lacks glucagon receptors. 

Therefore, glucagon promotes glycogenolysis primarily in the liver, whereas muscle glycogen breakdown is stimulated mainly by epinephrine and muscle contraction.', '["Glucagon","Insulin","Leptin","Aldosterone"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, 'Nutritional Biochemistry and Clinical Dietetics', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:35:20.323Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:35:20.323Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T10:05:44.858Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:35:20.323Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792663653577-6ss8f', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which of the following best describes the primary function of bile salts in lipid digestion?', 'Emulsify dietary fats and facilitate micelle formation', 'Bile salts are amphipathic molecules synthesized from cholesterol in the liver and stored in the gallbladder. Upon ingestion of dietary fat, they are released into the duodenum where they perform two major functions: 

• Emulsification – breaking large fat globules into smaller droplets, thereby increasing the surface area available for pancreatic lipase. 
• Micelle formation – surrounding fatty acids, monoglycerides, cholesterol, and fat-soluble vitamins to facilitate their transport to the intestinal brush border for absorption. 

Bile salts themselves do not digest fats; rather, they enhance the efficiency of pancreatic lipase and promote lipid absorption.', '["Emulsify dietary fats and facilitate micelle formation","Hydrolyze triglycerides into fatty acids","Digest proteins in the stomach","Activate pancreatic amylase"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, 'Nutritional Biochemistry and Clinical Dietetics', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:11:03.017Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:11:03.017Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T10:07:35.029Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:11:03.017Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792663850326-umk9r', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which of the following describes the maximum amount of bone tissue attained after skeletal development is completed and serves as an important determinant of bone health in adulthood?', 'Peak Bone Mass', 'Peak bone mass (PBM) is the maximum amount of bone tissue accumulated by an individual at the end of skeletal maturation, usually achieved during the late teens to early 30s, depending on the skeletal site, sex, genetics, and lifestyle factors. It represents the highest level of bone strength attained before the gradual age-related decline in bone mass begins. 

Peak bone mass is influenced by several factors, including: 
• Genetics (the strongest determinant) 
• Adequate calcium and vitamin D intake 
• Regular weight-bearing and resistance exercise 
• Hormonal status (especially estrogen and testosterone) 
• Adequate protein intake 
• Avoidance of smoking and excessive alcohol consumption

A higher peak bone mass reduces the risk of osteopenia, osteoporosis, and fragility fractures later in life. Therefore, childhood, adolescence, and young adulthood are considered critical periods for optimizing bone health.', '["Peak Bone Mass","Bone mineral content","Bone mineral density","Bone mass"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, 'Nutritional Biochemistry and Clinical Dietetics', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-23T10:29:26.334Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T10:29:26.334Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T10:10:52.015Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T10:29:26.517Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792663993534-s8949', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Protein recommendation for adults with pressure injuries or significant wounds to promote wound healing:', '1.5 – 2 g/kg', 'Protein is one of the most important nutrients for wound healing, as it is required for collagen synthesis, fibroblast proliferation, angiogenesis, immune function, and tissue repair. Inadequate protein intake delays wound healing, impairs immune responses, and increases the risk of wound infection and chronic non-healing wounds. Patients with pressure injuries, surgical wounds, burns, traumatic injuries, or chronic ulcers generally have increased protein requirements because of the increased metabolic demands associated with tissue repair. 

Current clinical nutrition guidelines recommend approximately 1.25–1.5 g/kg/day of protein for most patients with pressure injuries and up to 1.5–2.0 g/kg/day for individuals with extensive wounds, burns, or severe catabolic conditions, provided renal function is adequate.', '["1.5 – 2 g/kg","20-25% of TER","15-20% of TER","50% of TER"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, 'Nutritional Biochemistry and Clinical Dietetics', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-25T10:30:06.277Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T10:30:06.277Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T10:13:15.262Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T10:30:06.457Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792664150515-tfyqi', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which medication is most strongly associated with folate deficiency during long-term therapy?', 'Methotrexate', 'Methotrexate is an antimetabolite that competitively inhibits dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR), the enzyme responsible for converting dihydrofolate into tetrahydrofolate, the biologically active form of folate. This inhibition impairs the synthesis of purines and thymidylate, ultimately reducing DNA and RNA synthesis.

Because rapidly dividing cells rely heavily on folate-dependent DNA synthesis, long-term methotrexate therapy may lead to: 
• Folate deficiency 
• Megaloblastic anemia 
• Oral mucositis 
• Glossitis 
• Gastrointestinal disturbances
• Elevated homocysteine levels 

Methotrexate is widely used in the treatment of: 
• Rheumatoid arthritis 
• Psoriasis 
• Certain cancers 
• Other autoimmune diseases 

To reduce adverse effects while preserving therapeutic efficacy, patients receiving low-dose methotrexate for autoimmune diseases are commonly prescribed folic acid supplementation, usually on days when methotrexate is not taken or according to the physician''s protocol.', '["Methotrexate","Digoxin","Omeprazole","Metformin"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, 'Nutritional Biochemistry and Clinical Dietetics', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-23T10:29:50.920Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T10:29:50.920Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T10:15:52.498Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T10:29:51.091Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792664240726-6kbqv', 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which equation is currently recommended by KDIGO for estimating glomerular filtration rate (eGFR) in adults?', 'CKD-EPI 2021 equation', 'The Kidney Disease: Improving Global Outcomes (KDIGO) 2024 Clinical Practice Guideline recommends using the 2021 Chronic Kidney Disease Epidemiology Collaboration (CKD-EPI) creatinine equation as the preferred equation for estimating glomerular filtration rate (eGFR) in adults. 

The CKD-EPI 2021 equation: 
• Estimates kidney function using serum creatinine, age, and sex 
• Removes the race coefficient that was included in earlier equations 
• Provides more equitable assessment across different populations 
• Generally demonstrates better accuracy than the older MDRD equation, particularly at higher GFR values 

Estimated GFR is essential for: 
• Diagnosing chronic kidney disease (CKD) 
• Staging CKD 
• Monitoring disease progression 
• Guiding medication dosing 
• Planning nutrition therapy', '["CKD-EPI 2021 equation","Cockcroft–Gault equation","MDRD equation","Jelliffe equation"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, 'Nutritional Biochemistry and Clinical Dietetics', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:11:35.573Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:11:35.573Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T10:17:22.427Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:11:35.573Z"'::jsonb)
