('mc:what is the primary significance of the krebs cycle in energy metabolism?:::it produces nadh and fadh₂ for oxidative phosphorylation', 'what is the primary significance of the krebs cycle in energy metabolism?', 'It produces NADH and FADH₂ for oxidative phosphorylation', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"During one turn of the Krebs cycle, acetyl-CoA is condensed with oxaloacetate to form citrate, which undergoes a series of decarboxylation and oxidation-reduction reactions catalyzed by enzymes such as isocitrate dehydrogenase and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase. These reactions reduce NAD⁺ and FAD into NADH and FADH₂. These reduced coenzymes then donate their high-energy electrons to the mitochondrial electron transport chain, establishing a proton gradient that powers oxidative phosphorylation via ATP synthase to generate the vast majority of cellular ATP.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"It produces NADH and FADH₂ for oxidative phosphorylation\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"During one turn of the Krebs cycle, acetyl-CoA is condensed with oxaloacetate to form citrate, which undergoes a series of decarboxylation and oxidation-reduction reactions catalyzed by enzymes such as isocitrate dehydrogenase and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase. These reactions reduce NAD⁺ and FAD into NADH and FADH₂. These reduced coenzymes then donate their high-energy electrons to the mitochondrial electron transport chain, establishing a proton gradient that powers oxidative phosphorylation via ATP synthase to generate the vast majority of cellular ATP.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: It produces the majority of ATP directly, It converts glucose directly into lactate, It produces bile acids"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the principle which states that “each employee must know to whom he reports”, refers to:::', 'the principle which states that “each employee must know to whom he reports”, refers to', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Unity of command prevents conflicting directives, ambiguity in accountability, and operational confusion by establishing a singular, direct line of authority where a subordinate receives orders from and is accountable to only one immediate superior. In clinical and foodservice management, this structural hierarchy ensures clear communication channels, precise delegation of tasks, and efficient chain-of-command execution during critical operational or patient care workflows.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"unity of command\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Unity of command prevents conflicting directives, ambiguity in accountability, and operational confusion by establishing a singular, direct line of authority where a subordinate receives orders from and is accountable to only one immediate superior. In clinical and foodservice management, this structural hierarchy ensures clear communication channels, precise delegation of tasks, and efficient chain-of-command execution during critical operational or patient care workflows.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: departmentation, delegation of authority, delegation of responsibility"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the primary significance of the krebs cycle in energy metabolism?:::', 'what is the primary significance of the krebs cycle in energy metabolism?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"During one turn of the Krebs cycle, acetyl-CoA is condensed with oxaloacetate to form citrate, which undergoes a series of decarboxylation and oxidation-reduction reactions catalyzed by enzymes such as isocitrate dehydrogenase and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase. These reactions reduce NAD⁺ and FAD into NADH and FADH₂. These reduced coenzymes then donate their high-energy electrons to the mitochondrial electron transport chain, establishing a proton gradient that powers oxidative phosphorylation via ATP synthase to generate the vast majority of cellular ATP.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"It produces NADH and FADH₂ for oxidative phosphorylation\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"During one turn of the Krebs cycle, acetyl-CoA is condensed with oxaloacetate to form citrate, which undergoes a series of decarboxylation and oxidation-reduction reactions catalyzed by enzymes such as isocitrate dehydrogenase and alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase. These reactions reduce NAD⁺ and FAD into NADH and FADH₂. These reduced coenzymes then donate their high-energy electrons to the mitochondrial electron transport chain, establishing a proton gradient that powers oxidative phosphorylation via ATP synthase to generate the vast majority of cellular ATP.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: It produces the majority of ATP directly, It converts glucose directly into lactate, It produces bile acids"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a restaurant served 87 guests and made p 18, 705 sales. average check per guest was.:::p 215', 'a restaurant served 87 guests and made p 18, 705 sales. average check per guest was.', 'P 215', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In foodservice management and financial accounting, the average check (also termed cover average) measures the mean spending per customer. The standard mathematical formula is: Average Check = Total Sales Revenue / Total Number of Guests (Covers). Substituting the problem''s values: P 18,705 / 87 guests = P 215. This financial control metric allows managers to evaluate menu pricing efficiency, sales performance, and guest spending patterns.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"P 215\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In foodservice management and financial accounting, the average check (also termed cover average) measures the mean spending per customer. The standard mathematical formula is: Average Check = Total Sales Revenue / Total Number of Guests (Covers). Substituting the problem''s values: P 18,705 / 87 guests = P 215. This financial control metric allows managers to evaluate menu pricing efficiency, sales performance, and guest spending patterns.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: P 250, P 205, P 217"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a restaurant served 87 guests and made p 18, 705 sales. average check per guest was.:::', 'a restaurant served 87 guests and made p 18, 705 sales. average check per guest was.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In foodservice management and financial accounting, the average check (also termed cover average) measures the mean spending per customer. The standard mathematical formula is: Average Check = Total Sales Revenue / Total Number of Guests (Covers). Substituting the problem''s values: P 18,705 / 87 guests = P 215. This financial control metric allows managers to evaluate menu pricing efficiency, sales performance, and guest spending patterns.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"P 215\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In foodservice management and financial accounting, the average check (also termed cover average) measures the mean spending per customer. The standard mathematical formula is: Average Check = Total Sales Revenue / Total Number of Guests (Covers). Substituting the problem''s values: P 18,705 / 87 guests = P 215. This financial control metric allows managers to evaluate menu pricing efficiency, sales performance, and guest spending patterns.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: P 250, P 205, P 217"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the principle which states that “each employee must know to whom he reports”, refers to:::unity of command', 'the principle which states that “each employee must know to whom he reports”, refers to', 'unity of command', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Unity of command prevents conflicting directives, ambiguity in accountability, and operational confusion by establishing a singular, direct line of authority where a subordinate receives orders from and is accountable to only one immediate superior. In clinical and foodservice management, this structural hierarchy ensures clear communication channels, precise delegation of tasks, and efficient chain-of-command execution during critical operational or patient care workflows.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"unity of command\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Unity of command prevents conflicting directives, ambiguity in accountability, and operational confusion by establishing a singular, direct line of authority where a subordinate receives orders from and is accountable to only one immediate superior. In clinical and foodservice management, this structural hierarchy ensures clear communication channels, precise delegation of tasks, and efficient chain-of-command execution during critical operational or patient care workflows.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: departmentation, delegation of authority, delegation of responsibility"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:term in marketing that describes sales promotion::::', 'term in marketing that describes sales promotion:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Merchandising is the activity of promoting the sale of goods, especially by their presentation in retail outlets.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Merchandising\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Merchandising is the activity of promoting the sale of goods, especially by their presentation in retail outlets.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Branding, Benchmarking, None of the choices"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a detailed outline of routine tasks and side duties for each job position is called:::job description', 'a detailed outline of routine tasks and side duties for each job position is called', 'Job description', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A job description outlines the tasks, duties, responsibilities, and working conditions of a specific position. It acts as a detailed guide of what the employee is expected to do on a daily basis, including their routine tasks and any minor side duties.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Job description\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A job description outlines the tasks, duties, responsibilities, and working conditions of a specific position. It acts as a detailed guide of what the employee is expected to do on a daily basis, including their routine tasks and any minor side duties.\n\n\t• Job Specification: This focuses on the human qualities needed for the job. It lists the minimum qualifications, skills, education, experience, and physical traits a person must have to perform the tasks. (A simple trick: Descriptions detail the tasks; Specifications detail the person).\n\t• Job Standards: These define the expected level of performance or measurable targets for the work being done (e.g., \"wash 50 pans per hour\").\n\t• Job Classification: This is the system of grouping similar jobs together into classes or grades based on difficulty and responsibility, usually for determining pay scales.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Job standards, Job specification, Job classification"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a detailed outline of routine tasks and side duties for each job position is called:::', 'a detailed outline of routine tasks and side duties for each job position is called', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A job description outlines the tasks, duties, responsibilities, and working conditions of a specific position. It acts as a detailed guide of what the employee is expected to do on a daily basis, including their routine tasks and any minor side duties.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Job description\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A job description outlines the tasks, duties, responsibilities, and working conditions of a specific position. It acts as a detailed guide of what the employee is expected to do on a daily basis, including their routine tasks and any minor side duties.\n\n\t• Job Specification: This focuses on the human qualities needed for the job. It lists the minimum qualifications, skills, education, experience, and physical traits a person must have to perform the tasks. (A simple trick: Descriptions detail the tasks; Specifications detail the person).\n\t• Job Standards: These define the expected level of performance or measurable targets for the work being done (e.g., \"wash 50 pans per hour\").\n\t• Job Classification: This is the system of grouping similar jobs together into classes or grades based on difficulty and responsibility, usually for determining pay scales.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Job standards, Job specification, Job classification"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:it can bring about severe malabsorption::::laxatives', 'it can bring about severe malabsorption:', 'laxatives', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Chronic overuse or abuse of laxatives directly induces severe malabsorption syndromes. Laxatives unnaturally accelerate intestinal motility (peristalsis) or alter osmotic pressure, which forces food contents to rush through the gastrointestinal tract. This rapid transit time denies the small and large intestines the necessary window to digest and absorb essential macronutrients, fat-soluble vitamins (like Vitamin D), and electrolytes. Over time, this leads to structural steatorrhea, mineral depletion, and severe undernutrition.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"laxatives\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Chronic overuse or abuse of laxatives directly induces severe malabsorption syndromes. Laxatives unnaturally accelerate intestinal motility (peristalsis) or alter osmotic pressure, which forces food contents to rush through the gastrointestinal tract. This rapid transit time denies the small and large intestines the necessary window to digest and absorb essential macronutrients, fat-soluble vitamins (like Vitamin D), and electrolytes. Over time, this leads to structural steatorrhea, mineral depletion, and severe undernutrition.\n\n• Diuretics: While diuretics heavily interfere with fluid balance and can lead to severe potassium depletion or dehydration through increased urinary excretion, they impact renal function rather than causing global nutrient malabsorption in the gut.\n• Paracetamol: This is a standard over-the-counter analgesic and antipyretic. While acute toxicity or overdose causes severe hepatotoxicity (liver damage), standard therapeutic use does not impair the mucosal lining of the intestines or produce malabsorption.\n• Cough Syrup: Typically containing antitussives or expectorants, these formulations do not target or compromise the digestive mechanics or metabolic transport systems of the small intestine.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: diuretics, paracetamol, cough syrup"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:it can bring about severe malabsorption::::', 'it can bring about severe malabsorption:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Chronic overuse or abuse of laxatives directly induces severe malabsorption syndromes. Laxatives unnaturally accelerate intestinal motility (peristalsis) or alter osmotic pressure, which forces food contents to rush through the gastrointestinal tract. This rapid transit time denies the small and large intestines the necessary window to digest and absorb essential macronutrients, fat-soluble vitamins (like Vitamin D), and electrolytes. Over time, this leads to structural steatorrhea, mineral depletion, and severe undernutrition.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"laxatives\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Chronic overuse or abuse of laxatives directly induces severe malabsorption syndromes. Laxatives unnaturally accelerate intestinal motility (peristalsis) or alter osmotic pressure, which forces food contents to rush through the gastrointestinal tract. This rapid transit time denies the small and large intestines the necessary window to digest and absorb essential macronutrients, fat-soluble vitamins (like Vitamin D), and electrolytes. Over time, this leads to structural steatorrhea, mineral depletion, and severe undernutrition.\n\n• Diuretics: While diuretics heavily interfere with fluid balance and can lead to severe potassium depletion or dehydration through increased urinary excretion, they impact renal function rather than causing global nutrient malabsorption in the gut.\n• Paracetamol: This is a standard over-the-counter analgesic and antipyretic. While acute toxicity or overdose causes severe hepatotoxicity (liver damage), standard therapeutic use does not impair the mucosal lining of the intestines or produce malabsorption.\n• Cough Syrup: Typically containing antitussives or expectorants, these formulations do not target or compromise the digestive mechanics or metabolic transport systems of the small intestine.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: diuretics, paracetamol, cough syrup"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a patient with keratinized goosebump-like skin and a keratinized cornea (bitot’s spots) has trouble seeing in the dark. the biochemical deficiency most likely related to loss of vision in the dark is in the::::generation of all-trans retinal', 'a patient with keratinized goosebump-like skin and a keratinized cornea (bitot’s spots) has trouble seeing in the dark. the biochemical deficiency most likely related to loss of vision in the dark is in the:', 'generation of all-trans retinal', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The triad of keratinized \"goosebump-like\" skin (phrynoderma), keratinized conjunctival debris (Bitot''s spots), and difficulty seeing in dim light (nyctalopia/night blindness) represents a classic, textbook presentation of Vitamin A (retinol) deficiency.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"generation of all-trans retinal\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The triad of keratinized \"goosebump-like\" skin (phrynoderma), keratinized conjunctival debris (Bitot''s spots), and difficulty seeing in dim light (nyctalopia/night blindness) represents a classic, textbook presentation of Vitamin A (retinol) deficiency.\n\nVitamin A is an essential precursor for the visual pigments in the retina. Inside the photoreceptor cells (rods), 11-cis retinal binds to the protein opsin to form rhodopsin.\n\nWhen light hits the rod cells, 11-cis retinal absorbs the photon and undergoes a photochemical isomerization, converting into all-trans retinal. This precise shape change activates rhodopsin, triggering the nerve impulses sent to the brain that allow us to see in the dark. A chronic lack of vitamin A impairs the continuous recycling and generation of these retinal isomers, directly causing night blindness.\n\n• gamma carboxylation: This describes the biochemical mechanism of Vitamin K, which activates clotting factors, not Vitamin A.\n• alpha-tocopherol: This is the chemical name for Vitamin E. While it is a fat-soluble vitamin, a deficiency causes neurological issues and hemolytic anemia, not Bitot''s spots.\n• Zinc absorption: While zinc deficiency can secondary impair vitamin A mobilization from the liver, the explicit biochemical failure directly responsible for the loss of night vision is the visual cycle''s failure to generate the retinal pigment.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: absorption of zinc, absorption of alpha-tocopherol, gamma carboxylation of glutamate residues"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a genetic disorder characterized by a constant sense of hunger or hyperphagia that usually begins at two years of age.:::', 'a genetic disorder characterized by a constant sense of hunger or hyperphagia that usually begins at two years of age.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Prader-Willi Syndrome (PWS) is a complex genetic disorder caused by a lack of expression of paternally inherited genes on chromosome 15.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Prader-Willi Syndrome\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Prader-Willi Syndrome (PWS) is a complex genetic disorder caused by a lack of expression of paternally inherited genes on chromosome 15.\n\nWhile infants with PWS typically suffer from poor muscle tone (hypotonia) and poor sucking reflexes that cause feeding difficulties, they transition into a distinct second phase between 2 to 4 years of age. During this stage, a chronic, unrelenting, and insatiable sense of hunger (hyperphagia) develops due to hypothalamic dysfunction. Without strict environmental food controls, this drives severe early-childhood obesity.\n\n• Phenylketonuria: An inborn error of metabolism where the body cannot break down the amino acid phenylalanine. It leads to intellectual disabilities and neurological issues if untreated, not childhood hyperphagia.\n• G6PD Deficiency: An enzyme deficiency that triggers the premature breakdown of red blood cells (hemolysis) upon exposure to specific foods (like fava beans) or medications, having no impact on appetite regulation.\n• Bardet-Biedl Syndrome: While this genetic disorder also features childhood obesity and hyperphagia, its insatiable hunger typically begins much later in childhood and is classically accompanied by structural features like extra fingers or toes (polydactyly) and progressive vision loss.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Bardet-Biedl Syndrome, Phenylketonuria, G6PD"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is a branched-chain amino acid?:::isoleucine', 'which of the following is a branched-chain amino acid?', 'isoleucine', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"There are exactly three branched-chain amino acids (BCAAs) among the standard amino acids: leucine, isoleucine, and valine. They are named for their unique, branching aliphatic hydrocarbon side-chain molecular structure.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"isoleucine\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"There are exactly three branched-chain amino acids (BCAAs) among the standard amino acids: leucine, isoleucine, and valine. They are named for their unique, branching aliphatic hydrocarbon side-chain molecular structure.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: alanine, lysine, glutamine"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:organelle in the cell where protein and lipid synthesis take place.:::endoplasmic reticulum', 'organelle in the cell where protein and lipid synthesis take place.', 'endoplasmic reticulum', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The endoplasmic reticulum (ER) is a continuous network of membranes divided into two functionally distinct areas that handle biosynthesis:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"endoplasmic reticulum\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The endoplasmic reticulum (ER) is a continuous network of membranes divided into two functionally distinct areas that handle biosynthesis:\n\t• Rough ER: Studded with ribosomes, it is the primary site where protein synthesis and structural folding take place.\n\t• Smooth ER: Lacks ribosomes and contains specialized enzymes responsible for lipid synthesis (including phospholipids and cholesterol), steroid hormone production, and carbohydrate metabolism.\n\n• Golgi apparatus: Acts as the cell''s shipping center. It does not synthesize proteins or lipids from scratch; instead, it modifies, sorts, and packages the proteins and lipids received from the ER.\n• Nucleus: Serves as the control center containing the cell''s genetic blueprint (DNA). It is the site for DNA replication and transcription (RNA synthesis), not translation or lipid manufacturing.\n• Mitochondria: Known as the powerhouse of the cell, its primary function is generating energy in the form of ATP via aerobic cellular respiration.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: nucleus, Golgi apparatus, mitochondria"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:organelle in the cell where protein and lipid synthesis take place.:::', 'organelle in the cell where protein and lipid synthesis take place.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The endoplasmic reticulum (ER) is a continuous network of membranes divided into two functionally distinct areas that handle biosynthesis:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"endoplasmic reticulum\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The endoplasmic reticulum (ER) is a continuous network of membranes divided into two functionally distinct areas that handle biosynthesis:\n\t• Rough ER: Studded with ribosomes, it is the primary site where protein synthesis and structural folding take place.\n\t• Smooth ER: Lacks ribosomes and contains specialized enzymes responsible for lipid synthesis (including phospholipids and cholesterol), steroid hormone production, and carbohydrate metabolism.\n\n• Golgi apparatus: Acts as the cell''s shipping center. It does not synthesize proteins or lipids from scratch; instead, it modifies, sorts, and packages the proteins and lipids received from the ER.\n• Nucleus: Serves as the control center containing the cell''s genetic blueprint (DNA). It is the site for DNA replication and transcription (RNA synthesis), not translation or lipid manufacturing.\n• Mitochondria: Known as the powerhouse of the cell, its primary function is generating energy in the form of ATP via aerobic cellular respiration.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: nucleus, Golgi apparatus, mitochondria"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the rate-limiting step in glycolysis is::::the phosphorylation of fructose 6-phosphate', 'the rate-limiting step in glycolysis is:', 'the phosphorylation of fructose 6-phosphate', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The conversion of fructose 6-phosphate to fructose 1,6-bisphosphate is the key committed, irreversible, and rate-limiting step of glycolysis. This highly regulated step is catalyzed by the enzyme phosphofructokinase-1 (PFK-1). PFK-1 controls the overall speed of the pathway and is sensitive to the energy demands of the cell (inhibited by ATP and citrate, activated by AMP and fructose 2,6-bisphosphate).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"the phosphorylation of fructose 6-phosphate\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The conversion of fructose 6-phosphate to fructose 1,6-bisphosphate is the key committed, irreversible, and rate-limiting step of glycolysis. This highly regulated step is catalyzed by the enzyme phosphofructokinase-1 (PFK-1). PFK-1 controls the overall speed of the pathway and is sensitive to the energy demands of the cell (inhibited by ATP and citrate, activated by AMP and fructose 2,6-bisphosphate).\n\n• Isomerization of Glucose 6-phosphate: This is Step 2 of glycolysis catalyzed by phosphoglucose isomerase. It is a freely reversible reaction and is not a major regulatory or rate-limiting point.\n• Breakdown of Glycogen: This process is called glycogenolysis and occurs before glycolysis can begin when using stored carbohydrates. Its rate-limiting enzyme is glycogen phosphorylase, which belongs to a separate metabolic pathway altogether.\n• Phosphorylation of Glucose: This is Step 1 of glycolysis, catalyzed by hexokinase or glucokinase. While it is an irreversible, regulatory step that traps glucose inside the cell, it is not the primary rate-limiting step because glucose 6-phosphate can still leave the glycolytic pathway to enter other loops, such as the pentose phosphate pathway or glycogen synthesis.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: the breakdown of glycogen, the phosphorylation of glucose, the isomerization of glucose 6-phosphate to fructose 6-phosphate"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the rate-limiting step in glycolysis is::::', 'the rate-limiting step in glycolysis is:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The conversion of fructose 6-phosphate to fructose 1,6-bisphosphate is the key committed, irreversible, and rate-limiting step of glycolysis. This highly regulated step is catalyzed by the enzyme phosphofructokinase-1 (PFK-1). PFK-1 controls the overall speed of the pathway and is sensitive to the energy demands of the cell (inhibited by ATP and citrate, activated by AMP and fructose 2,6-bisphosphate).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"the phosphorylation of fructose 6-phosphate\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The conversion of fructose 6-phosphate to fructose 1,6-bisphosphate is the key committed, irreversible, and rate-limiting step of glycolysis. This highly regulated step is catalyzed by the enzyme phosphofructokinase-1 (PFK-1). PFK-1 controls the overall speed of the pathway and is sensitive to the energy demands of the cell (inhibited by ATP and citrate, activated by AMP and fructose 2,6-bisphosphate).\n\n• Isomerization of Glucose 6-phosphate: This is Step 2 of glycolysis catalyzed by phosphoglucose isomerase. It is a freely reversible reaction and is not a major regulatory or rate-limiting point.\n• Breakdown of Glycogen: This process is called glycogenolysis and occurs before glycolysis can begin when using stored carbohydrates. Its rate-limiting enzyme is glycogen phosphorylase, which belongs to a separate metabolic pathway altogether.\n• Phosphorylation of Glucose: This is Step 1 of glycolysis, catalyzed by hexokinase or glucokinase. While it is an irreversible, regulatory step that traps glucose inside the cell, it is not the primary rate-limiting step because glucose 6-phosphate can still leave the glycolytic pathway to enter other loops, such as the pentose phosphate pathway or glycogen synthesis.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: the breakdown of glycogen, the phosphorylation of glucose, the isomerization of glucose 6-phosphate to fructose 6-phosphate"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a patient with keratinized goosebump-like skin and a keratinized cornea (bitot’s spots) has trouble seeing in the dark. the biochemical deficiency most likely related to loss of vision in the dark is in the::::', 'a patient with keratinized goosebump-like skin and a keratinized cornea (bitot’s spots) has trouble seeing in the dark. the biochemical deficiency most likely related to loss of vision in the dark is in the:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The triad of keratinized \"goosebump-like\" skin (phrynoderma), keratinized conjunctival debris (Bitot''s spots), and difficulty seeing in dim light (nyctalopia/night blindness) represents a classic, textbook presentation of Vitamin A (retinol) deficiency.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"generation of all-trans retinal\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The triad of keratinized \"goosebump-like\" skin (phrynoderma), keratinized conjunctival debris (Bitot''s spots), and difficulty seeing in dim light (nyctalopia/night blindness) represents a classic, textbook presentation of Vitamin A (retinol) deficiency.\n\nVitamin A is an essential precursor for the visual pigments in the retina. Inside the photoreceptor cells (rods), 11-cis retinal binds to the protein opsin to form rhodopsin.\n\nWhen light hits the rod cells, 11-cis retinal absorbs the photon and undergoes a photochemical isomerization, converting into all-trans retinal. This precise shape change activates rhodopsin, triggering the nerve impulses sent to the brain that allow us to see in the dark. A chronic lack of vitamin A impairs the continuous recycling and generation of these retinal isomers, directly causing night blindness.\n\n• gamma carboxylation: This describes the biochemical mechanism of Vitamin K, which activates clotting factors, not Vitamin A.\n• alpha-tocopherol: This is the chemical name for Vitamin E. While it is a fat-soluble vitamin, a deficiency causes neurological issues and hemolytic anemia, not Bitot''s spots.\n• Zinc absorption: While zinc deficiency can secondary impair vitamin A mobilization from the liver, the explicit biochemical failure directly responsible for the loss of night vision is the visual cycle''s failure to generate the retinal pigment.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: absorption of zinc, absorption of alpha-tocopherol, gamma carboxylation of glutamate residues"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:how much protein is allowed for patients with gout?:::1 g/kg bw', 'how much protein is allowed for patients with gout?', '1 g/kg BW', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to nutritional standards and clinical practice guides for hyperuricemia and gout patients, the recommended daily dietary protein allowance is 1 g per kilogram of body weight (1 g/kg BW).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"1 g/kg BW\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to nutritional standards and clinical practice guides for hyperuricemia and gout patients, the recommended daily dietary protein allowance is 1 g per kilogram of body weight (1 g/kg BW).\n\nWhile a high total volume of protein is not responsible for raising uric acid levels, the source of the protein matters. Gout patients are encouraged to meet their standard protein requirement (1 g/kg BW) by focusing on low-purine or plant-based protein options (such as low-fat dairy, eggs, tofu, and legumes) while strictly limiting high-purine animal proteins like organ meats, red meat, and seafood.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 0.5 g/kg BW, 0.8 g/kg BW, 5 g/kg BW"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:how much protein is allowed for patients with gout?:::', 'how much protein is allowed for patients with gout?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to nutritional standards and clinical practice guides for hyperuricemia and gout patients, the recommended daily dietary protein allowance is 1 g per kilogram of body weight (1 g/kg BW).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"1 g/kg BW\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to nutritional standards and clinical practice guides for hyperuricemia and gout patients, the recommended daily dietary protein allowance is 1 g per kilogram of body weight (1 g/kg BW).\n\nWhile a high total volume of protein is not responsible for raising uric acid levels, the source of the protein matters. Gout patients are encouraged to meet their standard protein requirement (1 g/kg BW) by focusing on low-purine or plant-based protein options (such as low-fat dairy, eggs, tofu, and legumes) while strictly limiting high-purine animal proteins like organ meats, red meat, and seafood.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 0.5 g/kg BW, 0.8 g/kg BW, 5 g/kg BW"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the best prescription for a female secretary with a height of 5 ft. 4 inches, weight of 70kg, and fbs of 7mmol/l::::1400 kcal/day', 'the best prescription for a female secretary with a height of 5 ft. 4 inches, weight of 70kg, and fbs of 7mmol/l:', '1400 kcal/day', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Step-by-Step Breakdown Using Standard Clinical Nutrition Formulas","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"1400 kcal/day\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Step-by-Step Breakdown Using Standard Clinical Nutrition Formulas\n\nTo determine the best caloric prescription, we must calculate the patient''s Desirable Body Weight (DBW) and apply the correct activity factor adjusted for her clinical status (Overweight + Diabetic).\n\n1. Find the Desirable Body Weight (DBW) using Tannhauser''s Method:\n\t• Convert height to centimeters: 5 ft. 4 inches = 64 inches * 2.54 = 162.56 cm\n\t• Deduct 100 from height in cm: 162.56 - 100 = 62.56 kg\n\t• Deduct 10% for the final DBW: 62.56 - 6.25 = 56.31 kg (rounded to 56 kg)\n\n2. Evaluate her Clinical Status & Activity Level:\n\t• Current Weight: 70 kg (Since her DBW is 56 kg, she is significantly overweight).\n\t• FBS (Fasting Blood Sugar): 7.0 mmol/L (This meets the diagnostic threshold for Diabetes Mellitus).\n\t• Activity Level: As an office secretary, her physical activity is classified as Sedentary.\n\n3. Apply the Weight Reduction/Diabetic Caloric Factor:\n\t• For a normal weight individual with a sedentary lifestyle, the baseline factor is 30 kcal/kg DBW.\n\t• However, because she is an overweight diabetic, a low-calorie weight reduction regimen is required to optimize insulin sensitivity. The textbook factor for weight reduction in sedentary individuals is 25 kcal/kg DBW.\n\n4. Compute the Total Energy Requirement (TER):\n\t• TER = 56.31 kg (DBW) * 25 kcal/kg = 1407.75 kcal/day\n\nRounding to the nearest multiple of 100 as standard practice in dietetic prescriptions yields exactly 1400 kcal/day.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 800 kcal/day, 1000 kcal/day, 1200 kcal/day"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:all are dietary carbohydrates except::::', 'all are dietary carbohydrates except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dietary Fibers & Plant Carbs: Pectin, Starch, and Mucilages are carbohydrates synthesized by plants. Pectin and mucilages are classified as soluble dietary fibers, while starch is the primary digestible storage carbohydrate in plants.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"glycogen\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dietary Fibers & Plant Carbs: Pectin, Starch, and Mucilages are carbohydrates synthesized by plants. Pectin and mucilages are classified as soluble dietary fibers, while starch is the primary digestible storage carbohydrate in plants.\n\nGlycogen is the complex storage carbohydrate synthesized and found inside animal tissue (specifically liver and muscle cells).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: mucilages, starch, pectin"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the two simple sugars that make up maltose::::', 'the two simple sugars that make up maltose:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Also known as malt sugar, this disaccharide is formed by a covalent bond between two glucose molecules (specifically an α(1→4) glycosidic linkage). It is commonly produced during the digestion of starch by amylase.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"2 glucose\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Also known as malt sugar, this disaccharide is formed by a covalent bond between two glucose molecules (specifically an α(1→4) glycosidic linkage). It is commonly produced during the digestion of starch by amylase.\n\n• Glucose and Fructose: These two sugars combine to form sucrose (table sugar).\n• Glucose and Galactose: These two sugars combine to form lactose (milk sugar).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 2 fructose, glucose and fructose, glucose and galactose"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the best prescription for a female secretary with a height of 5 ft. 4 inches, weight of 70kg, and fbs of 7mmol/l::::', 'the best prescription for a female secretary with a height of 5 ft. 4 inches, weight of 70kg, and fbs of 7mmol/l:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Step-by-Step Breakdown Using Standard Clinical Nutrition Formulas","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"1400 kcal/day\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Step-by-Step Breakdown Using Standard Clinical Nutrition Formulas\n\nTo determine the best caloric prescription, we must calculate the patient''s Desirable Body Weight (DBW) and apply the correct activity factor adjusted for her clinical status (Overweight + Diabetic).\n\n1. Find the Desirable Body Weight (DBW) using Tannhauser''s Method:\n\t• Convert height to centimeters: 5 ft. 4 inches = 64 inches * 2.54 = 162.56 cm\n\t• Deduct 100 from height in cm: 162.56 - 100 = 62.56 kg\n\t• Deduct 10% for the final DBW: 62.56 - 6.25 = 56.31 kg (rounded to 56 kg)\n\n2. Evaluate her Clinical Status & Activity Level:\n\t• Current Weight: 70 kg (Since her DBW is 56 kg, she is significantly overweight).\n\t• FBS (Fasting Blood Sugar): 7.0 mmol/L (This meets the diagnostic threshold for Diabetes Mellitus).\n\t• Activity Level: As an office secretary, her physical activity is classified as Sedentary.\n\n3. Apply the Weight Reduction/Diabetic Caloric Factor:\n\t• For a normal weight individual with a sedentary lifestyle, the baseline factor is 30 kcal/kg DBW.\n\t• However, because she is an overweight diabetic, a low-calorie weight reduction regimen is required to optimize insulin sensitivity. The textbook factor for weight reduction in sedentary individuals is 25 kcal/kg DBW.\n\n4. Compute the Total Energy Requirement (TER):\n\t• TER = 56.31 kg (DBW) * 25 kcal/kg = 1407.75 kcal/day\n\nRounding to the nearest multiple of 100 as standard practice in dietetic prescriptions yields exactly 1400 kcal/day.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 800 kcal/day, 1000 kcal/day, 1200 kcal/day"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:azotemia is detected through the measurement of::::blood urea nitrogen', 'azotemia is detected through the measurement of:', 'blood urea nitrogen', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• Azotemia is a medical condition characterized by abnormally high levels of nitrogen-containing compounds (such as urea and creatinine) in the blood.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"blood urea nitrogen\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• Azotemia is a medical condition characterized by abnormally high levels of nitrogen-containing compounds (such as urea and creatinine) in the blood.\n• Detection: It is primarily detected through a Blood Urea Nitrogen (BUN) test or a serum creatinine test, both of which indicate how well the kidneys are filtering out waste products.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: calcium in blood, uric acid in urine, sodium in the spinal fluid"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:azotemia is detected through the measurement of::::', 'azotemia is detected through the measurement of:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• Azotemia is a medical condition characterized by abnormally high levels of nitrogen-containing compounds (such as urea and creatinine) in the blood.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"blood urea nitrogen\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• Azotemia is a medical condition characterized by abnormally high levels of nitrogen-containing compounds (such as urea and creatinine) in the blood.\n• Detection: It is primarily detected through a Blood Urea Nitrogen (BUN) test or a serum creatinine test, both of which indicate how well the kidneys are filtering out waste products.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: calcium in blood, uric acid in urine, sodium in the spinal fluid"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a 350-pound male is not seen while crossing the street and sustains bilateral femoral fractures. later, he develops cellulites. you would::::provide nutritional support to maintain body protein', 'a 350-pound male is not seen while crossing the street and sustains bilateral femoral fractures. later, he develops cellulites. you would:', 'provide nutritional support to maintain body protein', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"During acute metabolic stress, the body aggressively breaks down skeletal muscle to mobilize amino acids for immune function and tissue healing. Providing adequate nutritional support with an emphasis on high protein content is critical to attenuate this muscle wasting, support the immune response to fight the cellulitis, and promote bone healing.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"provide nutritional support to maintain body protein\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"During acute metabolic stress, the body aggressively breaks down skeletal muscle to mobilize amino acids for immune function and tissue healing. Providing adequate nutritional support with an emphasis on high protein content is critical to attenuate this muscle wasting, support the immune response to fight the cellulitis, and promote bone healing.\n\nEven though the patient is severely obese at 350 pounds, a period of acute, multi-system trauma is not the time to induce a caloric deficit for the purpose of long-term fat loss. Restricting nutrients or feeding him strictly for weight loss would starve the wound healing process and drastically worsen net protein catabolism.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: calculate requirement based upon ideal body weight, provide hypocaloric feedings to facilitate weight loss, none of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:these are purely ketogenic amino acids::::leucine and lysine', 'these are purely ketogenic amino acids:', 'Leucine and Lysine', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Leucine and Lysine are purely ketogenic amino acids that can be catabolized to form ketone bodies and yield acetyl CoA or acetoacetate.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Leucine and Lysine\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Leucine and Lysine are purely ketogenic amino acids that can be catabolized to form ketone bodies and yield acetyl CoA or acetoacetate.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Cysteine and Arginine, Leucine and Alanine"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a 350-pound male is not seen while crossing the street and sustains bilateral femoral fractures. later, he develops cellulites. you would::::', 'a 350-pound male is not seen while crossing the street and sustains bilateral femoral fractures. later, he develops cellulites. you would:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"During acute metabolic stress, the body aggressively breaks down skeletal muscle to mobilize amino acids for immune function and tissue healing. Providing adequate nutritional support with an emphasis on high protein content is critical to attenuate this muscle wasting, support the immune response to fight the cellulitis, and promote bone healing.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"provide nutritional support to maintain body protein\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"During acute metabolic stress, the body aggressively breaks down skeletal muscle to mobilize amino acids for immune function and tissue healing. Providing adequate nutritional support with an emphasis on high protein content is critical to attenuate this muscle wasting, support the immune response to fight the cellulitis, and promote bone healing.\n\nEven though the patient is severely obese at 350 pounds, a period of acute, multi-system trauma is not the time to induce a caloric deficit for the purpose of long-term fat loss. Restricting nutrients or feeding him strictly for weight loss would starve the wound healing process and drastically worsen net protein catabolism.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: calculate requirement based upon ideal body weight, provide hypocaloric feedings to facilitate weight loss, none of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the hormone responsible for stimulating the uptake of glucose into the cells and its storage as glycogen in the liver and muscles::::insulin', 'the hormone responsible for stimulating the uptake of glucose into the cells and its storage as glycogen in the liver and muscles:', 'insulin', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"This anabolic hormone is secreted by the beta cells of the pancreas in response to high blood sugar levels (such as after eating). It lowers blood glucose by stimulating its uptake into cells (via GLUT-4 translocation) and promoting glycogenesis—the storage of excess glucose as glycogen in the liver and skeletal muscles.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"insulin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"This anabolic hormone is secreted by the beta cells of the pancreas in response to high blood sugar levels (such as after eating). It lowers blood glucose by stimulating its uptake into cells (via GLUT-4 translocation) and promoting glycogenesis—the storage of excess glucose as glycogen in the liver and skeletal muscles.\n\n• Glucagon: Produced by the alpha cells of the pancreas, glucagon has the exact opposite effect. It is released during fasting or low blood sugar states to trigger the liver to break glycogen back down into glucose (glycogenolysis).\n• Epinephrine & Norepinephrine: Known as catecholamines, these stress hormones stimulate the breakdown of glycogen to rapidly elevate blood glucose levels for immediate energy during a fight-or-flight response.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: glucagon, epinephrine, norepinephrine"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb)
