1:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:diet for calcium oxalate urinary calculi.:::alkaline ash diet', 'diet for calcium oxalate urinary calculi.', 'alkaline ash diet', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Calcium oxalate stones are the most common type of kidney stones. They tend to form more easily in acidic urine. An alkaline ash diet increases the pH of the urine (making it less acidic), which helps keep calcium oxalate and uric acid crystals dissolved so they can be passed safely. This diet focuses on eating foods like vegetables, fruits, and legumes, while reducing acid-forming foods like meats, cheeses, and grains.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"alkaline ash diet\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Calcium oxalate stones are the most common type of kidney stones. They tend to form more easily in acidic urine. An alkaline ash diet increases the pH of the urine (making it less acidic), which helps keep calcium oxalate and uric acid crystals dissolved so they can be passed safely. This diet focuses on eating foods like vegetables, fruits, and legumes, while reducing acid-forming foods like meats, cheeses, and grains.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: acid ash diet, neutral diet, low calcium diet"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:diet for calcium oxalate urinary calculi.:::', 'diet for calcium oxalate urinary calculi.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Calcium oxalate stones are the most common type of kidney stones. They tend to form more easily in acidic urine. An alkaline ash diet increases the pH of the urine (making it less acidic), which helps keep calcium oxalate and uric acid crystals dissolved so they can be passed safely. This diet focuses on eating foods like vegetables, fruits, and legumes, while reducing acid-forming foods like meats, cheeses, and grains.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"alkaline ash diet\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Calcium oxalate stones are the most common type of kidney stones. They tend to form more easily in acidic urine. An alkaline ash diet increases the pH of the urine (making it less acidic), which helps keep calcium oxalate and uric acid crystals dissolved so they can be passed safely. This diet focuses on eating foods like vegetables, fruits, and legumes, while reducing acid-forming foods like meats, cheeses, and grains.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: acid ash diet, neutral diet, low calcium diet"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:it is given when nutrient requirements are greatly increased and the git is not functional.:::tpn', 'it is given when nutrient requirements are greatly increased and the git is not functional.', 'TPN', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When a patient''s gastrointestinal tract (GIT) is non-functional, blocked, or requires complete clinical rest, enteral options like a gastrostomy or a nasogastric tube (NGT) cannot be used safely. Bypassing the gut entirely becomes mandatory.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"TPN\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When a patient''s gastrointestinal tract (GIT) is non-functional, blocked, or requires complete clinical rest, enteral options like a gastrostomy or a nasogastric tube (NGT) cannot be used safely. Bypassing the gut entirely becomes mandatory.\n\nWhile basic \"intravenous feeding\" usually refers to peripheral lines giving short-term hydration or dilute nutrients (like PPN), TPN is a highly concentrated, hyperosmolar formula packed with complete macronutrients, vitamins, and minerals. Because it is designed to meet massive metabolic demands (such as in severe burns, sepsis, or major trauma), it must be safely administered through a large central vein.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: NGT, intravenous feeding, gastrostomy"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:in this style of tableservice, all food are prepared and portioned in the kitchen and attractively arranged on serving platters. food is served to individual plates using cutleries.:::russian', 'in this style of tableservice, all food are prepared and portioned in the kitchen and attractively arranged on serving platters. food is served to individual plates using cutleries.', 'Russian', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Russian style of service entails fully preparing food in the kitchen and placed in platters for service to guests.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Russian\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Russian style of service entails fully preparing food in the kitchen and placed in platters for service to guests.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: French, American, Blue plate"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:it is given when nutrient requirements are greatly increased and the git is not functional.:::', 'it is given when nutrient requirements are greatly increased and the git is not functional.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When a patient''s gastrointestinal tract (GIT) is non-functional, blocked, or requires complete clinical rest, enteral options like a gastrostomy or a nasogastric tube (NGT) cannot be used safely. Bypassing the gut entirely becomes mandatory.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"TPN\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When a patient''s gastrointestinal tract (GIT) is non-functional, blocked, or requires complete clinical rest, enteral options like a gastrostomy or a nasogastric tube (NGT) cannot be used safely. Bypassing the gut entirely becomes mandatory.\n\nWhile basic \"intravenous feeding\" usually refers to peripheral lines giving short-term hydration or dilute nutrients (like PPN), TPN is a highly concentrated, hyperosmolar formula packed with complete macronutrients, vitamins, and minerals. Because it is designed to meet massive metabolic demands (such as in severe burns, sepsis, or major trauma), it must be safely administered through a large central vein.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: NGT, intravenous feeding, gastrostomy"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the fat content of a 2,000 kcal diet providing 30% of calories from fat.:::67g', 'the fat content of a 2,000 kcal diet providing 30% of calories from fat.', '67g', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Step-by-Step Calculation:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"67g\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Step-by-Step Calculation:\n\n1. Find total calories from fat: Multiply the total calories by the fat percentage.\n2,000 kcal x 0.30 = 600 kcal from fat\n\n2. Convert calories to grams: Fat provides 9 calories per gram (9 kcal/g). Divide the fat calories by 9.\n600 kcal / 9 kcal/g = 66.67g\n\nWhen rounded to the nearest whole number, 66.67g gives you 67g.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 60g, 77g, 80g"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the fat content of a 2,000 kcal diet providing 30% of calories from fat.:::', 'the fat content of a 2,000 kcal diet providing 30% of calories from fat.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Step-by-Step Calculation:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"67g\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Step-by-Step Calculation:\n\n1. Find total calories from fat: Multiply the total calories by the fat percentage.\n2,000 kcal x 0.30 = 600 kcal from fat\n\n2. Convert calories to grams: Fat provides 9 calories per gram (9 kcal/g). Divide the fat calories by 9.\n600 kcal / 9 kcal/g = 66.67g\n\nWhen rounded to the nearest whole number, 66.67g gives you 67g.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 60g, 77g, 80g"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:refer to the following information. based on the fel, the diet of a burn patient contains the following meal pattern for lunch: 90g fried chicken; 160g rice; 45g sauteed squash; 250g fresh milk; and 103g ripe mango. how much is the cho content of the meal?:::71 g', 'refer to the following information. based on the fel, the diet of a burn patient contains the following meal pattern for lunch: 90g fried chicken; 160g rice; 45g sauteed squash; 250g fresh milk; and 103g ripe mango. how much is the cho content of the meal?', '71 g', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to the Philippine Food Exchange List (FEL), each food item is converted into exchange units to calculate its specific carbohydrate (CHO) content:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"71 g\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to the Philippine Food Exchange List (FEL), each food item is converted into exchange units to calculate its specific carbohydrate (CHO) content:\n\n1. 160g Rice (2 Rice Exchanges):\n• 1 exchange of cooked rice = 80g (contains 23g CHO)\n• 160g = 2 exchanges\n• CHO content: 2 x 23g = 46g\n\n2. 250g Fresh Milk (1 Whole Milk Exchange):\n• 1 exchange of whole milk = 250g (contains 12g CHO)\n• CHO content: 1 x 12g = 12g\n\n3. 103g Ripe Mango (1 Fruit Exchange):\n• 1 exchange of fruit = approx. 100g (contains 10g CHO)\n• CHO content: 1 x 10g = 10g\n\n4. 45g Sauteed Squash (1 Vegetable B Exchange):\n• 1 exchange of cooked Vegetable B = 45g (contains 3g CHO)\n• CHO content: 1 x 3g = 3g (Note: The fat exchange added for sauteing oil contains 0g CHO).\n\n5. 90g Fried Chicken (3 Medium-Fat Meat Exchanges):\n• Meat exchanges contain 0g CHO.\n\nFinal CHO Summation:\n\n• Rice: 46g\n• Fresh Milk: 12g\n• Ripe Mango: 10g\n• Sauteed Squash: 3g\n• Fried Chicken: 0g\n• Total CHO Content: 46g + 12g + 10g + 3g = 71g","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 69 g, 73 g, 81 g"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:refer to the following information. based on the fel, the diet of a burn patient contains the following meal pattern for lunch: 90g fried chicken; 160g rice; 45g sauteed squash; 250g fresh milk; and 103g ripe mango. how much is the cho content of the meal?:::', 'refer to the following information. based on the fel, the diet of a burn patient contains the following meal pattern for lunch: 90g fried chicken; 160g rice; 45g sauteed squash; 250g fresh milk; and 103g ripe mango. how much is the cho content of the meal?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to the Philippine Food Exchange List (FEL), each food item is converted into exchange units to calculate its specific carbohydrate (CHO) content:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"71 g\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to the Philippine Food Exchange List (FEL), each food item is converted into exchange units to calculate its specific carbohydrate (CHO) content:\n\n1. 160g Rice (2 Rice Exchanges):\n• 1 exchange of cooked rice = 80g (contains 23g CHO)\n• 160g = 2 exchanges\n• CHO content: 2 x 23g = 46g\n\n2. 250g Fresh Milk (1 Whole Milk Exchange):\n• 1 exchange of whole milk = 250g (contains 12g CHO)\n• CHO content: 1 x 12g = 12g\n\n3. 103g Ripe Mango (1 Fruit Exchange):\n• 1 exchange of fruit = approx. 100g (contains 10g CHO)\n• CHO content: 1 x 10g = 10g\n\n4. 45g Sauteed Squash (1 Vegetable B Exchange):\n• 1 exchange of cooked Vegetable B = 45g (contains 3g CHO)\n• CHO content: 1 x 3g = 3g (Note: The fat exchange added for sauteing oil contains 0g CHO).\n\n5. 90g Fried Chicken (3 Medium-Fat Meat Exchanges):\n• Meat exchanges contain 0g CHO.\n\nFinal CHO Summation:\n\n• Rice: 46g\n• Fresh Milk: 12g\n• Ripe Mango: 10g\n• Sauteed Squash: 3g\n• Fried Chicken: 0g\n• Total CHO Content: 46g + 12g + 10g + 3g = 71g","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 69 g, 73 g, 81 g"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:if the actual cost of a whole cake is p500, having a yield of 10 slices, and selling price is p90 per slice, what is the food cost percentage?:::', 'if the actual cost of a whole cake is p500, having a yield of 10 slices, and selling price is p90 per slice, what is the food cost percentage?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"(500/(90*10))*100","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"55.6%\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"(500/(90*10))*100","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 5.56%, 18%, None of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:refer to the following information. based on the fel, the diet of a burn patient contains the following meal pattern for lunch: 90g fried chicken; 160g rice; 45g sauteed squash; 250g fresh milk; and 103g ripe mango. how much is the fat content of the meal?:::33 g', 'refer to the following information. based on the fel, the diet of a burn patient contains the following meal pattern for lunch: 90g fried chicken; 160g rice; 45g sauteed squash; 250g fresh milk; and 103g ripe mango. how much is the fat content of the meal?', '33 g', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to the Philippine Food Exchange List (FEL), each food item is converted into exchange units to calculate its specific macronutrient content. Here is the breakdown for each item in the lunch pattern:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"33 g\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to the Philippine Food Exchange List (FEL), each food item is converted into exchange units to calculate its specific macronutrient content. Here is the breakdown for each item in the lunch pattern:\n\n1. 160g Rice (2 Rice Exchanges):\n• 1 exchange of cooked rice = 80g\n• 160g = 2 exchanges\n• Fat content = 0g\n\n2. 250g Fresh Milk (1 Whole Milk Exchange):\n• 1 exchange of whole milk = 250g\n• Fat content = 10g\n\n3. 103g Ripe Mango (1 Fruit Exchange):\n• 1 exchange of ripe mango = 1 slice/portion (approx. 100g)\n• Fat content = 0g\n\n4. 45g Sauteed Squash (1 Vegetable Exchange + 1 Fat Exchange):\n• 1 exchange of cooked squash = 45g (0g fat)\n• Since it is sauteed, the FEL rules require adding 1 fat exchange for the cooking oil.\n• 1 fat exchange = 5g\n\n5. 90g Fried Chicken (3 Medium-Fat Meat Exchanges):\n• 1 exchange of cooked meat = 30g\n• 90g = 3 exchanges\n• Chicken with skin or fried chicken is classified under Medium-Fat Meat, which contains 6g of fat per exchange.\n• 3 exchanges x 6g = 18g\n\nFinal Fat Summation:\n\n• Rice: 0g\n• Fresh Milk: 10g\n• Ripe Mango: 0g\n• Sauteed Squash (Oil): 5g\n• Fried Chicken: 18g\n• Total Fat Content: 10g + 5g + 18g = 33g","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 27 g, 30 g, 35 g"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:refer to the following information. based on the fel, the diet of a burn patient contains the following meal pattern for lunch: 90g fried chicken; 160g rice; 45g sauteed squash; 250g fresh milk; and 103g ripe mango. how much is the fat content of the meal?:::', 'refer to the following information. based on the fel, the diet of a burn patient contains the following meal pattern for lunch: 90g fried chicken; 160g rice; 45g sauteed squash; 250g fresh milk; and 103g ripe mango. how much is the fat content of the meal?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to the Philippine Food Exchange List (FEL), each food item is converted into exchange units to calculate its specific macronutrient content. Here is the breakdown for each item in the lunch pattern:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"33 g\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to the Philippine Food Exchange List (FEL), each food item is converted into exchange units to calculate its specific macronutrient content. Here is the breakdown for each item in the lunch pattern:\n\n1. 160g Rice (2 Rice Exchanges):\n• 1 exchange of cooked rice = 80g\n• 160g = 2 exchanges\n• Fat content = 0g\n\n2. 250g Fresh Milk (1 Whole Milk Exchange):\n• 1 exchange of whole milk = 250g\n• Fat content = 10g\n\n3. 103g Ripe Mango (1 Fruit Exchange):\n• 1 exchange of ripe mango = 1 slice/portion (approx. 100g)\n• Fat content = 0g\n\n4. 45g Sauteed Squash (1 Vegetable Exchange + 1 Fat Exchange):\n• 1 exchange of cooked squash = 45g (0g fat)\n• Since it is sauteed, the FEL rules require adding 1 fat exchange for the cooking oil.\n• 1 fat exchange = 5g\n\n5. 90g Fried Chicken (3 Medium-Fat Meat Exchanges):\n• 1 exchange of cooked meat = 30g\n• 90g = 3 exchanges\n• Chicken with skin or fried chicken is classified under Medium-Fat Meat, which contains 6g of fat per exchange.\n• 3 exchanges x 6g = 18g\n\nFinal Fat Summation:\n\n• Rice: 0g\n• Fresh Milk: 10g\n• Ripe Mango: 0g\n• Sauteed Squash (Oil): 5g\n• Fried Chicken: 18g\n• Total Fat Content: 10g + 5g + 18g = 33g","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 27 g, 30 g, 35 g"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:refer to the following information. based on the fel, the diet of a burn patient contains the following meal pattern for lunch: 90g fried chicken; 160g rice; 45g sauteed squash; 250g fresh milk; and 103g ripe mango. how many kilocalories does the meal contain?:::730 kcal', 'refer to the following information. based on the fel, the diet of a burn patient contains the following meal pattern for lunch: 90g fried chicken; 160g rice; 45g sauteed squash; 250g fresh milk; and 103g ripe mango. how many kilocalories does the meal contain?', '730 kcal', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Step-by-Step Breakdown using the Food Exchange List (FEL)","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"730 kcal\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Step-by-Step Breakdown using the Food Exchange List (FEL)\n\nTo find the total energy value of this meal pattern, we break down each food item into its exact base exchanges and adjust for the preparation methods:\n\n1. 160g Rice (Cooked Rice):\n\t• Exchange value: 1 exchange of cooked rice is equal to 80g (providing 100 kcal).\n\t• 160g / 80g = 2 Rice Exchanges.\n\t• Energy Contribution: 2 * 100 kcal = 200 kcal.\n\n2. 250g Fresh Milk (Whole Milk):\n\t• Exchange value: 1 glass or 1 cup of whole fresh milk is equal to 250g (providing 170 kcal).\n\t• 250g / 250g = 1 Whole Milk Exchange.\n\t• Energy Contribution: 1 * 170 kcal = 170 kcal.\n\n3. 103g Ripe Mango:\n\t• Exchange value: 1 exchange of ripe mango is equal to 103g gross weight (providing 40 kcal).\n\t• 103g / 103g = 1 Fruit Exchange.\n\t• Energy Contribution: 1 * 40 kcal = 40 kcal.\n\n4. 45g Sauteed Squash (Vegetable B + Sauteing Fat):\n\t• Exchange value: 1 cooked vegetable exchange is equal to 1/2 cup or 45g (providing 16 kcal).\n\t• Preparation Modifier: Because the squash is sauteed, it adds 1 Fat Exchange (providing 45 kcal) for the cooking oil.\n\t• Energy Contribution: 16 kcal + 45 kcal = 61 kcal.\n\n5. 90g Fried Chicken (Low-Fat Meat + Frying Fats):\n\t• Exchange value: Chicken is classified as a Low-Fat Meat, where 1 exchange equals 30g cooked meat (providing 41 kcal). Therefore, 90g / 30g = 3 Low-Fat Meat Exchanges.\n\t• Preparation Modifier: Because it is fried, each of the 3 meat exchanges absorbs approximately 1 Fat Exchange (3 * 45 kcal = 135 kcal).\n\t• Energy Contribution: (3 * 41 kcal) + 135 kcal = 258 kcal.\n\nTotal Energy Summation\nTotal Calorie Count = 200 kcal + 170 kcal + 40 kcal + 61 kcal + 258 kcal = 729 kcal\n\nRounding off to the nearest multiple of five as standard practice in FEL board examination questions, 729 kcal yields exactly 730 kcal.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 650 kcal, 700 kcal, 775 kcal"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:refer to the following information. based on the fel, the diet of a burn patient contains the following meal pattern for lunch: 90g fried chicken; 160g rice; 45g sauteed squash; 250g fresh milk; and 103g ripe mango. how many kilocalories does the meal contain?:::', 'refer to the following information. based on the fel, the diet of a burn patient contains the following meal pattern for lunch: 90g fried chicken; 160g rice; 45g sauteed squash; 250g fresh milk; and 103g ripe mango. how many kilocalories does the meal contain?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Step-by-Step Breakdown using the Food Exchange List (FEL)","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"730 kcal\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Step-by-Step Breakdown using the Food Exchange List (FEL)\n\nTo find the total energy value of this meal pattern, we break down each food item into its exact base exchanges and adjust for the preparation methods:\n\n1. 160g Rice (Cooked Rice):\n\t• Exchange value: 1 exchange of cooked rice is equal to 80g (providing 100 kcal).\n\t• 160g / 80g = 2 Rice Exchanges.\n\t• Energy Contribution: 2 * 100 kcal = 200 kcal.\n\n2. 250g Fresh Milk (Whole Milk):\n\t• Exchange value: 1 glass or 1 cup of whole fresh milk is equal to 250g (providing 170 kcal).\n\t• 250g / 250g = 1 Whole Milk Exchange.\n\t• Energy Contribution: 1 * 170 kcal = 170 kcal.\n\n3. 103g Ripe Mango:\n\t• Exchange value: 1 exchange of ripe mango is equal to 103g gross weight (providing 40 kcal).\n\t• 103g / 103g = 1 Fruit Exchange.\n\t• Energy Contribution: 1 * 40 kcal = 40 kcal.\n\n4. 45g Sauteed Squash (Vegetable B + Sauteing Fat):\n\t• Exchange value: 1 cooked vegetable exchange is equal to 1/2 cup or 45g (providing 16 kcal).\n\t• Preparation Modifier: Because the squash is sauteed, it adds 1 Fat Exchange (providing 45 kcal) for the cooking oil.\n\t• Energy Contribution: 16 kcal + 45 kcal = 61 kcal.\n\n5. 90g Fried Chicken (Low-Fat Meat + Frying Fats):\n\t• Exchange value: Chicken is classified as a Low-Fat Meat, where 1 exchange equals 30g cooked meat (providing 41 kcal). Therefore, 90g / 30g = 3 Low-Fat Meat Exchanges.\n\t• Preparation Modifier: Because it is fried, each of the 3 meat exchanges absorbs approximately 1 Fat Exchange (3 * 45 kcal = 135 kcal).\n\t• Energy Contribution: (3 * 41 kcal) + 135 kcal = 258 kcal.\n\nTotal Energy Summation\nTotal Calorie Count = 200 kcal + 170 kcal + 40 kcal + 61 kcal + 258 kcal = 729 kcal\n\nRounding off to the nearest multiple of five as standard practice in FEL board examination questions, 729 kcal yields exactly 730 kcal.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 650 kcal, 700 kcal, 775 kcal"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:cholesterol level in food considered safe for daily consumption.:::300mg', 'cholesterol level in food considered safe for daily consumption.', '300mg', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"For many years, traditional clinical nutrition guidelines and board manuals (such as the American Heart Association and national dietary guidelines) established a strict upper limit of 300 mg of dietary cholesterol per day for healthy adults to reduce the risk of cardiovascular disease.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"300mg\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"For many years, traditional clinical nutrition guidelines and board manuals (such as the American Heart Association and national dietary guidelines) established a strict upper limit of 300 mg of dietary cholesterol per day for healthy adults to reduce the risk of cardiovascular disease.\n\nWhile modern guidelines have shifted to focusing more broadly on limiting saturated fats, the classic dietary limit taught and tested in academic question banks for a \"safe daily consumption level\" remains 300 mg.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 450mg, 400mg, 475mg"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:cholesterol level in food considered safe for daily consumption.:::', 'cholesterol level in food considered safe for daily consumption.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"For many years, traditional clinical nutrition guidelines and board manuals (such as the American Heart Association and national dietary guidelines) established a strict upper limit of 300 mg of dietary cholesterol per day for healthy adults to reduce the risk of cardiovascular disease.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"300mg\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"For many years, traditional clinical nutrition guidelines and board manuals (such as the American Heart Association and national dietary guidelines) established a strict upper limit of 300 mg of dietary cholesterol per day for healthy adults to reduce the risk of cardiovascular disease.\n\nWhile modern guidelines have shifted to focusing more broadly on limiting saturated fats, the classic dietary limit taught and tested in academic question banks for a \"safe daily consumption level\" remains 300 mg.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 450mg, 400mg, 475mg"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:diet management for a person with esophageal reflux.:::low fat, low cholesterol', 'diet management for a person with esophageal reflux.', 'low fat, low cholesterol', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dietary fat stimulates the release of cholecystokinin (CCK) from the duodenal mucosa. CCK induces relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES), increasing transient LES relaxations (TLESRs) and facilitating the retrograde flow of acidic gastric contents into the esophagus. Furthermore, high-fat meals delay gastric emptying, prolonging the period of high gastric volume and pressure that promotes reflux episodes. Restricting dietary fat directly attenuates postprandial CCK surges, preserves basal LES tone, and accelerates gastric transit.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"low fat, low cholesterol\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dietary fat stimulates the release of cholecystokinin (CCK) from the duodenal mucosa. CCK induces relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES), increasing transient LES relaxations (TLESRs) and facilitating the retrograde flow of acidic gastric contents into the esophagus. Furthermore, high-fat meals delay gastric emptying, prolonging the period of high gastric volume and pressure that promotes reflux episodes. Restricting dietary fat directly attenuates postprandial CCK surges, preserves basal LES tone, and accelerates gastric transit.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: liquid diet, high calorie, high protein, regular diet with small frequent meals"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:diet management for a person with esophageal reflux.:::', 'diet management for a person with esophageal reflux.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dietary fat stimulates the release of cholecystokinin (CCK) from the duodenal mucosa. CCK induces relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES), increasing transient LES relaxations (TLESRs) and facilitating the retrograde flow of acidic gastric contents into the esophagus. Furthermore, high-fat meals delay gastric emptying, prolonging the period of high gastric volume and pressure that promotes reflux episodes. Restricting dietary fat directly attenuates postprandial CCK surges, preserves basal LES tone, and accelerates gastric transit.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"low fat, low cholesterol\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dietary fat stimulates the release of cholecystokinin (CCK) from the duodenal mucosa. CCK induces relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES), increasing transient LES relaxations (TLESRs) and facilitating the retrograde flow of acidic gastric contents into the esophagus. Furthermore, high-fat meals delay gastric emptying, prolonging the period of high gastric volume and pressure that promotes reflux episodes. Restricting dietary fat directly attenuates postprandial CCK surges, preserves basal LES tone, and accelerates gastric transit.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: liquid diet, high calorie, high protein, regular diet with small frequent meals"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:malnutrition due to esophageal tumor is primarily related to::::inadequate intake', 'malnutrition due to esophageal tumor is primarily related to:', 'inadequate intake', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The defining hallmark of an esophageal tumor is dysphagia (progressive difficulty swallowing) caused by the physical growth of the tumor narrowing the lumen of the esophagus.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"inadequate intake\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The defining hallmark of an esophageal tumor is dysphagia (progressive difficulty swallowing) caused by the physical growth of the tumor narrowing the lumen of the esophagus.\n\nWhile cancer cachexia can induce a catabolic state or alter systemic metabolism over time, the immediate and primary driver of severe protein-calorie malnutrition in these patients at the time of diagnosis is the simple, mechanical inability to physically swallow and consume enough food. This results in massive, structural inadequate food and fluid intake.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: decreased absorption, catabolic state, increased energy needs"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:malnutrition due to esophageal tumor is primarily related to::::', 'malnutrition due to esophageal tumor is primarily related to:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The defining hallmark of an esophageal tumor is dysphagia (progressive difficulty swallowing) caused by the physical growth of the tumor narrowing the lumen of the esophagus.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"inadequate intake\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The defining hallmark of an esophageal tumor is dysphagia (progressive difficulty swallowing) caused by the physical growth of the tumor narrowing the lumen of the esophagus.\n\nWhile cancer cachexia can induce a catabolic state or alter systemic metabolism over time, the immediate and primary driver of severe protein-calorie malnutrition in these patients at the time of diagnosis is the simple, mechanical inability to physically swallow and consume enough food. This results in massive, structural inadequate food and fluid intake.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: decreased absorption, catabolic state, increased energy needs"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:flatulence, cramps, or diabetes may be some unpleasant side effects upon the initiation of this diet::::high-fiber diet', 'flatulence, cramps, or diabetes may be some unpleasant side effects upon the initiation of this diet:', 'high-fiber diet', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When a person suddenly initiates a high-fiber diet, the digestive tract requires a transition period to adapt. The human body lacks the enzymes to digest dietary fiber, so it passes intact into the large intestine. There, resident gut bacteria ferment the fiber. A sudden influx of fiber causes rapid, aggressive bacterial fermentation, which produces large amounts of gas. This trapped gas directly results in flatulence, abdominal bloating, and painful cramps.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"high-fiber diet\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When a person suddenly initiates a high-fiber diet, the digestive tract requires a transition period to adapt. The human body lacks the enzymes to digest dietary fiber, so it passes intact into the large intestine. There, resident gut bacteria ferment the fiber. A sudden influx of fiber causes rapid, aggressive bacterial fermentation, which produces large amounts of gas. This trapped gas directly results in flatulence, abdominal bloating, and painful cramps.\n\nWhile fiber does not cause diabetes, a high-fiber diet is a primary clinical intervention utilized for the management of Diabetes Mellitus. Soluble fiber creates a gel-like substance in the gut that slows down the absorption of glucose, preventing rapid post-meal blood sugar spikes and improving insulin sensitivity.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: fiber-restricted diet, soft diet, elemental diet"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:patients taking medicines with mao inhibitors should be advised this diet::::tyramine-restricted', 'patients taking medicines with mao inhibitors should be advised this diet:', 'tyramine-restricted', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAOIs) are a class of medications used primarily as antidepressants. They work by blocking the enzyme monoamine oxidase, which normally breaks down neurotransmitters as well as tyramine (an amino acid byproduct found in aged and fermented foods).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"tyramine-restricted\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAOIs) are a class of medications used primarily as antidepressants. They work by blocking the enzyme monoamine oxidase, which normally breaks down neurotransmitters as well as tyramine (an amino acid byproduct found in aged and fermented foods).\n\nIf a patient taking an MAOI consumes high-tyramine foods, the excess tyramine cannot be broken down. This causes a massive release of norepinephrine, leading to severe vasoconstriction and a dangerous blood pressure spike known as a hypertensive crisis. Therefore, these patients must strictly follow a tyramine-restricted diet.\n\nAged cheeses (like cheddar, blue, or swiss), cured or smoked meats (like salami and ham), soy sauce, tofu, fermented items (like sauerkraut), tap beer, and red wine.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: protein-restricted, fat-restricted, phenylalanine-restricted"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:patients taking medicines with mao inhibitors should be advised this diet::::', 'patients taking medicines with mao inhibitors should be advised this diet:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAOIs) are a class of medications used primarily as antidepressants. They work by blocking the enzyme monoamine oxidase, which normally breaks down neurotransmitters as well as tyramine (an amino acid byproduct found in aged and fermented foods).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"tyramine-restricted\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAOIs) are a class of medications used primarily as antidepressants. They work by blocking the enzyme monoamine oxidase, which normally breaks down neurotransmitters as well as tyramine (an amino acid byproduct found in aged and fermented foods).\n\nIf a patient taking an MAOI consumes high-tyramine foods, the excess tyramine cannot be broken down. This causes a massive release of norepinephrine, leading to severe vasoconstriction and a dangerous blood pressure spike known as a hypertensive crisis. Therefore, these patients must strictly follow a tyramine-restricted diet.\n\nAged cheeses (like cheddar, blue, or swiss), cured or smoked meats (like salami and ham), soy sauce, tofu, fermented items (like sauerkraut), tap beer, and red wine.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: protein-restricted, fat-restricted, phenylalanine-restricted"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:exogenous sources of cholesterol include::::dairy and glandular only', 'exogenous sources of cholesterol include:', 'dairy and glandular only', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• Exogenous cholesterol refers to cholesterol that comes from outside the body through the food we eat.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"dairy and glandular only\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• Exogenous cholesterol refers to cholesterol that comes from outside the body through the food we eat.\n• The Golden Rule of Cholesterol: Cholesterol is only found in animal-based foods. Plants do not synthesize cholesterol.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: dairy products, glandular organs, nuts"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:exogenous sources of cholesterol include::::', 'exogenous sources of cholesterol include:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• Exogenous cholesterol refers to cholesterol that comes from outside the body through the food we eat.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"dairy and glandular only\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• Exogenous cholesterol refers to cholesterol that comes from outside the body through the food we eat.\n• The Golden Rule of Cholesterol: Cholesterol is only found in animal-based foods. Plants do not synthesize cholesterol.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: dairy products, glandular organs, nuts"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a gradual transition from an iv feeding to an oral diet is primarily designed to?::::ensure that nutrient needs will continue to be met', 'a gradual transition from an iv feeding to an oral diet is primarily designed to?:', 'ensure that nutrient needs will continue to be met', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Transitional feeding is carefully structured to manage a patient''s progression from intravenous (parenteral) nutrition to eating by mouth. The foremost priority is ensuring that the patient doesn''t suffer from a drop in required calories, protein, fluids, or micronutrients while they gradually re-learn to chew, swallow, or digest regular food.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"ensure that nutrient needs will continue to be met\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Transitional feeding is carefully structured to manage a patient''s progression from intravenous (parenteral) nutrition to eating by mouth. The foremost priority is ensuring that the patient doesn''t suffer from a drop in required calories, protein, fluids, or micronutrients while they gradually re-learn to chew, swallow, or digest regular food.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: improve the appetite, prevent hypoglycemia, prevent apprehension about eating"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a gradual transition from an iv feeding to an oral diet is primarily designed to?::::', 'a gradual transition from an iv feeding to an oral diet is primarily designed to?:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Transitional feeding is carefully structured to manage a patient''s progression from intravenous (parenteral) nutrition to eating by mouth. The foremost priority is ensuring that the patient doesn''t suffer from a drop in required calories, protein, fluids, or micronutrients while they gradually re-learn to chew, swallow, or digest regular food.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"ensure that nutrient needs will continue to be met\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Transitional feeding is carefully structured to manage a patient''s progression from intravenous (parenteral) nutrition to eating by mouth. The foremost priority is ensuring that the patient doesn''t suffer from a drop in required calories, protein, fluids, or micronutrients while they gradually re-learn to chew, swallow, or digest regular food.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: improve the appetite, prevent hypoglycemia, prevent apprehension about eating"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:clients undergoing colostomies and ileostomies may temporarily receive a low-fiber, bland diet to::::help the stoma heal', 'clients undergoing colostomies and ileostomies may temporarily receive a low-fiber, bland diet to:', 'help the stoma heal', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"After colostomy or ileostomy surgery, the bowel and the newly formed stoma are swollen, inflamed, and healing from surgical trauma. A low-fiber, bland diet minimizes irritation to the intestinal lining, reducing the workload on the gut so it can heal efficiently.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"help the stoma heal\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"After colostomy or ileostomy surgery, the bowel and the newly formed stoma are swollen, inflamed, and healing from surgical trauma. A low-fiber, bland diet minimizes irritation to the intestinal lining, reducing the workload on the gut so it can heal efficiently.\n\nHigh-fiber foods leave a large amount of indigestible residue (bulk) in the digestive tract. Because the internal lumen of a new stoma is narrowed due to post-operative swelling, a high-fiber intake poses a strict risk of causing a painful mechanical bowel blockage.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: increase intestinal motility, prevent malabsorption, prevent mineral deficiencies"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:clients undergoing colostomies and ileostomies may temporarily receive a low-fiber, bland diet to::::', 'clients undergoing colostomies and ileostomies may temporarily receive a low-fiber, bland diet to:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"After colostomy or ileostomy surgery, the bowel and the newly formed stoma are swollen, inflamed, and healing from surgical trauma. A low-fiber, bland diet minimizes irritation to the intestinal lining, reducing the workload on the gut so it can heal efficiently.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"help the stoma heal\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"After colostomy or ileostomy surgery, the bowel and the newly formed stoma are swollen, inflamed, and healing from surgical trauma. A low-fiber, bland diet minimizes irritation to the intestinal lining, reducing the workload on the gut so it can heal efficiently.\n\nHigh-fiber foods leave a large amount of indigestible residue (bulk) in the digestive tract. Because the internal lumen of a new stoma is narrowed due to post-operative swelling, a high-fiber intake poses a strict risk of causing a painful mechanical bowel blockage.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: increase intestinal motility, prevent malabsorption, prevent mineral deficiencies"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:diet therapy for chronic pancreatitis may include all of the following except::::a fat-restricted diet', 'diet therapy for chronic pancreatitis may include all of the following except:', 'a fat-restricted diet', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"International guidelines (such as ESPEN clinical guidelines) no longer recommend routine, strict dietary fat restriction for chronic pancreatitis. Because these patients suffer from severe malabsorption and weight loss, restricting fat overly constricts their caloric intake and worsens malnutrition. Instead, a normal, balanced physiological diet is prescribed in tandem with proper enzyme dosing. Fat restriction is only used as a temporary, short-term measure if painful fatty stools (steatorrhea) cannot be managed by medications.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"a fat-restricted diet\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"International guidelines (such as ESPEN clinical guidelines) no longer recommend routine, strict dietary fat restriction for chronic pancreatitis. Because these patients suffer from severe malabsorption and weight loss, restricting fat overly constricts their caloric intake and worsens malnutrition. Instead, a normal, balanced physiological diet is prescribed in tandem with proper enzyme dosing. Fat restriction is only used as a temporary, short-term measure if painful fatty stools (steatorrhea) cannot be managed by medications.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: enzyme replacements, small meals, a diet to control blood glucose"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:flatulence, cramps, or diabetes may be some unpleasant side effects upon the initiation of this diet::::', 'flatulence, cramps, or diabetes may be some unpleasant side effects upon the initiation of this diet:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When a person suddenly initiates a high-fiber diet, the digestive tract requires a transition period to adapt. The human body lacks the enzymes to digest dietary fiber, so it passes intact into the large intestine. There, resident gut bacteria ferment the fiber. A sudden influx of fiber causes rapid, aggressive bacterial fermentation, which produces large amounts of gas. This trapped gas directly results in flatulence, abdominal bloating, and painful cramps.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"high-fiber diet\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When a person suddenly initiates a high-fiber diet, the digestive tract requires a transition period to adapt. The human body lacks the enzymes to digest dietary fiber, so it passes intact into the large intestine. There, resident gut bacteria ferment the fiber. A sudden influx of fiber causes rapid, aggressive bacterial fermentation, which produces large amounts of gas. This trapped gas directly results in flatulence, abdominal bloating, and painful cramps.\n\nWhile fiber does not cause diabetes, a high-fiber diet is a primary clinical intervention utilized for the management of Diabetes Mellitus. Soluble fiber creates a gel-like substance in the gut that slows down the absorption of glucose, preventing rapid post-meal blood sugar spikes and improving insulin sensitivity.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: fiber-restricted diet, soft diet, elemental diet"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:diet therapy for chronic pancreatitis may include all of the following except::::', 'diet therapy for chronic pancreatitis may include all of the following except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"International guidelines (such as ESPEN clinical guidelines) no longer recommend routine, strict dietary fat restriction for chronic pancreatitis. Because these patients suffer from severe malabsorption and weight loss, restricting fat overly constricts their caloric intake and worsens malnutrition. Instead, a normal, balanced physiological diet is prescribed in tandem with proper enzyme dosing. Fat restriction is only used as a temporary, short-term measure if painful fatty stools (steatorrhea) cannot be managed by medications.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"a fat-restricted diet\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"International guidelines (such as ESPEN clinical guidelines) no longer recommend routine, strict dietary fat restriction for chronic pancreatitis. Because these patients suffer from severe malabsorption and weight loss, restricting fat overly constricts their caloric intake and worsens malnutrition. Instead, a normal, balanced physiological diet is prescribed in tandem with proper enzyme dosing. Fat restriction is only used as a temporary, short-term measure if painful fatty stools (steatorrhea) cannot be managed by medications.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: enzyme replacements, small meals, a diet to control blood glucose"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following may affect the nutrient needs of people with inflammatory bowel disease except::::dumping syndrome', 'the following may affect the nutrient needs of people with inflammatory bowel disease except:', 'dumping syndrome', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dumping syndrome is a complication that occurs after gastric surgeries (like a partial or total gastrectomy) where food moves too rapidly from the stomach into the small intestine. It does not directly result from or impact the nutrient needs of Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"dumping syndrome\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dumping syndrome is a complication that occurs after gastric surgeries (like a partial or total gastrectomy) where food moves too rapidly from the stomach into the small intestine. It does not directly result from or impact the nutrient needs of Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD).\n\n\t• Medications: Common IBD drugs like corticosteroids lower calcium and vitamin D absorption, while others like sulfasalazine impair folate absorption, altering daily nutrient requirements.\n\t• Steatorrhea: Excessive fat in the stool (common in Crohn''s disease due to bile acid malabsorption) causes a severe loss of fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K) and calories, increasing nutritional requirements.\n\t• Fistulas: These abnormal tissue tunnels can leak digestive fluids, causing high-volume losses of protein, electrolytes, and fluids, which drastically spike a patient''s nutrient needs.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: steatorrhea, medications, fistulas"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb)
