('mc:areas wherein closely related task are done by a person or persons.:::work center', 'areas wherein closely related task are done by a person or persons.', 'Work center', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Work center is the basic unit in a layout. Thus, these are areas wherein closely related tasks are done by a person or persons.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Work center\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Work center is the basic unit in a layout. Thus, these are areas wherein closely related tasks are done by a person or persons.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Work area, Section, Layout"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:areas wherein closely related task are done by a person or persons.:::', 'areas wherein closely related task are done by a person or persons.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Work center is the basic unit in a layout. Thus, these are areas wherein closely related tasks are done by a person or persons.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Work center\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Work center is the basic unit in a layout. Thus, these are areas wherein closely related tasks are done by a person or persons.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Work area, Section, Layout"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:system that identifies specific hazard and measures for their control to ensure the safety of food.:::', 'system that identifies specific hazard and measures for their control to ensure the safety of food.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"HACCP stands for Hazard Analysis and Critical Control Points which is a systematic system that identifies specific hazards and measures for their control to ensure food safety. This focuses more on prevention rather than relying mainly on end-product testing.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"HACCP\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"HACCP stands for Hazard Analysis and Critical Control Points which is a systematic system that identifies specific hazards and measures for their control to ensure food safety. This focuses more on prevention rather than relying mainly on end-product testing.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Codex, Food safety, GMP"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which is not a factor choice of distribution system?:::consumer''s capacity to pay', 'which is not a factor choice of distribution system?', 'Consumer''s capacity to pay', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In foodservice systems management, selecting the appropriate distribution and delivery system (the method of getting cooked food from the kitchen to the point of service) is an operational and architectural decision. It is dictated by structural, physical, and logistical factors within the establishment:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Consumer''s capacity to pay\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In foodservice systems management, selecting the appropriate distribution and delivery system (the method of getting cooked food from the kitchen to the point of service) is an operational and architectural decision. It is dictated by structural, physical, and logistical factors within the establishment:\n\n• Type of foodservice system: Whether an operation is Conventional, Commissary, Ready-Prepared, or Assembly/Serve directly dictates the assembly and distribution infrastructure required.\n• Size and layout of the facility: A multi-story hospital or large campus requires decentralized distribution (e.g., using rethermalization carts or satellite pantries), whereas a compact single-room diner can use a simple centralized counter distribution system.\n• Style of service: Self-service cafeterias, tray service, banquets, and vending machines each require distinct holding, temperature control, and physical transport distribution methods.\n\nWhile the target consumer''s financial capacity heavily influences menu pricing, decor tiering, food service classifications, and ingredient sourcing, it does not mechanically alter or dictate the internal engineering or logistical choice of the physical distribution system inside the facility.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Type of foodservice system, Size and lay out of facility, Style of service"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:another name for family style of service.:::', 'another name for family style of service.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Family or compromise style of service is a compromise between the Russian and English styles; some foods are served directly from the kitchen on individual dishes and some are served at the table. It is best used in small groups, not more than eight.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Compromise\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Family or compromise style of service is a compromise between the Russian and English styles; some foods are served directly from the kitchen on individual dishes and some are served at the table. It is best used in small groups, not more than eight.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Continental, English, Apartment"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which is not a factor choice of distribution system?:::', 'which is not a factor choice of distribution system?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In foodservice systems management, selecting the appropriate distribution and delivery system (the method of getting cooked food from the kitchen to the point of service) is an operational and architectural decision. It is dictated by structural, physical, and logistical factors within the establishment:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Consumer''s capacity to pay\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In foodservice systems management, selecting the appropriate distribution and delivery system (the method of getting cooked food from the kitchen to the point of service) is an operational and architectural decision. It is dictated by structural, physical, and logistical factors within the establishment:\n\n• Type of foodservice system: Whether an operation is Conventional, Commissary, Ready-Prepared, or Assembly/Serve directly dictates the assembly and distribution infrastructure required.\n• Size and layout of the facility: A multi-story hospital or large campus requires decentralized distribution (e.g., using rethermalization carts or satellite pantries), whereas a compact single-room diner can use a simple centralized counter distribution system.\n• Style of service: Self-service cafeterias, tray service, banquets, and vending machines each require distinct holding, temperature control, and physical transport distribution methods.\n\nWhile the target consumer''s financial capacity heavily influences menu pricing, decor tiering, food service classifications, and ingredient sourcing, it does not mechanically alter or dictate the internal engineering or logistical choice of the physical distribution system inside the facility.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Type of foodservice system, Size and lay out of facility, Style of service"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:income less cost of good sold is known as the::::gross profit', 'income less cost of good sold is known as the:', 'Gross profit', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Gross Profit = Sales/Income - Cost of Goods Sold (COGS)","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Gross profit\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Gross Profit = Sales/Income - Cost of Goods Sold (COGS)\n\nTo arrive at Net Profit/Loss, the operating expenses and other applicable expenses (such as interest and taxes) are then deducted from the gross profit: Sales/Income – COGS = Gross Profit \n\nGross Profit – Operating and Other Expenses = Net Profit/Loss","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Net profit/loss, Food cost, Prime cost"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:income less cost of good sold is known as the::::', 'income less cost of good sold is known as the:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Gross Profit = Sales/Income - Cost of Goods Sold (COGS)","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Gross profit\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Gross Profit = Sales/Income - Cost of Goods Sold (COGS)\n\nTo arrive at Net Profit/Loss, the operating expenses and other applicable expenses (such as interest and taxes) are then deducted from the gross profit: Sales/Income – COGS = Gross Profit \n\nGross Profit – Operating and Other Expenses = Net Profit/Loss","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Net profit/loss, Food cost, Prime cost"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:system that identifies specific hazard and measures for their control to ensure the safety of food.:::haccp', 'system that identifies specific hazard and measures for their control to ensure the safety of food.', 'HACCP', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"HACCP stands for Hazard Analysis and Critical Control Points which is a systematic system that identifies specific hazards and measures for their control to ensure food safety. This focuses more on prevention rather than relying mainly on end-product testing.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"HACCP\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"HACCP stands for Hazard Analysis and Critical Control Points which is a systematic system that identifies specific hazards and measures for their control to ensure food safety. This focuses more on prevention rather than relying mainly on end-product testing.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Codex, Food safety, GMP"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:another name for family style of service.:::compromise', 'another name for family style of service.', 'Compromise', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Family or compromise style of service is a compromise between the Russian and English styles; some foods are served directly from the kitchen on individual dishes and some are served at the table. It is best used in small groups, not more than eight.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Compromise\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Family or compromise style of service is a compromise between the Russian and English styles; some foods are served directly from the kitchen on individual dishes and some are served at the table. It is best used in small groups, not more than eight.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Continental, English, Apartment"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb)
