('mc:it is known as anti-hemolytic vitamin.:::', 'it is known as anti-hemolytic vitamin.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Vitamin E is historically recognized as the anti-hemolytic vitamin because it protects the cell membranes of red blood cells (erythrocytes) from oxidative damage caused by free radicals. Without adequate Vitamin E, the lipid bilayer undergoes lipid peroxidation, making the cells fragile and causing them to rupture easily (hemolytic anemia).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vitamin E\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Vitamin E is historically recognized as the anti-hemolytic vitamin because it protects the cell membranes of red blood cells (erythrocytes) from oxidative damage caused by free radicals. Without adequate Vitamin E, the lipid bilayer undergoes lipid peroxidation, making the cells fragile and causing them to rupture easily (hemolytic anemia).\n\n• Vitamin K is the anti-hemorrhagic vitamin (prevents bleeding/hemorrhage), which is a common point of confusion.\n• Folic acid deficiency leads to megaloblastic anemia, not hemolytic anemia.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Folic acid, Biotin, Vitamin K"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:vitamins known to lower the levels of homocysteine in the blood, a byproduct of methionine metabolism and a known risk factor to heart disease.:::vit b2, b6, b12', 'vitamins known to lower the levels of homocysteine in the blood, a byproduct of methionine metabolism and a known risk factor to heart disease.', 'Vit B2, B6, B12', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Vitamin B2 (Riboflavin): Serves as a key cofactor for the methylenetetrahydrofolate reductase (MTHFR) enzyme. This enzyme generates the active folate necessary to drive the remethylation of homocysteine.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vit B2, B6, B12\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Vitamin B2 (Riboflavin): Serves as a key cofactor for the methylenetetrahydrofolate reductase (MTHFR) enzyme. This enzyme generates the active folate necessary to drive the remethylation of homocysteine.\n\nVitamin B6 (Pyridoxine): Acts as a cofactor for cystathionine β-synthase (CBS), driving the transsulfuration pathway that converts homocysteine into cysteine.\n\nVitamin B12 (Cobalamin): Functions as a critical cofactor for methionine synthase, an enzyme that remethylates homocysteine back into methionine.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vit B2, B5, B9, Vit B3, B5, B7, Vit B3, B7, B12"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:vitamins known to lower the levels of homocysteine in the blood, a byproduct of methionine metabolism and a known risk factor to heart disease.:::', 'vitamins known to lower the levels of homocysteine in the blood, a byproduct of methionine metabolism and a known risk factor to heart disease.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Vitamin B2 (Riboflavin): Serves as a key cofactor for the methylenetetrahydrofolate reductase (MTHFR) enzyme. This enzyme generates the active folate necessary to drive the remethylation of homocysteine.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vit B2, B6, B12\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Vitamin B2 (Riboflavin): Serves as a key cofactor for the methylenetetrahydrofolate reductase (MTHFR) enzyme. This enzyme generates the active folate necessary to drive the remethylation of homocysteine.\n\nVitamin B6 (Pyridoxine): Acts as a cofactor for cystathionine β-synthase (CBS), driving the transsulfuration pathway that converts homocysteine into cysteine.\n\nVitamin B12 (Cobalamin): Functions as a critical cofactor for methionine synthase, an enzyme that remethylates homocysteine back into methionine.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vit B2, B5, B9, Vit B3, B5, B7, Vit B3, B7, B12"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:it is the pressure exerted by plasma proteins and keeps the fluid inside the blood.:::oncotic pressure', 'it is the pressure exerted by plasma proteins and keeps the fluid inside the blood.', 'Oncotic pressure', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Oncotic pressure is a colloid pressure that occurs at the venular end of the blood capillaries that prevents fluid from leaving and helps removing metabolic wastes from the tissues.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Oncotic pressure\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Oncotic pressure is a colloid pressure that occurs at the venular end of the blood capillaries that prevents fluid from leaving and helps removing metabolic wastes from the tissues.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Hydrostatic pressure, Osmotic pressure"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
