('mc:high intake of this mineral decreases the absorption of copper.:::iron', 'high intake of this mineral decreases the absorption of copper.', 'Iron', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"High dietary intake or supplementation of iron can competitively inhibit the intestinal absorption of copper (sharing pathways like DMT1 and CTR1).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Iron\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"High dietary intake or supplementation of iron can competitively inhibit the intestinal absorption of copper (sharing pathways like DMT1 and CTR1).\n\nExcessive iron levels can lead to a reduction in copper status and potentially cause copper deficiency.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Folic acid, Niacin, Zinc"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
