('mc:an antioxidant that works with vit c to prevent free radical formation and protects cell membranes against lipid peroxidation.:::vit e', 'an antioxidant that works with vit c to prevent free radical formation and protects cell membranes against lipid peroxidation.', 'Vit E', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Vitamin E maintains the intracellular membrane integrity by being an antioxidant and a free-radical scavenger, thus cell membranes are protected against lipid peroxidation.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vit E\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Vitamin E maintains the intracellular membrane integrity by being an antioxidant and a free-radical scavenger, thus cell membranes are protected against lipid peroxidation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vit B12, Iron"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:an antioxidant that works with vit c to prevent free radical formation and protects cell membranes against lipid peroxidation.:::', 'an antioxidant that works with vit c to prevent free radical formation and protects cell membranes against lipid peroxidation.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Vitamin E maintains the intracellular membrane integrity by being an antioxidant and a free-radical scavenger, thus cell membranes are protected against lipid peroxidation.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vit E\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Vitamin E maintains the intracellular membrane integrity by being an antioxidant and a free-radical scavenger, thus cell membranes are protected against lipid peroxidation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vit B12, Iron"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is not a primary function of vitamin b12?:::direct antioxidant protection of cell membranes', 'which of the following is not a primary function of vitamin b12?', 'Direct antioxidant protection of cell membranes', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Direct antioxidant protection of cell membranes is a primary function of lipid-soluble antioxidants like Vitamin E, which physically integrate into cell membranes to prevent lipid peroxidation. It is not a primary function of Vitamin B12.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Direct antioxidant protection of cell membranes\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Direct antioxidant protection of cell membranes is a primary function of lipid-soluble antioxidants like Vitamin E, which physically integrate into cell membranes to prevent lipid peroxidation. It is not a primary function of Vitamin B12.\n\n• Normal neurological function is a primary function of Vitamin B12, as it is directly required for the synthesis and maintenance of the myelin sheath surrounding nerve cells.\n• DNA synthesis is a primary function of Vitamin B12, where it serves as an indispensable coenzyme alongside folate in nucleotide production.\n• Normal red blood cell formation is a primary function of Vitamin B12, and a deficiency directly halts proper erythrocyte maturation, causing megaloblastic anemia.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: DNA synthesis, Normal neurological function, Normal red blood cell formation"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is not a primary function of vitamin b12?:::', 'which of the following is not a primary function of vitamin b12?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Direct antioxidant protection of cell membranes is a primary function of lipid-soluble antioxidants like Vitamin E, which physically integrate into cell membranes to prevent lipid peroxidation. It is not a primary function of Vitamin B12.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Direct antioxidant protection of cell membranes\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Direct antioxidant protection of cell membranes is a primary function of lipid-soluble antioxidants like Vitamin E, which physically integrate into cell membranes to prevent lipid peroxidation. It is not a primary function of Vitamin B12.\n\n• Normal neurological function is a primary function of Vitamin B12, as it is directly required for the synthesis and maintenance of the myelin sheath surrounding nerve cells.\n• DNA synthesis is a primary function of Vitamin B12, where it serves as an indispensable coenzyme alongside folate in nucleotide production.\n• Normal red blood cell formation is a primary function of Vitamin B12, and a deficiency directly halts proper erythrocyte maturation, causing megaloblastic anemia.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: DNA synthesis, Normal neurological function, Normal red blood cell formation"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which vitamin is synthesized in the skin from 7-dehydrocholesterol following exposure to ultraviolet b radiation?:::vitamin d3', 'which vitamin is synthesized in the skin from 7-dehydrocholesterol following exposure to ultraviolet b radiation?', 'Vitamin D3', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Vitamin D3 (Cholecalciferol) is specifically synthesized endogenously when human skin is exposed to ultraviolet B (UVB) radiation. The UVB light photolyzes 7-dehydrocholesterol (provitamin D3) in the plasma membrane of epidermal keratinocytes to form previtamin D3, which then thermally isomerizes into stable vitamin D3.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vitamin D3\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Vitamin D3 (Cholecalciferol) is specifically synthesized endogenously when human skin is exposed to ultraviolet B (UVB) radiation. The UVB light photolyzes 7-dehydrocholesterol (provitamin D3) in the plasma membrane of epidermal keratinocytes to form previtamin D3, which then thermally isomerizes into stable vitamin D3.\n\n• Vitamin D2 (Ergocalciferol) is a plant- and fungi-derived analog formed when the plant sterol ergosterol is exposed to UV light. It is not synthesized naturally in human skin.\n• Vitamins B6 and B12 are water-soluble nutrients that cannot be synthesized by human tissues upon solar exposure and must be obtained through the diet.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vitamin B12, Vitamin D2, Vitamin B6"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which vitamin is synthesized in the skin from 7-dehydrocholesterol following exposure to ultraviolet b radiation?:::', 'which vitamin is synthesized in the skin from 7-dehydrocholesterol following exposure to ultraviolet b radiation?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Vitamin D3 (Cholecalciferol) is specifically synthesized endogenously when human skin is exposed to ultraviolet B (UVB) radiation. The UVB light photolyzes 7-dehydrocholesterol (provitamin D3) in the plasma membrane of epidermal keratinocytes to form previtamin D3, which then thermally isomerizes into stable vitamin D3.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vitamin D3\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Vitamin D3 (Cholecalciferol) is specifically synthesized endogenously when human skin is exposed to ultraviolet B (UVB) radiation. The UVB light photolyzes 7-dehydrocholesterol (provitamin D3) in the plasma membrane of epidermal keratinocytes to form previtamin D3, which then thermally isomerizes into stable vitamin D3.\n\n• Vitamin D2 (Ergocalciferol) is a plant- and fungi-derived analog formed when the plant sterol ergosterol is exposed to UV light. It is not synthesized naturally in human skin.\n• Vitamins B6 and B12 are water-soluble nutrients that cannot be synthesized by human tissues upon solar exposure and must be obtained through the diet.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vitamin B12, Vitamin D2, Vitamin B6"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which b vitamin contains sulfur in its chemical structure?:::thiamin (b1)', 'which b vitamin contains sulfur in its chemical structure?', 'Thiamin (B1)', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Thiamin (Vitamin B1) contains a thiazole ring and a pyrimidine ring linked by a methylene bridge. The thiazole ring explicitly incorporates a sulfur atom into its chemical structure.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Thiamin (B1)\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Thiamin (Vitamin B1) contains a thiazole ring and a pyrimidine ring linked by a methylene bridge. The thiazole ring explicitly incorporates a sulfur atom into its chemical structure.\n\n• Riboflavin (B2), Pyridoxine (B6), and Niacin (B3) do not contain sulfur atoms in their molecular formulas.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Pyridoxine (B6), Niacin (B3), Riboflavin (B2)"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which b vitamin contains sulfur in its chemical structure?:::', 'which b vitamin contains sulfur in its chemical structure?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Thiamin (Vitamin B1) contains a thiazole ring and a pyrimidine ring linked by a methylene bridge. The thiazole ring explicitly incorporates a sulfur atom into its chemical structure.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Thiamin (B1)\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Thiamin (Vitamin B1) contains a thiazole ring and a pyrimidine ring linked by a methylene bridge. The thiazole ring explicitly incorporates a sulfur atom into its chemical structure.\n\n• Riboflavin (B2), Pyridoxine (B6), and Niacin (B3) do not contain sulfur atoms in their molecular formulas.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Pyridoxine (B6), Niacin (B3), Riboflavin (B2)"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which form of vitamin k is the major dietary form found in green leafy vegetables?:::phylloquinone (k1)', 'which form of vitamin k is the major dietary form found in green leafy vegetables?', 'Phylloquinone (K1)', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Phylloquinone (Vitamin K1) is the primary plant-derived form of vitamin K and serves as the major dietary source. It is found in high concentrations within the chloroplasts of dark, green leafy vegetables (such as spinach, kale, and collards) where it is directly involved in photosynthesis.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Phylloquinone (K1)\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Phylloquinone (Vitamin K1) is the primary plant-derived form of vitamin K and serves as the major dietary source. It is found in high concentrations within the chloroplasts of dark, green leafy vegetables (such as spinach, kale, and collards) where it is directly involved in photosynthesis.\n\n• Menaquinone (Vitamin K2) is primarily synthesized by bacteria and is found in fermented foods or animal products.\n• Menadione (K3) is a synthetic, lab-made analogue typically restricted to animal feed due to potential toxicity in humans.\n• Ergocalciferol (D2) is an entirely different nutrient (Vitamin D) derived from plants and fungi.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Menaquinone (K2), Menadione (K3), Ergocalciferol (D2)"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which form of vitamin k is the major dietary form found in green leafy vegetables?:::', 'which form of vitamin k is the major dietary form found in green leafy vegetables?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Phylloquinone (Vitamin K1) is the primary plant-derived form of vitamin K and serves as the major dietary source. It is found in high concentrations within the chloroplasts of dark, green leafy vegetables (such as spinach, kale, and collards) where it is directly involved in photosynthesis.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Phylloquinone (K1)\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Phylloquinone (Vitamin K1) is the primary plant-derived form of vitamin K and serves as the major dietary source. It is found in high concentrations within the chloroplasts of dark, green leafy vegetables (such as spinach, kale, and collards) where it is directly involved in photosynthesis.\n\n• Menaquinone (Vitamin K2) is primarily synthesized by bacteria and is found in fermented foods or animal products.\n• Menadione (K3) is a synthetic, lab-made analogue typically restricted to animal feed due to potential toxicity in humans.\n• Ergocalciferol (D2) is an entirely different nutrient (Vitamin D) derived from plants and fungi.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Menaquinone (K2), Menadione (K3), Ergocalciferol (D2)"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:food microorganisms grow well at::::4-60c', 'food microorganisms grow well at:', '4-60C', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In food safety, the standard Temperature Danger Zone expressed in Celsius is 4°C to 60°C (which directly translates to the standard 40°F to 140°F range traditionally used by agencies like the USDA). Within this specific window, bacteria and other foodborne microorganisms encounter optimal conditions to thrive, multiply, and can double their population numbers in as little as 20 minutes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"4-60C\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In food safety, the standard Temperature Danger Zone expressed in Celsius is 4°C to 60°C (which directly translates to the standard 40°F to 140°F range traditionally used by agencies like the USDA). Within this specific window, bacteria and other foodborne microorganisms encounter optimal conditions to thrive, multiply, and can double their population numbers in as little as 20 minutes.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 40-135C, 0-40C, 41-140C"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:food microorganisms grow well at::::', 'food microorganisms grow well at:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In food safety, the standard Temperature Danger Zone expressed in Celsius is 4°C to 60°C (which directly translates to the standard 40°F to 140°F range traditionally used by agencies like the USDA). Within this specific window, bacteria and other foodborne microorganisms encounter optimal conditions to thrive, multiply, and can double their population numbers in as little as 20 minutes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"4-60C\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In food safety, the standard Temperature Danger Zone expressed in Celsius is 4°C to 60°C (which directly translates to the standard 40°F to 140°F range traditionally used by agencies like the USDA). Within this specific window, bacteria and other foodborne microorganisms encounter optimal conditions to thrive, multiply, and can double their population numbers in as little as 20 minutes.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 40-135C, 0-40C, 41-140C"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following reduces the risk of foodborne illness cause by cross-contaminants?:::use clean utensils and color-coded chopping boards', 'which of the following reduces the risk of foodborne illness cause by cross-contaminants?', 'Use clean utensils and color-coded chopping boards', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Cross-contamination is the physical transfer of harmful bacteria or microorganisms from one food, piece of equipment, or surface to another.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Use clean utensils and color-coded chopping boards\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Cross-contamination is the physical transfer of harmful bacteria or microorganisms from one food, piece of equipment, or surface to another.\n\nUsing distinct, color-coded chopping boards (such as red for raw meat, blue for seafood, and green for vegetables) paired with clean utensils creates a strict barrier between raw and ready-to-eat foods. This directly stops pathogens like Salmonella on raw chicken from traveling onto fresh, uncooked foods like salad lettuce.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Buy from trusted suppliers, Discard food stored beyond the expiration date, Require sick personnel to go on leave"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following reduces the risk of foodborne illness cause by cross-contaminants?:::', 'which of the following reduces the risk of foodborne illness cause by cross-contaminants?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Cross-contamination is the physical transfer of harmful bacteria or microorganisms from one food, piece of equipment, or surface to another.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Use clean utensils and color-coded chopping boards\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Cross-contamination is the physical transfer of harmful bacteria or microorganisms from one food, piece of equipment, or surface to another.\n\nUsing distinct, color-coded chopping boards (such as red for raw meat, blue for seafood, and green for vegetables) paired with clean utensils creates a strict barrier between raw and ready-to-eat foods. This directly stops pathogens like Salmonella on raw chicken from traveling onto fresh, uncooked foods like salad lettuce.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Buy from trusted suppliers, Discard food stored beyond the expiration date, Require sick personnel to go on leave"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:clostridium botulinum toxin can be completely destroyed at:::boiling at 85c and higher', 'clostridium botulinum toxin can be completely destroyed at', 'boiling at 85C and higher', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"While the spores of Clostridium botulinum are notoriously tough and require temperatures above boiling under pressure (121°C or 250°F) to be destroyed, the actual botulinum neurotoxin itself is heat-sensitive (heat-labile).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"boiling at 85C and higher\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"While the spores of Clostridium botulinum are notoriously tough and require temperatures above boiling under pressure (121°C or 250°F) to be destroyed, the actual botulinum neurotoxin itself is heat-sensitive (heat-labile).\n\nAccording to guidelines from the World Health Organization (WHO), the pre-formed active toxin in food is completely inactivated and rendered harmless by boiling or thermal processing at internal temperatures of 85°C (185°F) or higher for at least 5 minutes.\n\n• Very high pH / Very alkaline condition: Botulinum toxin is highly stable in acidic to neutral environments. While extreme, non-physiological alkaline environments can eventually destabilize proteins, chemical manipulation is not a standardized, safe, or reliable operational method used to neutralize the toxin in food systems.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: very high pH, very alkaline condition, none of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:clostridium botulinum toxin can be completely destroyed at:::', 'clostridium botulinum toxin can be completely destroyed at', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"While the spores of Clostridium botulinum are notoriously tough and require temperatures above boiling under pressure (121°C or 250°F) to be destroyed, the actual botulinum neurotoxin itself is heat-sensitive (heat-labile).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"boiling at 85C and higher\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"While the spores of Clostridium botulinum are notoriously tough and require temperatures above boiling under pressure (121°C or 250°F) to be destroyed, the actual botulinum neurotoxin itself is heat-sensitive (heat-labile).\n\nAccording to guidelines from the World Health Organization (WHO), the pre-formed active toxin in food is completely inactivated and rendered harmless by boiling or thermal processing at internal temperatures of 85°C (185°F) or higher for at least 5 minutes.\n\n• Very high pH / Very alkaline condition: Botulinum toxin is highly stable in acidic to neutral environments. While extreme, non-physiological alkaline environments can eventually destabilize proteins, chemical manipulation is not a standardized, safe, or reliable operational method used to neutralize the toxin in food systems.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: very high pH, very alkaline condition, none of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:used to limit the migration of moisture into the product:::packaging', 'used to limit the migration of moisture into the product', 'packaging', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The primary purpose of food packaging (specifically moisture-barrier films, vacuum packs, and sealed containers) is to separate the food product from the surrounding environment.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"packaging\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The primary purpose of food packaging (specifically moisture-barrier films, vacuum packs, and sealed containers) is to separate the food product from the surrounding environment.\n\nIt acts as a physical shield that directly limits or blocks the migration of moisture—preventing dry foods (like crackers or chips) from absorbing damp air and becoming soggy, and preventing moist foods (like meat or bread) from drying out or developing freezer burn.\n\n• Drying: A preservation method that removes moisture already inside the food to lower water activity, but it does not stop external moisture from migrating back into the product afterward.\n• Heating: Used to cook food, destroy micro-organisms (pasteurization/sterilization), or drive off water vapor, but it offers no physical barrier protection once the heat source is removed.\n• Freezing: Turns liquid water inside the food into ice crystals to halt microbial growth. Without proper packaging, freezing actually accelerates moisture loss to the dry freezer air through sublimation (freezer burn).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: heating, freezing, drying"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:used to limit the migration of moisture into the product:::', 'used to limit the migration of moisture into the product', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The primary purpose of food packaging (specifically moisture-barrier films, vacuum packs, and sealed containers) is to separate the food product from the surrounding environment.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"packaging\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The primary purpose of food packaging (specifically moisture-barrier films, vacuum packs, and sealed containers) is to separate the food product from the surrounding environment.\n\nIt acts as a physical shield that directly limits or blocks the migration of moisture—preventing dry foods (like crackers or chips) from absorbing damp air and becoming soggy, and preventing moist foods (like meat or bread) from drying out or developing freezer burn.\n\n• Drying: A preservation method that removes moisture already inside the food to lower water activity, but it does not stop external moisture from migrating back into the product afterward.\n• Heating: Used to cook food, destroy micro-organisms (pasteurization/sterilization), or drive off water vapor, but it offers no physical barrier protection once the heat source is removed.\n• Freezing: Turns liquid water inside the food into ice crystals to halt microbial growth. Without proper packaging, freezing actually accelerates moisture loss to the dry freezer air through sublimation (freezer burn).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: heating, freezing, drying"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:food additive used to prevent both enzymatic and non-enzymatic discoloration.:::sulfur dioxide', 'food additive used to prevent both enzymatic and non-enzymatic discoloration.', 'Sulfur dioxide', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sulfur dioxide (along with sulfites) is a uniquely versatile food additive used extensively as an anti-browning agent because it blocks both pathways of discoloration:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sulfur dioxide\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sulfur dioxide (along with sulfites) is a uniquely versatile food additive used extensively as an anti-browning agent because it blocks both pathways of discoloration:\n\t• Enzymatic browning: It functions as a powerful enzyme inhibitor that permanently deactivates polyphenol oxidase (the enzyme responsible for turning cut apples, potatoes, and bananas brown).\n\t• Non-enzymatic browning: It acts as an oxygen scavenger and chemically reacts with carbonyl groups in reducing sugars, directly intercepting and halting both the Maillard reaction and caramelization pathways.\n\n• Sodium nitrites: Primarily used in meat curing to stabilize the pink color of myoglobin and prevent the growth of Clostridium botulinum. It does not prevent standard carbohydrate or plant-based enzymatic discoloration.\n• Benzoic acids: Synthetic chemical preservatives used primarily as antimicrobial agents to stop the growth of molds, yeasts, and bacteria in highly acidic foods like juices, carbonated beverages, and pickles.\n• Potassium nitrates: Like sodium nitrites, these are used as curing salts for meat preservation and color fixing rather than broad spectrum anti-browning.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Potassium nitrates, Sodium nitrites, Benzoic acids"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:food additive used to prevent both enzymatic and non-enzymatic discoloration.:::', 'food additive used to prevent both enzymatic and non-enzymatic discoloration.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sulfur dioxide (along with sulfites) is a uniquely versatile food additive used extensively as an anti-browning agent because it blocks both pathways of discoloration:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sulfur dioxide\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sulfur dioxide (along with sulfites) is a uniquely versatile food additive used extensively as an anti-browning agent because it blocks both pathways of discoloration:\n\t• Enzymatic browning: It functions as a powerful enzyme inhibitor that permanently deactivates polyphenol oxidase (the enzyme responsible for turning cut apples, potatoes, and bananas brown).\n\t• Non-enzymatic browning: It acts as an oxygen scavenger and chemically reacts with carbonyl groups in reducing sugars, directly intercepting and halting both the Maillard reaction and caramelization pathways.\n\n• Sodium nitrites: Primarily used in meat curing to stabilize the pink color of myoglobin and prevent the growth of Clostridium botulinum. It does not prevent standard carbohydrate or plant-based enzymatic discoloration.\n• Benzoic acids: Synthetic chemical preservatives used primarily as antimicrobial agents to stop the growth of molds, yeasts, and bacteria in highly acidic foods like juices, carbonated beverages, and pickles.\n• Potassium nitrates: Like sodium nitrites, these are used as curing salts for meat preservation and color fixing rather than broad spectrum anti-browning.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Potassium nitrates, Sodium nitrites, Benzoic acids"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb)
