('mc:all of the following are possible nutritional effects of intestinal resection among cancer patients except::::diabetes mellitus', 'all of the following are possible nutritional effects of intestinal resection among cancer patients except:', 'Diabetes mellitus', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Intestinal resection can lead to several gastrointestinal and nutritional complications depending on the site and extent of resection. These include diarrhea from reduced absorptive capacity, hyperoxaluria particularly after significant ileal resection, and blind loop syndrome when abnormal intestinal anatomy or stasis promotes bacterial overgrowth. Diabetes mellitus, however, is not a typical direct nutritional consequence of intestinal resection.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Diabetes mellitus\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Intestinal resection can lead to several gastrointestinal and nutritional complications depending on the site and extent of resection. These include diarrhea from reduced absorptive capacity, hyperoxaluria particularly after significant ileal resection, and blind loop syndrome when abnormal intestinal anatomy or stasis promotes bacterial overgrowth. Diabetes mellitus, however, is not a typical direct nutritional consequence of intestinal resection.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Blind loop syndrome, Hyperoxaluria, Diarrhea"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:all of the following are possible nutritional effects of intestinal resection among cancer patients except::::', 'all of the following are possible nutritional effects of intestinal resection among cancer patients except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Intestinal resection can lead to several gastrointestinal and nutritional complications depending on the site and extent of resection. These include diarrhea from reduced absorptive capacity, hyperoxaluria particularly after significant ileal resection, and blind loop syndrome when abnormal intestinal anatomy or stasis promotes bacterial overgrowth. Diabetes mellitus, however, is not a typical direct nutritional consequence of intestinal resection.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Diabetes mellitus\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Intestinal resection can lead to several gastrointestinal and nutritional complications depending on the site and extent of resection. These include diarrhea from reduced absorptive capacity, hyperoxaluria particularly after significant ileal resection, and blind loop syndrome when abnormal intestinal anatomy or stasis promotes bacterial overgrowth. Diabetes mellitus, however, is not a typical direct nutritional consequence of intestinal resection.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Blind loop syndrome, Hyperoxaluria, Diarrhea"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:hypoparathyroidism can lead to chvostek''s and trousseau''s syndrome. hyperparathyroidism, on the other hand, can cause osteopenia and osteoporosis.:::', 'hypoparathyroidism can lead to chvostek''s and trousseau''s syndrome. hyperparathyroidism, on the other hand, can cause osteopenia and osteoporosis.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Hypoparathyroidism causes decreased parathyroid hormone (PTH), leading to hypocalcemia, which increases neuromuscular excitability and may produce Chvostek’s and Trousseau’s syndrome. In contrast, prolonged hyperparathyroidism increases PTH-mediated bone resorption, which can reduce bone mineral density and contribute to osteopenia and osteoporosis.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"both statements are correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Hypoparathyroidism causes decreased parathyroid hormone (PTH), leading to hypocalcemia, which increases neuromuscular excitability and may produce Chvostek’s and Trousseau’s syndrome. In contrast, prolonged hyperparathyroidism increases PTH-mediated bone resorption, which can reduce bone mineral density and contribute to osteopenia and osteoporosis.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: only statement 1 is correct, only statement 2 is correct, both statements are incorrect"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a general principle in the prevention and management of an inborn error of metabolism if there is accumulation of precursor to toxic levels::::restrict source of precursor', 'a general principle in the prevention and management of an inborn error of metabolism if there is accumulation of precursor to toxic levels:', 'Restrict source of precursor', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When an inborn error of metabolism causes a precursor to accumulate to toxic concentrations because it cannot be adequately metabolized, a key treatment principle is to restrict the dietary source of that precursor. This reduces substrate entering the defective metabolic pathway and helps prevent further accumulation and toxicity. The specific dietary restriction depends on the metabolic disorder—for example, phenylalanine restriction in phenylketonuria (PKU).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Restrict source of precursor\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When an inborn error of metabolism causes a precursor to accumulate to toxic concentrations because it cannot be adequately metabolized, a key treatment principle is to restrict the dietary source of that precursor. This reduces substrate entering the defective metabolic pathway and helps prevent further accumulation and toxicity. The specific dietary restriction depends on the metabolic disorder—for example, phenylalanine restriction in phenylketonuria (PKU).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Provide replacement, Give increased quantities of the vitamin involved, Avoid exposure"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a general principle in the prevention and management of an inborn error of metabolism if there is accumulation of precursor to toxic levels::::', 'a general principle in the prevention and management of an inborn error of metabolism if there is accumulation of precursor to toxic levels:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When an inborn error of metabolism causes a precursor to accumulate to toxic concentrations because it cannot be adequately metabolized, a key treatment principle is to restrict the dietary source of that precursor. This reduces substrate entering the defective metabolic pathway and helps prevent further accumulation and toxicity. The specific dietary restriction depends on the metabolic disorder—for example, phenylalanine restriction in phenylketonuria (PKU).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Restrict source of precursor\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When an inborn error of metabolism causes a precursor to accumulate to toxic concentrations because it cannot be adequately metabolized, a key treatment principle is to restrict the dietary source of that precursor. This reduces substrate entering the defective metabolic pathway and helps prevent further accumulation and toxicity. The specific dietary restriction depends on the metabolic disorder—for example, phenylalanine restriction in phenylketonuria (PKU).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Provide replacement, Give increased quantities of the vitamin involved, Avoid exposure"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:how much energy can a 1 liter of 5% dextrose solution provide?:::170 kcal', 'how much energy can a 1 liter of 5% dextrose solution provide?', '170 kcal', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A 5% dextrose solution (D5W) contains 5 g dextrose per 100 mL, or 50 g/L. Dextrose provides approximately 3.4 kcal/g when given intravenously.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"170 kcal\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A 5% dextrose solution (D5W) contains 5 g dextrose per 100 mL, or 50 g/L. Dextrose provides approximately 3.4 kcal/g when given intravenously.\n\nTherefore: 50 g × 3.4 kcal/g = 170 kcal\n\nThus, 1 L of 5% dextrose provides approximately 170 kcal","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 100 kcal, 150 kcal, 200 kcal"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:how much energy can a 1 liter of 5% dextrose solution provide?:::', 'how much energy can a 1 liter of 5% dextrose solution provide?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A 5% dextrose solution (D5W) contains 5 g dextrose per 100 mL, or 50 g/L. Dextrose provides approximately 3.4 kcal/g when given intravenously.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"170 kcal\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A 5% dextrose solution (D5W) contains 5 g dextrose per 100 mL, or 50 g/L. Dextrose provides approximately 3.4 kcal/g when given intravenously.\n\nTherefore: 50 g × 3.4 kcal/g = 170 kcal\n\nThus, 1 L of 5% dextrose provides approximately 170 kcal","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 100 kcal, 150 kcal, 200 kcal"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:hypoparathyroidism can lead to chvostek''s and trousseau''s syndrome. hyperparathyroidism, on the other hand, can cause osteopenia and osteoporosis.:::both statements are correct', 'hypoparathyroidism can lead to chvostek''s and trousseau''s syndrome. hyperparathyroidism, on the other hand, can cause osteopenia and osteoporosis.', 'both statements are correct', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Hypoparathyroidism causes decreased parathyroid hormone (PTH), leading to hypocalcemia, which increases neuromuscular excitability and may produce Chvostek’s and Trousseau’s syndrome. In contrast, prolonged hyperparathyroidism increases PTH-mediated bone resorption, which can reduce bone mineral density and contribute to osteopenia and osteoporosis.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"both statements are correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Hypoparathyroidism causes decreased parathyroid hormone (PTH), leading to hypocalcemia, which increases neuromuscular excitability and may produce Chvostek’s and Trousseau’s syndrome. In contrast, prolonged hyperparathyroidism increases PTH-mediated bone resorption, which can reduce bone mineral density and contribute to osteopenia and osteoporosis.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: only statement 1 is correct, only statement 2 is correct, both statements are incorrect"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following are disease of the large intestines?:::all of the above', 'which of the following are disease of the large intestines?', 'All of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Diverticular disease, irritable bowel syndrome (IBS), and constipation are all disorders involving the large intestine. Diverticular disease involves the formation and/or inflammation of diverticula, typically in the colon. IBS is a disorder of gut-brain interaction characterized by abdominal pain associated with altered bowel habits, while constipation involves infrequent or difficult bowel movements and commonly reflects impaired colonic transit or evacuation.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Diverticular disease, irritable bowel syndrome (IBS), and constipation are all disorders involving the large intestine. Diverticular disease involves the formation and/or inflammation of diverticula, typically in the colon. IBS is a disorder of gut-brain interaction characterized by abdominal pain associated with altered bowel habits, while constipation involves infrequent or difficult bowel movements and commonly reflects impaired colonic transit or evacuation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Diverticular disease, Irritable bowel syndrome, Constipation"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following are disease of the large intestines?:::', 'which of the following are disease of the large intestines?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Diverticular disease, irritable bowel syndrome (IBS), and constipation are all disorders involving the large intestine. Diverticular disease involves the formation and/or inflammation of diverticula, typically in the colon. IBS is a disorder of gut-brain interaction characterized by abdominal pain associated with altered bowel habits, while constipation involves infrequent or difficult bowel movements and commonly reflects impaired colonic transit or evacuation.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Diverticular disease, irritable bowel syndrome (IBS), and constipation are all disorders involving the large intestine. Diverticular disease involves the formation and/or inflammation of diverticula, typically in the colon. IBS is a disorder of gut-brain interaction characterized by abdominal pain associated with altered bowel habits, while constipation involves infrequent or difficult bowel movements and commonly reflects impaired colonic transit or evacuation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Diverticular disease, Irritable bowel syndrome, Constipation"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following are included in the nutrition care guidelines for reducing gastroesophageal reflux disease?:::all of the above', 'which of the following are included in the nutrition care guidelines for reducing gastroesophageal reflux disease?', 'All of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Nutrition management of GERD focuses on reducing factors that can worsen reflux symptoms. Large and high-fat meals can delay gastric emptying and may promote reflux. Alcoholic beverages may aggravate symptoms in some individuals, while acidic or highly spiced foods may worsen discomfort, particularly when esophageal irritation is present. Dietary triggers are individualized, so foods that consistently provoke symptoms should be identified and limited.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Nutrition management of GERD focuses on reducing factors that can worsen reflux symptoms. Large and high-fat meals can delay gastric emptying and may promote reflux. Alcoholic beverages may aggravate symptoms in some individuals, while acidic or highly spiced foods may worsen discomfort, particularly when esophageal irritation is present. Dietary triggers are individualized, so foods that consistently provoke symptoms should be identified and limited.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Avoid large, high fat meals, Avoid acidic and highly spiced foods when inflammation exists, Avoid alcoholic beverages"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the best nutrition diagnosis?:::impaired nutrient utilization related to liver dysfunction as evidenced by steatorrhea', 'what is the best nutrition diagnosis?', 'Impaired nutrient utilization related to liver dysfunction as evidenced by steatorrhea', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Cirrhosis affects nutrient metabolism/absorption.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Impaired nutrient utilization related to liver dysfunction as evidenced by steatorrhea\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Cirrhosis affects nutrient metabolism/absorption.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Inadequate energy intake related to poor appetite as evidenced by weight loss, Inadequate oral intake related to ascites as evidenced by early satiety, Option D"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following are included in the nutrition care guidelines for reducing gastroesophageal reflux disease?:::', 'which of the following are included in the nutrition care guidelines for reducing gastroesophageal reflux disease?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Nutrition management of GERD focuses on reducing factors that can worsen reflux symptoms. Large and high-fat meals can delay gastric emptying and may promote reflux. Alcoholic beverages may aggravate symptoms in some individuals, while acidic or highly spiced foods may worsen discomfort, particularly when esophageal irritation is present. Dietary triggers are individualized, so foods that consistently provoke symptoms should be identified and limited.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Nutrition management of GERD focuses on reducing factors that can worsen reflux symptoms. Large and high-fat meals can delay gastric emptying and may promote reflux. Alcoholic beverages may aggravate symptoms in some individuals, while acidic or highly spiced foods may worsen discomfort, particularly when esophageal irritation is present. Dietary triggers are individualized, so foods that consistently provoke symptoms should be identified and limited.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Avoid large, high fat meals, Avoid acidic and highly spiced foods when inflammation exists, Avoid alcoholic beverages"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:views malnutrition as a disease which has to be cured by therapeutic approaches:::public health', 'views malnutrition as a disease which has to be cured by therapeutic approaches', 'Public Health', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• Social Welfare - malnutrition as deprivation of social safety net","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Public Health\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• Social Welfare - malnutrition as deprivation of social safety net\n• Agriculture - malnutrition due to limited food production\n• Education - malnutrition is due to poor nutrition knowledge and skills","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Social Welfare, Agriculture, Education"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which is the most important component of the medical nutrition theraphy for patients with celiac disease?:::all of the above', 'which is the most important component of the medical nutrition theraphy for patients with celiac disease?', 'All of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The cornerstone of medical nutrition therapy for celiac disease is a strict, lifelong gluten-free diet. This requires avoiding gluten-containing grains such as wheat, rye, and barley, carefully checking food labels for gluten-containing ingredients or contamination, and using naturally gluten-free alternatives such as rice, corn, and potatoes. Therefore, all three measures are important components of implementing a gluten-free diet.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The cornerstone of medical nutrition therapy for celiac disease is a strict, lifelong gluten-free diet. This requires avoiding gluten-containing grains such as wheat, rye, and barley, carefully checking food labels for gluten-containing ingredients or contamination, and using naturally gluten-free alternatives such as rice, corn, and potatoes. Therefore, all three measures are important components of implementing a gluten-free diet.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Omit sources of gluten as wheat, rye and barley, Reading labels to check if the product contains gluten, Substitute wheat flour with potato, rice, corn"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which is the most important component of the medical nutrition theraphy for patients with celiac disease?:::', 'which is the most important component of the medical nutrition theraphy for patients with celiac disease?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The cornerstone of medical nutrition therapy for celiac disease is a strict, lifelong gluten-free diet. This requires avoiding gluten-containing grains such as wheat, rye, and barley, carefully checking food labels for gluten-containing ingredients or contamination, and using naturally gluten-free alternatives such as rice, corn, and potatoes. Therefore, all three measures are important components of implementing a gluten-free diet.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The cornerstone of medical nutrition therapy for celiac disease is a strict, lifelong gluten-free diet. This requires avoiding gluten-containing grains such as wheat, rye, and barley, carefully checking food labels for gluten-containing ingredients or contamination, and using naturally gluten-free alternatives such as rice, corn, and potatoes. Therefore, all three measures are important components of implementing a gluten-free diet.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Omit sources of gluten as wheat, rye and barley, Reading labels to check if the product contains gluten, Substitute wheat flour with potato, rice, corn"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:childhood diarrhea plays a significant role in malnutrition. infections within the intestine can lead to loss of nutrients, damage to the gut mucosa, and impaired nutrient absorption. the who and unicef guidelines for managing diarrheal diseases in children include which of the following?:::all of the above', 'childhood diarrhea plays a significant role in malnutrition. infections within the intestine can lead to loss of nutrients, damage to the gut mucosa, and impaired nutrient absorption. the who and unicef guidelines for managing diarrheal diseases in children include which of the following?', 'All of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Management of acute diarrhea in children emphasizes preventing and treating dehydration with oral rehydration solution (ORS), continuing breastfeeding and age-appropriate feeding to prevent deterioration of nutritional status, and zinc supplementation to reduce the duration and severity of diarrhea and the risk of subsequent episodes. Therefore, all three are key components of recommended management.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Management of acute diarrhea in children emphasizes preventing and treating dehydration with oral rehydration solution (ORS), continuing breastfeeding and age-appropriate feeding to prevent deterioration of nutritional status, and zinc supplementation to reduce the duration and severity of diarrhea and the risk of subsequent episodes. Therefore, all three are key components of recommended management.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Fluid replacement, Continued feeding, Zinc supplementation"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:childhood diarrhea plays a significant role in malnutrition. infections within the intestine can lead to loss of nutrients, damage to the gut mucosa, and impaired nutrient absorption. the who and unicef guidelines for managing diarrheal diseases in children include which of the following?:::', 'childhood diarrhea plays a significant role in malnutrition. infections within the intestine can lead to loss of nutrients, damage to the gut mucosa, and impaired nutrient absorption. the who and unicef guidelines for managing diarrheal diseases in children include which of the following?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Management of acute diarrhea in children emphasizes preventing and treating dehydration with oral rehydration solution (ORS), continuing breastfeeding and age-appropriate feeding to prevent deterioration of nutritional status, and zinc supplementation to reduce the duration and severity of diarrhea and the risk of subsequent episodes. Therefore, all three are key components of recommended management.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Management of acute diarrhea in children emphasizes preventing and treating dehydration with oral rehydration solution (ORS), continuing breastfeeding and age-appropriate feeding to prevent deterioration of nutritional status, and zinc supplementation to reduce the duration and severity of diarrhea and the risk of subsequent episodes. Therefore, all three are key components of recommended management.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Fluid replacement, Continued feeding, Zinc supplementation"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is true about anemia? i. characterized by reduction in the size and quantity of red blood cells ii. result to decreased capacity of the blood to carry oxygen:::both statement are correct', 'which of the following is true about anemia? i. characterized by reduction in the size and quantity of red blood cells ii. result to decreased capacity of the blood to carry oxygen', 'both statement are correct', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Anemia is defined by a reduction in hemoglobin concentration, hematocrit, or red blood cell mass below the normal range, resulting in reduced oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"both statement are correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Anemia is defined by a reduction in hemoglobin concentration, hematocrit, or red blood cell mass below the normal range, resulting in reduced oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood. \n\nStatement I is incorrect because anemia does not necessarily involve a reduction in RBC size. RBC size may be normal (normocytic), small (microcytic), or large (macrocytic), depending on the underlying cause.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: only statement 1 is correct, only statement 2 is correct, both statement are incorrect"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is true about anemia? i. characterized by reduction in the size and quantity of red blood cells ii. result to decreased capacity of the blood to carry oxygen:::', 'which of the following is true about anemia? i. characterized by reduction in the size and quantity of red blood cells ii. result to decreased capacity of the blood to carry oxygen', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Anemia is defined by a reduction in hemoglobin concentration, hematocrit, or red blood cell mass below the normal range, resulting in reduced oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"both statement are correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Anemia is defined by a reduction in hemoglobin concentration, hematocrit, or red blood cell mass below the normal range, resulting in reduced oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood. \n\nStatement I is incorrect because anemia does not necessarily involve a reduction in RBC size. RBC size may be normal (normocytic), small (microcytic), or large (macrocytic), depending on the underlying cause.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: only statement 1 is correct, only statement 2 is correct, both statement are incorrect"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following proteins have an important role in the immune response?:::globulins', 'which of the following proteins have an important role in the immune response?', 'Globulins', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Globulins, particularly gamma globulins (immunoglobulins/antibodies), play an essential role in the immune response. Antibodies recognize and bind specific foreign antigens and help neutralize pathogens or facilitate their elimination. Other globulin proteins also include components involved in immune and inflammatory processes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Globulins\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Globulins, particularly gamma globulins (immunoglobulins/antibodies), play an essential role in the immune response. Antibodies recognize and bind specific foreign antigens and help neutralize pathogens or facilitate their elimination. Other globulin proteins also include components involved in immune and inflammatory processes.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Fibrinogen, Lipoproteins, Albumin"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following proteins have an important role in the immune response?:::', 'which of the following proteins have an important role in the immune response?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Globulins, particularly gamma globulins (immunoglobulins/antibodies), play an essential role in the immune response. Antibodies recognize and bind specific foreign antigens and help neutralize pathogens or facilitate their elimination. Other globulin proteins also include components involved in immune and inflammatory processes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Globulins\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Globulins, particularly gamma globulins (immunoglobulins/antibodies), play an essential role in the immune response. Antibodies recognize and bind specific foreign antigens and help neutralize pathogens or facilitate their elimination. Other globulin proteins also include components involved in immune and inflammatory processes.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Fibrinogen, Lipoproteins, Albumin"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:anemia that is induced by poor blood clotting::::hemorrhagic anemia', 'anemia that is induced by poor blood clotting:', 'Hemorrhagic anemia', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Hemorrhagic anemia results from excessive blood loss, which can occur when blood clotting is impaired. Poor clotting allows bleeding to continue or become excessive, leading to loss of red blood cells and hemoglobin. Causes may include coagulation disorders, severe vitamin K deficiency, anticoagulant therapy, or other conditions that impair hemostasis. However, technically, poor clotting is a cause of blood loss rather than the defining cause of hemorrhagic anemia.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Hemorrhagic anemia\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Hemorrhagic anemia results from excessive blood loss, which can occur when blood clotting is impaired. Poor clotting allows bleeding to continue or become excessive, leading to loss of red blood cells and hemoglobin. Causes may include coagulation disorders, severe vitamin K deficiency, anticoagulant therapy, or other conditions that impair hemostasis. However, technically, poor clotting is a cause of blood loss rather than the defining cause of hemorrhagic anemia.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Microcytic anemia, Pernicious anemia, Hemolytic anemia"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb)
