('mc:anemia that is induced by poor blood clotting::::', 'anemia that is induced by poor blood clotting:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Hemorrhagic anemia results from excessive blood loss, which can occur when blood clotting is impaired. Poor clotting allows bleeding to continue or become excessive, leading to loss of red blood cells and hemoglobin. Causes may include coagulation disorders, severe vitamin K deficiency, anticoagulant therapy, or other conditions that impair hemostasis. However, technically, poor clotting is a cause of blood loss rather than the defining cause of hemorrhagic anemia.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Hemorrhagic anemia\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Hemorrhagic anemia results from excessive blood loss, which can occur when blood clotting is impaired. Poor clotting allows bleeding to continue or become excessive, leading to loss of red blood cells and hemoglobin. Causes may include coagulation disorders, severe vitamin K deficiency, anticoagulant therapy, or other conditions that impair hemostasis. However, technically, poor clotting is a cause of blood loss rather than the defining cause of hemorrhagic anemia.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Microcytic anemia, Pernicious anemia, Hemolytic anemia"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:osteomalacia is result of::::all of the above', 'osteomalacia is result of:', 'all of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Osteomalacia is defective mineralization of bone in adults, most commonly associated with vitamin D deficiency, which can result from inadequate dietary intake or insufficient sunlight exposure. Parathyroid disorders, particularly conditions causing increased PTH activity, can also contribute to impaired bone mineralization through disturbances in calcium and phosphate metabolism. Therefore, all three factors can contribute to osteomalacia.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"all of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Osteomalacia is defective mineralization of bone in adults, most commonly associated with vitamin D deficiency, which can result from inadequate dietary intake or insufficient sunlight exposure. Parathyroid disorders, particularly conditions causing increased PTH activity, can also contribute to impaired bone mineralization through disturbances in calcium and phosphate metabolism. Therefore, all three factors can contribute to osteomalacia.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: dietary deficiency, inadequate sunlight exposure, parathyroid problems"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:osteomalacia is result of::::', 'osteomalacia is result of:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Osteomalacia is defective mineralization of bone in adults, most commonly associated with vitamin D deficiency, which can result from inadequate dietary intake or insufficient sunlight exposure. Parathyroid disorders, particularly conditions causing increased PTH activity, can also contribute to impaired bone mineralization through disturbances in calcium and phosphate metabolism. Therefore, all three factors can contribute to osteomalacia.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"all of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Osteomalacia is defective mineralization of bone in adults, most commonly associated with vitamin D deficiency, which can result from inadequate dietary intake or insufficient sunlight exposure. Parathyroid disorders, particularly conditions causing increased PTH activity, can also contribute to impaired bone mineralization through disturbances in calcium and phosphate metabolism. Therefore, all three factors can contribute to osteomalacia.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: dietary deficiency, inadequate sunlight exposure, parathyroid problems"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:dietary management for myasthenia gravis: i. adjusted food consistency depending on tolerance; nutrient-dense; small frequent feeding ii. avoid foods which contain i-dopa iii. limit dietary protein at breakfast and lunch iv. low glycemic index treatment:::', 'dietary management for myasthenia gravis: i. adjusted food consistency depending on tolerance; nutrient-dense; small frequent feeding ii. avoid foods which contain i-dopa iii. limit dietary protein at breakfast and lunch iv. low glycemic index treatment', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The major nutrition concerns in myasthenia gravis (MG) are fatigue and weakness of the muscles involved in chewing and swallowing, which can increase the risk of inadequate intake and aspiration. Appropriate management includes modifying food texture/consistency according to swallowing ability, choosing nutrient-dense foods, and using small, frequent meals to reduce fatigue during eating. Avoidance of L-dopa, protein restriction at specific meals, and low-glycemic-index treatment are not standard dietary therapies for MG.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"I only\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The major nutrition concerns in myasthenia gravis (MG) are fatigue and weakness of the muscles involved in chewing and swallowing, which can increase the risk of inadequate intake and aspiration. Appropriate management includes modifying food texture/consistency according to swallowing ability, choosing nutrient-dense foods, and using small, frequent meals to reduce fatigue during eating. Avoidance of L-dopa, protein restriction at specific meals, and low-glycemic-index treatment are not standard dietary therapies for MG.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: I and II only, I, II, III only, All of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:resting energy expenditure is the amount of energy required for the basal metabolic processes and tef per unit weight per unit time. it is also called resting metabolic rate.:::only statement 2 is correct', 'resting energy expenditure is the amount of energy required for the basal metabolic processes and tef per unit weight per unit time. it is also called resting metabolic rate.', 'only statement 2 is correct', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"REE and RMR are generally used interchangeably to describe the energy expended by the body at rest to maintain essential physiological functions. However, statement 1 is incorrect because REE does not include the thermic effect of food (TEF). ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"only statement 2 is correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"REE and RMR are generally used interchangeably to describe the energy expended by the body at rest to maintain essential physiological functions. However, statement 1 is incorrect because REE does not include the thermic effect of food (TEF). \n\nTEF is a separate component of total daily energy expenditure. Also, REE/RMR is usually expressed as kcal/day or kcal/kg/day, but “per unit weight per unit time” is not part of its defining concept. Commonly used REE or RMR Equations are Mifflin-St Jeor and Harris-Benedict, while TEF = REE + TEF + Activity Energy Expenditure.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: only statement 1 is correct, both statements are correct, both statements are incorrect"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a patient is receiving 250 mls of a 20% aa solution and 250 mls of 10% lipid emulsion. what is the total kcal provided?:::', 'a patient is receiving 250 mls of a 20% aa solution and 250 mls of 10% lipid emulsion. what is the total kcal provided?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"For PN calculations, the conventional values are:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"475 kcal\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"For PN calculations, the conventional values are:\n\nAmino acids: 4 kcal/g\nDextrose: 3.4 kcal/g\nIV lipid emulsion: 10 kcal/g\n\nProtein: 20% AA, 250 mL:\n250 mL × 20 g/100 mL = 50 g AA => 50 g × 4 kcal/g = 200 kcal\n\nLipids: 10% lipid emulsion, 250 mL:\n250 mL × 10 g/100 mL = 25 g lipid => 25 g × 10 kcal/g = 250 kcal\n\nTotal = 200 + 250 = 450 kcal","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 450 kcal, 550 kcal, 555 kcal"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:resting energy expenditure is the amount of energy required for the basal metabolic processes and tef per unit weight per unit time. it is also called resting metabolic rate.:::', 'resting energy expenditure is the amount of energy required for the basal metabolic processes and tef per unit weight per unit time. it is also called resting metabolic rate.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"REE and RMR are generally used interchangeably to describe the energy expended by the body at rest to maintain essential physiological functions. However, statement 1 is incorrect because REE does not include the thermic effect of food (TEF). ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"only statement 2 is correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"REE and RMR are generally used interchangeably to describe the energy expended by the body at rest to maintain essential physiological functions. However, statement 1 is incorrect because REE does not include the thermic effect of food (TEF). \n\nTEF is a separate component of total daily energy expenditure. Also, REE/RMR is usually expressed as kcal/day or kcal/kg/day, but “per unit weight per unit time” is not part of its defining concept. Commonly used REE or RMR Equations are Mifflin-St Jeor and Harris-Benedict, while TEF = REE + TEF + Activity Energy Expenditure.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: only statement 1 is correct, both statements are correct, both statements are incorrect"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which is not predisposing factor of gout::::low purine intake', 'which is not predisposing factor of gout:', 'low purine intake', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Low purine intake is a protective factor, not a predisposing factor. A strict low-purine diet decreases the amount of uric acid produced in the body, lowering the risk of developing gout and reducing the frequency of painful flare-ups.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"low purine intake\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Low purine intake is a protective factor, not a predisposing factor. A strict low-purine diet decreases the amount of uric acid produced in the body, lowering the risk of developing gout and reducing the frequency of painful flare-ups.\n\n• High fat diet and starvation: High saturated fat diets impair the intestinal and renal excretion of urate. Starvation triggers the production of ketone bodies (like beta-hydroxybutyrate), which directly compete with uric acid for excretion sites in the kidneys, causing uric acid levels to skyrocket.\n• Renal disorders: Roughly 90% of gout cases are caused by the kidneys'' inability to adequately excrete uric acid. Chronic kidney disease or renal insufficiency leads to hyperuricemia and crystal deposition.\n• Obesity: Being overweight or obese increases the body''s internal production of uric acid while simultaneously reducing how efficiently the kidneys can filter and excrete it.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: obesity, renal disorders, high fat diet, starvation"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the best nutrition diagnosis?:::', 'what is the best nutrition diagnosis?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Cirrhosis affects nutrient metabolism/absorption.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Impaired nutrient utilization related to liver dysfunction as evidenced by steatorrhea\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Cirrhosis affects nutrient metabolism/absorption.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Inadequate energy intake related to poor appetite as evidenced by weight loss, Inadequate oral intake related to ascites as evidenced by early satiety, Option D"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which is not predisposing factor of gout::::', 'which is not predisposing factor of gout:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Low purine intake is a protective factor, not a predisposing factor. A strict low-purine diet decreases the amount of uric acid produced in the body, lowering the risk of developing gout and reducing the frequency of painful flare-ups.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"low purine intake\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Low purine intake is a protective factor, not a predisposing factor. A strict low-purine diet decreases the amount of uric acid produced in the body, lowering the risk of developing gout and reducing the frequency of painful flare-ups.\n\n• High fat diet and starvation: High saturated fat diets impair the intestinal and renal excretion of urate. Starvation triggers the production of ketone bodies (like beta-hydroxybutyrate), which directly compete with uric acid for excretion sites in the kidneys, causing uric acid levels to skyrocket.\n• Renal disorders: Roughly 90% of gout cases are caused by the kidneys'' inability to adequately excrete uric acid. Chronic kidney disease or renal insufficiency leads to hyperuricemia and crystal deposition.\n• Obesity: Being overweight or obese increases the body''s internal production of uric acid while simultaneously reducing how efficiently the kidneys can filter and excrete it.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: obesity, renal disorders, high fat diet, starvation"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are dietary management for wernicke-korsakoff syndrome except::::high energy and protein tube feedings', 'the following are dietary management for wernicke-korsakoff syndrome except:', 'High energy and protein tube feedings', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome is associated with severe thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency, commonly in the setting of chronic alcohol use and malnutrition. Management includes prompt thiamine replacement, adequate hydration and nutritional rehabilitation, and alcohol avoidance. However, high-energy/high-protein tube feeding is not a routine specific dietary management measure; enteral feeding is used when oral intake is inadequate or unsafe, and nutrition should be advanced appropriately rather than automatically prescribing high-energy, high-protein tube feeding.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"High energy and protein tube feedings\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome is associated with severe thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency, commonly in the setting of chronic alcohol use and malnutrition. Management includes prompt thiamine replacement, adequate hydration and nutritional rehabilitation, and alcohol avoidance. However, high-energy/high-protein tube feeding is not a routine specific dietary management measure; enteral feeding is used when oral intake is inadequate or unsafe, and nutrition should be advanced appropriately rather than automatically prescribing high-energy, high-protein tube feeding.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Thiamine supplementation, Adequate hydration, Avoidance of alcohol"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are dietary management for wernicke-korsakoff syndrome except::::', 'the following are dietary management for wernicke-korsakoff syndrome except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome is associated with severe thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency, commonly in the setting of chronic alcohol use and malnutrition. Management includes prompt thiamine replacement, adequate hydration and nutritional rehabilitation, and alcohol avoidance. However, high-energy/high-protein tube feeding is not a routine specific dietary management measure; enteral feeding is used when oral intake is inadequate or unsafe, and nutrition should be advanced appropriately rather than automatically prescribing high-energy, high-protein tube feeding.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"High energy and protein tube feedings\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome is associated with severe thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency, commonly in the setting of chronic alcohol use and malnutrition. Management includes prompt thiamine replacement, adequate hydration and nutritional rehabilitation, and alcohol avoidance. However, high-energy/high-protein tube feeding is not a routine specific dietary management measure; enteral feeding is used when oral intake is inadequate or unsafe, and nutrition should be advanced appropriately rather than automatically prescribing high-energy, high-protein tube feeding.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Thiamine supplementation, Adequate hydration, Avoidance of alcohol"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:dietary management for myasthenia gravis: i. adjusted food consistency depending on tolerance; nutrient-dense; small frequent feeding ii. avoid foods which contain i-dopa iii. limit dietary protein at breakfast and lunch iv. low glycemic index treatment:::i only', 'dietary management for myasthenia gravis: i. adjusted food consistency depending on tolerance; nutrient-dense; small frequent feeding ii. avoid foods which contain i-dopa iii. limit dietary protein at breakfast and lunch iv. low glycemic index treatment', 'I only', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The major nutrition concerns in myasthenia gravis (MG) are fatigue and weakness of the muscles involved in chewing and swallowing, which can increase the risk of inadequate intake and aspiration. Appropriate management includes modifying food texture/consistency according to swallowing ability, choosing nutrient-dense foods, and using small, frequent meals to reduce fatigue during eating. Avoidance of L-dopa, protein restriction at specific meals, and low-glycemic-index treatment are not standard dietary therapies for MG.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"I only\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The major nutrition concerns in myasthenia gravis (MG) are fatigue and weakness of the muscles involved in chewing and swallowing, which can increase the risk of inadequate intake and aspiration. Appropriate management includes modifying food texture/consistency according to swallowing ability, choosing nutrient-dense foods, and using small, frequent meals to reduce fatigue during eating. Avoidance of L-dopa, protein restriction at specific meals, and low-glycemic-index treatment are not standard dietary therapies for MG.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: I and II only, I, II, III only, All of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:dietary recommendation for rheumatic fever: i. high kcal, high protein, high fluids, low fiber ii.high kcal, high protein, na and fluid controlled, vitamin and mineral supplements iii. high kcal, high protein, fat-restricted:::ii only', 'dietary recommendation for rheumatic fever: i. high kcal, high protein, high fluids, low fiber ii.high kcal, high protein, na and fluid controlled, vitamin and mineral supplements iii. high kcal, high protein, fat-restricted', 'II only', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"During acute rheumatic fever, nutrition should support growth, tissue repair, and recovery, so adequate energy and high-quality protein are important. When there is carditis or heart failure, sodium and fluid may need to be restricted to help control edema and fluid overload. Adequate vitamins and minerals should also be provided. There is no routine indication for a low-fiber diet or for fat restriction in rheumatic fever itself.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"II only\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"During acute rheumatic fever, nutrition should support growth, tissue repair, and recovery, so adequate energy and high-quality protein are important. When there is carditis or heart failure, sodium and fluid may need to be restricted to help control edema and fluid overload. Adequate vitamins and minerals should also be provided. There is no routine indication for a low-fiber diet or for fat restriction in rheumatic fever itself.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: I only, III only, All of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:all of these are essential amino acids for adults except: i. arginine ii. glutamine iii. lysine iv. leucine:::i and ii', 'all of these are essential amino acids for adults except: i. arginine ii. glutamine iii. lysine iv. leucine', 'I and II', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"🟢 Arginine (I) is conditionally essential. Adults can make enough of it on their own, though growing children need it in their diet.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"I and II\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"🟢 Arginine (I) is conditionally essential. Adults can make enough of it on their own, though growing children need it in their diet.\n🟢 Glutamine (II) is a non-essential amino acid. Your body creates it in sufficient amounts under normal conditions.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: II only, III only, None of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:dietary recommendation for rheumatic fever: i. high kcal, high protein, high fluids, low fiber ii.high kcal, high protein, na and fluid controlled, vitamin and mineral supplements iii. high kcal, high protein, fat-restricted:::', 'dietary recommendation for rheumatic fever: i. high kcal, high protein, high fluids, low fiber ii.high kcal, high protein, na and fluid controlled, vitamin and mineral supplements iii. high kcal, high protein, fat-restricted', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"During acute rheumatic fever, nutrition should support growth, tissue repair, and recovery, so adequate energy and high-quality protein are important. When there is carditis or heart failure, sodium and fluid may need to be restricted to help control edema and fluid overload. Adequate vitamins and minerals should also be provided. There is no routine indication for a low-fiber diet or for fat restriction in rheumatic fever itself.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"II only\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"During acute rheumatic fever, nutrition should support growth, tissue repair, and recovery, so adequate energy and high-quality protein are important. When there is carditis or heart failure, sodium and fluid may need to be restricted to help control edema and fluid overload. Adequate vitamins and minerals should also be provided. There is no routine indication for a low-fiber diet or for fat restriction in rheumatic fever itself.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: I only, III only, All of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:an 84 year-old client complains of chronic constipation. to improve bowel function, what could be a possible intervention?:::increasing fiber intake', 'an 84 year-old client complains of chronic constipation. to improve bowel function, what could be a possible intervention?', 'increasing fiber intake', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Increasing dietary fiber, together with adequate fluid intake and appropriate physical activity, is a first-line approach for managing constipation in older adults when there is no contraindication. Fiber increases stool bulk and can improve bowel regularity. Fiber should be increased gradually, with adequate fluid intake, to minimize bloating and discomfort. For an older adult with chronic constipation, the underlying cause and medications should also be assessed.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"increasing fiber intake\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Increasing dietary fiber, together with adequate fluid intake and appropriate physical activity, is a first-line approach for managing constipation in older adults when there is no contraindication. Fiber increases stool bulk and can improve bowel regularity. Fiber should be increased gradually, with adequate fluid intake, to minimize bloating and discomfort. For an older adult with chronic constipation, the underlying cause and medications should also be assessed.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: avoidance of binding foods such as cheese and chocolate, monitoring a balance between activity and rest, daily use of laxatives"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the difference between nitrogen intake and nitrogen excretion::::nitrogen balance', 'the difference between nitrogen intake and nitrogen excretion:', 'Nitrogen balance', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"At nitrogen equilibrium: N intake = N output; at positive nitrogen balance: N intake > N output; at negative nitrogen balance: N output > N intake.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Nitrogen balance\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"At nitrogen equilibrium: N intake = N output; at positive nitrogen balance: N intake > N output; at negative nitrogen balance: N output > N intake.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Protein digestibility, Amino acid score, Biologic value"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
