('mc:in patients with cirrhosis, which dietary strategy is most appropriate to help prevent protein-energy malnutrition?:::', 'in patients with cirrhosis, which dietary strategy is most appropriate to help prevent protein-energy malnutrition?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Patients with cirrhosis are at high risk of protein-energy malnutrition and muscle wasting. Providing adequate energy and protein helps maintain lean body mass and prevent catabolism. Small, frequent meals and a late-evening snack can help reduce prolonged fasting and provide a more continuous supply of energy, particularly because glycogen stores may be reduced in advanced liver disease.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Adequate energy and protein intake with small, frequent meals\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Patients with cirrhosis are at high risk of protein-energy malnutrition and muscle wasting. Providing adequate energy and protein helps maintain lean body mass and prevent catabolism. Small, frequent meals and a late-evening snack can help reduce prolonged fasting and provide a more continuous supply of energy, particularly because glycogen stores may be reduced in advanced liver disease.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Prolonged fasting between meals, Routine severe restriction of dietary protein, Elimination of carbohydrate-rich foods"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the main objective of doh ao 2019-0033?:::to establish uniform implementation of the nutrition care process (ncp) in hospitals', 'what is the main objective of doh ao 2019-0033?', 'To establish uniform implementation of the Nutrition Care Process (NCP) in hospitals', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The AO ensures standardized, evidence-based nutrition care through the NCP in all hospital settings.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"To establish uniform implementation of the Nutrition Care Process (NCP) in hospitals\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The AO ensures standardized, evidence-based nutrition care through the NCP in all hospital settings.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: To standardize medical documentation across all hospitals, Option C, To replace the role of dietitians with nutrition nurses"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the main objective of doh ao 2019-0033?:::', 'what is the main objective of doh ao 2019-0033?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The AO ensures standardized, evidence-based nutrition care through the NCP in all hospital settings.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"To establish uniform implementation of the Nutrition Care Process (NCP) in hospitals\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The AO ensures standardized, evidence-based nutrition care through the NCP in all hospital settings.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: To standardize medical documentation across all hospitals, Option C, To replace the role of dietitians with nutrition nurses"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what are the four core steps of the ncp?:::assessment, diagnosis, intervention, and monitoring & evaluation', 'what are the four core steps of the ncp?', 'Assessment, diagnosis, intervention, and monitoring & evaluation', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"These four steps make up the standardized NCP model recognized by DOH and the Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Assessment, diagnosis, intervention, and monitoring & evaluation\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"These four steps make up the standardized NCP model recognized by DOH and the Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Screening, assessment, evaluation, and referral, Planning, implementation, documentation, and discharge, Option D"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what are the four core steps of the ncp?:::', 'what are the four core steps of the ncp?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"These four steps make up the standardized NCP model recognized by DOH and the Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Assessment, diagnosis, intervention, and monitoring & evaluation\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"These four steps make up the standardized NCP model recognized by DOH and the Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Screening, assessment, evaluation, and referral, Planning, implementation, documentation, and discharge, Option D"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following best describes nutrition assessment according to the academy of nutrition and dietetics?:::collecting, analyzing, and interpreting data to determine a nutrition-related problem', 'which of the following best describes nutrition assessment according to the academy of nutrition and dietetics?', 'Collecting, analyzing, and interpreting data to determine a nutrition-related problem', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Nutrition Assessment is the systematic process of obtaining and interpreting data to identify nutrition problems, forming the basis for the nutrition diagnosis.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Collecting, analyzing, and interpreting data to determine a nutrition-related problem\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Nutrition Assessment is the systematic process of obtaining and interpreting data to identify nutrition problems, forming the basis for the nutrition diagnosis.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Option B, Planning and documenting diet prescriptions for the patient, Providing individualized counseling sessions"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:consumption of this food increases the risk for liver cancer:::moldy peanuts', 'consumption of this food increases the risk for liver cancer', 'Moldy peanuts', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Poorly stored grains and legumes—most notably peanuts—are highly susceptible to contamination by molds like Aspergillus flavus and Aspergillus parasiticus. These molds produce a potent, heat-stable mycotoxin known as aflatoxin (specifically Aflatoxin B1).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Moldy peanuts\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Poorly stored grains and legumes—most notably peanuts—are highly susceptible to contamination by molds like Aspergillus flavus and Aspergillus parasiticus. These molds produce a potent, heat-stable mycotoxin known as aflatoxin (specifically Aflatoxin B1).\n\nAflatoxin is classified as a definitive Group 1 human carcinogen. Once ingested, the liver processes this toxin, creating a highly reactive intermediate metabolite that directly binds to DNA, inducing specific mutations in the p53 tumor suppressor gene. This DNA damage impairs the body''s natural defense against cellular mutations, directly triggering the development of hepatocellular carcinoma (liver cancer).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Kimchi, Alcohol, Pork"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:consumption of this food increases the risk for liver cancer:::', 'consumption of this food increases the risk for liver cancer', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Poorly stored grains and legumes—most notably peanuts—are highly susceptible to contamination by molds like Aspergillus flavus and Aspergillus parasiticus. These molds produce a potent, heat-stable mycotoxin known as aflatoxin (specifically Aflatoxin B1).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Moldy peanuts\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Poorly stored grains and legumes—most notably peanuts—are highly susceptible to contamination by molds like Aspergillus flavus and Aspergillus parasiticus. These molds produce a potent, heat-stable mycotoxin known as aflatoxin (specifically Aflatoxin B1).\n\nAflatoxin is classified as a definitive Group 1 human carcinogen. Once ingested, the liver processes this toxin, creating a highly reactive intermediate metabolite that directly binds to DNA, inducing specific mutations in the p53 tumor suppressor gene. This DNA damage impairs the body''s natural defense against cellular mutations, directly triggering the development of hepatocellular carcinoma (liver cancer).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Kimchi, Alcohol, Pork"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are components of physical fitness except:::resilience', 'the following are components of physical fitness except', 'Resilience', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Resilience is a psychological and mental attribute that refers to a person''s ability to adapt, cope, and mentally bounce back from stress, adversity, or trauma. While it is highly beneficial for mental health, it is not classified as a physical component of fitness in physical education and sports science curriculum frameworks.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Resilience\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Resilience is a psychological and mental attribute that refers to a person''s ability to adapt, cope, and mentally bounce back from stress, adversity, or trauma. While it is highly beneficial for mental health, it is not classified as a physical component of fitness in physical education and sports science curriculum frameworks.\n\nThe Standard Components of Physical Fitness\n\nSkill-Related Fitness Components\nThese components focus on sports performance, motor skills, and technical precision:\n• Coordination: The ability to use different body parts together smoothly and efficiently (e.g., hand-eye coordination).\n• Speed: The ability to move your body or parts of your body quickly over a short distance.\n• Balance: The ability to maintain stability and control your body''s center of mass over a base of support.\n• Other components: Agility, Power, and Reaction Time\n\nHealth-Related Fitness Components\nThese focus on general physiological functioning, disease prevention, and daily bodily efficiency:\n• Cardiovascular Endurance\n• Muscular Strength\n• Muscular Endurance\n• Flexibility\n• Body Composition","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Balance, Coordination, Speed"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are components of physical fitness except:::', 'the following are components of physical fitness except', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Resilience is a psychological and mental attribute that refers to a person''s ability to adapt, cope, and mentally bounce back from stress, adversity, or trauma. While it is highly beneficial for mental health, it is not classified as a physical component of fitness in physical education and sports science curriculum frameworks.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Resilience\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Resilience is a psychological and mental attribute that refers to a person''s ability to adapt, cope, and mentally bounce back from stress, adversity, or trauma. While it is highly beneficial for mental health, it is not classified as a physical component of fitness in physical education and sports science curriculum frameworks.\n\nThe Standard Components of Physical Fitness\n\nSkill-Related Fitness Components\nThese components focus on sports performance, motor skills, and technical precision:\n• Coordination: The ability to use different body parts together smoothly and efficiently (e.g., hand-eye coordination).\n• Speed: The ability to move your body or parts of your body quickly over a short distance.\n• Balance: The ability to maintain stability and control your body''s center of mass over a base of support.\n• Other components: Agility, Power, and Reaction Time\n\nHealth-Related Fitness Components\nThese focus on general physiological functioning, disease prevention, and daily bodily efficiency:\n• Cardiovascular Endurance\n• Muscular Strength\n• Muscular Endurance\n• Flexibility\n• Body Composition","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Balance, Coordination, Speed"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the muscle action that requires the use of energy in the form of atp:::relaxation', 'the muscle action that requires the use of energy in the form of atp', 'Relaxation', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"During muscle contraction, myosin heads bind to actin and execute the power stroke via the utilization of ATP, but the detachment of the cross-bridge and the sliding motion itself are driven by stored energy release. Conversely, muscle relaxation cannot occur spontaneously; it requires the active transport of Ca2+ back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum via the Sarco/Endoplasmic Reticulum Calcium ATPase (SERCA) pump, which directly consumes one molecule of ATP per two calcium ions pumped. Additionally, ATP binding to the myosin head is strictly required to break the rigor complex and allow the muscle to relax.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Relaxation\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"During muscle contraction, myosin heads bind to actin and execute the power stroke via the utilization of ATP, but the detachment of the cross-bridge and the sliding motion itself are driven by stored energy release. Conversely, muscle relaxation cannot occur spontaneously; it requires the active transport of Ca2+ back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum via the Sarco/Endoplasmic Reticulum Calcium ATPase (SERCA) pump, which directly consumes one molecule of ATP per two calcium ions pumped. Additionally, ATP binding to the myosin head is strictly required to break the rigor complex and allow the muscle to relax.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Contraction, Flexing, Moving"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the muscle action that requires the use of energy in the form of atp:::', 'the muscle action that requires the use of energy in the form of atp', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"During muscle contraction, myosin heads bind to actin and execute the power stroke via the utilization of ATP, but the detachment of the cross-bridge and the sliding motion itself are driven by stored energy release. Conversely, muscle relaxation cannot occur spontaneously; it requires the active transport of Ca2+ back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum via the Sarco/Endoplasmic Reticulum Calcium ATPase (SERCA) pump, which directly consumes one molecule of ATP per two calcium ions pumped. Additionally, ATP binding to the myosin head is strictly required to break the rigor complex and allow the muscle to relax.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Relaxation\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"During muscle contraction, myosin heads bind to actin and execute the power stroke via the utilization of ATP, but the detachment of the cross-bridge and the sliding motion itself are driven by stored energy release. Conversely, muscle relaxation cannot occur spontaneously; it requires the active transport of Ca2+ back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum via the Sarco/Endoplasmic Reticulum Calcium ATPase (SERCA) pump, which directly consumes one molecule of ATP per two calcium ions pumped. Additionally, ATP binding to the myosin head is strictly required to break the rigor complex and allow the muscle to relax.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Contraction, Flexing, Moving"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a dietitian needs to determine a patient’s risk for cardiovascular disease. the following anthropometric measurements must be assessed except:::', 'a dietitian needs to determine a patient’s risk for cardiovascular disease. the following anthropometric measurements must be assessed except', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Exercise history is a behavioral or lifestyle component of a patient''s personal history, not a physical bodily measurement.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Exercise history\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Exercise history is a behavioral or lifestyle component of a patient''s personal history, not a physical bodily measurement.\n\nAnthropometric measurements are non-invasive, quantitative, physical measurements used to assess the size, shape, and composition of the human body.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Height and weight, Waist Circumference, Body fat analysis"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the type of muscle that compromises 40% of body weight and is involved in heat production during exercise and motion.:::skeletal muscle', 'the type of muscle that compromises 40% of body weight and is involved in heat production during exercise and motion.', 'Skeletal muscle', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In standard anatomy and physiology textbooks, skeletal muscle is the most abundant tissue type in the human body, accounting for approximately 40% of total body weight in a healthy adult.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Skeletal muscle\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In standard anatomy and physiology textbooks, skeletal muscle is the most abundant tissue type in the human body, accounting for approximately 40% of total body weight in a healthy adult.\n\nSkeletal muscle cells are heavily packed with mitochondria. During physical exercise and regular movement, these muscles rapidly break down adenosine triphosphate (ATP) to drive contractions. This metabolic process generates an immense amount of thermal energy, serving as the body''s primary mechanism for heat production to help maintain homeostatic core temperature.\n\n• Smooth muscle fiber: Found within the walls of hollow internal organs (like blood vessels and the gastrointestinal tract). Smooth muscle contracts involuntarily and accounts for only a tiny fraction of overall body weight.\n• Cardiac muscle fiber: Highly specialized, involuntary striated muscle tissue found exclusively in the walls of the heart. It does not make up a massive 40% share of body weight.\n• Muscle mass: This is a broad, non-specific macroscopic term measuring total muscular composition rather than defining the specific type of muscle tissue requested by the question.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Smooth muscle fiber, Cardiac muscle fiber, Muscle mass"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the type of muscle that compromises 40% of body weight and is involved in heat production during exercise and motion.:::', 'the type of muscle that compromises 40% of body weight and is involved in heat production during exercise and motion.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In standard anatomy and physiology textbooks, skeletal muscle is the most abundant tissue type in the human body, accounting for approximately 40% of total body weight in a healthy adult.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Skeletal muscle\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In standard anatomy and physiology textbooks, skeletal muscle is the most abundant tissue type in the human body, accounting for approximately 40% of total body weight in a healthy adult.\n\nSkeletal muscle cells are heavily packed with mitochondria. During physical exercise and regular movement, these muscles rapidly break down adenosine triphosphate (ATP) to drive contractions. This metabolic process generates an immense amount of thermal energy, serving as the body''s primary mechanism for heat production to help maintain homeostatic core temperature.\n\n• Smooth muscle fiber: Found within the walls of hollow internal organs (like blood vessels and the gastrointestinal tract). Smooth muscle contracts involuntarily and accounts for only a tiny fraction of overall body weight.\n• Cardiac muscle fiber: Highly specialized, involuntary striated muscle tissue found exclusively in the walls of the heart. It does not make up a massive 40% share of body weight.\n• Muscle mass: This is a broad, non-specific macroscopic term measuring total muscular composition rather than defining the specific type of muscle tissue requested by the question.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Smooth muscle fiber, Cardiac muscle fiber, Muscle mass"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a 60kg female patient with comorbidities needs to lose weight. what is the minimum target weight loss from baseline in 6 months?:::3kg', 'a 60kg female patient with comorbidities needs to lose weight. what is the minimum target weight loss from baseline in 6 months?', '3kg', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to the World Health Organization (WHO) and international clinical obesity guidelines (such as those outlined by the American Heart Association (AHA), American College of Cardiology (ACC), and MIMS Philippines), the standard, primary therapeutic recommendation for an adult with obesity or overweight and comorbidities is to target a minimum initial weight loss of 5% of their baseline body weight over a 6-month period. Achieving a modest 5% reduction is highly significant because it immediately triggers measurable improvements in glycemic control, blood pressure, and cardiovascular risk factors.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"3kg\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to the World Health Organization (WHO) and international clinical obesity guidelines (such as those outlined by the American Heart Association (AHA), American College of Cardiology (ACC), and MIMS Philippines), the standard, primary therapeutic recommendation for an adult with obesity or overweight and comorbidities is to target a minimum initial weight loss of 5% of their baseline body weight over a 6-month period. Achieving a modest 5% reduction is highly significant because it immediately triggers measurable improvements in glycemic control, blood pressure, and cardiovascular risk factors.\n\nPatient Baseline Weight = 60 kg\nMinimum Clinical Target Percentage = 5% (0.05)\n\nMinimum Target Weight Loss = 60 kg x 0.05 = 3 kg","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 6kg, 10kg, 5kg"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:this type of exercise does not require oxygen.:::anaerobic exercise', 'this type of exercise does not require oxygen.', 'Anaerobic exercise', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Anaerobic exercise literally means exercise \"without oxygen.\" During these short, high-intensity bursts of physical activity, the muscles demand energy much faster than the body can transport oxygen to them.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Anaerobic exercise\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Anaerobic exercise literally means exercise \"without oxygen.\" During these short, high-intensity bursts of physical activity, the muscles demand energy much faster than the body can transport oxygen to them.\n\nTo keep up with this extreme demand, the muscle cells rely entirely on immediate energy pathways that do not require oxygen, specifically the phosphagen (ATP-CP) system and anaerobic glycolysis (which breaks down glycogen and produces lactic acid). Examples include heavy weightlifting, sprinting, and high-intensity interval training (HIIT).\n\n• Cardio / Aerobic exercise: \"Aerobic\" literally means \"with oxygen.\" Cardio or aerobic activities (like jogging, cycling, or swimming) are sustained at a lower intensity, allowing the heart and lungs to continuously supply enough oxygen to the muscles to generate energy efficiently.\n• Moderate intensity exercise: Activities performed at a moderate intensity rely primarily on aerobic pathways because the cardiovascular system can successfully keep up with the body''s steady oxygen demands.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Aerobic exercise, Cardio exercise, Moderate intensity exercise"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:this type of exercise does not require oxygen.:::', 'this type of exercise does not require oxygen.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Anaerobic exercise literally means exercise \"without oxygen.\" During these short, high-intensity bursts of physical activity, the muscles demand energy much faster than the body can transport oxygen to them.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Anaerobic exercise\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Anaerobic exercise literally means exercise \"without oxygen.\" During these short, high-intensity bursts of physical activity, the muscles demand energy much faster than the body can transport oxygen to them.\n\nTo keep up with this extreme demand, the muscle cells rely entirely on immediate energy pathways that do not require oxygen, specifically the phosphagen (ATP-CP) system and anaerobic glycolysis (which breaks down glycogen and produces lactic acid). Examples include heavy weightlifting, sprinting, and high-intensity interval training (HIIT).\n\n• Cardio / Aerobic exercise: \"Aerobic\" literally means \"with oxygen.\" Cardio or aerobic activities (like jogging, cycling, or swimming) are sustained at a lower intensity, allowing the heart and lungs to continuously supply enough oxygen to the muscles to generate energy efficiently.\n• Moderate intensity exercise: Activities performed at a moderate intensity rely primarily on aerobic pathways because the cardiovascular system can successfully keep up with the body''s steady oxygen demands.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Aerobic exercise, Cardio exercise, Moderate intensity exercise"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a 35-year old filipina weighs 130lbs with a height measurement of 5 feet. what is her bmi?:::bmi 25.4, obese i', 'a 35-year old filipina weighs 130lbs with a height measurement of 5 feet. what is her bmi?', 'BMI 25.4, Obese I', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In this question, apply the specific Asia-Pacific BMI Classification Guidelines used for Asian populations, which have lower cut-offs due to a higher risk of cardiovascular disease and type 2 diabetes at lower body sizes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"BMI 25.4, Obese I\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In this question, apply the specific Asia-Pacific BMI Classification Guidelines used for Asian populations, which have lower cut-offs due to a higher risk of cardiovascular disease and type 2 diabetes at lower body sizes.\n\n1. Calculate the BMI.\n• Convert weight: 130 lbs / 2.2 = 59.09 kg\n• Convert height: 5 feet = 60 inches = 1.524 meters\n• Calculate BMI:\nBMI = 59.09 / (1.524²) = 25.4 kg/m²\n\n2. The Asia-Pacific Classification \n• Underweight: Below 18.5 kg/m² (Risk of nutritional deficiency and osteoporosis)\n• Normal Range: 18.5 – 22.9 kg/m² (Low health risk)\n• Overweight (At Risk): 23.0 – 24.9 kg/m² (Moderate risk for conditions like type 2 diabetes and high blood pressure)\n• Obese Class I: 25.0 – 29.9 kg/m² (High metabolic and cardiovascular risk)\n• Obese Class II: ≥ 30.0 kg/m² (Very high risk)\n\nBecause a value of 25.4 crosses the 25.0 threshold, the patient is clinically classified as Obese Class I under the regional guidelines rather than just overweight.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: BMI 25.4, Overweight, BMI 23, Normal, BMI 23, Overweight"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a 35-year old filipina weighs 130lbs with a height measurement of 5 feet. what is her bmi?:::', 'a 35-year old filipina weighs 130lbs with a height measurement of 5 feet. what is her bmi?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In this question, apply the specific Asia-Pacific BMI Classification Guidelines used for Asian populations, which have lower cut-offs due to a higher risk of cardiovascular disease and type 2 diabetes at lower body sizes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"BMI 25.4, Obese I\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In this question, apply the specific Asia-Pacific BMI Classification Guidelines used for Asian populations, which have lower cut-offs due to a higher risk of cardiovascular disease and type 2 diabetes at lower body sizes.\n\n1. Calculate the BMI.\n• Convert weight: 130 lbs / 2.2 = 59.09 kg\n• Convert height: 5 feet = 60 inches = 1.524 meters\n• Calculate BMI:\nBMI = 59.09 / (1.524²) = 25.4 kg/m²\n\n2. The Asia-Pacific Classification \n• Underweight: Below 18.5 kg/m² (Risk of nutritional deficiency and osteoporosis)\n• Normal Range: 18.5 – 22.9 kg/m² (Low health risk)\n• Overweight (At Risk): 23.0 – 24.9 kg/m² (Moderate risk for conditions like type 2 diabetes and high blood pressure)\n• Obese Class I: 25.0 – 29.9 kg/m² (High metabolic and cardiovascular risk)\n• Obese Class II: ≥ 30.0 kg/m² (Very high risk)\n\nBecause a value of 25.4 crosses the 25.0 threshold, the patient is clinically classified as Obese Class I under the regional guidelines rather than just overweight.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: BMI 25.4, Overweight, BMI 23, Normal, BMI 23, Overweight"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the hormone decreases appetite and increases the dissipation of energy through heat production also known as thermogenesis.:::leptin', 'the hormone decreases appetite and increases the dissipation of energy through heat production also known as thermogenesis.', 'Leptin', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Leptin is a peptide hormone produced and secreted by adipose tissue (fat cells) that acts directly on the hypothalamus in the brain. It functions as a long-term regulator of energy balance by decreasing appetite (inducing satiety) and simultaneously increasing energy expenditure.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Leptin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Leptin is a peptide hormone produced and secreted by adipose tissue (fat cells) that acts directly on the hypothalamus in the brain. It functions as a long-term regulator of energy balance by decreasing appetite (inducing satiety) and simultaneously increasing energy expenditure.\n\nLeptin stimulates the sympathetic nervous system, which triggers the upregulation of uncoupling proteins (like UCP1) in brown adipose tissue. This process increases the dissipation of stored energy directly as heat (thermogenesis) rather than storing it as fat.\n\n• Cortisol: A glucocorticoid stress hormone that typically increases appetite, favors visceral fat storage, and slows down long-term thermogenic energy expenditure.\n• Ghrelin: Known as the \"hunger hormone,\" ghrelin is produced primarily by the stomach fundus. It increases appetite and promotes energy storage, which is the exact opposite of leptin.\n• Neuropeptide Y: A potent neurotransmitter in the brain that actively stimulates food intake (an orexigenic effect) and decreases energy expenditure.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Neuropeptide Y, Ghrelin, Cortisol"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the hormone decreases appetite and increases the dissipation of energy through heat production also known as thermogenesis.:::', 'the hormone decreases appetite and increases the dissipation of energy through heat production also known as thermogenesis.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Leptin is a peptide hormone produced and secreted by adipose tissue (fat cells) that acts directly on the hypothalamus in the brain. It functions as a long-term regulator of energy balance by decreasing appetite (inducing satiety) and simultaneously increasing energy expenditure.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Leptin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Leptin is a peptide hormone produced and secreted by adipose tissue (fat cells) that acts directly on the hypothalamus in the brain. It functions as a long-term regulator of energy balance by decreasing appetite (inducing satiety) and simultaneously increasing energy expenditure.\n\nLeptin stimulates the sympathetic nervous system, which triggers the upregulation of uncoupling proteins (like UCP1) in brown adipose tissue. This process increases the dissipation of stored energy directly as heat (thermogenesis) rather than storing it as fat.\n\n• Cortisol: A glucocorticoid stress hormone that typically increases appetite, favors visceral fat storage, and slows down long-term thermogenic energy expenditure.\n• Ghrelin: Known as the \"hunger hormone,\" ghrelin is produced primarily by the stomach fundus. It increases appetite and promotes energy storage, which is the exact opposite of leptin.\n• Neuropeptide Y: A potent neurotransmitter in the brain that actively stimulates food intake (an orexigenic effect) and decreases energy expenditure.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Neuropeptide Y, Ghrelin, Cortisol"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are the criteria for anorexia nervosa except:::', 'the following are the criteria for anorexia nervosa except', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Recurrently consuming large amounts of food in a discrete period of time (e.g., within a 2-hour window) accompanied by a perceived lack of control is the fundamental, defining characteristic of Bulimia Nervosa or Binge Eating Disorder. It does not serve as a baseline criterion for Anorexia Nervosa.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Recurrent episodes of binge eating within 2 hours\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Recurrently consuming large amounts of food in a discrete period of time (e.g., within a 2-hour window) accompanied by a perceived lack of control is the fundamental, defining characteristic of Bulimia Nervosa or Binge Eating Disorder. It does not serve as a baseline criterion for Anorexia Nervosa.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Intense fear of weight gain, Amenorrhea, Denial"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:after a meal, our body’s energy expenditure increases for some time to breakdown the food into nutrients that the body can utilize and store. this process is known as:::thermogenic effect of food', 'after a meal, our body’s energy expenditure increases for some time to breakdown the food into nutrients that the body can utilize and store. this process is known as', 'Thermogenic Effect of Food', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The Thermogenic Effect of Food (TEF)—also traditionally referred to as the Specific Dynamic Action (SDA) of food—is the exact physiological process where energy expenditure rises above baseline for several hours following a meal. This metabolic energy is spent on the mechanical and chemical work of digesting, absorbing, transporting, metabolizing, and storing nutrients.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Thermogenic Effect of Food\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The Thermogenic Effect of Food (TEF)—also traditionally referred to as the Specific Dynamic Action (SDA) of food—is the exact physiological process where energy expenditure rises above baseline for several hours following a meal. This metabolic energy is spent on the mechanical and chemical work of digesting, absorbing, transporting, metabolizing, and storing nutrients.\n\nTEF accounts for roughly 10% of total daily energy expenditure in a standard diet. Protein triggers the highest thermic response (requiring 20–30% of its own energy to process), followed by carbohydrates (5–15%), and fats (0–3%).\n\n*While TEF is a form of thermogenesis, \"thermogenesis\" on its own is a wide category that also includes non-shivering thermogenesis (heat production from brown fat) and exercise-induced thermogenesis. Thermogenic Effect of Food (TEF) is the precise, targeted metabolic term for post-meal energy expenditure.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Metabolism, Digestion, Thermogenesis"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:after a meal, our body’s energy expenditure increases for some time to breakdown the food into nutrients that the body can utilize and store. this process is known as:::', 'after a meal, our body’s energy expenditure increases for some time to breakdown the food into nutrients that the body can utilize and store. this process is known as', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The Thermogenic Effect of Food (TEF)—also traditionally referred to as the Specific Dynamic Action (SDA) of food—is the exact physiological process where energy expenditure rises above baseline for several hours following a meal. This metabolic energy is spent on the mechanical and chemical work of digesting, absorbing, transporting, metabolizing, and storing nutrients.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Thermogenic Effect of Food\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The Thermogenic Effect of Food (TEF)—also traditionally referred to as the Specific Dynamic Action (SDA) of food—is the exact physiological process where energy expenditure rises above baseline for several hours following a meal. This metabolic energy is spent on the mechanical and chemical work of digesting, absorbing, transporting, metabolizing, and storing nutrients.\n\nTEF accounts for roughly 10% of total daily energy expenditure in a standard diet. Protein triggers the highest thermic response (requiring 20–30% of its own energy to process), followed by carbohydrates (5–15%), and fats (0–3%).\n\n*While TEF is a form of thermogenesis, \"thermogenesis\" on its own is a wide category that also includes non-shivering thermogenesis (heat production from brown fat) and exercise-induced thermogenesis. Thermogenic Effect of Food (TEF) is the precise, targeted metabolic term for post-meal energy expenditure.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Metabolism, Digestion, Thermogenesis"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:abundant uncoupling proteins in the body prevents weight gain. which type of fat releases stored energy and is considered metabolically active?:::brown adipose tissue', 'abundant uncoupling proteins in the body prevents weight gain. which type of fat releases stored energy and is considered metabolically active?', 'Brown adipose tissue', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Brown adipose tissue (BAT) contains a very high density of mitochondria packed with Uncoupling Protein 1 (also known as thermogenin). This protein uncouples the respiratory chain from ATP synthesis, causing the fat tissue to burn through stored lipids and glucose to release stored energy directly as heat (non-shivering thermogenesis) rather than storing it as chemical energy.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Brown adipose tissue\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Brown adipose tissue (BAT) contains a very high density of mitochondria packed with Uncoupling Protein 1 (also known as thermogenin). This protein uncouples the respiratory chain from ATP synthesis, causing the fat tissue to burn through stored lipids and glucose to release stored energy directly as heat (non-shivering thermogenesis) rather than storing it as chemical energy.\n\nBecause BAT actively expends calories to generate heat, an abundance or activation of brown fat significantly increases basal metabolic rate and helps prevent weight gain and metabolic dysfunction.\n\n• Beige adipose tissue: While beige fat cells also express UCP1 and burn energy, they are individual brown-like cells that develop within white adipose tissue clusters (a process called \"browning\") rather than forming the distinct, highly abundant classic brown fat depots primarily responsible for massive thermogenic dissipation.\n• Yellow adipose tissue: This is simply an alternative structural name for standard adult white fat, colored yellow due to the accumulation of dietary carotenes.\n• White adipose tissue: The primary site for long-term energy storage (triglycerides). White fat is not built for heat generation and expands during weight gain rather than preventing it.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Beige adipose tissue, Yellow adipose tissue, White adipose tissue"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:abundant uncoupling proteins in the body prevents weight gain. which type of fat releases stored energy and is considered metabolically active?:::', 'abundant uncoupling proteins in the body prevents weight gain. which type of fat releases stored energy and is considered metabolically active?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Brown adipose tissue (BAT) contains a very high density of mitochondria packed with Uncoupling Protein 1 (also known as thermogenin). This protein uncouples the respiratory chain from ATP synthesis, causing the fat tissue to burn through stored lipids and glucose to release stored energy directly as heat (non-shivering thermogenesis) rather than storing it as chemical energy.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Brown adipose tissue\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Brown adipose tissue (BAT) contains a very high density of mitochondria packed with Uncoupling Protein 1 (also known as thermogenin). This protein uncouples the respiratory chain from ATP synthesis, causing the fat tissue to burn through stored lipids and glucose to release stored energy directly as heat (non-shivering thermogenesis) rather than storing it as chemical energy.\n\nBecause BAT actively expends calories to generate heat, an abundance or activation of brown fat significantly increases basal metabolic rate and helps prevent weight gain and metabolic dysfunction.\n\n• Beige adipose tissue: While beige fat cells also express UCP1 and burn energy, they are individual brown-like cells that develop within white adipose tissue clusters (a process called \"browning\") rather than forming the distinct, highly abundant classic brown fat depots primarily responsible for massive thermogenic dissipation.\n• Yellow adipose tissue: This is simply an alternative structural name for standard adult white fat, colored yellow due to the accumulation of dietary carotenes.\n• White adipose tissue: The primary site for long-term energy storage (triglycerides). White fat is not built for heat generation and expands during weight gain rather than preventing it.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Beige adipose tissue, Yellow adipose tissue, White adipose tissue"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a dietitian needs to determine a patient’s risk for cardiovascular disease. the following anthropometric measurements must be assessed except:::exercise history', 'a dietitian needs to determine a patient’s risk for cardiovascular disease. the following anthropometric measurements must be assessed except', 'Exercise history', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Exercise history is a behavioral or lifestyle component of a patient''s personal history, not a physical bodily measurement.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Exercise history\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Exercise history is a behavioral or lifestyle component of a patient''s personal history, not a physical bodily measurement.\n\nAnthropometric measurements are non-invasive, quantitative, physical measurements used to assess the size, shape, and composition of the human body.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Height and weight, Waist Circumference, Body fat analysis"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a 45 years old female has a whr of 0.9 is:::all of the above', 'a 45 years old female has a whr of 0.9 is', 'All of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to the World Health Organization (WHO), a Waist-to-Hip Ratio (WHR) above 0.85 for females signifies abdominal (visceral) obesity. A 45-year-old female presenting with a WHR of 0.9 falls into a high-risk classification (apple-shaped fat distribution).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to the World Health Organization (WHO), a Waist-to-Hip Ratio (WHR) above 0.85 for females signifies abdominal (visceral) obesity. A 45-year-old female presenting with a WHR of 0.9 falls into a high-risk classification (apple-shaped fat distribution).\n\nVisceral fat accumulation surrounding the internal organs is highly metabolically active. It releases excess free fatty acids and inflammatory cytokines directly into the portal circulation, precipitating:\n\t• Insulin resistance, which significantly increases the risk for Diabetes mellitus.\n\t• Altered hepatic lipid synthesis, leading directly to elevated blood lipids, specifically placing the patient at risk for high triglycerides and hypercholesterolemia.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: At risk for high triglycerides, At risk for hypercholesterolemia, At risk for diabetes mellitus"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a 60kg female patient with comorbidities needs to lose weight. what is the minimum target weight loss from baseline in 6 months?:::', 'a 60kg female patient with comorbidities needs to lose weight. what is the minimum target weight loss from baseline in 6 months?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to the World Health Organization (WHO) and international clinical obesity guidelines (such as those outlined by the American Heart Association (AHA), American College of Cardiology (ACC), and MIMS Philippines), the standard, primary therapeutic recommendation for an adult with obesity or overweight and comorbidities is to target a minimum initial weight loss of 5% of their baseline body weight over a 6-month period. Achieving a modest 5% reduction is highly significant because it immediately triggers measurable improvements in glycemic control, blood pressure, and cardiovascular risk factors.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"3kg\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to the World Health Organization (WHO) and international clinical obesity guidelines (such as those outlined by the American Heart Association (AHA), American College of Cardiology (ACC), and MIMS Philippines), the standard, primary therapeutic recommendation for an adult with obesity or overweight and comorbidities is to target a minimum initial weight loss of 5% of their baseline body weight over a 6-month period. Achieving a modest 5% reduction is highly significant because it immediately triggers measurable improvements in glycemic control, blood pressure, and cardiovascular risk factors.\n\nPatient Baseline Weight = 60 kg\nMinimum Clinical Target Percentage = 5% (0.05)\n\nMinimum Target Weight Loss = 60 kg x 0.05 = 3 kg","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 6kg, 10kg, 5kg"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a 45 years old female has a whr of 0.9 is:::', 'a 45 years old female has a whr of 0.9 is', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to the World Health Organization (WHO), a Waist-to-Hip Ratio (WHR) above 0.85 for females signifies abdominal (visceral) obesity. A 45-year-old female presenting with a WHR of 0.9 falls into a high-risk classification (apple-shaped fat distribution).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to the World Health Organization (WHO), a Waist-to-Hip Ratio (WHR) above 0.85 for females signifies abdominal (visceral) obesity. A 45-year-old female presenting with a WHR of 0.9 falls into a high-risk classification (apple-shaped fat distribution).\n\nVisceral fat accumulation surrounding the internal organs is highly metabolically active. It releases excess free fatty acids and inflammatory cytokines directly into the portal circulation, precipitating:\n\t• Insulin resistance, which significantly increases the risk for Diabetes mellitus.\n\t• Altered hepatic lipid synthesis, leading directly to elevated blood lipids, specifically placing the patient at risk for high triglycerides and hypercholesterolemia.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: At risk for high triglycerides, At risk for hypercholesterolemia, At risk for diabetes mellitus"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are comorbidities related to obesity except:::psoriasis', 'the following are comorbidities related to obesity except', 'Psoriasis', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Psoriasis is a primary autoimmune skin disease characterized by rapid skin cell buildup that leads to scaling and inflammation. While obesity is a strong independent risk factor that can aggravate or worsen psoriasis through shared systemic inflammatory pathways, psoriasis itself is classified primarily as an autoimmune/genetic condition rather than a direct mechanical or metabolic comorbidity caused by obesity.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Psoriasis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Psoriasis is a primary autoimmune skin disease characterized by rapid skin cell buildup that leads to scaling and inflammation. While obesity is a strong independent risk factor that can aggravate or worsen psoriasis through shared systemic inflammatory pathways, psoriasis itself is classified primarily as an autoimmune/genetic condition rather than a direct mechanical or metabolic comorbidity caused by obesity.\n\n\t• Sleep apnea: Heavily driven by mechanical forces; excess fat deposits around the upper airway and neck physically compress the trachea during sleep, causing obstructive sleep apnea.\n\t• Osteoarthritis: Driven by both increased mechanical wear-and-tear on weight-bearing joints (such as knees and hips) and low-grade systemic inflammation that degrades cartilage.\n\t• Gallstones: Driven by metabolic shifts; obesity increases hepatic synthesis and secretion of cholesterol, supersaturating the bile and causing cholesterol gallstones to form.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sleep apnea, Gallstones, Osteoarthritis"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are comorbidities related to obesity except:::', 'the following are comorbidities related to obesity except', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Psoriasis is a primary autoimmune skin disease characterized by rapid skin cell buildup that leads to scaling and inflammation. While obesity is a strong independent risk factor that can aggravate or worsen psoriasis through shared systemic inflammatory pathways, psoriasis itself is classified primarily as an autoimmune/genetic condition rather than a direct mechanical or metabolic comorbidity caused by obesity.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Psoriasis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Psoriasis is a primary autoimmune skin disease characterized by rapid skin cell buildup that leads to scaling and inflammation. While obesity is a strong independent risk factor that can aggravate or worsen psoriasis through shared systemic inflammatory pathways, psoriasis itself is classified primarily as an autoimmune/genetic condition rather than a direct mechanical or metabolic comorbidity caused by obesity.\n\n\t• Sleep apnea: Heavily driven by mechanical forces; excess fat deposits around the upper airway and neck physically compress the trachea during sleep, causing obstructive sleep apnea.\n\t• Osteoarthritis: Driven by both increased mechanical wear-and-tear on weight-bearing joints (such as knees and hips) and low-grade systemic inflammation that degrades cartilage.\n\t• Gallstones: Driven by metabolic shifts; obesity increases hepatic synthesis and secretion of cholesterol, supersaturating the bile and causing cholesterol gallstones to form.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sleep apnea, Gallstones, Osteoarthritis"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:choose the statement that best represents the patient’s readiness for lifestyle change:::“please recommend some tips on how to do grocery shopping for fresh produce.”', 'choose the statement that best represents the patient’s readiness for lifestyle change', '“Please recommend some tips on how to do grocery shopping for fresh produce.”', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In behavioral counseling—specifically the Transtheoretical Model (Stages of Change)—this statement indicates that the patient has moved past merely thinking about a change and is actively preparing to execute it. By asking for specific, actionable grocery shopping strategies, they are demonstrating a high level of autonomous motivation and a direct readiness to implement lifestyle changes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"“Please recommend some tips on how to do grocery shopping for fresh produce.”\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In behavioral counseling—specifically the Transtheoretical Model (Stages of Change)—this statement indicates that the patient has moved past merely thinking about a change and is actively preparing to execute it. By asking for specific, actionable grocery shopping strategies, they are demonstrating a high level of autonomous motivation and a direct readiness to implement lifestyle changes.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: “I need to lose weight.”, “My doctor says I need to consult a dietitian.”, “How do I cook ginisang munggo?”"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:choose the statement that best represents the patient’s readiness for lifestyle change:::', 'choose the statement that best represents the patient’s readiness for lifestyle change', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In behavioral counseling—specifically the Transtheoretical Model (Stages of Change)—this statement indicates that the patient has moved past merely thinking about a change and is actively preparing to execute it. By asking for specific, actionable grocery shopping strategies, they are demonstrating a high level of autonomous motivation and a direct readiness to implement lifestyle changes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"“Please recommend some tips on how to do grocery shopping for fresh produce.”\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In behavioral counseling—specifically the Transtheoretical Model (Stages of Change)—this statement indicates that the patient has moved past merely thinking about a change and is actively preparing to execute it. By asking for specific, actionable grocery shopping strategies, they are demonstrating a high level of autonomous motivation and a direct readiness to implement lifestyle changes.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: “I need to lose weight.”, “My doctor says I need to consult a dietitian.”, “How do I cook ginisang munggo?”"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following recommendations for weight loss must be given by the rnd except:::take orlistat 2 to 3 times a day with meals', 'the following recommendations for weight loss must be given by the rnd except', 'Take Orlistat 2 to 3 times a day with meals', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Orlistat is a potent anti-obesity pharmacological drug. Under the legal and professional scope of practice for a Registered Nutritionist-Dietitian (RND) in clinical and public health frameworks, a dietitian is strictly authorized to provide Medical Nutrition Therapy (MNT), structural caloric adjustments, lifestyle modifications, and behavioral counseling. An RND cannot prescribe medications or specify pharmaceutical drug dosages. The prescription, initiation, and dosing oversight of anti-obesity drugs like Orlistat must be managed exclusively by a licensed physician or endocrinologist.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Take Orlistat 2 to 3 times a day with meals\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Orlistat is a potent anti-obesity pharmacological drug. Under the legal and professional scope of practice for a Registered Nutritionist-Dietitian (RND) in clinical and public health frameworks, a dietitian is strictly authorized to provide Medical Nutrition Therapy (MNT), structural caloric adjustments, lifestyle modifications, and behavioral counseling. An RND cannot prescribe medications or specify pharmaceutical drug dosages. The prescription, initiation, and dosing oversight of anti-obesity drugs like Orlistat must be managed exclusively by a licensed physician or endocrinologist.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: At least 30 minutes of moderate physical activity daily, Decrease Total Energy Requirement by 500 Kcal per day, Self-monitoring"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following recommendations for weight loss must be given by the rnd except:::', 'the following recommendations for weight loss must be given by the rnd except', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Orlistat is a potent anti-obesity pharmacological drug. Under the legal and professional scope of practice for a Registered Nutritionist-Dietitian (RND) in clinical and public health frameworks, a dietitian is strictly authorized to provide Medical Nutrition Therapy (MNT), structural caloric adjustments, lifestyle modifications, and behavioral counseling. An RND cannot prescribe medications or specify pharmaceutical drug dosages. The prescription, initiation, and dosing oversight of anti-obesity drugs like Orlistat must be managed exclusively by a licensed physician or endocrinologist.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Take Orlistat 2 to 3 times a day with meals\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Orlistat is a potent anti-obesity pharmacological drug. Under the legal and professional scope of practice for a Registered Nutritionist-Dietitian (RND) in clinical and public health frameworks, a dietitian is strictly authorized to provide Medical Nutrition Therapy (MNT), structural caloric adjustments, lifestyle modifications, and behavioral counseling. An RND cannot prescribe medications or specify pharmaceutical drug dosages. The prescription, initiation, and dosing oversight of anti-obesity drugs like Orlistat must be managed exclusively by a licensed physician or endocrinologist.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: At least 30 minutes of moderate physical activity daily, Decrease Total Energy Requirement by 500 Kcal per day, Self-monitoring"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:subs are::::', 'subs are:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Subs are sandwiches also known as hoagies or heroes, depending on regional terminology. Therefore all the listed descriptions apply.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"all of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Subs are sandwiches also known as hoagies or heroes, depending on regional terminology. Therefore all the listed descriptions apply.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Hoagies, Heros, Sandwiches"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which weight loss medications are approved by the us-fda?:::phentermine, orlistat, topamirate', 'which weight loss medications are approved by the us-fda?', 'Phentermine, orlistat, topamirate', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"There are well-established medications officially approved by the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) for weight management:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Phentermine, orlistat, topamirate\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"There are well-established medications officially approved by the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) for weight management:\n\t• Phentermine: Approved as a short-term, standalone appetite suppressant.\n\t• Orlistat: Approved for long-term weight management (available as prescription Xenical or over-the-counter Alli) by blocking intestinal fat absorption.\n\t• Topiramate: Approved by the FDA as a fixed-dose combination pill with phentermine (marketed under the brand name Qsymia) for chronic weight management.\n\nOther Choices:\n• Phenylpropanolamine: This drug was completely banned and ordered removed from the market by the FDA due to an increased risk of hemorrhagic stroke.\n• Sibutramine: Originally approved for weight loss under the name Meridia, it was completely withdrawn from the market due to its association with an increased risk of heart attacks and strokes.\n• Ibuprofen: This is a standard nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) used to relieve pain and reduce fever, carrying no clinical indication or approval for weight loss.\n• Aminorex: An amphetamine-like stimulant that was never FDA-approved for weight loss and was withdrawn globally after it was found to cause severe pulmonary hypertension.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Phentermine, phenylpropanolamine, orlistat, Phenylpropanolamine, topamirate, sibutramine, Ibuprofen, aminorex, orlistat"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which weight loss medications are approved by the us-fda?:::', 'which weight loss medications are approved by the us-fda?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"There are well-established medications officially approved by the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) for weight management:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Phentermine, orlistat, topamirate\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"There are well-established medications officially approved by the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) for weight management:\n\t• Phentermine: Approved as a short-term, standalone appetite suppressant.\n\t• Orlistat: Approved for long-term weight management (available as prescription Xenical or over-the-counter Alli) by blocking intestinal fat absorption.\n\t• Topiramate: Approved by the FDA as a fixed-dose combination pill with phentermine (marketed under the brand name Qsymia) for chronic weight management.\n\nOther Choices:\n• Phenylpropanolamine: This drug was completely banned and ordered removed from the market by the FDA due to an increased risk of hemorrhagic stroke.\n• Sibutramine: Originally approved for weight loss under the name Meridia, it was completely withdrawn from the market due to its association with an increased risk of heart attacks and strokes.\n• Ibuprofen: This is a standard nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) used to relieve pain and reduce fever, carrying no clinical indication or approval for weight loss.\n• Aminorex: An amphetamine-like stimulant that was never FDA-approved for weight loss and was withdrawn globally after it was found to cause severe pulmonary hypertension.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Phentermine, phenylpropanolamine, orlistat, Phenylpropanolamine, topamirate, sibutramine, Ibuprofen, aminorex, orlistat"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the one animal polysaccharide that is not present in foods but is generally stored in the liver and muscle::::glycogen', 'the one animal polysaccharide that is not present in foods but is generally stored in the liver and muscle:', 'Glycogen', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Glycogen is the storage form of carbohydrates in animals.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Glycogen\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Glycogen is the storage form of carbohydrates in animals.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Lignin, Polymer, Lecithin"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:criteria for bariatric surgery::::all of the above', 'criteria for bariatric surgery:', 'All of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Historically, candidate selection for metabolic and bariatric surgery has been anchored on established body size and health criteria.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Historically, candidate selection for metabolic and bariatric surgery has been anchored on established body size and health criteria.\n\t• A BMI between 35 and 39.9 with significant medical comorbidities (such as type 2 diabetes, severe sleep apnea, or cardiovascular disease) serves as a primary qualification parameter.\n\t• A BMI of 40 or greater represents severe obesity and satisfies the selection criteria independently, with or without secondary medical complications.\n\t• A BMI of 36 paired with Atherosclerosis fits precisely into the definition of the other option (a BMI above 35 accompanied by a serious cardiovascular comorbidity).\n\nClinical Update\n\nWhile older textbook questions focus heavily on this classic \"35 with comorbidities / 40 baseline\" framework, updated international surgical consensus guidelines from the American Society for Metabolic and Bariatric Surgery (ASMBS) and IFSO have expanded eligibility. Modern criteria now recommend bariatric surgery for anyone with a BMI over 35 regardless of comorbidities, and down to a BMI of 30 for individuals struggling with metabolic diseases like uncontrolled type 2 diabetes.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: BMI greater than 40, BMI greater than 35 with comorbidities, BMI 36 with Atherosclerosis"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:criteria for bariatric surgery::::', 'criteria for bariatric surgery:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Historically, candidate selection for metabolic and bariatric surgery has been anchored on established body size and health criteria.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Historically, candidate selection for metabolic and bariatric surgery has been anchored on established body size and health criteria.\n\t• A BMI between 35 and 39.9 with significant medical comorbidities (such as type 2 diabetes, severe sleep apnea, or cardiovascular disease) serves as a primary qualification parameter.\n\t• A BMI of 40 or greater represents severe obesity and satisfies the selection criteria independently, with or without secondary medical complications.\n\t• A BMI of 36 paired with Atherosclerosis fits precisely into the definition of the other option (a BMI above 35 accompanied by a serious cardiovascular comorbidity).\n\nClinical Update\n\nWhile older textbook questions focus heavily on this classic \"35 with comorbidities / 40 baseline\" framework, updated international surgical consensus guidelines from the American Society for Metabolic and Bariatric Surgery (ASMBS) and IFSO have expanded eligibility. Modern criteria now recommend bariatric surgery for anyone with a BMI over 35 regardless of comorbidities, and down to a BMI of 30 for individuals struggling with metabolic diseases like uncontrolled type 2 diabetes.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: BMI greater than 40, BMI greater than 35 with comorbidities, BMI 36 with Atherosclerosis"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:this type of bariatric surgery requires the placement of a foreign object in the form of a band:::lagb', 'this type of bariatric surgery requires the placement of a foreign object in the form of a band', 'LAGB', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"LAGB stands for Laparoscopic Adjustable Gastric Banding (commonly referred to by its commercial name, the Lap-Band).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"LAGB\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"LAGB stands for Laparoscopic Adjustable Gastric Banding (commonly referred to by its commercial name, the Lap-Band).\n\nThis specific bariatric procedure involves placing an inflatable, medical-grade silicone band entirely around the upper portion of the stomach. This creates a tiny stomach pouch that drastically restricts the volume of food a patient can eat at one time, forcing early satiety without removing any actual gastric tissue.\n\n• Roux-en-Y Gastric Bypass: A permanent, combination surgery where the stomach is stapled to create a small pouch and the small intestine is physically rerouted, requiring no baseline foreign bands.\n• BPD/DS: Biliopancreatic Diversion with Duodenal Switch is a complex, mostly malabsorptive surgery that resects a large portion of the stomach and drastically bypasses the small intestine.\n• Gastric Restrictive Procedure: This is the general umbrella category term for any surgery that limits stomach capacity (including the sleeve and vertical banded gastroplasty), whereas LAGB is the specific, targeted procedure abbreviation requested by the question.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Roux-en-Y Gastric Bypass, Gastric Restrictive Procedure, BPD/DS"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:this type of bariatric surgery requires the placement of a foreign object in the form of a band:::', 'this type of bariatric surgery requires the placement of a foreign object in the form of a band', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"LAGB stands for Laparoscopic Adjustable Gastric Banding (commonly referred to by its commercial name, the Lap-Band).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"LAGB\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"LAGB stands for Laparoscopic Adjustable Gastric Banding (commonly referred to by its commercial name, the Lap-Band).\n\nThis specific bariatric procedure involves placing an inflatable, medical-grade silicone band entirely around the upper portion of the stomach. This creates a tiny stomach pouch that drastically restricts the volume of food a patient can eat at one time, forcing early satiety without removing any actual gastric tissue.\n\n• Roux-en-Y Gastric Bypass: A permanent, combination surgery where the stomach is stapled to create a small pouch and the small intestine is physically rerouted, requiring no baseline foreign bands.\n• BPD/DS: Biliopancreatic Diversion with Duodenal Switch is a complex, mostly malabsorptive surgery that resects a large portion of the stomach and drastically bypasses the small intestine.\n• Gastric Restrictive Procedure: This is the general umbrella category term for any surgery that limits stomach capacity (including the sleeve and vertical banded gastroplasty), whereas LAGB is the specific, targeted procedure abbreviation requested by the question.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Roux-en-Y Gastric Bypass, Gastric Restrictive Procedure, BPD/DS"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:combination procedure in bariatric surgery results to hormonal influences. the production of ghrelin hormone is likewise decreased resulting to:::induced satiety', 'combination procedure in bariatric surgery results to hormonal influences. the production of ghrelin hormone is likewise decreased resulting to', 'Induced satiety', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Ghrelin is known as the orexigenic hormone (hunger hormone) that stimulates appetite and food intake. Because bariatric procedures (such as a sleeve gastrectomy or gastric bypass) involve anatomical resection or exclusion of the gastric fundus—where the vast majority of ghrelin is produced—the circulating levels of this hormone drop sharply post-surgery.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Induced satiety\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Ghrelin is known as the orexigenic hormone (hunger hormone) that stimulates appetite and food intake. Because bariatric procedures (such as a sleeve gastrectomy or gastric bypass) involve anatomical resection or exclusion of the gastric fundus—where the vast majority of ghrelin is produced—the circulating levels of this hormone drop sharply post-surgery.\n\nA significant reduction in ghrelin eliminates the biological signal that triggers hunger, thereby inducing satiety (prolonged feelings of fullness and diminished appetite).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Increased appetite, Orexigenic effect, Dumping syndrome"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:combination procedure in bariatric surgery results to hormonal influences. the production of ghrelin hormone is likewise decreased resulting to:::', 'combination procedure in bariatric surgery results to hormonal influences. the production of ghrelin hormone is likewise decreased resulting to', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Ghrelin is known as the orexigenic hormone (hunger hormone) that stimulates appetite and food intake. Because bariatric procedures (such as a sleeve gastrectomy or gastric bypass) involve anatomical resection or exclusion of the gastric fundus—where the vast majority of ghrelin is produced—the circulating levels of this hormone drop sharply post-surgery.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Induced satiety\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Ghrelin is known as the orexigenic hormone (hunger hormone) that stimulates appetite and food intake. Because bariatric procedures (such as a sleeve gastrectomy or gastric bypass) involve anatomical resection or exclusion of the gastric fundus—where the vast majority of ghrelin is produced—the circulating levels of this hormone drop sharply post-surgery.\n\nA significant reduction in ghrelin eliminates the biological signal that triggers hunger, thereby inducing satiety (prolonged feelings of fullness and diminished appetite).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Increased appetite, Orexigenic effect, Dumping syndrome"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which type of bariatric surgery can possibly exacerbate symptoms of gastroesophageal reflux?:::sleeve gastrectomy', 'which type of bariatric surgery can possibly exacerbate symptoms of gastroesophageal reflux?', 'Sleeve Gastrectomy', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A Sleeve Gastrectomy (or vertical sleeve gastrectomy) involves removing approximately 80% of the stomach, converting the remaining portion into a narrow, tube-like structure. This anatomical change drastically increases internal pressure within the stomach tube.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sleeve Gastrectomy\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A Sleeve Gastrectomy (or vertical sleeve gastrectomy) involves removing approximately 80% of the stomach, converting the remaining portion into a narrow, tube-like structure. This anatomical change drastically increases internal pressure within the stomach tube.\n\nThe high intragastric pressure, paired with changes to the angle where the esophagus meets the stomach (the angle of His) and potential weakening of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES), commonly causes de novo (new-onset) acid reflux or severely exacerbates preexisting symptoms of Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD).\n\n• Roux-en-Y Gastric Bypass: This is considered the gold-standard bariatric procedure for patients with severe GERD. Because it separates the acid-producing cells into a bypassed pouch and creates a low-pressure drainage system, it actively cures or significantly improves reflux symptoms. It is frequently used as a corrective surgery for sleeve patients who develop uncontrollable reflux.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Laparoscopic Adjustable Gastric Banding, Roux-en-Y Gastric Bypass, Biliopancreatic Diversion with Duodenal Switch"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which type of bariatric surgery can possibly exacerbate symptoms of gastroesophageal reflux?:::', 'which type of bariatric surgery can possibly exacerbate symptoms of gastroesophageal reflux?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A Sleeve Gastrectomy (or vertical sleeve gastrectomy) involves removing approximately 80% of the stomach, converting the remaining portion into a narrow, tube-like structure. This anatomical change drastically increases internal pressure within the stomach tube.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sleeve Gastrectomy\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A Sleeve Gastrectomy (or vertical sleeve gastrectomy) involves removing approximately 80% of the stomach, converting the remaining portion into a narrow, tube-like structure. This anatomical change drastically increases internal pressure within the stomach tube.\n\nThe high intragastric pressure, paired with changes to the angle where the esophagus meets the stomach (the angle of His) and potential weakening of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES), commonly causes de novo (new-onset) acid reflux or severely exacerbates preexisting symptoms of Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD).\n\n• Roux-en-Y Gastric Bypass: This is considered the gold-standard bariatric procedure for patients with severe GERD. Because it separates the acid-producing cells into a bypassed pouch and creates a low-pressure drainage system, it actively cures or significantly improves reflux symptoms. It is frequently used as a corrective surgery for sleeve patients who develop uncontrollable reflux.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Laparoscopic Adjustable Gastric Banding, Roux-en-Y Gastric Bypass, Biliopancreatic Diversion with Duodenal Switch"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the normal range of body temperature is:::', 'the normal range of body temperature is', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"While 37°C (98.6°F) is universally recognized as the standard average human body temperature, normal physiological baselines safely fluctuate within a broader dynamic range.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"36.1 to 37.2 degrees Celsius\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"While 37°C (98.6°F) is universally recognized as the standard average human body temperature, normal physiological baselines safely fluctuate within a broader dynamic range.\n\nThe medically accepted range of 97°F to 99°F directly converts to 36.1°C to 37.2°C. Temperatures within this bracket are considered afebrile (normal and without fever).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 35 to 37 degrees Celsius, 35.5 to 37.4 degrees Celsius, 36 to 36.8 degrees Celsius"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb)
