nb),
('mc:a patient on chemotherapy is complaining of mouth sores. the rnd must recommend:::cold beverages', 'a patient on chemotherapy is complaining of mouth sores. the rnd must recommend', 'Cold beverages', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Chemotherapy frequently induces oral mucositis, which causes the mucosal lining of the mouth to become highly inflamed, ulcerated, and painful. Cold beverages (and ice chips, popsicles, or chilled soft foods) help vasoconstrict the blood vessels in the oral cavity, which actively numbs the pain, reduces localized inflammation, and provides immediate soothing comfort.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Cold beverages\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Chemotherapy frequently induces oral mucositis, which causes the mucosal lining of the mouth to become highly inflamed, ulcerated, and painful. Cold beverages (and ice chips, popsicles, or chilled soft foods) help vasoconstrict the blood vessels in the oral cavity, which actively numbs the pain, reduces localized inflammation, and provides immediate soothing comfort.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Warm chicken soup, Ginger tea, Sinigang"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a patient on chemotherapy is complaining of mouth sores. the rnd must recommend:::', 'a patient on chemotherapy is complaining of mouth sores. the rnd must recommend', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Chemotherapy frequently induces oral mucositis, which causes the mucosal lining of the mouth to become highly inflamed, ulcerated, and painful. Cold beverages (and ice chips, popsicles, or chilled soft foods) help vasoconstrict the blood vessels in the oral cavity, which actively numbs the pain, reduces localized inflammation, and provides immediate soothing comfort.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Cold beverages\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Chemotherapy frequently induces oral mucositis, which causes the mucosal lining of the mouth to become highly inflamed, ulcerated, and painful. Cold beverages (and ice chips, popsicles, or chilled soft foods) help vasoconstrict the blood vessels in the oral cavity, which actively numbs the pain, reduces localized inflammation, and provides immediate soothing comfort.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Warm chicken soup, Ginger tea, Sinigang"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:fatty acids present in animal fats are said to be::::', 'fatty acids present in animal fats are said to be:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In animals, the carbon atoms in the fatty acids are saturated with hydrogen atoms. This allows them to lie straight, and the molecules will pack neatly into a solid arrangement.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Saturated fatty acids\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In animals, the carbon atoms in the fatty acids are saturated with hydrogen atoms. This allows them to lie straight, and the molecules will pack neatly into a solid arrangement.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Containing hundreds of double bonds, Linoleic acid, Unsaturated fatty acids"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a patient with brain cancer undergoing radiation therapy may experience the following nutrition related side effects except:::insomnia', 'a patient with brain cancer undergoing radiation therapy may experience the following nutrition related side effects except', 'Insomnia', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"While insomnia (difficulty falling or staying asleep) can certainly affect cancer patients due to general psychological stress, anxiety, or specific medications like high-dose corticosteroids (e.g., dexamethasone), it is classified primarily as a sleep/psychological disorder. It is not a direct nutrition-related side effect caused by localized radiation tissue damage.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Insomnia\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"While insomnia (difficulty falling or staying asleep) can certainly affect cancer patients due to general psychological stress, anxiety, or specific medications like high-dose corticosteroids (e.g., dexamethasone), it is classified primarily as a sleep/psychological disorder. It is not a direct nutrition-related side effect caused by localized radiation tissue damage.\n\n\t• Loss of appetite: Extremely common during brain radiation due to treatment-induced nausea, altered metabolism, or increased intracranial pressure.\n\t• Dry mouth: Radiation delivered to the head region can inadvertently clip or irritate adjacent salivary gland structures, leading to a temporary or persistent decline in saliva production (xerostomia), which directly impairs chewing and swallowing.\n\t• Anosmia and Dysgeusia: Cranial radiation therapy can directly disrupt olfactory nerve pathways and taste buds, causing anosmia (loss of smell) and dysgeusia (distortion of taste), which severely decreases the palatability of food.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Loss of appetite, Anosmia and Dysgeusia, Dry mouth"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a patient with brain cancer undergoing radiation therapy may experience the following nutrition related side effects except:::', 'a patient with brain cancer undergoing radiation therapy may experience the following nutrition related side effects except', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"While insomnia (difficulty falling or staying asleep) can certainly affect cancer patients due to general psychological stress, anxiety, or specific medications like high-dose corticosteroids (e.g., dexamethasone), it is classified primarily as a sleep/psychological disorder. It is not a direct nutrition-related side effect caused by localized radiation tissue damage.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Insomnia\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"While insomnia (difficulty falling or staying asleep) can certainly affect cancer patients due to general psychological stress, anxiety, or specific medications like high-dose corticosteroids (e.g., dexamethasone), it is classified primarily as a sleep/psychological disorder. It is not a direct nutrition-related side effect caused by localized radiation tissue damage.\n\n\t• Loss of appetite: Extremely common during brain radiation due to treatment-induced nausea, altered metabolism, or increased intracranial pressure.\n\t• Dry mouth: Radiation delivered to the head region can inadvertently clip or irritate adjacent salivary gland structures, leading to a temporary or persistent decline in saliva production (xerostomia), which directly impairs chewing and swallowing.\n\t• Anosmia and Dysgeusia: Cranial radiation therapy can directly disrupt olfactory nerve pathways and taste buds, causing anosmia (loss of smell) and dysgeusia (distortion of taste), which severely decreases the palatability of food.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Loss of appetite, Anosmia and Dysgeusia, Dry mouth"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:define immunotherapy or biological therapy for cancer treatment.:::it uses the body’s immune system to fight cancer cells', 'define immunotherapy or biological therapy for cancer treatment.', 'It uses the body’s immune system to fight cancer cells', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Immunotherapy, also known as biological therapy, is a type of cancer treatment designed to boost, direct, or restore the body''s natural defenses to recognize and destroy cancer cells.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"It uses the body’s immune system to fight cancer cells\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Immunotherapy, also known as biological therapy, is a type of cancer treatment designed to boost, direct, or restore the body''s natural defenses to recognize and destroy cancer cells.\n\nRather than targeting the tumor directly (like traditional chemotherapy or radiation), immunotherapy trains or enhances the patient''s own immune cells (such as T-cells) to seek out and eliminate the malignancy.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: It uses supplementation to kill cancer cells, It uses botanicals to kill cancer cells, It uses food as medicine in fighting cancer cells"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:define immunotherapy or biological therapy for cancer treatment.:::', 'define immunotherapy or biological therapy for cancer treatment.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Immunotherapy, also known as biological therapy, is a type of cancer treatment designed to boost, direct, or restore the body''s natural defenses to recognize and destroy cancer cells.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"It uses the body’s immune system to fight cancer cells\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Immunotherapy, also known as biological therapy, is a type of cancer treatment designed to boost, direct, or restore the body''s natural defenses to recognize and destroy cancer cells.\n\nRather than targeting the tumor directly (like traditional chemotherapy or radiation), immunotherapy trains or enhances the patient''s own immune cells (such as T-cells) to seek out and eliminate the malignancy.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: It uses supplementation to kill cancer cells, It uses botanicals to kill cancer cells, It uses food as medicine in fighting cancer cells"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a cancer survivor is seeking advice on how to prevent recurrence. choose the correct answer.:::fat consumption must be less than 30% of total energy requirement', 'a cancer survivor is seeking advice on how to prevent recurrence. choose the correct answer.', 'Fat consumption must be less than 30% of total energy requirement', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The three new guidelines, Saturated fatty acid and trans-fatty acid intake for adults and children, Total fat intake for the prevention of unhealthy weight gain in adults and children, and Carbohydrate intake for adults and children, contain recommendations that aim to reduce the risk of unhealthy weight gain and diet-related noncommunicable diseases, such as type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease, and certain types of cancer. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Fat consumption must be less than 30% of total energy requirement\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The three new guidelines, Saturated fatty acid and trans-fatty acid intake for adults and children, Total fat intake for the prevention of unhealthy weight gain in adults and children, and Carbohydrate intake for adults and children, contain recommendations that aim to reduce the risk of unhealthy weight gain and diet-related noncommunicable diseases, such as type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease, and certain types of cancer. \n\nWith its guidance on dietary fat, WHO notes that both quantity and quality are important for good health. WHO reaffirms that adults should limit total fat intake to 30% of total energy intake or less.  Fat consumed by everyone 2 years of age and older should be primarily unsaturated fatty acids, with no more than 10% of total energy intake coming from saturated fatty acids and no more than 1% of total energy intake from trans-fatty acids from both industrially produced and ruminant animal sources.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Use supplements to prevent cancer recurrence, Consume 35g of fiber from fruits and vegetables daily, Avoid alcohol"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what type of dietary management must be recommended to a patient on palliative care for end stage cancer?:::consider the patient’s informed preference', 'what type of dietary management must be recommended to a patient on palliative care for end stage cancer?', 'Consider the patient’s informed preference', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In end-stage cancer and palliative care, the primary focus of Medical Nutrition Therapy (MNT) shifts entirely from medical restoration, weight gain, or nutrient correction to maximizing comfort, dignity, and quality of life.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Consider the patient’s informed preference\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In end-stage cancer and palliative care, the primary focus of Medical Nutrition Therapy (MNT) shifts entirely from medical restoration, weight gain, or nutrient correction to maximizing comfort, dignity, and quality of life.\n\nDuring end-stage disease, patients often experience severe anorexia, metabolic changes, and a natural decline in the desire to eat or drink. Enforcing a strict, aggressive diet plan can cause physical distress (such as nausea, vomiting, or choking). Therefore, the Registered Nutritionist-Dietitian (RND) must always prioritize and consider the patient’s informed preference, honoring what they wish to eat, when they wish to eat, or their right to decline food entirely.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: High caloric diet, High protein diet, Follow doctor’s orders"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what type of dietary management must be recommended to a patient on palliative care for end stage cancer?:::', 'what type of dietary management must be recommended to a patient on palliative care for end stage cancer?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In end-stage cancer and palliative care, the primary focus of Medical Nutrition Therapy (MNT) shifts entirely from medical restoration, weight gain, or nutrient correction to maximizing comfort, dignity, and quality of life.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Consider the patient’s informed preference\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In end-stage cancer and palliative care, the primary focus of Medical Nutrition Therapy (MNT) shifts entirely from medical restoration, weight gain, or nutrient correction to maximizing comfort, dignity, and quality of life.\n\nDuring end-stage disease, patients often experience severe anorexia, metabolic changes, and a natural decline in the desire to eat or drink. Enforcing a strict, aggressive diet plan can cause physical distress (such as nausea, vomiting, or choking). Therefore, the Registered Nutritionist-Dietitian (RND) must always prioritize and consider the patient’s informed preference, honoring what they wish to eat, when they wish to eat, or their right to decline food entirely.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: High caloric diet, High protein diet, Follow doctor’s orders"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:post mastectomy, the breast cancer patient is complaining of pain. when is the best time to offer food?:::offer food when pain is at the lowest level', 'post mastectomy, the breast cancer patient is complaining of pain. when is the best time to offer food?', 'Offer food when pain is at the lowest level', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Severe or acute pain activates the body''s sympathetic nervous system, which naturally suppresses the digestive tract, diminishes appetite, and can trigger nausea. Attempting to feed a patient when their pain is peaking often leads to a refusal to eat or food aversion.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Offer food when pain is at the lowest level\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Severe or acute pain activates the body''s sympathetic nervous system, which naturally suppresses the digestive tract, diminishes appetite, and can trigger nausea. Attempting to feed a patient when their pain is peaking often leads to a refusal to eat or food aversion.\n\nTo optimize caloric intake and support wound healing post-mastectomy, the best time to offer food is when the patient''s pain is at its lowest level (such as shortly after pain medications have taken effect). When a patient is comfortable and rested, their appetite improves, and they can tolerate eating much more effectively.\n\n• Give small, frequent feedings: While small, frequent meals are an excellent supportive strategy for oncology and post-op patients with low appetites, the question specifically asks for the best time to introduce or offer food to a patient struggling with pain. Maximizing comfort during pain valleys remains the prioritized clinical timing principle.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Offer food to distract the patient while in pain, Discontinue food until pain free, Give small, frequent feedings throughout the day"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:post mastectomy, the breast cancer patient is complaining of pain. when is the best time to offer food?:::', 'post mastectomy, the breast cancer patient is complaining of pain. when is the best time to offer food?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Severe or acute pain activates the body''s sympathetic nervous system, which naturally suppresses the digestive tract, diminishes appetite, and can trigger nausea. Attempting to feed a patient when their pain is peaking often leads to a refusal to eat or food aversion.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Offer food when pain is at the lowest level\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Severe or acute pain activates the body''s sympathetic nervous system, which naturally suppresses the digestive tract, diminishes appetite, and can trigger nausea. Attempting to feed a patient when their pain is peaking often leads to a refusal to eat or food aversion.\n\nTo optimize caloric intake and support wound healing post-mastectomy, the best time to offer food is when the patient''s pain is at its lowest level (such as shortly after pain medications have taken effect). When a patient is comfortable and rested, their appetite improves, and they can tolerate eating much more effectively.\n\n• Give small, frequent feedings: While small, frequent meals are an excellent supportive strategy for oncology and post-op patients with low appetites, the question specifically asks for the best time to introduce or offer food to a patient struggling with pain. Maximizing comfort during pain valleys remains the prioritized clinical timing principle.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Offer food to distract the patient while in pain, Discontinue food until pain free, Give small, frequent feedings throughout the day"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:bone cells with an active role in releasing calcium into the blood.:::', 'bone cells with an active role in releasing calcium into the blood.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Osteocytes derive from osteoblasts that become trapped in the newly formed mineralized matrix. While historically viewed as passive bystanders, they form an extensive interconnected network via canaliculi that detects mechanical loading and orchestrates mineral homeostasis. Through the expression of parathyroid hormone (PTH) receptors, osteocytes respond to hypocalcemia by upregulating RANKL expression and actively participating in osteocytic osteolysis—a process where they secrete matrix-degrading enzymes (such as MMPs and cathepsins) to resorb surrounding perilacunar bone and mobilize calcium directly into the extracellular fluid and bloodstream.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Osteocytes\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Osteocytes derive from osteoblasts that become trapped in the newly formed mineralized matrix. While historically viewed as passive bystanders, they form an extensive interconnected network via canaliculi that detects mechanical loading and orchestrates mineral homeostasis. Through the expression of parathyroid hormone (PTH) receptors, osteocytes respond to hypocalcemia by upregulating RANKL expression and actively participating in osteocytic osteolysis—a process where they secrete matrix-degrading enzymes (such as MMPs and cathepsins) to resorb surrounding perilacunar bone and mobilize calcium directly into the extracellular fluid and bloodstream.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Osteoclast, Ostegenic cells, Osteoblasts"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:bone cells with an active role in releasing calcium into the blood.:::osteocytes', 'bone cells with an active role in releasing calcium into the blood.', 'Osteocytes', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Osteocytes derive from osteoblasts that become trapped in the newly formed mineralized matrix. While historically viewed as passive bystanders, they form an extensive interconnected network via canaliculi that detects mechanical loading and orchestrates mineral homeostasis. Through the expression of parathyroid hormone (PTH) receptors, osteocytes respond to hypocalcemia by upregulating RANKL expression and actively participating in osteocytic osteolysis—a process where they secrete matrix-degrading enzymes (such as MMPs and cathepsins) to resorb surrounding perilacunar bone and mobilize calcium directly into the extracellular fluid and bloodstream.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Osteocytes\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Osteocytes derive from osteoblasts that become trapped in the newly formed mineralized matrix. While historically viewed as passive bystanders, they form an extensive interconnected network via canaliculi that detects mechanical loading and orchestrates mineral homeostasis. Through the expression of parathyroid hormone (PTH) receptors, osteocytes respond to hypocalcemia by upregulating RANKL expression and actively participating in osteocytic osteolysis—a process where they secrete matrix-degrading enzymes (such as MMPs and cathepsins) to resorb surrounding perilacunar bone and mobilize calcium directly into the extracellular fluid and bloodstream.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Osteoclast, Ostegenic cells, Osteoblasts"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a female patient’s food frequency questionnaire shows daily intake of fried foods, beef and butter. she is high risk for what type of cancer?:::breast', 'a female patient’s food frequency questionnaire shows daily intake of fried foods, beef and butter. she is high risk for what type of cancer?', 'Breast', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A diet high in fried foods, red meat (beef), and saturated fats (butter) is significantly associated with an increased risk of breast cancer in women.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Breast\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A diet high in fried foods, red meat (beef), and saturated fats (butter) is significantly associated with an increased risk of breast cancer in women.\n\t• Frying and cooking red meats at high temperatures produce heterocyclic amines (HCAs) and polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs), which are known carcinogens.\n\t• High-fat and calorie-dense foods contribute to elevated systemic inflammation, weight gain, and increased circulating estrogen levels, which strongly drive hormone-receptor-positive breast cancers.\n\nx Stomach: While fried foods and high-salt diets are risk factors for gastric cancer, this specific cluster of a high-fat, Western-style pattern (beef, butter, fried foods) is a classic hallmark profile used in clinical vignettes focusing on breast and colorectal cancer risks.\nx Liver: Liver cancer is primarily linked to alcohol consumption, chronic hepatitis (B or C) infections, and aflatoxins rather than daily beef and butter intake.\nx Endometrium: While obesity and high fat intake do increase endometrial cancer risk, breast cancer remains the predominant epidemiological and textbook association for a female patient presenting with this specific high-saturated-fat and high-heat meat dietary profile.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Liver, Endometrium, Stomach"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a female patient’s food frequency questionnaire shows daily intake of fried foods, beef and butter. she is high risk for what type of cancer?:::', 'a female patient’s food frequency questionnaire shows daily intake of fried foods, beef and butter. she is high risk for what type of cancer?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A diet high in fried foods, red meat (beef), and saturated fats (butter) is significantly associated with an increased risk of breast cancer in women.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Breast\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A diet high in fried foods, red meat (beef), and saturated fats (butter) is significantly associated with an increased risk of breast cancer in women.\n\t• Frying and cooking red meats at high temperatures produce heterocyclic amines (HCAs) and polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs), which are known carcinogens.\n\t• High-fat and calorie-dense foods contribute to elevated systemic inflammation, weight gain, and increased circulating estrogen levels, which strongly drive hormone-receptor-positive breast cancers.\n\nx Stomach: While fried foods and high-salt diets are risk factors for gastric cancer, this specific cluster of a high-fat, Western-style pattern (beef, butter, fried foods) is a classic hallmark profile used in clinical vignettes focusing on breast and colorectal cancer risks.\nx Liver: Liver cancer is primarily linked to alcohol consumption, chronic hepatitis (B or C) infections, and aflatoxins rather than daily beef and butter intake.\nx Endometrium: While obesity and high fat intake do increase endometrial cancer risk, breast cancer remains the predominant epidemiological and textbook association for a female patient presenting with this specific high-saturated-fat and high-heat meat dietary profile.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Liver, Endometrium, Stomach"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:nausea and vomiting is a common side effect of cancer therapy. the following nutrition interventions are necessary except:::offer high fat food', 'nausea and vomiting is a common side effect of cancer therapy. the following nutrition interventions are necessary except', 'Offer high fat food', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"While managing nausea and vomiting during cancer therapies like chemotherapy or radiation, patients are advised to avoid oily, greasy, and high-fat foods. High-fat foods slow down gastric emptying (digestion), which can significantly worsen the feeling of nausea and trigger vomiting.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Offer high fat food\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"While managing nausea and vomiting during cancer therapies like chemotherapy or radiation, patients are advised to avoid oily, greasy, and high-fat foods. High-fat foods slow down gastric emptying (digestion), which can significantly worsen the feeling of nausea and trigger vomiting.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Offer bland food, Offer cold food, Offer clear fluids"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:nausea and vomiting is a common side effect of cancer therapy. the following nutrition interventions are necessary except:::', 'nausea and vomiting is a common side effect of cancer therapy. the following nutrition interventions are necessary except', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"While managing nausea and vomiting during cancer therapies like chemotherapy or radiation, patients are advised to avoid oily, greasy, and high-fat foods. High-fat foods slow down gastric emptying (digestion), which can significantly worsen the feeling of nausea and trigger vomiting.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Offer high fat food\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"While managing nausea and vomiting during cancer therapies like chemotherapy or radiation, patients are advised to avoid oily, greasy, and high-fat foods. High-fat foods slow down gastric emptying (digestion), which can significantly worsen the feeling of nausea and trigger vomiting.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Offer bland food, Offer cold food, Offer clear fluids"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:excessive intake of this polyunsaturated fatty acid increases the risk of colon cancer and pancreatic cancer.:::omega 6', 'excessive intake of this polyunsaturated fatty acid increases the risk of colon cancer and pancreatic cancer.', 'Omega 6', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Omega-6 polyunsaturated fatty acids (primarily linoleic acid and arachidonic acid, found abundantly in commercial vegetable oils like corn oil, soybean oil, and ultra-processed foods) act as direct precursors to pro-inflammatory eicosanoids.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Omega 6\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Omega-6 polyunsaturated fatty acids (primarily linoleic acid and arachidonic acid, found abundantly in commercial vegetable oils like corn oil, soybean oil, and ultra-processed foods) act as direct precursors to pro-inflammatory eicosanoids.\n\nExcessive intake of Omega-6 lipids alters cell membrane structures, leading to the overproduction of arachidonic acid metabolites like Prostaglandin E2 (PGE2) and Leukotriene B4 (LTB4). These specific molecules drive systemic inflammation, trigger cyclooxygenase-2 (COX-2) pathways, block cellular apoptosis (programmed cell death), and stimulate angiogenesis, which significantly fuels the development and progression of colon and pancreatic tumors.\n\n• Omega-3: Possesses powerful anti-inflammatory properties. High intakes of marine Omega-3 fatty acids (like EPA and DHA found in fish oil) are associated with a reduced risk of colorectal and pancreatic cancers by counteracting Omega-6 pathways and inducing cancer cell apoptosis.\n• Omega-9: Primarily monounsaturated fatty acids (like oleic acid in olive oil), which are not classified as polyunsaturated fatty acids.\n• Omega-16: This is a structurally non-existent fatty acid classification in standard human nutritional physiology.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Omega 3, Omega 9, Omega 16"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:excessive intake of this polyunsaturated fatty acid increases the risk of colon cancer and pancreatic cancer.:::', 'excessive intake of this polyunsaturated fatty acid increases the risk of colon cancer and pancreatic cancer.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Omega-6 polyunsaturated fatty acids (primarily linoleic acid and arachidonic acid, found abundantly in commercial vegetable oils like corn oil, soybean oil, and ultra-processed foods) act as direct precursors to pro-inflammatory eicosanoids.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Omega 6\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Omega-6 polyunsaturated fatty acids (primarily linoleic acid and arachidonic acid, found abundantly in commercial vegetable oils like corn oil, soybean oil, and ultra-processed foods) act as direct precursors to pro-inflammatory eicosanoids.\n\nExcessive intake of Omega-6 lipids alters cell membrane structures, leading to the overproduction of arachidonic acid metabolites like Prostaglandin E2 (PGE2) and Leukotriene B4 (LTB4). These specific molecules drive systemic inflammation, trigger cyclooxygenase-2 (COX-2) pathways, block cellular apoptosis (programmed cell death), and stimulate angiogenesis, which significantly fuels the development and progression of colon and pancreatic tumors.\n\n• Omega-3: Possesses powerful anti-inflammatory properties. High intakes of marine Omega-3 fatty acids (like EPA and DHA found in fish oil) are associated with a reduced risk of colorectal and pancreatic cancers by counteracting Omega-6 pathways and inducing cancer cell apoptosis.\n• Omega-9: Primarily monounsaturated fatty acids (like oleic acid in olive oil), which are not classified as polyunsaturated fatty acids.\n• Omega-16: This is a structurally non-existent fatty acid classification in standard human nutritional physiology.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Omega 3, Omega 9, Omega 16"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:after eating shrimps, a woman immediately experienced difficulty of breathing, increased heart beat and lightheadedness. she is experiencing a systemic allergic reaction called:::anaphylaxis', 'after eating shrimps, a woman immediately experienced difficulty of breathing, increased heart beat and lightheadedness. she is experiencing a systemic allergic reaction called', 'Anaphylaxis', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Anaphylaxis is a severe, life-threatening, and rapid-onset systemic allergic reaction. It involves multiple organ systems simultaneously (the respiratory system via difficulty breathing, the cardiovascular system via an increased heartbeat, and the neurological system via lightheadedness from a sudden drop in blood pressure).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Anaphylaxis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Anaphylaxis is a severe, life-threatening, and rapid-onset systemic allergic reaction. It involves multiple organ systems simultaneously (the respiratory system via difficulty breathing, the cardiovascular system via an increased heartbeat, and the neurological system via lightheadedness from a sudden drop in blood pressure).\n\nShrimps are a crustacean shellfish, which is one of the leading triggers for severe IgE-mediated anaphylactic reactions in adults. This requires immediate medical intervention with epinephrine.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Food sensitivity, Food allergy, Food toxicity"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:after eating shrimps, a woman immediately experienced difficulty of breathing, increased heart beat and lightheadedness. she is experiencing a systemic allergic reaction called:::', 'after eating shrimps, a woman immediately experienced difficulty of breathing, increased heart beat and lightheadedness. she is experiencing a systemic allergic reaction called', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Anaphylaxis is a severe, life-threatening, and rapid-onset systemic allergic reaction. It involves multiple organ systems simultaneously (the respiratory system via difficulty breathing, the cardiovascular system via an increased heartbeat, and the neurological system via lightheadedness from a sudden drop in blood pressure).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Anaphylaxis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Anaphylaxis is a severe, life-threatening, and rapid-onset systemic allergic reaction. It involves multiple organ systems simultaneously (the respiratory system via difficulty breathing, the cardiovascular system via an increased heartbeat, and the neurological system via lightheadedness from a sudden drop in blood pressure).\n\nShrimps are a crustacean shellfish, which is one of the leading triggers for severe IgE-mediated anaphylactic reactions in adults. This requires immediate medical intervention with epinephrine.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Food sensitivity, Food allergy, Food toxicity"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the interior of the intestinal tract is called:::', 'the interior of the intestinal tract is called', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The actual open, interior space inside a tubular structure like the intestines where food and fluids pass.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Lumen\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The actual open, interior space inside a tubular structure like the intestines where food and fluids pass.\n\n🔴 Ileum: The final section of the small intestine.\n🔴 Mucosa: The innermost tissue layer that surrounds the lumen.\n🔴 Lining: A general term for the membrane covering the internal surface.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Ileum, Mucosa, Lining"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:this is known as the true allergic reaction to food as a result of immune response:::food hypersensitivity', 'this is known as the true allergic reaction to food as a result of immune response', 'Food hypersensitivity', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Food hypersensitivity is characterized by an adverse, reproducible immune response to dietary proteins. During sensitization, antigen-presenting cells present food peptides to T-helper 2 (Th2) cells, which stimulate B cells to synthesize allergen-specific IgE antibodies. These IgE molecules bind to high-affinity FcεRI receptors on mast cells and basophils. Upon subsequent re-exposure, allergen cross-linking of IgE triggers degranulation, releasing preformed mediators like histamine, leukotrienes, and prostaglandins. Non-IgE-mediated hypersensitivities involve T-cell-mediated pathways (such as Food Protein-Induced Enterocolitis Syndrome). In clinical nomenclature, ''food hypersensitivity'' is the precise, overarching term for any adverse reaction to food that is mediated by these specific immunological mechanisms.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Food hypersensitivity\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Food hypersensitivity is characterized by an adverse, reproducible immune response to dietary proteins. During sensitization, antigen-presenting cells present food peptides to T-helper 2 (Th2) cells, which stimulate B cells to synthesize allergen-specific IgE antibodies. These IgE molecules bind to high-affinity FcεRI receptors on mast cells and basophils. Upon subsequent re-exposure, allergen cross-linking of IgE triggers degranulation, releasing preformed mediators like histamine, leukotrienes, and prostaglandins. Non-IgE-mediated hypersensitivities involve T-cell-mediated pathways (such as Food Protein-Induced Enterocolitis Syndrome). In clinical nomenclature, ''food hypersensitivity'' is the precise, overarching term for any adverse reaction to food that is mediated by these specific immunological mechanisms.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Food allergy, Food idiosyncrasy, Food toxicity"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the most common, simplest fatty in foods?:::stearic and acetic acid', 'what is the most common, simplest fatty in foods?', 'Stearic and acetic acid', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"🟢 Stearic acid is one of the most common long-chain saturated fatty acids found in everyday foods, appearing abundantly in both animal fats (like beef and butter) and plant fats (like cocoa butter).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Stearic and acetic acid\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"🟢 Stearic acid is one of the most common long-chain saturated fatty acids found in everyday foods, appearing abundantly in both animal fats (like beef and butter) and plant fats (like cocoa butter).\n\n🟢 Acetic acid is the simplest possible fatty acid because it contains only two carbon atoms (C2), making it the shortest and smallest carboxylic acid classified in this group.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Acetic and stearic acid, Butyric and acetic acid, Butyric and stearic acid"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:this is known as the true allergic reaction to food as a result of immune response:::', 'this is known as the true allergic reaction to food as a result of immune response', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Food hypersensitivity is characterized by an adverse, reproducible immune response to dietary proteins. During sensitization, antigen-presenting cells present food peptides to T-helper 2 (Th2) cells, which stimulate B cells to synthesize allergen-specific IgE antibodies. These IgE molecules bind to high-affinity FcεRI receptors on mast cells and basophils. Upon subsequent re-exposure, allergen cross-linking of IgE triggers degranulation, releasing preformed mediators like histamine, leukotrienes, and prostaglandins. Non-IgE-mediated hypersensitivities involve T-cell-mediated pathways (such as Food Protein-Induced Enterocolitis Syndrome). In clinical nomenclature, ''food hypersensitivity'' is the precise, overarching term for any adverse reaction to food that is mediated by these specific immunological mechanisms.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Food hypersensitivity\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Food hypersensitivity is characterized by an adverse, reproducible immune response to dietary proteins. During sensitization, antigen-presenting cells present food peptides to T-helper 2 (Th2) cells, which stimulate B cells to synthesize allergen-specific IgE antibodies. These IgE molecules bind to high-affinity FcεRI receptors on mast cells and basophils. Upon subsequent re-exposure, allergen cross-linking of IgE triggers degranulation, releasing preformed mediators like histamine, leukotrienes, and prostaglandins. Non-IgE-mediated hypersensitivities involve T-cell-mediated pathways (such as Food Protein-Induced Enterocolitis Syndrome). In clinical nomenclature, ''food hypersensitivity'' is the precise, overarching term for any adverse reaction to food that is mediated by these specific immunological mechanisms.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Food allergy, Food idiosyncrasy, Food toxicity"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are common causes of foodb allergies except:::buckwheat', 'the following are common causes of foodb allergies except', 'Buckwheat', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Buckwheat is not classified as one of the major globally recognized food allergens. While an allergy to buckwheat can occur (and is notably monitored under specialized regulatory frameworks in select countries like Japan), it falls entirely outside the standard \"Big 8\" or \"Big 9\" major allergens that are taught in public health nutrition.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Buckwheat\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Buckwheat is not classified as one of the major globally recognized food allergens. While an allergy to buckwheat can occur (and is notably monitored under specialized regulatory frameworks in select countries like Japan), it falls entirely outside the standard \"Big 8\" or \"Big 9\" major allergens that are taught in public health nutrition.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Shellfish, Egg, Peanuts"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are common causes of foodb allergies except:::', 'the following are common causes of foodb allergies except', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Buckwheat is not classified as one of the major globally recognized food allergens. While an allergy to buckwheat can occur (and is notably monitored under specialized regulatory frameworks in select countries like Japan), it falls entirely outside the standard \"Big 8\" or \"Big 9\" major allergens that are taught in public health nutrition.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Buckwheat\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Buckwheat is not classified as one of the major globally recognized food allergens. While an allergy to buckwheat can occur (and is notably monitored under specialized regulatory frameworks in select countries like Japan), it falls entirely outside the standard \"Big 8\" or \"Big 9\" major allergens that are taught in public health nutrition.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Shellfish, Egg, Peanuts"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:an important function of bile in digestion::::', 'an important function of bile in digestion:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Bile salts are emulsifying agents; they disperse fats and form water-soluble micelles, which solubilize the products of fat digestion.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Fat emulsification\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Bile salts are emulsifying agents; they disperse fats and form water-soluble micelles, which solubilize the products of fat digestion.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Fat hydrolysis, Fat absorption"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a patient who is hypersensitive to shellfish must be monitored for nutrient deficiencies of:::zinc and magnesium', 'a patient who is hypersensitive to shellfish must be monitored for nutrient deficiencies of', 'Zinc and Magnesium', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In clinical nutrition, when a patient eliminates an entire food group due to a severe allergy or hypersensitivity, they are at risk of missing out on the primary micronutrients concentrated in those specific foods.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Zinc and Magnesium\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In clinical nutrition, when a patient eliminates an entire food group due to a severe allergy or hypersensitivity, they are at risk of missing out on the primary micronutrients concentrated in those specific foods.\n\n\t• Zinc: Shellfish (especially bivalves like oysters and mussels, as well as crustaceans like crabs and lobsters) are among the densest dietary sources of highly bioavailable zinc found in nature.\n\t• Magnesium: Shellfish varieties are also loaded with essential trace minerals and macrominerals, including a significant amount of magnesium per standard serving size. Total elimination of these foods without proper meal planning directly sets a patient up for micro-gaps in these two elements.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vitamin D and Calcium, Manganese and Copper, Selenium and Vitamin B6"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a patient who is hypersensitive to shellfish must be monitored for nutrient deficiencies of:::', 'a patient who is hypersensitive to shellfish must be monitored for nutrient deficiencies of', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In clinical nutrition, when a patient eliminates an entire food group due to a severe allergy or hypersensitivity, they are at risk of missing out on the primary micronutrients concentrated in those specific foods.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Zinc and Magnesium\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In clinical nutrition, when a patient eliminates an entire food group due to a severe allergy or hypersensitivity, they are at risk of missing out on the primary micronutrients concentrated in those specific foods.\n\n\t• Zinc: Shellfish (especially bivalves like oysters and mussels, as well as crustaceans like crabs and lobsters) are among the densest dietary sources of highly bioavailable zinc found in nature.\n\t• Magnesium: Shellfish varieties are also loaded with essential trace minerals and macrominerals, including a significant amount of magnesium per standard serving size. Total elimination of these foods without proper meal planning directly sets a patient up for micro-gaps in these two elements.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vitamin D and Calcium, Manganese and Copper, Selenium and Vitamin B6"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are symptoms of anaphylactic shock except:::increased blood pressure', 'the following are symptoms of anaphylactic shock except', 'Increased blood pressure', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Anaphylactic shock causes a severe, life-threatening drop in blood pressure (hypotension), not an increase.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Increased blood pressure\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Anaphylactic shock causes a severe, life-threatening drop in blood pressure (hypotension), not an increase.\n\nDuring anaphylaxis, a massive systemic release of histamine and other chemical mediators causes widespread, rapid vasodilation (widening of blood vessels) and increased capillary permeability (fluid leaking out of blood vessels). This sudden collapse in vascular resistance leads to a profound drop in blood pressure, which restricts vital blood flow to major organs.\n\nTrue Symptoms\n• Decreased blood pressure: The defining cardiovascular hallmark of anaphylactic shock.\n• Skin rashes: Often manifest as hives (urticaria), intense itching, or flushing, which are classic early signs of an IgE-mediated systemic allergic reaction.\n• Difficulty of breathing: Driven by severe airway constriction (bronchospasm) and swelling of the throat and tongue (angioedema), which can obstruct the airway.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Skin rashes, Difficulty of breathing, Decreased blood pressure"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are symptoms of anaphylactic shock except:::', 'the following are symptoms of anaphylactic shock except', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Anaphylactic shock causes a severe, life-threatening drop in blood pressure (hypotension), not an increase.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Increased blood pressure\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Anaphylactic shock causes a severe, life-threatening drop in blood pressure (hypotension), not an increase.\n\nDuring anaphylaxis, a massive systemic release of histamine and other chemical mediators causes widespread, rapid vasodilation (widening of blood vessels) and increased capillary permeability (fluid leaking out of blood vessels). This sudden collapse in vascular resistance leads to a profound drop in blood pressure, which restricts vital blood flow to major organs.\n\nTrue Symptoms\n• Decreased blood pressure: The defining cardiovascular hallmark of anaphylactic shock.\n• Skin rashes: Often manifest as hives (urticaria), intense itching, or flushing, which are classic early signs of an IgE-mediated systemic allergic reaction.\n• Difficulty of breathing: Driven by severe airway constriction (bronchospasm) and swelling of the throat and tongue (angioedema), which can obstruct the airway.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Skin rashes, Difficulty of breathing, Decreased blood pressure"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which is not a theme of the philippine nutrition month?:::', 'which is not a theme of the philippine nutrition month?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• Malnutrisyon: tuklasin at pugsain (1990)","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Bayan at nutrisyon, alagaan natin ngayon\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• Malnutrisyon: tuklasin at pugsain (1990)\n• Buto at ngipin patibayin, gatas ating inumin (1996)\n• Kumain nang RIGHT, para maging batang BRIGHT (2006)","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Kumain nang RIGHT, para maging batang BRIGHT, Malnutrisyon: tuklasin at pugsain, Buto at ngipin patibayin, gatas ating inumin"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:diagnosis of food allergy can be determined using the oral challenge through what type of diet?:::hypoallergenic diet', 'diagnosis of food allergy can be determined using the oral challenge through what type of diet?', 'Hypoallergenic diet', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When identifying or confirming a suspected food allergy using an oral food challenge (OFC), a patient is first placed on a strictly controlled hypoallergenic diet (or elimination diet) for a couple of weeks to allow all baseline allergic symptoms to completely subside.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Hypoallergenic diet\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When identifying or confirming a suspected food allergy using an oral food challenge (OFC), a patient is first placed on a strictly controlled hypoallergenic diet (or elimination diet) for a couple of weeks to allow all baseline allergic symptoms to completely subside.\n\nOnce the patient is symptom-free, individual food allergens are systematically reintroduced (challenged) under close medical supervision to see if a specific food reproduces the allergic reaction.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Desensitization diet, Low residue diet, Gluten free diet"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:diagnosis of food allergy can be determined using the oral challenge through what type of diet?:::', 'diagnosis of food allergy can be determined using the oral challenge through what type of diet?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When identifying or confirming a suspected food allergy using an oral food challenge (OFC), a patient is first placed on a strictly controlled hypoallergenic diet (or elimination diet) for a couple of weeks to allow all baseline allergic symptoms to completely subside.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Hypoallergenic diet\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When identifying or confirming a suspected food allergy using an oral food challenge (OFC), a patient is first placed on a strictly controlled hypoallergenic diet (or elimination diet) for a couple of weeks to allow all baseline allergic symptoms to completely subside.\n\nOnce the patient is symptom-free, individual food allergens are systematically reintroduced (challenged) under close medical supervision to see if a specific food reproduces the allergic reaction.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Desensitization diet, Low residue diet, Gluten free diet"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:cushing''s disease is characterized by::::all of the above', 'cushing''s disease is characterized by:', 'All of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Cushing''s disease is a specific type of Cushing''s syndrome caused by a benign tumor (adenoma) in the pituitary gland. This tumor secretes excessive amounts of Adrenocorticotropic Hormone (ACTH), which constantly overstimulates the adrenal glands to produce massive amounts of cortisol (the body''s primary stress hormone).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Cushing''s disease is a specific type of Cushing''s syndrome caused by a benign tumor (adenoma) in the pituitary gland. This tumor secretes excessive amounts of Adrenocorticotropic Hormone (ACTH), which constantly overstimulates the adrenal glands to produce massive amounts of cortisol (the body''s primary stress hormone).\n\n• Menstrual irregularities: Chronic high cortisol levels disrupt the normal pulsatile release of gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH). This often leads to irregular periods or a complete absence of menstruation (amenorrhea) in women.\n• Wasting: Cortisol is a highly catabolic hormone. Prolonged exposure causes muscle wasting and weakness, particularly visible in the thin arms and legs of patients (proximal muscle wasting).\n• Low calcium levels: High levels of glucocorticoids significantly decrease intestinal absorption of calcium and increase its excretion through the kidneys. This contributes heavily to the bone loss, weakening, and osteoporosis seen in Cushing''s patients.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Low calcium levels, Wasting, Menstrual irregularities"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:this diet is adequate but excludes the food triggers.:::avoidance diet', 'this diet is adequate but excludes the food triggers.', 'Avoidance diet', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"An avoidance diet (or exclusion diet) is long-term, nutritionally adequate, and sustainable because it simply removes the specific, already-identified target allergens or food triggers while keeping the rest of the diet varied and complete.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Avoidance diet\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"An avoidance diet (or exclusion diet) is long-term, nutritionally adequate, and sustainable because it simply removes the specific, already-identified target allergens or food triggers while keeping the rest of the diet varied and complete.\n\nAn elimination diet is an intensive, short-term diagnostic tool. It removes a massive array of potential food groups (often leaving a very restricted, non-adequate pool of foods) to clear symptoms before a reintroduction phase. Once the definitive triggers are successfully unmasked, the patient transitions to a long-term avoidance diet.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Elimination diet, Hypoallergenic Diet, Desensitization diet"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:this diet is adequate but excludes the food triggers.:::', 'this diet is adequate but excludes the food triggers.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"An avoidance diet (or exclusion diet) is long-term, nutritionally adequate, and sustainable because it simply removes the specific, already-identified target allergens or food triggers while keeping the rest of the diet varied and complete.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Avoidance diet\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"An avoidance diet (or exclusion diet) is long-term, nutritionally adequate, and sustainable because it simply removes the specific, already-identified target allergens or food triggers while keeping the rest of the diet varied and complete.\n\nAn elimination diet is an intensive, short-term diagnostic tool. It removes a massive array of potential food groups (often leaving a very restricted, non-adequate pool of foods) to clear symptoms before a reintroduction phase. Once the definitive triggers are successfully unmasked, the patient transitions to a long-term avoidance diet.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Elimination diet, Hypoallergenic Diet, Desensitization diet"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a patient manifests dry, scaly skin. what possible nutrient deficiency does this patient have?:::vitamin a', 'a patient manifests dry, scaly skin. what possible nutrient deficiency does this patient have?', 'Vitamin A', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A lack of Vitamin A impairs normal skin cell turnover and moisture retention, frequently resulting in dry, rough, or scaly skin (as well as conditions like phrynoderma or follicular hyperkeratosis).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vitamin A\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A lack of Vitamin A impairs normal skin cell turnover and moisture retention, frequently resulting in dry, rough, or scaly skin (as well as conditions like phrynoderma or follicular hyperkeratosis).\n\n\t• Protein deficiency typically leads to changes like muscle wasting, edema, or flaky-paint dermatosis (in kwashiorkor) or dry/loose skin (in marasmus).\n\t• Omega-3 fatty acid deficiency can cause dry or inflamed skin, though Vitamin A is the classic multiple-choice answer for rough and scaly skin manifestations like phrynoderma.\n\t• Vitamin C deficiency results in scurvy, featuring petechiae, purpura, and bleeding gums rather than primary scaling.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vitamin C, Protein, Omega 3"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a patient manifests dry, scaly skin. what possible nutrient deficiency does this patient have?:::', 'a patient manifests dry, scaly skin. what possible nutrient deficiency does this patient have?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A lack of Vitamin A impairs normal skin cell turnover and moisture retention, frequently resulting in dry, rough, or scaly skin (as well as conditions like phrynoderma or follicular hyperkeratosis).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vitamin A\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A lack of Vitamin A impairs normal skin cell turnover and moisture retention, frequently resulting in dry, rough, or scaly skin (as well as conditions like phrynoderma or follicular hyperkeratosis).\n\n\t• Protein deficiency typically leads to changes like muscle wasting, edema, or flaky-paint dermatosis (in kwashiorkor) or dry/loose skin (in marasmus).\n\t• Omega-3 fatty acid deficiency can cause dry or inflamed skin, though Vitamin A is the classic multiple-choice answer for rough and scaly skin manifestations like phrynoderma.\n\t• Vitamin C deficiency results in scurvy, featuring petechiae, purpura, and bleeding gums rather than primary scaling.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vitamin C, Protein, Omega 3"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:physical fitness enables the body to perform the following activities except:::sit still', 'physical fitness enables the body to perform the following activities except', 'Sit still', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sitting still is a sedentary behavior that represents a state of rest or inactivity, requiring minimal to no muscular contraction or elevated energy expenditure. It does not rely on or demonstrate an individual''s physical fitness.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sit still\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sitting still is a sedentary behavior that represents a state of rest or inactivity, requiring minimal to no muscular contraction or elevated energy expenditure. It does not rely on or demonstrate an individual''s physical fitness.\n\n\t• Carry grocery bags filled to the brim: Directly requires muscular strength and muscular endurance to lift and bear heavy loads continuously.\n\t• Climb 3 flights of stairs: Relies heavily on cardiovascular endurance and lower-body muscular strength to handle repetitive vertical movement.\n\t• Run fast when being chased by a stray dog: This represents a classic \"extra reserve energy for emergencies\" scenario, drawing heavily on speed, reaction time, and immediate cardiovascular power.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Run fast when being chased by a stray dog, Carry grocery bags filled to the brim, Climb 3 flights of stairs"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a patient’s skin has goose flesh-like lesions. what type of food must the rnd recommend?:::yellow and orange fruits and vegetables', 'a patient’s skin has goose flesh-like lesions. what type of food must the rnd recommend?', 'Yellow and orange fruits and vegetables', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Goose flesh-like lesions (clinically diagnosed as follicular hyperkeratinization or phrynoderma) refer to rough, dry bumps that form around the hair follicles due to an abnormal buildup of keratin.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Yellow and orange fruits and vegetables\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Goose flesh-like lesions (clinically diagnosed as follicular hyperkeratinization or phrynoderma) refer to rough, dry bumps that form around the hair follicles due to an abnormal buildup of keratin.\n\nThis skin presentation is a classic textbook hallmark manifestation of Vitamin A deficiency. Vitamin A is structurally mandatory to maintain epithelial cellular differentiation and skin membrane health. When Vitamin A levels drop, skin cells overproduce keratin, plugging the follicles and creating a texture that mirrors goosebumps or sandpaper.\n\nTo resolve this deficiency, the Registered Nutritionist-Dietitian (RND) must recommend foods rich in beta-carotene (the plant-derived precursor that the human body converts into active Vitamin A). Yellow and orange fruits and vegetables (such as carrots, squash, sweet potatoes, and mangoes) are the primary dietary sources of beta-carotene.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Fish and lean meat, Whole grains, Tomato"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:it stores bile until needed::::gallbladder', 'it stores bile until needed:', 'Gallbladder', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The gallbladder acts like a small storage pouch or holding tank. It collects and concentrates bile between meals.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Gallbladder\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The gallbladder acts like a small storage pouch or holding tank. It collects and concentrates bile between meals.\n\nWhen you eat (especially foods high in fat), hormones trigger the gallbladder to contract, squeezing the stored bile through the bile ducts and into the small intestine to break down fats.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Liver, Pancreas, Stomach"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a patient’s skin has goose flesh-like lesions. what type of food must the rnd recommend?:::', 'a patient’s skin has goose flesh-like lesions. what type of food must the rnd recommend?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Goose flesh-like lesions (clinically diagnosed as follicular hyperkeratinization or phrynoderma) refer to rough, dry bumps that form around the hair follicles due to an abnormal buildup of keratin.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Yellow and orange fruits and vegetables\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Goose flesh-like lesions (clinically diagnosed as follicular hyperkeratinization or phrynoderma) refer to rough, dry bumps that form around the hair follicles due to an abnormal buildup of keratin.\n\nThis skin presentation is a classic textbook hallmark manifestation of Vitamin A deficiency. Vitamin A is structurally mandatory to maintain epithelial cellular differentiation and skin membrane health. When Vitamin A levels drop, skin cells overproduce keratin, plugging the follicles and creating a texture that mirrors goosebumps or sandpaper.\n\nTo resolve this deficiency, the Registered Nutritionist-Dietitian (RND) must recommend foods rich in beta-carotene (the plant-derived precursor that the human body converts into active Vitamin A). Yellow and orange fruits and vegetables (such as carrots, squash, sweet potatoes, and mangoes) are the primary dietary sources of beta-carotene.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Fish and lean meat, Whole grains, Tomato"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a teenager is complaining of blackheads all over her t-zone. the rnd advises him to avoid:::french fries', 'a teenager is complaining of blackheads all over her t-zone. the rnd advises him to avoid', 'French fries', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"French fries are an ultra-processed carbohydrate with a high Glycemic Index (GI) and glycemic load. Consuming high-GI foods triggers rapid spikes in blood sugar and circulating insulin levels.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"French fries\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"French fries are an ultra-processed carbohydrate with a high Glycemic Index (GI) and glycemic load. Consuming high-GI foods triggers rapid spikes in blood sugar and circulating insulin levels.\n\nThis surge elevations in Insulin-like Growth Factor 1 (IGF-1). Elevated IGF-1 levels directly stimulate the sebaceous glands to produce excess sebum (oil) while promoting abnormal skin cell shedding (follicular hyperkeratinization). In the T-zone, this excess sebum combined with dead skin cells oxidizes upon exposure to air, forming blackheads (open comedones).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Chicken, Nuts, Salmon"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a teenager is complaining of blackheads all over her t-zone. the rnd advises him to avoid:::', 'a teenager is complaining of blackheads all over her t-zone. the rnd advises him to avoid', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"French fries are an ultra-processed carbohydrate with a high Glycemic Index (GI) and glycemic load. Consuming high-GI foods triggers rapid spikes in blood sugar and circulating insulin levels.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"French fries\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"French fries are an ultra-processed carbohydrate with a high Glycemic Index (GI) and glycemic load. Consuming high-GI foods triggers rapid spikes in blood sugar and circulating insulin levels.\n\nThis surge elevations in Insulin-like Growth Factor 1 (IGF-1). Elevated IGF-1 levels directly stimulate the sebaceous glands to produce excess sebum (oil) while promoting abnormal skin cell shedding (follicular hyperkeratinization). In the T-zone, this excess sebum combined with dead skin cells oxidizes upon exposure to air, forming blackheads (open comedones).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Chicken, Nuts, Salmon"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is an example of soluble dietary fiber?:::', 'which of the following is an example of soluble dietary fiber?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Pectin is a structural polysaccharide found in the cell walls of fruits and vegetables (such as apples, citrus fruits, and carrots). It is highly water-soluble and absorbs water in the gastrointestinal tract to form a viscous, gel-like substance. This gelation slows down digestion, delays gastric emptying, stabilizes blood sugar levels, and binds to bile acids to help lower LDL cholesterol.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Pectin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Pectin is a structural polysaccharide found in the cell walls of fruits and vegetables (such as apples, citrus fruits, and carrots). It is highly water-soluble and absorbs water in the gastrointestinal tract to form a viscous, gel-like substance. This gelation slows down digestion, delays gastric emptying, stabilizes blood sugar levels, and binds to bile acids to help lower LDL cholesterol.\n\n❌ Cellulose: Insoluble structural plant polysaccharide; adds fecal bulk without dissolving or gelling.\n❌ Lignin: Insoluble, non-carbohydrate complex polymer (polyphenolic); remains completely intact throughout the GI tract.\n❌ Chitin: Insoluble structural polysaccharide found in fungal walls and arthropod exoskeletons; non-gel-forming.","whyWrongChoices":"Pectin is a structural polysaccharide found in the cell walls of fruits and vegetables (such as apples, citrus fruits, and carrots). It is highly water-soluble and absorbs water in the gastrointestinal tract to form a viscous, gel-like substance. This gelation slows down digestion, delays gastric emptying, stabilizes blood sugar levels, and binds to bile acids to help lower LDL cholesterol.\n\n❌ Cellulose: Insoluble structural plant polysaccharide; adds fecal bulk without dissolving or gelling.\n❌ Lignin: Insoluble, non-carbohydrate complex polymer (polyphenolic); remains completely intact throughout the GI tract.\n❌ Chitin: Insoluble structural polysaccharide found in fungal walls and arthropod exoskeletons; non-gel-forming."}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:physical fitness enables the body to perform the following activities except:::', 'physical fitness enables the body to perform the following activities except', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sitting still is a sedentary behavior that represents a state of rest or inactivity, requiring minimal to no muscular contraction or elevated energy expenditure. It does not rely on or demonstrate an individual''s physical fitness.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sit still\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sitting still is a sedentary behavior that represents a state of rest or inactivity, requiring minimal to no muscular contraction or elevated energy expenditure. It does not rely on or demonstrate an individual''s physical fitness.\n\n\t• Carry grocery bags filled to the brim: Directly requires muscular strength and muscular endurance to lift and bear heavy loads continuously.\n\t• Climb 3 flights of stairs: Relies heavily on cardiovascular endurance and lower-body muscular strength to handle repetitive vertical movement.\n\t• Run fast when being chased by a stray dog: This represents a classic \"extra reserve energy for emergencies\" scenario, drawing heavily on speed, reaction time, and immediate cardiovascular power.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Run fast when being chased by a stray dog, Carry grocery bags filled to the brim, Climb 3 flights of stairs"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb)
