('mc:following six months of nutrition education, caregivers demonstrated improved knowledge but household dietary diversity showed minimal improvement. which explanation is most appropriate?:::knowledge alone is insufficient; economic, environmental, cultural, and household factors also influence food choices.', 'following six months of nutrition education, caregivers demonstrated improved knowledge but household dietary diversity showed minimal improvement. which explanation is most appropriate?', 'Knowledge alone is insufficient; economic, environmental, cultural, and household factors also influence food choices.', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In public health and community nutrition, a critical distinction is always made between outputs (gaining knowledge) and outcomes (actual behavioral change). A caregiver can perfectly understand the value of a diverse diet, but if the household faces economic barriers (poverty), environmental constraints (lack of market access to fresh produce), or deep-seated cultural preferences, they will not be able to translate that knowledge into practice.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Knowledge alone is insufficient; economic, environmental, cultural, and household factors also influence food choices.\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In public health and community nutrition, a critical distinction is always made between outputs (gaining knowledge) and outcomes (actual behavioral change). A caregiver can perfectly understand the value of a diverse diet, but if the household faces economic barriers (poverty), environmental constraints (lack of market access to fresh produce), or deep-seated cultural preferences, they will not be able to translate that knowledge into practice.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Nutrition education should be discontinued., Participants should be required to attend more lectures., The educational materials should contain more technical information."}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:menke''s kinky hair syndrome is a deficiency in ?:::', 'menke''s kinky hair syndrome is a deficiency in ?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Menke''s kinky hair syndrome is the genetic disorder of copper metabolism/transport that results in severe copper deficiency, presenting classically with sparse, kinky, brittle hair along with neurodegenerative symptoms in affected infants.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Copper\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Menke''s kinky hair syndrome is the genetic disorder of copper metabolism/transport that results in severe copper deficiency, presenting classically with sparse, kinky, brittle hair along with neurodegenerative symptoms in affected infants.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Chromium, Calcium, Cobalt"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
