('mc:it is a culinary term which means to allow the evaporation of a liquid for a more concentrated sauce.:::', 'it is a culinary term which means to allow the evaporation of a liquid for a more concentrated sauce.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Reduction involves simmering or boiling a liquid (such as stock, wine, or sauce) to evaporate water and concentrate the flavors. This process thickens the liquid and intensifies its taste, resulting in a richer sauce or liquid base for dishes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Reduction\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Reduction involves simmering or boiling a liquid (such as stock, wine, or sauce) to evaporate water and concentrate the flavors. This process thickens the liquid and intensifies its taste, resulting in a richer sauce or liquid base for dishes.\n\n• Concasse: This term refers to a method of preparing tomatoes by blanching, peeling, and chopping them into small pieces. The technique is often used to create a base for sauces or as a fresh ingredient in various dishes.\n• Deglazing: This process involves adding liquid (like wine, broth, or vinegar) to a pan after sautéing or searing meat to loosen and dissolve the browned bits of food (Fond) stuck to the bottom. The resulting mixture can be used as a base for sauces.\n\n*Fond, the browned bits left on the bottom of a pan after cooking, is not typically carcinogenic. It forms from the Maillard reaction, a process where proteins and sugars in food brown and develop complex flavors. However, it’s important to distinguish fond from burnt food. If food is burned to the point of charring, it can produce potentially harmful compounds, including some that are carcinogenic, like acrylamide and polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons (PAHs). As long as the fond is browned and not charred, it is generally safe and adds flavor to dishes.\n\n• Dredging: This technique involves coating food, usually meat or fish, with a dry ingredient like flour, breadcrumbs, or cornmeal before cooking. Dredging helps create a crispy exterior when the food is fried or baked.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Concasse, Deglazing, Dredging"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:this process involves adding liquid to a pan after sauteing or searing meat to loosen and dissolve the browned bits of food stuck to the bottom. the resulting mixture can be used as a base for sauces.:::deglazing', 'this process involves adding liquid to a pan after sauteing or searing meat to loosen and dissolve the browned bits of food stuck to the bottom. the resulting mixture can be used as a base for sauces.', 'Deglazing', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• Dredging: This technique involves coating food, usually meat or fish, with a dry ingredient like flour, breadcrumbs, or cornmeal before cooking. Dredging helps create a crispy exterior when the food is fried or baked.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Deglazing\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• Dredging: This technique involves coating food, usually meat or fish, with a dry ingredient like flour, breadcrumbs, or cornmeal before cooking. Dredging helps create a crispy exterior when the food is fried or baked.\n• Reduction: This involves simmering or boiling a liquid (such as stock, wine, or sauce) to evaporate water and concentrate the flavors. This process thickens the liquid and intensifies its taste, resulting in a richer sauce or liquid base for dishes.\n• Concasse: This term refers to a method of preparing tomatoes by blanching, peeling, and chopping them into small pieces. The technique is often used to create a base for sauces or as a fresh ingredient in various dishes.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Reduction, Concasse, Dredging"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:this process involves adding liquid to a pan after sauteing or searing meat to loosen and dissolve the browned bits of food stuck to the bottom. the resulting mixture can be used as a base for sauces.:::', 'this process involves adding liquid to a pan after sauteing or searing meat to loosen and dissolve the browned bits of food stuck to the bottom. the resulting mixture can be used as a base for sauces.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• Dredging: This technique involves coating food, usually meat or fish, with a dry ingredient like flour, breadcrumbs, or cornmeal before cooking. Dredging helps create a crispy exterior when the food is fried or baked.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Deglazing\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• Dredging: This technique involves coating food, usually meat or fish, with a dry ingredient like flour, breadcrumbs, or cornmeal before cooking. Dredging helps create a crispy exterior when the food is fried or baked.\n• Reduction: This involves simmering or boiling a liquid (such as stock, wine, or sauce) to evaporate water and concentrate the flavors. This process thickens the liquid and intensifies its taste, resulting in a richer sauce or liquid base for dishes.\n• Concasse: This term refers to a method of preparing tomatoes by blanching, peeling, and chopping them into small pieces. The technique is often used to create a base for sauces or as a fresh ingredient in various dishes.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Reduction, Concasse, Dredging"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the emulsifying agent in egg yolk.:::lecithin', 'the emulsifying agent in egg yolk.', 'Lecithin', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The emulsifying agent in egg yolk is lecithin. Lecithin is a phospholipid present in egg yolk that helps stabilize emulsions by reducing the surface tension between immiscible liquids, such as oil and water. It allows for the formation and maintenance of a stable emulsion, such as in mayonnaise or hollandaise sauce, where oil and water are combined into a smooth, creamy mixture.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Lecithin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The emulsifying agent in egg yolk is lecithin. Lecithin is a phospholipid present in egg yolk that helps stabilize emulsions by reducing the surface tension between immiscible liquids, such as oil and water. It allows for the formation and maintenance of a stable emulsion, such as in mayonnaise or hollandaise sauce, where oil and water are combined into a smooth, creamy mixture.\n\n• Ovomucin: This is a glycoprotein found in egg whites. It contributes to the gel-like consistency of egg whites and has properties that help stabilize foams. It also plays a role in the structural integrity of the egg white.\n• Albumin: Albumin is a major protein found in egg whites (also known as egg albumen) and is responsible for the egg white’s ability to form foams and stabilize mixtures. It also helps in binding and thickening in various food preparations.\n• Lysozyme: Lysozyme is an enzyme found in egg whites and other bodily fluids. It has antimicrobial properties and can break down bacterial cell walls, contributing to the preservation and safety of the egg.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Ovomucin, Albumin, Lysozyme"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the emulsifying agent in egg yolk.:::', 'the emulsifying agent in egg yolk.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The emulsifying agent in egg yolk is lecithin. Lecithin is a phospholipid present in egg yolk that helps stabilize emulsions by reducing the surface tension between immiscible liquids, such as oil and water. It allows for the formation and maintenance of a stable emulsion, such as in mayonnaise or hollandaise sauce, where oil and water are combined into a smooth, creamy mixture.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Lecithin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The emulsifying agent in egg yolk is lecithin. Lecithin is a phospholipid present in egg yolk that helps stabilize emulsions by reducing the surface tension between immiscible liquids, such as oil and water. It allows for the formation and maintenance of a stable emulsion, such as in mayonnaise or hollandaise sauce, where oil and water are combined into a smooth, creamy mixture.\n\n• Ovomucin: This is a glycoprotein found in egg whites. It contributes to the gel-like consistency of egg whites and has properties that help stabilize foams. It also plays a role in the structural integrity of the egg white.\n• Albumin: Albumin is a major protein found in egg whites (also known as egg albumen) and is responsible for the egg white’s ability to form foams and stabilize mixtures. It also helps in binding and thickening in various food preparations.\n• Lysozyme: Lysozyme is an enzyme found in egg whites and other bodily fluids. It has antimicrobial properties and can break down bacterial cell walls, contributing to the preservation and safety of the egg.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Ovomucin, Albumin, Lysozyme"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:level in which the stock on hand is depleted before additional orders are place.:::reorder point', 'level in which the stock on hand is depleted before additional orders are place.', 'Reorder point', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Reorder point refers to the inventory level at which a new order should be placed to replenish stock before it runs out.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Reorder point\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Reorder point refers to the inventory level at which a new order should be placed to replenish stock before it runs out.\n\n• Par stock: The quantity of an item that should be kept in stock to meet immediate needs.\n• Maximum level: The highest level of inventory that should be maintained to avoid excessive holding costs.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Par stock, Maximum level, Both a and b"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a potential carcinogen on charcoal-broiled meat is:::benzopyrene', 'a potential carcinogen on charcoal-broiled meat is', 'benzopyrene', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The potential carcinogen formed on charcoal-broiled meat is benzopyrene. Benzopyrene is a type of polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbon (PAH) that forms when meat, especially fatty meat, is cooked at high temperatures such as grilling or broiling over charcoal or open flame. PAHs like benzopyrene are known to be carcinogenic and can pose health risks when consumed in significant quantities over time.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"benzopyrene\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The potential carcinogen formed on charcoal-broiled meat is benzopyrene. Benzopyrene is a type of polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbon (PAH) that forms when meat, especially fatty meat, is cooked at high temperatures such as grilling or broiling over charcoal or open flame. PAHs like benzopyrene are known to be carcinogenic and can pose health risks when consumed in significant quantities over time.\n\n• Aflatoxin is a different type of carcinogen produced by certain molds on food, particularly nuts and grains, under certain conditions.\n• Nitrosamines can form in certain preserved or processed meats, but they are not specifically associated with charcoal-broiled meats. Therefore, the correct answer to the question is benzopyrene.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: aflatoxin, nitrosamines, none of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a potential carcinogen on charcoal-broiled meat is:::', 'a potential carcinogen on charcoal-broiled meat is', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The potential carcinogen formed on charcoal-broiled meat is benzopyrene. Benzopyrene is a type of polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbon (PAH) that forms when meat, especially fatty meat, is cooked at high temperatures such as grilling or broiling over charcoal or open flame. PAHs like benzopyrene are known to be carcinogenic and can pose health risks when consumed in significant quantities over time.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"benzopyrene\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The potential carcinogen formed on charcoal-broiled meat is benzopyrene. Benzopyrene is a type of polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbon (PAH) that forms when meat, especially fatty meat, is cooked at high temperatures such as grilling or broiling over charcoal or open flame. PAHs like benzopyrene are known to be carcinogenic and can pose health risks when consumed in significant quantities over time.\n\n• Aflatoxin is a different type of carcinogen produced by certain molds on food, particularly nuts and grains, under certain conditions.\n• Nitrosamines can form in certain preserved or processed meats, but they are not specifically associated with charcoal-broiled meats. Therefore, the correct answer to the question is benzopyrene.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: aflatoxin, nitrosamines, none of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:when moisture removal is achieved by utilizing a chamber where heat and humidity are controlled, the process is known as::::dehydration', 'when moisture removal is achieved by utilizing a chamber where heat and humidity are controlled, the process is known as:', 'dehydration', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dehydration involves removing moisture from food products using controlled conditions of temperature and humidity. This process helps to preserve the food by inhibiting the growth of microorganisms that require moisture to thrive.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"dehydration\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dehydration involves removing moisture from food products using controlled conditions of temperature and humidity. This process helps to preserve the food by inhibiting the growth of microorganisms that require moisture to thrive.\n\n• Freeze-drying is a different preservation method that involves freezing the food and then subjecting it to a vacuum to remove ice crystals directly as vapor (sublimation). \n• Sun-drying involves drying food products using the heat of the sun, while candying involves preserving fruit or peel by coating it with sugar syrup.\n\nTherefore, the correct term for controlled moisture removal in a chamber is dehydration.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: freeze-drying, sun-drying, candying"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:when moisture removal is achieved by utilizing a chamber where heat and humidity are controlled, the process is known as::::', 'when moisture removal is achieved by utilizing a chamber where heat and humidity are controlled, the process is known as:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dehydration involves removing moisture from food products using controlled conditions of temperature and humidity. This process helps to preserve the food by inhibiting the growth of microorganisms that require moisture to thrive.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"dehydration\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dehydration involves removing moisture from food products using controlled conditions of temperature and humidity. This process helps to preserve the food by inhibiting the growth of microorganisms that require moisture to thrive.\n\n• Freeze-drying is a different preservation method that involves freezing the food and then subjecting it to a vacuum to remove ice crystals directly as vapor (sublimation). \n• Sun-drying involves drying food products using the heat of the sun, while candying involves preserving fruit or peel by coating it with sugar syrup.\n\nTherefore, the correct term for controlled moisture removal in a chamber is dehydration.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: freeze-drying, sun-drying, candying"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:level in which the stock on hand is depleted before additional orders are place.:::', 'level in which the stock on hand is depleted before additional orders are place.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Reorder point refers to the inventory level at which a new order should be placed to replenish stock before it runs out.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Reorder point\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Reorder point refers to the inventory level at which a new order should be placed to replenish stock before it runs out.\n\n• Par stock: The quantity of an item that should be kept in stock to meet immediate needs.\n• Maximum level: The highest level of inventory that should be maintained to avoid excessive holding costs.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Par stock, Maximum level, Both a and b"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is not one of the interlocking and interrelated roles of managers?:::organizational', 'which of the following is not one of the interlocking and interrelated roles of managers?', 'Organizational', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Henry Mintzberg, a management theorist, categorized managerial roles into three main categories:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Organizational\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Henry Mintzberg, a management theorist, categorized managerial roles into three main categories:\n\n• Interpersonal roles: Roles that involve interactions with other people, including figurehead, leader, and liaison roles.\n• Informational roles: Roles that involve receiving, collecting, and disseminating information, including monitor, disseminator, and spokesperson roles.\n• Decisional roles: Roles that involve making decisions to solve problems or take advantage of opportunities, including entrepreneur, disturbance handler, resource allocator, and negotiator roles.\n\n“Organizational” does not fit into any of these categories directly as described by Mintzberg. Therefore, Organizational is not one of the interlocking and interrelated roles of managers according to this framework.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Informational, Interpersonal, Decisional"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is not one of the interlocking and interrelated roles of managers?:::', 'which of the following is not one of the interlocking and interrelated roles of managers?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Henry Mintzberg, a management theorist, categorized managerial roles into three main categories:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Organizational\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Henry Mintzberg, a management theorist, categorized managerial roles into three main categories:\n\n• Interpersonal roles: Roles that involve interactions with other people, including figurehead, leader, and liaison roles.\n• Informational roles: Roles that involve receiving, collecting, and disseminating information, including monitor, disseminator, and spokesperson roles.\n• Decisional roles: Roles that involve making decisions to solve problems or take advantage of opportunities, including entrepreneur, disturbance handler, resource allocator, and negotiator roles.\n\n“Organizational” does not fit into any of these categories directly as described by Mintzberg. Therefore, Organizational is not one of the interlocking and interrelated roles of managers according to this framework.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Informational, Interpersonal, Decisional"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a device for collecting heat, smoke, steam, and grease vapors.:::hood', 'a device for collecting heat, smoke, steam, and grease vapors.', 'hood', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A hood is the main device positioned over cooking equipment to capture and remove heat, smoke, steam, and grease vapors.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"hood\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A hood is the main device positioned over cooking equipment to capture and remove heat, smoke, steam, and grease vapors.\n\n• Exhaust: Refers to the system that removes air, heat, and odors from a space, often connected to the hood in commercial kitchens.\n• Grease trap: A device used in plumbing systems to intercept grease and solids before they enter wastewater disposal systems.\n• Salamander: A type of broiler typically used in commercial kitchens to apply intense heat from above for browning or finishing dishes.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: exhaust, grease trap, salamander"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
