('mc:a parasitic worm that cause trichinellosis when raw or undercooked meat containing encysted larvae is consumed.:::', 'a parasitic worm that cause trichinellosis when raw or undercooked meat containing encysted larvae is consumed.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• Campylobacter jejuni: A bacterium commonly associated with foodborne illness from consumption of contaminated poultry, unpasteurized milk, and contaminated water.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"trichinella spiralis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• Campylobacter jejuni: A bacterium commonly associated with foodborne illness from consumption of contaminated poultry, unpasteurized milk, and contaminated water.\n• E. coli: A bacteria that naturally resides in the intestines of humans and animals. While most strains of E. coli are harmless, certain pathogenic strains, such as E. coli O157:H7, can cause foodborne illness when ingested through contaminated food or water. Fecal contamination of food or water sources can introduce these harmful strains, leading to outbreaks of foodborne illnesses.\n• Bacillus cereus: A bacterium that can cause food poisoning through the production of toxins in improperly stored or cooked foods, particularly rice and other starchy foods.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: campylobacter jejuni, e. coli, bacillus cereus"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:type of organization applicable to a growing organization, with diversified responsibilities, in need of coordinators and specialists to assist.:::', 'type of organization applicable to a growing organization, with diversified responsibilities, in need of coordinators and specialists to assist.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Line and staff type of organization combines elements of both line and staff functions. In addition to the direct line of authority (line functions), it includes specialized support and advisory roles (staff functions) such as specialists, coordinators, and support staff to assist the line managers.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"line and staff type\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Line and staff type of organization combines elements of both line and staff functions. In addition to the direct line of authority (line functions), it includes specialized support and advisory roles (staff functions) such as specialists, coordinators, and support staff to assist the line managers.\n\n• Line type: In a line organization, authority flows in a direct line from top to bottom, with each employee reporting to one supervisor. It is suitable for small organizations with simple structures and clear chains of command.\n• Functional type: This refers to organizations where employees are grouped by their specialty areas or functions (e.g., marketing, finance, operations). It is common in larger organizations where efficiency and specialization are key.\n• Line and functional type: This is not a standard organizational type. It may refer to a hybrid structure that combines line functions with functional departments.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: line type, functional type, line and functional type"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:skill that is most essential for top level management::::conceptual', 'skill that is most essential for top level management:', 'conceptual', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Conceptual skill involves the ability to think strategically, understand complex situations, and develop innovative solutions. Top-level managers need strong conceptual skills to envision the organization’s future direction, set goals, and formulate strategic plans.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"conceptual\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Conceptual skill involves the ability to think strategically, understand complex situations, and develop innovative solutions. Top-level managers need strong conceptual skills to envision the organization’s future direction, set goals, and formulate strategic plans.\n\n• Technical skill: Refers to specific knowledge and expertise in a particular field or industry. While technical skills are important for lower levels of management, they become less critical at the top level where managers focus more on overseeing and directing rather than performing technical tasks.\n• Human skill: Also known as interpersonal or people skills, human skills involve the ability to work effectively with others, motivate and lead teams, and build relationships. These skills are important at all levels of management but are particularly crucial for middle managers who directly supervise employees.\n• Administrative skill: Involves the ability to manage tasks, processes, and resources efficiently. It includes planning, organizing, coordinating, and controlling activities within the organization. Administrative skills are essential for all levels of management but are typically more operational in nature compared to conceptual skills.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: technical, human, administrative"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:skill that is most essential for top level management::::', 'skill that is most essential for top level management:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Conceptual skill involves the ability to think strategically, understand complex situations, and develop innovative solutions. Top-level managers need strong conceptual skills to envision the organization’s future direction, set goals, and formulate strategic plans.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"conceptual\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Conceptual skill involves the ability to think strategically, understand complex situations, and develop innovative solutions. Top-level managers need strong conceptual skills to envision the organization’s future direction, set goals, and formulate strategic plans.\n\n• Technical skill: Refers to specific knowledge and expertise in a particular field or industry. While technical skills are important for lower levels of management, they become less critical at the top level where managers focus more on overseeing and directing rather than performing technical tasks.\n• Human skill: Also known as interpersonal or people skills, human skills involve the ability to work effectively with others, motivate and lead teams, and build relationships. These skills are important at all levels of management but are particularly crucial for middle managers who directly supervise employees.\n• Administrative skill: Involves the ability to manage tasks, processes, and resources efficiently. It includes planning, organizing, coordinating, and controlling activities within the organization. Administrative skills are essential for all levels of management but are typically more operational in nature compared to conceptual skills.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: technical, human, administrative"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:inversion of sucrose can be facilitated by adding::::acid', 'inversion of sucrose can be facilitated by adding:', 'acid', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The inversion of sucrose (the process where sucrose is hydrolyzed into its component sugars glucose and fructose) can be facilitated by adding acid.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"acid\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The inversion of sucrose (the process where sucrose is hydrolyzed into its component sugars glucose and fructose) can be facilitated by adding acid.\n\nSucrose is a disaccharide composed of glucose and fructose. In the presence of an acid (such as lemon juice or cream of tartar), the acid catalyzes the hydrolysis of sucrose into its monosaccharide components, glucose, and fructose. This process is commonly used in cooking and baking to make syrups, candies, and other confections where the breakdown of sucrose into its sweeter-tasting components (glucose and fructose) enhances sweetness and texture.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: fat, table sugar, honey"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:inversion of sucrose can be facilitated by adding::::', 'inversion of sucrose can be facilitated by adding:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The inversion of sucrose (the process where sucrose is hydrolyzed into its component sugars glucose and fructose) can be facilitated by adding acid.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"acid\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The inversion of sucrose (the process where sucrose is hydrolyzed into its component sugars glucose and fructose) can be facilitated by adding acid.\n\nSucrose is a disaccharide composed of glucose and fructose. In the presence of an acid (such as lemon juice or cream of tartar), the acid catalyzes the hydrolysis of sucrose into its monosaccharide components, glucose, and fructose. This process is commonly used in cooking and baking to make syrups, candies, and other confections where the breakdown of sucrose into its sweeter-tasting components (glucose and fructose) enhances sweetness and texture.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: fat, table sugar, honey"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:drip is a method of making::::coffee', 'drip is a method of making:', 'coffee', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In coffee brewing, the drip method, also known as drip coffee or filter coffee, involves pouring hot water over ground coffee beans contained in a filter. The water drips through the coffee grounds and filter into a pot or carafe below, extracting flavor and oils from the coffee grounds as it passes through. This method is popular for its simplicity and ability to produce a clean, flavorful cup of coffee.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"coffee\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In coffee brewing, the drip method, also known as drip coffee or filter coffee, involves pouring hot water over ground coffee beans contained in a filter. The water drips through the coffee grounds and filter into a pot or carafe below, extracting flavor and oils from the coffee grounds as it passes through. This method is popular for its simplicity and ability to produce a clean, flavorful cup of coffee.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: tea, chocolate, breakfast certial"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:drip is a method of making::::', 'drip is a method of making:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In coffee brewing, the drip method, also known as drip coffee or filter coffee, involves pouring hot water over ground coffee beans contained in a filter. The water drips through the coffee grounds and filter into a pot or carafe below, extracting flavor and oils from the coffee grounds as it passes through. This method is popular for its simplicity and ability to produce a clean, flavorful cup of coffee.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"coffee\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In coffee brewing, the drip method, also known as drip coffee or filter coffee, involves pouring hot water over ground coffee beans contained in a filter. The water drips through the coffee grounds and filter into a pot or carafe below, extracting flavor and oils from the coffee grounds as it passes through. This method is popular for its simplicity and ability to produce a clean, flavorful cup of coffee.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: tea, chocolate, breakfast certial"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:tequila-based cocktail with lime juice and triple sec.:::margarita', 'tequila-based cocktail with lime juice and triple sec.', 'Margarita', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Margarita typically consists of tequila, lime juice (often freshly squeezed), and triple sec (an orange-flavored liqueur). It is usually served in a glass with a salted rim. The combination of tequila’s agave flavor, tart lime juice, and sweet triple sec creates a balanced and refreshing cocktail.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Margarita\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Margarita typically consists of tequila, lime juice (often freshly squeezed), and triple sec (an orange-flavored liqueur). It is usually served in a glass with a salted rim. The combination of tequila’s agave flavor, tart lime juice, and sweet triple sec creates a balanced and refreshing cocktail.\n\n• Martini: Typically made with gin and vermouth, garnished with an olive or a lemon twist.\n• Tequila Sunrise: Made with tequila, orange juice, and grenadine syrup, creating a layered sunrise effect.\n• Daiquiri: A classic cocktail made with rum, lime juice, and simple syrup, served straight up or on the rocks.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Martini, Tequila sunrise, Daiquiri"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:tequila-based cocktail with lime juice and triple sec.:::', 'tequila-based cocktail with lime juice and triple sec.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Margarita typically consists of tequila, lime juice (often freshly squeezed), and triple sec (an orange-flavored liqueur). It is usually served in a glass with a salted rim. The combination of tequila’s agave flavor, tart lime juice, and sweet triple sec creates a balanced and refreshing cocktail.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Margarita\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Margarita typically consists of tequila, lime juice (often freshly squeezed), and triple sec (an orange-flavored liqueur). It is usually served in a glass with a salted rim. The combination of tequila’s agave flavor, tart lime juice, and sweet triple sec creates a balanced and refreshing cocktail.\n\n• Martini: Typically made with gin and vermouth, garnished with an olive or a lemon twist.\n• Tequila Sunrise: Made with tequila, orange juice, and grenadine syrup, creating a layered sunrise effect.\n• Daiquiri: A classic cocktail made with rum, lime juice, and simple syrup, served straight up or on the rocks.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Martini, Tequila sunrise, Daiquiri"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:head of the kitchen brigade system.:::executive chef', 'head of the kitchen brigade system.', 'Executive Chef', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In the traditional kitchen brigade system, which was developed by Georges Auguste Escoffier, the Executive Chef holds the highest authority and responsibility in the kitchen. This role involves overseeing all aspects of the kitchen operations, including menu planning, food preparation, kitchen staff management, and ensuring culinary standards and quality control.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Executive Chef\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In the traditional kitchen brigade system, which was developed by Georges Auguste Escoffier, the Executive Chef holds the highest authority and responsibility in the kitchen. This role involves overseeing all aspects of the kitchen operations, including menu planning, food preparation, kitchen staff management, and ensuring culinary standards and quality control.\n\n• Garde Manger: The Garde Manger, also known as the pantry chef, is responsible for cold food preparation, such as salads, appetizers, and sometimes desserts.\n• Sommelier: A wine steward or wine expert responsible for selecting and serving wines in a restaurant.\n• Sous Chef: The second-in-command in the kitchen, assisting the Executive Chef in managing kitchen staff, overseeing food production, and ensuring kitchen operations run smoothly.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Garde manger, Sommelier, Sous Chef"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:typically refers to dishes originating from or in the style of nice, france. it can include various salads, seafood dishes, or other regional specialties.:::nicoise', 'typically refers to dishes originating from or in the style of nice, france. it can include various salads, seafood dishes, or other regional specialties.', 'Nicoise', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• Hors d’oeuvres: Refers to small dishes served before a main meal to stimulate the appetite. It can include a variety of items such as finger foods, dips, spreads, or small hot and cold dishes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Nicoise\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• Hors d’oeuvres: Refers to small dishes served before a main meal to stimulate the appetite. It can include a variety of items such as finger foods, dips, spreads, or small hot and cold dishes.\n• Canapés: Small pieces of bread or pastry topped with savory garnishes, often served as appetizers.\n• Macedoine: A culinary term that typically refers to a mixture or salad containing small diced vegetables or fruits.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Canapes, Hors d''oeuvres, Macedoine"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
