('mc:the fizz or bubbles in sparkling water is attributed to::::carbon dioxide', 'the fizz or bubbles in sparkling water is attributed to:', 'carbon dioxide', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Carbon dioxide (CO2) is dissolved under pressure in sparkling water. When the pressure is released, such as when the bottle is opened or the water is poured, the dissolved CO2 escapes as bubbles, creating the characteristic fizziness or effervescence in the water.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"carbon dioxide\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Carbon dioxide (CO2) is dissolved under pressure in sparkling water. When the pressure is released, such as when the bottle is opened or the water is poured, the dissolved CO2 escapes as bubbles, creating the characteristic fizziness or effervescence in the water.\n\n• Carbon carbonate: This is not a correct term; carbonates are compounds involving carbon and oxygen, but not directly related to the fizz in sparkling water.\n• Oxygen bromide: These are two separate chemical elements and do not contribute to the fizz in sparkling water.\n• Galactose: A type of sugar found in dairy products and some plants, not related to the fizz in sparkling water.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: carbon carbonate, oxygen bromide, galactose"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the fizz or bubbles in sparkling water is attributed to::::', 'the fizz or bubbles in sparkling water is attributed to:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Carbon dioxide (CO2) is dissolved under pressure in sparkling water. When the pressure is released, such as when the bottle is opened or the water is poured, the dissolved CO2 escapes as bubbles, creating the characteristic fizziness or effervescence in the water.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"carbon dioxide\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Carbon dioxide (CO2) is dissolved under pressure in sparkling water. When the pressure is released, such as when the bottle is opened or the water is poured, the dissolved CO2 escapes as bubbles, creating the characteristic fizziness or effervescence in the water.\n\n• Carbon carbonate: This is not a correct term; carbonates are compounds involving carbon and oxygen, but not directly related to the fizz in sparkling water.\n• Oxygen bromide: These are two separate chemical elements and do not contribute to the fizz in sparkling water.\n• Galactose: A type of sugar found in dairy products and some plants, not related to the fizz in sparkling water.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: carbon carbonate, oxygen bromide, galactose"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:assorted appetizers taken before the main dishes::::hors d''oeuvres', 'assorted appetizers taken before the main dishes:', 'Hors d''oeuvres', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Hors d’oeuvres refers to small dishes served before a main meal to stimulate the appetite. It can include a variety of items such as finger foods, dips, spreads, or small hot and cold dishes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Hors d''oeuvres\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Hors d’oeuvres refers to small dishes served before a main meal to stimulate the appetite. It can include a variety of items such as finger foods, dips, spreads, or small hot and cold dishes.\n\n• Canapés: Small pieces of bread or pastry topped with savory garnishes, often served as appetizers.\n• Macedoine: A culinary term that typically refers to a mixture or salad containing small diced vegetables or fruits.\n• Niçoise: Typically refers to dishes originating from or in the style of Nice, France. It can include various salads, seafood dishes, or other regional specialties.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Canapes, Macedoine, Nicoise"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
