('mc:the transformation of raw or processed foods into an acceptable finished product.:::food production', 'the transformation of raw or processed foods into an acceptable finished product.', 'Food Production', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Food production involves the actual preparation and assembly of ingredients and materials to create finished dishes or food items that are ready to be served to customers. It encompasses all activities related to cooking, baking, assembling, and garnishing food according to recipes and standards.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Food Production\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Food production involves the actual preparation and assembly of ingredients and materials to create finished dishes or food items that are ready to be served to customers. It encompasses all activities related to cooking, baking, assembling, and garnishing food according to recipes and standards.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Standardized recipe, Kitchen brigade, Forecasting"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the transformation of raw or processed foods into an acceptable finished product.:::', 'the transformation of raw or processed foods into an acceptable finished product.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Food production involves the actual preparation and assembly of ingredients and materials to create finished dishes or food items that are ready to be served to customers. It encompasses all activities related to cooking, baking, assembling, and garnishing food according to recipes and standards.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Food Production\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Food production involves the actual preparation and assembly of ingredients and materials to create finished dishes or food items that are ready to be served to customers. It encompasses all activities related to cooking, baking, assembling, and garnishing food according to recipes and standards.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Standardized recipe, Kitchen brigade, Forecasting"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what do you call it when fruits continue to ripen after harvesting (such as bananas)::::climacteric', 'what do you call it when fruits continue to ripen after harvesting (such as bananas):', 'Climacteric', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Climacteric fruits are capable of undergoing a ripening process even after being harvested. Bananas are a classic example of climacteric fruits; they are typically harvested while still green and then ripen further during storage due to the production of ethylene gas.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Climacteric\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Climacteric fruits are capable of undergoing a ripening process even after being harvested. Bananas are a classic example of climacteric fruits; they are typically harvested while still green and then ripen further during storage due to the production of ethylene gas.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Unripe, Artificial ripening process, Degree of maturity"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are dietary management for morning sickness during pregnancy except::::', 'the following are dietary management for morning sickness during pregnancy except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Citrus juice (high acidity), water (trigger the gag reflex), milk (aggravates nausea), coffee, or tea (can stimulate stomach acid secretion) if experiencing nausea.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Citrus juice, water, milk coffee or tea if experiencing nausea\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Citrus juice (high acidity), water (trigger the gag reflex), milk (aggravates nausea), coffee, or tea (can stimulate stomach acid secretion) if experiencing nausea.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Frequent small meals, Dry toast or crackers, Avoidance of foods with offensive odors"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what do you call it when fruits continue to ripen after harvesting (such as bananas)::::', 'what do you call it when fruits continue to ripen after harvesting (such as bananas):', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Climacteric fruits are capable of undergoing a ripening process even after being harvested. Bananas are a classic example of climacteric fruits; they are typically harvested while still green and then ripen further during storage due to the production of ethylene gas.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Climacteric\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Climacteric fruits are capable of undergoing a ripening process even after being harvested. Bananas are a classic example of climacteric fruits; they are typically harvested while still green and then ripen further during storage due to the production of ethylene gas.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Unripe, Artificial ripening process, Degree of maturity"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:desirable characteristic of fish::::crystal clear eyes; bright colored gills; whole fins; resilient flesh', 'desirable characteristic of fish:', 'Crystal clear eyes; bright colored gills; whole fins; resilient flesh', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The desirable characteristics of fresh fish are typically indicated by:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Crystal clear eyes; bright colored gills; whole fins; resilient flesh\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The desirable characteristics of fresh fish are typically indicated by:\n\n• Crystal clear eyes\n• Bright colored (red) gills\n• Whole and intact fins\n• Resilient and firm flesh","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sunken eyes; fishy smell; slimy gills; dry fins, Moist eyes; dark colored gills; fresh smell; remains indented when poked, all of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:desirable characteristic of fish::::', 'desirable characteristic of fish:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The desirable characteristics of fresh fish are typically indicated by:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Crystal clear eyes; bright colored gills; whole fins; resilient flesh\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The desirable characteristics of fresh fish are typically indicated by:\n\n• Crystal clear eyes\n• Bright colored (red) gills\n• Whole and intact fins\n• Resilient and firm flesh","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sunken eyes; fishy smell; slimy gills; dry fins, Moist eyes; dark colored gills; fresh smell; remains indented when poked, all of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the last date on which the product should be used::::expiration', 'the last date on which the product should be used:', 'Expiration', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Expiration date is the last date on which the product should be used or consumed. It is often used for products where safety is a concern, indicating that after this date, the product may no longer be safe to consume.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Expiration\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Expiration date is the last date on which the product should be used or consumed. It is often used for products where safety is a concern, indicating that after this date, the product may no longer be safe to consume.\n\n• Best used by: This date indicates the last date recommended for the use of the product at its peak quality. It does not refer to safety but rather optimal quality.\n• Packed on: This date indicates the date on which the product was packed or processed. It helps in tracking the freshness or age of the product.\n• Sell by: This date is the date by which the product should be sold to the consumer. It indicates to the retailer how long to display the product for sale.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Best used by, Packed on, Sell by"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the last date on which the product should be used::::', 'the last date on which the product should be used:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Expiration date is the last date on which the product should be used or consumed. It is often used for products where safety is a concern, indicating that after this date, the product may no longer be safe to consume.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Expiration\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Expiration date is the last date on which the product should be used or consumed. It is often used for products where safety is a concern, indicating that after this date, the product may no longer be safe to consume.\n\n• Best used by: This date indicates the last date recommended for the use of the product at its peak quality. It does not refer to safety but rather optimal quality.\n• Packed on: This date indicates the date on which the product was packed or processed. It helps in tracking the freshness or age of the product.\n• Sell by: This date is the date by which the product should be sold to the consumer. It indicates to the retailer how long to display the product for sale.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Best used by, Packed on, Sell by"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is true about basal metabolic rate?:::fever increases bmr', 'which of the following is true about basal metabolic rate?', 'Fever increases BMR', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When the body develops a fever, its internal temperature rises, which accelerates enzymatic and cellular activities to help fight off infection. As a general clinical rule, BMR increases by approximately 7% for every 1°F rise in body temperature.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Fever increases BMR\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When the body develops a fever, its internal temperature rises, which accelerates enzymatic and cellular activities to help fight off infection. As a general clinical rule, BMR increases by approximately 7% for every 1°F rise in body temperature.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Pregnancy decreases BMR, Fasting increases BMR, Females have higher BMR than males"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:you have only dried ground herb available, but the recipe call for 1 tbsp fresh herb. how will you substitute dried for fresh?:::use less - 1 tsp dried', 'you have only dried ground herb available, but the recipe call for 1 tbsp fresh herb. how will you substitute dried for fresh?', 'Use less - 1 tsp dried', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When substituting dried herbs for fresh herbs in a recipe, the general rule of thumb is to use less dried herb than the amount of fresh herb called for. This is because dried herbs are more concentrated in flavor than fresh herbs due to the drying process.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Use less - 1 tsp dried\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When substituting dried herbs for fresh herbs in a recipe, the general rule of thumb is to use less dried herb than the amount of fresh herb called for. This is because dried herbs are more concentrated in flavor than fresh herbs due to the drying process.\n\nThe typical substitution ratio is 1 part dried herb for 3 parts fresh herb.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Use the same amount, Use more - 2 tbsp, Use less – ½ tbsp"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:you have only dried ground herb available, but the recipe call for 1 tbsp fresh herb. how will you substitute dried for fresh?:::', 'you have only dried ground herb available, but the recipe call for 1 tbsp fresh herb. how will you substitute dried for fresh?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When substituting dried herbs for fresh herbs in a recipe, the general rule of thumb is to use less dried herb than the amount of fresh herb called for. This is because dried herbs are more concentrated in flavor than fresh herbs due to the drying process.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Use less - 1 tsp dried\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When substituting dried herbs for fresh herbs in a recipe, the general rule of thumb is to use less dried herb than the amount of fresh herb called for. This is because dried herbs are more concentrated in flavor than fresh herbs due to the drying process.\n\nThe typical substitution ratio is 1 part dried herb for 3 parts fresh herb.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Use the same amount, Use more - 2 tbsp, Use less – ½ tbsp"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a prediction of food needs for a day or other specific period of time.:::forecasting', 'a prediction of food needs for a day or other specific period of time.', 'Forecasting', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Forecasting in the context of food service involves estimating the quantity of food that will be required to meet customer demand over a certain period. This helps in planning and preparing the right amount of food to minimize waste while ensuring that enough food is available to meet customer orders.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Forecasting\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Forecasting in the context of food service involves estimating the quantity of food that will be required to meet customer demand over a certain period. This helps in planning and preparing the right amount of food to minimize waste while ensuring that enough food is available to meet customer orders.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Purchasing, Production, Recipe writing"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a prediction of food needs for a day or other specific period of time.:::', 'a prediction of food needs for a day or other specific period of time.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Forecasting in the context of food service involves estimating the quantity of food that will be required to meet customer demand over a certain period. This helps in planning and preparing the right amount of food to minimize waste while ensuring that enough food is available to meet customer orders.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Forecasting\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Forecasting in the context of food service involves estimating the quantity of food that will be required to meet customer demand over a certain period. This helps in planning and preparing the right amount of food to minimize waste while ensuring that enough food is available to meet customer orders.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Purchasing, Production, Recipe writing"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:when purchasing canned goods, a bulging end is an indication that the product may::::cause botulism', 'when purchasing canned goods, a bulging end is an indication that the product may:', 'Cause botulism', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Bulging cans can be a sign of botulism, a serious and potentially deadly illness caused by the toxin produced by Clostridium botulinum bacteria. These bacteria can thrive in anaerobic (low-oxygen) conditions, such as those found inside improperly sealed or damaged cans. Consuming food contaminated with botulinum toxin can lead to severe illness.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Cause botulism\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Bulging cans can be a sign of botulism, a serious and potentially deadly illness caused by the toxin produced by Clostridium botulinum bacteria. These bacteria can thrive in anaerobic (low-oxygen) conditions, such as those found inside improperly sealed or damaged cans. Consuming food contaminated with botulinum toxin can lead to severe illness.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Be ok to purchase, Be lower in weight than normal, May have parasitic infestation"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:when purchasing canned goods, a bulging end is an indication that the product may::::', 'when purchasing canned goods, a bulging end is an indication that the product may:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Bulging cans can be a sign of botulism, a serious and potentially deadly illness caused by the toxin produced by Clostridium botulinum bacteria. These bacteria can thrive in anaerobic (low-oxygen) conditions, such as those found inside improperly sealed or damaged cans. Consuming food contaminated with botulinum toxin can lead to severe illness.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Cause botulism\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Bulging cans can be a sign of botulism, a serious and potentially deadly illness caused by the toxin produced by Clostridium botulinum bacteria. These bacteria can thrive in anaerobic (low-oxygen) conditions, such as those found inside improperly sealed or damaged cans. Consuming food contaminated with botulinum toxin can lead to severe illness.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Be ok to purchase, Be lower in weight than normal, May have parasitic infestation"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:it is an example of curing salt::::prague powder', 'it is an example of curing salt:', 'Prague powder', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Prague powder, also known as pink curing salt, is a specific type of curing salt used in preserving meats such as bacon, ham, and sausages. It contains sodium nitrite, which helps prevent bacterial growth and gives cured meats their characteristic pink color and flavor.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Prague powder\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Prague powder, also known as pink curing salt, is a specific type of curing salt used in preserving meats such as bacon, ham, and sausages. It contains sodium nitrite, which helps prevent bacterial growth and gives cured meats their characteristic pink color and flavor.\n\n• Rub: A mixture of spices, herbs, and sometimes salt that is applied to the surface of meat to add flavor. It is not a curing salt but is used primarily for seasoning.\n• Kosher salt: A coarse-grained salt commonly used in cooking and for koshering meat. It is not typically used for curing but is favored for its clean flavor and ability to draw out moisture from foods.\n• Fleur de sel: A type of sea salt harvested from the surface of salt ponds. It is prized for its delicate flavor and texture and is often used as a finishing salt, but it is not used for curing.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: rub, Kosher salt, leur de sel"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:it is an example of curing salt::::', 'it is an example of curing salt:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Prague powder, also known as pink curing salt, is a specific type of curing salt used in preserving meats such as bacon, ham, and sausages. It contains sodium nitrite, which helps prevent bacterial growth and gives cured meats their characteristic pink color and flavor.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Prague powder\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Prague powder, also known as pink curing salt, is a specific type of curing salt used in preserving meats such as bacon, ham, and sausages. It contains sodium nitrite, which helps prevent bacterial growth and gives cured meats their characteristic pink color and flavor.\n\n• Rub: A mixture of spices, herbs, and sometimes salt that is applied to the surface of meat to add flavor. It is not a curing salt but is used primarily for seasoning.\n• Kosher salt: A coarse-grained salt commonly used in cooking and for koshering meat. It is not typically used for curing but is favored for its clean flavor and ability to draw out moisture from foods.\n• Fleur de sel: A type of sea salt harvested from the surface of salt ponds. It is prized for its delicate flavor and texture and is often used as a finishing salt, but it is not used for curing.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: rub, Kosher salt, leur de sel"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:gas that cause the fruit to ripen::::ethylene', 'gas that cause the fruit to ripen:', 'ethylene', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Ethylene is a natural plant hormone that plays a key role in the ripening process of fruits. It is produced by fruits themselves as they ripen and can also be released by other fruits nearby, leading to a chain reaction of ripening in a group of fruits.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"ethylene\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Ethylene is a natural plant hormone that plays a key role in the ripening process of fruits. It is produced by fruits themselves as they ripen and can also be released by other fruits nearby, leading to a chain reaction of ripening in a group of fruits.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: carbon monoxide, propane, nitrogen"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:ingredients in chocolate milk that comes from seaweed.:::carrageenan', 'ingredients in chocolate milk that comes from seaweed.', 'carrageenan', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Carrageenan is a natural food additive derived from various species of red seaweed. It is commonly used in the food industry as a thickening and stabilizing agent.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"carrageenan\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Carrageenan is a natural food additive derived from various species of red seaweed. It is commonly used in the food industry as a thickening and stabilizing agent.\n\nIn chocolate milk, carrageenan helps to prevent separation of the milk and cocoa particles, providing a smooth and creamy texture.\n\n• Glucose is a simple sugar that is often used as a sweetener in various food products. It is not derived from seaweed.\n• Sucrose is a common sugar, made up of glucose and fructose, typically derived from sugar cane or sugar beets. It is also not derived from seaweed.\n• Cocoa is the main ingredient in chocolate, derived from cocoa beans. It provides the chocolate flavor in chocolate milk but does not come from seaweed.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: glucose, sucrose, cocoa"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:ingredients in chocolate milk that comes from seaweed.:::', 'ingredients in chocolate milk that comes from seaweed.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Carrageenan is a natural food additive derived from various species of red seaweed. It is commonly used in the food industry as a thickening and stabilizing agent.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"carrageenan\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Carrageenan is a natural food additive derived from various species of red seaweed. It is commonly used in the food industry as a thickening and stabilizing agent.\n\nIn chocolate milk, carrageenan helps to prevent separation of the milk and cocoa particles, providing a smooth and creamy texture.\n\n• Glucose is a simple sugar that is often used as a sweetener in various food products. It is not derived from seaweed.\n• Sucrose is a common sugar, made up of glucose and fructose, typically derived from sugar cane or sugar beets. It is also not derived from seaweed.\n• Cocoa is the main ingredient in chocolate, derived from cocoa beans. It provides the chocolate flavor in chocolate milk but does not come from seaweed.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: glucose, sucrose, cocoa"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the process by which a liquid is separated from a gel; commonly observed during cooling.:::syneresis', 'the process by which a liquid is separated from a gel; commonly observed during cooling.', 'syneresis', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Syneresis occurs when a gel contracts or shrinks, causing the expulsion of liquid from the gel network. This separation of liquid from the gel can be seen as small droplets or pools of liquid forming on the surface of the gel or around its edges.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"syneresis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Syneresis occurs when a gel contracts or shrinks, causing the expulsion of liquid from the gel network. This separation of liquid from the gel can be seen as small droplets or pools of liquid forming on the surface of the gel or around its edges.\n\n• Gelation is the process of forming a gel from a liquid, usually by cooling or adding a gelling agent. This process is the opposite of syneresis, as it involves the formation of a gel rather than the separation of liquid from it.\n• Hydrolysis is a chemical process in which a molecule is broken down into smaller components by the addition of water. This term is more related to the breakdown of substances than to the separation of liquid from a gel.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: gelation, hydrolysis, gelatinization"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the process by which a liquid is separated from a gel; commonly observed during cooling.:::', 'the process by which a liquid is separated from a gel; commonly observed during cooling.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Syneresis occurs when a gel contracts or shrinks, causing the expulsion of liquid from the gel network. This separation of liquid from the gel can be seen as small droplets or pools of liquid forming on the surface of the gel or around its edges.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"syneresis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Syneresis occurs when a gel contracts or shrinks, causing the expulsion of liquid from the gel network. This separation of liquid from the gel can be seen as small droplets or pools of liquid forming on the surface of the gel or around its edges.\n\n• Gelation is the process of forming a gel from a liquid, usually by cooling or adding a gelling agent. This process is the opposite of syneresis, as it involves the formation of a gel rather than the separation of liquid from it.\n• Hydrolysis is a chemical process in which a molecule is broken down into smaller components by the addition of water. This term is more related to the breakdown of substances than to the separation of liquid from a gel.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: gelation, hydrolysis, gelatinization"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb)
