('mc:the astringent taste of certain fruits is due to their high content of::::tannins', 'the astringent taste of certain fruits is due to their high content of:', 'tannins', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Tannins are polyphenolic compounds found in plants, including fruits like grapes, apples, and some berries. They contribute to an astringent taste sensation, which can make the mouth feel dry or puckered. Tannins bind to proteins and other compounds in the mouth, causing this characteristic sensation.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"tannins\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Tannins are polyphenolic compounds found in plants, including fruits like grapes, apples, and some berries. They contribute to an astringent taste sensation, which can make the mouth feel dry or puckered. Tannins bind to proteins and other compounds in the mouth, causing this characteristic sensation.\n\n• Tannins are responsible for the astringent taste in certain fruits.\n• Acids contribute to sourness, not astringency.\n• Pectin is involved in gelling, not taste.\n• Fiber affects texture but not astringency.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: acids, pectin, fiber"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the astringent taste of certain fruits is due to their high content of::::', 'the astringent taste of certain fruits is due to their high content of:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Tannins are polyphenolic compounds found in plants, including fruits like grapes, apples, and some berries. They contribute to an astringent taste sensation, which can make the mouth feel dry or puckered. Tannins bind to proteins and other compounds in the mouth, causing this characteristic sensation.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"tannins\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Tannins are polyphenolic compounds found in plants, including fruits like grapes, apples, and some berries. They contribute to an astringent taste sensation, which can make the mouth feel dry or puckered. Tannins bind to proteins and other compounds in the mouth, causing this characteristic sensation.\n\n• Tannins are responsible for the astringent taste in certain fruits.\n• Acids contribute to sourness, not astringency.\n• Pectin is involved in gelling, not taste.\n• Fiber affects texture but not astringency.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: acids, pectin, fiber"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:freezer burn results from::::poor packaging resulting in moisture loss', 'freezer burn results from:', 'poor packaging resulting in moisture loss', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Freezer burn occurs when moisture evaporates from the surface of frozen food, leading to dehydration and the formation of ice crystals. This happens due to improper packaging that allows air to come into contact with the food, causing moisture loss and oxidation.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"poor packaging resulting in moisture loss\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Freezer burn occurs when moisture evaporates from the surface of frozen food, leading to dehydration and the formation of ice crystals. This happens due to improper packaging that allows air to come into contact with the food, causing moisture loss and oxidation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: chilling quickly, airtight moisture resistant packaging, enzymes present in food"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:freezer burn results from::::', 'freezer burn results from:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Freezer burn occurs when moisture evaporates from the surface of frozen food, leading to dehydration and the formation of ice crystals. This happens due to improper packaging that allows air to come into contact with the food, causing moisture loss and oxidation.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"poor packaging resulting in moisture loss\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Freezer burn occurs when moisture evaporates from the surface of frozen food, leading to dehydration and the formation of ice crystals. This happens due to improper packaging that allows air to come into contact with the food, causing moisture loss and oxidation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: chilling quickly, airtight moisture resistant packaging, enzymes present in food"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:sterilization is a process by which::::kills or removes all forms of microbial life', 'sterilization is a process by which:', 'kills or removes all forms of microbial life', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sterilization is the complete destruction or removal of all forms of microbial life, including bacteria, viruses, fungi, and spores. It aims to make an object or environment free from viable microorganisms to ensure it is completely safe and free from contamination.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"kills or removes all forms of microbial life\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sterilization is the complete destruction or removal of all forms of microbial life, including bacteria, viruses, fungi, and spores. It aims to make an object or environment free from viable microorganisms to ensure it is completely safe and free from contamination.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: kills only pathogenic microorganism, kills only microorganism that cause spoilage, kills only vegetative forms of bacteria but not spore"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:sterilization is a process by which::::', 'sterilization is a process by which:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sterilization is the complete destruction or removal of all forms of microbial life, including bacteria, viruses, fungi, and spores. It aims to make an object or environment free from viable microorganisms to ensure it is completely safe and free from contamination.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"kills or removes all forms of microbial life\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sterilization is the complete destruction or removal of all forms of microbial life, including bacteria, viruses, fungi, and spores. It aims to make an object or environment free from viable microorganisms to ensure it is completely safe and free from contamination.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: kills only pathogenic microorganism, kills only microorganism that cause spoilage, kills only vegetative forms of bacteria but not spore"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a cake is done if::::an inserted knife comes out clean', 'a cake is done if:', 'an inserted knife comes out clean', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When baking a cake, one common method to check for doneness is to insert a clean knife or toothpick into the center of the cake. If it comes out clean, with no batter sticking to it (a few crumbs are usually okay), then the cake is baked through and is done.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"an inserted knife comes out clean\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When baking a cake, one common method to check for doneness is to insert a clean knife or toothpick into the center of the cake. If it comes out clean, with no batter sticking to it (a few crumbs are usually okay), then the cake is baked through and is done.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: the mixture coats the inserted knife, the crust attains a golden brown color, the crust attains a dark brown color"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:element of the foodservice system that refers to the resources needed to be able to achieve the objectives of the organization:::', 'element of the foodservice system that refers to the resources needed to be able to achieve the objectives of the organization', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In the context of a foodservice system, “input” refers to the resources such as ingredients, equipment, labor, and facilities that are necessary to produce and deliver food and services to customers. These resources are essential for the functioning of the foodservice operation and for meeting its goals and objectives.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Input\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In the context of a foodservice system, “input” refers to the resources such as ingredients, equipment, labor, and facilities that are necessary to produce and deliver food and services to customers. These resources are essential for the functioning of the foodservice operation and for meeting its goals and objectives.\n\nInput refers to the resources (such as ingredients, equipment, labor, and capital) that are necessary for the foodservice system to operate and meet its goals. Inputs are essential for the production and delivery of food services.\n\n• Control involves managing and regulating the process to ensure that the desired outcomes are achieved.\n• Thruput (often referred to as “throughput”) refers to the amount of output or production that flows through a system in a given time period.\n• Feedback is information about the outcome or performance of the system that can be used to make adjustments and improvements.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Control, Thruput, Feedback"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:refers to specific knowledge and expertise in a particular field or industry, important for lower levels of management, they become less critical.:::technical skill', 'refers to specific knowledge and expertise in a particular field or industry, important for lower levels of management, they become less critical.', 'Technical skill', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Technical skill refers to specific knowledge and expertise in a particular field or industry. While technical skills are important for lower levels of management, they become less critical at the top level where managers focus more on overseeing and directing rather than performing technical tasks.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Technical skill\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Technical skill refers to specific knowledge and expertise in a particular field or industry. While technical skills are important for lower levels of management, they become less critical at the top level where managers focus more on overseeing and directing rather than performing technical tasks.\n\n• Conceptual skill: Involves the ability to think strategically, understand complex situations, and develop innovative solutions. Top-level managers need strong conceptual skills to envision the organization’s future direction, set goals, and formulate strategic plans.\n• Human skill: Also known as interpersonal or people skills, human skills involve the ability to work effectively with others, motivate and lead teams, and build relationships. These skills are important at all levels of management but are particularly crucial for middle managers who directly supervise employees.\n• Administrative skill: Involves the ability to manage tasks, processes, and resources efficiently. It includes planning, organizing, coordinating, and controlling activities within the organization. Administrative skills are essential for all levels of management but are typically more operational in nature compared to conceptual skills.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Conceptual skill, Human skill, Administrative skill"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
