, and controlling activities within the organization. Administrative skills are essential for all levels of management but are typically more operational in nature compared to conceptual skills.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Conceptual skill, Technical skill, Administrative skill"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a cake is done if::::', 'a cake is done if:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When baking a cake, one common method to check for doneness is to insert a clean knife or toothpick into the center of the cake. If it comes out clean, with no batter sticking to it (a few crumbs are usually okay), then the cake is baked through and is done.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"an inserted knife comes out clean\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When baking a cake, one common method to check for doneness is to insert a clean knife or toothpick into the center of the cake. If it comes out clean, with no batter sticking to it (a few crumbs are usually okay), then the cake is baked through and is done.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: the mixture coats the inserted knife, the crust attains a golden brown color, the crust attains a dark brown color"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:all foods are prepared and portioned in the kitchen and arranged on serving platters. food is then served to individual plates using cutlery. this method is characterized by its efficiency and the fact that all food is portioned and plated in the kitchen before being brought to the table.:::russian service', 'all foods are prepared and portioned in the kitchen and arranged on serving platters. food is then served to individual plates using cutlery. this method is characterized by its efficiency and the fact that all food is portioned and plated in the kitchen before being brought to the table.', 'Russian service', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In Russian table service (also known as \"platter service\" or \"service à la russe\"), dishes are brought to the table already portioned and arranged on serving platters or dishes. These platters are placed in front of the guests, and they serve themselves using utensils provided. This style allows for a more elegant presentation and individualized servings.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Russian service\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In Russian table service (also known as \"platter service\" or \"service à la russe\"), dishes are brought to the table already portioned and arranged on serving platters or dishes. These platters are placed in front of the guests, and they serve themselves using utensils provided. This style allows for a more elegant presentation and individualized servings.\n\n• French Service: In French service, dishes are often prepared and partially cooked in the kitchen, but the final preparation and plating might be done at the table. It involves more elaborate and formal presentation.\n• American Service: In American service, the food is completely prepared, portioned, and individually plated directly in the kitchen by the kitchen staff.\n• Blue Plate: This term often refers to a type of casual dining service or a meal served on a blue plate, but it is not a specific style of table service.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: American service, French service, Blue plate"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:all foods are prepared and portioned in the kitchen and arranged on serving platters. food is then served to individual plates using cutlery. this method is characterized by its efficiency and the fact that all food is portioned and plated in the kitchen before being brought to the table.:::', 'all foods are prepared and portioned in the kitchen and arranged on serving platters. food is then served to individual plates using cutlery. this method is characterized by its efficiency and the fact that all food is portioned and plated in the kitchen before being brought to the table.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In Russian table service (also known as \"platter service\" or \"service à la russe\"), dishes are brought to the table already portioned and arranged on serving platters or dishes. These platters are placed in front of the guests, and they serve themselves using utensils provided. This style allows for a more elegant presentation and individualized servings.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Russian service\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In Russian table service (also known as \"platter service\" or \"service à la russe\"), dishes are brought to the table already portioned and arranged on serving platters or dishes. These platters are placed in front of the guests, and they serve themselves using utensils provided. This style allows for a more elegant presentation and individualized servings.\n\n• French Service: In French service, dishes are often prepared and partially cooked in the kitchen, but the final preparation and plating might be done at the table. It involves more elaborate and formal presentation.\n• American Service: In American service, the food is completely prepared, portioned, and individually plated directly in the kitchen by the kitchen staff.\n• Blue Plate: This term often refers to a type of casual dining service or a meal served on a blue plate, but it is not a specific style of table service.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: American service, French service, Blue plate"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a mixture of spiced, herbs, and sometimes salt that is applied to the surface of meat to add flavor. it it not a curing salt but is used primarily for seasoning.:::rub', 'a mixture of spiced, herbs, and sometimes salt that is applied to the surface of meat to add flavor. it it not a curing salt but is used primarily for seasoning.', 'rub', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A rub is a blend of dry spices, herbs, sugars, and salts applied generously to the exterior surface of meat before it is cooked (often smoked, grilled, or roasted).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"rub\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A rub is a blend of dry spices, herbs, sugars, and salts applied generously to the exterior surface of meat before it is cooked (often smoked, grilled, or roasted).\n\nIts main purpose is to season the meat, enhance flavor, and form a flavorful crust or \"bark\" on the surface during the cooking process.\n\nUnlike curing agents (such as Prague powder), it does not act as a chemical preservative or color fixative for long-term meat preservation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Prague powder, kosher salt, fleur de sel"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a mixture of spiced, herbs, and sometimes salt that is applied to the surface of meat to add flavor. it it not a curing salt but is used primarily for seasoning.:::', 'a mixture of spiced, herbs, and sometimes salt that is applied to the surface of meat to add flavor. it it not a curing salt but is used primarily for seasoning.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A rub is a blend of dry spices, herbs, sugars, and salts applied generously to the exterior surface of meat before it is cooked (often smoked, grilled, or roasted).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"rub\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A rub is a blend of dry spices, herbs, sugars, and salts applied generously to the exterior surface of meat before it is cooked (often smoked, grilled, or roasted).\n\nIts main purpose is to season the meat, enhance flavor, and form a flavorful crust or \"bark\" on the surface during the cooking process.\n\nUnlike curing agents (such as Prague powder), it does not act as a chemical preservative or color fixative for long-term meat preservation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Prague powder, kosher salt, fleur de sel"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:element of the foodservice system that refers to the resources needed to be able to achieve the objectives of the organization:::input', 'element of the foodservice system that refers to the resources needed to be able to achieve the objectives of the organization', 'Input', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In the context of a foodservice system, “input” refers to the resources such as ingredients, equipment, labor, and facilities that are necessary to produce and deliver food and services to customers. These resources are essential for the functioning of the foodservice operation and for meeting its goals and objectives.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Input\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In the context of a foodservice system, “input” refers to the resources such as ingredients, equipment, labor, and facilities that are necessary to produce and deliver food and services to customers. These resources are essential for the functioning of the foodservice operation and for meeting its goals and objectives.\n\nInput refers to the resources (such as ingredients, equipment, labor, and capital) that are necessary for the foodservice system to operate and meet its goals. Inputs are essential for the production and delivery of food services.\n\n• Control involves managing and regulating the process to ensure that the desired outcomes are achieved.\n• Thruput (often referred to as “throughput”) refers to the amount of output or production that flows through a system in a given time period.\n• Feedback is information about the outcome or performance of the system that can be used to make adjustments and improvements.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Control, Thruput, Feedback"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a coarse-grained salt commonly used in cooking and for meat. it it not typically used for curing but is favored for its clean flavor and ability to draw out moisture from foods.:::kosher salt', 'a coarse-grained salt commonly used in cooking and for meat. it it not typically used for curing but is favored for its clean flavor and ability to draw out moisture from foods.', 'kosher salt', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Kosher salt gets its name from the traditional Jewish process of koshering meats (drawing blood out). Its coarse, flaky crystal structure lacks additives like iodine, yielding a very clean flavor.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"kosher salt\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Kosher salt gets its name from the traditional Jewish process of koshering meats (drawing blood out). Its coarse, flaky crystal structure lacks additives like iodine, yielding a very clean flavor.\n\nChefs prefer its tactile, large grains for even seasoning. Its specific surface structure makes it highly effective at drawing out surface moisture from meats via osmosis during dry-brining.\n\nWhile it is widely used in daily cooking, it is distinct from specialized curing salts like Prague powder (which contains sodium nitrite to preserve meats and prevent bacterial growth)","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Prague powder, rub, fleur de sel"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a coarse-grained salt commonly used in cooking and for meat. it it not typically used for curing but is favored for its clean flavor and ability to draw out moisture from foods.:::', 'a coarse-grained salt commonly used in cooking and for meat. it it not typically used for curing but is favored for its clean flavor and ability to draw out moisture from foods.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Kosher salt gets its name from the traditional Jewish process of koshering meats (drawing blood out). Its coarse, flaky crystal structure lacks additives like iodine, yielding a very clean flavor.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"kosher salt\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Kosher salt gets its name from the traditional Jewish process of koshering meats (drawing blood out). Its coarse, flaky crystal structure lacks additives like iodine, yielding a very clean flavor.\n\nChefs prefer its tactile, large grains for even seasoning. Its specific surface structure makes it highly effective at drawing out surface moisture from meats via osmosis during dry-brining.\n\nWhile it is widely used in daily cooking, it is distinct from specialized curing salts like Prague powder (which contains sodium nitrite to preserve meats and prevent bacterial growth)","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Prague powder, rub, fleur de sel"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a type of sea salt harvested from the surface of salt ponds. it is prized for its delicate flavor and texture and is often used as finishing salt, but is not used for curing.:::fleur de sel', 'a type of sea salt harvested from the surface of salt ponds. it is prized for its delicate flavor and texture and is often used as finishing salt, but is not used for curing.', 'fleur de sel', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Fleur de sel (literally \"flower of salt\" in French) forms as a thin, fragile crystalline crust on the surface of salt ponds as seawater evaporates under specific sun and wind conditions.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"fleur de sel\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Fleur de sel (literally \"flower of salt\" in French) forms as a thin, fragile crystalline crust on the surface of salt ponds as seawater evaporates under specific sun and wind conditions.\n\nIt is meticulously hand-harvested by skimming the water''s surface before the crystals sink.\n\nBecause it is expensive, labor-intensive, and possesses a delicate texture and complex mineral flavor, it is strictly used as a finishing salt to elevate dishes immediately before serving. It is never wasted in cooking processes like boiling water or curing meats.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Prague powder, rub, kosher salt"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a type of sea salt harvested from the surface of salt ponds. it is prized for its delicate flavor and texture and is often used as finishing salt, but is not used for curing.:::', 'a type of sea salt harvested from the surface of salt ponds. it is prized for its delicate flavor and texture and is often used as finishing salt, but is not used for curing.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Fleur de sel (literally \"flower of salt\" in French) forms as a thin, fragile crystalline crust on the surface of salt ponds as seawater evaporates under specific sun and wind conditions.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"fleur de sel\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Fleur de sel (literally \"flower of salt\" in French) forms as a thin, fragile crystalline crust on the surface of salt ponds as seawater evaporates under specific sun and wind conditions.\n\nIt is meticulously hand-harvested by skimming the water''s surface before the crystals sink.\n\nBecause it is expensive, labor-intensive, and possesses a delicate texture and complex mineral flavor, it is strictly used as a finishing salt to elevate dishes immediately before serving. It is never wasted in cooking processes like boiling water or curing meats.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Prague powder, rub, kosher salt"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:food in its raw state, minus that which is discarded (bones, fat, skins, and/or seeds)::::edible portion (ep)', 'food in its raw state, minus that which is discarded (bones, fat, skins, and/or seeds):', 'Edible portion (EP)', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The edible portion of food refers to the part of the food item that is consumed after preparation, excluding any inedible or non-consumable parts like bones, peels, seeds, or other undesirable parts.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Edible portion (EP)\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The edible portion of food refers to the part of the food item that is consumed after preparation, excluding any inedible or non-consumable parts like bones, peels, seeds, or other undesirable parts.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: As purchased (AP), Percentage Yield, Raw food weight"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:salted fish roe especially of sturgeon or cod.:::caviar', 'salted fish roe especially of sturgeon or cod.', 'caviar', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Caviar is a delicacy consisting of salt-cured fish roe (eggs), typically from sturgeon fish, although the term can also be used for roe from other fish species such as cod. It is known for its distinctive flavor, often described as briny and buttery, and is commonly served as a garnish or spread on dishes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"caviar\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Caviar is a delicacy consisting of salt-cured fish roe (eggs), typically from sturgeon fish, although the term can also be used for roe from other fish species such as cod. It is known for its distinctive flavor, often described as briny and buttery, and is commonly served as a garnish or spread on dishes.\n\n• Anchovies: Small, salted fish commonly used as a flavoring ingredient in various dishes.\n• Scampi: A type of lobster or shrimp-like crustacean.\n• Bagoong: Fermented fish or shrimp paste used in Filipino cuisine.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: anchovies, scampi, bagoong"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:salted fish roe especially of sturgeon or cod.:::', 'salted fish roe especially of sturgeon or cod.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Caviar is a delicacy consisting of salt-cured fish roe (eggs), typically from sturgeon fish, although the term can also be used for roe from other fish species such as cod. It is known for its distinctive flavor, often described as briny and buttery, and is commonly served as a garnish or spread on dishes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"caviar\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Caviar is a delicacy consisting of salt-cured fish roe (eggs), typically from sturgeon fish, although the term can also be used for roe from other fish species such as cod. It is known for its distinctive flavor, often described as briny and buttery, and is commonly served as a garnish or spread on dishes.\n\n• Anchovies: Small, salted fish commonly used as a flavoring ingredient in various dishes.\n• Scampi: A type of lobster or shrimp-like crustacean.\n• Bagoong: Fermented fish or shrimp paste used in Filipino cuisine.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: anchovies, scampi, bagoong"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:type of microorganism that will grow in orange marmalade.:::osmophilic', 'type of microorganism that will grow in orange marmalade.', 'Osmophilic', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Orange marmalade has a high sugar content, which creates an environment with low water activity. This condition is most favorable for osmophilic microorganisms. Osmophilic microorganisms thrive in environments with high osmotic pressure, such as those with high concentrations of sugar.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Osmophilic\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Orange marmalade has a high sugar content, which creates an environment with low water activity. This condition is most favorable for osmophilic microorganisms. Osmophilic microorganisms thrive in environments with high osmotic pressure, such as those with high concentrations of sugar.\n\nTherefore, the type of microorganism that will grow in orange marmalade is osmophilic.\n\n• Halophilic organisms thrive in high salt concentrations\n• Xerophilic organisms are adapted to survive in extremely dry environments\n• Thermophilic organisms flourish in high-temperature conditions","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Halophilic, Xerophilic, Thermophilic"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:type of microorganism that will grow in orange marmalade.:::', 'type of microorganism that will grow in orange marmalade.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Orange marmalade has a high sugar content, which creates an environment with low water activity. This condition is most favorable for osmophilic microorganisms. Osmophilic microorganisms thrive in environments with high osmotic pressure, such as those with high concentrations of sugar.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Osmophilic\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Orange marmalade has a high sugar content, which creates an environment with low water activity. This condition is most favorable for osmophilic microorganisms. Osmophilic microorganisms thrive in environments with high osmotic pressure, such as those with high concentrations of sugar.\n\nTherefore, the type of microorganism that will grow in orange marmalade is osmophilic.\n\n• Halophilic organisms thrive in high salt concentrations\n• Xerophilic organisms are adapted to survive in extremely dry environments\n• Thermophilic organisms flourish in high-temperature conditions","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Halophilic, Xerophilic, Thermophilic"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:milk which undergoes a mechanical process in order to break up fat globules and prevent them from coalescing is called::::homogenized milk', 'milk which undergoes a mechanical process in order to break up fat globules and prevent them from coalescing is called:', 'homogenized milk', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Homogenization is a process where milk is forced through a fine filter under high pressure to break up the fat globules into smaller sizes. This process helps to distribute the fat evenly throughout the milk, preventing cream from rising to the top and creating a smoother texture.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"homogenized milk\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Homogenization is a process where milk is forced through a fine filter under high pressure to break up the fat globules into smaller sizes. This process helps to distribute the fat evenly throughout the milk, preventing cream from rising to the top and creating a smoother texture.\n\n• Skimmed milk refers to milk from which most of the fat has been removed\n• Evaporated milk has had about 60% of its water content removed by evaporation\n• Powdered milk is milk that has been dehydrated to form a powder","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: skimmed milk, evaporated milk, powdered milk"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:milk which undergoes a mechanical process in order to break up fat globules and prevent them from coalescing is called::::', 'milk which undergoes a mechanical process in order to break up fat globules and prevent them from coalescing is called:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Homogenization is a process where milk is forced through a fine filter under high pressure to break up the fat globules into smaller sizes. This process helps to distribute the fat evenly throughout the milk, preventing cream from rising to the top and creating a smoother texture.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"homogenized milk\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Homogenization is a process where milk is forced through a fine filter under high pressure to break up the fat globules into smaller sizes. This process helps to distribute the fat evenly throughout the milk, preventing cream from rising to the top and creating a smoother texture.\n\n• Skimmed milk refers to milk from which most of the fat has been removed\n• Evaporated milk has had about 60% of its water content removed by evaporation\n• Powdered milk is milk that has been dehydrated to form a powder","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: skimmed milk, evaporated milk, powdered milk"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:“suahe” and “puti” are specifications for:::shrimps', '“suahe” and “puti” are specifications for', 'shrimps', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Suahe typically refers to a type of shrimp known as “white shrimp” in English. It’s a common term used in Filipino cuisine to describe certain varieties of shrimp.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"shrimps\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Suahe typically refers to a type of shrimp known as “white shrimp” in English. It’s a common term used in Filipino cuisine to describe certain varieties of shrimp.\n\nPuti means “white” in Filipino and is used to describe the color of the shrimp, often referring to shrimp that are light or white in color.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: crabs, clams, fish"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:“suahe” and “puti” are specifications for:::', '“suahe” and “puti” are specifications for', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Suahe typically refers to a type of shrimp known as “white shrimp” in English. It’s a common term used in Filipino cuisine to describe certain varieties of shrimp.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"shrimps\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Suahe typically refers to a type of shrimp known as “white shrimp” in English. It’s a common term used in Filipino cuisine to describe certain varieties of shrimp.\n\nPuti means “white” in Filipino and is used to describe the color of the shrimp, often referring to shrimp that are light or white in color.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: crabs, clams, fish"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the pigment responsible for the white color of fruits and vegetables:::anthoxanthin', 'the pigment responsible for the white color of fruits and vegetables', 'Anthoxanthin', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Anthoxanthins are a group of water-soluble pigments that give fruits and vegetables their white or cream color. They are often present in foods such as cauliflower, potatoes, onions, and turnips. Anthoxanthins are colorless in acidic conditions and turn yellowish or white in alkaline conditions.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Anthoxanthin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Anthoxanthins are a group of water-soluble pigments that give fruits and vegetables their white or cream color. They are often present in foods such as cauliflower, potatoes, onions, and turnips. Anthoxanthins are colorless in acidic conditions and turn yellowish or white in alkaline conditions.\n\n• Chlorophyll: Gives green color to plants and vegetables.\n• Anthocyanin: Responsible for red, purple, and blue colors in fruits and vegetables.\n• Carotenoid: Responsible for orange, yellow, and some red colors in fruits and vegetables.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Chlorophyll, Anthocyanin, Carotenoid"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:amount of calorie to reduce per day to effect weight loss of 1.5 lbs per week::::', 'amount of calorie to reduce per day to effect weight loss of 1.5 lbs per week:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• 500 kcal: This deficit results in a weight loss of 1 lb per week.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"750 kcal\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• 500 kcal: This deficit results in a weight loss of 1 lb per week.\n• 1,000 kcal: This deficit results in a weight loss of 2 lbs per week.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 500 kcal, 1000 kcal, 100 kcal"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the pigment responsible for the white color of fruits and vegetables:::', 'the pigment responsible for the white color of fruits and vegetables', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Anthoxanthins are a group of water-soluble pigments that give fruits and vegetables their white or cream color. They are often present in foods such as cauliflower, potatoes, onions, and turnips. Anthoxanthins are colorless in acidic conditions and turn yellowish or white in alkaline conditions.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Anthoxanthin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Anthoxanthins are a group of water-soluble pigments that give fruits and vegetables their white or cream color. They are often present in foods such as cauliflower, potatoes, onions, and turnips. Anthoxanthins are colorless in acidic conditions and turn yellowish or white in alkaline conditions.\n\n• Chlorophyll: Gives green color to plants and vegetables.\n• Anthocyanin: Responsible for red, purple, and blue colors in fruits and vegetables.\n• Carotenoid: Responsible for orange, yellow, and some red colors in fruits and vegetables.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Chlorophyll, Anthocyanin, Carotenoid"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a process that causes drying and hardening of cooked starch:::retrogradation', 'a process that causes drying and hardening of cooked starch', 'retrogradation', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Retrogradation is the process where gelatinized starch molecules, upon cooling, undergo re-association and realignment, resulting in the firming or hardening of the starch. This is often observed in foods like bread and starchy foods that have been cooked and then cooled.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"retrogradation\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Retrogradation is the process where gelatinized starch molecules, upon cooling, undergo re-association and realignment, resulting in the firming or hardening of the starch. This is often observed in foods like bread and starchy foods that have been cooked and then cooled.\n\n• Gelation: The process in which a gel forms when heated and then cools, resulting in a semi-solid structure.\n• Caramelization: The browning and breakdown of sugars under high heat, resulting in the formation of a characteristic caramel flavor and color.\n• Gelatinization: The process in which starch granules absorb water and swell when heated in the presence of water, resulting in the thickening of a liquid.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: gelation, caramelization, gelatinization"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a process that causes drying and hardening of cooked starch:::', 'a process that causes drying and hardening of cooked starch', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Retrogradation is the process where gelatinized starch molecules, upon cooling, undergo re-association and realignment, resulting in the firming or hardening of the starch. This is often observed in foods like bread and starchy foods that have been cooked and then cooled.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"retrogradation\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Retrogradation is the process where gelatinized starch molecules, upon cooling, undergo re-association and realignment, resulting in the firming or hardening of the starch. This is often observed in foods like bread and starchy foods that have been cooked and then cooled.\n\n• Gelation: The process in which a gel forms when heated and then cools, resulting in a semi-solid structure.\n• Caramelization: The browning and breakdown of sugars under high heat, resulting in the formation of a characteristic caramel flavor and color.\n• Gelatinization: The process in which starch granules absorb water and swell when heated in the presence of water, resulting in the thickening of a liquid.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: gelation, caramelization, gelatinization"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:effect of acid on chlorophyll:::changes green to olive green', 'effect of acid on chlorophyll', 'Changes green to olive green', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The addition of acid turns chlorophyll into greenish gray (pheophytin a) or to brownish green or olive drab color (pheophytin b), in which Mg is replaced by hydrogen.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Changes green to olive green\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The addition of acid turns chlorophyll into greenish gray (pheophytin a) or to brownish green or olive drab color (pheophytin b), in which Mg is replaced by hydrogen.\n\nThe addition of baking soda forms chlorophyllin, which has a bright green color.\n\n• Effect of Acid: Olive green\n• Effect of Alkali: Intensifies green color\n• Effect of Prolonged Cooking: Olive green\n• Solubility in Water: Slight","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Disintegration, Intensifies green color, No effect"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:effect of acid on chlorophyll:::', 'effect of acid on chlorophyll', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The addition of acid turns chlorophyll into greenish gray (pheophytin a) or to brownish green or olive drab color (pheophytin b), in which Mg is replaced by hydrogen.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Changes green to olive green\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The addition of acid turns chlorophyll into greenish gray (pheophytin a) or to brownish green or olive drab color (pheophytin b), in which Mg is replaced by hydrogen.\n\nThe addition of baking soda forms chlorophyllin, which has a bright green color.\n\n• Effect of Acid: Olive green\n• Effect of Alkali: Intensifies green color\n• Effect of Prolonged Cooking: Olive green\n• Solubility in Water: Slight","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Disintegration, Intensifies green color, No effect"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:heat transfer which involves the progressive transfer of heat from the source to the pan to the food is called::::conduction', 'heat transfer which involves the progressive transfer of heat from the source to the pan to the food is called:', 'conduction', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Conduction is the process by which heat energy is transferred through direct contact between materials of different temperatures. In cooking, this occurs when the heat from a stove burner or other heat source is conducted through the metal pan and then to the food being cooked.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"conduction\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Conduction is the process by which heat energy is transferred through direct contact between materials of different temperatures. In cooking, this occurs when the heat from a stove burner or other heat source is conducted through the metal pan and then to the food being cooked.\n\n• Convection: Convection involves the transfer of heat through a fluid (liquid or gas) by the circulation of currents. In cooking, this occurs when heat is transferred by the movement of air or liquid, such as in a convection oven or boiling water.\n• Radiation involves the transfer of heat through electromagnetic waves. In cooking, this can occur through infrared radiation from a broiler or microwave radiation in a microwave oven.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: convection, radiation, combination of convection and reduction"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:heat transfer which involves the progressive transfer of heat from the source to the pan to the food is called::::', 'heat transfer which involves the progressive transfer of heat from the source to the pan to the food is called:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Conduction is the process by which heat energy is transferred through direct contact between materials of different temperatures. In cooking, this occurs when the heat from a stove burner or other heat source is conducted through the metal pan and then to the food being cooked.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"conduction\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Conduction is the process by which heat energy is transferred through direct contact between materials of different temperatures. In cooking, this occurs when the heat from a stove burner or other heat source is conducted through the metal pan and then to the food being cooked.\n\n• Convection: Convection involves the transfer of heat through a fluid (liquid or gas) by the circulation of currents. In cooking, this occurs when heat is transferred by the movement of air or liquid, such as in a convection oven or boiling water.\n• Radiation involves the transfer of heat through electromagnetic waves. In cooking, this can occur through infrared radiation from a broiler or microwave radiation in a microwave oven.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: convection, radiation, combination of convection and reduction"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. in traditional ice cream making, salt is added to ice in the “garapinyera” during the freezing of ice cream mixture. sentence b. the addition of salt to ice prevents the melting of ice, thus ensuring quick freezing.:::sentence a is correct.', 'sentence a. in traditional ice cream making, salt is added to ice in the “garapinyera” during the freezing of ice cream mixture. sentence b. the addition of salt to ice prevents the melting of ice, thus ensuring quick freezing.', 'Sentence A is correct.', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sentence A is correct. In traditional ice cream making, particularly in the use of an ice cream maker like the ‘garapinyera,’ salt is added to ice to lower the freezing point of the ice, which helps in freezing the ice cream mixture.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sentence A is correct.\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sentence A is correct. In traditional ice cream making, particularly in the use of an ice cream maker like the ‘garapinyera,’ salt is added to ice to lower the freezing point of the ice, which helps in freezing the ice cream mixture.\n\nStatement B is incorrect. The addition of salt to ice actually causes the ice to melt at a lower temperature. The process of melting absorbs heat from the surrounding environment (in this case, the ice cream mixture), which helps to freeze the ice cream more quickly. Salt lowers the freezing point of water, which allows the ice to absorb more heat as it melts, leading to a more effective freezing process.\n\nWhen you add salt to ice, it lowers the freezing point of the water (a process known as freezing point depression). Normally, ice melts and freezes at 0°C (32°F), but when salt is added, the melting point drops below this temperature. As a result, the ice melts because the surrounding temperature is above the new, lower freezing point.\n\nHowever, as the ice melts, it absorbs heat from its surroundings (an endothermic process), which creates a colder environment. This is why the salted ice can freeze the ice cream mixture faster, even though it actually causes the ice to melt more quickly.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence B is correct., Both sentences are correct., Both sentences are incorrect."}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. in traditional ice cream making, salt is added to ice in the “garapinyera” during the freezing of ice cream mixture. sentence b. the addition of salt to ice prevents the melting of ice, thus ensuring quick freezing.:::', 'sentence a. in traditional ice cream making, salt is added to ice in the “garapinyera” during the freezing of ice cream mixture. sentence b. the addition of salt to ice prevents the melting of ice, thus ensuring quick freezing.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sentence A is correct. In traditional ice cream making, particularly in the use of an ice cream maker like the ‘garapinyera,’ salt is added to ice to lower the freezing point of the ice, which helps in freezing the ice cream mixture.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sentence A is correct.\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sentence A is correct. In traditional ice cream making, particularly in the use of an ice cream maker like the ‘garapinyera,’ salt is added to ice to lower the freezing point of the ice, which helps in freezing the ice cream mixture.\n\nStatement B is incorrect. The addition of salt to ice actually causes the ice to melt at a lower temperature. The process of melting absorbs heat from the surrounding environment (in this case, the ice cream mixture), which helps to freeze the ice cream more quickly. Salt lowers the freezing point of water, which allows the ice to absorb more heat as it melts, leading to a more effective freezing process.\n\nWhen you add salt to ice, it lowers the freezing point of the water (a process known as freezing point depression). Normally, ice melts and freezes at 0°C (32°F), but when salt is added, the melting point drops below this temperature. As a result, the ice melts because the surrounding temperature is above the new, lower freezing point.\n\nHowever, as the ice melts, it absorbs heat from its surroundings (an endothermic process), which creates a colder environment. This is why the salted ice can freeze the ice cream mixture faster, even though it actually causes the ice to melt more quickly.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence B is correct., Both sentences are correct., Both sentences are incorrect."}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. during rigor mortis, actomyosin dissociates to actin and myosin resulting in tough meat. sentence b. rigor mortis is the stiffening of muscle.:::sentence b is correct.', 'sentence a. during rigor mortis, actomyosin dissociates to actin and myosin resulting in tough meat. sentence b. rigor mortis is the stiffening of muscle.', 'Sentence B is correct.', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"“During rigor mortis, actomyosin dissociates to actin and myosin resulting in tough meat.”","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sentence B is correct.\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"“During rigor mortis, actomyosin dissociates to actin and myosin resulting in tough meat.”\n\nThis statement is incorrect. During rigor mortis, actomyosin, a complex formed by actin and myosin, is what actually causes muscle stiffening. The dissociation of actomyosin into actin and myosin happens after rigor mortis, during the resolution phase when the muscle starts to soften again.\n\n“Rigor mortis is the stiffening of muscle.”\n\nThis statement is correct. Rigor mortis is the post-mortem stiffening of muscles due to chemical changes in the muscle fibers, primarily the formation of actomyosin as ATP is depleted.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence A is correct., Both sentences are correct., Both sentences are incorrect."}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the process of milk synthesis and secretion of the mammary gland?:::lactogenesis', 'what is the process of milk synthesis and secretion of the mammary gland?', 'Lactogenesis', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Lactogenesis is the initiation/start of milk production.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Lactogenesis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Lactogenesis is the initiation/start of milk production.\n\nLactation is the entire, ongoing process of milk production and secretion.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Lactation, All of the above, None of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. during rigor mortis, actomyosin dissociates to actin and myosin resulting in tough meat. sentence b. rigor mortis is the stiffening of muscle.:::', 'sentence a. during rigor mortis, actomyosin dissociates to actin and myosin resulting in tough meat. sentence b. rigor mortis is the stiffening of muscle.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"“During rigor mortis, actomyosin dissociates to actin and myosin resulting in tough meat.”","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sentence B is correct.\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"“During rigor mortis, actomyosin dissociates to actin and myosin resulting in tough meat.”\n\nThis statement is incorrect. During rigor mortis, actomyosin, a complex formed by actin and myosin, is what actually causes muscle stiffening. The dissociation of actomyosin into actin and myosin happens after rigor mortis, during the resolution phase when the muscle starts to soften again.\n\n“Rigor mortis is the stiffening of muscle.”\n\nThis statement is correct. Rigor mortis is the post-mortem stiffening of muscles due to chemical changes in the muscle fibers, primarily the formation of actomyosin as ATP is depleted.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence A is correct., Both sentences are correct., Both sentences are incorrect."}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. in native cheese making, milk may be coagulated with the enzyme rennet. sentence b. in native cheese making, vinegar may be used to curdle the milk.:::both sentences are correct.', 'sentence a. in native cheese making, milk may be coagulated with the enzyme rennet. sentence b. in native cheese making, vinegar may be used to curdle the milk.', 'Both sentences are correct.', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"“In native cheese making, milk may be coagulated with the enzyme rennet.”","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Both sentences are correct.\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"“In native cheese making, milk may be coagulated with the enzyme rennet.”\n\nThis statement is correct. Rennet, which contains the enzyme chymosin, is commonly used in cheese making to coagulate milk, causing it to separate into curds and whey.\n\n“In native cheese making, vinegar may be used to curdle the milk.”\n\nThis statement is correct. Vinegar, an acidic substance, can be used to curdle milk by lowering its pH, which causes the proteins in the milk to coagulate and form curds.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence A is correct., Sentence B is correct., Both sentences are incorrect."}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. in native cheese making, milk may be coagulated with the enzyme rennet. sentence b. in native cheese making, vinegar may be used to curdle the milk.:::', 'sentence a. in native cheese making, milk may be coagulated with the enzyme rennet. sentence b. in native cheese making, vinegar may be used to curdle the milk.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"“In native cheese making, milk may be coagulated with the enzyme rennet.”","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Both sentences are correct.\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"“In native cheese making, milk may be coagulated with the enzyme rennet.”\n\nThis statement is correct. Rennet, which contains the enzyme chymosin, is commonly used in cheese making to coagulate milk, causing it to separate into curds and whey.\n\n“In native cheese making, vinegar may be used to curdle the milk.”\n\nThis statement is correct. Vinegar, an acidic substance, can be used to curdle milk by lowering its pH, which causes the proteins in the milk to coagulate and form curds.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence A is correct., Sentence B is correct., Both sentences are incorrect."}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:french word that means “everything in place”:::mise en place', 'french word that means “everything in place”', 'Mise en place', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"“Mise en place” is a culinary term that refers to the preparation and organization of ingredients and equipment before cooking. It involves arranging everything needed for cooking in an orderly and accessible manner to ensure efficiency and smooth workflow in the kitchen.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Mise en place\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"“Mise en place” is a culinary term that refers to the preparation and organization of ingredients and equipment before cooking. It involves arranging everything needed for cooking in an orderly and accessible manner to ensure efficiency and smooth workflow in the kitchen.\n\n• Mirepoix: A mixture of diced vegetables (typically onions, carrots, and celery) used as a flavor base in cooking.\n• Mise en poix: This term does not exist in culinary terminology.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Mirepoix, Mise en poix, None of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:french word that means “everything in place”:::', 'french word that means “everything in place”', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"“Mise en place” is a culinary term that refers to the preparation and organization of ingredients and equipment before cooking. It involves arranging everything needed for cooking in an orderly and accessible manner to ensure efficiency and smooth workflow in the kitchen.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Mise en place\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"“Mise en place” is a culinary term that refers to the preparation and organization of ingredients and equipment before cooking. It involves arranging everything needed for cooking in an orderly and accessible manner to ensure efficiency and smooth workflow in the kitchen.\n\n• Mirepoix: A mixture of diced vegetables (typically onions, carrots, and celery) used as a flavor base in cooking.\n• Mise en poix: This term does not exist in culinary terminology.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Mirepoix, Mise en poix, None of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:pasteurization is applied in processing foods that are generally::::acidic', 'pasteurization is applied in processing foods that are generally:', 'acidic', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Pasteurization is a process of heat treatment that aims to reduce the microbial load in food products without significantly affecting their taste or nutritional value. It is commonly used for acidic foods such as fruit juices, dairy products (like milk and yogurt), and certain sauces. The heat treatment destroys harmful bacteria and extends the shelf life of the product, making it safer for consumption.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"acidic\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Pasteurization is a process of heat treatment that aims to reduce the microbial load in food products without significantly affecting their taste or nutritional value. It is commonly used for acidic foods such as fruit juices, dairy products (like milk and yogurt), and certain sauces. The heat treatment destroys harmful bacteria and extends the shelf life of the product, making it safer for consumption.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: salty, basic, spicy"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb)
