('mc:foods allowed in tyramine-restricted diet::::some type of beans', 'foods allowed in tyramine-restricted diet:', 'Some type of beans', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A tyramine-restricted diet is commonly prescribed to individuals taking Monoamine Oxidase Inhibitors (MAOIs) to prevent a life-threatening hypertensive crisis. Tyramine builds up naturally in foods that are aged, fermented, cured, or smoked.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Some type of beans\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A tyramine-restricted diet is commonly prescribed to individuals taking Monoamine Oxidase Inhibitors (MAOIs) to prevent a life-threatening hypertensive crisis. Tyramine builds up naturally in foods that are aged, fermented, cured, or smoked.\n\nSome type of beans: While broad beans (like fava beans) contain high amounts of L-dopa/tyramine and must be strictly avoided, standard fresh or canned legumes (such as standard green beans, chickpeas, and lentils) are entirely safe and allowed on this diet.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Gouda cheese, Smoked ham, A and B only"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the folllowing are hormones of lactation except::::insulin', 'the folllowing are hormones of lactation except:', 'Insulin', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Insulin is associated with glucose metabolism and diabetes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Insulin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Insulin is associated with glucose metabolism and diabetes.\n\n• Prolactin - stimulates milk synthesis\n• Estrogen - contributes to mammary gland development\n• Oxytocin - triggers the milk ejection (let-down) reflex","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Prolactin, Estrogen, Oxytocin"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:diseases within the small intestine generally result in malabsorption because of the impaired function of the organ.:::', 'diseases within the small intestine generally result in malabsorption because of the impaired function of the organ.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The small intestine is the primary organ responsible for the chemical digestion and absorption of nutrients (such as carbohydrates, proteins, fats, vitamins, and minerals). It features specialized structural adaptations, namely villi and microvilli, which create a massive surface area for nutrient uptake.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"True\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The small intestine is the primary organ responsible for the chemical digestion and absorption of nutrients (such as carbohydrates, proteins, fats, vitamins, and minerals). It features specialized structural adaptations, namely villi and microvilli, which create a massive surface area for nutrient uptake.\n\nWhen a disease affects the small intestine (such as Celiac disease, Crohn''s disease, or Tropical sprue), it damages this delicate mucosal lining, blunts the villi, and causes widespread inflammation. This impaired structural function directly prevents the organ from absorbing nutrients effectively, leading to clinical malabsorption, nutrient deficiencies, weight loss, and chronic diarrhea.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: False, Maybe, Depends"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:it is the phase of stress response characterized by failure of adaption leading to death.:::exhaustion phase', 'it is the phase of stress response characterized by failure of adaption leading to death.', 'Exhaustion phase', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The Three Stages of Hans Selye''s General Adaptation Syndrome (GAS)","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Exhaustion phase\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The Three Stages of Hans Selye''s General Adaptation Syndrome (GAS)\n• Exhaustion Phase: This is the final stage of the stress response. If the stressor is severe, intense, or prolonged over a long period, the body''s energy reserves and compensatory resources become completely depleted. This failure of adaptation leads to immune suppression, systemic organ damage, and eventually death if left unchecked.\n\nThe Other Phases:\n\t• Alarm Reaction (Acute Phase): The initial \"fight-or-flight\" response where the sympathetic nervous system is triggered, releasing adrenaline and cortisol.\n\t• Resistance Phase (Adaptive Phase): The body attempts to adapt to the ongoing stressor, working to stabilize its physiological functions and return to a state of homeostasis.\n\t• Convalescence Phase: This refers to the recovery period after an illness or injury has passed, rather than a failure of stress management.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Acute phase, Adaptive phase, Convaslescence phase"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:it is the phase of stress response characterized by failure of adaption leading to death.:::', 'it is the phase of stress response characterized by failure of adaption leading to death.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The Three Stages of Hans Selye''s General Adaptation Syndrome (GAS)","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Exhaustion phase\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The Three Stages of Hans Selye''s General Adaptation Syndrome (GAS)\n• Exhaustion Phase: This is the final stage of the stress response. If the stressor is severe, intense, or prolonged over a long period, the body''s energy reserves and compensatory resources become completely depleted. This failure of adaptation leads to immune suppression, systemic organ damage, and eventually death if left unchecked.\n\nThe Other Phases:\n\t• Alarm Reaction (Acute Phase): The initial \"fight-or-flight\" response where the sympathetic nervous system is triggered, releasing adrenaline and cortisol.\n\t• Resistance Phase (Adaptive Phase): The body attempts to adapt to the ongoing stressor, working to stabilize its physiological functions and return to a state of homeostasis.\n\t• Convalescence Phase: This refers to the recovery period after an illness or injury has passed, rather than a failure of stress management.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Acute phase, Adaptive phase, Convaslescence phase"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:most government hospitals purchase food by::::bid buying', 'most government hospitals purchase food by:', 'Bid buying', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Government hospitals commonly use bid buying or competitive bidding to promote transparency and obtain acceptable terms under procurement rules.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Bid buying\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Government hospitals commonly use bid buying or competitive bidding to promote transparency and obtain acceptable terms under procurement rules.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Negotiated buying, Future contract, Blank check"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which body component is directly proportional to basal metabolic rate?:::lean body mass', 'which body component is directly proportional to basal metabolic rate?', 'Lean body mass', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Lean body mass (LBM), which includes skeletal muscle, organs, and bone, consists of highly metabolic tissue. Every kilogram of LBM requires a significant amount of oxygen and energy just to maintain itself at rest. Because of this, it is the single most important body composition component directly proportional to your Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR)—as lean mass increases, BMR increases.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Lean body mass\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Lean body mass (LBM), which includes skeletal muscle, organs, and bone, consists of highly metabolic tissue. Every kilogram of LBM requires a significant amount of oxygen and energy just to maintain itself at rest. Because of this, it is the single most important body composition component directly proportional to your Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR)—as lean mass increases, BMR increases.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Body fat, Extracellular volume, Total body mass"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb)
