('mc:a foodservice manager discovers that one of the following foods was left unrefrigerated for several hours. which food has the highest risk for bacterial growth, assuming all were initially free of pathogens?:::cooked chicken breast', 'a foodservice manager discovers that one of the following foods was left unrefrigerated for several hours. which food has the highest risk for bacterial growth, assuming all were initially free of pathogens?', 'Cooked chicken breast', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Cooked chicken provides several favorable conditions described by FAT TOM: food, available moisture, nutrients, and a favorable temperature/pH environment. The other foods have characteristics such as low moisture or high salt that inhibit microbial growth.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Cooked chicken breast\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Cooked chicken provides several favorable conditions described by FAT TOM: food, available moisture, nutrients, and a favorable temperature/pH environment. The other foods have characteristics such as low moisture or high salt that inhibit microbial growth.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Peanut butter, Salted dried fish, Pepper"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:during haccp development for a foodservice operation, the team determines that chicken must reach an internal temperature of at least 74°c for the specified cooking process. which haccp principle is being applied when this temperature is established as the criterion that separates safe from unsafe processing?:::establishing critical limits', 'during haccp development for a foodservice operation, the team determines that chicken must reach an internal temperature of at least 74°c for the specified cooking process. which haccp principle is being applied when this temperature is established as the criterion that separates safe from unsafe processing?', 'Establishing critical limits', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A critical limit is a measurable criterion that must be met at a CCP to control a significant hazard. Temperature and time are common examples.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Establishing critical limits\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A critical limit is a measurable criterion that must be met at a CCP to control a significant hazard. Temperature and time are common examples.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Conducting a hazard analysis, Identifying critical control points"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
