('mc:a symptom of several disease.:::diarrhea', 'a symptom of several disease.', 'Diarrhea', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Diarrhea is not a disease itself; it is a clinical symptom triggered by a wide variety of underlying localized or systemic pathologies.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Diarrhea\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Diarrhea is not a disease itself; it is a clinical symptom triggered by a wide variety of underlying localized or systemic pathologies.\n\nIt serves as a primary warning sign for numerous distinct conditions across the diagnostic spectrum, including bacterial or viral infections (food poisoning), inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn''s or Ulcerative Colitis), malabsorption disorders (Celiac disease), metabolic conditions (hyperthyroidism), and functional bowel issues (IBS).\n\nFrom a board exam standpoint, identifying diarrhea as a cross-categorical symptom is essential because its presence across multiple diseases uniformly dictates immediate nutritional interventions—specifically managing rapid fluid losses, replacing lost electrolytes, and mitigating the risk of acute malnutrition.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Constipation, Diverticulosis, Diverticula"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a symptom of several disease.:::', 'a symptom of several disease.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Diarrhea is not a disease itself; it is a clinical symptom triggered by a wide variety of underlying localized or systemic pathologies.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Diarrhea\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Diarrhea is not a disease itself; it is a clinical symptom triggered by a wide variety of underlying localized or systemic pathologies.\n\nIt serves as a primary warning sign for numerous distinct conditions across the diagnostic spectrum, including bacterial or viral infections (food poisoning), inflammatory bowel diseases (Crohn''s or Ulcerative Colitis), malabsorption disorders (Celiac disease), metabolic conditions (hyperthyroidism), and functional bowel issues (IBS).\n\nFrom a board exam standpoint, identifying diarrhea as a cross-categorical symptom is essential because its presence across multiple diseases uniformly dictates immediate nutritional interventions—specifically managing rapid fluid losses, replacing lost electrolytes, and mitigating the risk of acute malnutrition.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Constipation, Diverticulosis, Diverticula"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a procedure when the entire colon is bypassed, and opening is created from the ileum that is attached directly to the anus.:::', 'a procedure when the entire colon is bypassed, and opening is created from the ileum that is attached directly to the anus.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"An anastomosis is a surgical term that means connecting or joining two distinct structures inside the body.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Ileorectal anastomosis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"An anastomosis is a surgical term that means connecting or joining two distinct structures inside the body.\n\nWhen the entire colon is removed or bypassed, the small intestine (specifically the ileum) must be carefully brought down and surgically sewn directly to the remaining rectum or anus to allow waste to leave the body naturally without needing a permanent external pouch or stoma bag.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Tonsillectomy, Cholecystectomy, Hemorrhoidectomy"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:prevents the tendency to form blood clots in patient with cad, valve damage. example is aspirin.:::anticoagulants', 'prevents the tendency to form blood clots in patient with cad, valve damage. example is aspirin.', 'Anticoagulants', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In generalized board exam questions, Anticoagulants (and antithrombotic families) are standard classifications for medications designed to prevent the tendency to form blood clots inside blood vessels and heart chambers.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Anticoagulants\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In generalized board exam questions, Anticoagulants (and antithrombotic families) are standard classifications for medications designed to prevent the tendency to form blood clots inside blood vessels and heart chambers.\n\nThey are heavily utilized in patients with coronary artery disease (CAD) to keep plaque rupture from causing a complete vascular blockage (heart attack), and in patients with valve damage or mechanical valves where turbulent blood flow puts them at extreme risk for clot formation.\n\nNote on Pharmacology: While pharmacology textbooks distinguish between antiplatelet agents (like aspirin, which keeps platelets from clumping) and anticoagulants (like warfarin or heparin, which target coagulation cascade factors), standard board exam keys often use Anticoagulants as the macro-level matching category for clot-preventing medications when antiplatelets are not listed as a standalone choice.\n\n• Digitalis: A cardiac glycoside that increases the force of heart contractions and slows heart rate; used primarily to treat heart failure and atrial fibrillation, not to directly prevent clotting.\n• Beta-adrenergic (Beta-blockers): Medications that block epinephrine to lower heart rate and blood pressure, reducing the heart''s overall workload and oxygen demand.\n• Calcium-channel blockers: Medications that relax and widen blood vessels by blocking calcium entry into muscle cells, which helps treat hypertension and stable angina rather than halting clot synthesis.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Beta-adrenergic, Calcium-channel blockers, Digitalis"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:prevents the tendency to form blood clots in patient with cad, valve damage. example is aspirin.:::', 'prevents the tendency to form blood clots in patient with cad, valve damage. example is aspirin.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In generalized board exam questions, Anticoagulants (and antithrombotic families) are standard classifications for medications designed to prevent the tendency to form blood clots inside blood vessels and heart chambers.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Anticoagulants\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In generalized board exam questions, Anticoagulants (and antithrombotic families) are standard classifications for medications designed to prevent the tendency to form blood clots inside blood vessels and heart chambers.\n\nThey are heavily utilized in patients with coronary artery disease (CAD) to keep plaque rupture from causing a complete vascular blockage (heart attack), and in patients with valve damage or mechanical valves where turbulent blood flow puts them at extreme risk for clot formation.\n\nNote on Pharmacology: While pharmacology textbooks distinguish between antiplatelet agents (like aspirin, which keeps platelets from clumping) and anticoagulants (like warfarin or heparin, which target coagulation cascade factors), standard board exam keys often use Anticoagulants as the macro-level matching category for clot-preventing medications when antiplatelets are not listed as a standalone choice.\n\n• Digitalis: A cardiac glycoside that increases the force of heart contractions and slows heart rate; used primarily to treat heart failure and atrial fibrillation, not to directly prevent clotting.\n• Beta-adrenergic (Beta-blockers): Medications that block epinephrine to lower heart rate and blood pressure, reducing the heart''s overall workload and oxygen demand.\n• Calcium-channel blockers: Medications that relax and widen blood vessels by blocking calcium entry into muscle cells, which helps treat hypertension and stable angina rather than halting clot synthesis.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Beta-adrenergic, Calcium-channel blockers, Digitalis"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:blockers blocks norepinephrine receptors in heart muscle and thus reduce the strength of the heart beat and may correct certain arrhythmias.:::beta-adrenergic', 'blockers blocks norepinephrine receptors in heart muscle and thus reduce the strength of the heart beat and may correct certain arrhythmias.', 'Beta-adrenergic', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Beta-adrenergic blockers (commonly known as Beta-blockers, such as metoprolol or atenolol) work by directly binding to and blocking beta-1 adrenergic receptors on heart muscle cells.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Beta-adrenergic\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Beta-adrenergic blockers (commonly known as Beta-blockers, such as metoprolol or atenolol) work by directly binding to and blocking beta-1 adrenergic receptors on heart muscle cells.\n\nBy blocking the binding of stress hormones like norepinephrine and epinephrine, these medications decrease the force and strength of the heartbeat (negative inotropic effect) and lower the heart rate. This reduces the heart''s overall workload and oxygen demand while stabilizing electrical pathways to correct certain arrhythmias (irregular heartbeats).\n\n• Anticoagulants: Medications used to prevent blood clot formation, typically by disrupting the coagulation cascade. They have no effect on norepinephrine receptors or heartbeat strength.\n• Digitalis: A cardiac glycoside that does the opposite for strength—it increases the strength of the heartbeat (positive inotrope) while slowing the rate down, primarily used in heart failure management.\n• Calcium-channel blockers: These medications block calcium ions from entering heart and blood vessel muscle cells. While they can treat arrhythmias and lower blood pressure, they target calcium channels rather than norepinephrine receptors.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Anticoagulants, Calcium-channel blockers, Digitalis"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:promote good nutrition is a component of this program.:::ahmp', 'promote good nutrition is a component of this program.', 'AHMP', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The correct answer is AHMP (Anti-Hunger Mitigation Program), under which \"Promote Good Nutrition\" is listed as one of its core intervention components alongside food assistance and livelihood support strategies.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"AHMP\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The correct answer is AHMP (Anti-Hunger Mitigation Program), under which \"Promote Good Nutrition\" is listed as one of its core intervention components alongside food assistance and livelihood support strategies.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: IYCF, NGF, PSN"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:blockers blocks norepinephrine receptors in heart muscle and thus reduce the strength of the heart beat and may correct certain arrhythmias.:::', 'blockers blocks norepinephrine receptors in heart muscle and thus reduce the strength of the heart beat and may correct certain arrhythmias.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Beta-adrenergic blockers (commonly known as Beta-blockers, such as metoprolol or atenolol) work by directly binding to and blocking beta-1 adrenergic receptors on heart muscle cells.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Beta-adrenergic\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Beta-adrenergic blockers (commonly known as Beta-blockers, such as metoprolol or atenolol) work by directly binding to and blocking beta-1 adrenergic receptors on heart muscle cells.\n\nBy blocking the binding of stress hormones like norepinephrine and epinephrine, these medications decrease the force and strength of the heartbeat (negative inotropic effect) and lower the heart rate. This reduces the heart''s overall workload and oxygen demand while stabilizing electrical pathways to correct certain arrhythmias (irregular heartbeats).\n\n• Anticoagulants: Medications used to prevent blood clot formation, typically by disrupting the coagulation cascade. They have no effect on norepinephrine receptors or heartbeat strength.\n• Digitalis: A cardiac glycoside that does the opposite for strength—it increases the strength of the heartbeat (positive inotrope) while slowing the rate down, primarily used in heart failure management.\n• Calcium-channel blockers: These medications block calcium ions from entering heart and blood vessel muscle cells. While they can treat arrhythmias and lower blood pressure, they target calcium channels rather than norepinephrine receptors.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Anticoagulants, Calcium-channel blockers, Digitalis"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:permit only heat loss; no formation of adenosine, triphosphate; increase basal metabolic rate; resist weight gain.:::uncoupling proteins', 'permit only heat loss; no formation of adenosine, triphosphate; increase basal metabolic rate; resist weight gain.', 'Uncoupling proteins', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In standard cellular respiration, the mitochondria create an electrochemical proton gradient across their inner membrane, which powers ATP synthase to generate adenosine triphosphate (ATP). Uncoupling proteins (UCPs) act as specialized proton channels that short-circuit this system. They allow protons to leak back across the membrane, completely uncoupling the proton motive force from ATP synthesis.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Uncoupling proteins\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In standard cellular respiration, the mitochondria create an electrochemical proton gradient across their inner membrane, which powers ATP synthase to generate adenosine triphosphate (ATP). Uncoupling proteins (UCPs) act as specialized proton channels that short-circuit this system. They allow protons to leak back across the membrane, completely uncoupling the proton motive force from ATP synthesis.\n\nBecause the energy gradient is bypassed, no ATP is formed. Instead, 100% of the energy from food oxidation is dissipated strictly as heat loss. This massive cellular energy drain forces the body to burn through excess calories, which drastically increases the basal metabolic rate and naturally helps the organism resist weight gain.\n\n• Brown fat: Brown adipose tissue (BAT) is the specific tissue that contains a massive abundance of mitochondria packed with these uncoupling proteins. While brown fat is the site where this process happens, Uncoupling proteins are the exact molecular entities responsible for the chemical mechanism described in the prompt.\n• White fat: The primary energy-storing adipose tissue. It stores excess calories as large single droplets of triglycerides for later use rather than burning them off as heat.\n• Leptin: A hormone secreted by fat cells that signals the brain to suppress appetite and increase baseline metabolism, but it does not act as a mitochondrial membrane proton uncoupler.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Leptin, Brown fat, White fat"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:permit only heat loss; no formation of adenosine, triphosphate; increase basal metabolic rate; resist weight gain.:::', 'permit only heat loss; no formation of adenosine, triphosphate; increase basal metabolic rate; resist weight gain.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In standard cellular respiration, the mitochondria create an electrochemical proton gradient across their inner membrane, which powers ATP synthase to generate adenosine triphosphate (ATP). Uncoupling proteins (UCPs) act as specialized proton channels that short-circuit this system. They allow protons to leak back across the membrane, completely uncoupling the proton motive force from ATP synthesis.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Uncoupling proteins\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In standard cellular respiration, the mitochondria create an electrochemical proton gradient across their inner membrane, which powers ATP synthase to generate adenosine triphosphate (ATP). Uncoupling proteins (UCPs) act as specialized proton channels that short-circuit this system. They allow protons to leak back across the membrane, completely uncoupling the proton motive force from ATP synthesis.\n\nBecause the energy gradient is bypassed, no ATP is formed. Instead, 100% of the energy from food oxidation is dissipated strictly as heat loss. This massive cellular energy drain forces the body to burn through excess calories, which drastically increases the basal metabolic rate and naturally helps the organism resist weight gain.\n\n• Brown fat: Brown adipose tissue (BAT) is the specific tissue that contains a massive abundance of mitochondria packed with these uncoupling proteins. While brown fat is the site where this process happens, Uncoupling proteins are the exact molecular entities responsible for the chemical mechanism described in the prompt.\n• White fat: The primary energy-storing adipose tissue. It stores excess calories as large single droplets of triglycerides for later use rather than burning them off as heat.\n• Leptin: A hormone secreted by fat cells that signals the brain to suppress appetite and increase baseline metabolism, but it does not act as a mitochondrial membrane proton uncoupler.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Leptin, Brown fat, White fat"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:lipoprotein lipase is activated causing more weight gain after weight loss.:::weight cycling', 'lipoprotein lipase is activated causing more weight gain after weight loss.', 'Weight cycling', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Lipoprotein lipase (LPL) is the rate-limiting enzyme responsible for the uptake and storage of triglycerides into fat cells.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Weight cycling\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Lipoprotein lipase (LPL) is the rate-limiting enzyme responsible for the uptake and storage of triglycerides into fat cells.\n\nWhen an individual loses weight, the body views the loss of fat tissue as a threat to survival. In response, it increases adipose tissue LPL activity to aggressively pull fat from the bloodstream back into storage.\n\nBecause LPL activity skyrockets and remains elevated after weight reduction, it actively drives the body to regain the lost weight. This metabolic pull leads directly to weight cycling (commonly known as \"yo-yo dieting\"), where an individual falls into a repetitive pattern of losing weight only to rapidly regain it.\n\n• Dangerous drugs: Refers to hazardous chemical compounds or banned stimulants (like ephedra or PPA) used to abnormally accelerate metabolism, not the body''s natural enzymatic response to fat loss.\n• Fad diet: Represents a short-term, unscientific dietary pattern (like the cabbage soup diet). While fad diets frequently trigger weight cycling because they are unsustainable, the actual physiological activation of LPL is an enzymatic survival mechanism rather than a diet type.\n• Surgery: Refers to anatomical weight-loss interventions such as gastric bypass or sleeve gastrectomy.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Fad diet, Dangerous drugs, Surgery"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:lipoprotein lipase is activated causing more weight gain after weight loss.:::', 'lipoprotein lipase is activated causing more weight gain after weight loss.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Lipoprotein lipase (LPL) is the rate-limiting enzyme responsible for the uptake and storage of triglycerides into fat cells.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Weight cycling\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Lipoprotein lipase (LPL) is the rate-limiting enzyme responsible for the uptake and storage of triglycerides into fat cells.\n\nWhen an individual loses weight, the body views the loss of fat tissue as a threat to survival. In response, it increases adipose tissue LPL activity to aggressively pull fat from the bloodstream back into storage.\n\nBecause LPL activity skyrockets and remains elevated after weight reduction, it actively drives the body to regain the lost weight. This metabolic pull leads directly to weight cycling (commonly known as \"yo-yo dieting\"), where an individual falls into a repetitive pattern of losing weight only to rapidly regain it.\n\n• Dangerous drugs: Refers to hazardous chemical compounds or banned stimulants (like ephedra or PPA) used to abnormally accelerate metabolism, not the body''s natural enzymatic response to fat loss.\n• Fad diet: Represents a short-term, unscientific dietary pattern (like the cabbage soup diet). While fad diets frequently trigger weight cycling because they are unsustainable, the actual physiological activation of LPL is an enzymatic survival mechanism rather than a diet type.\n• Surgery: Refers to anatomical weight-loss interventions such as gastric bypass or sleeve gastrectomy.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Fad diet, Dangerous drugs, Surgery"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:like phenylpropanolamine, ephedra, and triiodothyroacetic acid.:::dangerous drugs', 'like phenylpropanolamine, ephedra, and triiodothyroacetic acid.', 'Dangerous drugs', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In the context of regulatory laws and professional practice boards, substances like phenylpropanolamine (PPA), ephedra, and triiodothyroacetic acid (TRIAC) are explicitly categorized under Dangerous drugs or banned/restricted pharmaceutical agents due to their severe, life-threatening toxicities.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Dangerous drugs\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In the context of regulatory laws and professional practice boards, substances like phenylpropanolamine (PPA), ephedra, and triiodothyroacetic acid (TRIAC) are explicitly categorized under Dangerous drugs or banned/restricted pharmaceutical agents due to their severe, life-threatening toxicities.\n\nRather than being treated as simple lifestyle components or mild dietary trends, health ministries and food and drug administrations classify these compounds strictly as high-hazard substances because of their devastating systemic impacts:\n\n\t• Phenylpropanolamine (PPA): Legally restricted and pulled from commercial availability globally due to its confirmed risk of causing hemorrhagic strokes and severe cardiovascular damage.\n\t• Ephedra: An unregulated herbal amphetamine-like stimulant explicitly banned by the FDA and international drug enforcement units following documented cases of arrhythmias, seizures, and sudden cardiac death.\n\t• Triiodothyroacetic acid (TRIAC): A highly potent thyroid hormone metabolite classified as a dangerous prescription-only or banned hormone derivative due to its risk of inducing thyrotoxicosis and metabolic shock.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Fad diet, Weight cycling, Surgery"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:like phenylpropanolamine, ephedra, and triiodothyroacetic acid.:::', 'like phenylpropanolamine, ephedra, and triiodothyroacetic acid.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In the context of regulatory laws and professional practice boards, substances like phenylpropanolamine (PPA), ephedra, and triiodothyroacetic acid (TRIAC) are explicitly categorized under Dangerous drugs or banned/restricted pharmaceutical agents due to their severe, life-threatening toxicities.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Dangerous drugs\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In the context of regulatory laws and professional practice boards, substances like phenylpropanolamine (PPA), ephedra, and triiodothyroacetic acid (TRIAC) are explicitly categorized under Dangerous drugs or banned/restricted pharmaceutical agents due to their severe, life-threatening toxicities.\n\nRather than being treated as simple lifestyle components or mild dietary trends, health ministries and food and drug administrations classify these compounds strictly as high-hazard substances because of their devastating systemic impacts:\n\n\t• Phenylpropanolamine (PPA): Legally restricted and pulled from commercial availability globally due to its confirmed risk of causing hemorrhagic strokes and severe cardiovascular damage.\n\t• Ephedra: An unregulated herbal amphetamine-like stimulant explicitly banned by the FDA and international drug enforcement units following documented cases of arrhythmias, seizures, and sudden cardiac death.\n\t• Triiodothyroacetic acid (TRIAC): A highly potent thyroid hormone metabolite classified as a dangerous prescription-only or banned hormone derivative due to its risk of inducing thyrotoxicosis and metabolic shock.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Fad diet, Weight cycling, Surgery"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:refers to the inflammation of the oral mucosa due to niacin, tryptophan, folic acid, or vit b12 deficiency.:::stomatitis', 'refers to the inflammation of the oral mucosa due to niacin, tryptophan, folic acid, or vit b12 deficiency.', 'Stomatitis', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Stomatitis is the broad medical term for any inflammation or swelling of the oral mucosa (the soft mucous membrane lining inside the mouth, including the cheeks, gums, inside of the lips, and palate).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Stomatitis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Stomatitis is the broad medical term for any inflammation or swelling of the oral mucosa (the soft mucous membrane lining inside the mouth, including the cheeks, gums, inside of the lips, and palate).\n\nRapidly dividing epithelial cells lining the mouth depend heavily on continuous coenzyme energy networks to replicate. Deficiencies in vitamins like niacin (and its amino acid precursor tryptophan), folic acid, or vitamin B12 disrupt cellular synthesis, causing the protective lining to break down. This leads directly to generalized redness, painful open sores, and burning swelling inside the mouth.\n\n• Angular stomatitis: This is a localized subcategory of inflammation that occurs strictly at the angles (corners) of the mouth, rather than representing a widespread inflammation of the entire oral mucosa. It is classically tied to riboflavin (B2) and iron deficits.\n• Cheilosis: This refers specifically to the structural drying, cracking, and splitting of the skin of the lips themselves, rather than targeting the interior mucous membranes lining the oral cavity.\n• Cleft lip/cleft palate: A congenital structural birth defect where the tissues of the lip or upper palate fail to fuse properly during embryonic development, unrelated to an acquired adult vitamin-deficiency inflammation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Cleft lip/cleft palate, Cheilosis, Angular stomatitis"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:refers to the inflammation of the oral mucosa due to niacin, tryptophan, folic acid, or vit b12 deficiency.:::', 'refers to the inflammation of the oral mucosa due to niacin, tryptophan, folic acid, or vit b12 deficiency.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Stomatitis is the broad medical term for any inflammation or swelling of the oral mucosa (the soft mucous membrane lining inside the mouth, including the cheeks, gums, inside of the lips, and palate).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Stomatitis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Stomatitis is the broad medical term for any inflammation or swelling of the oral mucosa (the soft mucous membrane lining inside the mouth, including the cheeks, gums, inside of the lips, and palate).\n\nRapidly dividing epithelial cells lining the mouth depend heavily on continuous coenzyme energy networks to replicate. Deficiencies in vitamins like niacin (and its amino acid precursor tryptophan), folic acid, or vitamin B12 disrupt cellular synthesis, causing the protective lining to break down. This leads directly to generalized redness, painful open sores, and burning swelling inside the mouth.\n\n• Angular stomatitis: This is a localized subcategory of inflammation that occurs strictly at the angles (corners) of the mouth, rather than representing a widespread inflammation of the entire oral mucosa. It is classically tied to riboflavin (B2) and iron deficits.\n• Cheilosis: This refers specifically to the structural drying, cracking, and splitting of the skin of the lips themselves, rather than targeting the interior mucous membranes lining the oral cavity.\n• Cleft lip/cleft palate: A congenital structural birth defect where the tissues of the lip or upper palate fail to fuse properly during embryonic development, unrelated to an acquired adult vitamin-deficiency inflammation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Cleft lip/cleft palate, Cheilosis, Angular stomatitis"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:refers to the inflammation of the supporting structure of the teeth with a resultant loss of alveolar bone.:::periodontitis', 'refers to the inflammation of the supporting structure of the teeth with a resultant loss of alveolar bone.', 'Periodontitis', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Periodontitis is a chronic inflammatory disease that directly affects the periodontium—the tissues, ligaments, and supporting structures that surround and anchor the teeth.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Periodontitis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Periodontitis is a chronic inflammatory disease that directly affects the periodontium—the tissues, ligaments, and supporting structures that surround and anchor the teeth.\n\nUnlike milder gum diseases, periodontitis involves the progressive destruction and loss of the alveolar bone surrounding the teeth. If left untreated, this structural breakdown causes the teeth to become loose and eventually fall out.\n\n• Tonsillitis: Refers to inflammation of the tonsils at the back of the throat.\n• Gingivitis: This is the early phase of gum inflammation. While the gums are red and swollen, the inflammation remains confined to the soft tissue and does not cause alveolar bone loss or attachment loss.\n• Glossitis: Refers strictly to inflammation or swelling of the tongue.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Glossitis, Gingivitis, Tonsilitis"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:promote good nutrition is a component of this program.:::', 'promote good nutrition is a component of this program.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The correct answer is AHMP (Anti-Hunger Mitigation Program), under which \"Promote Good Nutrition\" is listed as one of its core intervention components alongside food assistance and livelihood support strategies.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"AHMP\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The correct answer is AHMP (Anti-Hunger Mitigation Program), under which \"Promote Good Nutrition\" is listed as one of its core intervention components alongside food assistance and livelihood support strategies.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: IYCF, NGF, PSN"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:refers to the inflammation of the supporting structure of the teeth with a resultant loss of alveolar bone.:::', 'refers to the inflammation of the supporting structure of the teeth with a resultant loss of alveolar bone.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Periodontitis is a chronic inflammatory disease that directly affects the periodontium—the tissues, ligaments, and supporting structures that surround and anchor the teeth.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Periodontitis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Periodontitis is a chronic inflammatory disease that directly affects the periodontium—the tissues, ligaments, and supporting structures that surround and anchor the teeth.\n\nUnlike milder gum diseases, periodontitis involves the progressive destruction and loss of the alveolar bone surrounding the teeth. If left untreated, this structural breakdown causes the teeth to become loose and eventually fall out.\n\n• Tonsillitis: Refers to inflammation of the tonsils at the back of the throat.\n• Gingivitis: This is the early phase of gum inflammation. While the gums are red and swollen, the inflammation remains confined to the soft tissue and does not cause alveolar bone loss or attachment loss.\n• Glossitis: Refers strictly to inflammation or swelling of the tongue.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Glossitis, Gingivitis, Tonsilitis"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:pertains to the failure of the les to relax and open during swallowing.:::achalasia', 'pertains to the failure of the les to relax and open during swallowing.', 'Achalasia', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Achalasia is a specific esophageal motility disorder characterized by the selective loss of inhibitory neurons in the myenteric plexus. This neurological loss leads directly to the failure of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES) to relax and open completely when a person swallows.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Achalasia\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Achalasia is a specific esophageal motility disorder characterized by the selective loss of inhibitory neurons in the myenteric plexus. This neurological loss leads directly to the failure of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES) to relax and open completely when a person swallows.\n\nAlong with a tightly closed LES, the smooth muscle body of the esophagus fails to produce normal waves of contractions (aperistalsis). This combination traps food and liquids in the food pipe, causing it to dilate over time.\n\n• Esophagitis: An inflammation or irritation of the mucosal lining of the esophagus, often caused by acid reflux (GERD), infections, or allergies, rather than a failure of the sphincter muscle to relax.\n• Esophageal obstruction: A general term for a physical blockage in the food pipe, which can be caused by structural issues like strictures, webs, rings, or tumors.\n• Dysphagia: The broad clinical term for difficulty swallowing. While dysphagia is the primary symptom that a person with achalasia experiences, it is a symptom name rather than the specific muscular condition described.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Esophagitis, Dysphagia, Esophageal obstruction"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:pertains to the failure of the les to relax and open during swallowing.:::', 'pertains to the failure of the les to relax and open during swallowing.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Achalasia is a specific esophageal motility disorder characterized by the selective loss of inhibitory neurons in the myenteric plexus. This neurological loss leads directly to the failure of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES) to relax and open completely when a person swallows.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Achalasia\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Achalasia is a specific esophageal motility disorder characterized by the selective loss of inhibitory neurons in the myenteric plexus. This neurological loss leads directly to the failure of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES) to relax and open completely when a person swallows.\n\nAlong with a tightly closed LES, the smooth muscle body of the esophagus fails to produce normal waves of contractions (aperistalsis). This combination traps food and liquids in the food pipe, causing it to dilate over time.\n\n• Esophagitis: An inflammation or irritation of the mucosal lining of the esophagus, often caused by acid reflux (GERD), infections, or allergies, rather than a failure of the sphincter muscle to relax.\n• Esophageal obstruction: A general term for a physical blockage in the food pipe, which can be caused by structural issues like strictures, webs, rings, or tumors.\n• Dysphagia: The broad clinical term for difficulty swallowing. While dysphagia is the primary symptom that a person with achalasia experiences, it is a symptom name rather than the specific muscular condition described.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Esophagitis, Dysphagia, Esophageal obstruction"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:refers to the structure of the esophagus caused by fibrotic tissues, increased pressure or herniation from adjacent organs, and abnormal growth of tissues.:::esophageal obstruction', 'refers to the structure of the esophagus caused by fibrotic tissues, increased pressure or herniation from adjacent organs, and abnormal growth of tissues.', 'Esophageal obstruction', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"An esophageal obstruction describes any condition that physically narrows, compresses, or blocks the lumen of the esophagus, hindering the passage of food and liquids into the stomach.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Esophageal obstruction\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"An esophageal obstruction describes any condition that physically narrows, compresses, or blocks the lumen of the esophagus, hindering the passage of food and liquids into the stomach.\n\nThe prompt describes the specific mechanical pathways that lead to an obstruction:\n\t• Fibrotic tissues: Chronic irritation (like severe acid reflux) causes the mucosal lining to scar and stiffen into a tight ring, known as a peptic stricture.\n\t• Increased pressure or herniation: Conditions such as a large hiatal hernia or vascular anomalies push against the esophagus from the outside, creating external compression.\n\t• Abnormal growth of tissues: Malignant tumors (esophageal cancer) or benign polyps physically occupy space inside the tube, closing off the pathway.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Esophagitis, Dysphagia, Achalasia"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which one of the following statements is incorrect?:::', 'which one of the following statements is incorrect?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Answer should be 1/6 ~ 17%.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"According to Pinggang Pinoy, meat, fish, and alternatives should comprise one-third of the plate\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Answer should be 1/6 ~ 17%.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: According to Pinggang Pinoy, half of our plate should be include fruits and vegetable, The statement \"Eat lots of fruits and vegetables\" is a food-based dietary guideline, The statement \"Eat less sodium-no more than 2g/day\" is not food-based dietary guideline"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:refers to the structure of the esophagus caused by fibrotic tissues, increased pressure or herniation from adjacent organs, and abnormal growth of tissues.:::', 'refers to the structure of the esophagus caused by fibrotic tissues, increased pressure or herniation from adjacent organs, and abnormal growth of tissues.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"An esophageal obstruction describes any condition that physically narrows, compresses, or blocks the lumen of the esophagus, hindering the passage of food and liquids into the stomach.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Esophageal obstruction\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"An esophageal obstruction describes any condition that physically narrows, compresses, or blocks the lumen of the esophagus, hindering the passage of food and liquids into the stomach.\n\nThe prompt describes the specific mechanical pathways that lead to an obstruction:\n\t• Fibrotic tissues: Chronic irritation (like severe acid reflux) causes the mucosal lining to scar and stiffen into a tight ring, known as a peptic stricture.\n\t• Increased pressure or herniation: Conditions such as a large hiatal hernia or vascular anomalies push against the esophagus from the outside, creating external compression.\n\t• Abnormal growth of tissues: Malignant tumors (esophageal cancer) or benign polyps physically occupy space inside the tube, closing off the pathway.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Esophagitis, Dysphagia, Achalasia"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:refers to an eroded lesion in the gastric mucosa or duodenum.:::peptic ulcer', 'refers to an eroded lesion in the gastric mucosa or duodenum.', 'Peptic ulcer', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A peptic ulcer is an open sore or eroded lesion that develops when aggressive digestive juices (hydrochloric acid and pepsin) break through the tissue barriers and eat away at the protective inner lining.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Peptic ulcer\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A peptic ulcer is an open sore or eroded lesion that develops when aggressive digestive juices (hydrochloric acid and pepsin) break through the tissue barriers and eat away at the protective inner lining.\n\nIt is the standard umbrella medical term that encompasses both:\n\t• Gastric ulcers: Ulcerations that form inside the mucosal lining of the stomach.\n\t• Duodenal ulcers: Ulcerations that form in the upper portion of the small intestine (the duodenum).\n\n• Gastritis: This refers to the broad, generalized inflammation of the stomach lining. While prolonged erosive gastritis can eventually break down the mucosa and lead to an ulcer, gastritis itself describes tissue inflammation rather than a deep, well-defined open lesion or sore.\n• Gastrosucchorrhea: An older, specialized clinical term for the continuous, excessive secretion of gastric juices even when a person is completely fasting.\n• Hyperchlorhydria: As reinforced by your earlier answer key, this refers strictly to the high concentration or hypersecretion of gastric acid by the parietal cells, which functions as a biochemical cause of ulcers rather than being the physical eroded lesion itself.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Gastritis, Hyperchlorhydria, Gastrosucchorrhea"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:refers to an eroded lesion in the gastric mucosa or duodenum.:::', 'refers to an eroded lesion in the gastric mucosa or duodenum.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A peptic ulcer is an open sore or eroded lesion that develops when aggressive digestive juices (hydrochloric acid and pepsin) break through the tissue barriers and eat away at the protective inner lining.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Peptic ulcer\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A peptic ulcer is an open sore or eroded lesion that develops when aggressive digestive juices (hydrochloric acid and pepsin) break through the tissue barriers and eat away at the protective inner lining.\n\nIt is the standard umbrella medical term that encompasses both:\n\t• Gastric ulcers: Ulcerations that form inside the mucosal lining of the stomach.\n\t• Duodenal ulcers: Ulcerations that form in the upper portion of the small intestine (the duodenum).\n\n• Gastritis: This refers to the broad, generalized inflammation of the stomach lining. While prolonged erosive gastritis can eventually break down the mucosa and lead to an ulcer, gastritis itself describes tissue inflammation rather than a deep, well-defined open lesion or sore.\n• Gastrosucchorrhea: An older, specialized clinical term for the continuous, excessive secretion of gastric juices even when a person is completely fasting.\n• Hyperchlorhydria: As reinforced by your earlier answer key, this refers strictly to the high concentration or hypersecretion of gastric acid by the parietal cells, which functions as a biochemical cause of ulcers rather than being the physical eroded lesion itself.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Gastritis, Hyperchlorhydria, Gastrosucchorrhea"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:refers to the high secretion of gastric juice.:::hyperchlorhydria', 'refers to the high secretion of gastric juice.', 'Hyperchlorhydria', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Hyperchlorhydria is the medical term for the abnormally high or excessive secretion of gastric juice and hydrochloric acid (HCl) by the parietal cells in the stomach lining.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Hyperchlorhydria\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Hyperchlorhydria is the medical term for the abnormally high or excessive secretion of gastric juice and hydrochloric acid (HCl) by the parietal cells in the stomach lining.\n\nThis excess of highly acidic gastric juice lowers the stomach pH significantly. In clinical nutrition and dietetics, identifying hyperchlorhydria is crucial because this constant overproduction of digestive juices can irritate the stomach lining, leading directly to conditions like gastritis, acid reflux, or peptic ulcers if left unmanaged.\n\n• Peptic ulcer: A physical sore or lesion that forms on the lining of the stomach or duodenum. While it is frequently caused by long-term hyperchlorhydria, it is the structural damage itself rather than the secretion process.\n• Gastrosucchorrhea: An older, highly specific term for continuous gastric juice flow even when fasting, but it is not the standard clinical benchmark term used on board exams.\n• Gastritis: The broad medical term for the inflammation of the stomach wall lining, which can be triggered by excess acid, infection, or alcohol use.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Peptic ulcer, Gastritis, Gastrosucchorrhea"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which is an example of non-verbal communication?:::tone of voices', 'which is an example of non-verbal communication?', 'Tone of voices', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Tone of voice conveys meaning through vocal quality and inflection rather than through words themselves, making it a classic example of paralinguistic, non-verbal communication (body language, tone, facial expression). ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Tone of voices\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Tone of voice conveys meaning through vocal quality and inflection rather than through words themselves, making it a classic example of paralinguistic, non-verbal communication (body language, tone, facial expression). \n\nMaps, traffic signals, and reports are all forms of visual or written symbolic communication that convey explicit, standardized information.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Maps, Traffic signals, Reports"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb)
