('mc:based on the general management of childhood illness (imc) color-coded triage system, what color indicates urgent hospital referral or admission?:::pink', 'based on the general management of childhood illness (imc) color-coded triage system, what color indicates urgent hospital referral or admission?', 'Pink', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In the World Health Organization''s (WHO) Integrated Management of Childhood Illness (IMCI) guidelines, a distinct three-color-coded triage system is used to quickly classify a child''s illness and guide treatment protocols:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Pink\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In the World Health Organization''s (WHO) Integrated Management of Childhood Illness (IMCI) guidelines, a distinct three-color-coded triage system is used to quickly classify a child''s illness and guide treatment protocols:\n\n• PINK (Severe Classification): Indicates urgent hospital referral or admission. A pink row or box on the clinical chart indicates that the child exhibits general danger signs or severe symptoms (such as severe pneumonia, severe dehydration, or severe acute malnutrition). Immediate pre-referral treatment (like a first dose of antibiotics) is given before urgent transfer.\n\n• YELLOW (Specific Outpatient Treatment): Indicates that the child needs a specific medical treatment that can be managed at an outpatient health facility. Healthcare workers prescribe oral drugs and counsel the caregiver on how to administer them at home.\n\n• GREEN (Supportive Home Care): Indicates that the child has no signs of severe illness or conditions requiring specific medical treatments. The caregiver is given simple advice on supportive home management, such as continuous feeding and fluids.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Green, Yellow, Red"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:one of the typical nutritional problems among preschool children characterized by loss of subcutaneous fat and gross muscle wasting::::', 'one of the typical nutritional problems among preschool children characterized by loss of subcutaneous fat and gross muscle wasting:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"PEM – marasmus (wasting) and kwashiorkor (edema)","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"PEM\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"PEM – marasmus (wasting) and kwashiorkor (edema)\n\n• Parasitism - presence of worm; can contribute to malnutrition\n• Edema – fluid retention; different or opposite-appearing presentation of PEM\n• Hyperkinesis - hyper-active; unrelated to wasting","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Parasitism, Edema, Hyperkinesis"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:based on the general management of childhood illness (imc) color-coded triage system, what color indicates urgent hospital referral or admission?:::', 'based on the general management of childhood illness (imc) color-coded triage system, what color indicates urgent hospital referral or admission?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In the World Health Organization''s (WHO) Integrated Management of Childhood Illness (IMCI) guidelines, a distinct three-color-coded triage system is used to quickly classify a child''s illness and guide treatment protocols:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Pink\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In the World Health Organization''s (WHO) Integrated Management of Childhood Illness (IMCI) guidelines, a distinct three-color-coded triage system is used to quickly classify a child''s illness and guide treatment protocols:\n\n• PINK (Severe Classification): Indicates urgent hospital referral or admission. A pink row or box on the clinical chart indicates that the child exhibits general danger signs or severe symptoms (such as severe pneumonia, severe dehydration, or severe acute malnutrition). Immediate pre-referral treatment (like a first dose of antibiotics) is given before urgent transfer.\n\n• YELLOW (Specific Outpatient Treatment): Indicates that the child needs a specific medical treatment that can be managed at an outpatient health facility. Healthcare workers prescribe oral drugs and counsel the caregiver on how to administer them at home.\n\n• GREEN (Supportive Home Care): Indicates that the child has no signs of severe illness or conditions requiring specific medical treatments. The caregiver is given simple advice on supportive home management, such as continuous feeding and fluids.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Green, Yellow, Red"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are normal food behaviors of preschool children except::::preference for sweet and spicy', 'the following are normal food behaviors of preschool children except:', 'Preference for sweet and spicy', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Preschoolers typically show a preference for sweet and mild (not spicy) flavors, and an early preference for strongly spicy foods is not a typical, expected behavior at this age.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Preference for sweet and spicy\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Preschoolers typically show a preference for sweet and mild (not spicy) flavors, and an early preference for strongly spicy foods is not a typical, expected behavior at this age.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Erratic appetite, Unpredictable appetite, Food rituals are common"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are normal food behaviors of preschool children except::::', 'the following are normal food behaviors of preschool children except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Preschoolers typically show a preference for sweet and mild (not spicy) flavors, and an early preference for strongly spicy foods is not a typical, expected behavior at this age.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Preference for sweet and spicy\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Preschoolers typically show a preference for sweet and mild (not spicy) flavors, and an early preference for strongly spicy foods is not a typical, expected behavior at this age.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Erratic appetite, Unpredictable appetite, Food rituals are common"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:to meet the dietary allowances of the preschool children, the following are true except::::a and b are both true', 'to meet the dietary allowances of the preschool children, the following are true except:', 'A and B are both True', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Providing varied meals in amounts sufficient to satisfy appetite and offering nutritious mid-morning and afternoon snacks especially for active children are both established, evidence-based strategies for meeting preschoolers'' nutrient needs given their small stomach capacity and high activity levels.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"A and B are both True\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Providing varied meals in amounts sufficient to satisfy appetite and offering nutritious mid-morning and afternoon snacks especially for active children are both established, evidence-based strategies for meeting preschoolers'' nutrient needs given their small stomach capacity and high activity levels.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Provide meals with a variety of foods in amounts sufficient to satisfy the appetite, Offering nutritious snacks in the middle of the morning and afternoon, especially to active children, A and B are both False"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:to meet the dietary allowances of the preschool children, the following are true except::::', 'to meet the dietary allowances of the preschool children, the following are true except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Providing varied meals in amounts sufficient to satisfy appetite and offering nutritious mid-morning and afternoon snacks especially for active children are both established, evidence-based strategies for meeting preschoolers'' nutrient needs given their small stomach capacity and high activity levels.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"A and B are both True\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Providing varied meals in amounts sufficient to satisfy appetite and offering nutritious mid-morning and afternoon snacks especially for active children are both established, evidence-based strategies for meeting preschoolers'' nutrient needs given their small stomach capacity and high activity levels.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Provide meals with a variety of foods in amounts sufficient to satisfy the appetite, Offering nutritious snacks in the middle of the morning and afternoon, especially to active children, A and B are both False"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the period shortly after a slow growth period when the weight and height gains are likely to be faster than expected for age and gender.:::catch-up growth', 'the period shortly after a slow growth period when the weight and height gains are likely to be faster than expected for age and gender.', 'Catch-up Growth', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Catch-up growth is the accelerated growth following a period of growth faltering, allowing a child to \"catch up\" toward their expected growth trajectory.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Catch-up Growth\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Catch-up growth is the accelerated growth following a period of growth faltering, allowing a child to \"catch up\" toward their expected growth trajectory.\n\nGrowth spurt is the period of rapid growth (such as during adolescence) without necessarily following a preceding slow-growth or deficit period.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Growth Spurt, All of the above, None of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the period shortly after a slow growth period when the weight and height gains are likely to be faster than expected for age and gender.:::', 'the period shortly after a slow growth period when the weight and height gains are likely to be faster than expected for age and gender.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Catch-up growth is the accelerated growth following a period of growth faltering, allowing a child to \"catch up\" toward their expected growth trajectory.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Catch-up Growth\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Catch-up growth is the accelerated growth following a period of growth faltering, allowing a child to \"catch up\" toward their expected growth trajectory.\n\nGrowth spurt is the period of rapid growth (such as during adolescence) without necessarily following a preceding slow-growth or deficit period.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Growth Spurt, All of the above, None of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the protein requirement of children is::::relatively higher in relation to the body weight than those in adults', 'the protein requirement of children is:', 'Relatively higher in relation to the body weight than those in adults', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Children''s protein requirement is relatively higher in relation to body weight than that of adults, reflecting the additional protein needed to support rapid growth, tissue building, and development during childhood.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Relatively higher in relation to the body weight than those in adults\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Children''s protein requirement is relatively higher in relation to body weight than that of adults, reflecting the additional protein needed to support rapid growth, tissue building, and development during childhood.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Relatively lower in relation to the body weight than those in adults, A and B are both True, A and B are both False"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the protein requirement of children is::::', 'the protein requirement of children is:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Children''s protein requirement is relatively higher in relation to body weight than that of adults, reflecting the additional protein needed to support rapid growth, tissue building, and development during childhood.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Relatively higher in relation to the body weight than those in adults\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Children''s protein requirement is relatively higher in relation to body weight than that of adults, reflecting the additional protein needed to support rapid growth, tissue building, and development during childhood.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Relatively lower in relation to the body weight than those in adults, A and B are both True, A and B are both False"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the process of physical development from child to adult and refers to the maturation of the body.:::puberty', 'the process of physical development from child to adult and refers to the maturation of the body.', 'Puberty', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Puberty is the biological process during which the body physically matures into an adult capable of reproduction, involving hormonal, physical, and sexual maturation. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Puberty\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Puberty is the biological process during which the body physically matures into an adult capable of reproduction, involving hormonal, physical, and sexual maturation. \n\n• Adolescence is the broader psychosocial and developmental life stage encompassing puberty but also including cognitive, emotional, and social development beyond just physical maturation. \n• Pubescence is a less standard/precise term sometimes used interchangeably but not the preferred technical term. \n• Maturity is too general and does not specifically denote the physical maturation process described.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Adolescence, Pubescence, Maturity"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:psychological eating disorder that is also known as "george and purge":::', 'psychological eating disorder that is also known as "george and purge"', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Bulimia nervosa - cycles of binging (overeating) followed by compensatory behaviors (e.g., purging, excessive exercise).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Bulimia nervosa\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Bulimia nervosa - cycles of binging (overeating) followed by compensatory behaviors (e.g., purging, excessive exercise).\n\n• Anorexia nervosa - restriction leading to dangerously low body weight due to fear of fatness.\n• Binge eating - recurring episodes of uncontrolled eating (binging) without compensatory behaviors.\n• Pica - persistent consumption of non-food items (e.g., dirt, paint, paper).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Anorexia nervosa, Binge eating, Pica"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the process of physical development from child to adult and refers to the maturation of the body.:::', 'the process of physical development from child to adult and refers to the maturation of the body.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Puberty is the biological process during which the body physically matures into an adult capable of reproduction, involving hormonal, physical, and sexual maturation. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Puberty\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Puberty is the biological process during which the body physically matures into an adult capable of reproduction, involving hormonal, physical, and sexual maturation. \n\n• Adolescence is the broader psychosocial and developmental life stage encompassing puberty but also including cognitive, emotional, and social development beyond just physical maturation. \n• Pubescence is a less standard/precise term sometimes used interchangeably but not the preferred technical term. \n• Maturity is too general and does not specifically denote the physical maturation process described.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Adolescence, Pubescence, Maturity"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which is not true about brain development during school age?:::rapid growth of brain and head', 'which is not true about brain development during school age?', 'Rapid growth of brain and head', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The correct answer is rapid growth of the brain and head, because by school age brain growth has actually slowed considerably and is nearing completion, having occurred most rapidly during infancy and early childhood. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Rapid growth of brain and head\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The correct answer is rapid growth of the brain and head, because by school age brain growth has actually slowed considerably and is nearing completion, having occurred most rapidly during infancy and early childhood. \n\nSlowing down of brain/head growth and brain growth being essentially complete by school age are both accurate descriptions of this developmental stage.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Slowing down of growth of brain and head, Brain growth is essential complete during school age, All of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which is not true about brain development during school age?:::', 'which is not true about brain development during school age?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The correct answer is rapid growth of the brain and head, because by school age brain growth has actually slowed considerably and is nearing completion, having occurred most rapidly during infancy and early childhood. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Rapid growth of brain and head\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The correct answer is rapid growth of the brain and head, because by school age brain growth has actually slowed considerably and is nearing completion, having occurred most rapidly during infancy and early childhood. \n\nSlowing down of brain/head growth and brain growth being essentially complete by school age are both accurate descriptions of this developmental stage.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Slowing down of growth of brain and head, Brain growth is essential complete during school age, All of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:all are feeding problems prevalent in preschool-age except::::meal skipping', 'all are feeding problems prevalent in preschool-age except:', 'Meal skipping', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Meal skipping is more common among school children and adolescents.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Meal skipping\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Meal skipping is more common among school children and adolescents.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Food jags, Dawdling, Gagging"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
