('mc:under the mandatory food fortification program, rice is fortified with:::', 'under the mandatory food fortification program, rice is fortified with', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"See RA 8976 (Philippine Food Fortification Act of 2000):","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Iron\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"See RA 8976 (Philippine Food Fortification Act of 2000):\n\nA. Vitamin A - oil\nB. Iron – rice\nC. Iodine - salt\nD. Vitamin A and iron - wheat flour","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vit A, Iodine, Vit A and Iron"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what is an example of tertiary audience?:::religious leaders', 'what is an example of tertiary audience?', 'Religious leaders', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Religious leaders are the tertiary audience since they may encounter the message; has a general interest or stake.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Religious leaders\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Religious leaders are the tertiary audience since they may encounter the message; has a general interest or stake.\n\n• Mothers of malnourished children - primary audience (directly receives and acts on the message)\n• Barangay health workers - secondary (advises or influences the primary audience)\n• Daycare workers - secondary (advises or influences the primary audience)","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Mothers of malnourished children, Barangay health workers, Daycare workers"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what is an example of tertiary audience?:::', 'what is an example of tertiary audience?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Religious leaders are the tertiary audience since they may encounter the message; has a general interest or stake.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Religious leaders\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Religious leaders are the tertiary audience since they may encounter the message; has a general interest or stake.\n\n• Mothers of malnourished children - primary audience (directly receives and acts on the message)\n• Barangay health workers - secondary (advises or influences the primary audience)\n• Daycare workers - secondary (advises or influences the primary audience)","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Mothers of malnourished children, Barangay health workers, Daycare workers"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:international agreement signed at the first international conference on health promotion, organized by the who. it is the first of its type to consider all determinants of health and not just the absence of the disease.:::ottawa charter', 'international agreement signed at the first international conference on health promotion, organized by the who. it is the first of its type to consider all determinants of health and not just the absence of the disease.', 'Ottawa charter', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The Ottawa Charter for Health Promotion was presented and signed on November 21, 1986, at the World Health Organization''s (WHO) First International Conference on Health Promotion in Ottawa, Canada.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Ottawa charter\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The Ottawa Charter for Health Promotion was presented and signed on November 21, 1986, at the World Health Organization''s (WHO) First International Conference on Health Promotion in Ottawa, Canada.\n\nIt historically expanded the definition of health by stating that it is not merely the objective of living or the absence of disease, but a positive concept and resource for everyday life emphasizing social, personal, and physical capacities.\n\nThe agreement shifted public health focus heavily toward the determinants of health, identifying fundamental prerequisites such as peace, shelter, education, food, income, a stable ecosystem, and sustainable resources.\n\n• Zero Hunger Challenge - End hunger, malnutrition, and unsustainable food systems globally.\n• Rome Declaration - Commitments to eliminate food insecurity and poverty (outcome of WFS).\n• World Food Summit - Major UN conference aimed at eradicating hunger worldwide and ensuring food security","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Zero Hunger challenge, Rome Declaration, World Food Summit"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:international agreement signed at the first international conference on health promotion, organized by the who. it is the first of its type to consider all determinants of health and not just the absence of the disease.:::', 'international agreement signed at the first international conference on health promotion, organized by the who. it is the first of its type to consider all determinants of health and not just the absence of the disease.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The Ottawa Charter for Health Promotion was presented and signed on November 21, 1986, at the World Health Organization''s (WHO) First International Conference on Health Promotion in Ottawa, Canada.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Ottawa charter\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The Ottawa Charter for Health Promotion was presented and signed on November 21, 1986, at the World Health Organization''s (WHO) First International Conference on Health Promotion in Ottawa, Canada.\n\nIt historically expanded the definition of health by stating that it is not merely the objective of living or the absence of disease, but a positive concept and resource for everyday life emphasizing social, personal, and physical capacities.\n\nThe agreement shifted public health focus heavily toward the determinants of health, identifying fundamental prerequisites such as peace, shelter, education, food, income, a stable ecosystem, and sustainable resources.\n\n• Zero Hunger Challenge - End hunger, malnutrition, and unsustainable food systems globally.\n• Rome Declaration - Commitments to eliminate food insecurity and poverty (outcome of WFS).\n• World Food Summit - Major UN conference aimed at eradicating hunger worldwide and ensuring food security","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Zero Hunger challenge, Rome Declaration, World Food Summit"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the element of learning that involves preparedness of a person to handle change in behavior?:::readiness', 'what is the element of learning that involves preparedness of a person to handle change in behavior?', 'Readiness', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Readiness is the preparation or willingness (physical or mental) to learn or act, i.e., being motivated to study for an exam.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Readiness\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Readiness is the preparation or willingness (physical or mental) to learn or act, i.e., being motivated to study for an exam.\n\n• Situation - stimulus or context that initiates a behavior, i.e. seeing a tempting dessert.\n• Interpretation - meaning or understanding assigned to the situation; “That dessert is a reward\" OR \"That dessert is unhealthy.\"\n• Reaction to thwarting - behavioral response to obstacles preventing goal achievement, i.e. getting angry when a recipe fails.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Situation, Interpretation, Reaction to thwarting"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the element of learning that involves preparedness of a person to handle change in behavior?:::', 'what is the element of learning that involves preparedness of a person to handle change in behavior?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Readiness is the preparation or willingness (physical or mental) to learn or act, i.e., being motivated to study for an exam.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Readiness\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Readiness is the preparation or willingness (physical or mental) to learn or act, i.e., being motivated to study for an exam.\n\n• Situation - stimulus or context that initiates a behavior, i.e. seeing a tempting dessert.\n• Interpretation - meaning or understanding assigned to the situation; “That dessert is a reward\" OR \"That dessert is unhealthy.\"\n• Reaction to thwarting - behavioral response to obstacles preventing goal achievement, i.e. getting angry when a recipe fails.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Situation, Interpretation, Reaction to thwarting"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:"i can exercise" in social psychological theory is an example of::::self-efficacy', '"i can exercise" in social psychological theory is an example of:', 'Self-efficacy', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• Subjective norm - \"My friends think I should run this marathon.\"","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Self-efficacy\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• Subjective norm - \"My friends think I should run this marathon.\"\n• Goal intention - \"I intend to lose 10 pounds.\"\n• Facilitating condition - Having a nearby gym or readily available healthy food.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Subjective norm, Goal intention, Facilitating condition"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
