NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a high sodium consumption equivalent to grams salt/day contributes to high blood pressure.:::5', 'a high sodium consumption equivalent to grams salt/day contributes to high blood pressure.', '5', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"WHO recommends a reduction in sodium intake to reduce blood pressure and risk of cardiovascular disease, stroke and coronary heart disease in adults (strong recommendation). WHO recommends a reduction to <2 g/day sodium (5 g/day salt)","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"5\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"WHO recommends a reduction in sodium intake to reduce blood pressure and risk of cardiovascular disease, stroke and coronary heart disease in adults (strong recommendation). WHO recommends a reduction to <2 g/day sodium (5 g/day salt)\nin adults (strong recommendation).\n\nSee reference here:\nGuideline: Sodium intake for adults and children (2012): https://iris.who.int/server/api/core/bitstreams/d0f9feb5-ed78-44d1-9e06-533a93352012/content","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 2, 6.5"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a high sodium consumption equivalent to grams salt/day contributes to high blood pressure.:::', 'a high sodium consumption equivalent to grams salt/day contributes to high blood pressure.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"WHO recommends a reduction in sodium intake to reduce blood pressure and risk of cardiovascular disease, stroke and coronary heart disease in adults (strong recommendation). WHO recommends a reduction to <2 g/day sodium (5 g/day salt)","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"5\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"WHO recommends a reduction in sodium intake to reduce blood pressure and risk of cardiovascular disease, stroke and coronary heart disease in adults (strong recommendation). WHO recommends a reduction to <2 g/day sodium (5 g/day salt)\nin adults (strong recommendation).\n\nSee reference here:\nGuideline: Sodium intake for adults and children (2012): https://iris.who.int/server/api/core/bitstreams/d0f9feb5-ed78-44d1-9e06-533a93352012/content","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 2, 6.5"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:newborns weighing 2000 g or less at birth should be provided with kangaroo mother care as possible.:::close to continuous', 'newborns weighing 2000 g or less at birth should be provided with kangaroo mother care as possible.', 'Close to continuous', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The WHO publication Kangaroo Mother Care: A Practical Guide recommends initiation of short KMC sessions when the baby starts to recover, even if the baby still requires medical treatment such as intravenous fluids or oxygen. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Close to continuous\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The WHO publication Kangaroo Mother Care: A Practical Guide recommends initiation of short KMC sessions when the baby starts to recover, even if the baby still requires medical treatment such as intravenous fluids or oxygen. \n\nIt also recommends that continuous KMC should be initiated only after the baby is stable, meaning that the baby must be breathing spontaneously without additional oxygen. For the routine care of newborns weighing 2.0 kg or less at birth, the “WHO recommendations on interventions to improve preterm birth outcomes” recommend KMC, which should be initiated in health-care facilities as soon as the newborns are clinically stable. \n\nThese babies should be provided with as close to continuous KMC as possible. Currently, there is no recommendation for KMC for unstable neonates weighing less than 2.0 kg at birth.\n\nSee references here:\n\nKangaroo mother care: Implementation strategy for scale-up adaptable to different country contexts (WHO, 2023): https://iris.who.int/server/api/core/bitstreams/71660bad-f029-4d9e-a2a4-63d8c59a8ce1/content\n\nWHO Immediate KMC Study Group (2020). Impact of continuous Kangaroo Mother Care initiated immediately after birth (iKMC) on survival of newborns with birth weight between 1.0 to < 1.8 kg: study protocol for a randomized controlled trial. Trials, 21(1), 280. https://doi.org/10.1186/s13063-020-4101-1","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Continuous, Close, Continuous to close"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:newborns weighing 2000 g or less at birth should be provided with kangaroo mother care as possible.:::', 'newborns weighing 2000 g or less at birth should be provided with kangaroo mother care as possible.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The WHO publication Kangaroo Mother Care: A Practical Guide recommends initiation of short KMC sessions when the baby starts to recover, even if the baby still requires medical treatment such as intravenous fluids or oxygen. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Close to continuous\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The WHO publication Kangaroo Mother Care: A Practical Guide recommends initiation of short KMC sessions when the baby starts to recover, even if the baby still requires medical treatment such as intravenous fluids or oxygen. \n\nIt also recommends that continuous KMC should be initiated only after the baby is stable, meaning that the baby must be breathing spontaneously without additional oxygen. For the routine care of newborns weighing 2.0 kg or less at birth, the “WHO recommendations on interventions to improve preterm birth outcomes” recommend KMC, which should be initiated in health-care facilities as soon as the newborns are clinically stable. \n\nThese babies should be provided with as close to continuous KMC as possible. Currently, there is no recommendation for KMC for unstable neonates weighing less than 2.0 kg at birth.\n\nSee references here:\n\nKangaroo mother care: Implementation strategy for scale-up adaptable to different country contexts (WHO, 2023): https://iris.who.int/server/api/core/bitstreams/71660bad-f029-4d9e-a2a4-63d8c59a8ce1/content\n\nWHO Immediate KMC Study Group (2020). Impact of continuous Kangaroo Mother Care initiated immediately after birth (iKMC) on survival of newborns with birth weight between 1.0 to < 1.8 kg: study protocol for a randomized controlled trial. Trials, 21(1), 280. https://doi.org/10.1186/s13063-020-4101-1","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Continuous, Close, Continuous to close"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what is stated in the 5th nutritional guidelines for filipinos:::consume milk, milk product, and other calcium-rich foods, such as small fish and shellfish, every day for healthy bones and teeth', 'what is stated in the 5th nutritional guidelines for filipinos', 'Consume milk, milk product, and other calcium-rich foods, such as small fish and shellfish, every day for healthy bones and teeth', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Formulated by the Food and Nutrition Research Institute (FNRI-DOST), the 10 messages (popularly popularized as the 10 Kumainments) are chronologically ordered as follows:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Consume milk, milk product, and other calcium-rich foods, such as small fish and shellfish, every day for healthy bones and teeth\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Formulated by the Food and Nutrition Research Institute (FNRI-DOST), the 10 messages (popularly popularized as the 10 Kumainments) are chronologically ordered as follows:\n\n1. 1st Guideline: Eat a variety of foods everyday to get the nutrients needed by the body.\n2. 2nd Guideline: Breastfeed infants exclusively from birth up to 6 months, then give appropriate complementary foods.\n3. 3rd Guideline: Eat more vegetables and fruits every day.\n4. 4th Guideline: Consume fish, lean meat, poultry, egg, dried beans or nuts daily.\n5. 5th Guideline: Consume milk, milk products, and other calcium-rich foods.\n6. 6th Guideline: Consume safe foods and water to prevent diarrhea.\n7. 7th Guideline: Use iodized salt to prevent Iodine Deficiency Disorders.\n8. 8th Guideline: Limit intake of salty, fried, fatty, and sugar-rich foods.\n9. 9th Guideline: Attain normal body weight through proper diet and moderate physical activity.\n10. 10th Guideline: Be physically active, make healthy food choices, manage stress, avoid alcoholic beverages, and do not smoke.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Use iodized salt to prevent Iodine Deficiency Disorders, Eat more vegetables and fruits every day to get essential vitamins, minerals, and fiber for regulation of body processes., Limit intake of salty, fried, fatty, and sugar-rich foods to prevent cardiovascular disease"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what is stated in the 5th nutritional guidelines for filipinos:::', 'what is stated in the 5th nutritional guidelines for filipinos', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Formulated by the Food and Nutrition Research Institute (FNRI-DOST), the 10 messages (popularly popularized as the 10 Kumainments) are chronologically ordered as follows:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Consume milk, milk product, and other calcium-rich foods, such as small fish and shellfish, every day for healthy bones and teeth\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Formulated by the Food and Nutrition Research Institute (FNRI-DOST), the 10 messages (popularly popularized as the 10 Kumainments) are chronologically ordered as follows:\n\n1. 1st Guideline: Eat a variety of foods everyday to get the nutrients needed by the body.\n2. 2nd Guideline: Breastfeed infants exclusively from birth up to 6 months, then give appropriate complementary foods.\n3. 3rd Guideline: Eat more vegetables and fruits every day.\n4. 4th Guideline: Consume fish, lean meat, poultry, egg, dried beans or nuts daily.\n5. 5th Guideline: Consume milk, milk products, and other calcium-rich foods.\n6. 6th Guideline: Consume safe foods and water to prevent diarrhea.\n7. 7th Guideline: Use iodized salt to prevent Iodine Deficiency Disorders.\n8. 8th Guideline: Limit intake of salty, fried, fatty, and sugar-rich foods.\n9. 9th Guideline: Attain normal body weight through proper diet and moderate physical activity.\n10. 10th Guideline: Be physically active, make healthy food choices, manage stress, avoid alcoholic beverages, and do not smoke.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Use iodized salt to prevent Iodine Deficiency Disorders, Eat more vegetables and fruits every day to get essential vitamins, minerals, and fiber for regulation of body processes., Limit intake of salty, fried, fatty, and sugar-rich foods to prevent cardiovascular disease"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the current nutritional guildelines for filipinos was approved and adopted in what year?:::2012', 'the current nutritional guildelines for filipinos was approved and adopted in what year?', '2012', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Official name: Nutritional guidelines for Filipinos (Filipino: Mga Gabay sa Wastong Nutrisyon Para sa Pilipino).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"2012\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Official name: Nutritional guidelines for Filipinos (Filipino: Mga Gabay sa Wastong Nutrisyon Para sa Pilipino).\n\nPublication year: The Philippines published its first dietary guidelines in 1990. They were revised in 2000 and again in 2012.\n\nProcess and stakeholders\nThe Food and Nutrition Research Institute – Department of Science and Technology (FNRI-DOST) initiated and chaired the inter-agency and multidisciplinary Technical Working Group on the 2012 guidelines.\n\nThe guidelines were approved by the National Nutrition Council. The communication and dissemination is spearheaded by the National Nutrition Council and endorsed by various government agencies.\n\nSee reference here: https://www.fao.org/nutrition/education/food-dietary-guidelines/regions/countries/philippines/en/","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 2000, 2006, 2018"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the current nutritional guildelines for filipinos was approved and adopted in what year?:::', 'the current nutritional guildelines for filipinos was approved and adopted in what year?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Official name: Nutritional guidelines for Filipinos (Filipino: Mga Gabay sa Wastong Nutrisyon Para sa Pilipino).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"2012\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Official name: Nutritional guidelines for Filipinos (Filipino: Mga Gabay sa Wastong Nutrisyon Para sa Pilipino).\n\nPublication year: The Philippines published its first dietary guidelines in 1990. They were revised in 2000 and again in 2012.\n\nProcess and stakeholders\nThe Food and Nutrition Research Institute – Department of Science and Technology (FNRI-DOST) initiated and chaired the inter-agency and multidisciplinary Technical Working Group on the 2012 guidelines.\n\nThe guidelines were approved by the National Nutrition Council. The communication and dissemination is spearheaded by the National Nutrition Council and endorsed by various government agencies.\n\nSee reference here: https://www.fao.org/nutrition/education/food-dietary-guidelines/regions/countries/philippines/en/","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 2000, 2006, 2018"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:under the mandatory food fortification program, rice is fortified with:::iron', 'under the mandatory food fortification program, rice is fortified with', 'Iron', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"See RA 8976 (Philippine Food Fortification Act of 2000):","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Iron\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"See RA 8976 (Philippine Food Fortification Act of 2000):\n\nA. Vitamin A - oil\nB. Iron – rice\nC. Iodine - salt\nD. Vitamin A and iron - wheat flour","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vit A, Iodine, Vit A and Iron"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which age group is the target in operation timbang plus:::0-59 months', 'which age group is the target in operation timbang plus', '0-59 months', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Operation Timbang (OPT) Plus is the regular child growth assessment of all children 0-59 months old conducted annually in all barangays in the country to identify and locate families with malnourished preschool children and determine the overall nutrition situation in the area. It also involves planning, follow-up, and data quality check.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"0-59 months\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Operation Timbang (OPT) Plus is the regular child growth assessment of all children 0-59 months old conducted annually in all barangays in the country to identify and locate families with malnourished preschool children and determine the overall nutrition situation in the area. It also involves planning, follow-up, and data quality check.\n\nSee reference here: https://nnc.gov.ph/plans-and-programs/philippine-nutrition-surveillance-system-pnss/operation-timbang-plus-otp/","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 0-11 months, 0-23 months, 0-35 months"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which age group is the target in operation timbang plus:::', 'which age group is the target in operation timbang plus', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Operation Timbang (OPT) Plus is the regular child growth assessment of all children 0-59 months old conducted annually in all barangays in the country to identify and locate families with malnourished preschool children and determine the overall nutrition situation in the area. It also involves planning, follow-up, and data quality check.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"0-59 months\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Operation Timbang (OPT) Plus is the regular child growth assessment of all children 0-59 months old conducted annually in all barangays in the country to identify and locate families with malnourished preschool children and determine the overall nutrition situation in the area. It also involves planning, follow-up, and data quality check.\n\nSee reference here: https://nnc.gov.ph/plans-and-programs/philippine-nutrition-surveillance-system-pnss/operation-timbang-plus-otp/","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 0-11 months, 0-23 months, 0-35 months"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:who is not a member of the operation timbang plus team?:::mayor', 'who is not a member of the operation timbang plus team?', 'Mayor', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The correct answer is the Mayor, who serves in a broader local governance and oversight role as chair of the local nutrition committee. BHW, BNS, and DCW are all members of the OPT Plus team.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Mayor\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The correct answer is the Mayor, who serves in a broader local governance and oversight role as chair of the local nutrition committee. BHW, BNS, and DCW are all members of the OPT Plus team.\n\nSee reference here: https://nnc.gov.ph/plans-and-programs/philippine-nutrition-surveillance-system-pnss/operation-timbang-plus-otp/","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Barangay health worker, Barangay nutrition scholar, Day care worker"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:who is not a member of the operation timbang plus team?:::', 'who is not a member of the operation timbang plus team?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The correct answer is the Mayor, who serves in a broader local governance and oversight role as chair of the local nutrition committee. BHW, BNS, and DCW are all members of the OPT Plus team.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Mayor\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The correct answer is the Mayor, who serves in a broader local governance and oversight role as chair of the local nutrition committee. BHW, BNS, and DCW are all members of the OPT Plus team.\n\nSee reference here: https://nnc.gov.ph/plans-and-programs/philippine-nutrition-surveillance-system-pnss/operation-timbang-plus-otp/","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Barangay health worker, Barangay nutrition scholar, Day care worker"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:this program involves the provision of pharmaceutical preparations of vitamins and minerals.:::micronutrient supplementation', 'this program involves the provision of pharmaceutical preparations of vitamins and minerals.', 'Micronutrient Supplementation', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Micronutrient supplementation, defined specifically as providing vitamins and minerals in pharmaceutical form (such as capsules, syrups, or tablets) directly to individuals.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Micronutrient Supplementation\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Micronutrient supplementation, defined specifically as providing vitamins and minerals in pharmaceutical form (such as capsules, syrups, or tablets) directly to individuals.\n\nFood fortification instead adds nutrients to commonly consumed foods during processing. Livelihood assistance and food production are indirect, food-security-oriented interventions.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Food Fortification, Livelihood Assistance, Food Production"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:an instrument used to measure skinfold thickness:::', 'an instrument used to measure skinfold thickness', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The Lange caliper is a specific instrument designed and validated for measuring skinfold thickness at standardized body sites to estimate subcutaneous body fat. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Lange\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The Lange caliper is a specific instrument designed and validated for measuring skinfold thickness at standardized body sites to estimate subcutaneous body fat. \n\nBiceps, subscapular, and midaxillary are names of specific skinfold measurement sites on the body.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Biceps, Subscapular, Midaxillary"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:this program involves the provision of pharmaceutical preparations of vitamins and minerals.:::', 'this program involves the provision of pharmaceutical preparations of vitamins and minerals.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Micronutrient supplementation, defined specifically as providing vitamins and minerals in pharmaceutical form (such as capsules, syrups, or tablets) directly to individuals.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Micronutrient Supplementation\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Micronutrient supplementation, defined specifically as providing vitamins and minerals in pharmaceutical form (such as capsules, syrups, or tablets) directly to individuals.\n\nFood fortification instead adds nutrients to commonly consumed foods during processing. Livelihood assistance and food production are indirect, food-security-oriented interventions.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Food Fortification, Livelihood Assistance, Food Production"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:this relates to the process by which the nutritional quality of food crops is improved through agronomic practices, conventional plant breeding, or modern biotechnology.:::biofortification', 'this relates to the process by which the nutritional quality of food crops is improved through agronomic practices, conventional plant breeding, or modern biotechnology.', 'Biofortification', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The correct answer is biofortification, specifically defined by improving the nutrient content of crops themselves through agricultural or breeding methods (such as Golden Rice enriched with vitamin A precursor).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Biofortification\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The correct answer is biofortification, specifically defined by improving the nutrient content of crops themselves through agricultural or breeding methods (such as Golden Rice enriched with vitamin A precursor).\n\nSee reference here: https://www.who.int/tools/elena/bbc/biofortification","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Mandatory fortification, Voluntary fortification, Food fortification"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
