('mc:in planning for a supplementary feeding, the following are key decision points, except::::', 'in planning for a supplementary feeding, the following are key decision points, except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"“What technology of production will be promoted?” refers to a food production intervention.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"What technology of production will be promoted?\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"“What technology of production will be promoted?” refers to a food production intervention.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Type of commodity to be used to include for formulationss like Nutripak, Center-based vs home-based, Mechanism for community mobilization and particpation of parents"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a measure of years in perfect health lost::::daly', 'a measure of years in perfect health lost:', 'DALY', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"DALY - Disability-Adjusted Life Year (Burden of Disease): Measures the total healthy life years lost (YLL + YLD).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"DALY\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"DALY - Disability-Adjusted Life Year (Burden of Disease): Measures the total healthy life years lost (YLL + YLD).\n\n• YLL - Years of Life Lost (Mortality): Measures the number of years lost due to premature death.\n• YLD - Years Lived with Disability (Morbidity): Measures the number of years lived in less than full health due to illness or injury.\n• QALY - Quality-Adjusted Life Year (Health Economics): Measures the value of a year of life adjusted by its quality or utility.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: YLL, YLD, QALY"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:field visits, meeting, record keeping, reporting, plan implementation reviews and special studies are known approaches for::::monitoring and evaluation', 'field visits, meeting, record keeping, reporting, plan implementation reviews and special studies are known approaches for:', 'Monitoring and Evaluation', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The correct answer is monitoring and evaluation combined, since this specific list of activities (field visits, meetings, record keeping, reporting, implementation reviews, special studies) spans both the ongoing, process-tracking functions of monitoring and the more in-depth, periodic assessment functions of evaluation.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Monitoring and Evaluation\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The correct answer is monitoring and evaluation combined, since this specific list of activities (field visits, meetings, record keeping, reporting, implementation reviews, special studies) spans both the ongoing, process-tracking functions of monitoring and the more in-depth, periodic assessment functions of evaluation.\n\n• Monitoring alone or evaluation alone would not fully encompass this comprehensive list of approaches, since some (like routine field visits and record keeping) are more monitoring-oriented while others (like special studies) are more evaluation-oriented. \n• \"Monitoring or evaluation\" incorrectly implies these are separate, alternative approaches and not combined, integrated set of activities.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Monitoring, Evaluation, Monitoring or Evaluation"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:field visits, meeting, record keeping, reporting, plan implementation reviews and special studies are known approaches for::::', 'field visits, meeting, record keeping, reporting, plan implementation reviews and special studies are known approaches for:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The correct answer is monitoring and evaluation combined, since this specific list of activities (field visits, meetings, record keeping, reporting, implementation reviews, special studies) spans both the ongoing, process-tracking functions of monitoring and the more in-depth, periodic assessment functions of evaluation.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Monitoring and Evaluation\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The correct answer is monitoring and evaluation combined, since this specific list of activities (field visits, meetings, record keeping, reporting, implementation reviews, special studies) spans both the ongoing, process-tracking functions of monitoring and the more in-depth, periodic assessment functions of evaluation.\n\n• Monitoring alone or evaluation alone would not fully encompass this comprehensive list of approaches, since some (like routine field visits and record keeping) are more monitoring-oriented while others (like special studies) are more evaluation-oriented. \n• \"Monitoring or evaluation\" incorrectly implies these are separate, alternative approaches and not combined, integrated set of activities.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Monitoring, Evaluation, Monitoring or Evaluation"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:in the monitoring and evaluation of local level plan implementation (mellpi) of the national nutrition council, which one refers to the highest award given to an lgu?:::nutrition honor award', 'in the monitoring and evaluation of local level plan implementation (mellpi) of the national nutrition council, which one refers to the highest award given to an lgu?', 'Nutrition Honor Award', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The National Nutrition Council (NNC) utilizes the MELLPI framework to monitor Local Government Units (LGUs) and recognize them with progressive awards for outstanding performance in implementing the Philippine Plan of Action for Nutrition (PPAN):","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Nutrition Honor Award\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The National Nutrition Council (NNC) utilizes the MELLPI framework to monitor Local Government Units (LGUs) and recognize them with progressive awards for outstanding performance in implementing the Philippine Plan of Action for Nutrition (PPAN):\n\n• Nutrition Honor Award (NHA): This is the highest and most prestigious accolade given to an LGU. It is conferred only to LGUs that have maintained excellent nutrition program implementation for six (6) consecutive years, coming with a standard ₱1,000,000 cash prize and trophy.\n• Consistent Regional Outstanding Winner in Nutrition (CROWN) Award: Given to an LGU that has been a regional winner for three (3) consecutive years. After achieving the CROWN, an LGU must successfully clear 1st and 2nd Year CROWN Maintenance Evaluations before it can qualify for the final Nutrition Honor Award.\n• Green Banner Seal of Compliance: The initial tier of recognition awarded at the regional level to municipalities, cities, or provinces that excel in their annual nutrition performance benchmarks.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Green Banner Award, Consistent Regional Outstanding Winner in Nutrition, National Outstanding Nutrition Award"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a bns is trained volunteer community worker with the following qualifications except::::at least high school graduate', 'a bns is trained volunteer community worker with the following qualifications except:', 'At least high school graduate', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"At least high school graduate - the law states ‘elementary’ or ‘primary school’ (see PD 1569)","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"At least high school graduate\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"At least high school graduate - the law states ‘elementary’ or ‘primary school’ (see PD 1569)","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Bonadife resident of the barangay, Willingness to serve, part-time or full-time, More than 18 years old, but younger than 60 years old"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:in the monitoring and evaluation of local level plan implementation (mellpi) of the national nutrition council, which one refers to the highest award given to an lgu?:::', 'in the monitoring and evaluation of local level plan implementation (mellpi) of the national nutrition council, which one refers to the highest award given to an lgu?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The National Nutrition Council (NNC) utilizes the MELLPI framework to monitor Local Government Units (LGUs) and recognize them with progressive awards for outstanding performance in implementing the Philippine Plan of Action for Nutrition (PPAN):","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Nutrition Honor Award\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The National Nutrition Council (NNC) utilizes the MELLPI framework to monitor Local Government Units (LGUs) and recognize them with progressive awards for outstanding performance in implementing the Philippine Plan of Action for Nutrition (PPAN):\n\n• Nutrition Honor Award (NHA): This is the highest and most prestigious accolade given to an LGU. It is conferred only to LGUs that have maintained excellent nutrition program implementation for six (6) consecutive years, coming with a standard ₱1,000,000 cash prize and trophy.\n• Consistent Regional Outstanding Winner in Nutrition (CROWN) Award: Given to an LGU that has been a regional winner for three (3) consecutive years. After achieving the CROWN, an LGU must successfully clear 1st and 2nd Year CROWN Maintenance Evaluations before it can qualify for the final Nutrition Honor Award.\n• Green Banner Seal of Compliance: The initial tier of recognition awarded at the regional level to municipalities, cities, or provinces that excel in their annual nutrition performance benchmarks.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Green Banner Award, Consistent Regional Outstanding Winner in Nutrition, National Outstanding Nutrition Award"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:in 2014, the national nutrition council governing board approved and adopted the guidelines on local nutrition planning that direct lgus to come up with a local nutrition action plan.:::3 year', 'in 2014, the national nutrition council governing board approved and adopted the guidelines on local nutrition planning that direct lgus to come up with a local nutrition action plan.', '3 year', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The 2014 NNC guidelines for LGU-level Local Nutrition Action Plans, aligning with the local government''s typical elected term of office. Aligning the LNAP''s three-year cycle with local elected officials'' terms helps ensure political ownership and continuity of nutrition planning across the term of local leadership.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"3 year\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The 2014 NNC guidelines for LGU-level Local Nutrition Action Plans, aligning with the local government''s typical elected term of office. Aligning the LNAP''s three-year cycle with local elected officials'' terms helps ensure political ownership and continuity of nutrition planning across the term of local leadership.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 1 year, 2 year, 4 year"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:in 2014, the national nutrition council governing board approved and adopted the guidelines on local nutrition planning that direct lgus to come up with a local nutrition action plan.:::', 'in 2014, the national nutrition council governing board approved and adopted the guidelines on local nutrition planning that direct lgus to come up with a local nutrition action plan.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The 2014 NNC guidelines for LGU-level Local Nutrition Action Plans, aligning with the local government''s typical elected term of office. Aligning the LNAP''s three-year cycle with local elected officials'' terms helps ensure political ownership and continuity of nutrition planning across the term of local leadership.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"3 year\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The 2014 NNC guidelines for LGU-level Local Nutrition Action Plans, aligning with the local government''s typical elected term of office. Aligning the LNAP''s three-year cycle with local elected officials'' terms helps ensure political ownership and continuity of nutrition planning across the term of local leadership.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 1 year, 2 year, 4 year"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the lnap is best prepared on the first semester of an election year to ensure that the nutrition agenda is integrated in the executive legislative agenda which becomes the bases for local development plan and in-turn the annual investment plan. this is essential for::::local budgeting', 'the lnap is best prepared on the first semester of an election year to ensure that the nutrition agenda is integrated in the executive legislative agenda which becomes the bases for local development plan and in-turn the annual investment plan. this is essential for:', 'Local Budgeting', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Local budgeting because timely integration of the nutrition agenda into the Executive Legislative Agenda ensures that nutrition programs are properly reflected and funded in the subsequent Annual Investment Plan and local budget allocations. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Local Budgeting\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Local budgeting because timely integration of the nutrition agenda into the Executive Legislative Agenda ensures that nutrition programs are properly reflected and funded in the subsequent Annual Investment Plan and local budget allocations. \n\nWhile local planning, implementation, and monitoring/evaluation are all eventually affected by this timing, the question mentioning ELA and AIP points most directly to securing adequate budgeting for nutrition programs.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Local Planning, Local Implementation, Local Monitoring and evaluation"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
