('mc:the method used to analyze protein content of foods in the fct::::kjeldahl', 'the method used to analyze protein content of foods in the fct:', 'Kjeldahl', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Kjeldahl method is the standard, classic laboratory technique for determining protein content by measuring total nitrogen and applying a conversion factor, widely used in food composition analysis.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Kjeldahl\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Kjeldahl method is the standard, classic laboratory technique for determining protein content by measuring total nitrogen and applying a conversion factor, widely used in food composition analysis.\n\n• Soxhlet - fat/lipid extraction and analysis\n• Roes-Gottlieb - specific method for fat analysis in dairy products\n• Carr-Price - colorimetric method historically used for vitamin A analysis","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Soxhlet, Rose-Gottlieb, Carr-Price"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the method used to analyze protein content of foods in the fct::::', 'the method used to analyze protein content of foods in the fct:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Kjeldahl method is the standard, classic laboratory technique for determining protein content by measuring total nitrogen and applying a conversion factor, widely used in food composition analysis.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Kjeldahl\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Kjeldahl method is the standard, classic laboratory technique for determining protein content by measuring total nitrogen and applying a conversion factor, widely used in food composition analysis.\n\n• Soxhlet - fat/lipid extraction and analysis\n• Roes-Gottlieb - specific method for fat analysis in dairy products\n• Carr-Price - colorimetric method historically used for vitamin A analysis","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Soxhlet, Rose-Gottlieb, Carr-Price"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:sonia has a fasting blood sugar level of 118 mg/dl. according to the assessment criteria of who, this value can be classified as:::impaired', 'sonia has a fasting blood sugar level of 118 mg/dl. according to the assessment criteria of who, this value can be classified as', 'impaired', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to the World Health Organization (WHO) and standard medical assessment guidelines, intermediate states of glucose regulation are formally categorized using the term \"impaired\".","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"impaired\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to the World Health Organization (WHO) and standard medical assessment guidelines, intermediate states of glucose regulation are formally categorized using the term \"impaired\".\n• Impaired Fasting Glucose (IFG): Defined as a fasting blood sugar level between 100 mg/dL and 125 mg/dL (or 6.1 mmol/L to 6.9 mmol/L based on specific WHO thresholds). Since Sonia''s value is 118 mg/dl, it falls precisely within this category.\n• Impaired Glucose Tolerance (IGT): Refers to blood sugar levels that are elevated during a 2-hour postprandial or oral glucose tolerance test (140–199 mg/dL).\nIn a clinical or exam context, these clinical categories represent \"prediabetes,\" flagging that the individual''s glucose metabolism is altered but has not yet met the diagnostic cutoff for a diabetes mellitus classification (≥126 mg/dL).\n\nNormal Levels\n\t• Fasting Blood Sugar: Less than 100 mg/dL (< 5.6 mmol/L)\n\t• 2-Hour Oral Glucose Tolerance Test: Less than 140 mg/dL (< 7.8 mmol/L)\n\t• HbA1c: Less than 5.7% (< 39 mmol/mol)\n\n Impaired Levels (Prediabetes)\n\t• Fasting Blood Sugar: 100 to 125 mg/dL (5.6 to 6.9 mmol/L) — called Impaired Fasting Glucose (IFG)\n\t• 2-Hour Oral Glucose Tolerance Test: 140 to 199 mg/dL (7.8 to 11.0 mmol/L) — called Impaired Glucose Tolerance (IGT)\n\t• HbA1c: 5.7% to 6.4% (39 to 46 mmol/mol)\n\n Diabetes Mellitus\n\t• Fasting Blood Sugar: 126 mg/dL or higher (≥ 7.0 mmol/L)\n\t• 2-Hour Oral Glucose Tolerance Test: 200 mg/dL or higher (≥ 11.1 mmol/L)\n\t• HbA1c: 6.5% or higher (≥ 48 mmol/mol)\n\t• Random Blood Sugar: 200 mg/dL or higher when accompanied by severe symptoms like extreme thirst or frequent urination","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: desirable, borderline, high"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:sonia has a fasting blood sugar level of 118 mg/dl. according to the assessment criteria of who, this value can be classified as:::', 'sonia has a fasting blood sugar level of 118 mg/dl. according to the assessment criteria of who, this value can be classified as', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to the World Health Organization (WHO) and standard medical assessment guidelines, intermediate states of glucose regulation are formally categorized using the term \"impaired\".","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"impaired\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to the World Health Organization (WHO) and standard medical assessment guidelines, intermediate states of glucose regulation are formally categorized using the term \"impaired\".\n• Impaired Fasting Glucose (IFG): Defined as a fasting blood sugar level between 100 mg/dL and 125 mg/dL (or 6.1 mmol/L to 6.9 mmol/L based on specific WHO thresholds). Since Sonia''s value is 118 mg/dl, it falls precisely within this category.\n• Impaired Glucose Tolerance (IGT): Refers to blood sugar levels that are elevated during a 2-hour postprandial or oral glucose tolerance test (140–199 mg/dL).\nIn a clinical or exam context, these clinical categories represent \"prediabetes,\" flagging that the individual''s glucose metabolism is altered but has not yet met the diagnostic cutoff for a diabetes mellitus classification (≥126 mg/dL).\n\nNormal Levels\n\t• Fasting Blood Sugar: Less than 100 mg/dL (< 5.6 mmol/L)\n\t• 2-Hour Oral Glucose Tolerance Test: Less than 140 mg/dL (< 7.8 mmol/L)\n\t• HbA1c: Less than 5.7% (< 39 mmol/mol)\n\n Impaired Levels (Prediabetes)\n\t• Fasting Blood Sugar: 100 to 125 mg/dL (5.6 to 6.9 mmol/L) — called Impaired Fasting Glucose (IFG)\n\t• 2-Hour Oral Glucose Tolerance Test: 140 to 199 mg/dL (7.8 to 11.0 mmol/L) — called Impaired Glucose Tolerance (IGT)\n\t• HbA1c: 5.7% to 6.4% (39 to 46 mmol/mol)\n\n Diabetes Mellitus\n\t• Fasting Blood Sugar: 126 mg/dL or higher (≥ 7.0 mmol/L)\n\t• 2-Hour Oral Glucose Tolerance Test: 200 mg/dL or higher (≥ 11.1 mmol/L)\n\t• HbA1c: 6.5% or higher (≥ 48 mmol/mol)\n\t• Random Blood Sugar: 200 mg/dL or higher when accompanied by severe symptoms like extreme thirst or frequent urination","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: desirable, borderline, high"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:diagnostic test to determine the sufficiency of post nutriture to permit cells, tissues, organ systems or the host to optimally perform nutrient dependent biologic function.:::functional test', 'diagnostic test to determine the sufficiency of post nutriture to permit cells, tissues, organ systems or the host to optimally perform nutrient dependent biologic function.', 'Functional test', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Functional test is the measure of actual physiological/biological functions.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Functional test\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Functional test is the measure of actual physiological/biological functions.\n\n• Biochemical test - laboratory test\n• Clinical test - physical test\n• Static test - single-point-in-time biochemical measurement","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Biochemical test, Clinical test, Static test"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:diagnostic test to determine the sufficiency of post nutriture to permit cells, tissues, organ systems or the host to optimally perform nutrient dependent biologic function.:::', 'diagnostic test to determine the sufficiency of post nutriture to permit cells, tissues, organ systems or the host to optimally perform nutrient dependent biologic function.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Functional test is the measure of actual physiological/biological functions.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Functional test\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Functional test is the measure of actual physiological/biological functions.\n\n• Biochemical test - laboratory test\n• Clinical test - physical test\n• Static test - single-point-in-time biochemical measurement","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Biochemical test, Clinical test, Static test"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:an essential trace element for both humans and animals.:::molybdenum', 'an essential trace element for both humans and animals.', 'Molybdenum', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Molybdenum is a recognized essential trace element required by both humans and animals for enzymatic cofactor functions (such as in xanthine oxidase). ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Molybdenum\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Molybdenum is a recognized essential trace element required by both humans and animals for enzymatic cofactor functions (such as in xanthine oxidase). \n\nBromine, lithium, and vanadium are elements that have been studied for potential biological roles but are not established as essential trace elements with confirmed dietary requirement status in the same way molybdenum is officially recognized.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Bromine, Lithium, Vanadium"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
