('mc:diets that are consistency-modified, soft or bland are prescribed to treat disorders or conditions that primarily affect the::::', 'diets that are consistency-modified, soft or bland are prescribed to treat disorders or conditions that primarily affect the:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Diets that are consistency-modified (such as pureed or mechanical soft) or bland are carefully formulated to treat conditions primarily affecting the upper gastrointestinal tract (including the mouth, esophagus, and stomach).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Upper GIT\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Diets that are consistency-modified (such as pureed or mechanical soft) or bland are carefully formulated to treat conditions primarily affecting the upper gastrointestinal tract (including the mouth, esophagus, and stomach).\n\nThese structural alterations are designed to assist patients who struggle with chewing, swallowing, or chemical digestion due to upper GI pathologies like dysphagia (difficulty swallowing), esophageal strictures, gastritis, or peptic ulcer disease.\n\n• Why Lower GIT is Secondary: While low-fiber or low-residue diets are used for lower GI disorders (like Crohn''s flare-ups) to reduce stool volume, texture and seasoning modifications (soft/bland) are explicitly tailored to minimize mechanical and chemical irritation in the upper digestive path.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Musculoskeletal system, Urinary tract, Lower GIT"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:lily is experiencing a sudden bout of diarrhea. which of the following should be temporarily avoided by lily?:::whole wheat breads and cereals', 'lily is experiencing a sudden bout of diarrhea. which of the following should be temporarily avoided by lily?', 'whole wheat breads and cereals', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Whole wheat breads and cereals contain large amounts of insoluble fiber. When a person has diarrhea, insoluble fiber acts as a gastrointestinal irritant and can speed up bowel movements, making the diarrhea worse. During a sudden bout of diarrhea, it is best to stick to low-fiber, refined white grains.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"whole wheat breads and cereals\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Whole wheat breads and cereals contain large amounts of insoluble fiber. When a person has diarrhea, insoluble fiber acts as a gastrointestinal irritant and can speed up bowel movements, making the diarrhea worse. During a sudden bout of diarrhea, it is best to stick to low-fiber, refined white grains.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: adding salt to foods, noodles, banana"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:lily is experiencing a sudden bout of diarrhea. which of the following should be temporarily avoided by lily?:::', 'lily is experiencing a sudden bout of diarrhea. which of the following should be temporarily avoided by lily?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Whole wheat breads and cereals contain large amounts of insoluble fiber. When a person has diarrhea, insoluble fiber acts as a gastrointestinal irritant and can speed up bowel movements, making the diarrhea worse. During a sudden bout of diarrhea, it is best to stick to low-fiber, refined white grains.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"whole wheat breads and cereals\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Whole wheat breads and cereals contain large amounts of insoluble fiber. When a person has diarrhea, insoluble fiber acts as a gastrointestinal irritant and can speed up bowel movements, making the diarrhea worse. During a sudden bout of diarrhea, it is best to stick to low-fiber, refined white grains.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: adding salt to foods, noodles, banana"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:people on home enteral nutrition program commonly have::::neurological disorders that interfere with swallowing', 'people on home enteral nutrition program commonly have:', 'neurological disorders that interfere with swallowing', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Home Enteral Nutrition (HEN) provides liquid nutrition via a tube directly into a functioning gastrointestinal tract for patients who cannot physically eat or drink enough by mouth. According to global medical registries and guidelines, the most common clinical indication for long-term HEN is dysphagia (difficulty swallowing) caused by neurological disorders (such as strokes, amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS), Parkinson''s disease, or advanced dementia). In these patients, the stomach and intestines function perfectly, but their neurological condition makes oral eating unsafe due to severe choking and aspiration risks.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"neurological disorders that interfere with swallowing\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Home Enteral Nutrition (HEN) provides liquid nutrition via a tube directly into a functioning gastrointestinal tract for patients who cannot physically eat or drink enough by mouth. According to global medical registries and guidelines, the most common clinical indication for long-term HEN is dysphagia (difficulty swallowing) caused by neurological disorders (such as strokes, amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS), Parkinson''s disease, or advanced dementia). In these patients, the stomach and intestines function perfectly, but their neurological condition makes oral eating unsafe due to severe choking and aspiration risks.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Crohn’s disease, Short bowel syndrome, Diverticulitis"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:people on home enteral nutrition program commonly have::::', 'people on home enteral nutrition program commonly have:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Home Enteral Nutrition (HEN) provides liquid nutrition via a tube directly into a functioning gastrointestinal tract for patients who cannot physically eat or drink enough by mouth. According to global medical registries and guidelines, the most common clinical indication for long-term HEN is dysphagia (difficulty swallowing) caused by neurological disorders (such as strokes, amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS), Parkinson''s disease, or advanced dementia). In these patients, the stomach and intestines function perfectly, but their neurological condition makes oral eating unsafe due to severe choking and aspiration risks.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"neurological disorders that interfere with swallowing\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Home Enteral Nutrition (HEN) provides liquid nutrition via a tube directly into a functioning gastrointestinal tract for patients who cannot physically eat or drink enough by mouth. According to global medical registries and guidelines, the most common clinical indication for long-term HEN is dysphagia (difficulty swallowing) caused by neurological disorders (such as strokes, amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS), Parkinson''s disease, or advanced dementia). In these patients, the stomach and intestines function perfectly, but their neurological condition makes oral eating unsafe due to severe choking and aspiration risks.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Crohn’s disease, Short bowel syndrome, Diverticulitis"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:enteral nutrition includes:::oral and tube only', 'enteral nutrition includes', 'Oral and Tube Only', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Enteral nutrition includes both oral diets and tube feedings, as both deliver nutrients through the gastrointestinal tract, whereas intravenous nutrition is classified as parenteral.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Oral and Tube Only\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Enteral nutrition includes both oral diets and tube feedings, as both deliver nutrients through the gastrointestinal tract, whereas intravenous nutrition is classified as parenteral.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Intravenous nutrition, Oral diets, Tube feedings"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:enteral nutrition includes:::', 'enteral nutrition includes', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Enteral nutrition includes both oral diets and tube feedings, as both deliver nutrients through the gastrointestinal tract, whereas intravenous nutrition is classified as parenteral.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Oral and Tube Only\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Enteral nutrition includes both oral diets and tube feedings, as both deliver nutrients through the gastrointestinal tract, whereas intravenous nutrition is classified as parenteral.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Intravenous nutrition, Oral diets, Tube feedings"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a formula that approximates the osmolality of normal blood serum:::b and c', 'a formula that approximates the osmolality of normal blood serum', 'B and C', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Any enteral formula, IV fluid, or parenteral solution that closely mimics or approximates this normal biological range is classified as an isotonic solution. Because it exerts the same osmotic pressure as plasma, it prevents shifting fluid across cell membranes, minimizing gastrointestinal side effects like cramping or diarrhea.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"B and C\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Any enteral formula, IV fluid, or parenteral solution that closely mimics or approximates this normal biological range is classified as an isotonic solution. Because it exerts the same osmotic pressure as plasma, it prevents shifting fluid across cell membranes, minimizing gastrointestinal side effects like cramping or diarrhea.\n\nThe normal osmolality of human blood serum ranges between 275 and 295 mOsm/kg (clinically rounded to 300 mOsm/L for standard nutrition formulations).\n\n× A hypertonic formula is any enteral nutrition (tube feeding) or intravenous (IV) solution that has a higher concentration of solutes than normal human blood serum.\nWhile normal blood serum sits at approximately 275 to 295 mOsm/kg (clinically rounded to 300 mOsm/L), a hypertonic formula typically carries an osmolality greater than 300 to 400 mOsm/L.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Is called hypertonic formula, has an osmolality of approximately 300 millisosmoles per liter, is called an isotonic formula"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a formula that approximates the osmolality of normal blood serum:::', 'a formula that approximates the osmolality of normal blood serum', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Any enteral formula, IV fluid, or parenteral solution that closely mimics or approximates this normal biological range is classified as an isotonic solution. Because it exerts the same osmotic pressure as plasma, it prevents shifting fluid across cell membranes, minimizing gastrointestinal side effects like cramping or diarrhea.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"B and C\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Any enteral formula, IV fluid, or parenteral solution that closely mimics or approximates this normal biological range is classified as an isotonic solution. Because it exerts the same osmotic pressure as plasma, it prevents shifting fluid across cell membranes, minimizing gastrointestinal side effects like cramping or diarrhea.\n\nThe normal osmolality of human blood serum ranges between 275 and 295 mOsm/kg (clinically rounded to 300 mOsm/L for standard nutrition formulations).\n\n× A hypertonic formula is any enteral nutrition (tube feeding) or intravenous (IV) solution that has a higher concentration of solutes than normal human blood serum.\nWhile normal blood serum sits at approximately 275 to 295 mOsm/kg (clinically rounded to 300 mOsm/L), a hypertonic formula typically carries an osmolality greater than 300 to 400 mOsm/L.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Is called hypertonic formula, has an osmolality of approximately 300 millisosmoles per liter, is called an isotonic formula"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following feeding tube placement sites would be the most appropriate for a client who is expected to be on a tube feeding for less than 4 weeks?:::', 'which of the following feeding tube placement sites would be the most appropriate for a client who is expected to be on a tube feeding for less than 4 weeks?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A nasogastric (NG) tube is explicitly indicated for short-term enteral nutrition expected to last less than 4 weeks. Because it is non-invasive, cost-effective, and can be placed quickly at the bedside without surgical, endoscopic, or radiologic intervention, it is the primary choice for temporary feeding needs.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"nasogastric\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A nasogastric (NG) tube is explicitly indicated for short-term enteral nutrition expected to last less than 4 weeks. Because it is non-invasive, cost-effective, and can be placed quickly at the bedside without surgical, endoscopic, or radiologic intervention, it is the primary choice for temporary feeding needs.\n\n\t• Gastrostomy, Jejunostomy, and a tube enterostomy are invasive, surgically, radiologically, or endoscopically placed access routes. These options are reserved for long-term enteral nutrition support lasting longer than 4 to 6 weeks.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Gastrostomy, jejunostomy, tube enterostomy"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:hydrolyzed fromulas:::should be used only for clients who can’t adequately digest and absorb nutrients', 'hydrolyzed fromulas', 'should be used only for clients who can’t adequately digest and absorb nutrients', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Hydrolyzed formulas (also known as elemental or semi-elemental formulas) contain proteins that have been pre-digested or broken down into tiny peptide chains or individual amino acids. Because they are highly broken down, they are reserved strictly for patients with severe gastrointestinal dysfunction—such as short bowel syndrome, severe malabsorption, pancreatic insufficiency, or Crohn''s disease—who cannot process intact nutrients.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"should be used only for clients who can’t adequately digest and absorb nutrients\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Hydrolyzed formulas (also known as elemental or semi-elemental formulas) contain proteins that have been pre-digested or broken down into tiny peptide chains or individual amino acids. Because they are highly broken down, they are reserved strictly for patients with severe gastrointestinal dysfunction—such as short bowel syndrome, severe malabsorption, pancreatic insufficiency, or Crohn''s disease—who cannot process intact nutrients.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Are the best type to offer a client, Cost less than standard formula, are less likely than other formulas to cause complications"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:hydrolyzed fromulas:::', 'hydrolyzed fromulas', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Hydrolyzed formulas (also known as elemental or semi-elemental formulas) contain proteins that have been pre-digested or broken down into tiny peptide chains or individual amino acids. Because they are highly broken down, they are reserved strictly for patients with severe gastrointestinal dysfunction—such as short bowel syndrome, severe malabsorption, pancreatic insufficiency, or Crohn''s disease—who cannot process intact nutrients.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"should be used only for clients who can’t adequately digest and absorb nutrients\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Hydrolyzed formulas (also known as elemental or semi-elemental formulas) contain proteins that have been pre-digested or broken down into tiny peptide chains or individual amino acids. Because they are highly broken down, they are reserved strictly for patients with severe gastrointestinal dysfunction—such as short bowel syndrome, severe malabsorption, pancreatic insufficiency, or Crohn''s disease—who cannot process intact nutrients.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Are the best type to offer a client, Cost less than standard formula, are less likely than other formulas to cause complications"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the cost of materials that go directly into the production of the menu item.:::food cost', 'the cost of materials that go directly into the production of the menu item.', 'food cost', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Food cost is the cost of materials that directly enter into the production of a menu item; overhead costs are indirect operating expenses.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"food cost\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Food cost is the cost of materials that directly enter into the production of a menu item; overhead costs are indirect operating expenses.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: edible portion cost, as purchased cost, overhead cost"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is not eliminated on a gluten restricted diet:::rice', 'which of the following is not eliminated on a gluten restricted diet', 'rice', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Rice does not contain the protein gluten. It is completely safe to eat and is one of the most common carbohydrate substitutes used for patients on a gluten-restricted diet.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"rice\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Rice does not contain the protein gluten. It is completely safe to eat and is one of the most common carbohydrate substitutes used for patients on a gluten-restricted diet.\n\nThe Grains to Eliminate:\n\t• Barley and Wheat are primary sources of gluten and must be strictly eliminated to prevent damage to the small intestine.\n\t• Oats are typically eliminated or restricted in traditional clinical question banks (often recalled using the acronym BROW: Barley, Rye, Oats, Wheat) due to a remarkably high risk of agricultural cross-contamination with wheat during harvesting and processing.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Wheat, barley, Oats"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is not eliminated on a gluten restricted diet:::', 'which of the following is not eliminated on a gluten restricted diet', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Rice does not contain the protein gluten. It is completely safe to eat and is one of the most common carbohydrate substitutes used for patients on a gluten-restricted diet.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"rice\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Rice does not contain the protein gluten. It is completely safe to eat and is one of the most common carbohydrate substitutes used for patients on a gluten-restricted diet.\n\nThe Grains to Eliminate:\n\t• Barley and Wheat are primary sources of gluten and must be strictly eliminated to prevent damage to the small intestine.\n\t• Oats are typically eliminated or restricted in traditional clinical question banks (often recalled using the acronym BROW: Barley, Rye, Oats, Wheat) due to a remarkably high risk of agricultural cross-contamination with wheat during harvesting and processing.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Wheat, barley, Oats"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following foods would you recommend to someone interested in relieving constipation.:::legumes', 'which of the following foods would you recommend to someone interested in relieving constipation.', 'Legumes', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Legumes (which include beans, lentils, chickpeas, and peas) are packed with exceptionally high amounts of both soluble and insoluble dietary fiber.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Legumes\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Legumes (which include beans, lentils, chickpeas, and peas) are packed with exceptionally high amounts of both soluble and insoluble dietary fiber.\n\t• Insoluble fiber adds essential roughage and physical bulk to the stool, stimulating the intestinal walls to contract and push waste forward.\n\t• Soluble fiber absorbs water inside the colon, turning into a gel-like texture that softens hard, dry stools and makes them much easier to pass.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Milk, Chicken, White bread"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following foods would you recommend to someone interested in relieving constipation.:::', 'which of the following foods would you recommend to someone interested in relieving constipation.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Legumes (which include beans, lentils, chickpeas, and peas) are packed with exceptionally high amounts of both soluble and insoluble dietary fiber.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Legumes\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Legumes (which include beans, lentils, chickpeas, and peas) are packed with exceptionally high amounts of both soluble and insoluble dietary fiber.\n\t• Insoluble fiber adds essential roughage and physical bulk to the stool, stimulating the intestinal walls to contract and push waste forward.\n\t• Soluble fiber absorbs water inside the colon, turning into a gel-like texture that softens hard, dry stools and makes them much easier to pass.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Milk, Chicken, White bread"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the damage sugar does to teeth relates to::::all of the above', 'the damage sugar does to teeth relates to:', 'all of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dental caries (cavities) develop based on a combination of what you eat, how the food behaves structurally, and how long your teeth are exposed to it:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"all of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dental caries (cavities) develop based on a combination of what you eat, how the food behaves structurally, and how long your teeth are exposed to it:\n\n• The CHO (Carbohydrate) content of the food: Simple carbohydrates (sugars) serve as the primary fuel source for oral bacteria like Streptococcus mutans. These bacteria ferment the carbohydrates, generating an acidic byproduct that drops the pH in your mouth and erodes tooth enamel.\n• How sticky the food is: Sticky foods (such as dried fruits, caramels, or crackers) lodge tightly into the pits, fissures, and grooves of the teeth. This physical stickiness prevents saliva from washing the food away, creating a prolonged buffet for cavity-causing bacteria.\n• The length of time the food stays in the mouth: The total duration of exposure is critical. Sucking on hard candies, sipping sugary drinks slowly over hours, or having sticky food stuck to your teeth extends the \"acid attack\" window. Frequent, long-lasting exposure gives bacteria more time to produce acid, making it far more damaging than eating the same amount of sugar all at once.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: The CHO content of the food, the length of time the food stays in the mouth, how sticky the food is"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the damage sugar does to teeth relates to::::', 'the damage sugar does to teeth relates to:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dental caries (cavities) develop based on a combination of what you eat, how the food behaves structurally, and how long your teeth are exposed to it:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"all of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dental caries (cavities) develop based on a combination of what you eat, how the food behaves structurally, and how long your teeth are exposed to it:\n\n• The CHO (Carbohydrate) content of the food: Simple carbohydrates (sugars) serve as the primary fuel source for oral bacteria like Streptococcus mutans. These bacteria ferment the carbohydrates, generating an acidic byproduct that drops the pH in your mouth and erodes tooth enamel.\n• How sticky the food is: Sticky foods (such as dried fruits, caramels, or crackers) lodge tightly into the pits, fissures, and grooves of the teeth. This physical stickiness prevents saliva from washing the food away, creating a prolonged buffet for cavity-causing bacteria.\n• The length of time the food stays in the mouth: The total duration of exposure is critical. Sucking on hard candies, sipping sugary drinks slowly over hours, or having sticky food stuck to your teeth extends the \"acid attack\" window. Frequent, long-lasting exposure gives bacteria more time to produce acid, making it far more damaging than eating the same amount of sugar all at once.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: The CHO content of the food, the length of time the food stays in the mouth, how sticky the food is"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the major digestive enzyme secreted by the stomach is:::pepsin', 'the major digestive enzyme secreted by the stomach is', 'Pepsin', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Pepsin is the primary digestive enzyme produced and secreted by the stomach. Specialized cells in the stomach lining called chief cells release it in an inactive form called pepsinogen. The highly acidic hydrochloric acid (HCl) in the stomach then activates it into pepsin, allowing it to begin breaking down dietary proteins into smaller peptide chains.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Pepsin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Pepsin is the primary digestive enzyme produced and secreted by the stomach. Specialized cells in the stomach lining called chief cells release it in an inactive form called pepsinogen. The highly acidic hydrochloric acid (HCl) in the stomach then activates it into pepsin, allowing it to begin breaking down dietary proteins into smaller peptide chains.\n\n\t× Lipase: While the stomach secretes a small amount of gastric lipase, it plays a minor role. The overwhelming majority of lipid-digesting lipase is produced by the pancreas and active in the small intestine.\n\t× Disaccharides: This is a type of sugar (like lactose or maltose), not an enzyme. The enzymes that break them down (disaccharidases) are produced in the brush border of the small intestine.\n\t× Amylase: This starch-digesting enzyme is secreted by the salivary glands and the pancreas. Any salivary amylase swallowed in a food bolus is rapidly inactivated by the strong acid environment of the stomach.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Amylase, Lipase, Disaccharides"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the major digestive enzyme secreted by the stomach is:::', 'the major digestive enzyme secreted by the stomach is', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Pepsin is the primary digestive enzyme produced and secreted by the stomach. Specialized cells in the stomach lining called chief cells release it in an inactive form called pepsinogen. The highly acidic hydrochloric acid (HCl) in the stomach then activates it into pepsin, allowing it to begin breaking down dietary proteins into smaller peptide chains.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Pepsin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Pepsin is the primary digestive enzyme produced and secreted by the stomach. Specialized cells in the stomach lining called chief cells release it in an inactive form called pepsinogen. The highly acidic hydrochloric acid (HCl) in the stomach then activates it into pepsin, allowing it to begin breaking down dietary proteins into smaller peptide chains.\n\n\t× Lipase: While the stomach secretes a small amount of gastric lipase, it plays a minor role. The overwhelming majority of lipid-digesting lipase is produced by the pancreas and active in the small intestine.\n\t× Disaccharides: This is a type of sugar (like lactose or maltose), not an enzyme. The enzymes that break them down (disaccharidases) are produced in the brush border of the small intestine.\n\t× Amylase: This starch-digesting enzyme is secreted by the salivary glands and the pancreas. Any salivary amylase swallowed in a food bolus is rapidly inactivated by the strong acid environment of the stomach.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Amylase, Lipase, Disaccharides"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the foods which would be digested most quickly are:::sugar cookies', 'the foods which would be digested most quickly are', 'Sugar cookies', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sugar cookies are predominantly composed of refined flour and simple sugars. Simple sugars require very little chemical processing and are rapidly broken down by enzymes, leading to swift stomach emptying and fast absorption into the bloodstream.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sugar cookies\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sugar cookies are predominantly composed of refined flour and simple sugars. Simple sugars require very little chemical processing and are rapidly broken down by enzymes, leading to swift stomach emptying and fast absorption into the bloodstream.\n\nThe other options—Peanut butter sandwich and milk, Beef stew, and a Hamburger—contain high amounts of protein and complex fats. Fat is the slowest macronutrient to digest and significantly delays gastric emptying, keeping those foods in the stomach much longer.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Peanut butter sandwich and milk, Beef stew, Hamburger"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the foods which would be digested most quickly are:::', 'the foods which would be digested most quickly are', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sugar cookies are predominantly composed of refined flour and simple sugars. Simple sugars require very little chemical processing and are rapidly broken down by enzymes, leading to swift stomach emptying and fast absorption into the bloodstream.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sugar cookies\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sugar cookies are predominantly composed of refined flour and simple sugars. Simple sugars require very little chemical processing and are rapidly broken down by enzymes, leading to swift stomach emptying and fast absorption into the bloodstream.\n\nThe other options—Peanut butter sandwich and milk, Beef stew, and a Hamburger—contain high amounts of protein and complex fats. Fat is the slowest macronutrient to digest and significantly delays gastric emptying, keeping those foods in the stomach much longer.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Peanut butter sandwich and milk, Beef stew, Hamburger"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:you have a béarnaise recipe that makes seven 14-gram portions but you need to make enough to have twenty 8-gram portions. what is the recipe conversion factor?:::', 'you have a béarnaise recipe that makes seven 14-gram portions but you need to make enough to have twenty 8-gram portions. what is the recipe conversion factor?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Original yield is 7 × 14 g = 98 g. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"1.63\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Original yield is 7 × 14 g = 98 g. \n\nRequired yield is 20 × 8 g = 160 g. \n\nThe conversion factor is 160 ÷ 98 ≈ 1.63.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 0.61, 0.57, None of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:you are working with a client who complains of constipation. which of the following would you recommend?:::all of the above', 'you are working with a client who complains of constipation. which of the following would you recommend?', 'All of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Managing and preventing constipation effectively relies on a combination of foundational lifestyle and dietary adjustments:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Managing and preventing constipation effectively relies on a combination of foundational lifestyle and dietary adjustments:\n\n• Drink plenty of water: Adequate hydration is essential to soften stools and prevent the colon from absorbing excess water, making them easier to pass.\n• Eat more fiber: Dietary fiber adds bulk and holds fluids, which improves stool shape, weight, and transit through the colon.\n• Exercise more: Regular physical activity naturally stimulates blood flow to the abdomen and improves the muscular movements (peristalsis) of the colon to move stool forward.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Drink plenty of water, Exercise more, Eat more fiber"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:you are working with a client who complains of constipation. which of the following would you recommend?:::', 'you are working with a client who complains of constipation. which of the following would you recommend?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Managing and preventing constipation effectively relies on a combination of foundational lifestyle and dietary adjustments:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Managing and preventing constipation effectively relies on a combination of foundational lifestyle and dietary adjustments:\n\n• Drink plenty of water: Adequate hydration is essential to soften stools and prevent the colon from absorbing excess water, making them easier to pass.\n• Eat more fiber: Dietary fiber adds bulk and holds fluids, which improves stool shape, weight, and transit through the colon.\n• Exercise more: Regular physical activity naturally stimulates blood flow to the abdomen and improves the muscular movements (peristalsis) of the colon to move stool forward.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Drink plenty of water, Exercise more, Eat more fiber"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:food recording can particularly be valuable for:::overweight people', 'food recording can particularly be valuable for', 'Overweight people', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Food recording (also known as keeping a food diary or journal) is particularly valuable as a behavioral and therapeutic intervention for overweight people.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Overweight people\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Food recording (also known as keeping a food diary or journal) is particularly valuable as a behavioral and therapeutic intervention for overweight people.\n\nClinical research consistently shows that individuals tracking weight loss goals double their success rates simply by writing down what they eat and drink. Food records address the primary psychological and behavioral challenges of weight management by:\n\n1. Eliminating Mindless Eating: It forces individuals to pause and acknowledge every snack, beverage, or bite.\n2. Correcting Perception vs. Reality: Overweight individuals frequently under-report or underestimate their actual caloric intake. Logging food brings accurate awareness to portion sizes and hidden calories.\n3. Identifying Emotional Triggers: It highlights behavioral patterns, such as eating out of stress, boredom, or fatigue rather than actual physical hunger.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Teenagers, Older adults, Women"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:food recording can particularly be valuable for:::', 'food recording can particularly be valuable for', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Food recording (also known as keeping a food diary or journal) is particularly valuable as a behavioral and therapeutic intervention for overweight people.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Overweight people\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Food recording (also known as keeping a food diary or journal) is particularly valuable as a behavioral and therapeutic intervention for overweight people.\n\nClinical research consistently shows that individuals tracking weight loss goals double their success rates simply by writing down what they eat and drink. Food records address the primary psychological and behavioral challenges of weight management by:\n\n1. Eliminating Mindless Eating: It forces individuals to pause and acknowledge every snack, beverage, or bite.\n2. Correcting Perception vs. Reality: Overweight individuals frequently under-report or underestimate their actual caloric intake. Logging food brings accurate awareness to portion sizes and hidden calories.\n3. Identifying Emotional Triggers: It highlights behavioral patterns, such as eating out of stress, boredom, or fatigue rather than actual physical hunger.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Teenagers, Older adults, Women"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the muscular contractions that move food through the gi tract are called::::peristalsis', 'the muscular contractions that move food through the gi tract are called:', 'Peristalsis', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Peristalsis is a series of radially symmetrical, wave-like smooth muscle contractions and relaxations that automatically propel food, liquids, and waste forward through the entire gastrointestinal tract. It starts in the esophagus when you swallow a bolus of food and continues down through the stomach, small intestine, and large intestine.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Peristalsis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Peristalsis is a series of radially symmetrical, wave-like smooth muscle contractions and relaxations that automatically propel food, liquids, and waste forward through the entire gastrointestinal tract. It starts in the esophagus when you swallow a bolus of food and continues down through the stomach, small intestine, and large intestine.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Hydrolysis, Sphincters, Bowel movement"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:in people who are ill, significant weight loss may be masked by::::fluid retention', 'in people who are ill, significant weight loss may be masked by:', 'Fluid retention', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When a person is ill, the body often holds onto extra water in the tissues or body cavities—a condition known as fluid retention or edema. Water is heavy. If a patient is severely sick and actively losing actual fat and muscle mass (tissue wasting), the heavy accumulation of trapped fluid counterbalances that loss on the scale. As a result, their total body weight might appear stable or even look like it is increasing, completely masking the underlying malnutrition and tissue loss.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Fluid retention\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When a person is ill, the body often holds onto extra water in the tissues or body cavities—a condition known as fluid retention or edema. Water is heavy. If a patient is severely sick and actively losing actual fat and muscle mass (tissue wasting), the heavy accumulation of trapped fluid counterbalances that loss on the scale. As a result, their total body weight might appear stable or even look like it is increasing, completely masking the underlying malnutrition and tissue loss.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Dehydration, Abdominal obesity, drug therapy"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:in people who are ill, significant weight loss may be masked by::::', 'in people who are ill, significant weight loss may be masked by:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When a person is ill, the body often holds onto extra water in the tissues or body cavities—a condition known as fluid retention or edema. Water is heavy. If a patient is severely sick and actively losing actual fat and muscle mass (tissue wasting), the heavy accumulation of trapped fluid counterbalances that loss on the scale. As a result, their total body weight might appear stable or even look like it is increasing, completely masking the underlying malnutrition and tissue loss.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Fluid retention\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When a person is ill, the body often holds onto extra water in the tissues or body cavities—a condition known as fluid retention or edema. Water is heavy. If a patient is severely sick and actively losing actual fat and muscle mass (tissue wasting), the heavy accumulation of trapped fluid counterbalances that loss on the scale. As a result, their total body weight might appear stable or even look like it is increasing, completely masking the underlying malnutrition and tissue loss.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Dehydration, Abdominal obesity, drug therapy"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the continuing presence of nutrition-related disease makes it important that health professionals be able to assess nutritional status::::to identify who might benefit from nutrition intervention', 'the continuing presence of nutrition-related disease makes it important that health professionals be able to assess nutritional status:', 'to identify who might benefit from nutrition intervention', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The foundational clinical purpose of a nutritional assessment is to systematically evaluate an individual''s health status to pinpoint specific deficiencies, excesses, or metabolic risks. By collecting this baseline data, healthcare professionals can accurately identify exactly which patients are at risk and determine who will directly benefit from a targeted nutrition intervention or care plan.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"to identify who might benefit from nutrition intervention\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The foundational clinical purpose of a nutritional assessment is to systematically evaluate an individual''s health status to pinpoint specific deficiencies, excesses, or metabolic risks. By collecting this baseline data, healthcare professionals can accurately identify exactly which patients are at risk and determine who will directly benefit from a targeted nutrition intervention or care plan.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: To monitor and improve the nutritional status of its citizens, to prevent the increase risk of morbidity and mortality, To prepare for nutrition researches"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the continuing presence of nutrition-related disease makes it important that health professionals be able to assess nutritional status::::', 'the continuing presence of nutrition-related disease makes it important that health professionals be able to assess nutritional status:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The foundational clinical purpose of a nutritional assessment is to systematically evaluate an individual''s health status to pinpoint specific deficiencies, excesses, or metabolic risks. By collecting this baseline data, healthcare professionals can accurately identify exactly which patients are at risk and determine who will directly benefit from a targeted nutrition intervention or care plan.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"to identify who might benefit from nutrition intervention\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The foundational clinical purpose of a nutritional assessment is to systematically evaluate an individual''s health status to pinpoint specific deficiencies, excesses, or metabolic risks. By collecting this baseline data, healthcare professionals can accurately identify exactly which patients are at risk and determine who will directly benefit from a targeted nutrition intervention or care plan.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: To monitor and improve the nutritional status of its citizens, to prevent the increase risk of morbidity and mortality, To prepare for nutrition researches"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the muscular contractions that move food through the gi tract are called::::', 'the muscular contractions that move food through the gi tract are called:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Peristalsis is a series of radially symmetrical, wave-like smooth muscle contractions and relaxations that automatically propel food, liquids, and waste forward through the entire gastrointestinal tract. It starts in the esophagus when you swallow a bolus of food and continues down through the stomach, small intestine, and large intestine.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Peristalsis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Peristalsis is a series of radially symmetrical, wave-like smooth muscle contractions and relaxations that automatically propel food, liquids, and waste forward through the entire gastrointestinal tract. It starts in the esophagus when you swallow a bolus of food and continues down through the stomach, small intestine, and large intestine.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Hydrolysis, Sphincters, Bowel movement"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:conditions associated with dysphagia include::::all of the above', 'conditions associated with dysphagia include:', 'all of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dysphagia is the medical term for difficulty swallowing. It can be caused by structural blockages, neurological damage, or general muscular weakening:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"all of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dysphagia is the medical term for difficulty swallowing. It can be caused by structural blockages, neurological damage, or general muscular weakening:\n\n• Cancers of the head and neck: Tumors physically block the passage of food, and radiation therapy can scar or stiffen swallowing muscles.\n• Brain tumors: Tumors can compress cranial nerves or damage the brain stem and cerebral cortex, disrupting the neurological coordination needed to swallow safely.\n• Aging: Natural aging causes a decline in muscle mass and elasticity (presbyphagia), slower nerve reflexes, and reduced saliva production, making swallowing more difficult.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: aging, brain tumors, cancers of the head and neck"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:conditions associated with dysphagia include::::', 'conditions associated with dysphagia include:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dysphagia is the medical term for difficulty swallowing. It can be caused by structural blockages, neurological damage, or general muscular weakening:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"all of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dysphagia is the medical term for difficulty swallowing. It can be caused by structural blockages, neurological damage, or general muscular weakening:\n\n• Cancers of the head and neck: Tumors physically block the passage of food, and radiation therapy can scar or stiffen swallowing muscles.\n• Brain tumors: Tumors can compress cranial nerves or damage the brain stem and cerebral cortex, disrupting the neurological coordination needed to swallow safely.\n• Aging: Natural aging causes a decline in muscle mass and elasticity (presbyphagia), slower nerve reflexes, and reduced saliva production, making swallowing more difficult.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: aging, brain tumors, cancers of the head and neck"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:among the most useful physical signs of nutrition status are those that reflect::::dehydration and fluid retention', 'among the most useful physical signs of nutrition status are those that reflect:', 'Dehydration and fluid Retention', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In clinical nutrition assessments, the most immediate, reliable, and widely evaluated physical signs are those checking fluid status. Changes in dehydration and fluid retention (edema) manifest rapidly on the outside of the body. A clinician can easily evaluate them during a routine physical exam by checking skin turgor (elasticity), dry mucous membranes, sunken eyes, or pitting edema in the lower extremities.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Dehydration and fluid Retention\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In clinical nutrition assessments, the most immediate, reliable, and widely evaluated physical signs are those checking fluid status. Changes in dehydration and fluid retention (edema) manifest rapidly on the outside of the body. A clinician can easily evaluate them during a routine physical exam by checking skin turgor (elasticity), dry mucous membranes, sunken eyes, or pitting edema in the lower extremities.\n\nBecause severe fluid shifts can quickly mask true body weight changes and indicate critical changes in organ function, tracking them is fundamental to establishing a patient''s accurate nutrition profile.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vitamin A deficiency, Iron status, Vitamin D status"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:among the most useful physical signs of nutrition status are those that reflect::::', 'among the most useful physical signs of nutrition status are those that reflect:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In clinical nutrition assessments, the most immediate, reliable, and widely evaluated physical signs are those checking fluid status. Changes in dehydration and fluid retention (edema) manifest rapidly on the outside of the body. A clinician can easily evaluate them during a routine physical exam by checking skin turgor (elasticity), dry mucous membranes, sunken eyes, or pitting edema in the lower extremities.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Dehydration and fluid Retention\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In clinical nutrition assessments, the most immediate, reliable, and widely evaluated physical signs are those checking fluid status. Changes in dehydration and fluid retention (edema) manifest rapidly on the outside of the body. A clinician can easily evaluate them during a routine physical exam by checking skin turgor (elasticity), dry mucous membranes, sunken eyes, or pitting edema in the lower extremities.\n\nBecause severe fluid shifts can quickly mask true body weight changes and indicate critical changes in organ function, tracking them is fundamental to establishing a patient''s accurate nutrition profile.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vitamin A deficiency, Iron status, Vitamin D status"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following does not secrete digestive enzymes:::esophagus', 'which of the following does not secrete digestive enzymes', 'Esophagus', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The esophagus is purely a muscular transit tube. Its sole function is to transport food from the mouth to the stomach via rhythmic muscle contractions called peristalsis. It contains mucous glands to lubricate the food passage, but it does not produce or secrete any digestive enzymes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Esophagus\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The esophagus is purely a muscular transit tube. Its sole function is to transport food from the mouth to the stomach via rhythmic muscle contractions called peristalsis. It contains mucous glands to lubricate the food passage, but it does not produce or secrete any digestive enzymes.\n\n• Pancreas: Secretes major enzymes like pancreatic lipase, amylase, and proteases into the small intestine.\n• Stomach: Secretes gastric juice containing the protein-digesting enzyme pepsin.\n• Small intestine: Produces its own brush-border enzymes like maltase, sucrase, and lactase.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Small intestine, Pancreas, Stomach"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following does not secrete digestive enzymes:::', 'which of the following does not secrete digestive enzymes', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The esophagus is purely a muscular transit tube. Its sole function is to transport food from the mouth to the stomach via rhythmic muscle contractions called peristalsis. It contains mucous glands to lubricate the food passage, but it does not produce or secrete any digestive enzymes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Esophagus\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The esophagus is purely a muscular transit tube. Its sole function is to transport food from the mouth to the stomach via rhythmic muscle contractions called peristalsis. It contains mucous glands to lubricate the food passage, but it does not produce or secrete any digestive enzymes.\n\n• Pancreas: Secretes major enzymes like pancreatic lipase, amylase, and proteases into the small intestine.\n• Stomach: Secretes gastric juice containing the protein-digesting enzyme pepsin.\n• Small intestine: Produces its own brush-border enzymes like maltase, sucrase, and lactase.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Small intestine, Pancreas, Stomach"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:when nutrients enter the blood vessels from the small intestine, they are first transported to the::::liver', 'when nutrients enter the blood vessels from the small intestine, they are first transported to the:', 'Liver', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When water-soluble nutrients (like monosaccharides, amino acids, water-soluble vitamins, and minerals) are absorbed through the intestinal mucosa, they enter the capillaries of the mesenteric veins. This blood immediately pools into the hepatic portal vein, which transports the nutrients directly to the liver first. The liver acts as the body''s primary metabolic processing center, filtering toxins, storing nutrients, and regulating their release before they enter general circulation.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Liver\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When water-soluble nutrients (like monosaccharides, amino acids, water-soluble vitamins, and minerals) are absorbed through the intestinal mucosa, they enter the capillaries of the mesenteric veins. This blood immediately pools into the hepatic portal vein, which transports the nutrients directly to the liver first. The liver acts as the body''s primary metabolic processing center, filtering toxins, storing nutrients, and regulating their release before they enter general circulation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: kidney, cells of the body, brain"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following common beverages have high caffeine content except ::::cola drinks', 'the following common beverages have high caffeine content except :', 'Cola drinks', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When comparing standard serving sizes of these common beverages, cola drinks contain the lowest amount of caffeine, typically averaging only 20–40 mg per standard serving.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Cola drinks\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When comparing standard serving sizes of these common beverages, cola drinks contain the lowest amount of caffeine, typically averaging only 20–40 mg per standard serving.\n\n• Brewed Coffee: Contains the highest amount of caffeine, typically averaging 95–150 mg per 8 oz cup.\n• Instant Coffee: Contains a high amount of caffeine, typically averaging 60–80 mg per 8 oz cup.\n• Leaf Tea: Brewed black or green leaf tea contains a moderate amount of caffeine, typically averaging 30–50 mg per 8 oz cup.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Leaf Tea, Instant Coffee, Brewed coffee"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:when nutrients enter the blood vessels from the small intestine, they are first transported to the::::', 'when nutrients enter the blood vessels from the small intestine, they are first transported to the:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When water-soluble nutrients (like monosaccharides, amino acids, water-soluble vitamins, and minerals) are absorbed through the intestinal mucosa, they enter the capillaries of the mesenteric veins. This blood immediately pools into the hepatic portal vein, which transports the nutrients directly to the liver first. The liver acts as the body''s primary metabolic processing center, filtering toxins, storing nutrients, and regulating their release before they enter general circulation.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Liver\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When water-soluble nutrients (like monosaccharides, amino acids, water-soluble vitamins, and minerals) are absorbed through the intestinal mucosa, they enter the capillaries of the mesenteric veins. This blood immediately pools into the hepatic portal vein, which transports the nutrients directly to the liver first. The liver acts as the body''s primary metabolic processing center, filtering toxins, storing nutrients, and regulating their release before they enter general circulation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: kidney, cells of the body, brain"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:nutrients that are digested in the small intestine are::::carbohydrate, fat and protein', 'nutrients that are digested in the small intestine are:', 'Carbohydrate, fat and protein', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The small intestine serves as the primary site for the final chemical digestion and absorption of all three major macronutrients:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Carbohydrate, fat and protein\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The small intestine serves as the primary site for the final chemical digestion and absorption of all three major macronutrients:\n✓ Carbohydrates: Pancreatic amylase and enzymes along the intestinal brush border break down starches and complex sugars into simple sugars (like glucose).\n✓ Fats: Bile from the liver emulsifies fat globules, allowing pancreatic lipase to efficiently break them down into fatty acids and glycerol.\n✓ Proteins: While protein digestion begins in the stomach, pancreatic and intestinal enzymes (like trypsin and chymotrypsin) finish breaking down peptides into absorbable amino acids within the small intestine.\n\n× Water and Minerals: These items are absorbed by the body but do not undergo mechanical or enzymatic digestion (breakdown).\n× Fiber: Humans lack the digestive enzymes required to break down dietary fiber. Fiber passes through the small intestine largely untouched and travels down to the large intestine.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Fat, water and fiber, Protein, vitamins and fiber, Water, fiber and minerals"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:nutrients that are digested in the small intestine are::::', 'nutrients that are digested in the small intestine are:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The small intestine serves as the primary site for the final chemical digestion and absorption of all three major macronutrients:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Carbohydrate, fat and protein\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The small intestine serves as the primary site for the final chemical digestion and absorption of all three major macronutrients:\n✓ Carbohydrates: Pancreatic amylase and enzymes along the intestinal brush border break down starches and complex sugars into simple sugars (like glucose).\n✓ Fats: Bile from the liver emulsifies fat globules, allowing pancreatic lipase to efficiently break them down into fatty acids and glycerol.\n✓ Proteins: While protein digestion begins in the stomach, pancreatic and intestinal enzymes (like trypsin and chymotrypsin) finish breaking down peptides into absorbable amino acids within the small intestine.\n\n× Water and Minerals: These items are absorbed by the body but do not undergo mechanical or enzymatic digestion (breakdown).\n× Fiber: Humans lack the digestive enzymes required to break down dietary fiber. Fiber passes through the small intestine largely untouched and travels down to the large intestine.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Fat, water and fiber, Protein, vitamins and fiber, Water, fiber and minerals"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:absorbs water and electrolytes to form feces:::colon', 'absorbs water and electrolytes to form feces', 'colon', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The large intestine is anatomically divided into sections, with the colon acting as the primary site for the final stages of water and mineral reclamation:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"colon\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The large intestine is anatomically divided into sections, with the colon acting as the primary site for the final stages of water and mineral reclamation:\n\nAs the liquid remains of digested food pass through the colon, its mucosal walls actively absorb water and electrolytes (such as sodium and potassium) back into the bloodstream. This dehydration process compacts the remaining unabsorbed waste, transforming it into solid feces.\n\n\t• Rectum: Functions primarily as a temporary storage chamber for the formed feces until defecation occurs.\n\t• Anus: The external opening and sphincter muscular control point that regulates the elimination of feces out of the body.\n\t• Appendix: A small, finger-like pouch attached to the cecum that does not play a significant role in fluid or electrolyte absorption.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: anus, appendix, rectum"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the cost of materials that go directly into the production of the menu item.:::', 'the cost of materials that go directly into the production of the menu item.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Food cost is the cost of materials that directly enter into the production of a menu item; overhead costs are indirect operating expenses.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"food cost\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Food cost is the cost of materials that directly enter into the production of a menu item; overhead costs are indirect operating expenses.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: edible portion cost, as purchased cost, overhead cost"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:absorbs water and electrolytes to form feces:::', 'absorbs water and electrolytes to form feces', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The large intestine is anatomically divided into sections, with the colon acting as the primary site for the final stages of water and mineral reclamation:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"colon\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The large intestine is anatomically divided into sections, with the colon acting as the primary site for the final stages of water and mineral reclamation:\n\nAs the liquid remains of digested food pass through the colon, its mucosal walls actively absorb water and electrolytes (such as sodium and potassium) back into the bloodstream. This dehydration process compacts the remaining unabsorbed waste, transforming it into solid feces.\n\n\t• Rectum: Functions primarily as a temporary storage chamber for the formed feces until defecation occurs.\n\t• Anus: The external opening and sphincter muscular control point that regulates the elimination of feces out of the body.\n\t• Appendix: A small, finger-like pouch attached to the cecum that does not play a significant role in fluid or electrolyte absorption.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: anus, appendix, rectum"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:in the nutrition management of peptic ulcer, gastric stimulants should be limited except:::evaporated milk', 'in the nutrition management of peptic ulcer, gastric stimulants should be limited except', 'Evaporated Milk', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"While caffeine, alcohol, and even decaffeinated coffee are powerful, direct chemical triggers that prompt the stomach lining to secrete massive amounts of gastric acid, Evaporated Milk is chemically categorized as a food item rather than a pure gastric stimulant. Although the protein and calcium in milk can cause a delayed rebound acid secretion, traditional dietetic manuals classify it under regular dairy/protein food groups rather than standalone chemical irritants or stimulants.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Evaporated Milk\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"While caffeine, alcohol, and even decaffeinated coffee are powerful, direct chemical triggers that prompt the stomach lining to secrete massive amounts of gastric acid, Evaporated Milk is chemically categorized as a food item rather than a pure gastric stimulant. Although the protein and calcium in milk can cause a delayed rebound acid secretion, traditional dietetic manuals classify it under regular dairy/protein food groups rather than standalone chemical irritants or stimulants.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Caffeine, Alcohol, Decaffeinated Coffee"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:in the nutrition management of peptic ulcer, gastric stimulants should be limited except:::', 'in the nutrition management of peptic ulcer, gastric stimulants should be limited except', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"While caffeine, alcohol, and even decaffeinated coffee are powerful, direct chemical triggers that prompt the stomach lining to secrete massive amounts of gastric acid, Evaporated Milk is chemically categorized as a food item rather than a pure gastric stimulant. Although the protein and calcium in milk can cause a delayed rebound acid secretion, traditional dietetic manuals classify it under regular dairy/protein food groups rather than standalone chemical irritants or stimulants.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Evaporated Milk\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"While caffeine, alcohol, and even decaffeinated coffee are powerful, direct chemical triggers that prompt the stomach lining to secrete massive amounts of gastric acid, Evaporated Milk is chemically categorized as a food item rather than a pure gastric stimulant. Although the protein and calcium in milk can cause a delayed rebound acid secretion, traditional dietetic manuals classify it under regular dairy/protein food groups rather than standalone chemical irritants or stimulants.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Caffeine, Alcohol, Decaffeinated Coffee"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb)
