('mc:case scenario: jd, a 59-year-old female chief executive officer of a telecommunications office who is 5’5” tall and weighs 93 kg, was recently admitted to the hospital due to a transient ischemic attack (tia). using the ndap formula, jd’s recommended body weight is:::57.27 kg', 'case scenario: jd, a 59-year-old female chief executive officer of a telecommunications office who is 5’5” tall and weighs 93 kg, was recently admitted to the hospital due to a transient ischemic attack (tia). using the ndap formula, jd’s recommended body weight is', '57.27 kg', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The NDAP formula for females is 106 lbs for the first  5 feet (60 inches) of height ± 4 lbs for every inch above or below 5 feet.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"57.27 kg\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The NDAP formula for females is 106 lbs for the first  5 feet (60 inches) of height ± 4 lbs for every inch above or below 5 feet.\n\n1. Calculate the height allowance.\n5''5\" tall = 106 lbs + (5 inches x 4 lbs/inch) = 126 lbs\n\n2. Convert pounds to kilograms.\n126 lbs ÷ 2.2 = 57.27 kg","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 50 kg, 65 kg, 58.59 kg"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:case scenario: jd, a 59-year-old female chief executive officer of a telecommunications office who is 5’5” tall and weighs 93 kg, was recently admitted to the hospital due to a transient ischemic attack (tia). using the ndap formula, jd’s recommended body weight is:::', 'case scenario: jd, a 59-year-old female chief executive officer of a telecommunications office who is 5’5” tall and weighs 93 kg, was recently admitted to the hospital due to a transient ischemic attack (tia). using the ndap formula, jd’s recommended body weight is', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The NDAP formula for females is 106 lbs for the first  5 feet (60 inches) of height ± 4 lbs for every inch above or below 5 feet.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"57.27 kg\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The NDAP formula for females is 106 lbs for the first  5 feet (60 inches) of height ± 4 lbs for every inch above or below 5 feet.\n\n1. Calculate the height allowance.\n5''5\" tall = 106 lbs + (5 inches x 4 lbs/inch) = 126 lbs\n\n2. Convert pounds to kilograms.\n126 lbs ÷ 2.2 = 57.27 kg","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 50 kg, 65 kg, 58.59 kg"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:malnutrition in a developing country is reflected in the increased specific death rate of preschoolers due to::::', 'malnutrition in a developing country is reflected in the increased specific death rate of preschoolers due to:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Malnutrition in a developing country increases the preschool death rate primarily due to infectious diseases such as measles, one of the most contagious diseases (but preventable) diseases in the world.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"measles\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Malnutrition in a developing country increases the preschool death rate primarily due to infectious diseases such as measles, one of the most contagious diseases (but preventable) diseases in the world.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: diarrhea, tetanus, dengue"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:lr, a 67 year old man who has had depression was prescribed a maoi (monoamine oxidase inhibitor) the following foods are avoided except::::', 'lr, a 67 year old man who has had depression was prescribed a maoi (monoamine oxidase inhibitor) the following foods are avoided except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Green tea is the exception among the listed options because it contains minimal to negligible levels of tyramine and is primarily composed of catechins, flavonoids, and caffeine. Unlike fermented, aged, or protein-degraded foods, green tea does not provoke dangerous systemic vasoconstriction when combined with MAOIs.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Green Tea\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Green tea is the exception among the listed options because it contains minimal to negligible levels of tyramine and is primarily composed of catechins, flavonoids, and caffeine. Unlike fermented, aged, or protein-degraded foods, green tea does not provoke dangerous systemic vasoconstriction when combined with MAOIs.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Avocado, Bean curd, Aged Cheese"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:patients taking coumadin are advised not to increase or decrease their usual consumption of the following foods, except::::bologna', 'patients taking coumadin are advised not to increase or decrease their usual consumption of the following foods, except:', 'Bologna', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Coumadin works by inhibiting vitamin K-dependent blood clotting factors. Patients are advised to keep their consumption of foods containing Vitamin K or items that alter blood coagulation steady and consistent from week to week. Sudden changes can make the medicine less effective or increase bleeding risks.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Bologna\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Coumadin works by inhibiting vitamin K-dependent blood clotting factors. Patients are advised to keep their consumption of foods containing Vitamin K or items that alter blood coagulation steady and consistent from week to week. Sudden changes can make the medicine less effective or increase bleeding risks.\n\nBroccoli is high in Vitamin K, while green tea and onions can fluctuate or alter Coumadin''s efficacy. Therefore, patients are told not to drastically increase or decrease their intake of these foods.\n\nBologna is a processed meat sausage. Meats and processed deli products inherently contain extremely low to negligible amounts of Vitamin K. Altering the amount of bologna in a patient''s daily diet has no clinical impact on their international normalized ratio (INR) values or Coumadin''s blood-thinning behavior, making it the correct exception.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Green tea, Broccoli, Fried Onions"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:patients taking coumadin are advised not to increase or decrease their usual consumption of the following foods, except::::', 'patients taking coumadin are advised not to increase or decrease their usual consumption of the following foods, except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Coumadin works by inhibiting vitamin K-dependent blood clotting factors. Patients are advised to keep their consumption of foods containing Vitamin K or items that alter blood coagulation steady and consistent from week to week. Sudden changes can make the medicine less effective or increase bleeding risks.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Bologna\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Coumadin works by inhibiting vitamin K-dependent blood clotting factors. Patients are advised to keep their consumption of foods containing Vitamin K or items that alter blood coagulation steady and consistent from week to week. Sudden changes can make the medicine less effective or increase bleeding risks.\n\nBroccoli is high in Vitamin K, while green tea and onions can fluctuate or alter Coumadin''s efficacy. Therefore, patients are told not to drastically increase or decrease their intake of these foods.\n\nBologna is a processed meat sausage. Meats and processed deli products inherently contain extremely low to negligible amounts of Vitamin K. Altering the amount of bologna in a patient''s daily diet has no clinical impact on their international normalized ratio (INR) values or Coumadin''s blood-thinning behavior, making it the correct exception.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Green tea, Broccoli, Fried Onions"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:under the 105-day expanded maternity leave law, the father may enjoy as much as how many days of paid leave to take care of his child?:::', 'under the 105-day expanded maternity leave law, the father may enjoy as much as how many days of paid leave to take care of his child?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"RA 11210: A father may now enjoy as much as 14 days of paid leave to take care of his child, depending on his marital status with the mother.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"14 days\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"RA 11210: A father may now enjoy as much as 14 days of paid leave to take care of his child, depending on his marital status with the mother.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 0 days, 3 days, 7 days"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:patients on anti-tuberculosis treatment are at risk of which vitamin deficiency?:::vitamin b 6', 'patients on anti-tuberculosis treatment are at risk of which vitamin deficiency?', 'Vitamin B 6', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Patients undergoing standard anti-tuberculosis treatment are at a high risk for developing a Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine) deficiency.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vitamin B 6\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Patients undergoing standard anti-tuberculosis treatment are at a high risk for developing a Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine) deficiency.\n\nThis deficiency is directly caused by Isoniazid (INH), one of the primary first-line antibiotics used to treat tuberculosis. Isoniazid competes with and blocks Vitamin B6 metabolism in the body and accelerates its excretion through urine.\n\nIf Vitamin B6 is not supplemented during anti-TB therapy, the patient can develop severe peripheral neuropathy, which manifests as painful burning, numbness, and tingling sensations in the hands and feet.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vitamin B 1, water soluble vitamins, Vitamin B 12"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:fluoroquinolones (ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin) must not be taken with the following foods except::::papaya', 'fluoroquinolones (ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin) must not be taken with the following foods except:', 'Papaya', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Fluoroquinolones (such as ciprofloxacin and levofloxacin) are a powerful class of broad-spectrum antibiotic medications. They work by blocking bacterial enzymes, which completely stop bacteria from replicating and repairing their DNA, effectively killing off the infection.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Papaya\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Fluoroquinolones (such as ciprofloxacin and levofloxacin) are a powerful class of broad-spectrum antibiotic medications. They work by blocking bacterial enzymes, which completely stop bacteria from replicating and repairing their DNA, effectively killing off the infection.\n\nBecause fluoroquinolones are highly reactive chemicals, what you put in your stomach at the same time matters immensely. The drug molecules have a strong tendency to attach themselves tightly to divalent minerals like calcium.\n\nPapaya contains no significant minerals or compounds that chelate or alter the breakdown of fluoroquinolone antibiotics, making it safe to eat.\n\n• Dairy Products, Yogurt, and Cottage Cheese: Dairy products are packed with calcium. Calcium binds directly to fluoroquinolones in the gut (a process called chelation). This binding forms an insoluble compound that the body cannot absorb, which severely drops the drug levels and makes the antibiotic ineffective.\n• Caffeine: Fluoroquinolones block the liver pathways that break down caffeine. If you consume caffeine while taking these antibiotics, caffeine accumulates in your body, risking severe side effects like a dangerously fast heartbeat, extreme jitteriness, and insomnia.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Yogurt, Cottage Cheese, Caffeine, Dairy Products"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:fluoroquinolones (ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin) must not be taken with the following foods except::::', 'fluoroquinolones (ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin) must not be taken with the following foods except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Fluoroquinolones (such as ciprofloxacin and levofloxacin) are a powerful class of broad-spectrum antibiotic medications. They work by blocking bacterial enzymes, which completely stop bacteria from replicating and repairing their DNA, effectively killing off the infection.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Papaya\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Fluoroquinolones (such as ciprofloxacin and levofloxacin) are a powerful class of broad-spectrum antibiotic medications. They work by blocking bacterial enzymes, which completely stop bacteria from replicating and repairing their DNA, effectively killing off the infection.\n\nBecause fluoroquinolones are highly reactive chemicals, what you put in your stomach at the same time matters immensely. The drug molecules have a strong tendency to attach themselves tightly to divalent minerals like calcium.\n\nPapaya contains no significant minerals or compounds that chelate or alter the breakdown of fluoroquinolone antibiotics, making it safe to eat.\n\n• Dairy Products, Yogurt, and Cottage Cheese: Dairy products are packed with calcium. Calcium binds directly to fluoroquinolones in the gut (a process called chelation). This binding forms an insoluble compound that the body cannot absorb, which severely drops the drug levels and makes the antibiotic ineffective.\n• Caffeine: Fluoroquinolones block the liver pathways that break down caffeine. If you consume caffeine while taking these antibiotics, caffeine accumulates in your body, risking severe side effects like a dangerously fast heartbeat, extreme jitteriness, and insomnia.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Yogurt, Cottage Cheese, Caffeine, Dairy Products"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:jcaho targeted the following drugs for specific monitoring for in and out patients except:::allopurinol', 'jcaho targeted the following drugs for specific monitoring for in and out patients except', 'Allopurinol', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The Joint Commission (formerly JCAHO) establishes strict National Patient Safety Goals tracking high-alert medications that present elevated risks of patient harm if managed improperly.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Allopurinol\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The Joint Commission (formerly JCAHO) establishes strict National Patient Safety Goals tracking high-alert medications that present elevated risks of patient harm if managed improperly.\n\nAllopurinol is designated as the target exception because the question is grouping high-monitoring medications based on their potential to cause a critical, common clinical side effect that dietitians must track: Drug-Induced Hyperglycemia (Spiking Blood Sugar) or severe, immediate metabolic parameter shifts. Allopurinol is a xanthine oxidase inhibitor used to treat gout by lowering uric acid. While it requires kidney tracking, it does not disrupt a patient''s glycemic/blood glucose control mechanics like the other choices do.\n\nCorticosteroids like Dexamethasone severely alter glucose pathways and trigger massive blood sugar spikes, while Sucralfate is a dense aluminum-sucrose complex that physically alters how carbohydrates are absorbed in the gut. Warfarin demands intensive systemic monitoring.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Warfarin, Dexamethasone, Sucralfate"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:jcaho targeted the following drugs for specific monitoring for in and out patients except:::', 'jcaho targeted the following drugs for specific monitoring for in and out patients except', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The Joint Commission (formerly JCAHO) establishes strict National Patient Safety Goals tracking high-alert medications that present elevated risks of patient harm if managed improperly.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Allopurinol\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The Joint Commission (formerly JCAHO) establishes strict National Patient Safety Goals tracking high-alert medications that present elevated risks of patient harm if managed improperly.\n\nAllopurinol is designated as the target exception because the question is grouping high-monitoring medications based on their potential to cause a critical, common clinical side effect that dietitians must track: Drug-Induced Hyperglycemia (Spiking Blood Sugar) or severe, immediate metabolic parameter shifts. Allopurinol is a xanthine oxidase inhibitor used to treat gout by lowering uric acid. While it requires kidney tracking, it does not disrupt a patient''s glycemic/blood glucose control mechanics like the other choices do.\n\nCorticosteroids like Dexamethasone severely alter glucose pathways and trigger massive blood sugar spikes, while Sucralfate is a dense aluminum-sucrose complex that physically alters how carbohydrates are absorbed in the gut. Warfarin demands intensive systemic monitoring.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Warfarin, Dexamethasone, Sucralfate"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:patients on anti-tuberculosis treatment are at risk of which vitamin deficiency?:::', 'patients on anti-tuberculosis treatment are at risk of which vitamin deficiency?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Patients undergoing standard anti-tuberculosis treatment are at a high risk for developing a Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine) deficiency.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vitamin B 6\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Patients undergoing standard anti-tuberculosis treatment are at a high risk for developing a Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine) deficiency.\n\nThis deficiency is directly caused by Isoniazid (INH), one of the primary first-line antibiotics used to treat tuberculosis. Isoniazid competes with and blocks Vitamin B6 metabolism in the body and accelerates its excretion through urine.\n\nIf Vitamin B6 is not supplemented during anti-TB therapy, the patient can develop severe peripheral neuropathy, which manifests as painful burning, numbness, and tingling sensations in the hands and feet.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vitamin B 1, water soluble vitamins, Vitamin B 12"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:ek, is a 27 year old woman, admitted to the hospital with severe crohn’s disease and an unintentional weight loss of 20 pounds over the past two months despite a good appetite. assuming that ek has steatorrhea, what is the most likely cause of her weight loss?:::malabsorption', 'ek, is a 27 year old woman, admitted to the hospital with severe crohn’s disease and an unintentional weight loss of 20 pounds over the past two months despite a good appetite. assuming that ek has steatorrhea, what is the most likely cause of her weight loss?', 'Malabsorption', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Crohn’s disease is a type of inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) that causes chronic inflammation and ulceration along the gastrointestinal tract, most frequently affecting the ileum (the lower part of the small intestine).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Malabsorption\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Crohn’s disease is a type of inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) that causes chronic inflammation and ulceration along the gastrointestinal tract, most frequently affecting the ileum (the lower part of the small intestine).\n\nWhen the small intestine is severely inflamed, it cannot properly absorb nutrients from food into the bloodstream. The presence of steatorrhea (excess fat in the stool) is a classic indicator that fats and fat-soluble vitamins are not being digested or absorbed properly. Even though the patient has a good appetite and is eating well, the body is expelling the nutrients rather than utilizing them, leading to severe, unintentional weight loss due to malabsorption.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Obstruction, Cancer, AIDS"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:ek, is a 27 year old woman, admitted to the hospital with severe crohn’s disease and an unintentional weight loss of 20 pounds over the past two months despite a good appetite. assuming that ek has steatorrhea, what is the most likely cause of her weight loss?:::', 'ek, is a 27 year old woman, admitted to the hospital with severe crohn’s disease and an unintentional weight loss of 20 pounds over the past two months despite a good appetite. assuming that ek has steatorrhea, what is the most likely cause of her weight loss?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Crohn’s disease is a type of inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) that causes chronic inflammation and ulceration along the gastrointestinal tract, most frequently affecting the ileum (the lower part of the small intestine).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Malabsorption\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Crohn’s disease is a type of inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) that causes chronic inflammation and ulceration along the gastrointestinal tract, most frequently affecting the ileum (the lower part of the small intestine).\n\nWhen the small intestine is severely inflamed, it cannot properly absorb nutrients from food into the bloodstream. The presence of steatorrhea (excess fat in the stool) is a classic indicator that fats and fat-soluble vitamins are not being digested or absorbed properly. Even though the patient has a good appetite and is eating well, the body is expelling the nutrients rather than utilizing them, leading to severe, unintentional weight loss due to malabsorption.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Obstruction, Cancer, AIDS"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following common beverages have high caffeine content except ::::', 'the following common beverages have high caffeine content except :', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When comparing standard serving sizes of these common beverages, cola drinks contain the lowest amount of caffeine, typically averaging only 20–40 mg per standard serving.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Cola drinks\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When comparing standard serving sizes of these common beverages, cola drinks contain the lowest amount of caffeine, typically averaging only 20–40 mg per standard serving.\n\n• Brewed Coffee: Contains the highest amount of caffeine, typically averaging 95–150 mg per 8 oz cup.\n• Instant Coffee: Contains a high amount of caffeine, typically averaging 60–80 mg per 8 oz cup.\n• Leaf Tea: Brewed black or green leaf tea contains a moderate amount of caffeine, typically averaging 30–50 mg per 8 oz cup.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Leaf Tea, Instant Coffee, Brewed coffee"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are anthropometric measurements for determining nutritional status except;:::', 'the following are anthropometric measurements for determining nutritional status except;', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Anthropometric measurements are non-invasive, physical dimensions taken directly from the human body to evaluate size, shape, and muscle-to-fat composition.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Creatinine Height Index\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Anthropometric measurements are non-invasive, physical dimensions taken directly from the human body to evaluate size, shape, and muscle-to-fat composition.\n\nCreatinine Height Index is a biochemical (laboratory) test, not an anthropometric measurement. It requires measuring the 24-hour excretion of creatinine in a patient''s urine sample and comparing it against standard height markers to estimate total skeletal muscle depletion.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: % Body weight, Triceps Skinfold measurement, Mid Arm muscle Circumference"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:pr, a 50-year-old woman with chronic pancreatitis, is hospitalized for surgical evaluation. pr was given pancreatic enzymes and can tolerate food. what diet order should be prescribed at this time?:::low fat diet', 'pr, a 50-year-old woman with chronic pancreatitis, is hospitalized for surgical evaluation. pr was given pancreatic enzymes and can tolerate food. what diet order should be prescribed at this time?', 'Low Fat Diet', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Chronic pancreatitis is the long-term inflammation of the pancreas, which severely damages its ability to produce digestive enzymes—particularly lipase, the enzyme required to break down and absorb dietary fats.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Low Fat Diet\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Chronic pancreatitis is the long-term inflammation of the pancreas, which severely damages its ability to produce digestive enzymes—particularly lipase, the enzyme required to break down and absorb dietary fats.\n\nWhen fat enters the small intestine, it stimulates the pancreas to secrete digestive enzymes. If the pancreas is inflamed, this stimulation causes severe, stabbing abdominal pain and can worsen tissue inflammation. Furthermore, undigested fat causes steatorrhea (foul-smelling, fatty stools) and nutrient malabsorption. A low-fat diet minimizes the workload on the pancreas, controls abdominal pain, and manages malabsorption.\n\nEven though PR is taking supplemental pancreatic enzymes to help digest her food, keeping dietary fat intake low remains the primary nutritional therapy during an inpatient hospitalization or surgical evaluation period.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Low Sodium Diet, Low Protein Diet, Low Fiber Diet"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:pr, a 50-year-old woman with chronic pancreatitis, is hospitalized for surgical evaluation. pr was given pancreatic enzymes and can tolerate food. what diet order should be prescribed at this time?:::', 'pr, a 50-year-old woman with chronic pancreatitis, is hospitalized for surgical evaluation. pr was given pancreatic enzymes and can tolerate food. what diet order should be prescribed at this time?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Chronic pancreatitis is the long-term inflammation of the pancreas, which severely damages its ability to produce digestive enzymes—particularly lipase, the enzyme required to break down and absorb dietary fats.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Low Fat Diet\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Chronic pancreatitis is the long-term inflammation of the pancreas, which severely damages its ability to produce digestive enzymes—particularly lipase, the enzyme required to break down and absorb dietary fats.\n\nWhen fat enters the small intestine, it stimulates the pancreas to secrete digestive enzymes. If the pancreas is inflamed, this stimulation causes severe, stabbing abdominal pain and can worsen tissue inflammation. Furthermore, undigested fat causes steatorrhea (foul-smelling, fatty stools) and nutrient malabsorption. A low-fat diet minimizes the workload on the pancreas, controls abdominal pain, and manages malabsorption.\n\nEven though PR is taking supplemental pancreatic enzymes to help digest her food, keeping dietary fat intake low remains the primary nutritional therapy during an inpatient hospitalization or surgical evaluation period.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Low Sodium Diet, Low Protein Diet, Low Fiber Diet"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:patients on long term use of steroids are recommended to take supplements::::calcium', 'patients on long term use of steroids are recommended to take supplements:', 'Calcium', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Long-term corticosteroid therapy (such as prednisone) severely impacts bone metabolism and mineral density, putting patients at a very high risk for Steroid-Induced Osteoporosis. Steroids cause bone loss through multiple pathways:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Calcium\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Long-term corticosteroid therapy (such as prednisone) severely impacts bone metabolism and mineral density, putting patients at a very high risk for Steroid-Induced Osteoporosis. Steroids cause bone loss through multiple pathways:\n\n1. They directly block the intestines from absorbing dietary Calcium.\n2. They prompt the kidneys to flush out more calcium through urine.\n3. They inhibit bone-building cells (osteoblasts) while stimulating bone-resorbing cells (osteoclasts).\n\nTo counteract this rapid bone wasting, clinical guidelines recommend that patients on long-term steroid therapy take Calcium and Vitamin D to protect bone integrity and prevent fractures.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vitamin D, Vitamin C, Vitamin E"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:patients on long term use of steroids are recommended to take supplements::::', 'patients on long term use of steroids are recommended to take supplements:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Long-term corticosteroid therapy (such as prednisone) severely impacts bone metabolism and mineral density, putting patients at a very high risk for Steroid-Induced Osteoporosis. Steroids cause bone loss through multiple pathways:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Calcium\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Long-term corticosteroid therapy (such as prednisone) severely impacts bone metabolism and mineral density, putting patients at a very high risk for Steroid-Induced Osteoporosis. Steroids cause bone loss through multiple pathways:\n\n1. They directly block the intestines from absorbing dietary Calcium.\n2. They prompt the kidneys to flush out more calcium through urine.\n3. They inhibit bone-building cells (osteoblasts) while stimulating bone-resorbing cells (osteoclasts).\n\nTo counteract this rapid bone wasting, clinical guidelines recommend that patients on long-term steroid therapy take Calcium and Vitamin D to protect bone integrity and prevent fractures.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vitamin D, Vitamin C, Vitamin E"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:chronic malnutrition is a deficiency in:::calories', 'chronic malnutrition is a deficiency in', 'Calories', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Chronic energy deficiency forces the body into metabolic conservation by downregulating basal metabolic rate, reducing physical activity, and halting linear growth in pediatric populations. Prolonged caloric deficit causes the endocrine axis to suppress the growth hormone-insulin-like growth factor 1 (IGF-1) cascade, shunting minimal available energy toward vital organ function rather than tissue growth or maintenance.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Calories\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Chronic energy deficiency forces the body into metabolic conservation by downregulating basal metabolic rate, reducing physical activity, and halting linear growth in pediatric populations. Prolonged caloric deficit causes the endocrine axis to suppress the growth hormone-insulin-like growth factor 1 (IGF-1) cascade, shunting minimal available energy toward vital organ function rather than tissue growth or maintenance.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Protein, Calories and Protein, Marasmic Kwashiorkor"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:hypersensitivity reaction involving the skin and mucous membrane:::stevens johnson syndrome', 'hypersensitivity reaction involving the skin and mucous membrane', 'Stevens Johnson Syndrome', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS) is a rare, life-threatening Type IV (delayed-type) cell-mediated hypersensitivity reaction. It is classically triggered by an adverse reaction to specific medications (like allopurinol, anti-seizure drugs, or antibiotics) or severe infections.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Stevens Johnson Syndrome\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS) is a rare, life-threatening Type IV (delayed-type) cell-mediated hypersensitivity reaction. It is classically triggered by an adverse reaction to specific medications (like allopurinol, anti-seizure drugs, or antibiotics) or severe infections.\n\nThe defining clinical characteristic of SJS is its aggressive, painful destruction of both the skin and mucous membranes. It causes severe, blistering lesions, rash, and epidermal peeling across the skin, alongside excruciating sores in the moist linings of the mouth, throat, eyes, nose, and genitals.\n\n• Lupus Erythematosus: This is a chronic Type III immune-complex autoimmune disease, not primarily a delayed hypersensitivity reaction of the mucocutaneous linings. While it causes a distinctive \"butterfly rash\" on the face, it lacks the acute, severe blister-peeling mucous membrane destruction seen in SJS.\n• Food Sensitivity Reaction: True food allergies are Type I (immediate-type) IgE-mediated reactions that typically manifest as hives (urticaria), swelling (angioedema), or system-wide anaphylaxis within minutes of ingestion. They do not follow the severe multi-mucosal blistering disease spectrum characteristic of SJS.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Food Sensitivity reaction, Lupus Erythematosus, None of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:hypersensitivity reaction involving the skin and mucous membrane:::', 'hypersensitivity reaction involving the skin and mucous membrane', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS) is a rare, life-threatening Type IV (delayed-type) cell-mediated hypersensitivity reaction. It is classically triggered by an adverse reaction to specific medications (like allopurinol, anti-seizure drugs, or antibiotics) or severe infections.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Stevens Johnson Syndrome\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS) is a rare, life-threatening Type IV (delayed-type) cell-mediated hypersensitivity reaction. It is classically triggered by an adverse reaction to specific medications (like allopurinol, anti-seizure drugs, or antibiotics) or severe infections.\n\nThe defining clinical characteristic of SJS is its aggressive, painful destruction of both the skin and mucous membranes. It causes severe, blistering lesions, rash, and epidermal peeling across the skin, alongside excruciating sores in the moist linings of the mouth, throat, eyes, nose, and genitals.\n\n• Lupus Erythematosus: This is a chronic Type III immune-complex autoimmune disease, not primarily a delayed hypersensitivity reaction of the mucocutaneous linings. While it causes a distinctive \"butterfly rash\" on the face, it lacks the acute, severe blister-peeling mucous membrane destruction seen in SJS.\n• Food Sensitivity Reaction: True food allergies are Type I (immediate-type) IgE-mediated reactions that typically manifest as hives (urticaria), swelling (angioedema), or system-wide anaphylaxis within minutes of ingestion. They do not follow the severe multi-mucosal blistering disease spectrum characteristic of SJS.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Food Sensitivity reaction, Lupus Erythematosus, None of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following drugs are known to interact with grapefruit juice except::::metformin', 'the following drugs are known to interact with grapefruit juice except:', 'Metformin', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Grapefruit juice interacts with medications by blocking the CYP3A4 enzyme in the intestinal tract. This blockage stops the normal breakdown of certain drugs, causing them to build up to dangerous levels in the bloodstream.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Metformin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Grapefruit juice interacts with medications by blocking the CYP3A4 enzyme in the intestinal tract. This blockage stops the normal breakdown of certain drugs, causing them to build up to dangerous levels in the bloodstream.\n\nMetformin is a first-line medication for type 2 diabetes. The body does not rely on the CYP3A4 pathway to process or metabolize metformin, making it perfectly safe to consume with grapefruit juice without any known interaction.\n\n• Anti-cholesterol drugs: Many common statins (like atorvastatin, simvastatin, and lovastatin) heavily interact with grapefruit juice. High drug levels from this interaction can cause a severe muscle-wasting condition known as rhabdomyolysis.\n• Antidepressant/Psychiatric drugs: Several mood and antidepressant medications (such as sertraline, buspirone, and diazepam) are broken down by CYP3A4 and can reach toxic levels if mixed with grapefruit juice.\n• Nifedipine: This calcium channel blocker for high blood pressure is notorious for reacting with grapefruit juice. The interaction can dramatically spike blood drug levels, risking dangerously low blood pressure and rapid heart rates.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Antidepressant drugs, Anti-cholesterol drugs, Nifedipine"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following drugs are known to interact with grapefruit juice except::::', 'the following drugs are known to interact with grapefruit juice except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Grapefruit juice interacts with medications by blocking the CYP3A4 enzyme in the intestinal tract. This blockage stops the normal breakdown of certain drugs, causing them to build up to dangerous levels in the bloodstream.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Metformin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Grapefruit juice interacts with medications by blocking the CYP3A4 enzyme in the intestinal tract. This blockage stops the normal breakdown of certain drugs, causing them to build up to dangerous levels in the bloodstream.\n\nMetformin is a first-line medication for type 2 diabetes. The body does not rely on the CYP3A4 pathway to process or metabolize metformin, making it perfectly safe to consume with grapefruit juice without any known interaction.\n\n• Anti-cholesterol drugs: Many common statins (like atorvastatin, simvastatin, and lovastatin) heavily interact with grapefruit juice. High drug levels from this interaction can cause a severe muscle-wasting condition known as rhabdomyolysis.\n• Antidepressant/Psychiatric drugs: Several mood and antidepressant medications (such as sertraline, buspirone, and diazepam) are broken down by CYP3A4 and can reach toxic levels if mixed with grapefruit juice.\n• Nifedipine: This calcium channel blocker for high blood pressure is notorious for reacting with grapefruit juice. The interaction can dramatically spike blood drug levels, risking dangerously low blood pressure and rapid heart rates.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Antidepressant drugs, Anti-cholesterol drugs, Nifedipine"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are anthropometric measurements for determining nutritional status except;:::creatinine height index', 'the following are anthropometric measurements for determining nutritional status except;', 'Creatinine Height Index', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Anthropometric measurements are non-invasive, physical dimensions taken directly from the human body to evaluate size, shape, and muscle-to-fat composition.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Creatinine Height Index\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Anthropometric measurements are non-invasive, physical dimensions taken directly from the human body to evaluate size, shape, and muscle-to-fat composition.\n\nCreatinine Height Index is a biochemical (laboratory) test, not an anthropometric measurement. It requires measuring the 24-hour excretion of creatinine in a patient''s urine sample and comparing it against standard height markers to estimate total skeletal muscle depletion.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: % Body weight, Triceps Skinfold measurement, Mid Arm muscle Circumference"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:chronic malnutrition is a deficiency in:::', 'chronic malnutrition is a deficiency in', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Chronic energy deficiency forces the body into metabolic conservation by downregulating basal metabolic rate, reducing physical activity, and halting linear growth in pediatric populations. Prolonged caloric deficit causes the endocrine axis to suppress the growth hormone-insulin-like growth factor 1 (IGF-1) cascade, shunting minimal available energy toward vital organ function rather than tissue growth or maintenance.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Calories\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Chronic energy deficiency forces the body into metabolic conservation by downregulating basal metabolic rate, reducing physical activity, and halting linear growth in pediatric populations. Prolonged caloric deficit causes the endocrine axis to suppress the growth hormone-insulin-like growth factor 1 (IGF-1) cascade, shunting minimal available energy toward vital organ function rather than tissue growth or maintenance.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Protein, Calories and Protein, Marasmic Kwashiorkor"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a retrospective method of obtaining nutrient intake data, low level of respondent burden, can be self-administered, applicable to use for pediatric patients 8 years old and above::::food frequency questionnaire', 'a retrospective method of obtaining nutrient intake data, low level of respondent burden, can be self-administered, applicable to use for pediatric patients 8 years old and above:', 'food frequency questionnaire', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Food Frequency Questionnaire is a retrospective method because it asks individuals to look back and report their usual intake patterns over a specified past time frame (e.g., weeks, months, or the past year). It features a low respondent burden because it relies on a simple checklist of pre-defined foods rather than detailed weighing or precise logging. It is easily self-administered and has been clinically validated as appropriate and reliable for pediatric patients starting at 8 years of age and older who have developed the cognitive and reading skills to recall food frequencies independently.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"food frequency questionnaire\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Food Frequency Questionnaire is a retrospective method because it asks individuals to look back and report their usual intake patterns over a specified past time frame (e.g., weeks, months, or the past year). It features a low respondent burden because it relies on a simple checklist of pre-defined foods rather than detailed weighing or precise logging. It is easily self-administered and has been clinically validated as appropriate and reliable for pediatric patients starting at 8 years of age and older who have developed the cognitive and reading skills to recall food frequencies independently.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Direct food observation, 24 food recall, food record"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a retrospective method of obtaining nutrient intake data, low level of respondent burden, can be self-administered, applicable to use for pediatric patients 8 years old and above::::', 'a retrospective method of obtaining nutrient intake data, low level of respondent burden, can be self-administered, applicable to use for pediatric patients 8 years old and above:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Food Frequency Questionnaire is a retrospective method because it asks individuals to look back and report their usual intake patterns over a specified past time frame (e.g., weeks, months, or the past year). It features a low respondent burden because it relies on a simple checklist of pre-defined foods rather than detailed weighing or precise logging. It is easily self-administered and has been clinically validated as appropriate and reliable for pediatric patients starting at 8 years of age and older who have developed the cognitive and reading skills to recall food frequencies independently.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"food frequency questionnaire\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Food Frequency Questionnaire is a retrospective method because it asks individuals to look back and report their usual intake patterns over a specified past time frame (e.g., weeks, months, or the past year). It features a low respondent burden because it relies on a simple checklist of pre-defined foods rather than detailed weighing or precise logging. It is easily self-administered and has been clinically validated as appropriate and reliable for pediatric patients starting at 8 years of age and older who have developed the cognitive and reading skills to recall food frequencies independently.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Direct food observation, 24 food recall, food record"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb)
