('mc:ef, 90 year old female, admitted at the hospital with a diagnosis of irritable bowel syndrome, with a history of 5 episodes of loose bowel movement, height 5’ 2” weight 49.9 kg serum albumin 2.8 g/dl (normal level 3.5 – 5.0 g/dl). compute for the patient’s bmi, choose the best answer:::', 'ef, 90 year old female, admitted at the hospital with a diagnosis of irritable bowel syndrome, with a history of 5 episodes of loose bowel movement, height 5’ 2” weight 49.9 kg serum albumin 2.8 g/dl (normal level 3.5 – 5.0 g/dl). compute for the patient’s bmi, choose the best answer', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"To find the Body Mass Index (BMI), use the standard formula: BMI = Weight (kg) / Height (m²).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"BMI 20\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"To find the Body Mass Index (BMI), use the standard formula: BMI = Weight (kg) / Height (m²).\n\n1. Convert Height to Meters:\n\t• 5 feet 2 inches = 62 inches\n\t• 62 inches × 0.0254 = 1.575 meters\n2. Calculate BMI:\n• BMI = 49.9 / 1.575²\n• BMI = 49.9 / 2.48\n• BMI = 20.12 ~ 20 kg/m²","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: BMI 22, BMI 21, BMI 19"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:related to case study on # 59, patient ef latest cbc shows that hemoglobin 121 g/l, hematocrit 0.41, white blood cells (wbc) 12.6, lymphocytes 0.16%. compute for her total lymphocyte count:::2016', 'related to case study on # 59, patient ef latest cbc shows that hemoglobin 121 g/l, hematocrit 0.41, white blood cells (wbc) 12.6, lymphocytes 0.16%. compute for her total lymphocyte count', '2016', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"To compute the Total Lymphocyte Count (TLC), multiply the total White Blood Cell (WBC) count by the percentage of lymphocytes in the differential count:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"2016\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"To compute the Total Lymphocyte Count (TLC), multiply the total White Blood Cell (WBC) count by the percentage of lymphocytes in the differential count:\n\n1. Convert the WBC count: A WBC value of 12.6 in a standard CBC represents 12,600 cells/µL (or 12.6 times 10^9).\n2. Convert the percentage: The lymphocyte proportion is 16% (expressed as a decimal, 0.16).\n3. Calculate:\nTLC = 12,600 x 0.16 = 2,016 cells/µL","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 201.6, 016, 2017"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:related to case study on # 59, patient ef latest cbc shows that hemoglobin 121 g/l, hematocrit 0.41, white blood cells (wbc) 12.6, lymphocytes 0.16%. compute for her total lymphocyte count:::', 'related to case study on # 59, patient ef latest cbc shows that hemoglobin 121 g/l, hematocrit 0.41, white blood cells (wbc) 12.6, lymphocytes 0.16%. compute for her total lymphocyte count', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"To compute the Total Lymphocyte Count (TLC), multiply the total White Blood Cell (WBC) count by the percentage of lymphocytes in the differential count:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"2016\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"To compute the Total Lymphocyte Count (TLC), multiply the total White Blood Cell (WBC) count by the percentage of lymphocytes in the differential count:\n\n1. Convert the WBC count: A WBC value of 12.6 in a standard CBC represents 12,600 cells/µL (or 12.6 times 10^9).\n2. Convert the percentage: The lymphocyte proportion is 16% (expressed as a decimal, 0.16).\n3. Calculate:\nTLC = 12,600 x 0.16 = 2,016 cells/µL","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 201.6, 016, 2017"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following nutrition-focused physical examination (nfpe) findings reveals possible nutrition-related causes like deficiencies in iron, zinc, biotin, and protein?:::alopecia', 'which of the following nutrition-focused physical examination (nfpe) findings reveals possible nutrition-related causes like deficiencies in iron, zinc, biotin, and protein?', 'Alopecia', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In a Nutrition-Focused Physical Examination (NFPE), specialized hair changes serve as excellent indicators of specific micronutrient and macronutrient depletion.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Alopecia\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In a Nutrition-Focused Physical Examination (NFPE), specialized hair changes serve as excellent indicators of specific micronutrient and macronutrient depletion.\n\nAlopecia describes thin, sparse, patchy, or significant hair loss. Healthy hair follicle replication depends heavily on rapid cellular division. A deficiency in iron, zinc, biotin, or protein halts this process, causing follicles to enter a premature shedding phase, leading directly to thinning and hair loss.\n\n\t• Flag sign: Characterized by alternating bands of dark and light pigment, this specific change is caused purely by intermittent protein-calorie malnutrition (Kwashiorkor) affecting melanin synthesis.\n\t• Lanugo: Fine, soft, downy body hair that develops as an evolutionary response to severe overall calorie starvation (commonly seen in anorexia nervosa) to help conserve body heat.\n\t• Corkscrew hair: Coiled, tightly wound, swan-necked hairs are a unique structural marker caused exclusively by Vitamin C deficiency (Scurvy).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Lanugo, Flag sign (alternating banding of dark and light colors in hair), Corkscrew hair"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following nutrition-focused physical examination (nfpe) findings reveals possible nutrition-related causes like deficiencies in iron, zinc, biotin, and protein?:::', 'which of the following nutrition-focused physical examination (nfpe) findings reveals possible nutrition-related causes like deficiencies in iron, zinc, biotin, and protein?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In a Nutrition-Focused Physical Examination (NFPE), specialized hair changes serve as excellent indicators of specific micronutrient and macronutrient depletion.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Alopecia\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In a Nutrition-Focused Physical Examination (NFPE), specialized hair changes serve as excellent indicators of specific micronutrient and macronutrient depletion.\n\nAlopecia describes thin, sparse, patchy, or significant hair loss. Healthy hair follicle replication depends heavily on rapid cellular division. A deficiency in iron, zinc, biotin, or protein halts this process, causing follicles to enter a premature shedding phase, leading directly to thinning and hair loss.\n\n\t• Flag sign: Characterized by alternating bands of dark and light pigment, this specific change is caused purely by intermittent protein-calorie malnutrition (Kwashiorkor) affecting melanin synthesis.\n\t• Lanugo: Fine, soft, downy body hair that develops as an evolutionary response to severe overall calorie starvation (commonly seen in anorexia nervosa) to help conserve body heat.\n\t• Corkscrew hair: Coiled, tightly wound, swan-necked hairs are a unique structural marker caused exclusively by Vitamin C deficiency (Scurvy).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Lanugo, Flag sign (alternating banding of dark and light colors in hair), Corkscrew hair"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:in nfpe, the following determines fluid accumulation in the body except:::skin', 'in nfpe, the following determines fluid accumulation in the body except', 'Skin', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Under standard AND/ASPEN guidelines for a Nutrition-Focused Physical Exam (NFPE), fluid status is split into different clinical assessment categories:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Skin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Under standard AND/ASPEN guidelines for a Nutrition-Focused Physical Exam (NFPE), fluid status is split into different clinical assessment categories:\n\n1. Fluid Accumulation (Edema): When checking specifically for fluid accumulation to satisfy malnutrition criteria, a dietitian looks at distinct, localized anatomical sites where fluid settles due to gravity or pressure. This includes the Lower Body Extremities (ankles, feet, and shin/tibia) for walking patients, the Sacral region (lower back) for bedridden patients, and sometimes the Upper body (e.g., puffiness or fluid retention around the eyelids, face, or hands in generalized anasarca).\n\n2. Skin: While you physically touch the skin during the exam, the skin itself as a category is examined under its own specific domains in an NFPE:\n\t• Hydration Status: Skin turgor (pinching the skin to check for tenting) determines dehydration, not edema/fluid accumulation.\n\t• Micronutrient Deficiencies: The skin is inspected for clinical signs of vitamin or mineral issues, such as scaling (essential fatty acids), petechiae/bruising (Vitamin C), or pallor (Iron/Folate).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Upper body, Lower Body Extremities, Sacral"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:in nfpe, the following determines fluid accumulation in the body except:::', 'in nfpe, the following determines fluid accumulation in the body except', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Under standard AND/ASPEN guidelines for a Nutrition-Focused Physical Exam (NFPE), fluid status is split into different clinical assessment categories:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Skin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Under standard AND/ASPEN guidelines for a Nutrition-Focused Physical Exam (NFPE), fluid status is split into different clinical assessment categories:\n\n1. Fluid Accumulation (Edema): When checking specifically for fluid accumulation to satisfy malnutrition criteria, a dietitian looks at distinct, localized anatomical sites where fluid settles due to gravity or pressure. This includes the Lower Body Extremities (ankles, feet, and shin/tibia) for walking patients, the Sacral region (lower back) for bedridden patients, and sometimes the Upper body (e.g., puffiness or fluid retention around the eyelids, face, or hands in generalized anasarca).\n\n2. Skin: While you physically touch the skin during the exam, the skin itself as a category is examined under its own specific domains in an NFPE:\n\t• Hydration Status: Skin turgor (pinching the skin to check for tenting) determines dehydration, not edema/fluid accumulation.\n\t• Micronutrient Deficiencies: The skin is inspected for clinical signs of vitamin or mineral issues, such as scaling (essential fatty acids), petechiae/bruising (Vitamin C), or pallor (Iron/Folate).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Upper body, Lower Body Extremities, Sacral"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is a gluten free diet:::camote pudding, puto, sago gulaman', 'which of the following is a gluten free diet', 'Camote pudding, Puto, Sago Gulaman', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A gluten-free diet strictly excludes the protein gluten, which is found natively in grains like wheat, barley, and rye.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Camote pudding, Puto, Sago Gulaman\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A gluten-free diet strictly excludes the protein gluten, which is found natively in grains like wheat, barley, and rye.\n\nThe following food items consist of traditional Filipino dishes made from naturally gluten-free root crops and starches:\n\t• Camote pudding uses sweet potato (camote), a root crop.\n\t• Puto is traditionally made using fermented ground rice flour (galapong).\n\t• Sago Gulaman relies on sago pearls (derived from tropical palm stems) and gulaman (agar seaweed jelly), which contain no wheat or grains.\n\n\t• Arrowroot itself is gluten-free, but commercial baking of arrowroot biscuits almost always incorporates standard wheat flour to form the structural dough texture.\n\t• Couscous is not a whole grain; it is small granules made from durum wheat semolina, meaning it is very high in gluten.\n\t• Muesli is a breakfast mix built primarily around raw, standard rolled oats, which are frequently heavily cross-contaminated with wheat during commercial processing unless certified gluten-free.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Muesli with yogurt, nuts, dried fruits, Scrambled Egg and Couscous, Custard, Arrow root biscuit"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is a gluten free diet:::', 'which of the following is a gluten free diet', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A gluten-free diet strictly excludes the protein gluten, which is found natively in grains like wheat, barley, and rye.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Camote pudding, Puto, Sago Gulaman\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A gluten-free diet strictly excludes the protein gluten, which is found natively in grains like wheat, barley, and rye.\n\nThe following food items consist of traditional Filipino dishes made from naturally gluten-free root crops and starches:\n\t• Camote pudding uses sweet potato (camote), a root crop.\n\t• Puto is traditionally made using fermented ground rice flour (galapong).\n\t• Sago Gulaman relies on sago pearls (derived from tropical palm stems) and gulaman (agar seaweed jelly), which contain no wheat or grains.\n\n\t• Arrowroot itself is gluten-free, but commercial baking of arrowroot biscuits almost always incorporates standard wheat flour to form the structural dough texture.\n\t• Couscous is not a whole grain; it is small granules made from durum wheat semolina, meaning it is very high in gluten.\n\t• Muesli is a breakfast mix built primarily around raw, standard rolled oats, which are frequently heavily cross-contaminated with wheat during commercial processing unless certified gluten-free.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Muesli with yogurt, nuts, dried fruits, Scrambled Egg and Couscous, Custard, Arrow root biscuit"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following foods contain tartrazine except:::coconut jam', 'the following foods contain tartrazine except', 'Coconut jam', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Tartrazine (also known as FD&C Yellow No. 5 or E102) is a widely used synthetic, lemon-yellow azo food dye derived from petroleum or coal tar. It is added to commercial foods to provide a bright yellow or green color, or to help create creamy, blended under-shades.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Coconut jam\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Tartrazine (also known as FD&C Yellow No. 5 or E102) is a widely used synthetic, lemon-yellow azo food dye derived from petroleum or coal tar. It is added to commercial foods to provide a bright yellow or green color, or to help create creamy, blended under-shades.\n\nUnder formal industrial food-identity standards, commercial coconut jam is strictly defined as an emulsion made from coconut milk, sugar, and starch. It is legally categorized as a product that may or may not contain added natural colorings (like pandan leaf juice, annatto, or chlorophyllin) or caramel. Because its natural color profile is white-to-brownish or green (if pandan is used), it does not require synthetic yellow dye formulation. It is therefore categorized by examiners as the primary inherently tartrazine-free food group.\n\nIn contrast, mass-manufactured yellow/green colored marshmallows, translucent flavored carbonated beverages (such as yellow energy drinks or citrus sodas), and even commercial baking components like rainbow/colored chocolate chips routinely utilize synthetic FD&C Yellow No. 5 (Tartrazine) to hold stable artificial pigmentation under processing heat and shelf storage.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Chocolate chips, Flavored carbonated beverages, Colored Marshmallow"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following foods contain tartrazine except:::', 'the following foods contain tartrazine except', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Tartrazine (also known as FD&C Yellow No. 5 or E102) is a widely used synthetic, lemon-yellow azo food dye derived from petroleum or coal tar. It is added to commercial foods to provide a bright yellow or green color, or to help create creamy, blended under-shades.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Coconut jam\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Tartrazine (also known as FD&C Yellow No. 5 or E102) is a widely used synthetic, lemon-yellow azo food dye derived from petroleum or coal tar. It is added to commercial foods to provide a bright yellow or green color, or to help create creamy, blended under-shades.\n\nUnder formal industrial food-identity standards, commercial coconut jam is strictly defined as an emulsion made from coconut milk, sugar, and starch. It is legally categorized as a product that may or may not contain added natural colorings (like pandan leaf juice, annatto, or chlorophyllin) or caramel. Because its natural color profile is white-to-brownish or green (if pandan is used), it does not require synthetic yellow dye formulation. It is therefore categorized by examiners as the primary inherently tartrazine-free food group.\n\nIn contrast, mass-manufactured yellow/green colored marshmallows, translucent flavored carbonated beverages (such as yellow energy drinks or citrus sodas), and even commercial baking components like rainbow/colored chocolate chips routinely utilize synthetic FD&C Yellow No. 5 (Tartrazine) to hold stable artificial pigmentation under processing heat and shelf storage.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Chocolate chips, Flavored carbonated beverages, Colored Marshmallow"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:in a common bacterium inhabiting the gastrointestinal area around the pyloric valve and is a major determinant of chronic acute gastritis is:::helicobactor pylori', 'in a common bacterium inhabiting the gastrointestinal area around the pyloric valve and is a major determinant of chronic acute gastritis is', 'Helicobactor Pylori', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Helicobacter pylori is a spiral-shaped, Gram-negative bacterium that colonizes the protective mucous lining of the human stomach, specifically targeting the pyloric antrum area near the pyloric valve. It produces an enzyme called urease, which neutralizes stomach acid and allows it to survive in the harsh environment. H. pylori infection is the leading, globally recognized cause of chronic and acute gastritis, as well as peptic ulcer disease and gastric cancer.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Helicobactor Pylori\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Helicobacter pylori is a spiral-shaped, Gram-negative bacterium that colonizes the protective mucous lining of the human stomach, specifically targeting the pyloric antrum area near the pyloric valve. It produces an enzyme called urease, which neutralizes stomach acid and allows it to survive in the harsh environment. H. pylori infection is the leading, globally recognized cause of chronic and acute gastritis, as well as peptic ulcer disease and gastric cancer.\n\n\t• Helicobacter felis and Helicobacter salmonis: These are different species of Helicobacter that primarily infect animals (cats/dogs and fish, respectively) rather than humans.\n\t• Clostridium difficile: This bacterium inhabits the large intestine (colon), not the stomach, and causes severe antibiotic-associated diarrhea and pseudomembranous colitis.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Clostridium difficile, Helicobator Felis, H. Salmonis"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:in a common bacterium inhabiting the gastrointestinal area around the pyloric valve and is a major determinant of chronic acute gastritis is:::', 'in a common bacterium inhabiting the gastrointestinal area around the pyloric valve and is a major determinant of chronic acute gastritis is', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Helicobacter pylori is a spiral-shaped, Gram-negative bacterium that colonizes the protective mucous lining of the human stomach, specifically targeting the pyloric antrum area near the pyloric valve. It produces an enzyme called urease, which neutralizes stomach acid and allows it to survive in the harsh environment. H. pylori infection is the leading, globally recognized cause of chronic and acute gastritis, as well as peptic ulcer disease and gastric cancer.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Helicobactor Pylori\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Helicobacter pylori is a spiral-shaped, Gram-negative bacterium that colonizes the protective mucous lining of the human stomach, specifically targeting the pyloric antrum area near the pyloric valve. It produces an enzyme called urease, which neutralizes stomach acid and allows it to survive in the harsh environment. H. pylori infection is the leading, globally recognized cause of chronic and acute gastritis, as well as peptic ulcer disease and gastric cancer.\n\n\t• Helicobacter felis and Helicobacter salmonis: These are different species of Helicobacter that primarily infect animals (cats/dogs and fish, respectively) rather than humans.\n\t• Clostridium difficile: This bacterium inhabits the large intestine (colon), not the stomach, and causes severe antibiotic-associated diarrhea and pseudomembranous colitis.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Clostridium difficile, Helicobator Felis, H. Salmonis"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:for eradication of h. pylori in the gastrointestinal area, in how many weeks of treatment with an acid suppressing drug and antibiotics will it be:::two weeks', 'for eradication of h. pylori in the gastrointestinal area, in how many weeks of treatment with an acid suppressing drug and antibiotics will it be', 'Two weeks', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The standard first-line treatment for the eradication of Helicobacter pylori involves a combination therapy protocol pairing an acid-suppressing drug (like a Proton Pump Inhibitor) with antibiotics to eliminate the bacteria.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Two weeks\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The standard first-line treatment for the eradication of Helicobacter pylori involves a combination therapy protocol pairing an acid-suppressing drug (like a Proton Pump Inhibitor) with antibiotics to eliminate the bacteria.\n\nClinical guidelines generally establish that a duration of treatment corresponding to two weeks provides an effective balance for bacterial eradication rates while managing side effects and antibiotic resistance.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 20 days, Three weeks, 25 days"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:for eradication of h. pylori in the gastrointestinal area, in how many weeks of treatment with an acid suppressing drug and antibiotics will it be:::', 'for eradication of h. pylori in the gastrointestinal area, in how many weeks of treatment with an acid suppressing drug and antibiotics will it be', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The standard first-line treatment for the eradication of Helicobacter pylori involves a combination therapy protocol pairing an acid-suppressing drug (like a Proton Pump Inhibitor) with antibiotics to eliminate the bacteria.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Two weeks\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The standard first-line treatment for the eradication of Helicobacter pylori involves a combination therapy protocol pairing an acid-suppressing drug (like a Proton Pump Inhibitor) with antibiotics to eliminate the bacteria.\n\nClinical guidelines generally establish that a duration of treatment corresponding to two weeks provides an effective balance for bacterial eradication rates while managing side effects and antibiotic resistance.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 20 days, Three weeks, 25 days"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following medications are classified as histamine h2 receptor antagonists (h2 blockers) commonly used to treat peptic ulcers, except::::cetirizine', 'the following medications are classified as histamine h2 receptor antagonists (h2 blockers) commonly used to treat peptic ulcers, except:', 'Cetirizine', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"While cetirizine is an antihistamine, it is a selective H1 blocker. H1 receptors are located in the respiratory tract, blood vessels, and immune cells. Therefore, cetirizine is used exclusively to treat allergy symptoms (like hives, runny nose, and itching) and has absolutely zero effect on stomach acid secretion or peptic ulcer healing.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Cetirizine\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"While cetirizine is an antihistamine, it is a selective H1 blocker. H1 receptors are located in the respiratory tract, blood vessels, and immune cells. Therefore, cetirizine is used exclusively to treat allergy symptoms (like hives, runny nose, and itching) and has absolutely zero effect on stomach acid secretion or peptic ulcer healing.\n\nRanitidine and Cimetidine are classic H2 blockers that block the histamine receptors in the stomach lining to reduce gastric acid production. While Omeprazole is a Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI) rather than an H2 blocker, it is a primary drug used to treat peptic ulcers, making Cetirizine the absolute standout exception because it has no role in gastrointestinal therapy whatsoever.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Cimetidine, Omeprazole, Ranitidine"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following medications are classified as histamine h2 receptor antagonists (h2 blockers) commonly used to treat peptic ulcers, except::::', 'the following medications are classified as histamine h2 receptor antagonists (h2 blockers) commonly used to treat peptic ulcers, except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"While cetirizine is an antihistamine, it is a selective H1 blocker. H1 receptors are located in the respiratory tract, blood vessels, and immune cells. Therefore, cetirizine is used exclusively to treat allergy symptoms (like hives, runny nose, and itching) and has absolutely zero effect on stomach acid secretion or peptic ulcer healing.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Cetirizine\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"While cetirizine is an antihistamine, it is a selective H1 blocker. H1 receptors are located in the respiratory tract, blood vessels, and immune cells. Therefore, cetirizine is used exclusively to treat allergy symptoms (like hives, runny nose, and itching) and has absolutely zero effect on stomach acid secretion or peptic ulcer healing.\n\nRanitidine and Cimetidine are classic H2 blockers that block the histamine receptors in the stomach lining to reduce gastric acid production. While Omeprazole is a Proton Pump Inhibitor (PPI) rather than an H2 blocker, it is a primary drug used to treat peptic ulcers, making Cetirizine the absolute standout exception because it has no role in gastrointestinal therapy whatsoever.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Cimetidine, Omeprazole, Ranitidine"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:in nutrition management of radiation enteritis, we provide the following interventions includes the following but not:::avoid oxalate containing foods', 'in nutrition management of radiation enteritis, we provide the following interventions includes the following but not', 'Avoid oxalate containing foods', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Restricting dietary oxalates (found in spinach, beets, chocolate, and nuts) is a specific therapeutic intervention used to manage conditions like Calcium Oxalate Kidney Stones or severe fat malabsorption in short bowel syndrome to prevent kidney stones. It is not a standard, primary nutritional intervention for managing acute radiation enteritis damage.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Avoid oxalate containing foods\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Restricting dietary oxalates (found in spinach, beets, chocolate, and nuts) is a specific therapeutic intervention used to manage conditions like Calcium Oxalate Kidney Stones or severe fat malabsorption in short bowel syndrome to prevent kidney stones. It is not a standard, primary nutritional intervention for managing acute radiation enteritis damage.\n\nTrue Interventions\n\t• Small frequent feedings: This is essential because it reduces the absolute volume workload on the blunted, inflamed intestinal villi at any single time, preventing vomiting, severe dumping, and cramping.\n\t• MCT for better absorption: Medium-Chain Triglycerides (MCTs) can be directly absorbed by the mucosal cells without requiring bile salts or complex micelle pancreatic breakdown, which is ideal because the intestines lose standard fat absorption capacity during radiation damage.\n\t• Folate supplementation: Rapidly dividing mucosal cells require intense folate synthesis for DNA repair and healing. Because radiation enteritis compromises the intestinal segments where folate is absorbed, targeted supplementation is highly indicated to prevent deficiency and aid epithelial recovery.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Small frequent feeding, Folate supplementation, MCT for better absorption"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:in nutrition management of radiation enteritis, we provide the following interventions includes the following but not:::', 'in nutrition management of radiation enteritis, we provide the following interventions includes the following but not', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Restricting dietary oxalates (found in spinach, beets, chocolate, and nuts) is a specific therapeutic intervention used to manage conditions like Calcium Oxalate Kidney Stones or severe fat malabsorption in short bowel syndrome to prevent kidney stones. It is not a standard, primary nutritional intervention for managing acute radiation enteritis damage.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Avoid oxalate containing foods\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Restricting dietary oxalates (found in spinach, beets, chocolate, and nuts) is a specific therapeutic intervention used to manage conditions like Calcium Oxalate Kidney Stones or severe fat malabsorption in short bowel syndrome to prevent kidney stones. It is not a standard, primary nutritional intervention for managing acute radiation enteritis damage.\n\nTrue Interventions\n\t• Small frequent feedings: This is essential because it reduces the absolute volume workload on the blunted, inflamed intestinal villi at any single time, preventing vomiting, severe dumping, and cramping.\n\t• MCT for better absorption: Medium-Chain Triglycerides (MCTs) can be directly absorbed by the mucosal cells without requiring bile salts or complex micelle pancreatic breakdown, which is ideal because the intestines lose standard fat absorption capacity during radiation damage.\n\t• Folate supplementation: Rapidly dividing mucosal cells require intense folate synthesis for DNA repair and healing. Because radiation enteritis compromises the intestinal segments where folate is absorbed, targeted supplementation is highly indicated to prevent deficiency and aid epithelial recovery.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Small frequent feeding, Folate supplementation, MCT for better absorption"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:cathartics are substances that accelerate defecation, while laxatives loosen stools and increase bowel movements, which does not belong to this group of catartics:::methyl cellulose', 'cathartics are substances that accelerate defecation, while laxatives loosen stools and increase bowel movements, which does not belong to this group of catartics', 'Methyl Cellulose', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• Osmotic / Saline Agents: Lactulose, Magnesium Sulfate, and Magnesium Hydroxide all function by the same primary mechanism. They act as osmotic (or saline) laxatives. They are poorly absorbed in the intestinal tract and exert osmotic pressure to draw water into the lumen, softening the stool and promoting rapid evacuation.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Methyl Cellulose\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• Osmotic / Saline Agents: Lactulose, Magnesium Sulfate, and Magnesium Hydroxide all function by the same primary mechanism. They act as osmotic (or saline) laxatives. They are poorly absorbed in the intestinal tract and exert osmotic pressure to draw water into the lumen, softening the stool and promoting rapid evacuation.\n\n• Bulk-Forming Agent: Methyl Cellulose operates under a completely different mechanism. It is a bulk-forming agent composed of non-digestible cellulose fibers. It absorbs water inside the bowel to swell, mechanically expanding the fecal mass to naturally stimulate peristalsis.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Magnesium Hydroxide, Magnesium Sulfate, Lactulose"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:cathartics are substances that accelerate defecation, while laxatives loosen stools and increase bowel movements, which does not belong to this group of catartics:::', 'cathartics are substances that accelerate defecation, while laxatives loosen stools and increase bowel movements, which does not belong to this group of catartics', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• Osmotic / Saline Agents: Lactulose, Magnesium Sulfate, and Magnesium Hydroxide all function by the same primary mechanism. They act as osmotic (or saline) laxatives. They are poorly absorbed in the intestinal tract and exert osmotic pressure to draw water into the lumen, softening the stool and promoting rapid evacuation.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Methyl Cellulose\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• Osmotic / Saline Agents: Lactulose, Magnesium Sulfate, and Magnesium Hydroxide all function by the same primary mechanism. They act as osmotic (or saline) laxatives. They are poorly absorbed in the intestinal tract and exert osmotic pressure to draw water into the lumen, softening the stool and promoting rapid evacuation.\n\n• Bulk-Forming Agent: Methyl Cellulose operates under a completely different mechanism. It is a bulk-forming agent composed of non-digestible cellulose fibers. It absorbs water inside the bowel to swell, mechanically expanding the fecal mass to naturally stimulate peristalsis.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Magnesium Hydroxide, Magnesium Sulfate, Lactulose"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is not true in a low residue diet?:::rolled oats, soft boiled egg, gelatin', 'which of the following is not true in a low residue diet?', 'Rolled Oats, Soft Boiled Egg, gelatin', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Oatmeal and rolled oats are whole grains that are naturally very high in fiber and roughage. Whole grains leave behind a high amount of undigested material (residue) in the colon, which directly violates the purpose of a low-residue diet.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Rolled Oats, Soft Boiled Egg, gelatin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Oatmeal and rolled oats are whole grains that are naturally very high in fiber and roughage. Whole grains leave behind a high amount of undigested material (residue) in the colon, which directly violates the purpose of a low-residue diet.\n\n\t• Baked macaroni (made from refined white flour) and angel food cake (a low-fat, plain sponge cake) are classic low-residue staples.\n\t• Canned or cooked fruits and vegetables without skins or seeds (like peaches in syrup and cooked cucumber) along with eggs are completely allowed.\n\t• Sports drinks like Gatorade, electrolyte (ionized) drinks, and plain clear water are excellent for maintaining hydration on this diet and leave zero solid residue behind.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Peaches in light syrup, cooked cucumber, poached egg,, Ionized drink, Gatorade, Blu water, Baked Macaroni, angel food cake"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is not true in a low residue diet?:::', 'which of the following is not true in a low residue diet?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Oatmeal and rolled oats are whole grains that are naturally very high in fiber and roughage. Whole grains leave behind a high amount of undigested material (residue) in the colon, which directly violates the purpose of a low-residue diet.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Rolled Oats, Soft Boiled Egg, gelatin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Oatmeal and rolled oats are whole grains that are naturally very high in fiber and roughage. Whole grains leave behind a high amount of undigested material (residue) in the colon, which directly violates the purpose of a low-residue diet.\n\n\t• Baked macaroni (made from refined white flour) and angel food cake (a low-fat, plain sponge cake) are classic low-residue staples.\n\t• Canned or cooked fruits and vegetables without skins or seeds (like peaches in syrup and cooked cucumber) along with eggs are completely allowed.\n\t• Sports drinks like Gatorade, electrolyte (ionized) drinks, and plain clear water are excellent for maintaining hydration on this diet and leave zero solid residue behind.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Peaches in light syrup, cooked cucumber, poached egg,, Ionized drink, Gatorade, Blu water, Baked Macaroni, angel food cake"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is true in a soft diet::::', 'which of the following is true in a soft diet:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Chicken Nilaga features chicken meat that has been boiled for a long time until its connective tissues completely break down, making it extremely tender and effortless to chew. Cooked potatoes and soft asparagus tips (unlike the tough, fibrous lower stalks) are highly digestible, standard soft-diet vegetables. Cathedral Windows is a traditional Filipino gelatin dessert made of soft, multi-colored agar blocks set in a light, creamy gelatin base. Because gelatin completely melts at body temperature, it is a quintessential soft and light-to-digest menu item.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Chicken Nilaga with Asparagus tips and Potato, Cathedral Windows\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Chicken Nilaga features chicken meat that has been boiled for a long time until its connective tissues completely break down, making it extremely tender and effortless to chew. Cooked potatoes and soft asparagus tips (unlike the tough, fibrous lower stalks) are highly digestible, standard soft-diet vegetables. Cathedral Windows is a traditional Filipino gelatin dessert made of soft, multi-colored agar blocks set in a light, creamy gelatin base. Because gelatin completely melts at body temperature, it is a quintessential soft and light-to-digest menu item.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Cooked Asparagus tips, Beef Steak with Onion Rings, Fruit Salad, Pork Sinigang with Kangkong and Radish, Angel Cake, Fish Escabeche with carrots and sayote, Cooked Jackfruit in Light Syrup"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the semi liquid, partially digested food that travels through the intestinal tract is called::::chyme', 'the semi liquid, partially digested food that travels through the intestinal tract is called:', 'Chyme', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Chyme is the medical term used to describe the thick, semi-liquid mass of partially digested food, water, and acidic gastric juices that moves through the gastrointestinal tract.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Chyme\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Chyme is the medical term used to describe the thick, semi-liquid mass of partially digested food, water, and acidic gastric juices that moves through the gastrointestinal tract.\n\nAs you eat, chewed food travels down to the stomach (where it is initially called a bolus). The stomach''s muscular walls dynamically churn and pulverize this food while mixing it with highly acidic hydrochloric acid and enzymes. This chemical and mechanical breakdown liquifies the food into chyme, which is then slowly released through the pyloric valve into the intestines for further digestion and nutrient absorption.\n\n\t• Lymph: A clear fluid containing white blood cells that circulates through the lymphatic immune system.\n\t• Secretin: A hormone secreted by the small intestine that triggers the pancreas to release bicarbonate to neutralize the acidic chyme.\n\t• Bile: A digestive fluid produced by the liver that emulsifies fats in the small intestine but is not a food mass itself.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Bile, lymph, secretin"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the semi liquid, partially digested food that travels through the intestinal tract is called::::', 'the semi liquid, partially digested food that travels through the intestinal tract is called:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Chyme is the medical term used to describe the thick, semi-liquid mass of partially digested food, water, and acidic gastric juices that moves through the gastrointestinal tract.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Chyme\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Chyme is the medical term used to describe the thick, semi-liquid mass of partially digested food, water, and acidic gastric juices that moves through the gastrointestinal tract.\n\nAs you eat, chewed food travels down to the stomach (where it is initially called a bolus). The stomach''s muscular walls dynamically churn and pulverize this food while mixing it with highly acidic hydrochloric acid and enzymes. This chemical and mechanical breakdown liquifies the food into chyme, which is then slowly released through the pyloric valve into the intestines for further digestion and nutrient absorption.\n\n\t• Lymph: A clear fluid containing white blood cells that circulates through the lymphatic immune system.\n\t• Secretin: A hormone secreted by the small intestine that triggers the pancreas to release bicarbonate to neutralize the acidic chyme.\n\t• Bile: A digestive fluid produced by the liver that emulsifies fats in the small intestine but is not a food mass itself.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Bile, lymph, secretin"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:all blood leaving the gi tract travels first to the::::liver', 'all blood leaving the gi tract travels first to the:', 'Liver', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"All blood that flows through the stomach, small intestine, and large intestine picks up absorbed nutrients, water, and toxins. Instead of going straight back to the heart, this nutrient-rich blood is collected into a specialized network of veins called the hepatic portal system.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Liver\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"All blood that flows through the stomach, small intestine, and large intestine picks up absorbed nutrients, water, and toxins. Instead of going straight back to the heart, this nutrient-rich blood is collected into a specialized network of veins called the hepatic portal system.\n\nThe portal system routes 100% of this blood directly into the liver first. The liver acts as the body''s primary chemical processing plant—it metabolizes and stores the absorbed nutrients, removes waste products, and neutralizes dangerous drugs or toxins before the blood is safely allowed to return to the heart and circulate throughout the rest of the body.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: heart, kidneys, Pancreas"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:all blood leaving the gi tract travels first to the::::', 'all blood leaving the gi tract travels first to the:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"All blood that flows through the stomach, small intestine, and large intestine picks up absorbed nutrients, water, and toxins. Instead of going straight back to the heart, this nutrient-rich blood is collected into a specialized network of veins called the hepatic portal system.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Liver\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"All blood that flows through the stomach, small intestine, and large intestine picks up absorbed nutrients, water, and toxins. Instead of going straight back to the heart, this nutrient-rich blood is collected into a specialized network of veins called the hepatic portal system.\n\nThe portal system routes 100% of this blood directly into the liver first. The liver acts as the body''s primary chemical processing plant—it metabolizes and stores the absorbed nutrients, removes waste products, and neutralizes dangerous drugs or toxins before the blood is safely allowed to return to the heart and circulate throughout the rest of the body.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: heart, kidneys, Pancreas"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which nutrients leave the gastrointestinal tract by way of the lymphatic system::::fats and fat soluble vitamins', 'which nutrients leave the gastrointestinal tract by way of the lymphatic system:', 'fats and fat soluble vitamins', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"While water-soluble nutrients (like simple sugars, amino acids, water-soluble vitamins, and minerals) are absorbed directly into the bloodstream via the hepatic portal vein, large lipid molecules cannot dissolve in blood:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"fats and fat soluble vitamins\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"While water-soluble nutrients (like simple sugars, amino acids, water-soluble vitamins, and minerals) are absorbed directly into the bloodstream via the hepatic portal vein, large lipid molecules cannot dissolve in blood:\n\nProducts of fat digestion (monoglycerides and long-chain fatty acids) are reassembled inside intestinal cells into transport packets called chylomylomicrons. These large structures, along with fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), are too large to pass through the tight walls of regular blood capillaries. Instead, they enter specialized lymphatic vessels in the intestinal villi called lacteals.\n\nThe lymphatic system transports these fats through the thoracic duct, emptying them safely into the bloodstream near the heart, completely bypassing the liver initially.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: water and minerals, proteins and minerals, all vitamins and minerals"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which nutrients leave the gastrointestinal tract by way of the lymphatic system::::', 'which nutrients leave the gastrointestinal tract by way of the lymphatic system:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"While water-soluble nutrients (like simple sugars, amino acids, water-soluble vitamins, and minerals) are absorbed directly into the bloodstream via the hepatic portal vein, large lipid molecules cannot dissolve in blood:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"fats and fat soluble vitamins\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"While water-soluble nutrients (like simple sugars, amino acids, water-soluble vitamins, and minerals) are absorbed directly into the bloodstream via the hepatic portal vein, large lipid molecules cannot dissolve in blood:\n\nProducts of fat digestion (monoglycerides and long-chain fatty acids) are reassembled inside intestinal cells into transport packets called chylomylomicrons. These large structures, along with fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), are too large to pass through the tight walls of regular blood capillaries. Instead, they enter specialized lymphatic vessels in the intestinal villi called lacteals.\n\nThe lymphatic system transports these fats through the thoracic duct, emptying them safely into the bloodstream near the heart, completely bypassing the liver initially.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: water and minerals, proteins and minerals, all vitamins and minerals"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. gelatin is soluble in cold water sentence b. gelatin is immediately poured in boiling water during cooking.:::', 'sentence a. gelatin is soluble in cold water sentence b. gelatin is immediately poured in boiling water during cooking.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Gelatin absorbs cold water and blooms but is not truly dissolved simply by cold water. It should also not be immediately boiled; excessive heat can reduce its gelling strength.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Both sentences are incorrect\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Gelatin absorbs cold water and blooms but is not truly dissolved simply by cold water. It should also not be immediately boiled; excessive heat can reduce its gelling strength.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence A is correct, Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are correct"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:digestion and absorption are coordinated by the::::hormonal system and the nervous system', 'digestion and absorption are coordinated by the:', 'hormonal system and the nervous system', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The complex processes of digestion and absorption require precise coordination to ensure that digestive juices are secreted only when food is present. This homeostatic regulation is managed by two master systems working in tandem:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"hormonal system and the nervous system\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The complex processes of digestion and absorption require precise coordination to ensure that digestive juices are secreted only when food is present. This homeostatic regulation is managed by two master systems working in tandem:\n\n• The Hormonal (Endocrine) System: Secretes chemical messengers directly into the bloodstream to regulate digestive functions. For example, the hormone gastrin stimulates the stomach to release acid, while secretin and cholecystokinin (CCK) signal the pancreas and gallbladder to release digestive enzymes and bile when food enters the small intestine.\n• The Nervous System: Coordinates digestion through the enteric nervous system (the gut''s built-in neural network) and the autonomic nervous system. It regulates the mechanical churning of the stomach, triggers peristalsis to move food along the tract, and responds to the sight, smell, or presence of food by stimulating salivary and gastric secretions.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: pancreas and kidneys, liver and gallbladder, vascular system and the lymphatic system"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:digestion and absorption are coordinated by the::::', 'digestion and absorption are coordinated by the:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The complex processes of digestion and absorption require precise coordination to ensure that digestive juices are secreted only when food is present. This homeostatic regulation is managed by two master systems working in tandem:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"hormonal system and the nervous system\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The complex processes of digestion and absorption require precise coordination to ensure that digestive juices are secreted only when food is present. This homeostatic regulation is managed by two master systems working in tandem:\n\n• The Hormonal (Endocrine) System: Secretes chemical messengers directly into the bloodstream to regulate digestive functions. For example, the hormone gastrin stimulates the stomach to release acid, while secretin and cholecystokinin (CCK) signal the pancreas and gallbladder to release digestive enzymes and bile when food enters the small intestine.\n• The Nervous System: Coordinates digestion through the enteric nervous system (the gut''s built-in neural network) and the autonomic nervous system. It regulates the mechanical churning of the stomach, triggers peristalsis to move food along the tract, and responds to the sight, smell, or presence of food by stimulating salivary and gastric secretions.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: pancreas and kidneys, liver and gallbladder, vascular system and the lymphatic system"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb)
