('mc:it is a prediction of food needs for a day or other specific period of time.:::forecasting', 'it is a prediction of food needs for a day or other specific period of time.', 'Forecasting', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In foodservice and operations management, forecasting is defined as the process of predicting or estimating future food needs for a day or another specific period of time. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Forecasting\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In foodservice and operations management, forecasting is defined as the process of predicting or estimating future food needs for a day or another specific period of time. \n\nIt serves as a data-driven tool to ensure the purchasing department orders the correct volume of ingredients and the kitchen staff prepares the right amount of food, which directly minimizes food waste and prevents overproduction.\n\n• Organizing: The management function focused on assembling resources, assigning tasks, and establishing structure to carry out plans.\n• Planning: The broader process of defining objectives and determining the overall courses of action to meet them. While planning is based on a forecast, the act of predicting specific time-bound needs is forecasting itself.\n• Managing: The overarching process of directing, controlling, and coordinating an organization or system.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Planning, Organizing, Managing"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:who is the current chair of the professional regulatory board of nutrition and dietetics?:::', 'who is the current chair of the professional regulatory board of nutrition and dietetics?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Socorro Ignacio (Dr. Ma. Socorro Endrina-Ignacio), who was appointed as Chairperson of the Professional Regulatory Board of Nutrition and Dietetics under the Professional Regulation Commission (PRC).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Socorro Ignacio\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Socorro Ignacio (Dr. Ma. Socorro Endrina-Ignacio), who was appointed as Chairperson of the Professional Regulatory Board of Nutrition and Dietetics under the Professional Regulation Commission (PRC).\n\n• Chairperson: Ma. Socorro E. Ignacio\n• Members: Imelda A. Agdeppa and Rhoda Joy R. Buenviaje\n• (Note: Virgith B. Buena previously served as the long-time Chairperson prior to recent board reorganization).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Imelda Angeles-Agdeppa, Rhoda Joy Buenviaje, Virgith Buena"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:it is a prediction of food needs for a day or other specific period of time.:::', 'it is a prediction of food needs for a day or other specific period of time.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In foodservice and operations management, forecasting is defined as the process of predicting or estimating future food needs for a day or another specific period of time. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Forecasting\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In foodservice and operations management, forecasting is defined as the process of predicting or estimating future food needs for a day or another specific period of time. \n\nIt serves as a data-driven tool to ensure the purchasing department orders the correct volume of ingredients and the kitchen staff prepares the right amount of food, which directly minimizes food waste and prevents overproduction.\n\n• Organizing: The management function focused on assembling resources, assigning tasks, and establishing structure to carry out plans.\n• Planning: The broader process of defining objectives and determining the overall courses of action to meet them. While planning is based on a forecast, the act of predicting specific time-bound needs is forecasting itself.\n• Managing: The overarching process of directing, controlling, and coordinating an organization or system.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Planning, Organizing, Managing"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:it is the process of getting the right product into a facility at the right time and in a form that meets pre-established standards for quantity, quality, and price.:::purchasing', 'it is the process of getting the right product into a facility at the right time and in a form that meets pre-established standards for quantity, quality, and price.', 'Purchasing', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In foodservice and meal management systems, Purchasing (or procurement) is officially defined as the administrative process of securing the right product, in the right amount, at the right time, from the right supplier, and at the right price. It includes the strategic decision-making involved in establishing product specifications and negotiating market costs prior to the delivery of goods.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Purchasing\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In foodservice and meal management systems, Purchasing (or procurement) is officially defined as the administrative process of securing the right product, in the right amount, at the right time, from the right supplier, and at the right price. It includes the strategic decision-making involved in establishing product specifications and negotiating market costs prior to the delivery of goods.\n\n• Food Production: The operational stage where raw or prepared ingredients are transformed into finished menu items using various cooking methods.\n• Receiving: The specific operation where a facility inspects delivered goods to ensure they match the exact quality, quantity, and price specs previously agreed upon during the purchasing stage. (While Receiving physically \"gets\" the product into the building, Purchasing is the entire managerial process that orchestrates it).\n• Menu planning: The foundational phase where food items are selected and designed on paper based on the nutritional and financial goals of the facility.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Menu planning, Food Production, Receiving"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:it is the process of getting the right product into a facility at the right time and in a form that meets pre-established standards for quantity, quality, and price.:::', 'it is the process of getting the right product into a facility at the right time and in a form that meets pre-established standards for quantity, quality, and price.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In foodservice and meal management systems, Purchasing (or procurement) is officially defined as the administrative process of securing the right product, in the right amount, at the right time, from the right supplier, and at the right price. It includes the strategic decision-making involved in establishing product specifications and negotiating market costs prior to the delivery of goods.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Purchasing\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In foodservice and meal management systems, Purchasing (or procurement) is officially defined as the administrative process of securing the right product, in the right amount, at the right time, from the right supplier, and at the right price. It includes the strategic decision-making involved in establishing product specifications and negotiating market costs prior to the delivery of goods.\n\n• Food Production: The operational stage where raw or prepared ingredients are transformed into finished menu items using various cooking methods.\n• Receiving: The specific operation where a facility inspects delivered goods to ensure they match the exact quality, quantity, and price specs previously agreed upon during the purchasing stage. (While Receiving physically \"gets\" the product into the building, Purchasing is the entire managerial process that orchestrates it).\n• Menu planning: The foundational phase where food items are selected and designed on paper based on the nutritional and financial goals of the facility.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Menu planning, Food Production, Receiving"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are the criteria for determining whether a food is spoiled, except::::none of the above', 'the following are the criteria for determining whether a food is spoiled, except:', 'None of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"To classify a food as \"unspoiled\" or acceptable, it must satisfy all three of the positive benchmarks listed in options A, B, and C. Therefore, none of them are exceptions.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"None of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"To classify a food as \"unspoiled\" or acceptable, it must satisfy all three of the positive benchmarks listed in options A, B, and C. Therefore, none of them are exceptions.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Freedom from objectable, but not unsafe, contaminants, Freedom from decomposition resulting from enzymatic reactions inherent in the food or reactions between the food and its environment or decomposition from microbial growth, Freedom from physical damage"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following is the purpose of producing quality foods in large volumes, except::::improve aesthetic quality', 'the following is the purpose of producing quality foods in large volumes, except:', 'Improve aesthetic quality', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In quantity food production (such as institutional catering or commercial food service), food items must be processed in bulk to feed a large number of people efficiently. While massive industrial production successfully drives safety, flavor modification, and nutrient conservation via standardized operational formulas, it strips away the ability to execute delicate, artistic, or customized plating.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Improve aesthetic quality\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In quantity food production (such as institutional catering or commercial food service), food items must be processed in bulk to feed a large number of people efficiently. While massive industrial production successfully drives safety, flavor modification, and nutrient conservation via standardized operational formulas, it strips away the ability to execute delicate, artistic, or customized plating.\n\nProducing food in massive batches requires prioritizing uniform output speed, thermal safety, and broad scale-stability over intricate visual designs. Because bulk distribution environments (like hospital lines, cafeteria trays, or packaging conveyor belts) inherently limit individual presentation, enhancing or customizing the refined aesthetic beauty of a dish is generally not an achievable technical objective or purpose of mass cooking.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Develop, enhance, or alter flavor, Minimize quality losses, Destroy harmful microorganisms"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following is the purpose of producing quality foods in large volumes, except::::', 'the following is the purpose of producing quality foods in large volumes, except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In quantity food production (such as institutional catering or commercial food service), food items must be processed in bulk to feed a large number of people efficiently. While massive industrial production successfully drives safety, flavor modification, and nutrient conservation via standardized operational formulas, it strips away the ability to execute delicate, artistic, or customized plating.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Improve aesthetic quality\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In quantity food production (such as institutional catering or commercial food service), food items must be processed in bulk to feed a large number of people efficiently. While massive industrial production successfully drives safety, flavor modification, and nutrient conservation via standardized operational formulas, it strips away the ability to execute delicate, artistic, or customized plating.\n\nProducing food in massive batches requires prioritizing uniform output speed, thermal safety, and broad scale-stability over intricate visual designs. Because bulk distribution environments (like hospital lines, cafeteria trays, or packaging conveyor belts) inherently limit individual presentation, enhancing or customizing the refined aesthetic beauty of a dish is generally not an achievable technical objective or purpose of mass cooking.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Develop, enhance, or alter flavor, Minimize quality losses, Destroy harmful microorganisms"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following is the importance of food safety, except::::to reverse the spread of diseases', 'the following is the importance of food safety, except:', 'To reverse the spread of diseases', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Food safety is a preventive discipline, not a curative treatment. While proper food handling, sanitation, and temperature control work effectively to prevent the transmission of foodborne pathogens, food safety measures alone cannot \"reverse\" or cure the spread of diseases once a population or individual is infected. Dealing with the active spread or reversal of clinical diseases falls under the scope of medical treatments, epidemiology, pharmaceutical interventions, and clinical healthcare protocols.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"To reverse the spread of diseases\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Food safety is a preventive discipline, not a curative treatment. While proper food handling, sanitation, and temperature control work effectively to prevent the transmission of foodborne pathogens, food safety measures alone cannot \"reverse\" or cure the spread of diseases once a population or individual is infected. Dealing with the active spread or reversal of clinical diseases falls under the scope of medical treatments, epidemiology, pharmaceutical interventions, and clinical healthcare protocols.\n\n• To create and maintain healthful and hygienic conditions for the family: This is a fundamental, domestic goal of everyday food hygiene to protect household health.\n• To gain market access: Under national regulations such as the Food Safety Act of 2013 (RA 10611), ensuring rigid food safety compliance is a legal mandatory prerequisite to protect consumer health and facilitate market access for local commercial food products globally and locally.\n• To prevent food contamination and food spoilage: This is a primary technical purpose of food safety frameworks—keeping biological, chemical, and physical hazards out of the food supply chain.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: To create and maintain healthful and hygienic conditions for the family, To prevent food contamination and food spoilage, To gain market access"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following is the importance of food safety, except::::', 'the following is the importance of food safety, except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Food safety is a preventive discipline, not a curative treatment. While proper food handling, sanitation, and temperature control work effectively to prevent the transmission of foodborne pathogens, food safety measures alone cannot \"reverse\" or cure the spread of diseases once a population or individual is infected. Dealing with the active spread or reversal of clinical diseases falls under the scope of medical treatments, epidemiology, pharmaceutical interventions, and clinical healthcare protocols.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"To reverse the spread of diseases\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Food safety is a preventive discipline, not a curative treatment. While proper food handling, sanitation, and temperature control work effectively to prevent the transmission of foodborne pathogens, food safety measures alone cannot \"reverse\" or cure the spread of diseases once a population or individual is infected. Dealing with the active spread or reversal of clinical diseases falls under the scope of medical treatments, epidemiology, pharmaceutical interventions, and clinical healthcare protocols.\n\n• To create and maintain healthful and hygienic conditions for the family: This is a fundamental, domestic goal of everyday food hygiene to protect household health.\n• To gain market access: Under national regulations such as the Food Safety Act of 2013 (RA 10611), ensuring rigid food safety compliance is a legal mandatory prerequisite to protect consumer health and facilitate market access for local commercial food products globally and locally.\n• To prevent food contamination and food spoilage: This is a primary technical purpose of food safety frameworks—keeping biological, chemical, and physical hazards out of the food supply chain.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: To create and maintain healthful and hygienic conditions for the family, To prevent food contamination and food spoilage, To gain market access"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the right temperature for dry storage?:::60 – 70ºf', 'what is the right temperature for dry storage?', '60 – 70ºF', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In foodservice and meal management, dry storage areas are designed to preserve dry staples and canned goods by protecting them from heat and moisture.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"60 – 70ºF\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In foodservice and meal management, dry storage areas are designed to preserve dry staples and canned goods by protecting them from heat and moisture.\n\nStandard foodservice guidelines specify that dry storage temperatures should ideally be maintained between 50ºF and 70ºF (10ºC to 21ºC).\n\n60 – 70ºF fits securely within this recommended parameters. Keeping the room within this temperature range slows chemical reactions, prevents premature food spoilage, and extends the overall shelf life of dry goods.\n\n• 29 to 33ºF: This represents the temperature range used for chilled or super-chilled food storage (such as fresh fish, meat, or poultry holding sections), which is just slightly above or at freezing point.\n• 34 – 40ºF: This is the standard operational temperature range for refrigeration / cold storage to prevent bacterial multiplication in perishable commodities.\n• 0 to -10ºF: This is the legal threshold required for freezer storage to ensure food remains solidly frozen and safe over long periods.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 34 – 40ºF, 0 to -10ºF, 29 to 33ºF"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:who is the current chair of the professional regulatory board of nutrition and dietetics?:::socorro ignacio', 'who is the current chair of the professional regulatory board of nutrition and dietetics?', 'Socorro Ignacio', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Socorro Ignacio (Dr. Ma. Socorro Endrina-Ignacio), who was appointed as Chairperson of the Professional Regulatory Board of Nutrition and Dietetics under the Professional Regulation Commission (PRC).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Socorro Ignacio\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Socorro Ignacio (Dr. Ma. Socorro Endrina-Ignacio), who was appointed as Chairperson of the Professional Regulatory Board of Nutrition and Dietetics under the Professional Regulation Commission (PRC).\n\n• Chairperson: Ma. Socorro E. Ignacio\n• Members: Imelda A. Agdeppa and Rhoda Joy R. Buenviaje\n• (Note: Virgith B. Buena previously served as the long-time Chairperson prior to recent board reorganization).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Imelda Angeles-Agdeppa, Rhoda Joy Buenviaje, Virgith Buena"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the right temperature for dry storage?:::', 'what is the right temperature for dry storage?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In foodservice and meal management, dry storage areas are designed to preserve dry staples and canned goods by protecting them from heat and moisture.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"60 – 70ºF\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In foodservice and meal management, dry storage areas are designed to preserve dry staples and canned goods by protecting them from heat and moisture.\n\nStandard foodservice guidelines specify that dry storage temperatures should ideally be maintained between 50ºF and 70ºF (10ºC to 21ºC).\n\n60 – 70ºF fits securely within this recommended parameters. Keeping the room within this temperature range slows chemical reactions, prevents premature food spoilage, and extends the overall shelf life of dry goods.\n\n• 29 to 33ºF: This represents the temperature range used for chilled or super-chilled food storage (such as fresh fish, meat, or poultry holding sections), which is just slightly above or at freezing point.\n• 34 – 40ºF: This is the standard operational temperature range for refrigeration / cold storage to prevent bacterial multiplication in perishable commodities.\n• 0 to -10ºF: This is the legal threshold required for freezer storage to ensure food remains solidly frozen and safe over long periods.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 34 – 40ºF, 0 to -10ºF, 29 to 33ºF"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:it is the science and art of preventing disease, prolonging life, and promoting health through organized community effort.:::public health', 'it is the science and art of preventing disease, prolonging life, and promoting health through organized community effort.', 'public health', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Public health has been classically defined as the science and art of preventing disease, prolonging life, and promoting health through the organized efforts of society (Winslow, 1920).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"public health\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Public health has been classically defined as the science and art of preventing disease, prolonging life, and promoting health through the organized efforts of society (Winslow, 1920).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: public health nutrition, community nutrition, community health"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
