('mc:the eccd system act (ra 12199) that strengthens the country''s eccd system to address gaps in healthcare, nutrition, early childhood education, safety, security, and social services for children aged five and below. this law also transferred the eccd council under which agency?:::dilg', 'the eccd system act (ra 12199) that strengthens the country''s eccd system to address gaps in healthcare, nutrition, early childhood education, safety, security, and social services for children aged five and below. this law also transferred the eccd council under which agency?', 'DILG', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Under the Early Childhood Care and Development (ECCD) System Act (Republic Act No. 12199), the ECCD Council was officially attached to the Department of the Interior and Local Government (DILG).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"DILG\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Under the Early Childhood Care and Development (ECCD) System Act (Republic Act No. 12199), the ECCD Council was officially attached to the Department of the Interior and Local Government (DILG).\n\nPreviously, under the Early Years Act of 2013 (RA 10410), the ECCD Council was attached to the Department of Education (DepEd). The strategic shift to the DILG was made to strengthen local government unit (LGU) accountability and compliance, ensuring that local communal Day Care and Child Development Centers across every barangay are fully funded, maintained, and operational.\n\nRepublic Act No. 12199, the Early Childhood Care and Development (ECCD) System Act signed in May 2025, repealed the earlier Early Years Act of 2013 (RA 10410) and reorganized comprehensive health, nutrition, and early education services for children from conception through age eight, assigning children below five to the ECCD Council and those aged five to eight to the Department of Education (Republic Act No. 12199, 2025).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: DepED, DSWD, DOH"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the eccd system act (ra 12199) that strengthens the country''s eccd system to address gaps in healthcare, nutrition, early childhood education, safety, security, and social services for children aged five and below. this law also transferred the eccd council under which agency?:::', 'the eccd system act (ra 12199) that strengthens the country''s eccd system to address gaps in healthcare, nutrition, early childhood education, safety, security, and social services for children aged five and below. this law also transferred the eccd council under which agency?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Under the Early Childhood Care and Development (ECCD) System Act (Republic Act No. 12199), the ECCD Council was officially attached to the Department of the Interior and Local Government (DILG).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"DILG\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Under the Early Childhood Care and Development (ECCD) System Act (Republic Act No. 12199), the ECCD Council was officially attached to the Department of the Interior and Local Government (DILG).\n\nPreviously, under the Early Years Act of 2013 (RA 10410), the ECCD Council was attached to the Department of Education (DepEd). The strategic shift to the DILG was made to strengthen local government unit (LGU) accountability and compliance, ensuring that local communal Day Care and Child Development Centers across every barangay are fully funded, maintained, and operational.\n\nRepublic Act No. 12199, the Early Childhood Care and Development (ECCD) System Act signed in May 2025, repealed the earlier Early Years Act of 2013 (RA 10410) and reorganized comprehensive health, nutrition, and early education services for children from conception through age eight, assigning children below five to the ECCD Council and those aged five to eight to the Department of Education (Republic Act No. 12199, 2025).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: DepED, DSWD, DOH"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a study wherein the subjects nor the experimenter knows that treatment is being given until after the experiment is called a::::double-blind study', 'a study wherein the subjects nor the experimenter knows that treatment is being given until after the experiment is called a:', 'double-blind study', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A double-blind study is one in which neither the research subjects nor the investigators administering treatment know who is receiving the active intervention versus the control until after data collection is complete, minimizing bias from both participant expectation and researcher influence. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"double-blind study\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A double-blind study is one in which neither the research subjects nor the investigators administering treatment know who is receiving the active intervention versus the control until after data collection is complete, minimizing bias from both participant expectation and researcher influence. \n\nThis differs from a single-blind study, where only the subject (and not the researcher) is unaware of group assignment.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: placebo control trial, single-blind study, hawthorne study"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a study wherein the subjects nor the experimenter knows that treatment is being given until after the experiment is called a::::', 'a study wherein the subjects nor the experimenter knows that treatment is being given until after the experiment is called a:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A double-blind study is one in which neither the research subjects nor the investigators administering treatment know who is receiving the active intervention versus the control until after data collection is complete, minimizing bias from both participant expectation and researcher influence. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"double-blind study\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A double-blind study is one in which neither the research subjects nor the investigators administering treatment know who is receiving the active intervention versus the control until after data collection is complete, minimizing bias from both participant expectation and researcher influence. \n\nThis differs from a single-blind study, where only the subject (and not the researcher) is unaware of group assignment.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: placebo control trial, single-blind study, hawthorne study"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:it is the practical measure to assess iodine status.:::urinary iodine excretion', 'it is the practical measure to assess iodine status.', 'urinary iodine excretion', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Urinary iodine concentration is the recommended, practical population-level indicator of iodine status because more than 90% of ingested iodine is excreted in urine, making it a direct and cost-effective reflection of recent dietary iodine intake (World Health Organization, UNICEF, & ICCIDD, 2007). ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"urinary iodine excretion\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Urinary iodine concentration is the recommended, practical population-level indicator of iodine status because more than 90% of ingested iodine is excreted in urine, making it a direct and cost-effective reflection of recent dietary iodine intake (World Health Organization, UNICEF, & ICCIDD, 2007). \n\nSerum thyroglobulin, TSH, and thyroxine are useful but more costly, less practical biochemical markers usually reserved for confirmatory or clinical assessment.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: serum thyroglobulin, serum thyroid stimulating hormone, serum thyroxine"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:it is the practical measure to assess iodine status.:::', 'it is the practical measure to assess iodine status.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Urinary iodine concentration is the recommended, practical population-level indicator of iodine status because more than 90% of ingested iodine is excreted in urine, making it a direct and cost-effective reflection of recent dietary iodine intake (World Health Organization, UNICEF, & ICCIDD, 2007). ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"urinary iodine excretion\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Urinary iodine concentration is the recommended, practical population-level indicator of iodine status because more than 90% of ingested iodine is excreted in urine, making it a direct and cost-effective reflection of recent dietary iodine intake (World Health Organization, UNICEF, & ICCIDD, 2007). \n\nSerum thyroglobulin, TSH, and thyroxine are useful but more costly, less practical biochemical markers usually reserved for confirmatory or clinical assessment.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: serum thyroglobulin, serum thyroid stimulating hormone, serum thyroxine"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:it is a type of evaluation that assesses program effectiveness in achieving its ultimate goals.:::impact', 'it is a type of evaluation that assesses program effectiveness in achieving its ultimate goals.', 'Impact', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In public health and program management frameworks, different levels of evaluation target different stages of a program''s results:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Impact\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In public health and program management frameworks, different levels of evaluation target different stages of a program''s results:\n\n• Impact Evaluation specifically assesses the degree to which a program meets its ultimate, long-term goals (such as a structural reduction in disease morbidity, changes in mortality rates, or permanent environmental shifts).\n• Outcome Evaluation measures the intermediate, direct effectiveness of a program in meeting its objectives among the target population, focusing primarily on short-to-medium-term changes in knowledge, attitudes, and specific behaviors.\n• Summative Evaluation is a broader umbrella category done at the end of a program to make overall summary judgments about its worth or funding, which includes both outcome and impact parameters.\n• Formative Evaluation is conducted during development or early implementation to check feasibility and optimize the program''s design before full rollout.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Formative, Outcome, Summative"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the recommended amount of fish and alternatives based on the pinggang pinoy is .:::½ cup', 'the recommended amount of fish and alternatives based on the pinggang pinoy is .', '½ cup', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Under the Pinggang Pinoy food exchange guide developed by the DOST Food and Nutrition Research Institute, one serving from the fish and alternatives (\"Grow\") food group, such as a small piece of fish, a matchbox-sized portion of meat, or an equivalent like shrimp or legumes, corresponds to approximately half a cup, consistent with the food guide''s exchange list of household measures (DOST-FNRI, n.d.).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"½ cup\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Under the Pinggang Pinoy food exchange guide developed by the DOST Food and Nutrition Research Institute, one serving from the fish and alternatives (\"Grow\") food group, such as a small piece of fish, a matchbox-sized portion of meat, or an equivalent like shrimp or legumes, corresponds to approximately half a cup, consistent with the food guide''s exchange list of household measures (DOST-FNRI, n.d.).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: ¼ cup, ⅓ cup, ¾ cup"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:it is a type of evaluation that assesses program effectiveness in achieving its ultimate goals.:::', 'it is a type of evaluation that assesses program effectiveness in achieving its ultimate goals.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In public health and program management frameworks, different levels of evaluation target different stages of a program''s results:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Impact\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In public health and program management frameworks, different levels of evaluation target different stages of a program''s results:\n\n• Impact Evaluation specifically assesses the degree to which a program meets its ultimate, long-term goals (such as a structural reduction in disease morbidity, changes in mortality rates, or permanent environmental shifts).\n• Outcome Evaluation measures the intermediate, direct effectiveness of a program in meeting its objectives among the target population, focusing primarily on short-to-medium-term changes in knowledge, attitudes, and specific behaviors.\n• Summative Evaluation is a broader umbrella category done at the end of a program to make overall summary judgments about its worth or funding, which includes both outcome and impact parameters.\n• Formative Evaluation is conducted during development or early implementation to check feasibility and optimize the program''s design before full rollout.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Formative, Outcome, Summative"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a newborn infant has adequate reserve of::::vitamin a', 'a newborn infant has adequate reserve of:', 'vitamin A', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Newborns are typically born with adequate hepatic stores of vitamin A transferred from the mother during gestation.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"vitamin A\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Newborns are typically born with adequate hepatic stores of vitamin A transferred from the mother during gestation.\n\nVitamin A is efficiently transferred across the placenta toward the end of pregnancy, allowing full-term newborn infants to build up a substantial hepatic (liver) reserve that supports their rapid early development, skin, vision, and immune function.\n\nUnder normal nutritional conditions, healthy full-term infants are born with enough stored vitamin A to meet their needs during the initial months of life.\n\n• Vitamin K: This is the most crucial incorrect option. Newborns have minimal to negligible vitamin K reserves at birth because it does not cross the placenta efficiently and their intestinal tracts are sterile. This puts them at immediate risk for Vitamin K Deficiency Bleeding (VKDB), which is why a prophylactic intramuscular vitamin K shot is routinely given within 6 hours of birth.\n• Vitamin C: Being a water-soluble vitamin, vitamin C is not stored in significant quantities in fetal or infant tissues. The baby relies entirely on continuous daily intake via breast milk or formula.\n• Vitamin E: While some vitamin E is transferred late in pregnancy, plasma levels in newborns are relatively low compared to adult levels, and they do not carry an abundance of \"adequate reserve\" compared to vitamin A.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: vitamin C, vitamin K, vitamin E"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a newborn infant has adequate reserve of::::', 'a newborn infant has adequate reserve of:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Newborns are typically born with adequate hepatic stores of vitamin A transferred from the mother during gestation.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"vitamin A\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Newborns are typically born with adequate hepatic stores of vitamin A transferred from the mother during gestation.\n\nVitamin A is efficiently transferred across the placenta toward the end of pregnancy, allowing full-term newborn infants to build up a substantial hepatic (liver) reserve that supports their rapid early development, skin, vision, and immune function.\n\nUnder normal nutritional conditions, healthy full-term infants are born with enough stored vitamin A to meet their needs during the initial months of life.\n\n• Vitamin K: This is the most crucial incorrect option. Newborns have minimal to negligible vitamin K reserves at birth because it does not cross the placenta efficiently and their intestinal tracts are sterile. This puts them at immediate risk for Vitamin K Deficiency Bleeding (VKDB), which is why a prophylactic intramuscular vitamin K shot is routinely given within 6 hours of birth.\n• Vitamin C: Being a water-soluble vitamin, vitamin C is not stored in significant quantities in fetal or infant tissues. The baby relies entirely on continuous daily intake via breast milk or formula.\n• Vitamin E: While some vitamin E is transferred late in pregnancy, plasma levels in newborns are relatively low compared to adult levels, and they do not carry an abundance of \"adequate reserve\" compared to vitamin A.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: vitamin C, vitamin K, vitamin E"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:sa palabas ng abs-cbn foundation na dok ricky, pedia ng barangay, sino ang gumanap na dok riki bautista na katambal ni dominic ochoa?:::alessandra de rossi', 'sa palabas ng abs-cbn foundation na dok ricky, pedia ng barangay, sino ang gumanap na dok riki bautista na katambal ni dominic ochoa?', 'Alessandra de Rossi', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Ang tamang sagot ay Alessandra de Rossi, na gumanap bilang si Dra. Marikina \"Dok Riki\" Bautista sa \"sitcom-mentary\" na palabas na Dok Ricky, Pedia ng Barangay.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Alessandra de Rossi\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Ang tamang sagot ay Alessandra de Rossi, na gumanap bilang si Dra. Marikina \"Dok Riki\" Bautista sa \"sitcom-mentary\" na palabas na Dok Ricky, Pedia ng Barangay.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Yen Santos, Arci Muñoz, Bella Padilla"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the main role of the agricultural sector in nutrition programs?:::encourage home production of fruits and vegetables', 'what is the main role of the agricultural sector in nutrition programs?', 'Encourage home production of fruits and vegetables', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Within multisectoral nutrition programming, the agriculture sector''s principal contribution is nutrition-sensitive agriculture, most notably promoting household and community food production (e.g., backyard and communal vegetable gardens) to directly improve dietary diversity and access to nutrient-dense foods at the household level. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Encourage home production of fruits and vegetables\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Within multisectoral nutrition programming, the agriculture sector''s principal contribution is nutrition-sensitive agriculture, most notably promoting household and community food production (e.g., backyard and communal vegetable gardens) to directly improve dietary diversity and access to nutrient-dense foods at the household level. \n\nFood fortification and food research fall primarily under the health/science sectors, while livelihood engagement is a broader poverty-reduction, rather than nutrition-specific, function.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Conduct research on foods rich in nutrients, Fortification of staple foods, Engagement to rural livelihood programs"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the main role of the agricultural sector in nutrition programs?:::', 'what is the main role of the agricultural sector in nutrition programs?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Within multisectoral nutrition programming, the agriculture sector''s principal contribution is nutrition-sensitive agriculture, most notably promoting household and community food production (e.g., backyard and communal vegetable gardens) to directly improve dietary diversity and access to nutrient-dense foods at the household level. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Encourage home production of fruits and vegetables\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Within multisectoral nutrition programming, the agriculture sector''s principal contribution is nutrition-sensitive agriculture, most notably promoting household and community food production (e.g., backyard and communal vegetable gardens) to directly improve dietary diversity and access to nutrient-dense foods at the household level. \n\nFood fortification and food research fall primarily under the health/science sectors, while livelihood engagement is a broader poverty-reduction, rather than nutrition-specific, function.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Conduct research on foods rich in nutrients, Fortification of staple foods, Engagement to rural livelihood programs"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
