('mc:sentence a. bacteria alone can be used as a coagulant in cheese. sentence b. bacteria causes holes in swiss cheese.:::sentence b is correct', 'sentence a. bacteria alone can be used as a coagulant in cheese. sentence b. bacteria causes holes in swiss cheese.', 'Sentence B is correct', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Primarily, Rennet or acids: Milk is turned into solid curds and liquid whey using rennet (an enzyme) or acids like vinegar or lemon juice, rather than bacteria alone.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sentence B is correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Primarily, Rennet or acids: Milk is turned into solid curds and liquid whey using rennet (an enzyme) or acids like vinegar or lemon juice, rather than bacteria alone.\n\nLactic acid bacteria: Starter cultures of bacteria are added to milk to consume lactose and produce lactic acid, which helps with acidification and flavor. This acid environment prepares the milk, but coagulation itself relies on rennet or direct acidifiers\n\nCertain cultures also produce gases that create the characteristic holes in Swiss-type cheeses.\n\nThe holes in Swiss cheese are created by carbon dioxide gas released during fermentation by the bacterium Propionibacterium freudenreichii.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence A is correct, Both sentences are correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. bacteria alone can be used as a coagulant in cheese. sentence b. bacteria causes holes in swiss cheese.:::', 'sentence a. bacteria alone can be used as a coagulant in cheese. sentence b. bacteria causes holes in swiss cheese.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Primarily, Rennet or acids: Milk is turned into solid curds and liquid whey using rennet (an enzyme) or acids like vinegar or lemon juice, rather than bacteria alone.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sentence B is correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Primarily, Rennet or acids: Milk is turned into solid curds and liquid whey using rennet (an enzyme) or acids like vinegar or lemon juice, rather than bacteria alone.\n\nLactic acid bacteria: Starter cultures of bacteria are added to milk to consume lactose and produce lactic acid, which helps with acidification and flavor. This acid environment prepares the milk, but coagulation itself relies on rennet or direct acidifiers\n\nCertain cultures also produce gases that create the characteristic holes in Swiss-type cheeses.\n\nThe holes in Swiss cheese are created by carbon dioxide gas released during fermentation by the bacterium Propionibacterium freudenreichii.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence A is correct, Both sentences are correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. in pinipig, the ability to gelatinize is increased. sentence b. this is due to the breakdown of starches into dextrins during the process of making pinipig.:::sentence b is correct', 'sentence a. in pinipig, the ability to gelatinize is increased. sentence b. this is due to the breakdown of starches into dextrins during the process of making pinipig.', 'Sentence B is correct', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Pinipig – parboiled waxy rice; pounded, winnowed, and toasted","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sentence B is correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Pinipig – parboiled waxy rice; pounded, winnowed, and toasted\n\nToasting = example of dextrinization\n\nWhen pinipig (pounded young glutinous rice) undergoes dextrinization, the long-chain starch molecules (amylose and amylopectin) are chemically broken down into shorter, smaller carbohydrate fragments called dextrins. \n\nIn dry-heat cooking, e.g. toasting, starch is partly degraded to dextrins that have less ability to swell and gelatinize. Because gelatinization requires intact, long-chain starch granules to swell, burst, and trap water into a thick, cohesive network, the fragmented structure of dextrinized starch prevents this from happening.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence A is correct, Both sentences are correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. in pinipig, the ability to gelatinize is increased. sentence b. this is due to the breakdown of starches into dextrins during the process of making pinipig.:::', 'sentence a. in pinipig, the ability to gelatinize is increased. sentence b. this is due to the breakdown of starches into dextrins during the process of making pinipig.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Pinipig – parboiled waxy rice; pounded, winnowed, and toasted","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sentence B is correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Pinipig – parboiled waxy rice; pounded, winnowed, and toasted\n\nToasting = example of dextrinization\n\nWhen pinipig (pounded young glutinous rice) undergoes dextrinization, the long-chain starch molecules (amylose and amylopectin) are chemically broken down into shorter, smaller carbohydrate fragments called dextrins. \n\nIn dry-heat cooking, e.g. toasting, starch is partly degraded to dextrins that have less ability to swell and gelatinize. Because gelatinization requires intact, long-chain starch granules to swell, burst, and trap water into a thick, cohesive network, the fragmented structure of dextrinized starch prevents this from happening.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence A is correct, Both sentences are correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a coddled egg is one which has been::::cooked in water after it has stopped boiling', 'a coddled egg is one which has been:', 'cooked in water after it has stopped boiling', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A coddled egg is gently cooked by residual heat after the water has been brought to a boil, producing a softly set egg. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"cooked in water after it has stopped boiling\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A coddled egg is gently cooked by residual heat after the water has been brought to a boil, producing a softly set egg. \n\nProlonged boiling produces a harder-cooked egg rather than a coddled egg.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: cooked in boiling water for 10-12 minutes, cooked slowly in boiling water for 20 minutes, boiled for 5 minutes in a covered pot"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. less tender cuts of meat usually contain more connective tissue. sentence b. elastin hydrolyzes during moist heat cooking.:::', 'sentence a. less tender cuts of meat usually contain more connective tissue. sentence b. elastin hydrolyzes during moist heat cooking.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sentence A is correct because less tender cuts usually contain more connective tissue. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sentence A is correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sentence A is correct because less tender cuts usually contain more connective tissue. \n\nSentence B is incorrect because collagen can be converted to gelatin during moist heating, whereas elastin is much more heat resistant and does not readily tenderize.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. amount of salt must be reduced if fine iodized salt will be used to replace rock salt. sentence b. fine iodized salt is saltier than rock salt.:::both sentences are correct', 'sentence a. amount of salt must be reduced if fine iodized salt will be used to replace rock salt. sentence b. fine iodized salt is saltier than rock salt.', 'Both sentences are correct', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Fine iodized salt packs more densely than coarse rock salt, so substituting it volume-for-volume may require reducing the amount. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Both sentences are correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Fine iodized salt packs more densely than coarse rock salt, so substituting it volume-for-volume may require reducing the amount. \n\nThe perceived difference is related mainly to crystal size and packing rather than iodine itself.\n\nFine salt can taste saltier than rock salt by volume because the tiny grains pack tightly into a spoon, meaning you get more actual salt than the same spoonful of chunky rock salt.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence A is correct, Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
