is correct, Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. chicken fat contains soft fats. sentence b. chicken fat is more plastic than pork fat.:::sentence a is correct', 'sentence a. chicken fat contains soft fats. sentence b. chicken fat is more plastic than pork fat.', 'Sentence A is correct', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• Sentence A is CORRECT: Chicken fat contains a very high concentration of monounsaturated and polyunsaturated fatty acids. These are chemically classified as \"soft fats\" because they have low melting points, making chicken fat soft and oily at room temperature.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sentence A is correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• Sentence A is CORRECT: Chicken fat contains a very high concentration of monounsaturated and polyunsaturated fatty acids. These are chemically classified as \"soft fats\" because they have low melting points, making chicken fat soft and oily at room temperature.\n\n• Sentence B is INCORRECT: Because chicken fat turns into liquid oil so easily, it cannot retain its shape under manual pressure. Pork fat (lard) is actually more plastic than chicken fat. Lard forms a stable crystalline matrix at room temperature that allows it to be pliable, spreadable, and hold structural shape—which is the textbook definition of a plastic fat in food science.\n\nPork Fat (Lard): Higher saturated fat content (around 39–45%) gives it a firmer, more stable crystalline structure at room temperature, allowing it to hold a shape and perform well as a plastic shortening in baking and meat processing. \n\nChicken Fat: Higher unsaturated fat content (around 65–68%) gives it a much lower melting point (melting around 23–27°C or lower) and causes it to liquefy rapidly. \n\nBecause it turns to liquid oil so quickly, it has a narrower solid-to-liquid range and lower structural plasticity than pork fat.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. chicken fat contains soft fats. sentence b. chicken fat is more plastic than pork fat.:::', 'sentence a. chicken fat contains soft fats. sentence b. chicken fat is more plastic than pork fat.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• Sentence A is CORRECT: Chicken fat contains a very high concentration of monounsaturated and polyunsaturated fatty acids. These are chemically classified as \"soft fats\" because they have low melting points, making chicken fat soft and oily at room temperature.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sentence A is correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• Sentence A is CORRECT: Chicken fat contains a very high concentration of monounsaturated and polyunsaturated fatty acids. These are chemically classified as \"soft fats\" because they have low melting points, making chicken fat soft and oily at room temperature.\n\n• Sentence B is INCORRECT: Because chicken fat turns into liquid oil so easily, it cannot retain its shape under manual pressure. Pork fat (lard) is actually more plastic than chicken fat. Lard forms a stable crystalline matrix at room temperature that allows it to be pliable, spreadable, and hold structural shape—which is the textbook definition of a plastic fat in food science.\n\nPork Fat (Lard): Higher saturated fat content (around 39–45%) gives it a firmer, more stable crystalline structure at room temperature, allowing it to hold a shape and perform well as a plastic shortening in baking and meat processing. \n\nChicken Fat: Higher unsaturated fat content (around 65–68%) gives it a much lower melting point (melting around 23–27°C or lower) and causes it to liquefy rapidly. \n\nBecause it turns to liquid oil so quickly, it has a narrower solid-to-liquid range and lower structural plasticity than pork fat.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. moist heat cooking is used in tough or less tender meat. sentence b. water solubilizes collagen making meat tender.:::sentence a is correct', 'sentence a. moist heat cooking is used in tough or less tender meat. sentence b. water solubilizes collagen making meat tender.', 'Sentence A is correct', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Moist-heat cooking is suitable for less tender cuts because prolonged heating in the presence of moisture converts collagen into gelatin, thereby improving tenderness.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sentence A is correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Moist-heat cooking is suitable for less tender cuts because prolonged heating in the presence of moisture converts collagen into gelatin, thereby improving tenderness.\n\nWater alone is not enough to dissolve collagen. When meat reaches temperatures between 160°F to 205°F (71°C to 96°C) range, the tightly wound collagen proteins begin to uncoil and denature.\n\nThe presence of water is chemically required to break the molecular bonds of the uncoiled collagen. Through a process called partial hydrolysis, the water solubilizes the tough collagen and converts it into gelatin.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. moist heat cooking is used in tough or less tender meat. sentence b. water solubilizes collagen making meat tender.:::', 'sentence a. moist heat cooking is used in tough or less tender meat. sentence b. water solubilizes collagen making meat tender.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Moist-heat cooking is suitable for less tender cuts because prolonged heating in the presence of moisture converts collagen into gelatin, thereby improving tenderness.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sentence A is correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Moist-heat cooking is suitable for less tender cuts because prolonged heating in the presence of moisture converts collagen into gelatin, thereby improving tenderness.\n\nWater alone is not enough to dissolve collagen. When meat reaches temperatures between 160°F to 205°F (71°C to 96°C) range, the tightly wound collagen proteins begin to uncoil and denature.\n\nThe presence of water is chemically required to break the molecular bonds of the uncoiled collagen. Through a process called partial hydrolysis, the water solubilizes the tough collagen and converts it into gelatin.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. milk flavour is generally bland or less sweet. sentence b. bland milk flavour is due to the bound glucose in galactose molecule.:::sentence a is correct', 'sentence a. milk flavour is generally bland or less sweet. sentence b. bland milk flavour is due to the bound glucose in galactose molecule.', 'Sentence A is correct', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sentence A is correct because milk has a relatively mild, only slightly sweet flavor. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sentence A is correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sentence A is correct because milk has a relatively mild, only slightly sweet flavor. \n\nSentence B is incorrect because the sweetness of milk is primarily due to lactose, not to ''bound glucose in galactose''.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
