('mc:which should you start planning for first?:::entrée', 'which should you start planning for first?', 'Entrée', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Meal planning generally starts with the entrée because it is usually the focal point around which the remaining meal components are selected.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Entrée\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Meal planning generally starts with the entrée because it is usually the focal point around which the remaining meal components are selected.\n\nThe main dish dictates the overall flavor profile of the meal. Once the entrée is selected, you can curate appetizers, soups, and salads that complement, cleanse the palate for, or contrast its flavor notes instead of competing with them.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Salads, Appetizer, None of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which should you start planning for first?:::', 'which should you start planning for first?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Meal planning generally starts with the entrée because it is usually the focal point around which the remaining meal components are selected.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Entrée\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Meal planning generally starts with the entrée because it is usually the focal point around which the remaining meal components are selected.\n\nThe main dish dictates the overall flavor profile of the meal. Once the entrée is selected, you can curate appetizers, soups, and salads that complement, cleanse the palate for, or contrast its flavor notes instead of competing with them.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Salads, Appetizer, None of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is arranged according to the prescribed menu pattern for lunch?:::chicken pastel, steamed broccoli, saffron rice, jasmine tea', 'which of the following is arranged according to the prescribed menu pattern for lunch?', 'Chicken Pastel, Steamed Broccoli, Saffron Rice, Jasmine Tea', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The conventional menu sequence for lunch presents the food items in a specific sequential order:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Chicken Pastel, Steamed Broccoli, Saffron Rice, Jasmine Tea\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The conventional menu sequence for lunch presents the food items in a specific sequential order:\n\n1. Soup or Appetizer (if included)\n2. Main Course / Protein (Chicken Pastel)\n3. Vegetables or Salad (Steamed Broccoli)\n4. Cereal or Rice (Saffron Rice)\n5. Dessert (if included)\n6. Beverage (Jasmine Tea)","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Jasmine Tea, Chicken Pastel, Steamed Broccoli, Saffron Rice, Chicken Pastel, Saffron Rice, Steamed Broccoli, Jasmine Tea, None of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is arranged according to the prescribed menu pattern for lunch?:::', 'which of the following is arranged according to the prescribed menu pattern for lunch?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The conventional menu sequence for lunch presents the food items in a specific sequential order:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Chicken Pastel, Steamed Broccoli, Saffron Rice, Jasmine Tea\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The conventional menu sequence for lunch presents the food items in a specific sequential order:\n\n1. Soup or Appetizer (if included)\n2. Main Course / Protein (Chicken Pastel)\n3. Vegetables or Salad (Steamed Broccoli)\n4. Cereal or Rice (Saffron Rice)\n5. Dessert (if included)\n6. Beverage (Jasmine Tea)","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Jasmine Tea, Chicken Pastel, Steamed Broccoli, Saffron Rice, Chicken Pastel, Saffron Rice, Steamed Broccoli, Jasmine Tea, None of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the last recommended day of retail sale is referred to as::::pull date', 'the last recommended day of retail sale is referred to as:', 'pull date', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A pull date is the last recommended date for retail display or sale before the product should be removed from shelves.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"pull date\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A pull date is the last recommended date for retail display or sale before the product should be removed from shelves.\n\n• Expiration: This is the last date on which the product should be used or consumed. It is often used for products where safety is a concern, indicating that after this date, the product may no longer be safe to consume.\n• Best before and Freshness Date: both indicate the last date recommended for the use of the product at its peak quality. It does not refer to safety but rather optimal quality.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: expiration date, freshness date, best before date"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the last recommended day of retail sale is referred to as::::', 'the last recommended day of retail sale is referred to as:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A pull date is the last recommended date for retail display or sale before the product should be removed from shelves.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"pull date\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A pull date is the last recommended date for retail display or sale before the product should be removed from shelves.\n\n• Expiration: This is the last date on which the product should be used or consumed. It is often used for products where safety is a concern, indicating that after this date, the product may no longer be safe to consume.\n• Best before and Freshness Date: both indicate the last date recommended for the use of the product at its peak quality. It does not refer to safety but rather optimal quality.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: expiration date, freshness date, best before date"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a recipe of buffalo wings makes 30 pcs (3 servings). what would you do to lower the food cost of the menu item?:::make the portion size 6 pcs/serving', 'a recipe of buffalo wings makes 30 pcs (3 servings). what would you do to lower the food cost of the menu item?', 'Make the portion size 6 pcs/serving', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Reducing the number of pieces per plate from 10 pieces down to 6 pieces uses fewer raw ingredients per order. Using fewer chicken wings directly decreases the cost to make each individual plate.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Make the portion size 6 pcs/serving\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Reducing the number of pieces per plate from 10 pieces down to 6 pieces uses fewer raw ingredients per order. Using fewer chicken wings directly decreases the cost to make each individual plate.\n\n• 15 pcs/serving: Increasing the portion size uses more ingredients, which raises the food cost per plate.\n• Prefabricated products: Pre-made or pre-cut items usually cost more because you pay extra for the supplier''s labor.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Make the portion size 15 pcs/serving, Use prefabricated products, None of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a recipe of buffalo wings makes 30 pcs (3 servings). what would you do to lower the food cost of the menu item?:::', 'a recipe of buffalo wings makes 30 pcs (3 servings). what would you do to lower the food cost of the menu item?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Reducing the number of pieces per plate from 10 pieces down to 6 pieces uses fewer raw ingredients per order. Using fewer chicken wings directly decreases the cost to make each individual plate.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Make the portion size 6 pcs/serving\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Reducing the number of pieces per plate from 10 pieces down to 6 pieces uses fewer raw ingredients per order. Using fewer chicken wings directly decreases the cost to make each individual plate.\n\n• 15 pcs/serving: Increasing the portion size uses more ingredients, which raises the food cost per plate.\n• Prefabricated products: Pre-made or pre-cut items usually cost more because you pay extra for the supplier''s labor.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Make the portion size 15 pcs/serving, Use prefabricated products, None of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a menu item that sells for p70.00 has a food cost percent of 20%. what would the food cost percent be if the selling price was increased to p150.00?:::9.3%', 'a menu item that sells for p70.00 has a food cost percent of 20%. what would the food cost percent be if the selling price was increased to p150.00?', '9.3%', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Original Food Cost Percentage (Php70.00x 0.2 = 14.00 PHP)","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"9.3%\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Original Food Cost Percentage (Php70.00x 0.2 = 14.00 PHP)\n\nNew FCP= (Food Cost/New SP) x100\n\nNew FCP = (14.00/150.00) x 100 = 9.3%","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 10.3%, 11.3%, 8.3%"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a menu item that sells for p70.00 has a food cost percent of 20%. what would the food cost percent be if the selling price was increased to p150.00?:::', 'a menu item that sells for p70.00 has a food cost percent of 20%. what would the food cost percent be if the selling price was increased to p150.00?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Original Food Cost Percentage (Php70.00x 0.2 = 14.00 PHP)","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"9.3%\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Original Food Cost Percentage (Php70.00x 0.2 = 14.00 PHP)\n\nNew FCP= (Food Cost/New SP) x100\n\nNew FCP = (14.00/150.00) x 100 = 9.3%","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 10.3%, 11.3%, 8.3%"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:your recipe calls for 3 kg diced carrots. if a vendor sells a 10 kg case of carrots for p499.00, how much will the carrots purchased for this recipe cost? yield percent of carrots is 81.3%:::p184.13', 'your recipe calls for 3 kg diced carrots. if a vendor sells a 10 kg case of carrots for p499.00, how much will the carrots purchased for this recipe cost? yield percent of carrots is 81.3%', 'P184.13', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The edible yield required is 3 kg. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"P184.13\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The edible yield required is 3 kg. \n\nWith an 81.3% yield, the as-purchased quantity needed is 3 ÷ 0.813 ≈ 3.69 kg. \n\nAt ₱499 per 10 kg, the cost is approximately ₱184.13.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: P149.70, P121.70, None of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:your recipe calls for 3 kg diced carrots. if a vendor sells a 10 kg case of carrots for p499.00, how much will the carrots purchased for this recipe cost? yield percent of carrots is 81.3%:::', 'your recipe calls for 3 kg diced carrots. if a vendor sells a 10 kg case of carrots for p499.00, how much will the carrots purchased for this recipe cost? yield percent of carrots is 81.3%', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The edible yield required is 3 kg. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"P184.13\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The edible yield required is 3 kg. \n\nWith an 81.3% yield, the as-purchased quantity needed is 3 ÷ 0.813 ≈ 3.69 kg. \n\nAt ₱499 per 10 kg, the cost is approximately ₱184.13.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: P149.70, P121.70, None of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:you have a béarnaise recipe that makes seven 14-gram portions but you need to make enough to have twenty 8-gram portions. what is the recipe conversion factor?:::1.63', 'you have a béarnaise recipe that makes seven 14-gram portions but you need to make enough to have twenty 8-gram portions. what is the recipe conversion factor?', '1.63', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Original yield is 7 × 14 g = 98 g. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"1.63\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Original yield is 7 × 14 g = 98 g. \n\nRequired yield is 20 × 8 g = 160 g. \n\nThe conversion factor is 160 ÷ 98 ≈ 1.63.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 0.61, 0.57, None of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a salsa recipe calls for 3 oz chopped fresh cilantro to serve 48 guests. if chopped cilantro gives a 48% yield, how many servings of salsa can you make using 1 ½ lbs of cilantro you have purchased?:::184 servings', 'a salsa recipe calls for 3 oz chopped fresh cilantro to serve 48 guests. if chopped cilantro gives a 48% yield, how many servings of salsa can you make using 1 ½ lbs of cilantro you have purchased?', '184 servings', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"1 ½ lb equals 24 oz AP. At 48% yield, usable cilantro is 11.52 oz. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"184 servings\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"1 ½ lb equals 24 oz AP. At 48% yield, usable cilantro is 11.52 oz. \n\nAt 3 oz per 48 guests, this is about 184 servings.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 11 servings, 12 servings, 194 servings"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a salsa recipe calls for 3 oz chopped fresh cilantro to serve 48 guests. if chopped cilantro gives a 48% yield, how many servings of salsa can you make using 1 ½ lbs of cilantro you have purchased?:::', 'a salsa recipe calls for 3 oz chopped fresh cilantro to serve 48 guests. if chopped cilantro gives a 48% yield, how many servings of salsa can you make using 1 ½ lbs of cilantro you have purchased?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"1 ½ lb equals 24 oz AP. At 48% yield, usable cilantro is 11.52 oz. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"184 servings\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"1 ½ lb equals 24 oz AP. At 48% yield, usable cilantro is 11.52 oz. \n\nAt 3 oz per 48 guests, this is about 184 servings.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 11 servings, 12 servings, 194 servings"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:tea-like beverage using herbs/plants other than camella sinensis:::tisanes', 'tea-like beverage using herbs/plants other than camella sinensis', 'Tisanes', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Tisanes, commonly known as herbal teas, are beverages made from the infusion or decoction of herbs, spices, fruits, or other plant material in hot water. Unlike true teas (such as Oolong and Sencha), they do not contain leaves from the Camellia sinensis plant.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Tisanes\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Tisanes, commonly known as herbal teas, are beverages made from the infusion or decoction of herbs, spices, fruits, or other plant material in hot water. Unlike true teas (such as Oolong and Sencha), they do not contain leaves from the Camellia sinensis plant.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Oolong, Tea imitations, Sencha"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:tea-like beverage using herbs/plants other than camella sinensis:::', 'tea-like beverage using herbs/plants other than camella sinensis', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Tisanes, commonly known as herbal teas, are beverages made from the infusion or decoction of herbs, spices, fruits, or other plant material in hot water. Unlike true teas (such as Oolong and Sencha), they do not contain leaves from the Camellia sinensis plant.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Tisanes\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Tisanes, commonly known as herbal teas, are beverages made from the infusion or decoction of herbs, spices, fruits, or other plant material in hot water. Unlike true teas (such as Oolong and Sencha), they do not contain leaves from the Camellia sinensis plant.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Oolong, Tea imitations, Sencha"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:basis for recruiting personnel:::', 'basis for recruiting personnel', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Job description – an organized list of duties, skills and responsibilities required in a specific position. This should be written for every position and should be reviewed and updated periodically.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Job description\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Job description – an organized list of duties, skills and responsibilities required in a specific position. This should be written for every position and should be reviewed and updated periodically.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Performance rating scale, Procedure manual, Organizational chart"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:café au lait is similar to which italian coffee concoction:::caffe latte', 'café au lait is similar to which italian coffee concoction', 'Caffe Latte', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Café au lait is coffee served with hot milk and is most similar to caffè latte in the Italian coffee family. It is made by mixing equal parts of strongly brewed coffee (often from a French press or drip filter) and steamed milk.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Caffe Latte\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Café au lait is coffee served with hot milk and is most similar to caffè latte in the Italian coffee family. It is made by mixing equal parts of strongly brewed coffee (often from a French press or drip filter) and steamed milk.\n\n• Macchiato: An espresso shot \"stained\" or marked with just a tiny dollop of frothed milk or foam on top, making it significantly stronger and less milky than a latte.\n• Ristretto: A short, highly concentrated shot of espresso extracted with half the amount of water but using the same amount of coffee grounds, resulting in a sweeter, richer flavor.\n• Espresso: A concentrated, full-bodied coffee shot brewed by forcing hot water under high pressure through finely-ground coffee beans. It contains no milk at all.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Espresso, Macchiato, Ristretto"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:café au lait is similar to which italian coffee concoction:::', 'café au lait is similar to which italian coffee concoction', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Café au lait is coffee served with hot milk and is most similar to caffè latte in the Italian coffee family. It is made by mixing equal parts of strongly brewed coffee (often from a French press or drip filter) and steamed milk.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Caffe Latte\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Café au lait is coffee served with hot milk and is most similar to caffè latte in the Italian coffee family. It is made by mixing equal parts of strongly brewed coffee (often from a French press or drip filter) and steamed milk.\n\n• Macchiato: An espresso shot \"stained\" or marked with just a tiny dollop of frothed milk or foam on top, making it significantly stronger and less milky than a latte.\n• Ristretto: A short, highly concentrated shot of espresso extracted with half the amount of water but using the same amount of coffee grounds, resulting in a sweeter, richer flavor.\n• Espresso: A concentrated, full-bodied coffee shot brewed by forcing hot water under high pressure through finely-ground coffee beans. It contains no milk at all.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Espresso, Macchiato, Ristretto"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a detailed work plan showing the arrangement of equipment is called::::layout', 'a detailed work plan showing the arrangement of equipment is called:', 'Layout', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A layout is a detailed work plan showing the physical arrangement of equipment and work areas. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Layout\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A layout is a detailed work plan showing the physical arrangement of equipment and work areas. \n\nA blueprint is a technical drawing, while specifications describe requirements.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Blueprint, Specifications, Design"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a detailed work plan showing the arrangement of equipment is called::::', 'a detailed work plan showing the arrangement of equipment is called:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A layout is a detailed work plan showing the physical arrangement of equipment and work areas. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Layout\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A layout is a detailed work plan showing the physical arrangement of equipment and work areas. \n\nA blueprint is a technical drawing, while specifications describe requirements.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Blueprint, Specifications, Design"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the principle of “span of supervision” recognizes that::::there is a limit to a person’s capacity to supervise effectively', 'the principle of “span of supervision” recognizes that:', 'There is a limit to a person’s capacity to supervise effectively', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Span of supervision recognizes that there is a practical limit to the number of subordinates one person can supervise effectively.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"There is a limit to a person’s capacity to supervise effectively\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Span of supervision recognizes that there is a practical limit to the number of subordinates one person can supervise effectively.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: The more subordinates under one supervisor the better, The less subordinates, the better, The supervisor should be near the subordinates at all times"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the principle of “span of supervision” recognizes that::::', 'the principle of “span of supervision” recognizes that:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Span of supervision recognizes that there is a practical limit to the number of subordinates one person can supervise effectively.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"There is a limit to a person’s capacity to supervise effectively\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Span of supervision recognizes that there is a practical limit to the number of subordinates one person can supervise effectively.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: The more subordinates under one supervisor the better, The less subordinates, the better, The supervisor should be near the subordinates at all times"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a no.2 can of fruit juice contains:::2 ¼ cups', 'a no.2 can of fruit juice contains', '2 ¼ cups', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In standard Food Service and Institutional Management measurements, a No. 2 can has the following characteristics:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"2 ¼ cups\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In standard Food Service and Institutional Management measurements, a No. 2 can has the following characteristics:\n• Weight/Volume: Approximately 20 ounces (1 lb 4 oz)\n• Volume in Cups: Approximately 2 ½ cups (2.5 cups)\n• Average Servings: Offers about 5 servings of 4 ounces each.\n\nOther Common Can Sizes for Comparison:\n\n• No. 1 can: Contains about 1⅓ cups\n• No. 2 ½ can: Contains about 3 ½ cups\n• No. 3 can: Contains about 4 to 5 ¾ cups (often used for single-strength juices)\n• No. 10 can: Contains about 12 to 13 cups (the massive institutional standard can)","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 1 cup, 1 gallon, 2 gallons"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a no.2 can of fruit juice contains:::', 'a no.2 can of fruit juice contains', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In standard Food Service and Institutional Management measurements, a No. 2 can has the following characteristics:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"2 ¼ cups\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In standard Food Service and Institutional Management measurements, a No. 2 can has the following characteristics:\n• Weight/Volume: Approximately 20 ounces (1 lb 4 oz)\n• Volume in Cups: Approximately 2 ½ cups (2.5 cups)\n• Average Servings: Offers about 5 servings of 4 ounces each.\n\nOther Common Can Sizes for Comparison:\n\n• No. 1 can: Contains about 1⅓ cups\n• No. 2 ½ can: Contains about 3 ½ cups\n• No. 3 can: Contains about 4 to 5 ¾ cups (often used for single-strength juices)\n• No. 10 can: Contains about 12 to 13 cups (the massive institutional standard can)","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 1 cup, 1 gallon, 2 gallons"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:appropriate storage temperature for ripe fruits (except bananas).:::50ºf', 'appropriate storage temperature for ripe fruits (except bananas).', '50ºF', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"50ºF (approx. 10ºC) falls perfectly into the \"cool storage\" range (40–55°F) used to slow down the respiration and ripening process of many ripe, perishable fruits without causing chilling injuries or freezing.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"50ºF\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"50ºF (approx. 10ºC) falls perfectly into the \"cool storage\" range (40–55°F) used to slow down the respiration and ripening process of many ripe, perishable fruits without causing chilling injuries or freezing.\n\n20ºF is well below freezing and would destroy the cellular structure of the fruit, ruining its texture.\n\n50ºC (122ºF) is extremely hot and would cook the fruit, leading to rapid decay.\n\nRoom temperature accelerates spoiling and rot once a fruit is already fully ripe.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 20ºF, 50ºC, Roomtemperature"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:appropriate storage temperature for ripe fruits (except bananas).:::', 'appropriate storage temperature for ripe fruits (except bananas).', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"50ºF (approx. 10ºC) falls perfectly into the \"cool storage\" range (40–55°F) used to slow down the respiration and ripening process of many ripe, perishable fruits without causing chilling injuries or freezing.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"50ºF\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"50ºF (approx. 10ºC) falls perfectly into the \"cool storage\" range (40–55°F) used to slow down the respiration and ripening process of many ripe, perishable fruits without causing chilling injuries or freezing.\n\n20ºF is well below freezing and would destroy the cellular structure of the fruit, ruining its texture.\n\n50ºC (122ºF) is extremely hot and would cook the fruit, leading to rapid decay.\n\nRoom temperature accelerates spoiling and rot once a fruit is already fully ripe.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 20ºF, 50ºC, Roomtemperature"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:minimum internal cooking temperature of pork chop:::145ºf', 'minimum internal cooking temperature of pork chop', '145ºF', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to the USDA Safe Minimum Internal Temperature Chart, fresh whole muscle cuts of pork—including pork chops, pork roasts, and pork loins—must be cooked to a minimum internal temperature of 145°F (63°C).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"145ºF\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to the USDA Safe Minimum Internal Temperature Chart, fresh whole muscle cuts of pork—including pork chops, pork roasts, and pork loins—must be cooked to a minimum internal temperature of 145°F (63°C).\n\nTo safely hit this baseline, you must also allow the meat to rest for at least 3 minutes after removing it from the heat source. During this resting period, the internal temperature remains constant or continues to rise slightly, destroying harmful foodborne pathogens while keeping the meat juicy and tender.\n\n• 155ºF: This is the standard minimum internal cooking temperature for ground meats (such as ground pork, ground beef, or sausage) under regulatory food safety frameworks like ServSafe. Ground meats require a higher temperature because the grinding process distributes surface bacteria throughout the meat.\n• 165ºF: This is the required safety threshold for all poultry (chicken, turkey, duck), stuffed meats, and reheated leftovers.\n• 170ºF: This is an older, outdated standard for pork. Cooking modern, lean pork to 170°F results in a dry, tough, and overcooked piece of meat.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 170ºF, 155ºF, 165ºF"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:in the satellite food production system,:::food procurement and pre-preparation are centralized, but final preparation and service are decentralized', 'in the satellite food production system,', 'Food procurement and pre-preparation are
 centralized, but final preparation and service
 are decentralized', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"All bulk food procurement, inventory storage, and primary pre-preparation (and often major cooking) are handled entirely at a single, heavily equipped central facility.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Food procurement and pre-preparation are\n centralized, but final preparation and service\n are decentralized\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"All bulk food procurement, inventory storage, and primary pre-preparation (and often major cooking) are handled entirely at a single, heavily equipped central facility.\n\nThe prepared or semi-prepared menu items are then systematically transported to separate, remote satellite units (such as individual schools, hospitals, or smaller branches). These decentralized satellite sites handle the final preparation (like rethermalizing or portioning) and immediate service to the consumers.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: All the centers are decentralized, Food is prepared and then frozen until needed, Food procurement and pre-preparation are centralized, but final preparation and service are decentralized"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:minimum internal cooking temperature of pork chop:::', 'minimum internal cooking temperature of pork chop', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to the USDA Safe Minimum Internal Temperature Chart, fresh whole muscle cuts of pork—including pork chops, pork roasts, and pork loins—must be cooked to a minimum internal temperature of 145°F (63°C).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"145ºF\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to the USDA Safe Minimum Internal Temperature Chart, fresh whole muscle cuts of pork—including pork chops, pork roasts, and pork loins—must be cooked to a minimum internal temperature of 145°F (63°C).\n\nTo safely hit this baseline, you must also allow the meat to rest for at least 3 minutes after removing it from the heat source. During this resting period, the internal temperature remains constant or continues to rise slightly, destroying harmful foodborne pathogens while keeping the meat juicy and tender.\n\n• 155ºF: This is the standard minimum internal cooking temperature for ground meats (such as ground pork, ground beef, or sausage) under regulatory food safety frameworks like ServSafe. Ground meats require a higher temperature because the grinding process distributes surface bacteria throughout the meat.\n• 165ºF: This is the required safety threshold for all poultry (chicken, turkey, duck), stuffed meats, and reheated leftovers.\n• 170ºF: This is an older, outdated standard for pork. Cooking modern, lean pork to 170°F results in a dry, tough, and overcooked piece of meat.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 170ºF, 155ºF, 165ºF"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:minimum internal cooking temperature of salmon:::145ºf', 'minimum internal cooking temperature of salmon', '145ºF', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Salmon: 145°F (63°C) or cook until the flesh is opaque and flakes easily with a fork.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"145ºF\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Salmon: 145°F (63°C) or cook until the flesh is opaque and flakes easily with a fork.\n\n• 165ºF: The safe baseline for all poultry (chicken, turkey) and leftovers.\n• 155ºF: The standard food safety threshold often taught for ground meats (like pork or beef) under regional food codes before the general public safety chart rounds it to 160°F.\n• 170ºF: The internal temperature indicator for completely well-done beef or game meat.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 170ºF, 155ºF, 165ºF"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:minimum internal cooking temperature of salmon:::', 'minimum internal cooking temperature of salmon', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Salmon: 145°F (63°C) or cook until the flesh is opaque and flakes easily with a fork.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"145ºF\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Salmon: 145°F (63°C) or cook until the flesh is opaque and flakes easily with a fork.\n\n• 165ºF: The safe baseline for all poultry (chicken, turkey) and leftovers.\n• 155ºF: The standard food safety threshold often taught for ground meats (like pork or beef) under regional food codes before the general public safety chart rounds it to 160°F.\n• 170ºF: The internal temperature indicator for completely well-done beef or game meat.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 170ºF, 155ºF, 165ºF"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the total raw food cost of a recipe yielding 50 serving portions is ₱200. to keep raw food costs at 40%, the selling price per portion should be::::₱10.00', 'the total raw food cost of a recipe yielding 50 serving portions is ₱200. to keep raw food costs at 40%, the selling price per portion should be:', '₱10.00', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Food cost per serving is ₱200 ÷ 50 = ₱4. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"₱10.00\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Food cost per serving is ₱200 ÷ 50 = ₱4. \n\nIf ₱4 represents 40% of selling price, selling price = ₱4 ÷ 0.40 = ₱10.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: ₱4.00, ₱8.00, ₱80.00"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the total raw food cost of a recipe yielding 50 serving portions is ₱200. to keep raw food costs at 40%, the selling price per portion should be::::', 'the total raw food cost of a recipe yielding 50 serving portions is ₱200. to keep raw food costs at 40%, the selling price per portion should be:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Food cost per serving is ₱200 ÷ 50 = ₱4. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"₱10.00\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Food cost per serving is ₱200 ÷ 50 = ₱4. \n\nIf ₱4 represents 40% of selling price, selling price = ₱4 ÷ 0.40 = ₱10.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: ₱4.00, ₱8.00, ₱80.00"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:this equipment is useful when food production and service areas are on separate floors.:::dumbwaiter', 'this equipment is useful when food production and service areas are on separate floors.', 'Dumbwaiter', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A dumbwaiter is a small, specialized freight elevator or lift system designed to transport food, dishes, and kitchen supplies vertically between multiple floors of a building.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Dumbwaiter\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A dumbwaiter is a small, specialized freight elevator or lift system designed to transport food, dishes, and kitchen supplies vertically between multiple floors of a building.\n\nIn hospitality, hospitals, and multi-story restaurant settings, the main food production kitchen is often located in the basement or ground floor, while the service or dining areas are located on upper levels. A dumbwaiter allows hot meals to be quickly and safely dispatched to service stations on separate floors without relying on staff manually carrying heavy trays up and down stairs or taking up passenger elevators.\n\n• Hood: A kitchen exhaust ventilation canopy positioned directly above cooking equipment to remove steam, smoke, heat, and airborne grease.\n• Doilies: Small, decorative, ornamental mats made of paper, lace, or fabric used in food presentation (such as being placed on a saucer beneath a coffee cup or a dessert bowl).\n• Bain-marie: A specialized water bath system or hot well table used to hold, warm, or gently cook hot foods at a stable temperature in production or buffet service lines. It does not move food across different floors.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Doilies, Hood, Bainmarie"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:this equipment is useful when food production and service areas are on separate floors.:::', 'this equipment is useful when food production and service areas are on separate floors.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A dumbwaiter is a small, specialized freight elevator or lift system designed to transport food, dishes, and kitchen supplies vertically between multiple floors of a building.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Dumbwaiter\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A dumbwaiter is a small, specialized freight elevator or lift system designed to transport food, dishes, and kitchen supplies vertically between multiple floors of a building.\n\nIn hospitality, hospitals, and multi-story restaurant settings, the main food production kitchen is often located in the basement or ground floor, while the service or dining areas are located on upper levels. A dumbwaiter allows hot meals to be quickly and safely dispatched to service stations on separate floors without relying on staff manually carrying heavy trays up and down stairs or taking up passenger elevators.\n\n• Hood: A kitchen exhaust ventilation canopy positioned directly above cooking equipment to remove steam, smoke, heat, and airborne grease.\n• Doilies: Small, decorative, ornamental mats made of paper, lace, or fabric used in food presentation (such as being placed on a saucer beneath a coffee cup or a dessert bowl).\n• Bain-marie: A specialized water bath system or hot well table used to hold, warm, or gently cook hot foods at a stable temperature in production or buffet service lines. It does not move food across different floors.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Doilies, Hood, Bainmarie"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:bacteria commonly found in shellfish from contaminated water that can cause gastroenteritis or primary septicemia among patients with cirrhosis or diabetes.:::vibrio vulnificus', 'bacteria commonly found in shellfish from contaminated water that can cause gastroenteritis or primary septicemia among patients with cirrhosis or diabetes.', 'Vibrio vulnificus', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Vibrio vulnificus is a halophilic (salt-loving), Gram-negative bacterium naturally found in warm coastal and brackish waters. It frequently contaminates filter-feeding shellfish like oysters and clams.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vibrio vulnificus\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Vibrio vulnificus is a halophilic (salt-loving), Gram-negative bacterium naturally found in warm coastal and brackish waters. It frequently contaminates filter-feeding shellfish like oysters and clams.\n\nWhile it can cause mild gastroenteritis in healthy individuals, it poses an extreme danger to individuals with underlying health conditions—especially liver cirrhosis, chronic liver disease, or diabetes.\n\nIn these high-risk patients, eating contaminated raw/undercooked seafood can allow the bacteria to cross the intestinal wall into the bloodstream. This triggers primary septicemia (blood poisoning), which progresses rapidly into septic shock, severe blistering skin lesions (hemorrhagic bullae), and multiorgan failure. It carries a staggering mortality rate of over 50%.\n\n• Listeria monocytogenes: Known for contaminating deli meats, unpasteurized milk, and soft cheeses. While it causes severe illness in pregnant women, newborns, and immunocompromised individuals (listeriosis), it is not a hallmark cause of primary shellfish-borne septicemia in cirrhotic patients.\n• Shigella spp: A bacterium primarily transmitted via the fecal-oral route through contaminated food or water. It causes bacillary dysentery (severe diarrhea with blood/mucus) but does not typically present as marine-acquired septicemia.\n• Hepatitis A: A virus (not a bacterium) that can be contracted from shellfish harvested from sewage-polluted waters. It causes acute liver inflammation (infectious hepatitis) rather than bacterial gastroenteritis or rapid septic shock.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Shigella spp, Hepatitis A, Listeria monocytogenes"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:bacteria commonly found in shellfish from contaminated water that can cause gastroenteritis or primary septicemia among patients with cirrhosis or diabetes.:::', 'bacteria commonly found in shellfish from contaminated water that can cause gastroenteritis or primary septicemia among patients with cirrhosis or diabetes.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Vibrio vulnificus is a halophilic (salt-loving), Gram-negative bacterium naturally found in warm coastal and brackish waters. It frequently contaminates filter-feeding shellfish like oysters and clams.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vibrio vulnificus\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Vibrio vulnificus is a halophilic (salt-loving), Gram-negative bacterium naturally found in warm coastal and brackish waters. It frequently contaminates filter-feeding shellfish like oysters and clams.\n\nWhile it can cause mild gastroenteritis in healthy individuals, it poses an extreme danger to individuals with underlying health conditions—especially liver cirrhosis, chronic liver disease, or diabetes.\n\nIn these high-risk patients, eating contaminated raw/undercooked seafood can allow the bacteria to cross the intestinal wall into the bloodstream. This triggers primary septicemia (blood poisoning), which progresses rapidly into septic shock, severe blistering skin lesions (hemorrhagic bullae), and multiorgan failure. It carries a staggering mortality rate of over 50%.\n\n• Listeria monocytogenes: Known for contaminating deli meats, unpasteurized milk, and soft cheeses. While it causes severe illness in pregnant women, newborns, and immunocompromised individuals (listeriosis), it is not a hallmark cause of primary shellfish-borne septicemia in cirrhotic patients.\n• Shigella spp: A bacterium primarily transmitted via the fecal-oral route through contaminated food or water. It causes bacillary dysentery (severe diarrhea with blood/mucus) but does not typically present as marine-acquired septicemia.\n• Hepatitis A: A virus (not a bacterium) that can be contracted from shellfish harvested from sewage-polluted waters. It causes acute liver inflammation (infectious hepatitis) rather than bacterial gastroenteritis or rapid septic shock.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Shigella spp, Hepatitis A, Listeria monocytogenes"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:in the satellite food production system,:::', 'in the satellite food production system,', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"All bulk food procurement, inventory storage, and primary pre-preparation (and often major cooking) are handled entirely at a single, heavily equipped central facility.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Food procurement and pre-preparation are\n centralized, but final preparation and service\n are decentralized\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"All bulk food procurement, inventory storage, and primary pre-preparation (and often major cooking) are handled entirely at a single, heavily equipped central facility.\n\nThe prepared or semi-prepared menu items are then systematically transported to separate, remote satellite units (such as individual schools, hospitals, or smaller branches). These decentralized satellite sites handle the final preparation (like rethermalizing or portioning) and immediate service to the consumers.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: All the centers are decentralized, Food is prepared and then frozen until needed, Food procurement and pre-preparation are centralized, but final preparation and service are decentralized"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:when fish fillet is on the menu, you need fish that will be::::deboned and sliced', 'when fish fillet is on the menu, you need fish that will be:', 'deboned and sliced', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Fish fillets should be boneless and cut into appropriate portions or slices because a fillet is a boneless section of fish flesh.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"deboned and sliced\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Fish fillets should be boneless and cut into appropriate portions or slices because a fillet is a boneless section of fish flesh.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: served whole, flaked, stuffed"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb)
