('mc:sentence a. basi is fermented sugar cane. sentence b. lambanog is distilled basi.:::sentence a is correct', 'sentence a. basi is fermented sugar cane. sentence b. lambanog is distilled basi.', 'Sentence A is correct', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Basi – fermented drink from sugar cane. Very popular among the lIocanos in Northern regions.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sentence A is correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Basi – fermented drink from sugar cane. Very popular among the lIocanos in Northern regions.\na) Basing babae – sweet wine\nb) Basing lalake – dry wine\n\nLambanog – distilled drink from tuba; alcohol content is higher than tuba; common in Southern Luzon\n\nOther Philippine Alcoholic Beverages:\n• Tuba – sweet, fermented coconut sap obtained by tapping the young, flowering spathe of the coconut palm\n• Tapuy – rice wine common in the Cordillera region","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. basi is fermented sugar cane. sentence b. lambanog is distilled basi.:::', 'sentence a. basi is fermented sugar cane. sentence b. lambanog is distilled basi.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Basi – fermented drink from sugar cane. Very popular among the lIocanos in Northern regions.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sentence A is correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Basi – fermented drink from sugar cane. Very popular among the lIocanos in Northern regions.\na) Basing babae – sweet wine\nb) Basing lalake – dry wine\n\nLambanog – distilled drink from tuba; alcohol content is higher than tuba; common in Southern Luzon\n\nOther Philippine Alcoholic Beverages:\n• Tuba – sweet, fermented coconut sap obtained by tapping the young, flowering spathe of the coconut palm\n• Tapuy – rice wine common in the Cordillera region","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:processed cheese is predominantly made with:::', 'processed cheese is predominantly made with', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Processed cheese is predominantly made with natural cheese, typically accounting for about 50–60% of its composition).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"None of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Processed cheese is predominantly made with natural cheese, typically accounting for about 50–60% of its composition).\n\nWater, dairy solids, emulsifying salts, and sometimes vegetable oils or fats make up the remaining portion.\n\nMilk: While derived from milk originally via the natural cheese base, processed cheese is blended from finished cheese rather than fluid milk.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Milk, Vegetable oil, Water"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:deterioration that is formed when unwrapped or uncovered food items are stored in low temperature.:::freezer burn', 'deterioration that is formed when unwrapped or uncovered food items are stored in low temperature.', 'freezer burn', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When food items are left unwrapped or uncovered in a low-temperature environment (specifically a freezer), the cold, dry air continuously pulls moisture out from the surface of the food. This leads to sublimation (where ice crystals turn directly into water vapor), leaving behind dehydrated, dry, and spongy pockets on the food''s surface.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"freezer burn\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When food items are left unwrapped or uncovered in a low-temperature environment (specifically a freezer), the cold, dry air continuously pulls moisture out from the surface of the food. This leads to sublimation (where ice crystals turn directly into water vapor), leaving behind dehydrated, dry, and spongy pockets on the food''s surface.\n\n• Case hardening: A structural defect that occurs during high-temperature drying or dehydration. If the heat is turned up too high too quickly, the outer surface of the food dries out and forms a hard, impermeable crust, trapping moisture inside and preventing proper preservation.\n• Oxidative rancidity: The chemical breakdown of fats and oils caused by exposure to oxygen, resulting in an off-odor and unpleasant flavor. While open freezing accelerates oxygen exposure, freezer burn is the direct, visible physical deterioration that forms on uncovered foods.\n• Putrefaction: The biological decomposition of proteins by anaerobic bacteria, resulting in foul odors. This happens in warm or improper storage conditions, as freezing temperatures typically halt or drastically slow down putrefactive bacterial growth.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: oxidative rancidity, case hardening, Putrefaction"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:deterioration that is formed when unwrapped or uncovered food items are stored in low temperature.:::', 'deterioration that is formed when unwrapped or uncovered food items are stored in low temperature.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When food items are left unwrapped or uncovered in a low-temperature environment (specifically a freezer), the cold, dry air continuously pulls moisture out from the surface of the food. This leads to sublimation (where ice crystals turn directly into water vapor), leaving behind dehydrated, dry, and spongy pockets on the food''s surface.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"freezer burn\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When food items are left unwrapped or uncovered in a low-temperature environment (specifically a freezer), the cold, dry air continuously pulls moisture out from the surface of the food. This leads to sublimation (where ice crystals turn directly into water vapor), leaving behind dehydrated, dry, and spongy pockets on the food''s surface.\n\n• Case hardening: A structural defect that occurs during high-temperature drying or dehydration. If the heat is turned up too high too quickly, the outer surface of the food dries out and forms a hard, impermeable crust, trapping moisture inside and preventing proper preservation.\n• Oxidative rancidity: The chemical breakdown of fats and oils caused by exposure to oxygen, resulting in an off-odor and unpleasant flavor. While open freezing accelerates oxygen exposure, freezer burn is the direct, visible physical deterioration that forms on uncovered foods.\n• Putrefaction: The biological decomposition of proteins by anaerobic bacteria, resulting in foul odors. This happens in warm or improper storage conditions, as freezing temperatures typically halt or drastically slow down putrefactive bacterial growth.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: oxidative rancidity, case hardening, Putrefaction"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:certification that a food safety management system is in place:::iso 22000', 'certification that a food safety management system is in place', 'ISO 22000', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"ISO 22000 is the correct answer because it specifically defines the international requirements for a Food Safety Management System (FSMS). ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"ISO 22000\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"ISO 22000 is the correct answer because it specifically defines the international requirements for a Food Safety Management System (FSMS). \n\n• Good Manufacturing Practices (GMP): Operational guidelines and baseline conditions for safe food processing facilities, rather than an overarching management system certification.\n• Good Agricultural Practices (GAP): Principles for safe and sustainable farming practices at the primary production level.\n• CODEX Alimentarius: A collection of international standards, guidelines, and codes of practice adopted by the FAO/WHO, rather than a certifiable management system itself.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Good Manufacturing practices, Good Agricultural practices, CODEX Alimentarius"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:certification that a food safety management system is in place:::', 'certification that a food safety management system is in place', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"ISO 22000 is the correct answer because it specifically defines the international requirements for a Food Safety Management System (FSMS). ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"ISO 22000\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"ISO 22000 is the correct answer because it specifically defines the international requirements for a Food Safety Management System (FSMS). \n\n• Good Manufacturing Practices (GMP): Operational guidelines and baseline conditions for safe food processing facilities, rather than an overarching management system certification.\n• Good Agricultural Practices (GAP): Principles for safe and sustainable farming practices at the primary production level.\n• CODEX Alimentarius: A collection of international standards, guidelines, and codes of practice adopted by the FAO/WHO, rather than a certifiable management system itself.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Good Manufacturing practices, Good Agricultural practices, CODEX Alimentarius"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:processed cheese is predominantly made with:::none of the above', 'processed cheese is predominantly made with', 'None of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Processed cheese is predominantly made with natural cheese, typically accounting for about 50–60% of its composition).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"None of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Processed cheese is predominantly made with natural cheese, typically accounting for about 50–60% of its composition).\n\nWater, dairy solids, emulsifying salts, and sometimes vegetable oils or fats make up the remaining portion.\n\nMilk: While derived from milk originally via the natural cheese base, processed cheese is blended from finished cheese rather than fluid milk.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Milk, Vegetable oil, Water"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:precipitating agent of pectin causing it to form network of the jelly:::sugar', 'precipitating agent of pectin causing it to form network of the jelly', 'Sugar', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sugar acts as the precipitating agent that draws water away from the pectin molecules, allowing them to bond and form the network of the gel.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sugar\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sugar acts as the precipitating agent that draws water away from the pectin molecules, allowing them to bond and form the network of the gel.\n\nIt attracts and holds water, breaking the pectin-water equilibrium and forcing the pectin chains to link together.\n\n• Acid: Neutralizes the negative charges on pectin molecules so they do not repel each other, assisting the set.\n• High heat activates the gelling properties of pectin so it can form a molecular web that traps the remaining liquid and thickens the jam. The ideal setting temperature for homemade jam is  around 220°F (105°C) at sea level, but may vary depending on type of fruit used.\n• Water naturally exists inside fruit cells and acts as the liquid medium where sugar and natural pectin dissolve.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Acid, Heat, water"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:precipitating agent of pectin causing it to form network of the jelly:::', 'precipitating agent of pectin causing it to form network of the jelly', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sugar acts as the precipitating agent that draws water away from the pectin molecules, allowing them to bond and form the network of the gel.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sugar\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sugar acts as the precipitating agent that draws water away from the pectin molecules, allowing them to bond and form the network of the gel.\n\nIt attracts and holds water, breaking the pectin-water equilibrium and forcing the pectin chains to link together.\n\n• Acid: Neutralizes the negative charges on pectin molecules so they do not repel each other, assisting the set.\n• High heat activates the gelling properties of pectin so it can form a molecular web that traps the remaining liquid and thickens the jam. The ideal setting temperature for homemade jam is  around 220°F (105°C) at sea level, but may vary depending on type of fruit used.\n• Water naturally exists inside fruit cells and acts as the liquid medium where sugar and natural pectin dissolve.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Acid, Heat, water"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the process of jelly making involves boiling fruit to::::extract pectin, obtain maximum juice and extract flavor', 'the process of jelly making involves boiling fruit to:', 'extract pectin, obtain maximum juice and extract flavor', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Boiling fruit in jelly making helps extract pectin and juice and develop the characteristic flavor of the finished jelly.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"extract pectin, obtain maximum juice and extract flavor\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Boiling fruit in jelly making helps extract pectin and juice and develop the characteristic flavor of the finished jelly.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: hydrolyze pectic acid, dissolve the fruit pulp and extract color, dissolve the pectic acid, extract the sugar and flavor, volatize the aroma, extract pulp and hydrolyze the protopectin"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the process of jelly making involves boiling fruit to::::', 'the process of jelly making involves boiling fruit to:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Boiling fruit in jelly making helps extract pectin and juice and develop the characteristic flavor of the finished jelly.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"extract pectin, obtain maximum juice and extract flavor\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Boiling fruit in jelly making helps extract pectin and juice and develop the characteristic flavor of the finished jelly.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: hydrolyze pectic acid, dissolve the fruit pulp and extract color, dissolve the pectic acid, extract the sugar and flavor, volatize the aroma, extract pulp and hydrolyze the protopectin"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:minimum internal cooking temperature of eggs:::', 'minimum internal cooking temperature of eggs', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Eggs: 160°F (71°C) for combined dishes like casseroles or quiches; for whole, fried, or scrambled eggs, cook until both the white and the yolk are firm.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"160ºF\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Eggs: 160°F (71°C) for combined dishes like casseroles or quiches; for whole, fried, or scrambled eggs, cook until both the white and the yolk are firm.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 140ºF, 150ºF, 170ºF"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are all vegetable dishes except::::none of the above', 'the following are all vegetable dishes except:', 'None of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Laswa, bulanglang, and utan are vegetable dishes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"None of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Laswa, bulanglang, and utan are vegetable dishes.\n\n• Laswa: A famous, clear-broth vegetable soup native to the Western Visayas (Iloilo). It is packed with local vegetables like okra, saluyot, squash, and eggplant, and seasoned simply with salt or fish sauce.\n• Utan: The native Visayan and Mindanao term for a clear, healthy vegetable broth (most commonly Utan Bisaya or Utan Kamunggay), featuring leafy greens, squash, and root vegetables.\n• Bulanglang: A traditional Southern Tagalog (specifically Batangas) vegetable stew made by boiling a medley of local crops like calabaza squash, okra, sitaw, and moringa leaves in rice-washing water seasoned with bagoong.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Laswa, Bulanglang, Utan"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are all vegetable dishes except::::', 'the following are all vegetable dishes except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Laswa, bulanglang, and utan are vegetable dishes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"None of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Laswa, bulanglang, and utan are vegetable dishes.\n\n• Laswa: A famous, clear-broth vegetable soup native to the Western Visayas (Iloilo). It is packed with local vegetables like okra, saluyot, squash, and eggplant, and seasoned simply with salt or fish sauce.\n• Utan: The native Visayan and Mindanao term for a clear, healthy vegetable broth (most commonly Utan Bisaya or Utan Kamunggay), featuring leafy greens, squash, and root vegetables.\n• Bulanglang: A traditional Southern Tagalog (specifically Batangas) vegetable stew made by boiling a medley of local crops like calabaza squash, okra, sitaw, and moringa leaves in rice-washing water seasoned with bagoong.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Laswa, Bulanglang, Utan"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are all noodle dishes, except::::picadillo', 'the following are all noodle dishes, except:', 'Picadillo', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Picadillo is a savory, traditional ground meat dish found in Spain and Latin America. In the Philippines, Picadillo is known locally as giniling, it is often cooked as a hearty soup or dry sauté with diced potatoes, carrots, and green peas and served over steamed white rice or as arroz a la cubana alongside a fried egg.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Picadillo\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Picadillo is a savory, traditional ground meat dish found in Spain and Latin America. In the Philippines, Picadillo is known locally as giniling, it is often cooked as a hearty soup or dry sauté with diced potatoes, carrots, and green peas and served over steamed white rice or as arroz a la cubana alongside a fried egg.\n\n• La Paz Batchoy: A legendary Filipino noodle soup that originated in the district of La Paz, Iloilo City. It is made with fresh egg noodles (miki), a rich pork and shrimp paste (ginamos) broth, sliced pork innards, and a heavy topping of crushed pork cracklings (chicharon).\n• Bami: Also known as Pancit Bami, this is a classic Visayan (particularly Cebuano) stir-fried noodle dish. It features a unique combination of two types of noodles tossed together: thick egg noodles (miki) and translucent mung bean thread noodles (sotanghon).\n• Laksa: A widely popular Southeast Asian spicy noodle soup predominant in Peranakan cuisine (Malaysia, Singapore, and Indonesia). It features thick rice noodles submerged in either a rich, fiery coconut curry broth or a sour, tamarind-based fish broth.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Bami, La Paz Batchoy, Laksa"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are all noodle dishes, except::::', 'the following are all noodle dishes, except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Picadillo is a savory, traditional ground meat dish found in Spain and Latin America. In the Philippines, Picadillo is known locally as giniling, it is often cooked as a hearty soup or dry sauté with diced potatoes, carrots, and green peas and served over steamed white rice or as arroz a la cubana alongside a fried egg.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Picadillo\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Picadillo is a savory, traditional ground meat dish found in Spain and Latin America. In the Philippines, Picadillo is known locally as giniling, it is often cooked as a hearty soup or dry sauté with diced potatoes, carrots, and green peas and served over steamed white rice or as arroz a la cubana alongside a fried egg.\n\n• La Paz Batchoy: A legendary Filipino noodle soup that originated in the district of La Paz, Iloilo City. It is made with fresh egg noodles (miki), a rich pork and shrimp paste (ginamos) broth, sliced pork innards, and a heavy topping of crushed pork cracklings (chicharon).\n• Bami: Also known as Pancit Bami, this is a classic Visayan (particularly Cebuano) stir-fried noodle dish. It features a unique combination of two types of noodles tossed together: thick egg noodles (miki) and translucent mung bean thread noodles (sotanghon).\n• Laksa: A widely popular Southeast Asian spicy noodle soup predominant in Peranakan cuisine (Malaysia, Singapore, and Indonesia). It features thick rice noodles submerged in either a rich, fiery coconut curry broth or a sour, tamarind-based fish broth.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Bami, La Paz Batchoy, Laksa"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a dish of rice, ground pork and bananas.:::arroz a la cubana', 'a dish of rice, ground pork and bananas.', 'Arroz a la Cubana', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Arroz a la Cubana (literally \"Cuban-style rice\") is a classic comfort dish that is highly popular in Filipino cuisine. It traditionally consists of a visual layout featuring a mound of white rice, a savory ground meat hash (picadillo/giniling usually made with ground pork or beef sautéed with raisins and peas), fried saba bananas (plantains), and a fried egg.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Arroz a la Cubana\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Arroz a la Cubana (literally \"Cuban-style rice\") is a classic comfort dish that is highly popular in Filipino cuisine. It traditionally consists of a visual layout featuring a mound of white rice, a savory ground meat hash (picadillo/giniling usually made with ground pork or beef sautéed with raisins and peas), fried saba bananas (plantains), and a fried egg.\n\n• Paella: A famous Spanish rice dish cooked in a wide, shallow pan, typically flavored with saffron and loaded with seafood, chicken, and vegetables.\n• Torta: In Filipino cuisine, a torta refers to an omelet or pan-fried patty (such as tortang talong made with eggplant, or tortang giniling made with ground meat mixed into beaten eggs). It is not served as a specific configuration of rice and bananas.\n• Arroz Valenciana: A traditional Filipino festive rice dish heavily influenced by Spanish paella. It features glutinous (sticky) rice cooked with coconut milk, turmeric (or annatto oil), chicken, chorizo, and boiled eggs, but it does not feature ground meat or fried bananas.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Arroz Valenciana, Torta, Paella"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a dish of rice, ground pork and bananas.:::', 'a dish of rice, ground pork and bananas.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Arroz a la Cubana (literally \"Cuban-style rice\") is a classic comfort dish that is highly popular in Filipino cuisine. It traditionally consists of a visual layout featuring a mound of white rice, a savory ground meat hash (picadillo/giniling usually made with ground pork or beef sautéed with raisins and peas), fried saba bananas (plantains), and a fried egg.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Arroz a la Cubana\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Arroz a la Cubana (literally \"Cuban-style rice\") is a classic comfort dish that is highly popular in Filipino cuisine. It traditionally consists of a visual layout featuring a mound of white rice, a savory ground meat hash (picadillo/giniling usually made with ground pork or beef sautéed with raisins and peas), fried saba bananas (plantains), and a fried egg.\n\n• Paella: A famous Spanish rice dish cooked in a wide, shallow pan, typically flavored with saffron and loaded with seafood, chicken, and vegetables.\n• Torta: In Filipino cuisine, a torta refers to an omelet or pan-fried patty (such as tortang talong made with eggplant, or tortang giniling made with ground meat mixed into beaten eggs). It is not served as a specific configuration of rice and bananas.\n• Arroz Valenciana: A traditional Filipino festive rice dish heavily influenced by Spanish paella. It features glutinous (sticky) rice cooked with coconut milk, turmeric (or annatto oil), chicken, chorizo, and boiled eggs, but it does not feature ground meat or fried bananas.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Arroz Valenciana, Torta, Paella"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:filipino dish traditionally made of chicken, rice washing, green papaya, pepper leaves, and ginger.:::tinola', 'filipino dish traditionally made of chicken, rice washing, green papaya, pepper leaves, and ginger.', 'Tinola', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Tinola (specifically Tinolang Manok) is a traditional Filipino soup that serves as the quintessential comfort food. It features chicken pieces simmered in rice washing (the water saved from rinsing raw rice, which acts as a starchy, flavorful broth base) heavily flavored with crushed ginger, garlic, and onions. It is traditionally garnished with wedged green papaya (or chayote) and fresh chili pepper leaves (siling labuyo leaves).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Tinola\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Tinola (specifically Tinolang Manok) is a traditional Filipino soup that serves as the quintessential comfort food. It features chicken pieces simmered in rice washing (the water saved from rinsing raw rice, which acts as a starchy, flavorful broth base) heavily flavored with crushed ginger, garlic, and onions. It is traditionally garnished with wedged green papaya (or chayote) and fresh chili pepper leaves (siling labuyo leaves).\n\n• Suam: A traditional Kapampangan soup (like Suam na Mais) made with shredded sweet corn, meat, and spinach or chili leaves. While it uses ginger and rice washing, its main structural component is corn rather than a clear chicken broth with papaya.\n• Pesa: A ginger-based fish or chicken stew (like Pesang Dalag) that is cooked with cabbage, potatoes, pechay, and whole black peppercorns. It is traditionally served alongside a side dipping sauce made of fermented soybean cake (miso).\n• Nilaga: A broad term for a boiled Filipino meat soup (usually beef or pork). While it features a clear broth, it is seasoned predominantly with whole black peppercorns and onions rather than a heavy ginger base, and it uses vegetables like cabbage, pechay, potatoes, and saba bananas.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Suam, Pesa, Nilaga"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:filipino dish traditionally made of chicken, rice washing, green papaya, pepper leaves, and ginger.:::', 'filipino dish traditionally made of chicken, rice washing, green papaya, pepper leaves, and ginger.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Tinola (specifically Tinolang Manok) is a traditional Filipino soup that serves as the quintessential comfort food. It features chicken pieces simmered in rice washing (the water saved from rinsing raw rice, which acts as a starchy, flavorful broth base) heavily flavored with crushed ginger, garlic, and onions. It is traditionally garnished with wedged green papaya (or chayote) and fresh chili pepper leaves (siling labuyo leaves).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Tinola\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Tinola (specifically Tinolang Manok) is a traditional Filipino soup that serves as the quintessential comfort food. It features chicken pieces simmered in rice washing (the water saved from rinsing raw rice, which acts as a starchy, flavorful broth base) heavily flavored with crushed ginger, garlic, and onions. It is traditionally garnished with wedged green papaya (or chayote) and fresh chili pepper leaves (siling labuyo leaves).\n\n• Suam: A traditional Kapampangan soup (like Suam na Mais) made with shredded sweet corn, meat, and spinach or chili leaves. While it uses ginger and rice washing, its main structural component is corn rather than a clear chicken broth with papaya.\n• Pesa: A ginger-based fish or chicken stew (like Pesang Dalag) that is cooked with cabbage, potatoes, pechay, and whole black peppercorns. It is traditionally served alongside a side dipping sauce made of fermented soybean cake (miso).\n• Nilaga: A broad term for a boiled Filipino meat soup (usually beef or pork). While it features a clear broth, it is seasoned predominantly with whole black peppercorns and onions rather than a heavy ginger base, and it uses vegetables like cabbage, pechay, potatoes, and saba bananas.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Suam, Pesa, Nilaga"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:management by objective implies the need to have:::clearly defined key result areas and time', 'management by objective implies the need to have', 'Clearly defined key result areas and time', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Management by Objectives (MBO) is a strategic management model that aims to improve organizational performance by aligning employee goals with the overall objectives of the company. It fundamentally relies on setting specific, measurable targets (Key Result Areas) within a designated timeframe so that progress can be tracked objectively.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Clearly defined key result areas and time\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Management by Objectives (MBO) is a strategic management model that aims to improve organizational performance by aligning employee goals with the overall objectives of the company. It fundamentally relies on setting specific, measurable targets (Key Result Areas) within a designated timeframe so that progress can be tracked objectively.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: A good manager, Clear organizational chart, Adequate staff"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:management by objective implies the need to have:::', 'management by objective implies the need to have', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Management by Objectives (MBO) is a strategic management model that aims to improve organizational performance by aligning employee goals with the overall objectives of the company. It fundamentally relies on setting specific, measurable targets (Key Result Areas) within a designated timeframe so that progress can be tracked objectively.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Clearly defined key result areas and time\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Management by Objectives (MBO) is a strategic management model that aims to improve organizational performance by aligning employee goals with the overall objectives of the company. It fundamentally relies on setting specific, measurable targets (Key Result Areas) within a designated timeframe so that progress can be tracked objectively.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: A good manager, Clear organizational chart, Adequate staff"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:tsokolate espeso refers to::::thick chocolate drink', 'tsokolate espeso refers to:', 'thick chocolate drink', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In Filipino culinary tradition (heavily influenced by Spanish terminology), the word espeso translates to \"thick\" or \"dense.\" Therefore, Tsokolate Espeso refers to a rich, velvety, and thick hot chocolate beverage traditionally prepared by melting pure cacao tablets (tabliya) in a special copper pitcher called a tsokolatera and frothing it vigorously with a wooden whisk called a batidor.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"thick chocolate drink\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In Filipino culinary tradition (heavily influenced by Spanish terminology), the word espeso translates to \"thick\" or \"dense.\" Therefore, Tsokolate Espeso refers to a rich, velvety, and thick hot chocolate beverage traditionally prepared by melting pure cacao tablets (tabliya) in a special copper pitcher called a tsokolatera and frothing it vigorously with a wooden whisk called a batidor.\n\n• Café Mocha / Mochaccino: These are espresso-based coffee drinks that combine milk and chocolate syrup or cocoa powder. They are not purely cacao-based.\n• Thin chocolate drink: A thin, lighter version of traditional hot chocolate is instead called Tsokolate Ahon (or tsokolate aguado, meaning watery).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Café Mocha, Mochaccino, thin chocolate drink"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:porto is a::::wine', 'porto is a:', 'Wine', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Vinho do Porto (commonly known simply as Porto or Port wine) is a Portuguese fortified wine produced exclusively in the Douro Valley in the northern provinces of Portugal.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Wine\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Vinho do Porto (commonly known simply as Porto or Port wine) is a Portuguese fortified wine produced exclusively in the Douro Valley in the northern provinces of Portugal.\n\nIt is called a fortified wine because a neutral grape spirit (known as aguardente, similar to brandy) is added during the fermentation process. This stops fermentation early, leaving residual sugar in the wine and boosting its alcohol content—usually to around 19% to 22% ABV.\n\nIt is typically a sweet, rich red wine served as a digestif or dessert wine, though it also comes in dry, semi-dry, and white varieties. It gets its name from the coastal city of Porto, where the wine is aged and shipped globally.\n\n• Whiskey: A distilled alcoholic beverage made from fermented grain mash (such as barley, corn, rye, or wheat).\n• Beer: An alcoholic beverage produced by brewing and fermenting starches, mainly derived from cereal grains—most commonly malted barley, though wheat, maize (corn), rice, and oats are also used.\n• Brandy: A spirit produced by distilling wine or fermented fruit mash. While brandy (specifically a clear grape spirit) is added to Porto to stop its fermentation, Porto itself remains classified as a fortified wine rather than a distilled spirit like brandy.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Beer, Whiskey, Brandy"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:porto is a::::', 'porto is a:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Vinho do Porto (commonly known simply as Porto or Port wine) is a Portuguese fortified wine produced exclusively in the Douro Valley in the northern provinces of Portugal.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Wine\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Vinho do Porto (commonly known simply as Porto or Port wine) is a Portuguese fortified wine produced exclusively in the Douro Valley in the northern provinces of Portugal.\n\nIt is called a fortified wine because a neutral grape spirit (known as aguardente, similar to brandy) is added during the fermentation process. This stops fermentation early, leaving residual sugar in the wine and boosting its alcohol content—usually to around 19% to 22% ABV.\n\nIt is typically a sweet, rich red wine served as a digestif or dessert wine, though it also comes in dry, semi-dry, and white varieties. It gets its name from the coastal city of Porto, where the wine is aged and shipped globally.\n\n• Whiskey: A distilled alcoholic beverage made from fermented grain mash (such as barley, corn, rye, or wheat).\n• Beer: An alcoholic beverage produced by brewing and fermenting starches, mainly derived from cereal grains—most commonly malted barley, though wheat, maize (corn), rice, and oats are also used.\n• Brandy: A spirit produced by distilling wine or fermented fruit mash. While brandy (specifically a clear grape spirit) is added to Porto to stop its fermentation, Porto itself remains classified as a fortified wine rather than a distilled spirit like brandy.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Beer, Whiskey, Brandy"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are liqueurs except::::grenadine', 'the following are liqueurs except:', 'Grenadine', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Kahlúa, crème de menthe, and Baileys are liqueurs. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Grenadine\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Kahlúa, crème de menthe, and Baileys are liqueurs. \n\nGrenadine is a sweet syrup used as a cocktail ingredient and is not itself a liqueur.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Kahlua, Crème de Menthe, Baileys"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are liqueurs except::::', 'the following are liqueurs except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Kahlúa, crème de menthe, and Baileys are liqueurs. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Grenadine\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Kahlúa, crème de menthe, and Baileys are liqueurs. \n\nGrenadine is a sweet syrup used as a cocktail ingredient and is not itself a liqueur.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Kahlua, Crème de Menthe, Baileys"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a flapjack refers to::::pancake', 'a flapjack refers to:', 'Pancake', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"\"Flapjack\" is a traditional, centuries-old colloquial term used primarily in North America to refer to a pancake or griddle cake.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Pancake\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"\"Flapjack\" is a traditional, centuries-old colloquial term used primarily in North America to refer to a pancake or griddle cake.\n\n• Hash brown: Shredded or finely chopped potatoes that are pan-fried until golden brown.\n• Shoe string potatoes: Extremely thin, finely cut strips of potato that are deep-fried until very crisp (similar to thin French fries).\n• Popcorn: Puffed, heated corn kernels.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: shoe string potatoes, popcorn, hashbrown"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a flapjack refers to::::', 'a flapjack refers to:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"\"Flapjack\" is a traditional, centuries-old colloquial term used primarily in North America to refer to a pancake or griddle cake.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Pancake\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"\"Flapjack\" is a traditional, centuries-old colloquial term used primarily in North America to refer to a pancake or griddle cake.\n\n• Hash brown: Shredded or finely chopped potatoes that are pan-fried until golden brown.\n• Shoe string potatoes: Extremely thin, finely cut strips of potato that are deep-fried until very crisp (similar to thin French fries).\n• Popcorn: Puffed, heated corn kernels.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: shoe string potatoes, popcorn, hashbrown"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:tsokolate espeso refers to::::', 'tsokolate espeso refers to:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In Filipino culinary tradition (heavily influenced by Spanish terminology), the word espeso translates to \"thick\" or \"dense.\" Therefore, Tsokolate Espeso refers to a rich, velvety, and thick hot chocolate beverage traditionally prepared by melting pure cacao tablets (tabliya) in a special copper pitcher called a tsokolatera and frothing it vigorously with a wooden whisk called a batidor.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"thick chocolate drink\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In Filipino culinary tradition (heavily influenced by Spanish terminology), the word espeso translates to \"thick\" or \"dense.\" Therefore, Tsokolate Espeso refers to a rich, velvety, and thick hot chocolate beverage traditionally prepared by melting pure cacao tablets (tabliya) in a special copper pitcher called a tsokolatera and frothing it vigorously with a wooden whisk called a batidor.\n\n• Café Mocha / Mochaccino: These are espresso-based coffee drinks that combine milk and chocolate syrup or cocoa powder. They are not purely cacao-based.\n• Thin chocolate drink: A thin, lighter version of traditional hot chocolate is instead called Tsokolate Ahon (or tsokolate aguado, meaning watery).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Café Mocha, Mochaccino, thin chocolate drink"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:churros is more like which filipino fritter:::bitso-bitso', 'churros is more like which filipino fritter', 'Bitso-bitso', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Churros are closest in form to bitso-bitso, a fried Filipino dough snack, rather than vegetable fritters or toasted bread products.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Bitso-bitso\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Churros are closest in form to bitso-bitso, a fried Filipino dough snack, rather than vegetable fritters or toasted bread products.\n\n Classic Filipino bitso-bitso is made by twisting or shaping yeast- or flour-based dough into sticks, deep-frying them until golden and crisp, and then dusting them with white sugar or coating them in a caramel glaze. It mirrors the exact preparation mechanism and eating experience of a Spanish churro.\n\n• Biscocho: A twice-baked, crunchy Filipino bread topper (similar to biscotti or melba toast) that is brushed with butter and sugar. It is baked dry until crisp, not deep-fried.\n• Gurgurya: A small, hard, shell-like Filipino pastry or cookie that is deep-fried and then coated in a sugary glaze. While fried, it has a dense, hard cookie crunch rather than the soft-on-the-inside, crisp-on-the-outside structural profile of a churro.\n• Okoy: A distinctly savory Filipino street food fritter made of shredded vegetables, bean sprouts, and small whole shrimp tossed in an egg-and-flour batter and fried flat.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Okoy, Gurgurya, Biscocho"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:churros is more like which filipino fritter:::', 'churros is more like which filipino fritter', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Churros are closest in form to bitso-bitso, a fried Filipino dough snack, rather than vegetable fritters or toasted bread products.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Bitso-bitso\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Churros are closest in form to bitso-bitso, a fried Filipino dough snack, rather than vegetable fritters or toasted bread products.\n\n Classic Filipino bitso-bitso is made by twisting or shaping yeast- or flour-based dough into sticks, deep-frying them until golden and crisp, and then dusting them with white sugar or coating them in a caramel glaze. It mirrors the exact preparation mechanism and eating experience of a Spanish churro.\n\n• Biscocho: A twice-baked, crunchy Filipino bread topper (similar to biscotti or melba toast) that is brushed with butter and sugar. It is baked dry until crisp, not deep-fried.\n• Gurgurya: A small, hard, shell-like Filipino pastry or cookie that is deep-fried and then coated in a sugary glaze. While fried, it has a dense, hard cookie crunch rather than the soft-on-the-inside, crisp-on-the-outside structural profile of a churro.\n• Okoy: A distinctly savory Filipino street food fritter made of shredded vegetables, bean sprouts, and small whole shrimp tossed in an egg-and-flour batter and fried flat.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Okoy, Gurgurya, Biscocho"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:open market buying means:::after a canvass, purchases are made from the vendor who offers the best terms', 'open market buying means', 'After a canvass, purchases are made from the vendor who offers the best terms', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Open market buying is a flexible procurement method commonly used in food service and small businesses. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"After a canvass, purchases are made from the vendor who offers the best terms\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Open market buying is a flexible procurement method commonly used in food service and small businesses. \n\nInstead of locking into a long-term contract with a single supplier, the buyer \"canvasses\" (requests price quotes from) multiple vendors and places the order with whoever provides the best price, quality, and delivery terms for that specific purchase.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: The buyer has to go to the market, Purchasing through a middle man, Purchasing from one source only"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb)
