('mc:subs are::::all of the above', 'subs are:', 'all of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Subs are sandwiches also known as hoagies or heroes, depending on regional terminology. Therefore all the listed descriptions apply.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"all of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Subs are sandwiches also known as hoagies or heroes, depending on regional terminology. Therefore all the listed descriptions apply.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Hoagies, Heros, Sandwiches"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:during prolonged starvation, which of the following substances can serve as an energy source? i. β-hydroxybutyric acid ii. glucose iii. acetoacetic acid:::i and ii only', 'during prolonged starvation, which of the following substances can serve as an energy source? i. β-hydroxybutyric acid ii. glucose iii. acetoacetic acid', 'I and II only', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"During prolonged starvation, the body undergoes metabolic adaptations to preserve lean body mass while ensuring a continuous supply of energy to vital organs. Initially, glycogen stores are depleted within approximately 24 hours. Thereafter, gluconeogenesis becomes the major source of blood glucose, using amino acids, lactate, and glycerol as substrates.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"I and II only\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"During prolonged starvation, the body undergoes metabolic adaptations to preserve lean body mass while ensuring a continuous supply of energy to vital organs. Initially, glycogen stores are depleted within approximately 24 hours. Thereafter, gluconeogenesis becomes the major source of blood glucose, using amino acids, lactate, and glycerol as substrates.\n\nAlthough glucose production decreases over time to conserve muscle protein, it remains essential because certain tissues cannot utilize fatty acids. The liver simultaneously increases ketogenesis, producing the ketone bodies beta-hydroxybutyrate and acetoacetate from acetyl-CoA generated by beta-oxidation of fatty acids.\n\nThese ketone bodies become major fuels for the brain after several days of starvation, reducing its dependence on glucose and sparing muscle protein.\n\nThus:\n• β-Hydroxybutyrate is an important alternative fuel during starvation.\n• Acetoacetate is another major ketone body used for energy.\n• Glucose continues to be required by tissues such as red blood cells, the renal medulla, portions of the brain, and other glucose-dependent cells.\n\nTherefore, all three substances serve as energy sources during starvation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: I only, II and III only, All of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:during prolonged starvation, which of the following substances can serve as an energy source? i. β-hydroxybutyric acid ii. glucose iii. acetoacetic acid:::', 'during prolonged starvation, which of the following substances can serve as an energy source? i. β-hydroxybutyric acid ii. glucose iii. acetoacetic acid', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"During prolonged starvation, the body undergoes metabolic adaptations to preserve lean body mass while ensuring a continuous supply of energy to vital organs. Initially, glycogen stores are depleted within approximately 24 hours. Thereafter, gluconeogenesis becomes the major source of blood glucose, using amino acids, lactate, and glycerol as substrates.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"I and II only\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"During prolonged starvation, the body undergoes metabolic adaptations to preserve lean body mass while ensuring a continuous supply of energy to vital organs. Initially, glycogen stores are depleted within approximately 24 hours. Thereafter, gluconeogenesis becomes the major source of blood glucose, using amino acids, lactate, and glycerol as substrates.\n\nAlthough glucose production decreases over time to conserve muscle protein, it remains essential because certain tissues cannot utilize fatty acids. The liver simultaneously increases ketogenesis, producing the ketone bodies beta-hydroxybutyrate and acetoacetate from acetyl-CoA generated by beta-oxidation of fatty acids.\n\nThese ketone bodies become major fuels for the brain after several days of starvation, reducing its dependence on glucose and sparing muscle protein.\n\nThus:\n• β-Hydroxybutyrate is an important alternative fuel during starvation.\n• Acetoacetate is another major ketone body used for energy.\n• Glucose continues to be required by tissues such as red blood cells, the renal medulla, portions of the brain, and other glucose-dependent cells.\n\nTherefore, all three substances serve as energy sources during starvation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: I only, II and III only, All of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is true for bmr?:::pregnancy increases bmr', 'which of the following is true for bmr?', 'Pregnancy increases BMR', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR) represents the minimum amount of energy required to maintain vital physiologic functions while the body is at complete rest, in the post-absorptive state, and under thermoneutral conditions. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Pregnancy increases BMR\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR) represents the minimum amount of energy required to maintain vital physiologic functions while the body is at complete rest, in the post-absorptive state, and under thermoneutral conditions. \n\nPregnancy increases BMR because of: \n• fetal growth, \n• placental metabolism, \n• expansion of maternal tissues, \n• increased cardiac and respiratory work, \n• elevated hormonal activity. \n\nBMR may increase by approximately 15–20% during pregnancy, particularly in the second and third trimesters.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Puberty decreases BMR, Fasting increases BMR, Females have higher BMR than males"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is true for bmr?:::', 'which of the following is true for bmr?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR) represents the minimum amount of energy required to maintain vital physiologic functions while the body is at complete rest, in the post-absorptive state, and under thermoneutral conditions. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Pregnancy increases BMR\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR) represents the minimum amount of energy required to maintain vital physiologic functions while the body is at complete rest, in the post-absorptive state, and under thermoneutral conditions. \n\nPregnancy increases BMR because of: \n• fetal growth, \n• placental metabolism, \n• expansion of maternal tissues, \n• increased cardiac and respiratory work, \n• elevated hormonal activity. \n\nBMR may increase by approximately 15–20% during pregnancy, particularly in the second and third trimesters.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Puberty decreases BMR, Fasting increases BMR, Females have higher BMR than males"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:these nutrients are essential for wound healing except::::', 'these nutrients are essential for wound healing except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Arginine, glutamine, and vitamin C support collagen synthesis, immune function, and tissue repair. Sodium is important for fluid balance but is not considered a specific nutrient that enhances wound healing.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sodium\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Arginine, glutamine, and vitamin C support collagen synthesis, immune function, and tissue repair. Sodium is important for fluid balance but is not considered a specific nutrient that enhances wound healing.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Glutamine, Arginine, Vitamin C"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a mechanism by which vitamin c acts as antioxidant to protect the body is by: i. reacts directly with peroxynitrite ii. reacts directly with hypochlorite-derived oxidants iii. scavenges reactive oxygen species such as superoxide and hydroxyl radicals:::all of the above', 'a mechanism by which vitamin c acts as antioxidant to protect the body is by: i. reacts directly with peroxynitrite ii. reacts directly with hypochlorite-derived oxidants iii. scavenges reactive oxygen species such as superoxide and hydroxyl radicals', 'All of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) is a water-soluble antioxidant that protects cells from oxidative damage by donating electrons to neutralize reactive oxygen and nitrogen species. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) is a water-soluble antioxidant that protects cells from oxidative damage by donating electrons to neutralize reactive oxygen and nitrogen species. \n\nIts antioxidant actions include: \n• scavenging superoxide radicals, \n• scavenging hydroxyl radicals, \n• reacting with peroxynitrite, \n• neutralizing hypochlorite-derived oxidants generated during inflammatory responses, \n• regenerating oxidized vitamin E, thereby helping maintain membrane antioxidant defenses. \n\nThese mechanisms contribute to the protection of lipids, proteins, DNA, and other cellular components from oxidative injury.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: I only, II only, III only"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a mechanism by which vitamin c acts as antioxidant to protect the body is by: i. reacts directly with peroxynitrite ii. reacts directly with hypochlorite-derived oxidants iii. scavenges reactive oxygen species such as superoxide and hydroxyl radicals:::', 'a mechanism by which vitamin c acts as antioxidant to protect the body is by: i. reacts directly with peroxynitrite ii. reacts directly with hypochlorite-derived oxidants iii. scavenges reactive oxygen species such as superoxide and hydroxyl radicals', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) is a water-soluble antioxidant that protects cells from oxidative damage by donating electrons to neutralize reactive oxygen and nitrogen species. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) is a water-soluble antioxidant that protects cells from oxidative damage by donating electrons to neutralize reactive oxygen and nitrogen species. \n\nIts antioxidant actions include: \n• scavenging superoxide radicals, \n• scavenging hydroxyl radicals, \n• reacting with peroxynitrite, \n• neutralizing hypochlorite-derived oxidants generated during inflammatory responses, \n• regenerating oxidized vitamin E, thereby helping maintain membrane antioxidant defenses. \n\nThese mechanisms contribute to the protection of lipids, proteins, DNA, and other cellular components from oxidative injury.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: I only, II only, III only"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following are sources of acetyl-coa? i. β-oxidation of fatty acids ii. oxidative decarboxylation of pyruvate iii. catabolism of ketogenic amino acids:::all of the above', 'which of the following are sources of acetyl-coa? i. β-oxidation of fatty acids ii. oxidative decarboxylation of pyruvate iii. catabolism of ketogenic amino acids', 'All of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Acetyl-CoA is a central metabolic intermediate that links carbohydrate, lipid, and protein metabolism. It enters the tricarboxylic acid (TCA) cycle for ATP production or serves as a precursor for ketone bodies, cholesterol, and fatty acid synthesis. The major sources of acetyl-CoA include: ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Acetyl-CoA is a central metabolic intermediate that links carbohydrate, lipid, and protein metabolism. It enters the tricarboxylic acid (TCA) cycle for ATP production or serves as a precursor for ketone bodies, cholesterol, and fatty acid synthesis. The major sources of acetyl-CoA include: \n\nI. β-Oxidation of fatty acids: Fatty acids undergo β-oxidation in the mitochondria, sequentially removing two-carbon fragments as acetyl-CoA. \nII. Oxidative decarboxylation of pyruvate: Pyruvate, the end product of glycolysis, is converted to acetyl-CoA by the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex before entering the TCA cycle. \nIII. Ketogenic amino acids: The exclusively ketogenic amino acids leucine and lysine, as well as portions of isoleucine, phenylalanine, tryptophan, tyrosine, and threonine, are metabolized to acetyl-CoA or acetoacetate.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: I and II, II and  III, I and III"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following are sources of acetyl-coa? i. β-oxidation of fatty acids ii. oxidative decarboxylation of pyruvate iii. catabolism of ketogenic amino acids:::', 'which of the following are sources of acetyl-coa? i. β-oxidation of fatty acids ii. oxidative decarboxylation of pyruvate iii. catabolism of ketogenic amino acids', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Acetyl-CoA is a central metabolic intermediate that links carbohydrate, lipid, and protein metabolism. It enters the tricarboxylic acid (TCA) cycle for ATP production or serves as a precursor for ketone bodies, cholesterol, and fatty acid synthesis. The major sources of acetyl-CoA include: ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Acetyl-CoA is a central metabolic intermediate that links carbohydrate, lipid, and protein metabolism. It enters the tricarboxylic acid (TCA) cycle for ATP production or serves as a precursor for ketone bodies, cholesterol, and fatty acid synthesis. The major sources of acetyl-CoA include: \n\nI. β-Oxidation of fatty acids: Fatty acids undergo β-oxidation in the mitochondria, sequentially removing two-carbon fragments as acetyl-CoA. \nII. Oxidative decarboxylation of pyruvate: Pyruvate, the end product of glycolysis, is converted to acetyl-CoA by the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex before entering the TCA cycle. \nIII. Ketogenic amino acids: The exclusively ketogenic amino acids leucine and lysine, as well as portions of isoleucine, phenylalanine, tryptophan, tyrosine, and threonine, are metabolized to acetyl-CoA or acetoacetate.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: I and II, II and  III, I and III"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a 55-year-old filipino male laboratory technician diagnosed with type 2 diabetes mellitus visited the nutrition clinic for counseling. his anthropometric measurements are as follows: height = 5''7" (170 cm), weight = 75 kg, and waist circumference = 36 inches. his laboratory results revealed the following: hba1c = 9%; total cholesterol = 250 mg/dl; ldl cholesterol = 300 mg/dl; and uric acid = 9 mg/dl. he also reported joint pain, lower back pain, and swelling of the joints. the patient''s hba1c result is indicative of glucose control.:::', 'a 55-year-old filipino male laboratory technician diagnosed with type 2 diabetes mellitus visited the nutrition clinic for counseling. his anthropometric measurements are as follows: height = 5''7" (170 cm), weight = 75 kg, and waist circumference = 36 inches. his laboratory results revealed the following: hba1c = 9%; total cholesterol = 250 mg/dl; ldl cholesterol = 300 mg/dl; and uric acid = 9 mg/dl. he also reported joint pain, lower back pain, and swelling of the joints. the patient''s hba1c result is indicative of glucose control.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Hemoglobin A1c (HbA1c), also known as glycated hemoglobin, reflects the average blood glucose concentration over the previous 2–3 months, corresponding to the lifespan of red blood cells. It is one of the most important laboratory parameters used to assess long-term glycemic control in patients with diabetes mellitus. An HbA1c of 9% indicates poor glycemic control, suggesting that blood glucose levels have remained persistently elevated over the past several months. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Poor\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Hemoglobin A1c (HbA1c), also known as glycated hemoglobin, reflects the average blood glucose concentration over the previous 2–3 months, corresponding to the lifespan of red blood cells. It is one of the most important laboratory parameters used to assess long-term glycemic control in patients with diabetes mellitus. An HbA1c of 9% indicates poor glycemic control, suggesting that blood glucose levels have remained persistently elevated over the past several months. \n\nFor most nonpregnant adults with diabetes, the American Diabetes Association (ADA) recommends an HbA1c target of <7%, although individualized goals may be appropriate depending on age, comorbidities, risk of hypoglycemia, and life expectancy.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Good, Fair, Moderate"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:this mineral potentiates the action of insulin, allowing glucose entry into cells, and facilitates insulin binding to cell surfaces.:::chromium', 'this mineral potentiates the action of insulin, allowing glucose entry into cells, and facilitates insulin binding to cell surfaces.', 'Chromium', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Chromium is an essential trace mineral involved in carbohydrate, lipid, and protein metabolism. It enhances the action of insulin by improving insulin receptor activity and intracellular signaling, thereby promoting glucose uptake by cells. Although chromium deficiency is uncommon, severe deficiency may lead to impaired glucose tolerance and insulin resistance, particularly in patients receiving long-term parenteral nutrition without adequate chromium supplementation.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Chromium\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Chromium is an essential trace mineral involved in carbohydrate, lipid, and protein metabolism. It enhances the action of insulin by improving insulin receptor activity and intracellular signaling, thereby promoting glucose uptake by cells. Although chromium deficiency is uncommon, severe deficiency may lead to impaired glucose tolerance and insulin resistance, particularly in patients receiving long-term parenteral nutrition without adequate chromium supplementation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Cobalt, Molybdenum, Zinc"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:this mineral potentiates the action of insulin, allowing glucose entry into cells, and facilitates insulin binding to cell surfaces.:::', 'this mineral potentiates the action of insulin, allowing glucose entry into cells, and facilitates insulin binding to cell surfaces.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Chromium is an essential trace mineral involved in carbohydrate, lipid, and protein metabolism. It enhances the action of insulin by improving insulin receptor activity and intracellular signaling, thereby promoting glucose uptake by cells. Although chromium deficiency is uncommon, severe deficiency may lead to impaired glucose tolerance and insulin resistance, particularly in patients receiving long-term parenteral nutrition without adequate chromium supplementation.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Chromium\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Chromium is an essential trace mineral involved in carbohydrate, lipid, and protein metabolism. It enhances the action of insulin by improving insulin receptor activity and intracellular signaling, thereby promoting glucose uptake by cells. Although chromium deficiency is uncommon, severe deficiency may lead to impaired glucose tolerance and insulin resistance, particularly in patients receiving long-term parenteral nutrition without adequate chromium supplementation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Cobalt, Molybdenum, Zinc"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:all of the following are components of the body''s buffer systems that help prevent drastic changes in blood ph, except::::lipids', 'all of the following are components of the body''s buffer systems that help prevent drastic changes in blood ph, except:', 'Lipids', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The human body maintains a tightly regulated blood pH of approximately 7.35–7.45 through several physiologic buffer systems that minimize sudden changes in hydrogen ion (H⁺) concentration. These buffers act immediately before respiratory and renal compensatory mechanisms take effect. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Lipids\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The human body maintains a tightly regulated blood pH of approximately 7.35–7.45 through several physiologic buffer systems that minimize sudden changes in hydrogen ion (H⁺) concentration. These buffers act immediately before respiratory and renal compensatory mechanisms take effect. \n\nThe three major chemical buffer systems are: \n• Bicarbonate–carbonic acid buffer system – the principal extracellular buffer responsible for maintaining blood pH. \n• Protein buffer system – includes plasma proteins and intracellular proteins such as hemoglobin, which can bind or release hydrogen ions. \n• Phosphate buffer system – functions primarily within cells and in the renal tubules, where it helps regulate acid excretion. \n\nLipids are not considered a physiologic buffer system. Although lipids have numerous biological functions—including energy storage, cell membrane structure, and signaling—they do not play a significant role in buffering hydrogen ions or maintaining acid-base balance.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Bicarbonate–carbonic acid buffer system, Protein buffer system, Phosphate buffer system"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:all of the following are components of the body''s buffer systems that help prevent drastic changes in blood ph, except::::', 'all of the following are components of the body''s buffer systems that help prevent drastic changes in blood ph, except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The human body maintains a tightly regulated blood pH of approximately 7.35–7.45 through several physiologic buffer systems that minimize sudden changes in hydrogen ion (H⁺) concentration. These buffers act immediately before respiratory and renal compensatory mechanisms take effect. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Lipids\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The human body maintains a tightly regulated blood pH of approximately 7.35–7.45 through several physiologic buffer systems that minimize sudden changes in hydrogen ion (H⁺) concentration. These buffers act immediately before respiratory and renal compensatory mechanisms take effect. \n\nThe three major chemical buffer systems are: \n• Bicarbonate–carbonic acid buffer system – the principal extracellular buffer responsible for maintaining blood pH. \n• Protein buffer system – includes plasma proteins and intracellular proteins such as hemoglobin, which can bind or release hydrogen ions. \n• Phosphate buffer system – functions primarily within cells and in the renal tubules, where it helps regulate acid excretion. \n\nLipids are not considered a physiologic buffer system. Although lipids have numerous biological functions—including energy storage, cell membrane structure, and signaling—they do not play a significant role in buffering hydrogen ions or maintaining acid-base balance.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Bicarbonate–carbonic acid buffer system, Protein buffer system, Phosphate buffer system"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the total protein requirement/day of a 7-year-old child using npc method?:::44 grams/day', 'what is the total protein requirement/day of a 7-year-old child using npc method?', '44 grams/day', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Using the NPC method, first determine the child''s desirable body weight (DBW): ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"44 grams/day\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Using the NPC method, first determine the child''s desirable body weight (DBW): \n\nDBW = (age in years * 2) + 8 \nFor a 7-year-old: DBW = (7 * 2) + 8 = 22 kg \n\nUsing the 2.0 g protein/kg DBW/day recommendation from the NDAP Diet Manual, 5th edition: CHON req’t = DBW * 2.0 g/kgDBW \n\nTherefore, the child''s total protein requirement is 44 g/day. This question is specifically testing the clinical computation convention used in the NDAP Diet Manual, rather than asking for the current general population protein RDA/RNI.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 20 grams/day, 24.2 grams/day, 33 grams/day"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is needed when the body experiences stress?:::growth hormone and insulin-like factors provision', 'which of the following is needed when the body experiences stress?', 'Growth hormone and insulin-like factors provision', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Physiologic stress resulting from trauma, burns, major surgery, sepsis, severe infection, or critical illness activates a complex neuroendocrine response that promotes survival by mobilizing energy stores and maintaining vital organ function.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Growth hormone and insulin-like factors provision\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Physiologic stress resulting from trauma, burns, major surgery, sepsis, severe infection, or critical illness activates a complex neuroendocrine response that promotes survival by mobilizing energy stores and maintaining vital organ function.\n\nThe stress response is characterized by increased secretion of several counter-regulatory hormones, including:\n• Growth hormone (GH)\n• Cortisol\n• Catecholamines (epinephrine and norepinephrine)\n• Glucagon\n\nThe growth hormone–insulin-like growth factor (GH–IGF) axis is also altered during stress. Although GH secretion generally increases, severe illness is often associated with GH resistance, resulting in reduced hepatic production of IGF-1 despite elevated GH concentrations.\n\nThese hormonal changes contribute to:\n• Increased protein catabolism\n• Increased gluconeogenesis\n• Increased lipolysis\n• Increased resting energy expenditure\n• Mobilization of amino acids for immune function and tissue repair\n\nAmong the available options, Growth hormone and insulin-like factors is the best answer because it refers to the endocrine mediators involved in the physiologic stress response. It should be noted, however, that this question has a psychometric limitation. From a nutrition management perspective, the more appropriate answer would be adequate energy and increased protein intake, but this option is not provided.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Minimum calorie, Low protein, Fat-soluble vitamins and supplementation"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:stimulates sodium retention while stimulates water retention.:::aldosterone, vasopressin', 'stimulates sodium retention while stimulates water retention.', 'Aldosterone, vasopressin', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Aldosterone is a steroid hormone produced by the adrenal cortex. Its primary physiological role is to act on the kidneys (specifically the distal tubules and collecting ducts) to stimulate sodium retention (reabsorption). As it pulls sodium back into the blood, potassium is excreted.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Aldosterone, vasopressin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Aldosterone is a steroid hormone produced by the adrenal cortex. Its primary physiological role is to act on the kidneys (specifically the distal tubules and collecting ducts) to stimulate sodium retention (reabsorption). As it pulls sodium back into the blood, potassium is excreted.\n\nVasopressin, also known as Antidiuretic Hormone (ADH), is synthesized in the hypothalamus and released by the posterior pituitary. Its primary role is to insert aquaporins (water channels) into the collecting ducts of the kidneys to stimulate water retention (reabsorption) independently of sodium, helping to dilute blood osmolality.\n\n• Renin is an enzyme (not a hormone) secreted by the kidneys that cleaves angiotensinogen into Angiotensin I. It triggers a cascade but does not directly retain sodium or water on its own.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vasopressin, aldosterone, Renin, aldosterone, Renin, angiotensin"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:stimulates sodium retention while stimulates water retention.:::', 'stimulates sodium retention while stimulates water retention.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Aldosterone is a steroid hormone produced by the adrenal cortex. Its primary physiological role is to act on the kidneys (specifically the distal tubules and collecting ducts) to stimulate sodium retention (reabsorption). As it pulls sodium back into the blood, potassium is excreted.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Aldosterone, vasopressin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Aldosterone is a steroid hormone produced by the adrenal cortex. Its primary physiological role is to act on the kidneys (specifically the distal tubules and collecting ducts) to stimulate sodium retention (reabsorption). As it pulls sodium back into the blood, potassium is excreted.\n\nVasopressin, also known as Antidiuretic Hormone (ADH), is synthesized in the hypothalamus and released by the posterior pituitary. Its primary role is to insert aquaporins (water channels) into the collecting ducts of the kidneys to stimulate water retention (reabsorption) independently of sodium, helping to dilute blood osmolality.\n\n• Renin is an enzyme (not a hormone) secreted by the kidneys that cleaves angiotensinogen into Angiotensin I. It triggers a cascade but does not directly retain sodium or water on its own.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vasopressin, aldosterone, Renin, aldosterone, Renin, angiotensin"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:vitamin losses increase due to the following conditions except::::cooking below the temperature of boiling water with excessive agitation', 'vitamin losses increase due to the following conditions except:', 'Cooking below the temperature of boiling water with excessive agitation', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"While boiling water (100°C) combined with excessive agitation drastically accelerates the leaching and thermal destruction of water-soluble vitamins (like Vitamin C and Thiamine), cooking below the boiling point (such as poaching or gentle simmering) exposes the food to less thermal stress. Even with agitation, keeping the temperature lower is a primary strategy used to preserve heat-sensitive nutrients compared to full boiling.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Cooking below the temperature of boiling water with excessive agitation\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"While boiling water (100°C) combined with excessive agitation drastically accelerates the leaching and thermal destruction of water-soluble vitamins (like Vitamin C and Thiamine), cooking below the boiling point (such as poaching or gentle simmering) exposes the food to less thermal stress. Even with agitation, keeping the temperature lower is a primary strategy used to preserve heat-sensitive nutrients compared to full boiling.\n\n\t• Increased exposure to high temperatures or sunshine: Heat and ultraviolet (UV) light rapidly degrade photosensitive and heat-labile vitamins (such as Riboflavin/B2 and Vitamin C).\n\t• Increased exposure to air: Oxygen triggers oxidative degradation, which quickly destroys atmospheric-sensitive vitamins like Vitamin C, A, and E.\n\t• Increased length of storage: Prolonged storage allows baseline chemical degradation and oxidation to occur over time, steadily reducing the total nutrient potency of the food.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Increased exposure to high temperatures or sunshine during storage, Increased length of storage, Increased exposure to air"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:vitamin losses increase due to the following conditions except::::', 'vitamin losses increase due to the following conditions except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"While boiling water (100°C) combined with excessive agitation drastically accelerates the leaching and thermal destruction of water-soluble vitamins (like Vitamin C and Thiamine), cooking below the boiling point (such as poaching or gentle simmering) exposes the food to less thermal stress. Even with agitation, keeping the temperature lower is a primary strategy used to preserve heat-sensitive nutrients compared to full boiling.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Cooking below the temperature of boiling water with excessive agitation\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"While boiling water (100°C) combined with excessive agitation drastically accelerates the leaching and thermal destruction of water-soluble vitamins (like Vitamin C and Thiamine), cooking below the boiling point (such as poaching or gentle simmering) exposes the food to less thermal stress. Even with agitation, keeping the temperature lower is a primary strategy used to preserve heat-sensitive nutrients compared to full boiling.\n\n\t• Increased exposure to high temperatures or sunshine: Heat and ultraviolet (UV) light rapidly degrade photosensitive and heat-labile vitamins (such as Riboflavin/B2 and Vitamin C).\n\t• Increased exposure to air: Oxygen triggers oxidative degradation, which quickly destroys atmospheric-sensitive vitamins like Vitamin C, A, and E.\n\t• Increased length of storage: Prolonged storage allows baseline chemical degradation and oxidation to occur over time, steadily reducing the total nutrient potency of the food.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Increased exposure to high temperatures or sunshine during storage, Increased length of storage, Increased exposure to air"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the total protein requirement/day of a 7-year-old child using npc method?:::', 'what is the total protein requirement/day of a 7-year-old child using npc method?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Using the NPC method, first determine the child''s desirable body weight (DBW): ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"44 grams/day\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Using the NPC method, first determine the child''s desirable body weight (DBW): \n\nDBW = (age in years * 2) + 8 \nFor a 7-year-old: DBW = (7 * 2) + 8 = 22 kg \n\nUsing the 2.0 g protein/kg DBW/day recommendation from the NDAP Diet Manual, 5th edition: CHON req’t = DBW * 2.0 g/kgDBW \n\nTherefore, the child''s total protein requirement is 44 g/day. This question is specifically testing the clinical computation convention used in the NDAP Diet Manual, rather than asking for the current general population protein RDA/RNI.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 20 grams/day, 24.2 grams/day, 33 grams/day"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is needed when the body experiences stress?:::', 'which of the following is needed when the body experiences stress?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Physiologic stress resulting from trauma, burns, major surgery, sepsis, severe infection, or critical illness activates a complex neuroendocrine response that promotes survival by mobilizing energy stores and maintaining vital organ function.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Growth hormone and insulin-like factors provision\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Physiologic stress resulting from trauma, burns, major surgery, sepsis, severe infection, or critical illness activates a complex neuroendocrine response that promotes survival by mobilizing energy stores and maintaining vital organ function.\n\nThe stress response is characterized by increased secretion of several counter-regulatory hormones, including:\n• Growth hormone (GH)\n• Cortisol\n• Catecholamines (epinephrine and norepinephrine)\n• Glucagon\n\nThe growth hormone–insulin-like growth factor (GH–IGF) axis is also altered during stress. Although GH secretion generally increases, severe illness is often associated with GH resistance, resulting in reduced hepatic production of IGF-1 despite elevated GH concentrations.\n\nThese hormonal changes contribute to:\n• Increased protein catabolism\n• Increased gluconeogenesis\n• Increased lipolysis\n• Increased resting energy expenditure\n• Mobilization of amino acids for immune function and tissue repair\n\nAmong the available options, Growth hormone and insulin-like factors is the best answer because it refers to the endocrine mediators involved in the physiologic stress response. It should be noted, however, that this question has a psychometric limitation. From a nutrition management perspective, the more appropriate answer would be adequate energy and increased protein intake, but this option is not provided.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Minimum calorie, Low protein, Fat-soluble vitamins and supplementation"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:high cortisol stimulates::::mobilization of free fatty acid', 'high cortisol stimulates:', 'Mobilization of free fatty acid', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Cortisol is a major stress hormone that functions to provide energy substrates to the body during flight-or-fight situations or prolonged stress. It directly stimulates lipolysis—the breakdown of stored fats in adipose tissue—which results in the mobilization of free fatty acids into the bloodstream to be used as an alternative fuel source by peripheral tissues.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Mobilization of free fatty acid\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Cortisol is a major stress hormone that functions to provide energy substrates to the body during flight-or-fight situations or prolonged stress. It directly stimulates lipolysis—the breakdown of stored fats in adipose tissue—which results in the mobilization of free fatty acids into the bloodstream to be used as an alternative fuel source by peripheral tissues.\n\n• Use of glucose except for the brain: Cortisol actually decreases or inhibits the peripheral utilization of glucose in tissues like muscle and fat. It does this precisely to save or spare glucose exclusively for the brain. Therefore, this statement is factually backwards; it decreases glucose use in peripheral tissues to safeguard it for the brain.\n• Retention of water and sodium: Cortisol does exhibit weak mineralocorticoid activity at very high pathological levels (which can cause secondary sodium retention and water buildup), but its primary, direct metabolic action across basic physiology and nutrition textbooks is the mobilization of metabolic fuels, making free fatty acid mobilization the classical intended answer.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Use of glucose except for the brain, Retention of water, Retention of sodium"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:high cortisol stimulates::::', 'high cortisol stimulates:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Cortisol is a major stress hormone that functions to provide energy substrates to the body during flight-or-fight situations or prolonged stress. It directly stimulates lipolysis—the breakdown of stored fats in adipose tissue—which results in the mobilization of free fatty acids into the bloodstream to be used as an alternative fuel source by peripheral tissues.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Mobilization of free fatty acid\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Cortisol is a major stress hormone that functions to provide energy substrates to the body during flight-or-fight situations or prolonged stress. It directly stimulates lipolysis—the breakdown of stored fats in adipose tissue—which results in the mobilization of free fatty acids into the bloodstream to be used as an alternative fuel source by peripheral tissues.\n\n• Use of glucose except for the brain: Cortisol actually decreases or inhibits the peripheral utilization of glucose in tissues like muscle and fat. It does this precisely to save or spare glucose exclusively for the brain. Therefore, this statement is factually backwards; it decreases glucose use in peripheral tissues to safeguard it for the brain.\n• Retention of water and sodium: Cortisol does exhibit weak mineralocorticoid activity at very high pathological levels (which can cause secondary sodium retention and water buildup), but its primary, direct metabolic action across basic physiology and nutrition textbooks is the mobilization of metabolic fuels, making free fatty acid mobilization the classical intended answer.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Use of glucose except for the brain, Retention of water, Retention of sodium"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:these nutrients are essential for wound healing except::::sodium', 'these nutrients are essential for wound healing except:', 'Sodium', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Arginine, glutamine, and vitamin C support collagen synthesis, immune function, and tissue repair. Sodium is important for fluid balance but is not considered a specific nutrient that enhances wound healing.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sodium\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Arginine, glutamine, and vitamin C support collagen synthesis, immune function, and tissue repair. Sodium is important for fluid balance but is not considered a specific nutrient that enhances wound healing.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Glutamine, Arginine, Vitamin C"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which omega-3 fatty acid found in fish oil is primarily responsible for reducing the production of pro-inflammatory cytokines?:::eicosapentaenoic acid (epa)', 'which omega-3 fatty acid found in fish oil is primarily responsible for reducing the production of pro-inflammatory cytokines?', 'Eicosapentaenoic Acid (EPA)', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"EPA competes with arachidonic acid for cyclooxygenase and lipoxygenase enzymes, resulting in the production of less inflammatory eicosanoids. DHA also has anti-inflammatory effects but EPA is more directly associated with reducing inflammatory cytokine production.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Eicosapentaenoic Acid (EPA)\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"EPA competes with arachidonic acid for cyclooxygenase and lipoxygenase enzymes, resulting in the production of less inflammatory eicosanoids. DHA also has anti-inflammatory effects but EPA is more directly associated with reducing inflammatory cytokine production.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: DocosaHexaenoic Acid (DHA), Arachidonic Acid, All of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which omega-3 fatty acid found in fish oil is primarily responsible for reducing the production of pro-inflammatory cytokines?:::', 'which omega-3 fatty acid found in fish oil is primarily responsible for reducing the production of pro-inflammatory cytokines?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"EPA competes with arachidonic acid for cyclooxygenase and lipoxygenase enzymes, resulting in the production of less inflammatory eicosanoids. DHA also has anti-inflammatory effects but EPA is more directly associated with reducing inflammatory cytokine production.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Eicosapentaenoic Acid (EPA)\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"EPA competes with arachidonic acid for cyclooxygenase and lipoxygenase enzymes, resulting in the production of less inflammatory eicosanoids. DHA also has anti-inflammatory effects but EPA is more directly associated with reducing inflammatory cytokine production.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: DocosaHexaenoic Acid (DHA), Arachidonic Acid, All of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which mineral reduces intestinal absorption of fluoride by forming insoluble complexes?:::calcium', 'which mineral reduces intestinal absorption of fluoride by forming insoluble complexes?', 'Calcium', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Calcium binds fluoride in the gastrointestinal tract, decreasing its absorption. This interaction is clinically relevant when fluoride supplements are prescribed.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Calcium\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Calcium binds fluoride in the gastrointestinal tract, decreasing its absorption. This interaction is clinically relevant when fluoride supplements are prescribed.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Magnesium, Iron, Manganese"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which mineral reduces intestinal absorption of fluoride by forming insoluble complexes?:::', 'which mineral reduces intestinal absorption of fluoride by forming insoluble complexes?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Calcium binds fluoride in the gastrointestinal tract, decreasing its absorption. This interaction is clinically relevant when fluoride supplements are prescribed.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Calcium\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Calcium binds fluoride in the gastrointestinal tract, decreasing its absorption. This interaction is clinically relevant when fluoride supplements are prescribed.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Magnesium, Iron, Manganese"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:not allowed in tyramine-restricted diets::::fava beans', 'not allowed in tyramine-restricted diets:', 'Fava beans', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Fava beans (broad beans) contain high amounts of L-dopa, which can metabolize into tyramine and other compounds that interact dangerously with Monoamine Oxidase Inhibitors (MAOIs). Additionally, the pods of broad beans are explicitly restricted on low-tyramine diets because they accumulate toxic levels of tyramine as they age.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Fava beans\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Fava beans (broad beans) contain high amounts of L-dopa, which can metabolize into tyramine and other compounds that interact dangerously with Monoamine Oxidase Inhibitors (MAOIs). Additionally, the pods of broad beans are explicitly restricted on low-tyramine diets because they accumulate toxic levels of tyramine as they age.\n\nWhen a patient taking an MAOI medication consumes high-tyramine foods, the tyramine cannot be safely broken down in the gut. It enters the bloodstream and causes a massive release of norepinephrine, leading to a life-threatening hypertensive crisis (a dangerous spike in blood pressure).\n\nOther foods commonly restricted include aged cheeses, cured and fermented meats, yeast extracts, fermented soy products, sauerkraut, kimchi, certain alcoholic beverages (especially tap beers and some red wines), and improperly stored or spoiled protein-rich foods. Current recommendations emphasize that tyramine content varies with aging, fermentation, ripening, and storage conditions. Fresh foods are generally lower in tyramine than aged or fermented products.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Grapefruit juice, Simple sugars, Banana"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:not allowed in tyramine-restricted diets::::', 'not allowed in tyramine-restricted diets:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Fava beans (broad beans) contain high amounts of L-dopa, which can metabolize into tyramine and other compounds that interact dangerously with Monoamine Oxidase Inhibitors (MAOIs). Additionally, the pods of broad beans are explicitly restricted on low-tyramine diets because they accumulate toxic levels of tyramine as they age.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Fava beans\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Fava beans (broad beans) contain high amounts of L-dopa, which can metabolize into tyramine and other compounds that interact dangerously with Monoamine Oxidase Inhibitors (MAOIs). Additionally, the pods of broad beans are explicitly restricted on low-tyramine diets because they accumulate toxic levels of tyramine as they age.\n\nWhen a patient taking an MAOI medication consumes high-tyramine foods, the tyramine cannot be safely broken down in the gut. It enters the bloodstream and causes a massive release of norepinephrine, leading to a life-threatening hypertensive crisis (a dangerous spike in blood pressure).\n\nOther foods commonly restricted include aged cheeses, cured and fermented meats, yeast extracts, fermented soy products, sauerkraut, kimchi, certain alcoholic beverages (especially tap beers and some red wines), and improperly stored or spoiled protein-rich foods. Current recommendations emphasize that tyramine content varies with aging, fermentation, ripening, and storage conditions. Fresh foods are generally lower in tyramine than aged or fermented products.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Grapefruit juice, Simple sugars, Banana"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:products that contain live microorganisms in sufficient numbers to alter the intestinal microbiota and promote microbial balance are known as::::probiotics', 'products that contain live microorganisms in sufficient numbers to alter the intestinal microbiota and promote microbial balance are known as:', 'Probiotics', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Probiotics are live microorganisms that, when administered in adequate amounts, confer a health benefit on the host. Common probiotic organisms include species of Lactobacillus, Bifidobacterium, Saccharomyces boulardii, and certain strains of Bacillus.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Probiotics\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Probiotics are live microorganisms that, when administered in adequate amounts, confer a health benefit on the host. Common probiotic organisms include species of Lactobacillus, Bifidobacterium, Saccharomyces boulardii, and certain strains of Bacillus.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Antibiotics, Fruits and vegetables, Digestive enzymes"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:phytochemicals found in berries, black tea, and citrus fruits that act as antioxidants and scavenge carcinogens.:::flavonoids', 'phytochemicals found in berries, black tea, and citrus fruits that act as antioxidants and scavenge carcinogens.', 'Flavonoids', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Flavonoids are a diverse class of polyphenolic plant compounds widely distributed in foods like berries, citrus fruits, and black or green tea.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Flavonoids\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Flavonoids are a diverse class of polyphenolic plant compounds widely distributed in foods like berries, citrus fruits, and black or green tea.\n\nThey are heavily studied in nutrition and clinical biology for their powerful ability to act as direct antioxidants. They scavenge free radicals and carcinogens, alter cellular signaling pathways to trigger carcinogen excretion, and suppress abnormal cellular multiplication to reduce chronic disease risks.\n\n• Phytic acid: Phytic acid is primarily found in the bran and germ layers of whole grains and legumes, where it acts as a mineral-chelating agent.\n• Phenols: While flavonoids are structurally a subgroup of larger phenolic compounds, \"Phenols\" (misspelled as Phenlos in the option) is a broad umbrella category. Flavonoids is the more distinct, precise, and targeted textbook choice for this specific combination of food sources (berries, tea, and citrus).\n• Phytosterols: These are plant-derived sterols structurally identical to cholesterol. Their primary therapeutic mechanism is blocking the absorption of dietary cholesterol in the small intestine, not acting as primary carcinogen scavengers.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Phenols, Phytic acid, Phytosterols"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb)
