('mc:phytochemicals found in berries, black tea, and citrus fruits that act as antioxidants and scavenge carcinogens.:::', 'phytochemicals found in berries, black tea, and citrus fruits that act as antioxidants and scavenge carcinogens.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Flavonoids are a diverse class of polyphenolic plant compounds widely distributed in foods like berries, citrus fruits, and black or green tea.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Flavonoids\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Flavonoids are a diverse class of polyphenolic plant compounds widely distributed in foods like berries, citrus fruits, and black or green tea.\n\nThey are heavily studied in nutrition and clinical biology for their powerful ability to act as direct antioxidants. They scavenge free radicals and carcinogens, alter cellular signaling pathways to trigger carcinogen excretion, and suppress abnormal cellular multiplication to reduce chronic disease risks.\n\n• Phytic acid: Phytic acid is primarily found in the bran and germ layers of whole grains and legumes, where it acts as a mineral-chelating agent.\n• Phenols: While flavonoids are structurally a subgroup of larger phenolic compounds, \"Phenols\" (misspelled as Phenlos in the option) is a broad umbrella category. Flavonoids is the more distinct, precise, and targeted textbook choice for this specific combination of food sources (berries, tea, and citrus).\n• Phytosterols: These are plant-derived sterols structurally identical to cholesterol. Their primary therapeutic mechanism is blocking the absorption of dietary cholesterol in the small intestine, not acting as primary carcinogen scavengers.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Phenols, Phytic acid, Phytosterols"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:galactosemia is an inherited disorder characterized by the inability to metabolize galactose due to deficiency of the enzyme galactose-1-phosphate uridylyltransferase (galt). which of the following is a characteristic manifestation of this condition?:::growth failure', 'galactosemia is an inherited disorder characterized by the inability to metabolize galactose due to deficiency of the enzyme galactose-1-phosphate uridylyltransferase (galt). which of the following is a characteristic manifestation of this condition?', 'Growth failure', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Classic galactosemia is an autosomal recessive metabolic disorder caused by a deficiency in the GALT enzyme. This deficiency prevents the infant from safely breaking down galactose (a component of lactose found in breast milk and standard formulas).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Growth failure\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Classic galactosemia is an autosomal recessive metabolic disorder caused by a deficiency in the GALT enzyme. This deficiency prevents the infant from safely breaking down galactose (a component of lactose found in breast milk and standard formulas).\n\nBecause the infant cannot convert galactose into glucose for energy, toxic metabolites like galactose-1-phosphate accumulate rapidly in tissues. This causes immediate feeding intolerance, severe vomiting, and systemic illness within days of birth. This inability to utilize nutrients manifests primarily and consistently as growth failure (failure to thrive).\n\nImmediate elimination of lactose and galactose from the diet is essential. Starting this dietary intervention early is the cornerstone of treatment to reverse growth failure, resolve acute symptoms like jaundice, and prevent severe, life-threatening complications like E. coli sepsis.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Mental retardation, Apathy, Lack of pigmentation"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:galactosemia is an inherited disorder characterized by the inability to metabolize galactose due to deficiency of the enzyme galactose-1-phosphate uridylyltransferase (galt). which of the following is a characteristic manifestation of this condition?:::', 'galactosemia is an inherited disorder characterized by the inability to metabolize galactose due to deficiency of the enzyme galactose-1-phosphate uridylyltransferase (galt). which of the following is a characteristic manifestation of this condition?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Classic galactosemia is an autosomal recessive metabolic disorder caused by a deficiency in the GALT enzyme. This deficiency prevents the infant from safely breaking down galactose (a component of lactose found in breast milk and standard formulas).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Growth failure\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Classic galactosemia is an autosomal recessive metabolic disorder caused by a deficiency in the GALT enzyme. This deficiency prevents the infant from safely breaking down galactose (a component of lactose found in breast milk and standard formulas).\n\nBecause the infant cannot convert galactose into glucose for energy, toxic metabolites like galactose-1-phosphate accumulate rapidly in tissues. This causes immediate feeding intolerance, severe vomiting, and systemic illness within days of birth. This inability to utilize nutrients manifests primarily and consistently as growth failure (failure to thrive).\n\nImmediate elimination of lactose and galactose from the diet is essential. Starting this dietary intervention early is the cornerstone of treatment to reverse growth failure, resolve acute symptoms like jaundice, and prevent severe, life-threatening complications like E. coli sepsis.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Mental retardation, Apathy, Lack of pigmentation"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a patient is prescribed a diet containing 1,500 mg of sodium. how many meq of sodium are contained in the diet?:::', 'a patient is prescribed a diet containing 1,500 mg of sodium. how many meq of sodium are contained in the diet?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"To convert milligrams of sodium to milliequivalents (mEq), use: mEq = (mg * valence) ÷ atomic weight ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"65 mEq Na\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"To convert milligrams of sodium to milliequivalents (mEq), use: mEq = (mg * valence) ÷ atomic weight \n\nSodium (Na⁺) has: \n• Atomic weight ≈ 23 \n• Valence = 1 \n\nTherefore: mEq Na = (1500 * 1) ÷ 23 = 65.2 mEq \n\nThus, the calculated value is approximately 65 mEq.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 55 mEq Na, 60 mEq Na, 70 mEq Na"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:products that contain live microorganisms in sufficient numbers to alter the intestinal microbiota and promote microbial balance are known as::::', 'products that contain live microorganisms in sufficient numbers to alter the intestinal microbiota and promote microbial balance are known as:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Probiotics are live microorganisms that, when administered in adequate amounts, confer a health benefit on the host. Common probiotic organisms include species of Lactobacillus, Bifidobacterium, Saccharomyces boulardii, and certain strains of Bacillus.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Probiotics\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Probiotics are live microorganisms that, when administered in adequate amounts, confer a health benefit on the host. Common probiotic organisms include species of Lactobacillus, Bifidobacterium, Saccharomyces boulardii, and certain strains of Bacillus.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Antibiotics, Fruits and vegetables, Digestive enzymes"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is true about megaloblastic anemia? i. it may be clinically manifested by neuropathy. ii. vitamin e is required in its dietary management.:::only statement 1 is correct.', 'which of the following is true about megaloblastic anemia? i. it may be clinically manifested by neuropathy. ii. vitamin e is required in its dietary management.', 'Only statement 1 is correct.', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Megaloblastic anemia is primarily caused by a deficiency in Vitamin B12 (cobalamin) or Vitamin B9 (folate). When it stems from a Vitamin B12 deficiency, it uniquely presents with neurological complications, such as peripheral neuropathy (tingling, numbness, or a \"pins and needles\" sensation in the hands and feet), loss of vibratory sense, and gait imbalances. This occurs because Vitamin B12 is mandatory for maintaining the structural integrity of the myelin sheath that insulates nerves.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Only statement 1 is correct.\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Megaloblastic anemia is primarily caused by a deficiency in Vitamin B12 (cobalamin) or Vitamin B9 (folate). When it stems from a Vitamin B12 deficiency, it uniquely presents with neurological complications, such as peripheral neuropathy (tingling, numbness, or a \"pins and needles\" sensation in the hands and feet), loss of vibratory sense, and gait imbalances. This occurs because Vitamin B12 is mandatory for maintaining the structural integrity of the myelin sheath that insulates nerves.\n\n• Statement 2 is INCORRECT: Vitamin E plays no direct physiological role in the development or dietary management of megaloblastic anemia. Vitamin E deficiency causes hemolytic anemia (where fragile red blood cells prematurely rupture due to oxidative damage to their cell membranes), not megaloblastic anemia. The dietary management of megaloblastic anemia strictly requires therapeutic supplementation of Vitamin B12 or Folic Acid.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Only statement 2 is correct., Both statements are correct., Both statements are incorrect."}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is true about megaloblastic anemia? i. it may be clinically manifested by neuropathy. ii. vitamin e is required in its dietary management.:::', 'which of the following is true about megaloblastic anemia? i. it may be clinically manifested by neuropathy. ii. vitamin e is required in its dietary management.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Megaloblastic anemia is primarily caused by a deficiency in Vitamin B12 (cobalamin) or Vitamin B9 (folate). When it stems from a Vitamin B12 deficiency, it uniquely presents with neurological complications, such as peripheral neuropathy (tingling, numbness, or a \"pins and needles\" sensation in the hands and feet), loss of vibratory sense, and gait imbalances. This occurs because Vitamin B12 is mandatory for maintaining the structural integrity of the myelin sheath that insulates nerves.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Only statement 1 is correct.\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Megaloblastic anemia is primarily caused by a deficiency in Vitamin B12 (cobalamin) or Vitamin B9 (folate). When it stems from a Vitamin B12 deficiency, it uniquely presents with neurological complications, such as peripheral neuropathy (tingling, numbness, or a \"pins and needles\" sensation in the hands and feet), loss of vibratory sense, and gait imbalances. This occurs because Vitamin B12 is mandatory for maintaining the structural integrity of the myelin sheath that insulates nerves.\n\n• Statement 2 is INCORRECT: Vitamin E plays no direct physiological role in the development or dietary management of megaloblastic anemia. Vitamin E deficiency causes hemolytic anemia (where fragile red blood cells prematurely rupture due to oxidative damage to their cell membranes), not megaloblastic anemia. The dietary management of megaloblastic anemia strictly requires therapeutic supplementation of Vitamin B12 or Folic Acid.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Only statement 2 is correct., Both statements are correct., Both statements are incorrect."}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is/are nutritional effects of chemotherapy? i. dysgeusia ii. diarrhea and constipation iii. esophagitis with dysphagia:::all of the above', 'which of the following is/are nutritional effects of chemotherapy? i. dysgeusia ii. diarrhea and constipation iii. esophagitis with dysphagia', 'All of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Chemotherapy drugs target rapidly dividing cells throughout the body. Because the cells lining the entire gastrointestinal (GI) tract and the taste buds regenerate quickly, they are highly vulnerable to chemotherapy, leading to several significant nutritional side effects:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Chemotherapy drugs target rapidly dividing cells throughout the body. Because the cells lining the entire gastrointestinal (GI) tract and the taste buds regenerate quickly, they are highly vulnerable to chemotherapy, leading to several significant nutritional side effects:\n\n• Dysgeusia: Chemotherapy frequently damages the taste receptor cells in the mouth, causing an altered or metallic taste. This severely diminishes appetite and food enjoyment.\n• Diarrhea and constipation: Chemotherapy can disrupt normal bowel motility and damage the intestinal mucosal lining, leading to severe diarrhea. Conversely, the use of anti-nausea medications (antiemetics) given alongside chemotherapy often causes severe constipation.\n• Esophagitis with dysphagia: The treatment commonly induces mucositis (inflammation and ulceration of the mucous membranes) along the esophagus. This painful swelling (esophagitis) directly results in dysphagia (difficulty swallowing), making oral food intake very challenging.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: I only, I and III only, None of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is/are nutritional effects of chemotherapy? i. dysgeusia ii. diarrhea and constipation iii. esophagitis with dysphagia:::', 'which of the following is/are nutritional effects of chemotherapy? i. dysgeusia ii. diarrhea and constipation iii. esophagitis with dysphagia', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Chemotherapy drugs target rapidly dividing cells throughout the body. Because the cells lining the entire gastrointestinal (GI) tract and the taste buds regenerate quickly, they are highly vulnerable to chemotherapy, leading to several significant nutritional side effects:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Chemotherapy drugs target rapidly dividing cells throughout the body. Because the cells lining the entire gastrointestinal (GI) tract and the taste buds regenerate quickly, they are highly vulnerable to chemotherapy, leading to several significant nutritional side effects:\n\n• Dysgeusia: Chemotherapy frequently damages the taste receptor cells in the mouth, causing an altered or metallic taste. This severely diminishes appetite and food enjoyment.\n• Diarrhea and constipation: Chemotherapy can disrupt normal bowel motility and damage the intestinal mucosal lining, leading to severe diarrhea. Conversely, the use of anti-nausea medications (antiemetics) given alongside chemotherapy often causes severe constipation.\n• Esophagitis with dysphagia: The treatment commonly induces mucositis (inflammation and ulceration of the mucous membranes) along the esophagus. This painful swelling (esophagitis) directly results in dysphagia (difficulty swallowing), making oral food intake very challenging.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: I only, I and III only, None of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:in copper-deficiency anemia, iron can not be released because::::ceruloplasmin is lacking', 'in copper-deficiency anemia, iron can not be released because:', 'Ceruloplasmin is lacking', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Copper deficiency is an uncommon but recognized cause of anemia. Copper is an essential trace mineral required for the activity of several enzymes involved in iron metabolism, hematopoiesis, and cellular respiration. One of the most important copper-containing enzymes is ceruloplasmin, a ferroxidase that oxidizes ferrous iron (Fe²⁺) to ferric iron (Fe³⁺). This conversion is necessary for iron to bind to transferrin, the principal iron transport protein in plasma. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Ceruloplasmin is lacking\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Copper deficiency is an uncommon but recognized cause of anemia. Copper is an essential trace mineral required for the activity of several enzymes involved in iron metabolism, hematopoiesis, and cellular respiration. One of the most important copper-containing enzymes is ceruloplasmin, a ferroxidase that oxidizes ferrous iron (Fe²⁺) to ferric iron (Fe³⁺). This conversion is necessary for iron to bind to transferrin, the principal iron transport protein in plasma. \n\nWhen copper is deficient: \n• Ceruloplasmin activity decreases. \n• Iron cannot be efficiently mobilized from storage sites such as the liver and macrophages. \n• Iron transport to the bone marrow is impaired. \n• Hemoglobin synthesis becomes inadequate despite normal or even increased body iron stores. \n\nAs a result, patients may develop microcytic, normocytic, or occasionally macrocytic anemia, often accompanied by neutropenia. Copper deficiency should be suspected in patients with unexplained anemia who have undergone bariatric surgery, have prolonged parenteral nutrition without adequate copper supplementation, malabsorptive disorders, or excessive zinc intake, which interferes with copper absorption.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Protein is lacking, Vitamin B6 is lacking, Clotting factors are lacking"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:in copper-deficiency anemia, iron can not be released because::::', 'in copper-deficiency anemia, iron can not be released because:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Copper deficiency is an uncommon but recognized cause of anemia. Copper is an essential trace mineral required for the activity of several enzymes involved in iron metabolism, hematopoiesis, and cellular respiration. One of the most important copper-containing enzymes is ceruloplasmin, a ferroxidase that oxidizes ferrous iron (Fe²⁺) to ferric iron (Fe³⁺). This conversion is necessary for iron to bind to transferrin, the principal iron transport protein in plasma. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Ceruloplasmin is lacking\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Copper deficiency is an uncommon but recognized cause of anemia. Copper is an essential trace mineral required for the activity of several enzymes involved in iron metabolism, hematopoiesis, and cellular respiration. One of the most important copper-containing enzymes is ceruloplasmin, a ferroxidase that oxidizes ferrous iron (Fe²⁺) to ferric iron (Fe³⁺). This conversion is necessary for iron to bind to transferrin, the principal iron transport protein in plasma. \n\nWhen copper is deficient: \n• Ceruloplasmin activity decreases. \n• Iron cannot be efficiently mobilized from storage sites such as the liver and macrophages. \n• Iron transport to the bone marrow is impaired. \n• Hemoglobin synthesis becomes inadequate despite normal or even increased body iron stores. \n\nAs a result, patients may develop microcytic, normocytic, or occasionally macrocytic anemia, often accompanied by neutropenia. Copper deficiency should be suspected in patients with unexplained anemia who have undergone bariatric surgery, have prolonged parenteral nutrition without adequate copper supplementation, malabsorptive disorders, or excessive zinc intake, which interferes with copper absorption.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Protein is lacking, Vitamin B6 is lacking, Clotting factors are lacking"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:it describes the bone after the developmental period is completed and often used for bone monitoring in adults::::bone mineral density', 'it describes the bone after the developmental period is completed and often used for bone monitoring in adults:', 'Bone mineral density', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Bone mineral density (BMD) is the absolute \"gold standard\" metric used in clinical settings to monitor adult skeletal health over time. It measures the amount of mineral matter (like calcium) per square centimeter of bone tissue via diagnostic tools such as Dual-Energy X-ray Absorptiometry (DEXA scans).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Bone mineral density\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Bone mineral density (BMD) is the absolute \"gold standard\" metric used in clinical settings to monitor adult skeletal health over time. It measures the amount of mineral matter (like calcium) per square centimeter of bone tissue via diagnostic tools such as Dual-Energy X-ray Absorptiometry (DEXA scans).\n\nWhile bones stop growing in length and reach their ultimate structural ceiling at the end of the growth period, the tracking of a patient''s current bone status throughout mature adulthood focuses heavily on their BMD values to screen for conditions like osteopenia and osteoporosis.\n\n• Peak Bone Mass: Peak Bone Mass refers exclusively to the maximum amount of bone tissue an individual can physically accumulate by the end of their developmental phase (typically around ages 20 to 30). Because it describes a specific, static historical milestone rather than a dynamic continuous variable, it is not what is tracked during Routine adult bone monitoring.\n• Bone mineral content (BMC) measures total mineral weight rather than concentration/density. In standard diagnostic jargon, Bone mass is a conceptual term often used interchangeably with BMD, but BMD is the official diagnostic metric quantified for patient monitoring.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Bone mineral content, Peak Bone Mass, Bone mass"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:protein recommendation for wound healing?:::20-25% of ter', 'protein recommendation for wound healing?', '20-25% of TER', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Adequate protein is essential for collagen synthesis, immune function, and tissue repair. Patients with severe wounds or extensive burns may require protein intakes as high as 2.0–3.0 g/kg/day, while protein may also account for approximately 20–25% of total energy intake depending on the clinical condition.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"20-25% of TER\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Adequate protein is essential for collagen synthesis, immune function, and tissue repair. Patients with severe wounds or extensive burns may require protein intakes as high as 2.0–3.0 g/kg/day, while protein may also account for approximately 20–25% of total energy intake depending on the clinical condition.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 1.5 – 2 g/kg, 15-20% of TER, 50 % of TER"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:wilson’s disease is the inability of the body to metabolize , while hemochromatosis is a disorder in metabolism.:::', 'wilson’s disease is the inability of the body to metabolize , while hemochromatosis is a disorder in metabolism.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Wilson''s Disease (Copper Accumulation): This is an autosomal recessive genetic disorder caused by a mutation in the ATP7B gene. This mutation disrupts the liver''s ability to transport and excrete excess copper into bile. As a result, toxic levels of copper build up primarily in the liver, brain, and the corneas of the eyes (forming classic golden-brown Kayser-Fleischer rings).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Copper, iron\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Wilson''s Disease (Copper Accumulation): This is an autosomal recessive genetic disorder caused by a mutation in the ATP7B gene. This mutation disrupts the liver''s ability to transport and excrete excess copper into bile. As a result, toxic levels of copper build up primarily in the liver, brain, and the corneas of the eyes (forming classic golden-brown Kayser-Fleischer rings).\n\nHemochromatosis (Iron Overload): This metabolic disorder is characterized by a failure in iron-sensing mechanisms (often due to mutations in the HFE gene), which leads to the uncontrolled, excessive absorption of dietary iron from the gastrointestinal tract. The excess iron gets deposited heavily into vital organs such as the liver, heart, and pancreas, which can cause cirrhosis, cardiac arrhythmias, and diabetes (traditionally termed \"bronze diabetes\").","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Iron, copper, Calcium, iron, Iron, calcium"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:it describes the bone after the developmental period is completed and often used for bone monitoring in adults::::', 'it describes the bone after the developmental period is completed and often used for bone monitoring in adults:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Bone mineral density (BMD) is the absolute \"gold standard\" metric used in clinical settings to monitor adult skeletal health over time. It measures the amount of mineral matter (like calcium) per square centimeter of bone tissue via diagnostic tools such as Dual-Energy X-ray Absorptiometry (DEXA scans).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Bone mineral density\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Bone mineral density (BMD) is the absolute \"gold standard\" metric used in clinical settings to monitor adult skeletal health over time. It measures the amount of mineral matter (like calcium) per square centimeter of bone tissue via diagnostic tools such as Dual-Energy X-ray Absorptiometry (DEXA scans).\n\nWhile bones stop growing in length and reach their ultimate structural ceiling at the end of the growth period, the tracking of a patient''s current bone status throughout mature adulthood focuses heavily on their BMD values to screen for conditions like osteopenia and osteoporosis.\n\n• Peak Bone Mass: Peak Bone Mass refers exclusively to the maximum amount of bone tissue an individual can physically accumulate by the end of their developmental phase (typically around ages 20 to 30). Because it describes a specific, static historical milestone rather than a dynamic continuous variable, it is not what is tracked during Routine adult bone monitoring.\n• Bone mineral content (BMC) measures total mineral weight rather than concentration/density. In standard diagnostic jargon, Bone mass is a conceptual term often used interchangeably with BMD, but BMD is the official diagnostic metric quantified for patient monitoring.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Bone mineral content, Peak Bone Mass, Bone mass"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the vertebrae and bones of the jaw are classified as because of their shapes::::irregular bones', 'the vertebrae and bones of the jaw are classified as because of their shapes:', 'Irregular bones', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Irregular bones are defined by their complex, highly specialized shapes that do not cleanly fit into standard geometric categories like long, short, or flat. They typically feature a mix of ridges, notches, and projections.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Irregular bones\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Irregular bones are defined by their complex, highly specialized shapes that do not cleanly fit into standard geometric categories like long, short, or flat. They typically feature a mix of ridges, notches, and projections.\n\nEach vertebra in the spine consists of a thick vertebral body, an arch, and multiple bony projections (such as the spinous and transverse processes) that serve as crucial muscle attachment points and shield the spinal cord.\n\nThe jawbones are part of the facial skeleton. The mandible (lower jaw) and maxilla (upper jaw) feature a highly irregular, irregular layout tailored to house tooth sockets, support facial muscles, and form parts of the nasal and oral cavities.\n\n• Flat bones: These are thin, flattened, and usually slightly curved bones designed for extensive muscle attachment or organ protection (e.g., the ribs, sternum, and most cranial skull bones). While some older literatures loosely lump the flat portions of the jaw with flat bones, modern structural taxonomy officially classifies the facial bones—including the mandible—as irregular.\n• Short bones: These are roughly cube-shaped, having an approximately equal length, width, and thickness to provide stability and support (e.g., the carpals in the wrist and tarsals in the ankle).\n• Long bones: These are elongated, cylindrical shafts that are noticeably longer than they are wide, acting as levers to facilitate body movement (e.g., the femur, humerus, and phalanges).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Long bones, Short bones, Flat bones"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the vertebrae and bones of the jaw are classified as because of their shapes::::', 'the vertebrae and bones of the jaw are classified as because of their shapes:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Irregular bones are defined by their complex, highly specialized shapes that do not cleanly fit into standard geometric categories like long, short, or flat. They typically feature a mix of ridges, notches, and projections.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Irregular bones\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Irregular bones are defined by their complex, highly specialized shapes that do not cleanly fit into standard geometric categories like long, short, or flat. They typically feature a mix of ridges, notches, and projections.\n\nEach vertebra in the spine consists of a thick vertebral body, an arch, and multiple bony projections (such as the spinous and transverse processes) that serve as crucial muscle attachment points and shield the spinal cord.\n\nThe jawbones are part of the facial skeleton. The mandible (lower jaw) and maxilla (upper jaw) feature a highly irregular, irregular layout tailored to house tooth sockets, support facial muscles, and form parts of the nasal and oral cavities.\n\n• Flat bones: These are thin, flattened, and usually slightly curved bones designed for extensive muscle attachment or organ protection (e.g., the ribs, sternum, and most cranial skull bones). While some older literatures loosely lump the flat portions of the jaw with flat bones, modern structural taxonomy officially classifies the facial bones—including the mandible—as irregular.\n• Short bones: These are roughly cube-shaped, having an approximately equal length, width, and thickness to provide stability and support (e.g., the carpals in the wrist and tarsals in the ankle).\n• Long bones: These are elongated, cylindrical shafts that are noticeably longer than they are wide, acting as levers to facilitate body movement (e.g., the femur, humerus, and phalanges).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Long bones, Short bones, Flat bones"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:wilson’s disease is the inability of the body to metabolize , while hemochromatosis is a disorder in metabolism.:::copper, iron', 'wilson’s disease is the inability of the body to metabolize , while hemochromatosis is a disorder in metabolism.', 'Copper, iron', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Wilson''s Disease (Copper Accumulation): This is an autosomal recessive genetic disorder caused by a mutation in the ATP7B gene. This mutation disrupts the liver''s ability to transport and excrete excess copper into bile. As a result, toxic levels of copper build up primarily in the liver, brain, and the corneas of the eyes (forming classic golden-brown Kayser-Fleischer rings).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Copper, iron\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Wilson''s Disease (Copper Accumulation): This is an autosomal recessive genetic disorder caused by a mutation in the ATP7B gene. This mutation disrupts the liver''s ability to transport and excrete excess copper into bile. As a result, toxic levels of copper build up primarily in the liver, brain, and the corneas of the eyes (forming classic golden-brown Kayser-Fleischer rings).\n\nHemochromatosis (Iron Overload): This metabolic disorder is characterized by a failure in iron-sensing mechanisms (often due to mutations in the HFE gene), which leads to the uncontrolled, excessive absorption of dietary iron from the gastrointestinal tract. The excess iron gets deposited heavily into vital organs such as the liver, heart, and pancreas, which can cause cirrhosis, cardiac arrhythmias, and diabetes (traditionally termed \"bronze diabetes\").","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Iron, copper, Calcium, iron, Iron, calcium"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:protein recommendation for wound healing?:::', 'protein recommendation for wound healing?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Adequate protein is essential for collagen synthesis, immune function, and tissue repair. Patients with severe wounds or extensive burns may require protein intakes as high as 2.0–3.0 g/kg/day, while protein may also account for approximately 20–25% of total energy intake depending on the clinical condition.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"20-25% of TER\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Adequate protein is essential for collagen synthesis, immune function, and tissue repair. Patients with severe wounds or extensive burns may require protein intakes as high as 2.0–3.0 g/kg/day, while protein may also account for approximately 20–25% of total energy intake depending on the clinical condition.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 1.5 – 2 g/kg, 15-20% of TER, 50 % of TER"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:it is also known as lou gehrig disease::::amyotrophic lateral sclerosis', 'it is also known as lou gehrig disease:', 'Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS), also known as Lou Gehrig''s disease, is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder characterized by the degeneration of upper and lower motor neurons. The disease leads to progressive muscle weakness, muscle wasting, spasticity, dysarthria, dysphagia, and eventually respiratory failure. Although cognition is preserved in many patients, some develop frontotemporal cognitive impairment or dementia.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS), also known as Lou Gehrig''s disease, is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder characterized by the degeneration of upper and lower motor neurons. The disease leads to progressive muscle weakness, muscle wasting, spasticity, dysarthria, dysphagia, and eventually respiratory failure. Although cognition is preserved in many patients, some develop frontotemporal cognitive impairment or dementia.\n\nFrom a nutrition perspective, ALS is associated with:\n• Progressive dysphagia, increasing the risk of aspiration.\n• Weight loss due to reduced oral intake and increased energy expenditure.\n• Loss of muscle mass and protein-energy malnutrition.\n• Increased risk of dehydration.\n\nMedical nutrition therapy aims to:\n• Maintain body weight and preserve lean body mass.\n• Provide high-energy, high-protein meals when appropriate.\n• Modify food texture and liquid consistency according to swallowing ability.\n• Recommend oral nutrition supplements when intake is inadequate.\n• Consider enteral nutrition (e.g., gastrostomy feeding) when oral intake becomes unsafe or insufficient.\n\nEarly nutrition intervention is associated with improved nutritional status and may contribute to a better quality of life.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Alzheimer''s disease, Cerebral palsy, Parkinson''s Disease"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:it is also known as lou gehrig disease::::', 'it is also known as lou gehrig disease:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS), also known as Lou Gehrig''s disease, is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder characterized by the degeneration of upper and lower motor neurons. The disease leads to progressive muscle weakness, muscle wasting, spasticity, dysarthria, dysphagia, and eventually respiratory failure. Although cognition is preserved in many patients, some develop frontotemporal cognitive impairment or dementia.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS), also known as Lou Gehrig''s disease, is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder characterized by the degeneration of upper and lower motor neurons. The disease leads to progressive muscle weakness, muscle wasting, spasticity, dysarthria, dysphagia, and eventually respiratory failure. Although cognition is preserved in many patients, some develop frontotemporal cognitive impairment or dementia.\n\nFrom a nutrition perspective, ALS is associated with:\n• Progressive dysphagia, increasing the risk of aspiration.\n• Weight loss due to reduced oral intake and increased energy expenditure.\n• Loss of muscle mass and protein-energy malnutrition.\n• Increased risk of dehydration.\n\nMedical nutrition therapy aims to:\n• Maintain body weight and preserve lean body mass.\n• Provide high-energy, high-protein meals when appropriate.\n• Modify food texture and liquid consistency according to swallowing ability.\n• Recommend oral nutrition supplements when intake is inadequate.\n• Consider enteral nutrition (e.g., gastrostomy feeding) when oral intake becomes unsafe or insufficient.\n\nEarly nutrition intervention is associated with improved nutritional status and may contribute to a better quality of life.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Alzheimer''s disease, Cerebral palsy, Parkinson''s Disease"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:water losses of of total body weight can be fatal.:::', 'water losses of of total body weight can be fatal.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Water is the largest component of the human body, accounting for approximately 50–60% of body weight in adults, although this varies with age, sex, and body composition. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"9-12%\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Water is the largest component of the human body, accounting for approximately 50–60% of body weight in adults, although this varies with age, sex, and body composition. \n\nMaintaining fluid balance is essential for normal physiologic functions, including: \n• Regulation of body temperature \n• Transport of nutrients and waste products \n• Maintenance of blood volume and blood pressure \n• Cellular metabolism \n• Electrolyte balance \n\nThe severity of dehydration is often expressed as the percentage of body weight lost due to water deficit. Therefore, water losses of approximately 9–12% of body weight can become life-threatening and may be fatal if not rapidly corrected.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 2-3%, 3%, 5%"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are general dietary management for fevers and infections, except::::low fat', 'the following are general dietary management for fevers and infections, except:', 'Low fat', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"During fever and infection, the body enters a hypermetabolic and catabolic state, resulting in increased energy expenditure, accelerated protein breakdown, and increased fluid losses. Nutrition therapy aims to support immune function, preserve lean body mass, promote recovery, and prevent malnutrition. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Low fat\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"During fever and infection, the body enters a hypermetabolic and catabolic state, resulting in increased energy expenditure, accelerated protein breakdown, and increased fluid losses. Nutrition therapy aims to support immune function, preserve lean body mass, promote recovery, and prevent malnutrition. \n\nA low-fat diet is not routinely recommended for patients with fever or infection. Dietary fat is an important source of concentrated energy, particularly when appetite is poor. Fat restriction is indicated only in specific clinical conditions such as pancreatitis, fat malabsorption, or certain hepatobiliary disorders—not as part of the general management of fever and infection.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: High Kcal, High Protein, High fluids"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:osmolality of body fluids.:::', 'osmolality of body fluids.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Osmolality is the concentration of osmotically active particles per kilogram of solvent (water) and is expressed as milliosmoles per kilogram (mOsm/kg). It reflects the body''s water balance and is tightly regulated to maintain normal cellular function. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"290 mOsm/kg\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Osmolality is the concentration of osmotically active particles per kilogram of solvent (water) and is expressed as milliosmoles per kilogram (mOsm/kg). It reflects the body''s water balance and is tightly regulated to maintain normal cellular function. \n\nThe normal plasma osmolality is approximately: \n• 275–295 mOsm/kg, with an average value of 290 mOsm/kg","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 400 mOsm/kg, 480 mOsm/kg, 200 mOsm/kg"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are general dietary management for fevers and infections, except::::', 'the following are general dietary management for fevers and infections, except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"During fever and infection, the body enters a hypermetabolic and catabolic state, resulting in increased energy expenditure, accelerated protein breakdown, and increased fluid losses. Nutrition therapy aims to support immune function, preserve lean body mass, promote recovery, and prevent malnutrition. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Low fat\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"During fever and infection, the body enters a hypermetabolic and catabolic state, resulting in increased energy expenditure, accelerated protein breakdown, and increased fluid losses. Nutrition therapy aims to support immune function, preserve lean body mass, promote recovery, and prevent malnutrition. \n\nA low-fat diet is not routinely recommended for patients with fever or infection. Dietary fat is an important source of concentrated energy, particularly when appetite is poor. Fat restriction is indicated only in specific clinical conditions such as pancreatitis, fat malabsorption, or certain hepatobiliary disorders—not as part of the general management of fever and infection.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: High Kcal, High Protein, High fluids"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:an immunologic disease of the joints and related structures:::rheumatoid arthritis', 'an immunologic disease of the joints and related structures', 'Rheumatoid Arthritis', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Rheumatoid Arthritis (RA) is a chronic, systemic autoimmune (immunologic) disorder. In RA, the body''s immune system mistakenly attacks the synovium—the thin membrane that lines the joints. This leads to severe, painful inflammation, cartilage destruction, and bone erosion.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Rheumatoid Arthritis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Rheumatoid Arthritis (RA) is a chronic, systemic autoimmune (immunologic) disorder. In RA, the body''s immune system mistakenly attacks the synovium—the thin membrane that lines the joints. This leads to severe, painful inflammation, cartilage destruction, and bone erosion.\n\n\t• Osteoporosis: This is a metabolic bone disease characterized by a loss of bone density and increased fragility, typically caused by hormonal changes, aging, or nutritional deficiencies rather than an immune system attack.\n\t• Sjögren’s syndrome: While this is an immunologic/autoimmune disease, its primary targets are the moisture-producing glands (such as salivary and tear glands), leading to severe dry eyes and dry mouth. Although it can be associated with joint pain, the primary immunologic joint disease listed is RA.\n\t• Gout: This is a metabolic form of inflammatory arthritis caused by the accumulation and deposition of uric acid (monosodium urate) crystals in the joints, not an immunologic or autoimmune malfunction.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Gout, Sjogren’s syndrome, Osteoporosis"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:an immunologic disease of the joints and related structures:::', 'an immunologic disease of the joints and related structures', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Rheumatoid Arthritis (RA) is a chronic, systemic autoimmune (immunologic) disorder. In RA, the body''s immune system mistakenly attacks the synovium—the thin membrane that lines the joints. This leads to severe, painful inflammation, cartilage destruction, and bone erosion.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Rheumatoid Arthritis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Rheumatoid Arthritis (RA) is a chronic, systemic autoimmune (immunologic) disorder. In RA, the body''s immune system mistakenly attacks the synovium—the thin membrane that lines the joints. This leads to severe, painful inflammation, cartilage destruction, and bone erosion.\n\n\t• Osteoporosis: This is a metabolic bone disease characterized by a loss of bone density and increased fragility, typically caused by hormonal changes, aging, or nutritional deficiencies rather than an immune system attack.\n\t• Sjögren’s syndrome: While this is an immunologic/autoimmune disease, its primary targets are the moisture-producing glands (such as salivary and tear glands), leading to severe dry eyes and dry mouth. Although it can be associated with joint pain, the primary immunologic joint disease listed is RA.\n\t• Gout: This is a metabolic form of inflammatory arthritis caused by the accumulation and deposition of uric acid (monosodium urate) crystals in the joints, not an immunologic or autoimmune malfunction.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Gout, Sjogren’s syndrome, Osteoporosis"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the addition of 15 grams salt in the diet increases sodium content by::::', 'the addition of 15 grams salt in the diet increases sodium content by:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Table salt is chemically known as sodium chloride (NaCl). By molecular weight, pure table salt is composed of approximately 40% sodium and 60% chloride.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"6 grams\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Table salt is chemically known as sodium chloride (NaCl). By molecular weight, pure table salt is composed of approximately 40% sodium and 60% chloride.\n\nTo determine how much sodium is present in a given mass of table salt, you multiply the total mass by 40% (0.40):\n15 grams of salt x 0.40 = 6 grams of sodium","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 3,000 mg, 2 grams, 8 grams"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is not a general dietary recommendation for patients with hiv/aids?:::fiber-restricted', 'which of the following is not a general dietary recommendation for patients with hiv/aids?', 'Fiber-restricted', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Nutrition therapy is an essential component of the management of HIV/AIDS, helping maintain body weight, preserve lean body mass, support immune function, and improve quality of life. Energy and protein requirements are often increased because of chronic infection, inflammation, opportunistic infections, and the increased metabolic demands associated with HIV. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Fiber-restricted\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Nutrition therapy is an essential component of the management of HIV/AIDS, helping maintain body weight, preserve lean body mass, support immune function, and improve quality of life. Energy and protein requirements are often increased because of chronic infection, inflammation, opportunistic infections, and the increased metabolic demands associated with HIV. \n\nGeneral nutrition recommendations include: \n• High-calorie diet to prevent or manage unintended weight loss. \n• High-protein diet to preserve muscle mass and promote tissue repair. \n• Adequate intake of vitamins, minerals, and fluids. \n• Food safety practices to reduce the risk of foodborne illness. \n• Nutrition interventions tailored to treatment-related side effects such as nausea, diarrhea, or oral lesions.\n\nDietary fiber provides important benefits, including promoting bowel health, improving glycemic control, and supporting a healthy gut microbiota. Fiber restriction is reserved for specific clinical situations, such as severe diarrhea, intestinal obstruction, or acute gastrointestinal complications, and is not recommended as a standard dietary approach for all patients with HIV/AIDS.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: High calorie, High protein, Fat-controlled"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is not a general dietary recommendation for patients with hiv/aids?:::', 'which of the following is not a general dietary recommendation for patients with hiv/aids?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Nutrition therapy is an essential component of the management of HIV/AIDS, helping maintain body weight, preserve lean body mass, support immune function, and improve quality of life. Energy and protein requirements are often increased because of chronic infection, inflammation, opportunistic infections, and the increased metabolic demands associated with HIV. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Fiber-restricted\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Nutrition therapy is an essential component of the management of HIV/AIDS, helping maintain body weight, preserve lean body mass, support immune function, and improve quality of life. Energy and protein requirements are often increased because of chronic infection, inflammation, opportunistic infections, and the increased metabolic demands associated with HIV. \n\nGeneral nutrition recommendations include: \n• High-calorie diet to prevent or manage unintended weight loss. \n• High-protein diet to preserve muscle mass and promote tissue repair. \n• Adequate intake of vitamins, minerals, and fluids. \n• Food safety practices to reduce the risk of foodborne illness. \n• Nutrition interventions tailored to treatment-related side effects such as nausea, diarrhea, or oral lesions.\n\nDietary fiber provides important benefits, including promoting bowel health, improving glycemic control, and supporting a healthy gut microbiota. Fiber restriction is reserved for specific clinical situations, such as severe diarrhea, intestinal obstruction, or acute gastrointestinal complications, and is not recommended as a standard dietary approach for all patients with HIV/AIDS.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: High calorie, High protein, Fat-controlled"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are dietary recommendations for dengue fever, except::::fat-restricted', 'the following are dietary recommendations for dengue fever, except:', 'Fat-restricted', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Nutrition management is an important component of supportive care for dengue fever, particularly because patients commonly experience fever, anorexia, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, fatigue, and dehydration. The goals of nutrition therapy are to maintain hydration, provide adequate energy and protein, and support recovery. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Fat-restricted\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Nutrition management is an important component of supportive care for dengue fever, particularly because patients commonly experience fever, anorexia, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, fatigue, and dehydration. The goals of nutrition therapy are to maintain hydration, provide adequate energy and protein, and support recovery. \n\nGeneral dietary recommendations include: \n• High-calorie diet to meet the increased energy demands associated with acute infection and fever. \n• High-protein diet to support tissue repair and preserve lean body mass, especially in patients with poor oral intake. \n• Adjusted food consistency according to tolerance, such as soft or liquid diets for patients with nausea, vomiting, sore throat, or poor appetite. \n• Adequate oral fluids (or intravenous fluids when indicated) to prevent or treat dehydration, which is a major concern in dengue. \n\nThere is no routine recommendation to restrict dietary fat in patients with dengue fever. Fat restriction is reserved for specific gastrointestinal or hepatobiliary conditions and is not part of standard nutrition management for uncomplicated dengue infection.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: High calorie, High protein, Adjusted food consistency depending on tolerance"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are dietary recommendations for dengue fever, except::::', 'the following are dietary recommendations for dengue fever, except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Nutrition management is an important component of supportive care for dengue fever, particularly because patients commonly experience fever, anorexia, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, fatigue, and dehydration. The goals of nutrition therapy are to maintain hydration, provide adequate energy and protein, and support recovery. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Fat-restricted\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Nutrition management is an important component of supportive care for dengue fever, particularly because patients commonly experience fever, anorexia, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, fatigue, and dehydration. The goals of nutrition therapy are to maintain hydration, provide adequate energy and protein, and support recovery. \n\nGeneral dietary recommendations include: \n• High-calorie diet to meet the increased energy demands associated with acute infection and fever. \n• High-protein diet to support tissue repair and preserve lean body mass, especially in patients with poor oral intake. \n• Adjusted food consistency according to tolerance, such as soft or liquid diets for patients with nausea, vomiting, sore throat, or poor appetite. \n• Adequate oral fluids (or intravenous fluids when indicated) to prevent or treat dehydration, which is a major concern in dengue. \n\nThere is no routine recommendation to restrict dietary fat in patients with dengue fever. Fat restriction is reserved for specific gastrointestinal or hepatobiliary conditions and is not part of standard nutrition management for uncomplicated dengue infection.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: High calorie, High protein, Adjusted food consistency depending on tolerance"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is not a source of data for nutrition assessment?:::none of the above', 'which of the following is not a source of data for nutrition assessment?', 'None of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"All of the options listed are actually valid and standard sources of data used by dietitians and healthcare professionals when performing a thorough Nutrition Assessment:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"None of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"All of the options listed are actually valid and standard sources of data used by dietitians and healthcare professionals when performing a thorough Nutrition Assessment:\n\n• Family history: Part of the Client History (CH) domain. It provides vital background on hereditary risks for chronic metabolic conditions like diabetes, cardiovascular diseases, or inborn errors of metabolism.\n• Medical record: This serves as a primary hub for objective data including patient demographics, medical history, clinical diagnoses, surgical histories, and Biochemical Data/Medical Tests (such as lab panels or imaging).\n• Referral form: A referral from a physician or another healthcare provider frequently contains initial clinical data, anthropometric measurements, the explicit medical reason for nutrition intervention, and immediate dietary constraints.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Family history, Medical record, Referral form"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which specific storage protein (prolamin) in wheat triggers the autoimmune enteropathy in celiac disease?:::', 'which specific storage protein (prolamin) in wheat triggers the autoimmune enteropathy in celiac disease?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Celiac disease is triggered by the ingestion of gluten prolamins: Gliadin in wheat, Secalin in rye, and Hordein in barley. These induce immune-mediated small intestinal mucosal inflammation and villous atrophy.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Gliadin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Celiac disease is triggered by the ingestion of gluten prolamins: Gliadin in wheat, Secalin in rye, and Hordein in barley. These induce immune-mediated small intestinal mucosal inflammation and villous atrophy.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Glutenin, Casein, Zein"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is not a source of data for nutrition assessment?:::', 'which of the following is not a source of data for nutrition assessment?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"All of the options listed are actually valid and standard sources of data used by dietitians and healthcare professionals when performing a thorough Nutrition Assessment:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"None of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"All of the options listed are actually valid and standard sources of data used by dietitians and healthcare professionals when performing a thorough Nutrition Assessment:\n\n• Family history: Part of the Client History (CH) domain. It provides vital background on hereditary risks for chronic metabolic conditions like diabetes, cardiovascular diseases, or inborn errors of metabolism.\n• Medical record: This serves as a primary hub for objective data including patient demographics, medical history, clinical diagnoses, surgical histories, and Biochemical Data/Medical Tests (such as lab panels or imaging).\n• Referral form: A referral from a physician or another healthcare provider frequently contains initial clinical data, anthropometric measurements, the explicit medical reason for nutrition intervention, and immediate dietary constraints.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Family history, Medical record, Referral form"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:dietary management for children with severe acute malnutrition during nutritional rehabilitation: i. aim to provide 100–200 kcal/kg actual body weight per day, depending on the phase of treatment and clinical condition. ii. provide approximately 120–150 ml fluid/kg actual body weight per day, with adjustments based on hydration status and clinical judgment. iii. use food items that promote early satiety.:::i and ii only', 'dietary management for children with severe acute malnutrition during nutritional rehabilitation: i. aim to provide 100–200 kcal/kg actual body weight per day, depending on the phase of treatment and clinical condition. ii. provide approximately 120–150 ml fluid/kg actual body weight per day, with adjustments based on hydration status and clinical judgment. iii. use food items that promote early satiety.', 'I and II only', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Children with severe acute malnutrition (SAM) require carefully planned nutrition therapy that restores nutritional status while minimizing complications such as refeeding syndrome, heart failure, and electrolyte imbalance. Management follows a phased approach consisting of stabilization, transition, and rehabilitation. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"I and II only\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Children with severe acute malnutrition (SAM) require carefully planned nutrition therapy that restores nutritional status while minimizing complications such as refeeding syndrome, heart failure, and electrolyte imbalance. Management follows a phased approach consisting of stabilization, transition, and rehabilitation. \n\nStatement I is correct. During the rehabilitation phase, children require high-energy diets to achieve catch-up growth. Energy requirements generally range from 100 to 200 kcal/kg/day, depending on the stage of treatment, clinical status, and feeding regimen. Therapeutic foods such as F-100 or Ready-to-Use Therapeutic Food (RUTF) are commonly used during rehabilitation. \n\nStatement II is correct. Fluid management is equally important because children with severe malnutrition have altered fluid and electrolyte balance. Total fluid intake commonly ranges from 120–150 mL/kg/day, but it should always be individualized according to hydration status, ongoing losses, cardiac function, and the phase of treatment. Excessive fluid administration should be avoided because malnourished children are susceptible to fluid overload. \n\nStatement III is incorrect. The goal of nutrition therapy is to maximize nutrient and energy intake, not to promote early satiety. Foods that cause early fullness may reduce overall caloric intake and delay nutritional recovery. Instead, children should receive energy-dense, nutrient-dense foods in frequent, small feedings that are well tolerated.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: I only, II only, All of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:dietary management for children with severe acute malnutrition during nutritional rehabilitation: i. aim to provide 100–200 kcal/kg actual body weight per day, depending on the phase of treatment and clinical condition. ii. provide approximately 120–150 ml fluid/kg actual body weight per day, with adjustments based on hydration status and clinical judgment. iii. use food items that promote early satiety.:::', 'dietary management for children with severe acute malnutrition during nutritional rehabilitation: i. aim to provide 100–200 kcal/kg actual body weight per day, depending on the phase of treatment and clinical condition. ii. provide approximately 120–150 ml fluid/kg actual body weight per day, with adjustments based on hydration status and clinical judgment. iii. use food items that promote early satiety.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Children with severe acute malnutrition (SAM) require carefully planned nutrition therapy that restores nutritional status while minimizing complications such as refeeding syndrome, heart failure, and electrolyte imbalance. Management follows a phased approach consisting of stabilization, transition, and rehabilitation. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"I and II only\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Children with severe acute malnutrition (SAM) require carefully planned nutrition therapy that restores nutritional status while minimizing complications such as refeeding syndrome, heart failure, and electrolyte imbalance. Management follows a phased approach consisting of stabilization, transition, and rehabilitation. \n\nStatement I is correct. During the rehabilitation phase, children require high-energy diets to achieve catch-up growth. Energy requirements generally range from 100 to 200 kcal/kg/day, depending on the stage of treatment, clinical status, and feeding regimen. Therapeutic foods such as F-100 or Ready-to-Use Therapeutic Food (RUTF) are commonly used during rehabilitation. \n\nStatement II is correct. Fluid management is equally important because children with severe malnutrition have altered fluid and electrolyte balance. Total fluid intake commonly ranges from 120–150 mL/kg/day, but it should always be individualized according to hydration status, ongoing losses, cardiac function, and the phase of treatment. Excessive fluid administration should be avoided because malnourished children are susceptible to fluid overload. \n\nStatement III is incorrect. The goal of nutrition therapy is to maximize nutrient and energy intake, not to promote early satiety. Foods that cause early fullness may reduce overall caloric intake and delay nutritional recovery. Instead, children should receive energy-dense, nutrient-dense foods in frequent, small feedings that are well tolerated.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: I only, II only, All of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are characteristics of ketogenic diet, except::::provides all the vitamins and minerals needed', 'the following are characteristics of ketogenic diet, except:', 'Provides all the vitamins and minerals needed', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A classic ketogenic diet is highly restrictive because it eliminates major food groups like most fruits, starchy vegetables, whole grains, and dairy products. Because of this extreme restriction, the diet is notoriously deficient in essential micronutrients (such as calcium, vitamin D, selenium, zinc, and water-soluble B vitamins). Patients on a clinical ketogenic diet must take targeted, sugar-free vitamin and mineral supplements to avoid severe deficiencies.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Provides all the vitamins and minerals needed\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A classic ketogenic diet is highly restrictive because it eliminates major food groups like most fruits, starchy vegetables, whole grains, and dairy products. Because of this extreme restriction, the diet is notoriously deficient in essential micronutrients (such as calcium, vitamin D, selenium, zinc, and water-soluble B vitamins). Patients on a clinical ketogenic diet must take targeted, sugar-free vitamin and mineral supplements to avoid severe deficiencies.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Planned diet provides adequate calories, Contains 1-2 grams of protein per kilogram body weight, Provides ketogenic ration of 3-4 grams fat for every 1 gram of carbohydrate and protein"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are characteristics of ketogenic diet, except::::', 'the following are characteristics of ketogenic diet, except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A classic ketogenic diet is highly restrictive because it eliminates major food groups like most fruits, starchy vegetables, whole grains, and dairy products. Because of this extreme restriction, the diet is notoriously deficient in essential micronutrients (such as calcium, vitamin D, selenium, zinc, and water-soluble B vitamins). Patients on a clinical ketogenic diet must take targeted, sugar-free vitamin and mineral supplements to avoid severe deficiencies.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Provides all the vitamins and minerals needed\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A classic ketogenic diet is highly restrictive because it eliminates major food groups like most fruits, starchy vegetables, whole grains, and dairy products. Because of this extreme restriction, the diet is notoriously deficient in essential micronutrients (such as calcium, vitamin D, selenium, zinc, and water-soluble B vitamins). Patients on a clinical ketogenic diet must take targeted, sugar-free vitamin and mineral supplements to avoid severe deficiencies.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Planned diet provides adequate calories, Contains 1-2 grams of protein per kilogram body weight, Provides ketogenic ration of 3-4 grams fat for every 1 gram of carbohydrate and protein"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:dietary management for pancreatitis::::npo, enzyme replacement, low fat, avoidance of stimulants', 'dietary management for pancreatitis:', 'NPO, enzyme replacement, low fat, avoidance of stimulants', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Pancreatitis is inflammation of the pancreas and may be classified as acute or chronic. Although both conditions require nutrition management, their dietary recommendations differ according to disease stage and severity. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"NPO, enzyme replacement, low fat, avoidance of stimulants\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Pancreatitis is inflammation of the pancreas and may be classified as acute or chronic. Although both conditions require nutrition management, their dietary recommendations differ according to disease stage and severity. \n\nDuring acute pancreatitis, patients with moderate to severe disease may initially require bowel rest if oral feeding is not tolerated because of severe abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, or ileus. However, current guidelines recommend early oral or enteral feeding once clinically tolerated, as prolonged fasting is no longer routinely advised. In chronic pancreatitis, nutrition therapy aims to prevent malnutrition, reduce pancreatic stimulation, and improve nutrient absorption.\n\n• NPO (Nil Per Os / Nothing by Mouth): During acute episodes or severe flare-ups of pancreatitis, bowel rest (keeping the patient NPO) is primary to minimize pancreatic stimulation and reduce auto-digestion of the organ.\n• Enzyme Replacement: For patients with exocrine pancreatic insufficiency (EPI) or chronic pancreatitis, taking Pancreatic Enzyme Replacement Therapy (PERT) with meals is necessary to facilitate proper macronutrient digestion and nutrient absorption.\n• Low Fat: Fat is a potent stimulant of pancreatic enzyme secretion. Restricting fat intake (often between 30–50 grams per day) and spreading it across small, frequent meals helps alleviate abdominal pain and prevent fatty stools (steatorrhea).\n• Avoidance of Stimulants: Patients must strictly avoid pancreatic irritants and stimulants, most notably alcohol and nicotine, as they directly exacerbate inflammation and disease progression.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: High kcal, low protein, high carbohydrate, high fiber, High kcal, high protein, low fat, Liquid diet, iron supplementation, low fiber"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:dietary management for pancreatitis::::', 'dietary management for pancreatitis:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Pancreatitis is inflammation of the pancreas and may be classified as acute or chronic. Although both conditions require nutrition management, their dietary recommendations differ according to disease stage and severity. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"NPO, enzyme replacement, low fat, avoidance of stimulants\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Pancreatitis is inflammation of the pancreas and may be classified as acute or chronic. Although both conditions require nutrition management, their dietary recommendations differ according to disease stage and severity. \n\nDuring acute pancreatitis, patients with moderate to severe disease may initially require bowel rest if oral feeding is not tolerated because of severe abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, or ileus. However, current guidelines recommend early oral or enteral feeding once clinically tolerated, as prolonged fasting is no longer routinely advised. In chronic pancreatitis, nutrition therapy aims to prevent malnutrition, reduce pancreatic stimulation, and improve nutrient absorption.\n\n• NPO (Nil Per Os / Nothing by Mouth): During acute episodes or severe flare-ups of pancreatitis, bowel rest (keeping the patient NPO) is primary to minimize pancreatic stimulation and reduce auto-digestion of the organ.\n• Enzyme Replacement: For patients with exocrine pancreatic insufficiency (EPI) or chronic pancreatitis, taking Pancreatic Enzyme Replacement Therapy (PERT) with meals is necessary to facilitate proper macronutrient digestion and nutrient absorption.\n• Low Fat: Fat is a potent stimulant of pancreatic enzyme secretion. Restricting fat intake (often between 30–50 grams per day) and spreading it across small, frequent meals helps alleviate abdominal pain and prevent fatty stools (steatorrhea).\n• Avoidance of Stimulants: Patients must strictly avoid pancreatic irritants and stimulants, most notably alcohol and nicotine, as they directly exacerbate inflammation and disease progression.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: High kcal, low protein, high carbohydrate, high fiber, High kcal, high protein, low fat, Liquid diet, iron supplementation, low fiber"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:dietary recommendation for biliary dyskinesia caused by increased contraction of the gallbladder::::low fat', 'dietary recommendation for biliary dyskinesia caused by increased contraction of the gallbladder:', 'Low fat', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Biliary dyskinesia is a functional disorder of the gallbladder characterized by abnormal gallbladder motility, resulting in impaired bile flow and biliary-type pain in the absence of gallstones or structural abnormalities. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Low fat\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Biliary dyskinesia is a functional disorder of the gallbladder characterized by abnormal gallbladder motility, resulting in impaired bile flow and biliary-type pain in the absence of gallstones or structural abnormalities. \n\nIn some patients, symptoms are associated with exaggerated gallbladder contraction, particularly after consuming fatty meals. Dietary fat stimulates the release of cholecystokinin (CCK) from the duodenum. CCK causes the gallbladder to contract and release bile into the small intestine. In patients with biliary dyskinesia, especially those whose symptoms are triggered by gallbladder contraction, high-fat meals can exacerbate pain and biliary colic. \n\nTherefore, a low-fat diet is commonly recommended to: \n• Reduce gallbladder stimulation \n• Minimize biliary pain after meals \n• Improve tolerance to oral intake \n• Decrease the frequency of symptomatic episodes \n\nAlthough a low-fat diet helps manage symptoms, it should still provide adequate essential fatty acids and fat-soluble vitamins, and prolonged severe fat restriction is generally not recommended unless clinically indicated.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: High fat, Low protein, High protein"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:dietary recommendation for biliary dyskinesia caused by increased contraction of the gallbladder::::', 'dietary recommendation for biliary dyskinesia caused by increased contraction of the gallbladder:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Biliary dyskinesia is a functional disorder of the gallbladder characterized by abnormal gallbladder motility, resulting in impaired bile flow and biliary-type pain in the absence of gallstones or structural abnormalities. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Low fat\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Biliary dyskinesia is a functional disorder of the gallbladder characterized by abnormal gallbladder motility, resulting in impaired bile flow and biliary-type pain in the absence of gallstones or structural abnormalities. \n\nIn some patients, symptoms are associated with exaggerated gallbladder contraction, particularly after consuming fatty meals. Dietary fat stimulates the release of cholecystokinin (CCK) from the duodenum. CCK causes the gallbladder to contract and release bile into the small intestine. In patients with biliary dyskinesia, especially those whose symptoms are triggered by gallbladder contraction, high-fat meals can exacerbate pain and biliary colic. \n\nTherefore, a low-fat diet is commonly recommended to: \n• Reduce gallbladder stimulation \n• Minimize biliary pain after meals \n• Improve tolerance to oral intake \n• Decrease the frequency of symptomatic episodes \n\nAlthough a low-fat diet helps manage symptoms, it should still provide adequate essential fatty acids and fat-soluble vitamins, and prolonged severe fat restriction is generally not recommended unless clinically indicated.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: High fat, Low protein, High protein"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a 55-year-old filipino male laboratory technician diagnosed with type 2 diabetes mellitus visited the nutrition clinic for counseling. his anthropometric measurements are as follows: height = 5''7" (170 cm), weight = 75 kg, and waist circumference = 36 inches. his laboratory results revealed the following: hba1c = 9%; total cholesterol = 250 mg/dl; ldl cholesterol = 300 mg/dl; and uric acid = 9 mg/dl. he also reported joint pain, lower back pain, and swelling of the joints. based on the who bmi classification, what is the patient''s nutritional status?:::overweight', 'a 55-year-old filipino male laboratory technician diagnosed with type 2 diabetes mellitus visited the nutrition clinic for counseling. his anthropometric measurements are as follows: height = 5''7" (170 cm), weight = 75 kg, and waist circumference = 36 inches. his laboratory results revealed the following: hba1c = 9%; total cholesterol = 250 mg/dl; ldl cholesterol = 300 mg/dl; and uric acid = 9 mg/dl. he also reported joint pain, lower back pain, and swelling of the joints. based on the who bmi classification, what is the patient''s nutritional status?', 'Overweight', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Body Mass Index (BMI) is a simple anthropometric index used to classify the nutritional status of adults. It is calculated using the formula: BMI = Weight (kg) ÷ Height (m)² ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Overweight\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Body Mass Index (BMI) is a simple anthropometric index used to classify the nutritional status of adults. It is calculated using the formula: BMI = Weight (kg) ÷ Height (m)² \n\nFor this patient: \n• Height = 170 cm = 1.70 m \n• Weight = 75 kg \nBMI = 75 ÷ (1.70)² = 75 ÷ 2.89 = 25.95 kg/m² \n\nUsing the World Health Organization (WHO) international BMI classification, a BMI of 25.95 kg/m² falls under Overweight (25.0–29.9 kg/m²).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Normal, Obese, Morbidly obese"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:dietary management for typhoid fever: i. high-calorie, high-protein, high-fluid, and low-fiber diet ii. high-calorie, high-protein diet with sodium and fluid restriction, plus vitamin and mineral supplementation iii. high-calorie, high-protein, fat-restricted diet:::i only', 'dietary management for typhoid fever: i. high-calorie, high-protein, high-fluid, and low-fiber diet ii. high-calorie, high-protein diet with sodium and fluid restriction, plus vitamin and mineral supplementation iii. high-calorie, high-protein, fat-restricted diet', 'I only', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Typhoid fever is a systemic bacterial infection caused by Salmonella enterica serovar Typhi. Patients commonly experience prolonged fever, anorexia, abdominal discomfort, diarrhea or constipation, and generalized weakness, all of which may contribute to poor nutritional intake and increased metabolic demands. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"I only\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Typhoid fever is a systemic bacterial infection caused by Salmonella enterica serovar Typhi. Patients commonly experience prolonged fever, anorexia, abdominal discomfort, diarrhea or constipation, and generalized weakness, all of which may contribute to poor nutritional intake and increased metabolic demands. \n\nRoutine sodium and fluid restriction is not recommended in typhoid fever. Instead, maintaining adequate hydration is an important component of treatment. Fluid restriction would only be indicated in unrelated conditions such as heart failure or severe renal disease. \n\nRoutine fat restriction is also not recommended for uncomplicated typhoid fever. Fat intake may be modified based on individual tolerance, but there is no evidence-based recommendation to prescribe a fat-restricted diet solely because of typhoid fever.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: II only, III only, All of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:dietary management for typhoid fever: i. high-calorie, high-protein, high-fluid, and low-fiber diet ii. high-calorie, high-protein diet with sodium and fluid restriction, plus vitamin and mineral supplementation iii. high-calorie, high-protein, fat-restricted diet:::', 'dietary management for typhoid fever: i. high-calorie, high-protein, high-fluid, and low-fiber diet ii. high-calorie, high-protein diet with sodium and fluid restriction, plus vitamin and mineral supplementation iii. high-calorie, high-protein, fat-restricted diet', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Typhoid fever is a systemic bacterial infection caused by Salmonella enterica serovar Typhi. Patients commonly experience prolonged fever, anorexia, abdominal discomfort, diarrhea or constipation, and generalized weakness, all of which may contribute to poor nutritional intake and increased metabolic demands. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"I only\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Typhoid fever is a systemic bacterial infection caused by Salmonella enterica serovar Typhi. Patients commonly experience prolonged fever, anorexia, abdominal discomfort, diarrhea or constipation, and generalized weakness, all of which may contribute to poor nutritional intake and increased metabolic demands. \n\nRoutine sodium and fluid restriction is not recommended in typhoid fever. Instead, maintaining adequate hydration is an important component of treatment. Fluid restriction would only be indicated in unrelated conditions such as heart failure or severe renal disease. \n\nRoutine fat restriction is also not recommended for uncomplicated typhoid fever. Fat intake may be modified based on individual tolerance, but there is no evidence-based recommendation to prescribe a fat-restricted diet solely because of typhoid fever.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: II only, III only, All of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:aggressive nutrition support is required for burned patients with::::burns exceeding 20 % of the body surface area', 'aggressive nutrition support is required for burned patients with:', 'Burns exceeding 20 % of the body surface area', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Major burn injuries trigger one of the most intense hypermetabolic and hypercatabolic responses known in clinical medicine. This metabolic surge significantly accelerates protein breakdown and energy expenditure. Clinical guidelines (such as ESPEN and ASPEN) establish that burns exceeding 20% of the Total Body Surface Area (TBSA) represent critical, severe trauma that demands immediate, aggressive nutrition support (often via early enteral nutrition) to prevent severe muscle wasting, support wound healing, and minimize infection risks.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Burns exceeding 20 % of the body surface area\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Major burn injuries trigger one of the most intense hypermetabolic and hypercatabolic responses known in clinical medicine. This metabolic surge significantly accelerates protein breakdown and energy expenditure. Clinical guidelines (such as ESPEN and ASPEN) establish that burns exceeding 20% of the Total Body Surface Area (TBSA) represent critical, severe trauma that demands immediate, aggressive nutrition support (often via early enteral nutrition) to prevent severe muscle wasting, support wound healing, and minimize infection risks.\n\n• 10% TBSA: Minor burns involving 10% or less of the body surface area generally do not induce a systemic, high-grade hypermetabolic crisis. Most of these patients can meet their elevated nutritional needs through a high-calorie, high-protein oral diet without requiring aggressive specialized nutritional support interventions (like tube feeding or parenteral nutrition).\n• Diarrhea: While diarrhea is a clinical concern that causes fluid and electrolyte losses, it is a gastrointestinal symptom rather than an automatic primary indicator for aggressive nutrition support. In fact, if a burn patient develops severe diarrhea, enteral tube feeding rates might need to be adjusted or formulas changed rather than aggressively increased.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Burns with 10 % of the body surface area, Diarrhea, All of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:aggressive nutrition support is required for burned patients with::::', 'aggressive nutrition support is required for burned patients with:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Major burn injuries trigger one of the most intense hypermetabolic and hypercatabolic responses known in clinical medicine. This metabolic surge significantly accelerates protein breakdown and energy expenditure. Clinical guidelines (such as ESPEN and ASPEN) establish that burns exceeding 20% of the Total Body Surface Area (TBSA) represent critical, severe trauma that demands immediate, aggressive nutrition support (often via early enteral nutrition) to prevent severe muscle wasting, support wound healing, and minimize infection risks.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Burns exceeding 20 % of the body surface area\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Major burn injuries trigger one of the most intense hypermetabolic and hypercatabolic responses known in clinical medicine. This metabolic surge significantly accelerates protein breakdown and energy expenditure. Clinical guidelines (such as ESPEN and ASPEN) establish that burns exceeding 20% of the Total Body Surface Area (TBSA) represent critical, severe trauma that demands immediate, aggressive nutrition support (often via early enteral nutrition) to prevent severe muscle wasting, support wound healing, and minimize infection risks.\n\n• 10% TBSA: Minor burns involving 10% or less of the body surface area generally do not induce a systemic, high-grade hypermetabolic crisis. Most of these patients can meet their elevated nutritional needs through a high-calorie, high-protein oral diet without requiring aggressive specialized nutritional support interventions (like tube feeding or parenteral nutrition).\n• Diarrhea: While diarrhea is a clinical concern that causes fluid and electrolyte losses, it is a gastrointestinal symptom rather than an automatic primary indicator for aggressive nutrition support. In fact, if a burn patient develops severe diarrhea, enteral tube feeding rates might need to be adjusted or formulas changed rather than aggressively increased.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Burns with 10 % of the body surface area, Diarrhea, All of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:if a recipe costs $1.80 per portion to produce and the target food cost percentage is 30%, what is the selling price using the factor pricing method?:::', 'if a recipe costs $1.80 per portion to produce and the target food cost percentage is 30%, what is the selling price using the factor pricing method?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Markup Factor = 100 ÷ Target Food Cost % = 100 ÷ 30 = 3.333. Selling Price = Raw Food Cost × Markup Factor = $1.80 × 3.333 = $6.00 (Alternatively: $1.80 ÷ 0.30 = $6.00).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"$6.00\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Markup Factor = 100 ÷ Target Food Cost % = 100 ÷ 30 = 3.333. Selling Price = Raw Food Cost × Markup Factor = $1.80 × 3.333 = $6.00 (Alternatively: $1.80 ÷ 0.30 = $6.00).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: $5.40, $7.20, $4.50"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:may decrease folate levels::::aspirin', 'may decrease folate levels:', 'Aspirin', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In the bloodstream, aspirin and its active metabolite (salicylic acid) bind heavily to plasma proteins. This presence competitively displaces folate from its primary transport proteins, leading to an increased clearance and higher urinary excretion of unbound folate.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Aspirin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In the bloodstream, aspirin and its active metabolite (salicylic acid) bind heavily to plasma proteins. This presence competitively displaces folate from its primary transport proteins, leading to an increased clearance and higher urinary excretion of unbound folate.\n\nResearch indicates that aspirin down-regulates Dihydrofolate Reductase (DHFR), a critical enzyme required to convert dietary folate into its biologically active form. Additionally, salicylic acid acts as a low-affinity inhibitor of the Reduced Folate Carrier-1 (RFC-1), which actively transports active folates into tissues and across the blood-brain barrier.\n\nBecause of this dual-action interference—accelerating the excretion of circulating folate while simultaneously hindering its activation and cellular uptake—chronic or high-dose aspirin users face a documented clinical risk of impaired folate status and depletion.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Antacid, Laxative, Warfarin"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb)
