('mc:may decrease folate levels::::', 'may decrease folate levels:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In the bloodstream, aspirin and its active metabolite (salicylic acid) bind heavily to plasma proteins. This presence competitively displaces folate from its primary transport proteins, leading to an increased clearance and higher urinary excretion of unbound folate.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Aspirin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In the bloodstream, aspirin and its active metabolite (salicylic acid) bind heavily to plasma proteins. This presence competitively displaces folate from its primary transport proteins, leading to an increased clearance and higher urinary excretion of unbound folate.\n\nResearch indicates that aspirin down-regulates Dihydrofolate Reductase (DHFR), a critical enzyme required to convert dietary folate into its biologically active form. Additionally, salicylic acid acts as a low-affinity inhibitor of the Reduced Folate Carrier-1 (RFC-1), which actively transports active folates into tissues and across the blood-brain barrier.\n\nBecause of this dual-action interference—accelerating the excretion of circulating folate while simultaneously hindering its activation and cellular uptake—chronic or high-dose aspirin users face a documented clinical risk of impaired folate status and depletion.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Antacid, Laxative, Warfarin"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:nutrition intervention for patient with inborn error of metabolism if there is accumulation of precursor to toxic levels::::restrict source of precursor', 'nutrition intervention for patient with inborn error of metabolism if there is accumulation of precursor to toxic levels:', 'Restrict source of precursor', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Inborn errors of metabolism (IEMs) are inherited genetic disorders caused by deficiencies or dysfunction of enzymes involved in metabolic pathways. When an enzyme is absent or has markedly reduced activity, the substrate (or metabolic precursor) cannot be adequately metabolized, resulting in its accumulation to toxic concentrations while the downstream product may become deficient. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Restrict source of precursor\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Inborn errors of metabolism (IEMs) are inherited genetic disorders caused by deficiencies or dysfunction of enzymes involved in metabolic pathways. When an enzyme is absent or has markedly reduced activity, the substrate (or metabolic precursor) cannot be adequately metabolized, resulting in its accumulation to toxic concentrations while the downstream product may become deficient. \n\nA cornerstone of nutrition management is restricting the dietary source of the accumulating precursor to prevent toxicity while still providing sufficient nutrients for normal growth and development. Examples include: \n• Phenylketonuria (PKU): Restrict phenylalanine \n• Galactosemia: Eliminate galactose and lactose \n• Maple syrup urine disease (MSUD): Restrict branched-chain amino acids \n• Hereditary fructose intolerance: Eliminate fructose, sucrose, and sorbitol \n\nBecause many precursor nutrients are essential, dietary restriction should be carefully individualized and supervised by a metabolic dietitian to prevent deficiency while maintaining metabolic control.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Increase the amount of substrate, Avoid exposure, All of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:nutrition intervention for patient with inborn error of metabolism if there is accumulation of precursor to toxic levels::::', 'nutrition intervention for patient with inborn error of metabolism if there is accumulation of precursor to toxic levels:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Inborn errors of metabolism (IEMs) are inherited genetic disorders caused by deficiencies or dysfunction of enzymes involved in metabolic pathways. When an enzyme is absent or has markedly reduced activity, the substrate (or metabolic precursor) cannot be adequately metabolized, resulting in its accumulation to toxic concentrations while the downstream product may become deficient. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Restrict source of precursor\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Inborn errors of metabolism (IEMs) are inherited genetic disorders caused by deficiencies or dysfunction of enzymes involved in metabolic pathways. When an enzyme is absent or has markedly reduced activity, the substrate (or metabolic precursor) cannot be adequately metabolized, resulting in its accumulation to toxic concentrations while the downstream product may become deficient. \n\nA cornerstone of nutrition management is restricting the dietary source of the accumulating precursor to prevent toxicity while still providing sufficient nutrients for normal growth and development. Examples include: \n• Phenylketonuria (PKU): Restrict phenylalanine \n• Galactosemia: Eliminate galactose and lactose \n• Maple syrup urine disease (MSUD): Restrict branched-chain amino acids \n• Hereditary fructose intolerance: Eliminate fructose, sucrose, and sorbitol \n\nBecause many precursor nutrients are essential, dietary restriction should be carefully individualized and supervised by a metabolic dietitian to prevent deficiency while maintaining metabolic control.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Increase the amount of substrate, Avoid exposure, All of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:compute for the kcal of 850 ml 5% amino acid:::170 kcal', 'compute for the kcal of 850 ml 5% amino acid', '170 kcal', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Step-by-Step Calculation","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"170 kcal\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Step-by-Step Calculation\n\nIn clinical nutrition and parenteral nutrition calculations, a 5% amino acid solution means there are 5 grams of pure amino acid in every 100 mL of fluid. Amino acids provide 4 kcal per gram.\n\n1. Calculate the total grams of amino acid:\n\t• Total grams = 850 mL x 0.05 (5%)\n\t• Total grams = 42.5 grams\n\n2. Calculate the total calories (kcal):\n\t• Total kcal = 42.5 grams x 4 kcal/gram","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 200 kcal, 300 kcal, 250 kcal"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are characteristics of marasmus, except::::minimal problem in brain development', 'the following are characteristics of marasmus, except:', 'Minimal problem in brain development', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Marasmus is a severe form of protein-energy malnutrition (PEM) resulting primarily from chronic deficiency of both energy and protein. It is characterized by marked wasting of adipose tissue and skeletal muscle, leading to severe weight loss and failure to thrive. Typical clinical features include: ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Minimal problem in brain development\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Marasmus is a severe form of protein-energy malnutrition (PEM) resulting primarily from chronic deficiency of both energy and protein. It is characterized by marked wasting of adipose tissue and skeletal muscle, leading to severe weight loss and failure to thrive. Typical clinical features include: \n\n• Severe wasting (\"skin and bones\" appearance) \n• Marked loss of subcutaneous fat \n• Muscle wasting \n• Growth retardation \n• Poor weight gain \n• Irritability or apathy \n• Increased susceptibility to infection\n\nAmong the choices, minimal problem in brain development is the exception because brain development is not minimally affected.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Low disease resistance, Occurs most commonly in children aged 6-18 months, Results in little or no fat under the skin to insulate against cold"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are characteristics of marasmus, except::::', 'the following are characteristics of marasmus, except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Marasmus is a severe form of protein-energy malnutrition (PEM) resulting primarily from chronic deficiency of both energy and protein. It is characterized by marked wasting of adipose tissue and skeletal muscle, leading to severe weight loss and failure to thrive. Typical clinical features include: ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Minimal problem in brain development\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Marasmus is a severe form of protein-energy malnutrition (PEM) resulting primarily from chronic deficiency of both energy and protein. It is characterized by marked wasting of adipose tissue and skeletal muscle, leading to severe weight loss and failure to thrive. Typical clinical features include: \n\n• Severe wasting (\"skin and bones\" appearance) \n• Marked loss of subcutaneous fat \n• Muscle wasting \n• Growth retardation \n• Poor weight gain \n• Irritability or apathy \n• Increased susceptibility to infection\n\nAmong the choices, minimal problem in brain development is the exception because brain development is not minimally affected.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Low disease resistance, Occurs most commonly in children aged 6-18 months, Results in little or no fat under the skin to insulate against cold"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following statement/s is/are true in describing carbohydrates in en?:::all of the above', 'which of the following statement/s is/are true in describing carbohydrates in en?', 'All of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Carbohydrates are the primary source of energy in most enteral nutrition (EN) formulas, typically providing 40–60% of total calories. The type of carbohydrate used depends on the formula''s intended purpose, desired osmolality, digestibility, and patient tolerance. Common carbohydrate sources include: ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Carbohydrates are the primary source of energy in most enteral nutrition (EN) formulas, typically providing 40–60% of total calories. The type of carbohydrate used depends on the formula''s intended purpose, desired osmolality, digestibility, and patient tolerance. Common carbohydrate sources include: \n• Corn syrup solids \n• Maltodextrin \n• Hydrolyzed cornstarch \n• Modified starches \n\nThese ingredients are preferred because they: \n• Are easily digested and absorbed \n• Contribute relatively low osmolality compared with simple sugars \n• Provide a readily available energy source \n• Produce desirable physical properties in the formula \n\nSome oral nutritional supplements (ONS) and oral enteral formulas may also contain sucrose or other simple sugars to improve taste and increase patient acceptance. However, sucrose is generally used less frequently in tube-feeding formulas because complex carbohydrates are usually better tolerated and produce lower osmolality.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Usually corn syrup solids, Hydrolyed formulas from cornstarch or maltodextrin, Sucrose added in formulas meant for oral consumption"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following statement/s is/are true in describing carbohydrates in en?:::', 'which of the following statement/s is/are true in describing carbohydrates in en?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Carbohydrates are the primary source of energy in most enteral nutrition (EN) formulas, typically providing 40–60% of total calories. The type of carbohydrate used depends on the formula''s intended purpose, desired osmolality, digestibility, and patient tolerance. Common carbohydrate sources include: ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Carbohydrates are the primary source of energy in most enteral nutrition (EN) formulas, typically providing 40–60% of total calories. The type of carbohydrate used depends on the formula''s intended purpose, desired osmolality, digestibility, and patient tolerance. Common carbohydrate sources include: \n• Corn syrup solids \n• Maltodextrin \n• Hydrolyzed cornstarch \n• Modified starches \n\nThese ingredients are preferred because they: \n• Are easily digested and absorbed \n• Contribute relatively low osmolality compared with simple sugars \n• Provide a readily available energy source \n• Produce desirable physical properties in the formula \n\nSome oral nutritional supplements (ONS) and oral enteral formulas may also contain sucrose or other simple sugars to improve taste and increase patient acceptance. However, sucrose is generally used less frequently in tube-feeding formulas because complex carbohydrates are usually better tolerated and produce lower osmolality.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Usually corn syrup solids, Hydrolyed formulas from cornstarch or maltodextrin, Sucrose added in formulas meant for oral consumption"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a 55-year-old filipino male laboratory technician diagnosed with type 2 diabetes mellitus visited the nutrition clinic for counseling. his anthropometric measurements are as follows: height = 5''7" (170 cm), weight = 75 kg, and waist circumference = 36 inches. his laboratory results revealed the following: hba1c = 9%; total cholesterol = 250 mg/dl; ldl cholesterol = 300 mg/dl; and uric acid = 9 mg/dl. he also reported joint pain, lower back pain, and swelling of the joints. aside from prescribing a consistent carbohydrate diet, the dietitian advised him to increase his dietary fiber intake. what is the recommended daily fiber intake for this patient?:::35 grams', 'a 55-year-old filipino male laboratory technician diagnosed with type 2 diabetes mellitus visited the nutrition clinic for counseling. his anthropometric measurements are as follows: height = 5''7" (170 cm), weight = 75 kg, and waist circumference = 36 inches. his laboratory results revealed the following: hba1c = 9%; total cholesterol = 250 mg/dl; ldl cholesterol = 300 mg/dl; and uric acid = 9 mg/dl. he also reported joint pain, lower back pain, and swelling of the joints. aside from prescribing a consistent carbohydrate diet, the dietitian advised him to increase his dietary fiber intake. what is the recommended daily fiber intake for this patient?', '35 grams', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dietary fiber is an important component of Medical Nutrition Therapy (MNT) for individuals with Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus. Increasing fiber intake has been shown to improve glycemic control, promote satiety, reduce postprandial blood glucose excursions, improve bowel function, and contribute to lowering total and LDL cholesterol levels. These benefits are particularly important for this patient, who has poor glycemic control (HbA1c = 9%) and dyslipidemia. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"35 grams\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dietary fiber is an important component of Medical Nutrition Therapy (MNT) for individuals with Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus. Increasing fiber intake has been shown to improve glycemic control, promote satiety, reduce postprandial blood glucose excursions, improve bowel function, and contribute to lowering total and LDL cholesterol levels. These benefits are particularly important for this patient, who has poor glycemic control (HbA1c = 9%) and dyslipidemia. \n\nCurrent evidence recommends that adults with diabetes consume at least 14 g of dietary fiber per 1,000 kcal, which generally translates to approximately 25–38 g/day, depending on age, sex, and energy requirements. For adult men, a daily intake of about 30–38 g/day is generally recommended.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 45 grams, 55 grams, 65 grams"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:not included in the list of complications to watch out in en.:::none of the above', 'not included in the list of complications to watch out in en.', 'None of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In clinical dietetics and nutrition support, enteral nutrition (EN) complications are broadly classified into three main categories: mechanical, gastrointestinal (GI), and metabolic.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"None of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In clinical dietetics and nutrition support, enteral nutrition (EN) complications are broadly classified into three main categories: mechanical, gastrointestinal (GI), and metabolic.\n\n• Aspiration, tube displacement, or tube occlusion (Mechanical Complications): Aspiration of formula into the lungs is the most critical and life-threatening complication of EN, potentially leading to aspiration pneumonia. Accidental tube dislodgement/displacement and tube clogging (occlusion) from medication or viscous formulas are highly common mechanical issues that require routine tracking and preventative flushing protocols.\n\n• Diarrhea or constipation (Gastrointestinal Complications): GI intolerance is exceptionally frequent during tube feeding. Diarrhea can stem from high osmolarity formulas, rapid administration rates, bacterial contamination, or medications (like sorbitol). Constipation can occur due to inadequate fluid or fiber delivery, or low patient mobility.\n\n• Dehydration (Metabolic Complications): Dehydration is a recognized metabolic issue that can manifest if a patient is given high-protein or hyperosmolar enteral formulas without adequate free water flushes to compensate for the solute load, or if they are losing excessive fluid through unchecked diarrhea or vomiting.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Aspiration, tube displacement, or tube occlusion (clogging), Diarrhea or constipation, Dehydration"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:not included in the list of complications to watch out in en.:::', 'not included in the list of complications to watch out in en.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In clinical dietetics and nutrition support, enteral nutrition (EN) complications are broadly classified into three main categories: mechanical, gastrointestinal (GI), and metabolic.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"None of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In clinical dietetics and nutrition support, enteral nutrition (EN) complications are broadly classified into three main categories: mechanical, gastrointestinal (GI), and metabolic.\n\n• Aspiration, tube displacement, or tube occlusion (Mechanical Complications): Aspiration of formula into the lungs is the most critical and life-threatening complication of EN, potentially leading to aspiration pneumonia. Accidental tube dislodgement/displacement and tube clogging (occlusion) from medication or viscous formulas are highly common mechanical issues that require routine tracking and preventative flushing protocols.\n\n• Diarrhea or constipation (Gastrointestinal Complications): GI intolerance is exceptionally frequent during tube feeding. Diarrhea can stem from high osmolarity formulas, rapid administration rates, bacterial contamination, or medications (like sorbitol). Constipation can occur due to inadequate fluid or fiber delivery, or low patient mobility.\n\n• Dehydration (Metabolic Complications): Dehydration is a recognized metabolic issue that can manifest if a patient is given high-protein or hyperosmolar enteral formulas without adequate free water flushes to compensate for the solute load, or if they are losing excessive fluid through unchecked diarrhea or vomiting.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Aspiration, tube displacement, or tube occlusion (clogging), Diarrhea or constipation, Dehydration"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following foods are considered high biologic value?:::fish and chicken leg', 'which of the following foods are considered high biologic value?', 'Fish and chicken leg', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"High biological value (HBV) proteins, also known as complete proteins, contain all nine essential amino acids in sufficient amounts to support normal growth, tissue repair, enzyme and hormone synthesis, and maintenance of body tissues. They are also generally highly digestible and efficiently utilized by the body. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Fish and chicken leg\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"High biological value (HBV) proteins, also known as complete proteins, contain all nine essential amino acids in sufficient amounts to support normal growth, tissue repair, enzyme and hormone synthesis, and maintenance of body tissues. They are also generally highly digestible and efficiently utilized by the body. \n\nMost animal-derived foods are considered HBV protein sources, including: \n• Fish \n• Chicken \n• Beef \n• Pork \n• Eggs \n• Milk and dairy products \n\nSince both fish and chicken provide complete, high-quality protein, this is the correct answer.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Tofu and chicken leg, Fish and beans, Chicken and cured ham"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following foods are considered high biologic value?:::', 'which of the following foods are considered high biologic value?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"High biological value (HBV) proteins, also known as complete proteins, contain all nine essential amino acids in sufficient amounts to support normal growth, tissue repair, enzyme and hormone synthesis, and maintenance of body tissues. They are also generally highly digestible and efficiently utilized by the body. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Fish and chicken leg\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"High biological value (HBV) proteins, also known as complete proteins, contain all nine essential amino acids in sufficient amounts to support normal growth, tissue repair, enzyme and hormone synthesis, and maintenance of body tissues. They are also generally highly digestible and efficiently utilized by the body. \n\nMost animal-derived foods are considered HBV protein sources, including: \n• Fish \n• Chicken \n• Beef \n• Pork \n• Eggs \n• Milk and dairy products \n\nSince both fish and chicken provide complete, high-quality protein, this is the correct answer.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Tofu and chicken leg, Fish and beans, Chicken and cured ham"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:for an adult patient with chronic kidney disease (ckd) and an estimated glomerular filtration rate (egfr) of <25 ml/min/1.73 m² who is not receiving dialysis, what is the recommended dietary protein intake?:::0.6 to 0.7 g/kg/day', 'for an adult patient with chronic kidney disease (ckd) and an estimated glomerular filtration rate (egfr) of <25 ml/min/1.73 m² who is not receiving dialysis, what is the recommended dietary protein intake?', '0.6 to 0.7 g/kg/day', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In patients with advanced chronic kidney disease (CKD) who are not on dialysis, dietary protein restriction is recommended to reduce the accumulation of nitrogenous waste products, decrease uremic symptoms, and potentially slow the progression of kidney disease. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"0.6 to 0.7 g/kg/day\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In patients with advanced chronic kidney disease (CKD) who are not on dialysis, dietary protein restriction is recommended to reduce the accumulation of nitrogenous waste products, decrease uremic symptoms, and potentially slow the progression of kidney disease. \n\nThe 2020 KDOQI Clinical Practice Guideline for Nutrition in CKD recommends: \n• 0.55–0.60 g/kg ideal body weight/day for metabolically stable adults with CKD stages 3–5 who are not on dialysis. \n• A slightly higher intake (0.6–0.8 g/kg/day) may be appropriate in selected patients, particularly those with diabetes, older adults, or individuals at risk of protein-energy wasting. \n\nTherefore, among the choices provided, 0.6–0.7 g/kg/day is the best answer.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 0.28 g/kg/day with EAA supplementation, 0 to 1.2 g/kg/day with dialysis, None of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:for an adult patient with chronic kidney disease (ckd) and an estimated glomerular filtration rate (egfr) of <25 ml/min/1.73 m² who is not receiving dialysis, what is the recommended dietary protein intake?:::', 'for an adult patient with chronic kidney disease (ckd) and an estimated glomerular filtration rate (egfr) of <25 ml/min/1.73 m² who is not receiving dialysis, what is the recommended dietary protein intake?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In patients with advanced chronic kidney disease (CKD) who are not on dialysis, dietary protein restriction is recommended to reduce the accumulation of nitrogenous waste products, decrease uremic symptoms, and potentially slow the progression of kidney disease. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"0.6 to 0.7 g/kg/day\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In patients with advanced chronic kidney disease (CKD) who are not on dialysis, dietary protein restriction is recommended to reduce the accumulation of nitrogenous waste products, decrease uremic symptoms, and potentially slow the progression of kidney disease. \n\nThe 2020 KDOQI Clinical Practice Guideline for Nutrition in CKD recommends: \n• 0.55–0.60 g/kg ideal body weight/day for metabolically stable adults with CKD stages 3–5 who are not on dialysis. \n• A slightly higher intake (0.6–0.8 g/kg/day) may be appropriate in selected patients, particularly those with diabetes, older adults, or individuals at risk of protein-energy wasting. \n\nTherefore, among the choices provided, 0.6–0.7 g/kg/day is the best answer.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 0.28 g/kg/day with EAA supplementation, 0 to 1.2 g/kg/day with dialysis, None of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following may suppress the appetite?:::amphetamines', 'which of the following may suppress the appetite?', 'Amphetamines', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Amphetamines are central nervous system (CNS) stimulants that increase the release and inhibit the reuptake of dopamine and norepinephrine in the brain. These neurotransmitters act on the hypothalamus, reducing hunger signals and increasing satiety. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Amphetamines\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Amphetamines are central nervous system (CNS) stimulants that increase the release and inhibit the reuptake of dopamine and norepinephrine in the brain. These neurotransmitters act on the hypothalamus, reducing hunger signals and increasing satiety. \n\nBecause of this mechanism, amphetamines produce appetite suppression, which can lead to: \n• Reduced food intake \n• Weight loss \n• Poor nutritional status with prolonged use \n• Growth impairment in children receiving long-term stimulant therapy","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Phenobarbital, Digitalis, Methotrexate"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following may suppress the appetite?:::', 'which of the following may suppress the appetite?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Amphetamines are central nervous system (CNS) stimulants that increase the release and inhibit the reuptake of dopamine and norepinephrine in the brain. These neurotransmitters act on the hypothalamus, reducing hunger signals and increasing satiety. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Amphetamines\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Amphetamines are central nervous system (CNS) stimulants that increase the release and inhibit the reuptake of dopamine and norepinephrine in the brain. These neurotransmitters act on the hypothalamus, reducing hunger signals and increasing satiety. \n\nBecause of this mechanism, amphetamines produce appetite suppression, which can lead to: \n• Reduced food intake \n• Weight loss \n• Poor nutritional status with prolonged use \n• Growth impairment in children receiving long-term stimulant therapy","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Phenobarbital, Digitalis, Methotrexate"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the protein requirement for a male, 65 years old, who stands 5’5" and has a dry weight of 48 kg? his serum creatinine is 5.0 mg/dl.:::0.28 g/kg/day with eaa supplementation', 'what is the protein requirement for a male, 65 years old, who stands 5’5" and has a dry weight of 48 kg? his serum creatinine is 5.0 mg/dl.', '0.28 g/kg/day with EAA supplementation', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Step-by-Step Rationale","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"0.28 g/kg/day with EAA supplementation\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Step-by-Step Rationale\n\n1. Assess Kidney Function (GFR):\nUsing the standard Cockcroft-Gault equation to estimate the Glomerular Filtration Rate (GFR) based on the client''s metrics:\n\nGFR = [(140 - Age) x Weight (kg)] / [72 x Serum Creatinine]\nGFR = [(140 - 65) x 48] / [72 x 5.0] = [75 x 48] / 360 = 10.0 mL/min\n\nA GFR of 10.0 mL/min confirms the patient is in Stage 5 Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD) / End-Stage Renal Disease (ESRD) and is currently in the pre-dialysis phase.\n\n2. Determine Protein Requirements:\nFor a patient with advanced renal failure who is not yet on active dialysis, minimizing nitrogenous waste build-up while preventing lean muscle wasting is critical. Clinical guidelines specify a Very Low-Protein Diet (VLPD) targeting 0.28 g/kg/day, which must be supplemented with Essential Amino Acids (EAA) or ketoacid analogs to fulfill basic physiological needs.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 0.6 to 0.7 g/kg/day, 0 to 1.2 g/kg/day with dialysis, None of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:all of the following are true of the basal energy expenditure (bee) except:::loss of muscle in old age which may lead to decreased bee is inevitable', 'all of the following are true of the basal energy expenditure (bee) except', 'Loss of muscle in old age which may lead to decreased BEE is inevitable', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The decrease in muscle mass (sarcopenia) and the resulting drop in BEE during old age is not inevitable. Regular resistance training, proper physical activity, and a targeted protein intake can preserve, build, or slow down the loss of lean muscle mass as a person ages, maintaining a higher baseline metabolism.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Loss of muscle in old age which may lead to decreased BEE is inevitable\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The decrease in muscle mass (sarcopenia) and the resulting drop in BEE during old age is not inevitable. Regular resistance training, proper physical activity, and a targeted protein intake can preserve, build, or slow down the loss of lean muscle mass as a person ages, maintaining a higher baseline metabolism.\n\n• It is highest in the first years of life: Size-adjusted BEE peaks sharply during infancy (up to around 15 months of age), running roughly 50 percent higher than adult metabolic values due to rapid growth and development.\n• Men have higher BEE than women: Lean body mass is a principal driver of metabolism. Because men generally possess a higher percentage of lean muscle mass and less body fat than women of an equal age and surface area, their baseline BEE is naturally higher.\n• It is measured in the fasted state: True clinical measurement of BEE requires the subject to be at absolute rest in a comfortable room temperature after a 12-hour fast to eliminate the thermal effects of digestion.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: It is highest in the first years of life, It is measured in the fasted state, Men have higher BEE than women"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the protein requirement for a male, 65 years old, who stands 5’5" and has a dry weight of 48 kg? his serum creatinine is 5.0 mg/dl.:::', 'what is the protein requirement for a male, 65 years old, who stands 5’5" and has a dry weight of 48 kg? his serum creatinine is 5.0 mg/dl.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Step-by-Step Rationale","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"0.28 g/kg/day with EAA supplementation\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Step-by-Step Rationale\n\n1. Assess Kidney Function (GFR):\nUsing the standard Cockcroft-Gault equation to estimate the Glomerular Filtration Rate (GFR) based on the client''s metrics:\n\nGFR = [(140 - Age) x Weight (kg)] / [72 x Serum Creatinine]\nGFR = [(140 - 65) x 48] / [72 x 5.0] = [75 x 48] / 360 = 10.0 mL/min\n\nA GFR of 10.0 mL/min confirms the patient is in Stage 5 Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD) / End-Stage Renal Disease (ESRD) and is currently in the pre-dialysis phase.\n\n2. Determine Protein Requirements:\nFor a patient with advanced renal failure who is not yet on active dialysis, minimizing nitrogenous waste build-up while preventing lean muscle wasting is critical. Clinical guidelines specify a Very Low-Protein Diet (VLPD) targeting 0.28 g/kg/day, which must be supplemented with Essential Amino Acids (EAA) or ketoacid analogs to fulfill basic physiological needs.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 0.6 to 0.7 g/kg/day, 0 to 1.2 g/kg/day with dialysis, None of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following statement summarizes the ncp?:::ncp is intended to establish a standardized process and provide consistency', 'which of the following statement summarizes the ncp?', 'NCP is intended to establish a standardized process and provide consistency', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The Nutrition Care Process (NCP) is a systematic, evidence-based framework developed by the Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics to guide registered nutritionist-dietitians in delivering high-quality, individualized nutrition care. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"NCP is intended to establish a standardized process and provide consistency\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The Nutrition Care Process (NCP) is a systematic, evidence-based framework developed by the Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics to guide registered nutritionist-dietitians in delivering high-quality, individualized nutrition care. \n\nThe primary purpose of the NCP is to: \n• Establish a standardized approach to nutrition care \n• Improve consistency among practitioners \n• Enhance communication among healthcare professionals \n• Promote evidence-based decision-making \n• Improve patient outcomes","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: NCP is a systematic method intended to standardize nutrition care for clients, NCP sequence is linear with a definitive start and stop not requiring reassessment, NCP consists of five steps, is non-linear and is considered continual and ongoing"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following statement summarizes the ncp?:::', 'which of the following statement summarizes the ncp?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The Nutrition Care Process (NCP) is a systematic, evidence-based framework developed by the Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics to guide registered nutritionist-dietitians in delivering high-quality, individualized nutrition care. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"NCP is intended to establish a standardized process and provide consistency\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The Nutrition Care Process (NCP) is a systematic, evidence-based framework developed by the Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics to guide registered nutritionist-dietitians in delivering high-quality, individualized nutrition care. \n\nThe primary purpose of the NCP is to: \n• Establish a standardized approach to nutrition care \n• Improve consistency among practitioners \n• Enhance communication among healthcare professionals \n• Promote evidence-based decision-making \n• Improve patient outcomes","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: NCP is a systematic method intended to standardize nutrition care for clients, NCP sequence is linear with a definitive start and stop not requiring reassessment, NCP consists of five steps, is non-linear and is considered continual and ongoing"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:water losses of of total body weight can be fatal.:::9-12%', 'water losses of of total body weight can be fatal.', '9-12%', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Water is the largest component of the human body, accounting for approximately 50–60% of body weight in adults, although this varies with age, sex, and body composition. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"9-12%\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Water is the largest component of the human body, accounting for approximately 50–60% of body weight in adults, although this varies with age, sex, and body composition. \n\nMaintaining fluid balance is essential for normal physiologic functions, including: \n• Regulation of body temperature \n• Transport of nutrients and waste products \n• Maintenance of blood volume and blood pressure \n• Cellular metabolism \n• Electrolyte balance \n\nThe severity of dehydration is often expressed as the percentage of body weight lost due to water deficit. Therefore, water losses of approximately 9–12% of body weight can become life-threatening and may be fatal if not rapidly corrected.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 2-3%, 3%, 5%"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a 55-year-old filipino male laboratory technician diagnosed with type 2 diabetes mellitus visited the nutrition clinic for counseling. his anthropometric measurements are as follows: height = 5''7" (170 cm), weight = 75 kg, and waist circumference = 36 inches. his laboratory results revealed the following: hba1c = 9%; total cholesterol = 250 mg/dl; ldl cholesterol = 300 mg/dl; and uric acid = 9 mg/dl. he also reported joint pain, lower back pain, and swelling of the joints. the patient''s hba1c result is indicative of glucose control.:::poor', 'a 55-year-old filipino male laboratory technician diagnosed with type 2 diabetes mellitus visited the nutrition clinic for counseling. his anthropometric measurements are as follows: height = 5''7" (170 cm), weight = 75 kg, and waist circumference = 36 inches. his laboratory results revealed the following: hba1c = 9%; total cholesterol = 250 mg/dl; ldl cholesterol = 300 mg/dl; and uric acid = 9 mg/dl. he also reported joint pain, lower back pain, and swelling of the joints. the patient''s hba1c result is indicative of glucose control.', 'Poor', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Hemoglobin A1c (HbA1c), also known as glycated hemoglobin, reflects the average blood glucose concentration over the previous 2–3 months, corresponding to the lifespan of red blood cells. It is one of the most important laboratory parameters used to assess long-term glycemic control in patients with diabetes mellitus. An HbA1c of 9% indicates poor glycemic control, suggesting that blood glucose levels have remained persistently elevated over the past several months. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Poor\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Hemoglobin A1c (HbA1c), also known as glycated hemoglobin, reflects the average blood glucose concentration over the previous 2–3 months, corresponding to the lifespan of red blood cells. It is one of the most important laboratory parameters used to assess long-term glycemic control in patients with diabetes mellitus. An HbA1c of 9% indicates poor glycemic control, suggesting that blood glucose levels have remained persistently elevated over the past several months. \n\nFor most nonpregnant adults with diabetes, the American Diabetes Association (ADA) recommends an HbA1c target of <7%, although individualized goals may be appropriate depending on age, comorbidities, risk of hypoglycemia, and life expectancy.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Good, Fair, Moderate"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which is not true about enzymes? i. chemical substances from the endocrine glands that can stimulate reactions in the body ii. primarily protein catalysts of biochemical reactions (with some rna molecules acting as catalytic ribozymes) iii. facilitate the making and breaking of chemical bonds during biochemical reactions iv. identified by the place they come from and the compounds they work on:::i only', 'which is not true about enzymes? i. chemical substances from the endocrine glands that can stimulate reactions in the body ii. primarily protein catalysts of biochemical reactions (with some rna molecules acting as catalytic ribozymes) iii. facilitate the making and breaking of chemical bonds during biochemical reactions iv. identified by the place they come from and the compounds they work on', 'I only', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Enzymes are biological catalysts that accelerate biochemical reactions by lowering the activation energy required for a reaction to occur. They are essential for virtually all metabolic processes, including digestion, energy production, DNA synthesis, and cellular repair. Most enzymes are proteins, although a small number of RNA molecules called ribozymes also possess catalytic activity. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"I only\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Enzymes are biological catalysts that accelerate biochemical reactions by lowering the activation energy required for a reaction to occur. They are essential for virtually all metabolic processes, including digestion, energy production, DNA synthesis, and cellular repair. Most enzymes are proteins, although a small number of RNA molecules called ribozymes also possess catalytic activity. \n\nStatement I is incorrect because it actually describes hormones, not enzymes. \n\nHormones are: \n• Chemical messengers \n• Secreted by endocrine glands \n• Transported through the bloodstream \n• Responsible for regulating physiologic processes \n\nIn contrast, enzymes: \n• Catalyze chemical reactions \n• Are not secreted primarily for signaling \n• Are generally specific to particular substrates \n• Remain unchanged after the reaction","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: II only, III only, None of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which is not true about enzymes? i. chemical substances from the endocrine glands that can stimulate reactions in the body ii. primarily protein catalysts of biochemical reactions (with some rna molecules acting as catalytic ribozymes) iii. facilitate the making and breaking of chemical bonds during biochemical reactions iv. identified by the place they come from and the compounds they work on:::', 'which is not true about enzymes? i. chemical substances from the endocrine glands that can stimulate reactions in the body ii. primarily protein catalysts of biochemical reactions (with some rna molecules acting as catalytic ribozymes) iii. facilitate the making and breaking of chemical bonds during biochemical reactions iv. identified by the place they come from and the compounds they work on', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Enzymes are biological catalysts that accelerate biochemical reactions by lowering the activation energy required for a reaction to occur. They are essential for virtually all metabolic processes, including digestion, energy production, DNA synthesis, and cellular repair. Most enzymes are proteins, although a small number of RNA molecules called ribozymes also possess catalytic activity. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"I only\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Enzymes are biological catalysts that accelerate biochemical reactions by lowering the activation energy required for a reaction to occur. They are essential for virtually all metabolic processes, including digestion, energy production, DNA synthesis, and cellular repair. Most enzymes are proteins, although a small number of RNA molecules called ribozymes also possess catalytic activity. \n\nStatement I is incorrect because it actually describes hormones, not enzymes. \n\nHormones are: \n• Chemical messengers \n• Secreted by endocrine glands \n• Transported through the bloodstream \n• Responsible for regulating physiologic processes \n\nIn contrast, enzymes: \n• Catalyze chemical reactions \n• Are not secreted primarily for signaling \n• Are generally specific to particular substrates \n• Remain unchanged after the reaction","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: II only, III only, None of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:easy to digest snack food – they are rich in cho but generally low in protein, fiber, and many other nutrients found in a well-balanced diet.:::energy bars', 'easy to digest snack food – they are rich in cho but generally low in protein, fiber, and many other nutrients found in a well-balanced diet.', 'Energy bars', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In sports nutrition and dietetics review frameworks, conventional energy bars are primarily designed to deliver quick, easily accessible fuel to working muscles. They are heavily packed with simple and refined carbohydrates (CHO) to provide a rapid blood glucose spike before or during endurance performance.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Energy bars\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In sports nutrition and dietetics review frameworks, conventional energy bars are primarily designed to deliver quick, easily accessible fuel to working muscles. They are heavily packed with simple and refined carbohydrates (CHO) to provide a rapid blood glucose spike before or during endurance performance.\n\nUnlike meal replacements or protein bars, traditional energy bars are kept purposefully low in protein, dietary fiber, and healthy fats. Fiber and protein naturally slow down gastric emptying and delay digestion. By keeping these nutrients minimal, the bar functions as an easy-to-digest snack that leaves the stomach rapidly, providing fast energy without causing heavy gastrointestinal distress, cramping, or bloating during physical exertion.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Protein shakes, Whey protein, High-quality protein"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:easy to digest snack food – they are rich in cho but generally low in protein, fiber, and many other nutrients found in a well-balanced diet.:::', 'easy to digest snack food – they are rich in cho but generally low in protein, fiber, and many other nutrients found in a well-balanced diet.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In sports nutrition and dietetics review frameworks, conventional energy bars are primarily designed to deliver quick, easily accessible fuel to working muscles. They are heavily packed with simple and refined carbohydrates (CHO) to provide a rapid blood glucose spike before or during endurance performance.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Energy bars\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In sports nutrition and dietetics review frameworks, conventional energy bars are primarily designed to deliver quick, easily accessible fuel to working muscles. They are heavily packed with simple and refined carbohydrates (CHO) to provide a rapid blood glucose spike before or during endurance performance.\n\nUnlike meal replacements or protein bars, traditional energy bars are kept purposefully low in protein, dietary fiber, and healthy fats. Fiber and protein naturally slow down gastric emptying and delay digestion. By keeping these nutrients minimal, the bar functions as an easy-to-digest snack that leaves the stomach rapidly, providing fast energy without causing heavy gastrointestinal distress, cramping, or bloating during physical exertion.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Protein shakes, Whey protein, High-quality protein"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is not a recommended dietary practice for marathon or other endurance athletes?:::consume a high-protein, low-carbohydrate diet.', 'which of the following is not a recommended dietary practice for marathon or other endurance athletes?', 'Consume a high-protein, low-carbohydrate diet.', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Endurance events such as marathons rely heavily on carbohydrates as the primary fuel source, particularly during moderate- to high-intensity exercise. Adequate carbohydrate intake helps maintain blood glucose levels, replenish muscle glycogen stores, delay fatigue, and improve endurance performance. Consequently, dietary strategies that severely restrict carbohydrates are generally not recommended for marathon runners. Although protein is essential for muscle repair, recovery, and adaptation to training, consuming excessive protein at the expense of carbohydrates may impair glycogen storage and reduce exercise performance. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Consume a high-protein, low-carbohydrate diet.\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Endurance events such as marathons rely heavily on carbohydrates as the primary fuel source, particularly during moderate- to high-intensity exercise. Adequate carbohydrate intake helps maintain blood glucose levels, replenish muscle glycogen stores, delay fatigue, and improve endurance performance. Consequently, dietary strategies that severely restrict carbohydrates are generally not recommended for marathon runners. Although protein is essential for muscle repair, recovery, and adaptation to training, consuming excessive protein at the expense of carbohydrates may impair glycogen storage and reduce exercise performance. \n\nCurrent sports nutrition guidelines recommend that endurance athletes consume sufficient carbohydrates based on their training load, moderate amounts of protein to support recovery, and healthy fats to meet energy needs and maintain overall health. For the NDLE, remember that nutrition recommendations for endurance athletes emphasize adequate carbohydrate availability, not high-protein or carbohydrate-restricted diets. While individualized nutrition plans are increasingly common, carbohydrates remain the cornerstone of endurance performance.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: High-protein diets with minimal carbohydrate intake are not recommended for endurance athletes., Follow a balanced diet consisting of approximately 15–20% protein, 30% fat, and 50–55% carbohydrates., Provide adequate carbohydrates to fuel training and long-distance running days."}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is not a recommended dietary practice for marathon or other endurance athletes?:::', 'which of the following is not a recommended dietary practice for marathon or other endurance athletes?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Endurance events such as marathons rely heavily on carbohydrates as the primary fuel source, particularly during moderate- to high-intensity exercise. Adequate carbohydrate intake helps maintain blood glucose levels, replenish muscle glycogen stores, delay fatigue, and improve endurance performance. Consequently, dietary strategies that severely restrict carbohydrates are generally not recommended for marathon runners. Although protein is essential for muscle repair, recovery, and adaptation to training, consuming excessive protein at the expense of carbohydrates may impair glycogen storage and reduce exercise performance. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Consume a high-protein, low-carbohydrate diet.\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Endurance events such as marathons rely heavily on carbohydrates as the primary fuel source, particularly during moderate- to high-intensity exercise. Adequate carbohydrate intake helps maintain blood glucose levels, replenish muscle glycogen stores, delay fatigue, and improve endurance performance. Consequently, dietary strategies that severely restrict carbohydrates are generally not recommended for marathon runners. Although protein is essential for muscle repair, recovery, and adaptation to training, consuming excessive protein at the expense of carbohydrates may impair glycogen storage and reduce exercise performance. \n\nCurrent sports nutrition guidelines recommend that endurance athletes consume sufficient carbohydrates based on their training load, moderate amounts of protein to support recovery, and healthy fats to meet energy needs and maintain overall health. For the NDLE, remember that nutrition recommendations for endurance athletes emphasize adequate carbohydrate availability, not high-protein or carbohydrate-restricted diets. While individualized nutrition plans are increasingly common, carbohydrates remain the cornerstone of endurance performance.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: High-protein diets with minimal carbohydrate intake are not recommended for endurance athletes., Follow a balanced diet consisting of approximately 15–20% protein, 30% fat, and 50–55% carbohydrates., Provide adequate carbohydrates to fuel training and long-distance running days."}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is true on the effects of certain nutrients on drug efficacy?:::all of the above', 'which of the following is true on the effects of certain nutrients on drug efficacy?', 'All of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Food and nutrients can significantly influence the absorption, distribution, metabolism, and excretion (ADME) of medications, thereby affecting their efficacy and safety. These interactions may enhance or reduce the therapeutic effects of drugs and, in some cases, increase the risk of adverse reactions. Several mechanisms explain nutrient–drug interactions: ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Food and nutrients can significantly influence the absorption, distribution, metabolism, and excretion (ADME) of medications, thereby affecting their efficacy and safety. These interactions may enhance or reduce the therapeutic effects of drugs and, in some cases, increase the risk of adverse reactions. Several mechanisms explain nutrient–drug interactions: \n\n• Alteration of gastric acidity. Certain foods and nutrients can modify gastric pH, influencing the dissolution and absorption of orally administered medications. Drugs that require an acidic environment, for example, may be less effectively absorbed when gastric pH is increased. \n• Changes in urine pH. Diets rich in certain foods can acidify or alkalinize the urine, affecting the renal excretion of weak acids and weak bases. Consequently, the duration and concentration of some drugs in the body may change. \n• Competition for absorption or transport sites. Some nutrients compete with drugs for intestinal transport mechanisms or form insoluble complexes that reduce drug absorption. A classic example is calcium binding with tetracycline or fluoroquinolone antibiotics, significantly decreasing their absorption. \n\nFor the NDLE, nutrient–drug interactions are commonly tested because they directly influence nutrition care planning, patient counseling, and medication administration. Nutritionist-dietitians should recognize common interactions and provide appropriate dietary advice to optimize therapeutic outcomes.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Nutrients from food that change stomach acidity causes alteration in orally administered drugs, Nutrients may alter urine pH which leads to drug excretion, Nutrients may compete with the absorptive sites"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is true on the effects of certain nutrients on drug efficacy?:::', 'which of the following is true on the effects of certain nutrients on drug efficacy?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Food and nutrients can significantly influence the absorption, distribution, metabolism, and excretion (ADME) of medications, thereby affecting their efficacy and safety. These interactions may enhance or reduce the therapeutic effects of drugs and, in some cases, increase the risk of adverse reactions. Several mechanisms explain nutrient–drug interactions: ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Food and nutrients can significantly influence the absorption, distribution, metabolism, and excretion (ADME) of medications, thereby affecting their efficacy and safety. These interactions may enhance or reduce the therapeutic effects of drugs and, in some cases, increase the risk of adverse reactions. Several mechanisms explain nutrient–drug interactions: \n\n• Alteration of gastric acidity. Certain foods and nutrients can modify gastric pH, influencing the dissolution and absorption of orally administered medications. Drugs that require an acidic environment, for example, may be less effectively absorbed when gastric pH is increased. \n• Changes in urine pH. Diets rich in certain foods can acidify or alkalinize the urine, affecting the renal excretion of weak acids and weak bases. Consequently, the duration and concentration of some drugs in the body may change. \n• Competition for absorption or transport sites. Some nutrients compete with drugs for intestinal transport mechanisms or form insoluble complexes that reduce drug absorption. A classic example is calcium binding with tetracycline or fluoroquinolone antibiotics, significantly decreasing their absorption. \n\nFor the NDLE, nutrient–drug interactions are commonly tested because they directly influence nutrition care planning, patient counseling, and medication administration. Nutritionist-dietitians should recognize common interactions and provide appropriate dietary advice to optimize therapeutic outcomes.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Nutrients from food that change stomach acidity causes alteration in orally administered drugs, Nutrients may alter urine pH which leads to drug excretion, Nutrients may compete with the absorptive sites"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which noodle is traditionally used in lomi?:::', 'which noodle is traditionally used in lomi?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Miki is the thick fresh egg noodle commonly associated with lomi.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Miki\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Miki is the thick fresh egg noodle commonly associated with lomi.\n\n• Sotanghon is a glass or cellophane noodle made from mungbean.\n• Bihon is a thin rice vermicelli noodle.\n• Miswa is a thin wheat noodle and is not the traditional noodle identified for lomi.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Miswa, Sotanghon, Bihon"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:all of the following are true of the basal energy expenditure (bee) except:::', 'all of the following are true of the basal energy expenditure (bee) except', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The decrease in muscle mass (sarcopenia) and the resulting drop in BEE during old age is not inevitable. Regular resistance training, proper physical activity, and a targeted protein intake can preserve, build, or slow down the loss of lean muscle mass as a person ages, maintaining a higher baseline metabolism.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Loss of muscle in old age which may lead to decreased BEE is inevitable\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The decrease in muscle mass (sarcopenia) and the resulting drop in BEE during old age is not inevitable. Regular resistance training, proper physical activity, and a targeted protein intake can preserve, build, or slow down the loss of lean muscle mass as a person ages, maintaining a higher baseline metabolism.\n\n• It is highest in the first years of life: Size-adjusted BEE peaks sharply during infancy (up to around 15 months of age), running roughly 50 percent higher than adult metabolic values due to rapid growth and development.\n• Men have higher BEE than women: Lean body mass is a principal driver of metabolism. Because men generally possess a higher percentage of lean muscle mass and less body fat than women of an equal age and surface area, their baseline BEE is naturally higher.\n• It is measured in the fasted state: True clinical measurement of BEE requires the subject to be at absolute rest in a comfortable room temperature after a 12-hour fast to eliminate the thermal effects of digestion.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: It is highest in the first years of life, It is measured in the fasted state, Men have higher BEE than women"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:all of the following are scanning laboratory techniques used to measure body composition except::::bioelectrical impedance analysis (bia)', 'all of the following are scanning laboratory techniques used to measure body composition except:', 'Bioelectrical Impedance Analysis (BIA)', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Several imaging or scanning techniques are available to assess body composition by quantifying fat mass, lean body mass, bone mineral content, and, in some cases, regional fat distribution. Computed Tomography (CT), Dual-Energy X-ray Absorptiometry (DXA), and Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) are considered scanning or imaging techniques because they generate anatomical images that can accurately distinguish between different body tissues. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Bioelectrical Impedance Analysis (BIA)\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Several imaging or scanning techniques are available to assess body composition by quantifying fat mass, lean body mass, bone mineral content, and, in some cases, regional fat distribution. Computed Tomography (CT), Dual-Energy X-ray Absorptiometry (DXA), and Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) are considered scanning or imaging techniques because they generate anatomical images that can accurately distinguish between different body tissues. \n\nIn contrast, Bioelectrical Impedance Analysis (BIA) is not a scanning technique. Instead, it estimates body composition by passing a low-level electrical current through the body and measuring electrical impedance. Since lean tissue contains more water and electrolytes than fat tissue, it conducts electricity more readily.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Computed Tomography (CT), Dual-Energy X-ray Absorptiometry (DXA), Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI)"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:all of the following are scanning laboratory techniques used to measure body composition except::::', 'all of the following are scanning laboratory techniques used to measure body composition except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Several imaging or scanning techniques are available to assess body composition by quantifying fat mass, lean body mass, bone mineral content, and, in some cases, regional fat distribution. Computed Tomography (CT), Dual-Energy X-ray Absorptiometry (DXA), and Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) are considered scanning or imaging techniques because they generate anatomical images that can accurately distinguish between different body tissues. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Bioelectrical Impedance Analysis (BIA)\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Several imaging or scanning techniques are available to assess body composition by quantifying fat mass, lean body mass, bone mineral content, and, in some cases, regional fat distribution. Computed Tomography (CT), Dual-Energy X-ray Absorptiometry (DXA), and Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) are considered scanning or imaging techniques because they generate anatomical images that can accurately distinguish between different body tissues. \n\nIn contrast, Bioelectrical Impedance Analysis (BIA) is not a scanning technique. Instead, it estimates body composition by passing a low-level electrical current through the body and measuring electrical impedance. Since lean tissue contains more water and electrolytes than fat tissue, it conducts electricity more readily.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Computed Tomography (CT), Dual-Energy X-ray Absorptiometry (DXA), Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI)"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the immediate goal of dietary management in acute diarrhea is::::prevent dehydration', 'the immediate goal of dietary management in acute diarrhea is:', 'prevent dehydration', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In acute diarrhea, the rapid and massive loss of water and critical electrolytes (like sodium, potassium, and bicarbonate) through the stool pose an immediate risk of hypovolemic shock and cardiovascular collapse, particularly in vulnerable populations.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"prevent dehydration\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In acute diarrhea, the rapid and massive loss of water and critical electrolytes (like sodium, potassium, and bicarbonate) through the stool pose an immediate risk of hypovolemic shock and cardiovascular collapse, particularly in vulnerable populations.\n\nWhile preventing nutritional deficiencies and restoring long-term nutritional status are essential milestones during the subsequent convalescent phase, they cannot be addressed if the patient is acutely unstable. Therefore, the absolute primary, immediate goal of clinical dietary management is maintaining fluid volume and preventing or correcting dehydration through the immediate administration of oral rehydration solutions (ORS).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: restore nutritional status, prevent deficiency, withhold food"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the immediate goal of dietary management in acute diarrhea is::::', 'the immediate goal of dietary management in acute diarrhea is:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In acute diarrhea, the rapid and massive loss of water and critical electrolytes (like sodium, potassium, and bicarbonate) through the stool pose an immediate risk of hypovolemic shock and cardiovascular collapse, particularly in vulnerable populations.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"prevent dehydration\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In acute diarrhea, the rapid and massive loss of water and critical electrolytes (like sodium, potassium, and bicarbonate) through the stool pose an immediate risk of hypovolemic shock and cardiovascular collapse, particularly in vulnerable populations.\n\nWhile preventing nutritional deficiencies and restoring long-term nutritional status are essential milestones during the subsequent convalescent phase, they cannot be addressed if the patient is acutely unstable. Therefore, the absolute primary, immediate goal of clinical dietary management is maintaining fluid volume and preventing or correcting dehydration through the immediate administration of oral rehydration solutions (ORS).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: restore nutritional status, prevent deficiency, withhold food"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a 55-year-old filipino male laboratory technician diagnosed with type 2 diabetes mellitus visited the nutrition clinic for counseling. his anthropometric measurements are as follows: height = 5''7" (170 cm), weight = 75 kg, and waist circumference = 36 inches. his laboratory results revealed the following: hba1c = 9%; total cholesterol = 250 mg/dl; ldl cholesterol = 300 mg/dl; and uric acid = 9 mg/dl. he also reported joint pain, lower back pain, and swelling of the joints. based on the who bmi classification, what is the patient''s nutritional status?:::', 'a 55-year-old filipino male laboratory technician diagnosed with type 2 diabetes mellitus visited the nutrition clinic for counseling. his anthropometric measurements are as follows: height = 5''7" (170 cm), weight = 75 kg, and waist circumference = 36 inches. his laboratory results revealed the following: hba1c = 9%; total cholesterol = 250 mg/dl; ldl cholesterol = 300 mg/dl; and uric acid = 9 mg/dl. he also reported joint pain, lower back pain, and swelling of the joints. based on the who bmi classification, what is the patient''s nutritional status?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Body Mass Index (BMI) is a simple anthropometric index used to classify the nutritional status of adults. It is calculated using the formula: BMI = Weight (kg) ÷ Height (m)² ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Overweight\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Body Mass Index (BMI) is a simple anthropometric index used to classify the nutritional status of adults. It is calculated using the formula: BMI = Weight (kg) ÷ Height (m)² \n\nFor this patient: \n• Height = 170 cm = 1.70 m \n• Weight = 75 kg \nBMI = 75 ÷ (1.70)² = 75 ÷ 2.89 = 25.95 kg/m² \n\nUsing the World Health Organization (WHO) international BMI classification, a BMI of 25.95 kg/m² falls under Overweight (25.0–29.9 kg/m²).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Normal, Obese, Morbidly obese"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the registered dietitian nutritionist (rdn) is reviewing a patient''s understanding of and adherence to a prescribed consistent carbohydrate diet. three months later, the patient''s hemoglobin a1c (hba1c) is reassessed, and progress toward the target range is evaluated. this scenario describes which step of the nutrition care process (ncp)?:::nutrition monitoring and evaluation', 'the registered dietitian nutritionist (rdn) is reviewing a patient''s understanding of and adherence to a prescribed consistent carbohydrate diet. three months later, the patient''s hemoglobin a1c (hba1c) is reassessed, and progress toward the target range is evaluated. this scenario describes which step of the nutrition care process (ncp)?', 'Nutrition Monitoring and Evaluation', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In this scenario, the RDN is reviewing the patient''s adherence to the prescribed nutrition intervention and reassessing HbA1c after three months to determine whether the nutrition goals have been achieved. These activities are characteristic of Nutrition Monitoring and Evaluation, which involves measuring and comparing outcomes against established nutrition goals and determining whether modifications to the nutrition care plan are necessary. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Nutrition Monitoring and Evaluation\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In this scenario, the RDN is reviewing the patient''s adherence to the prescribed nutrition intervention and reassessing HbA1c after three months to determine whether the nutrition goals have been achieved. These activities are characteristic of Nutrition Monitoring and Evaluation, which involves measuring and comparing outcomes against established nutrition goals and determining whether modifications to the nutrition care plan are necessary. \n\nHemoglobin A1c is an important monitoring indicator because it reflects the patient''s average blood glucose over the previous 2–3 months. Therefore, reassessing HbA1c after three months is clinically appropriate to evaluate the effectiveness of a consistent carbohydrate meal plan and the patient''s adherence to nutrition recommendations.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Nutrition Assessment, Nutrition Diagnosis, Nutrition Intervention"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the registered dietitian nutritionist (rdn) is reviewing a patient''s understanding of and adherence to a prescribed consistent carbohydrate diet. three months later, the patient''s hemoglobin a1c (hba1c) is reassessed, and progress toward the target range is evaluated. this scenario describes which step of the nutrition care process (ncp)?:::', 'the registered dietitian nutritionist (rdn) is reviewing a patient''s understanding of and adherence to a prescribed consistent carbohydrate diet. three months later, the patient''s hemoglobin a1c (hba1c) is reassessed, and progress toward the target range is evaluated. this scenario describes which step of the nutrition care process (ncp)?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In this scenario, the RDN is reviewing the patient''s adherence to the prescribed nutrition intervention and reassessing HbA1c after three months to determine whether the nutrition goals have been achieved. These activities are characteristic of Nutrition Monitoring and Evaluation, which involves measuring and comparing outcomes against established nutrition goals and determining whether modifications to the nutrition care plan are necessary. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Nutrition Monitoring and Evaluation\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In this scenario, the RDN is reviewing the patient''s adherence to the prescribed nutrition intervention and reassessing HbA1c after three months to determine whether the nutrition goals have been achieved. These activities are characteristic of Nutrition Monitoring and Evaluation, which involves measuring and comparing outcomes against established nutrition goals and determining whether modifications to the nutrition care plan are necessary. \n\nHemoglobin A1c is an important monitoring indicator because it reflects the patient''s average blood glucose over the previous 2–3 months. Therefore, reassessing HbA1c after three months is clinically appropriate to evaluate the effectiveness of a consistent carbohydrate meal plan and the patient''s adherence to nutrition recommendations.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Nutrition Assessment, Nutrition Diagnosis, Nutrition Intervention"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a 55-year-old filipino male laboratory technician diagnosed with type 2 diabetes mellitus visited the nutrition clinic for counseling. his anthropometric measurements are as follows: height = 5''7" (170 cm), weight = 75 kg, and waist circumference = 36 inches. his laboratory results revealed the following: hba1c = 9%; total cholesterol = 250 mg/dl; ldl cholesterol = 300 mg/dl; and uric acid = 9 mg/dl. he also reported joint pain, lower back pain, and swelling of the joints. aside from prescribing a consistent carbohydrate diet, the dietitian advised him to increase his dietary fiber intake. what is the recommended daily fiber intake for this patient?:::', 'a 55-year-old filipino male laboratory technician diagnosed with type 2 diabetes mellitus visited the nutrition clinic for counseling. his anthropometric measurements are as follows: height = 5''7" (170 cm), weight = 75 kg, and waist circumference = 36 inches. his laboratory results revealed the following: hba1c = 9%; total cholesterol = 250 mg/dl; ldl cholesterol = 300 mg/dl; and uric acid = 9 mg/dl. he also reported joint pain, lower back pain, and swelling of the joints. aside from prescribing a consistent carbohydrate diet, the dietitian advised him to increase his dietary fiber intake. what is the recommended daily fiber intake for this patient?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dietary fiber is an important component of Medical Nutrition Therapy (MNT) for individuals with Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus. Increasing fiber intake has been shown to improve glycemic control, promote satiety, reduce postprandial blood glucose excursions, improve bowel function, and contribute to lowering total and LDL cholesterol levels. These benefits are particularly important for this patient, who has poor glycemic control (HbA1c = 9%) and dyslipidemia. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"35 grams\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dietary fiber is an important component of Medical Nutrition Therapy (MNT) for individuals with Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus. Increasing fiber intake has been shown to improve glycemic control, promote satiety, reduce postprandial blood glucose excursions, improve bowel function, and contribute to lowering total and LDL cholesterol levels. These benefits are particularly important for this patient, who has poor glycemic control (HbA1c = 9%) and dyslipidemia. \n\nCurrent evidence recommends that adults with diabetes consume at least 14 g of dietary fiber per 1,000 kcal, which generally translates to approximately 25–38 g/day, depending on age, sex, and energy requirements. For adult men, a daily intake of about 30–38 g/day is generally recommended.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 45 grams, 55 grams, 65 grams"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a 32-year-old nurse was referred to the nutrition clinic for nutrition counseling. she is currently in her second trimester of pregnancy and was diagnosed with gestational diabetes mellitus (gdm) based on elevated oral glucose tolerance test (ogtt) and fasting blood glucose (fbg) results. her medical history revealed a family history of diabetes mellitus (father with type 2 diabetes mellitus), a pre-pregnancy bmi of 32 kg/m², and asian ethnicity. which of the following are the non-modifiable risk factors for gdm present in this patient? i. age ii. family history of diabetes mellitus iii. pre-pregnancy bmi iv. race:::i, ii and iv only', 'a 32-year-old nurse was referred to the nutrition clinic for nutrition counseling. she is currently in her second trimester of pregnancy and was diagnosed with gestational diabetes mellitus (gdm) based on elevated oral glucose tolerance test (ogtt) and fasting blood glucose (fbg) results. her medical history revealed a family history of diabetes mellitus (father with type 2 diabetes mellitus), a pre-pregnancy bmi of 32 kg/m², and asian ethnicity. which of the following are the non-modifiable risk factors for gdm present in this patient? i. age ii. family history of diabetes mellitus iii. pre-pregnancy bmi iv. race', 'I, II and IV only', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Gestational Diabetes Mellitus (GDM) is glucose intolerance first recognized during pregnancy. Several maternal characteristics increase the risk of developing GDM and are classified as either modifiable or non-modifiable. In this patient: ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"I, II and IV only\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Gestational Diabetes Mellitus (GDM) is glucose intolerance first recognized during pregnancy. Several maternal characteristics increase the risk of developing GDM and are classified as either modifiable or non-modifiable. In this patient: \n• Age (32 years) – Although she is younger than the commonly cited threshold of ≥35 years, maternal age itself is considered a non-modifiable risk factor because it cannot be changed. Increasing maternal age is associated with a higher risk of GDM. \n• Family history of diabetes mellitus – A first-degree relative with Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus significantly increases the risk because of genetic predisposition. This is a non-modifiable risk factor. \n• Race/Ethnicity (Asian descent) – Women of Asian ancestry have a higher prevalence of GDM than some other populations, making ethnicity another non-modifiable risk factor. \n\n• Pre-pregnancy BMI of 32 kg/m² – This represents obesity, which is a well-established risk factor for GDM. However, it is considered modifiable, as body weight can be improved before pregnancy through appropriate lifestyle interventions.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: I and IV only, II and III only, II, III and IV only"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a 32-year-old nurse was referred to the nutrition clinic for nutrition counseling. she is currently in her second trimester of pregnancy and was diagnosed with gestational diabetes mellitus (gdm) based on elevated oral glucose tolerance test (ogtt) and fasting blood glucose (fbg) results. her medical history revealed a family history of diabetes mellitus (father with type 2 diabetes mellitus), a pre-pregnancy bmi of 32 kg/m², and asian ethnicity. which of the following are the non-modifiable risk factors for gdm present in this patient? i. age ii. family history of diabetes mellitus iii. pre-pregnancy bmi iv. race:::', 'a 32-year-old nurse was referred to the nutrition clinic for nutrition counseling. she is currently in her second trimester of pregnancy and was diagnosed with gestational diabetes mellitus (gdm) based on elevated oral glucose tolerance test (ogtt) and fasting blood glucose (fbg) results. her medical history revealed a family history of diabetes mellitus (father with type 2 diabetes mellitus), a pre-pregnancy bmi of 32 kg/m², and asian ethnicity. which of the following are the non-modifiable risk factors for gdm present in this patient? i. age ii. family history of diabetes mellitus iii. pre-pregnancy bmi iv. race', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Gestational Diabetes Mellitus (GDM) is glucose intolerance first recognized during pregnancy. Several maternal characteristics increase the risk of developing GDM and are classified as either modifiable or non-modifiable. In this patient: ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"I, II and IV only\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Gestational Diabetes Mellitus (GDM) is glucose intolerance first recognized during pregnancy. Several maternal characteristics increase the risk of developing GDM and are classified as either modifiable or non-modifiable. In this patient: \n• Age (32 years) – Although she is younger than the commonly cited threshold of ≥35 years, maternal age itself is considered a non-modifiable risk factor because it cannot be changed. Increasing maternal age is associated with a higher risk of GDM. \n• Family history of diabetes mellitus – A first-degree relative with Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus significantly increases the risk because of genetic predisposition. This is a non-modifiable risk factor. \n• Race/Ethnicity (Asian descent) – Women of Asian ancestry have a higher prevalence of GDM than some other populations, making ethnicity another non-modifiable risk factor. \n\n• Pre-pregnancy BMI of 32 kg/m² – This represents obesity, which is a well-established risk factor for GDM. However, it is considered modifiable, as body weight can be improved before pregnancy through appropriate lifestyle interventions.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: I and IV only, II and III only, II, III and IV only"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a 32-year-old nurse was referred to the nutrition clinic for nutrition counseling. she is currently in her second trimester of pregnancy and was diagnosed with gestational diabetes mellitus (gdm) based on elevated oral glucose tolerance test (ogtt) and fasting blood glucose (fbg) results. other information noted were a family history of diabetes mellitus (father with type 2 diabetes mellitus), a pre-pregnancy bmi of 32 kg/m², and asian ethnicity. which of the following should be included in the dietary recommendations for this patient?:::carbohydrates should be 50 – 60% of ter', 'a 32-year-old nurse was referred to the nutrition clinic for nutrition counseling. she is currently in her second trimester of pregnancy and was diagnosed with gestational diabetes mellitus (gdm) based on elevated oral glucose tolerance test (ogtt) and fasting blood glucose (fbg) results. other information noted were a family history of diabetes mellitus (father with type 2 diabetes mellitus), a pre-pregnancy bmi of 32 kg/m², and asian ethnicity. which of the following should be included in the dietary recommendations for this patient?', 'Carbohydrates should be 50 – 60% of TER', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Medical Nutrition Therapy (MNT) is the primary treatment for Gestational Diabetes Mellitus (GDM). The goals are to maintain normal maternal blood glucose levels, provide adequate nutrients for fetal growth and development, prevent ketosis, and achieve appropriate gestational weight gain. Traditionally, dietary recommendations for women with GDM include providing approximately 50–60% of the total energy requirement from carbohydrates, with the remainder supplied by protein and fat. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Carbohydrates should be 50 – 60% of TER\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Medical Nutrition Therapy (MNT) is the primary treatment for Gestational Diabetes Mellitus (GDM). The goals are to maintain normal maternal blood glucose levels, provide adequate nutrients for fetal growth and development, prevent ketosis, and achieve appropriate gestational weight gain. Traditionally, dietary recommendations for women with GDM include providing approximately 50–60% of the total energy requirement from carbohydrates, with the remainder supplied by protein and fat. \n\nCarbohydrates should come primarily from complex, high-fiber sources such as whole grains, legumes, vegetables, fruits, and low-fat dairy products. In addition, carbohydrate intake should be distributed evenly throughout the day by consuming three meals and appropriate snacks to minimize postprandial hyperglycemia.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Amount of carbohydrates per meal should be limited to 30 grams, Fruits should be taken as snack, Simple sugars should be eliminated from the diet"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a 32-year-old nurse was referred to the nutrition clinic for nutrition counseling. she is currently in her second trimester of pregnancy and was diagnosed with gestational diabetes mellitus (gdm) based on elevated oral glucose tolerance test (ogtt) and fasting blood glucose (fbg) results. other information noted were a family history of diabetes mellitus (father with type 2 diabetes mellitus), a pre-pregnancy bmi of 32 kg/m², and asian ethnicity. which of the following should be included in the dietary recommendations for this patient?:::', 'a 32-year-old nurse was referred to the nutrition clinic for nutrition counseling. she is currently in her second trimester of pregnancy and was diagnosed with gestational diabetes mellitus (gdm) based on elevated oral glucose tolerance test (ogtt) and fasting blood glucose (fbg) results. other information noted were a family history of diabetes mellitus (father with type 2 diabetes mellitus), a pre-pregnancy bmi of 32 kg/m², and asian ethnicity. which of the following should be included in the dietary recommendations for this patient?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Medical Nutrition Therapy (MNT) is the primary treatment for Gestational Diabetes Mellitus (GDM). The goals are to maintain normal maternal blood glucose levels, provide adequate nutrients for fetal growth and development, prevent ketosis, and achieve appropriate gestational weight gain. Traditionally, dietary recommendations for women with GDM include providing approximately 50–60% of the total energy requirement from carbohydrates, with the remainder supplied by protein and fat. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Carbohydrates should be 50 – 60% of TER\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Medical Nutrition Therapy (MNT) is the primary treatment for Gestational Diabetes Mellitus (GDM). The goals are to maintain normal maternal blood glucose levels, provide adequate nutrients for fetal growth and development, prevent ketosis, and achieve appropriate gestational weight gain. Traditionally, dietary recommendations for women with GDM include providing approximately 50–60% of the total energy requirement from carbohydrates, with the remainder supplied by protein and fat. \n\nCarbohydrates should come primarily from complex, high-fiber sources such as whole grains, legumes, vegetables, fruits, and low-fat dairy products. In addition, carbohydrate intake should be distributed evenly throughout the day by consuming three meals and appropriate snacks to minimize postprandial hyperglycemia.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Amount of carbohydrates per meal should be limited to 30 grams, Fruits should be taken as snack, Simple sugars should be eliminated from the diet"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which is not an immediate cause of malnutrition?:::low income', 'which is not an immediate cause of malnutrition?', 'Low income', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to the official UNICEF framework:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Low income\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to the official UNICEF framework:\n\n• Immediate Causes (Individual level): These operate at the level of the individual human body. There are only two immediate causes of malnutrition:\n\t1. Inadequate intake\n\t2. Disease\n\n• Underlying Causes (Household/Community level): These are the structural situations that lead to inadequate intake or disease. They include maternal and childcare practices, inadequate access to food, and poor water/sanitation services.\n\n• Basic Causes (Societal level): These are the root socioeconomic and political factors, such as low income, poverty, and lack of resources.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Inadequate intake, Maternal and childcare, Disease"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which is not an immediate cause of malnutrition?:::', 'which is not an immediate cause of malnutrition?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to the official UNICEF framework:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Low income\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to the official UNICEF framework:\n\n• Immediate Causes (Individual level): These operate at the level of the individual human body. There are only two immediate causes of malnutrition:\n\t1. Inadequate intake\n\t2. Disease\n\n• Underlying Causes (Household/Community level): These are the structural situations that lead to inadequate intake or disease. They include maternal and childcare practices, inadequate access to food, and poor water/sanitation services.\n\n• Basic Causes (Societal level): These are the root socioeconomic and political factors, such as low income, poverty, and lack of resources.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Inadequate intake, Maternal and childcare, Disease"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following happened in the oral cavity during digestion? i. food mastication ii. salivary glands lubricating the food iii. tongue tastes and move the food around iv. produced an end product called bolus:::all of the above', 'which of the following happened in the oral cavity during digestion? i. food mastication ii. salivary glands lubricating the food iii. tongue tastes and move the food around iv. produced an end product called bolus', 'All of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The oral cavity is the entry point of the digestive tract, where both mechanical and chemical digestion simultaneously begin. All four statements accurately describe the complex physiological processes that occur here:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The oral cavity is the entry point of the digestive tract, where both mechanical and chemical digestion simultaneously begin. All four statements accurately describe the complex physiological processes that occur here:\n\n• I. Food mastication: Chewing (mastication) is the primary mechanical function of the mouth. The teeth break down solid food into smaller fragments to dramatically increase the surface area available for digestive enzymes.\n• II. Salivary glands lubricating the food: The parotid, submandibular, and sublingual salivary glands continuously produce and secrete saliva. Saliva contains water, electrolytes, and mucin, which moistens and lubricates the dry food mass so it can glide easily through the pharynx and esophagus during swallowing.\n• III. Tongue tastes and move the food around: The surface of the tongue contains sensory gustatory receptors (taste buds) that identify food flavors. Mechanically, the tongue plays a vital role by manipulating and moving the chewed food particles around the oral cavity, positioning them between the teeth for efficient grinding.\n• IV. Produced an end product called bolus: As the tongue manipulates the thoroughly chewed food and mixes it completely with lubricating saliva, it shapes the mixture into a soft, cohesive, swallowable mass. This specific structural end product of oral cavity processing is officially termed a bolus.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: I and II only, I, II and III only, II and III only"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following happened in the oral cavity during digestion? i. food mastication ii. salivary glands lubricating the food iii. tongue tastes and move the food around iv. produced an end product called bolus:::', 'which of the following happened in the oral cavity during digestion? i. food mastication ii. salivary glands lubricating the food iii. tongue tastes and move the food around iv. produced an end product called bolus', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The oral cavity is the entry point of the digestive tract, where both mechanical and chemical digestion simultaneously begin. All four statements accurately describe the complex physiological processes that occur here:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The oral cavity is the entry point of the digestive tract, where both mechanical and chemical digestion simultaneously begin. All four statements accurately describe the complex physiological processes that occur here:\n\n• I. Food mastication: Chewing (mastication) is the primary mechanical function of the mouth. The teeth break down solid food into smaller fragments to dramatically increase the surface area available for digestive enzymes.\n• II. Salivary glands lubricating the food: The parotid, submandibular, and sublingual salivary glands continuously produce and secrete saliva. Saliva contains water, electrolytes, and mucin, which moistens and lubricates the dry food mass so it can glide easily through the pharynx and esophagus during swallowing.\n• III. Tongue tastes and move the food around: The surface of the tongue contains sensory gustatory receptors (taste buds) that identify food flavors. Mechanically, the tongue plays a vital role by manipulating and moving the chewed food particles around the oral cavity, positioning them between the teeth for efficient grinding.\n• IV. Produced an end product called bolus: As the tongue manipulates the thoroughly chewed food and mixes it completely with lubricating saliva, it shapes the mixture into a soft, cohesive, swallowable mass. This specific structural end product of oral cavity processing is officially termed a bolus.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: I and II only, I, II and III only, II and III only"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb)
