('mc:how can the stomach protect itself from the strong acidity of hcl?:::mucus secretion forms a protective coat over the mucosa', 'how can the stomach protect itself from the strong acidity of hcl?', 'Mucus secretion forms a protective coat over the mucosa', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The cells lining the inner wall of the stomach (surface mucous cells and mucous neck cells) continuously secrete a thick, gel-like layer of mucus.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Mucus secretion forms a protective coat over the mucosa\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The cells lining the inner wall of the stomach (surface mucous cells and mucous neck cells) continuously secrete a thick, gel-like layer of mucus.\n\nThis mucus layer is rich in bicarbonate ions (HCO₃⁻). The alkaline bicarbonate is trapped within the sticky mucus gel, creating a highly effective physical and chemical buffer system. While the liquid inside the stomach cavity is highly acidic with a pH of 1.5 to 2.0, this specialized protective coat maintains a near-neutral pH of around 7.0 right at the surface of the stomach''s epithelial cells, preventing hydrochloric acid (HCl) and pepsin from digesting the stomach wall.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Secretin stimulates the release of sodium bicarbonate to neutralize acid, Stomach is covered by thick microvilli, Pancreatic enzymes protect the stomach"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:how can the stomach protect itself from the strong acidity of hcl?:::', 'how can the stomach protect itself from the strong acidity of hcl?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The cells lining the inner wall of the stomach (surface mucous cells and mucous neck cells) continuously secrete a thick, gel-like layer of mucus.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Mucus secretion forms a protective coat over the mucosa\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The cells lining the inner wall of the stomach (surface mucous cells and mucous neck cells) continuously secrete a thick, gel-like layer of mucus.\n\nThis mucus layer is rich in bicarbonate ions (HCO₃⁻). The alkaline bicarbonate is trapped within the sticky mucus gel, creating a highly effective physical and chemical buffer system. While the liquid inside the stomach cavity is highly acidic with a pH of 1.5 to 2.0, this specialized protective coat maintains a near-neutral pH of around 7.0 right at the surface of the stomach''s epithelial cells, preventing hydrochloric acid (HCl) and pepsin from digesting the stomach wall.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Secretin stimulates the release of sodium bicarbonate to neutralize acid, Stomach is covered by thick microvilli, Pancreatic enzymes protect the stomach"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:an essential fatty acid derived from plant oils such as corn oil:::linoleic acid', 'an essential fatty acid derived from plant oils such as corn oil', 'Linoleic acid', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Linoleic acid (LA) is an essential omega-6 polyunsaturated fatty acid (PUFA) that must be obtained from the diet because the human body cannot synthesize it. It is abundant in plant oils, particularly corn oil, soybean oil, sunflower oil, safflower oil, and cottonseed oil. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Linoleic acid\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Linoleic acid (LA) is an essential omega-6 polyunsaturated fatty acid (PUFA) that must be obtained from the diet because the human body cannot synthesize it. It is abundant in plant oils, particularly corn oil, soybean oil, sunflower oil, safflower oil, and cottonseed oil. \n\nLinoleic acid plays important roles in maintaining cell membrane integrity, supporting growth and development, promoting skin health, and serving as the precursor of arachidonic acid, which is involved in the production of eicosanoids (prostaglandins, thromboxanes, and leukotrienes). \n\nAnother essential fatty acid is α-linolenic acid (ALA), an omega-3 fatty acid, which is commonly found in flaxseed, chia seeds, walnuts, canola oil, and soybean oil. Both linoleic acid and α-linolenic acid are considered essential because they cannot be synthesized by humans.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: α-Linolenic acid, Arachidonic acid, Palmitic acid"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:an essential fatty acid derived from plant oils such as corn oil:::', 'an essential fatty acid derived from plant oils such as corn oil', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Linoleic acid (LA) is an essential omega-6 polyunsaturated fatty acid (PUFA) that must be obtained from the diet because the human body cannot synthesize it. It is abundant in plant oils, particularly corn oil, soybean oil, sunflower oil, safflower oil, and cottonseed oil. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Linoleic acid\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Linoleic acid (LA) is an essential omega-6 polyunsaturated fatty acid (PUFA) that must be obtained from the diet because the human body cannot synthesize it. It is abundant in plant oils, particularly corn oil, soybean oil, sunflower oil, safflower oil, and cottonseed oil. \n\nLinoleic acid plays important roles in maintaining cell membrane integrity, supporting growth and development, promoting skin health, and serving as the precursor of arachidonic acid, which is involved in the production of eicosanoids (prostaglandins, thromboxanes, and leukotrienes). \n\nAnother essential fatty acid is α-linolenic acid (ALA), an omega-3 fatty acid, which is commonly found in flaxseed, chia seeds, walnuts, canola oil, and soybean oil. Both linoleic acid and α-linolenic acid are considered essential because they cannot be synthesized by humans.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: α-Linolenic acid, Arachidonic acid, Palmitic acid"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:compute the percent shrinkage of pork kasim if the raw weight is 850 g and the cooked weight is 600 g.:::29%', 'compute the percent shrinkage of pork kasim if the raw weight is 850 g and the cooked weight is 600 g.', '29%', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Formula: (raw weight - cooked weight) / raw weight * 100","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"29%\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Formula: (raw weight - cooked weight) / raw weight * 100\n= (850–600 ÷ 850) × 100 = 29%","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 25%, 31%, 33%"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the immediate substrate that feeds into the tca cycle?:::acetyl coa', 'what is the immediate substrate that feeds into the tca cycle?', 'Acetyl CoA', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The Tricarboxylic Acid (TCA) cycle, also known as the Citric Acid Cycle or Krebs Cycle, is the central metabolic pathway responsible for the oxidation of carbohydrates, fats, and proteins to produce energy. The immediate substrate that enters the TCA cycle is acetyl-CoA. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Acetyl CoA\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The Tricarboxylic Acid (TCA) cycle, also known as the Citric Acid Cycle or Krebs Cycle, is the central metabolic pathway responsible for the oxidation of carbohydrates, fats, and proteins to produce energy. The immediate substrate that enters the TCA cycle is acetyl-CoA. \n\nAcetyl-CoA is produced from several metabolic pathways: \n• Carbohydrates: Glucose → Glycolysis → Pyruvate → Acetyl-CoA \n• Fats: β-oxidation of fatty acids → Acetyl-CoA \n• Proteins: Ketogenic amino acids → Acetyl-CoA \n\nOnce formed, acetyl-CoA combines with oxaloacetate, catalyzed by citrate synthase, to produce citrate, the first compound of the TCA cycle. The cycle then proceeds through a series of reactions that generate NADH, FADH₂, and GTP (or ATP), which are subsequently used in oxidative phosphorylation to produce ATP. \n\nFor the NDLE, remember that acetyl-CoA is the metabolic crossroads because it links carbohydrate, lipid, and protein metabolism.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Pyruvate, Citrate, Oxaloacetate"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:recommended high protein regimen for wound healing::::a and c', 'recommended high protein regimen for wound healing:', 'A and C', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Adequate protein is essential for collagen synthesis, immune function, and tissue repair. Patients with severe wounds or extensive burns may require protein intakes as high as 2.0–3.0 g/kg/day, while protein may also account for approximately 20–25% of total energy intake depending on the clinical condition.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"A and C\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Adequate protein is essential for collagen synthesis, immune function, and tissue repair. Patients with severe wounds or extensive burns may require protein intakes as high as 2.0–3.0 g/kg/day, while protein may also account for approximately 20–25% of total energy intake depending on the clinical condition.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 20-25% of TER, 15-20% of TER, 2.5-3 g/kg"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the immediate substrate that feeds into the tca cycle?:::', 'what is the immediate substrate that feeds into the tca cycle?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The Tricarboxylic Acid (TCA) cycle, also known as the Citric Acid Cycle or Krebs Cycle, is the central metabolic pathway responsible for the oxidation of carbohydrates, fats, and proteins to produce energy. The immediate substrate that enters the TCA cycle is acetyl-CoA. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Acetyl CoA\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The Tricarboxylic Acid (TCA) cycle, also known as the Citric Acid Cycle or Krebs Cycle, is the central metabolic pathway responsible for the oxidation of carbohydrates, fats, and proteins to produce energy. The immediate substrate that enters the TCA cycle is acetyl-CoA. \n\nAcetyl-CoA is produced from several metabolic pathways: \n• Carbohydrates: Glucose → Glycolysis → Pyruvate → Acetyl-CoA \n• Fats: β-oxidation of fatty acids → Acetyl-CoA \n• Proteins: Ketogenic amino acids → Acetyl-CoA \n\nOnce formed, acetyl-CoA combines with oxaloacetate, catalyzed by citrate synthase, to produce citrate, the first compound of the TCA cycle. The cycle then proceeds through a series of reactions that generate NADH, FADH₂, and GTP (or ATP), which are subsequently used in oxidative phosphorylation to produce ATP. \n\nFor the NDLE, remember that acetyl-CoA is the metabolic crossroads because it links carbohydrate, lipid, and protein metabolism.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Pyruvate, Citrate, Oxaloacetate"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:in the electron transport system (ets), electrons from hydrogen carriers pass through a series of electron carriers, ultimately driving the conversion of adp to atp. this process is called::::oxidative phosphorylation', 'in the electron transport system (ets), electrons from hydrogen carriers pass through a series of electron carriers, ultimately driving the conversion of adp to atp. this process is called:', 'Oxidative phosphorylation', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Oxidative phosphorylation is the process by which energy released from the transfer of electrons through the electron transport chain (ETC) is used to generate ATP. During the oxidation of carbohydrates, fats, and proteins, hydrogen/electron carriers such as NADH and FADH₂ deliver high-energy electrons to the ETC located in the inner mitochondrial membrane. As electrons pass through the electron carriers, the released energy is used to pump protons (H⁺) across the membrane, creating a proton gradient. The return of H⁺ through ATP synthase provides the energy needed to synthesize ATP from ADP + inorganic phosphate (Pi). Thus, the process described in the question is oxidative phosphorylation.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Oxidative phosphorylation\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Oxidative phosphorylation is the process by which energy released from the transfer of electrons through the electron transport chain (ETC) is used to generate ATP. During the oxidation of carbohydrates, fats, and proteins, hydrogen/electron carriers such as NADH and FADH₂ deliver high-energy electrons to the ETC located in the inner mitochondrial membrane. As electrons pass through the electron carriers, the released energy is used to pump protons (H⁺) across the membrane, creating a proton gradient. The return of H⁺ through ATP synthase provides the energy needed to synthesize ATP from ADP + inorganic phosphate (Pi). Thus, the process described in the question is oxidative phosphorylation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Oxidative decarboxylation, Gluconeogenesis, Glycogenesis"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:in the electron transport system (ets), electrons from hydrogen carriers pass through a series of electron carriers, ultimately driving the conversion of adp to atp. this process is called::::', 'in the electron transport system (ets), electrons from hydrogen carriers pass through a series of electron carriers, ultimately driving the conversion of adp to atp. this process is called:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Oxidative phosphorylation is the process by which energy released from the transfer of electrons through the electron transport chain (ETC) is used to generate ATP. During the oxidation of carbohydrates, fats, and proteins, hydrogen/electron carriers such as NADH and FADH₂ deliver high-energy electrons to the ETC located in the inner mitochondrial membrane. As electrons pass through the electron carriers, the released energy is used to pump protons (H⁺) across the membrane, creating a proton gradient. The return of H⁺ through ATP synthase provides the energy needed to synthesize ATP from ADP + inorganic phosphate (Pi). Thus, the process described in the question is oxidative phosphorylation.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Oxidative phosphorylation\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Oxidative phosphorylation is the process by which energy released from the transfer of electrons through the electron transport chain (ETC) is used to generate ATP. During the oxidation of carbohydrates, fats, and proteins, hydrogen/electron carriers such as NADH and FADH₂ deliver high-energy electrons to the ETC located in the inner mitochondrial membrane. As electrons pass through the electron carriers, the released energy is used to pump protons (H⁺) across the membrane, creating a proton gradient. The return of H⁺ through ATP synthase provides the energy needed to synthesize ATP from ADP + inorganic phosphate (Pi). Thus, the process described in the question is oxidative phosphorylation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Oxidative decarboxylation, Gluconeogenesis, Glycogenesis"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:non-digestible food ingredients that encourage the growth of beneficial bacteria::::', 'non-digestible food ingredients that encourage the growth of beneficial bacteria:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Prebiotics are substrates that are selectively utilized by host microorganisms and provide a health benefit. Many are non-digestible carbohydrates, such as certain oligosaccharides and inulin-type fructans, that reach the colon and are fermented by beneficial microorganisms. This fermentation can produce short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs), which contribute to intestinal and metabolic health. Thus, the description in the question corresponds to a prebiotic, not a probiotic. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Prebiotic\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Prebiotics are substrates that are selectively utilized by host microorganisms and provide a health benefit. Many are non-digestible carbohydrates, such as certain oligosaccharides and inulin-type fructans, that reach the colon and are fermented by beneficial microorganisms. This fermentation can produce short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs), which contribute to intestinal and metabolic health. Thus, the description in the question corresponds to a prebiotic, not a probiotic. \n\nFor NDLE purposes, distinguish the three terms clearly: \n\nPrebiotics are substrates that beneficial microorganisms use; \nProbiotics are live microorganisms that provide a health benefit when administered in adequate amounts; and \nMicrobiota refers to the community of microorganisms living in a particular environment, such as the gastrointestinal tract.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Probiotic, Microbiota, None of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are functions of sodium, except::::participation in detoxification reactions', 'the following are functions of sodium, except:', 'Participation in detoxification reactions', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sodium is the major extracellular cation and plays an important role in maintaining extracellular fluid volume and osmotic balance. It is also essential for nerve impulse transmission and muscle contraction through its role in generating membrane potentials. Sodium-dependent transporters in the intestinal epithelium also facilitate the absorption of glucose and other nutrients, such as certain amino acids. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Participation in detoxification reactions\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sodium is the major extracellular cation and plays an important role in maintaining extracellular fluid volume and osmotic balance. It is also essential for nerve impulse transmission and muscle contraction through its role in generating membrane potentials. Sodium-dependent transporters in the intestinal epithelium also facilitate the absorption of glucose and other nutrients, such as certain amino acids. \n\nHowever, detoxification reactions are not a primary function of sodium. Detoxification involves processes primarily carried out by the liver, including enzymatic reactions such as oxidation, reduction, hydrolysis, and conjugation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Maintenance of normal extracellular fluid balance, Nerve impulse transmission and muscular contraction, Absorption of glucose and transport of other nutrients"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the primary risk factor for copd is::::cigarette smoking', 'the primary risk factor for copd is:', 'cigarette smoking', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Cigarette smoking is the most important and common risk factor for COPD. Chronic exposure to tobacco smoke causes airway inflammation, destruction of lung tissue, and progressive, largely irreversible airflow limitation. Other inhaled pollutants can also contribute, particularly occupational exposure to dusts, fumes, and chemicals, but cigarette smoking remains the major preventable risk factor. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"cigarette smoking\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Cigarette smoking is the most important and common risk factor for COPD. Chronic exposure to tobacco smoke causes airway inflammation, destruction of lung tissue, and progressive, largely irreversible airflow limitation. Other inhaled pollutants can also contribute, particularly occupational exposure to dusts, fumes, and chemicals, but cigarette smoking remains the major preventable risk factor. \n\nAlpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency is an important genetic risk factor for COPD, particularly emphysema at a younger age, but it is relatively uncommon. Frequent respiratory infections may worsen respiratory health but are not the primary risk factor for developing COPD.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: alpha-I antitrypsin deficiency, occupational exposure to dusts or chemicals, frequent respiratory infections"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the primary risk factor for copd is::::', 'the primary risk factor for copd is:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Cigarette smoking is the most important and common risk factor for COPD. Chronic exposure to tobacco smoke causes airway inflammation, destruction of lung tissue, and progressive, largely irreversible airflow limitation. Other inhaled pollutants can also contribute, particularly occupational exposure to dusts, fumes, and chemicals, but cigarette smoking remains the major preventable risk factor. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"cigarette smoking\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Cigarette smoking is the most important and common risk factor for COPD. Chronic exposure to tobacco smoke causes airway inflammation, destruction of lung tissue, and progressive, largely irreversible airflow limitation. Other inhaled pollutants can also contribute, particularly occupational exposure to dusts, fumes, and chemicals, but cigarette smoking remains the major preventable risk factor. \n\nAlpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency is an important genetic risk factor for COPD, particularly emphysema at a younger age, but it is relatively uncommon. Frequent respiratory infections may worsen respiratory health but are not the primary risk factor for developing COPD.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: alpha-I antitrypsin deficiency, occupational exposure to dusts or chemicals, frequent respiratory infections"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the weight loss and wasting that often occur in copd can be caused by::::all of the above', 'the weight loss and wasting that often occur in copd can be caused by:', 'all of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Weight loss and muscle wasting are common in advanced COPD and may result from several interacting factors. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"all of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Weight loss and muscle wasting are common in advanced COPD and may result from several interacting factors. \n• Reduced food intake may occur because of dyspnea, fatigue, early satiety, or difficulty eating while breathing. \n• Increased metabolic rate can occur because the work of breathing is increased, raising energy expenditure. \n• Reduced exercise tolerance decreases physical activity and contributes to loss of muscle mass and strength. \n\nThese factors can create a cycle of poor intake → weight loss → muscle loss → reduced respiratory muscle strength → greater difficulty breathing. Therefore, nutrition management aims to provide adequate energy and protein while avoiding overfeeding.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: reduced food intake, increased metabolic rate, reduced exercise tolerance"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:compute the percent shrinkage of pork kasim if the raw weight is 850 g and the cooked weight is 600 g.:::', 'compute the percent shrinkage of pork kasim if the raw weight is 850 g and the cooked weight is 600 g.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Formula: (raw weight - cooked weight) / raw weight * 100","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"29%\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Formula: (raw weight - cooked weight) / raw weight * 100\n= (850–600 ÷ 850) × 100 = 29%","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 25%, 31%, 33%"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the weight loss and wasting that often occur in copd can be caused by::::', 'the weight loss and wasting that often occur in copd can be caused by:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Weight loss and muscle wasting are common in advanced COPD and may result from several interacting factors. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"all of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Weight loss and muscle wasting are common in advanced COPD and may result from several interacting factors. \n• Reduced food intake may occur because of dyspnea, fatigue, early satiety, or difficulty eating while breathing. \n• Increased metabolic rate can occur because the work of breathing is increased, raising energy expenditure. \n• Reduced exercise tolerance decreases physical activity and contributes to loss of muscle mass and strength. \n\nThese factors can create a cycle of poor intake → weight loss → muscle loss → reduced respiratory muscle strength → greater difficulty breathing. Therefore, nutrition management aims to provide adequate energy and protein while avoiding overfeeding.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: reduced food intake, increased metabolic rate, reduced exercise tolerance"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:nutrition therapy for a person with respiratory failure includes::::careful attention to providing enough, but not too much energy', 'nutrition therapy for a person with respiratory failure includes:', 'careful attention to providing enough, but not too much energy', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In patients with acute or chronic respiratory failure, providing too much total energy (overfeeding) is dangerous. Excess calories—especially from carbohydrates but also from fats and proteins—accelerate lipogenesis, which drives up carbon dioxide (CO₂) production. Since the patient''s compromised respiratory system cannot handle the increased respiratory workload needed to blow off this extra CO₂, overfeeding can trigger hypercapnia and make it extremely difficult to wean them off a mechanical ventilator.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"careful attention to providing enough, but not too much energy\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In patients with acute or chronic respiratory failure, providing too much total energy (overfeeding) is dangerous. Excess calories—especially from carbohydrates but also from fats and proteins—accelerate lipogenesis, which drives up carbon dioxide (CO₂) production. Since the patient''s compromised respiratory system cannot handle the increased respiratory workload needed to blow off this extra CO₂, overfeeding can trigger hypercapnia and make it extremely difficult to wean them off a mechanical ventilator.\n\nAt the same time, underfeeding must be strictly avoided. If a patient does not receive enough calories, their body will enter a catabolic state, breaking down skeletal muscle—including the diaphragm and intercostal respiratory muscles—which worsens muscle weakness and leads to respiratory muscle failure. Therefore, nutrition therapy must be balanced to provide enough energy to meet metabolic needs without exceeding them.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: a generous fluid intake to facilitate mucus clearance, a high fat intake to prevent weight loss and wasting, a high carbohydrate intake to increase carbon dioxide production"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:nutrition therapy for a person with respiratory failure includes::::', 'nutrition therapy for a person with respiratory failure includes:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In patients with acute or chronic respiratory failure, providing too much total energy (overfeeding) is dangerous. Excess calories—especially from carbohydrates but also from fats and proteins—accelerate lipogenesis, which drives up carbon dioxide (CO₂) production. Since the patient''s compromised respiratory system cannot handle the increased respiratory workload needed to blow off this extra CO₂, overfeeding can trigger hypercapnia and make it extremely difficult to wean them off a mechanical ventilator.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"careful attention to providing enough, but not too much energy\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In patients with acute or chronic respiratory failure, providing too much total energy (overfeeding) is dangerous. Excess calories—especially from carbohydrates but also from fats and proteins—accelerate lipogenesis, which drives up carbon dioxide (CO₂) production. Since the patient''s compromised respiratory system cannot handle the increased respiratory workload needed to blow off this extra CO₂, overfeeding can trigger hypercapnia and make it extremely difficult to wean them off a mechanical ventilator.\n\nAt the same time, underfeeding must be strictly avoided. If a patient does not receive enough calories, their body will enter a catabolic state, breaking down skeletal muscle—including the diaphragm and intercostal respiratory muscles—which worsens muscle weakness and leads to respiratory muscle failure. Therefore, nutrition therapy must be balanced to provide enough energy to meet metabolic needs without exceeding them.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: a generous fluid intake to facilitate mucus clearance, a high fat intake to prevent weight loss and wasting, a high carbohydrate intake to increase carbon dioxide production"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:consider the following statements about indoles: statement 1: indoles are phytochemicals found in broccoli and other cruciferous vegetables that induce the production of detoxification enzymes, helping protect cells against carcinogens. statement 2: indoles may influence estrogen metabolism and modulate estrogen activity.:::both statements are correct.', 'consider the following statements about indoles: statement 1: indoles are phytochemicals found in broccoli and other cruciferous vegetables that induce the production of detoxification enzymes, helping protect cells against carcinogens. statement 2: indoles may influence estrogen metabolism and modulate estrogen activity.', 'Both statements are correct.', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• Statement 1 is CORRECT: Indoles, such as indole-3-carbinol (I3C) and its metabolic derivative 3,3''-diindolylmethane (DIM), are potent sulfur-containing phytochemicals derived from the breakdown of glucosinolates found heavily in cruciferous vegetables like broccoli, cabbage, cauliflower, and Brussels sprouts. Biochemically, they stimulate and upregulate Phase I and Phase II biotransformation (detoxification) enzymes in the liver and gastrointestinal tract. This metabolic acceleration helps the body break down, neutralize, and safely eliminate carcinogens, xenobiotics, and cellular toxins before they can cause DNA damage.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Both statements are correct.\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• Statement 1 is CORRECT: Indoles, such as indole-3-carbinol (I3C) and its metabolic derivative 3,3''-diindolylmethane (DIM), are potent sulfur-containing phytochemicals derived from the breakdown of glucosinolates found heavily in cruciferous vegetables like broccoli, cabbage, cauliflower, and Brussels sprouts. Biochemically, they stimulate and upregulate Phase I and Phase II biotransformation (detoxification) enzymes in the liver and gastrointestinal tract. This metabolic acceleration helps the body break down, neutralize, and safely eliminate carcinogens, xenobiotics, and cellular toxins before they can cause DNA damage.\n\n• Statement 2 is CORRECT: Indoles are heavily researched for their distinct capacity to influence estrogen metabolism and modulate overall estrogen activity. They alter the pathways of hormone breakdown in the liver, effectively driving the conversion of strong, highly active estrogens into less potent, protective metabolites (favoring 2-hydroxyestrone over the more harmful 16-alpha-hydroxyestrone). By acting as natural estrogen modulators or blockers, dietary indoles provide systemic chemopreventive traits against hormone-dependent conditions, including breast and endometrial cancers.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Only statement 1 is correct., Only statement 2 is correct., Both statements are incorrect."}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:consider the following statements about indoles: statement 1: indoles are phytochemicals found in broccoli and other cruciferous vegetables that induce the production of detoxification enzymes, helping protect cells against carcinogens. statement 2: indoles may influence estrogen metabolism and modulate estrogen activity.:::', 'consider the following statements about indoles: statement 1: indoles are phytochemicals found in broccoli and other cruciferous vegetables that induce the production of detoxification enzymes, helping protect cells against carcinogens. statement 2: indoles may influence estrogen metabolism and modulate estrogen activity.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• Statement 1 is CORRECT: Indoles, such as indole-3-carbinol (I3C) and its metabolic derivative 3,3''-diindolylmethane (DIM), are potent sulfur-containing phytochemicals derived from the breakdown of glucosinolates found heavily in cruciferous vegetables like broccoli, cabbage, cauliflower, and Brussels sprouts. Biochemically, they stimulate and upregulate Phase I and Phase II biotransformation (detoxification) enzymes in the liver and gastrointestinal tract. This metabolic acceleration helps the body break down, neutralize, and safely eliminate carcinogens, xenobiotics, and cellular toxins before they can cause DNA damage.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Both statements are correct.\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• Statement 1 is CORRECT: Indoles, such as indole-3-carbinol (I3C) and its metabolic derivative 3,3''-diindolylmethane (DIM), are potent sulfur-containing phytochemicals derived from the breakdown of glucosinolates found heavily in cruciferous vegetables like broccoli, cabbage, cauliflower, and Brussels sprouts. Biochemically, they stimulate and upregulate Phase I and Phase II biotransformation (detoxification) enzymes in the liver and gastrointestinal tract. This metabolic acceleration helps the body break down, neutralize, and safely eliminate carcinogens, xenobiotics, and cellular toxins before they can cause DNA damage.\n\n• Statement 2 is CORRECT: Indoles are heavily researched for their distinct capacity to influence estrogen metabolism and modulate overall estrogen activity. They alter the pathways of hormone breakdown in the liver, effectively driving the conversion of strong, highly active estrogens into less potent, protective metabolites (favoring 2-hydroxyestrone over the more harmful 16-alpha-hydroxyestrone). By acting as natural estrogen modulators or blockers, dietary indoles provide systemic chemopreventive traits against hormone-dependent conditions, including breast and endometrial cancers.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Only statement 1 is correct., Only statement 2 is correct., Both statements are incorrect."}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:phytochemicals found in whole grains that minds to minerals and prevents free radical formation.:::phytic acid', 'phytochemicals found in whole grains that minds to minerals and prevents free radical formation.', 'Phytic acid', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Phytic acid (also known as phytate) is a unique compound found in the bran and germ layer of whole grains, nuts, and legumes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Phytic acid\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Phytic acid (also known as phytate) is a unique compound found in the bran and germ layer of whole grains, nuts, and legumes.\n\nIt is well-known for its ability to bind (chelate) to minerals like iron, zinc, calcium, and magnesium.\n\nWhile this mineral-binding property often labels it as an \"antinutrient\" because it reduces mineral absorption, it also gives phytic acid a potent antioxidant function. By specifically binding to free iron, it suppresses iron-catalyzed oxidative reactions, thereby preventing the formation of harmful free radicals.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Phenols, Flavonoids, Phytosterols"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:phytochemicals found in whole grains that minds to minerals and prevents free radical formation.:::', 'phytochemicals found in whole grains that minds to minerals and prevents free radical formation.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Phytic acid (also known as phytate) is a unique compound found in the bran and germ layer of whole grains, nuts, and legumes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Phytic acid\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Phytic acid (also known as phytate) is a unique compound found in the bran and germ layer of whole grains, nuts, and legumes.\n\nIt is well-known for its ability to bind (chelate) to minerals like iron, zinc, calcium, and magnesium.\n\nWhile this mineral-binding property often labels it as an \"antinutrient\" because it reduces mineral absorption, it also gives phytic acid a potent antioxidant function. By specifically binding to free iron, it suppresses iron-catalyzed oxidative reactions, thereby preventing the formation of harmful free radicals.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Phenols, Flavonoids, Phytosterols"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:recommended high protein regimen for wound healing::::', 'recommended high protein regimen for wound healing:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Adequate protein is essential for collagen synthesis, immune function, and tissue repair. Patients with severe wounds or extensive burns may require protein intakes as high as 2.0–3.0 g/kg/day, while protein may also account for approximately 20–25% of total energy intake depending on the clinical condition.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"A and C\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Adequate protein is essential for collagen synthesis, immune function, and tissue repair. Patients with severe wounds or extensive burns may require protein intakes as high as 2.0–3.0 g/kg/day, while protein may also account for approximately 20–25% of total energy intake depending on the clinical condition.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 20-25% of TER, 15-20% of TER, 2.5-3 g/kg"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following are included in the nutrition management of peptic ulcer?:::all of the above', 'which of the following are included in the nutrition management of peptic ulcer?', 'All of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The nutritional management of Peptic Ulcer Disease (PUD) heavily focuses on reducing foods and beverages that stimulate the overproduction of stomach acid, cause direct irritation to the gastric mucosa, or delay the healing process:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The nutritional management of Peptic Ulcer Disease (PUD) heavily focuses on reducing foods and beverages that stimulate the overproduction of stomach acid, cause direct irritation to the gastric mucosa, or delay the healing process:\n\n• Decrease consumption of coffee and caffeine: Both caffeinated and decaffeinated coffee trigger gastric acid secretion and can worsen upper gastrointestinal discomfort.\n• Decrease consumption of spices: Irritating spices—especially black pepper, chili powder, and red pepper—can induce severe dyspemia (indigestion) and irritate the open lining of an existing gastric or duodenal ulcer.\n• Decrease consumption of alcohol: Alcohol can directly damage the protective mucosal barrier of the stomach, exacerbate inflammation, and increase susceptibility to acid damage.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Decrease consumption of alcohol, Decrease consumption of spices, Decrease consumption of coffee and caffeine"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following are included in the nutrition management of peptic ulcer?:::', 'which of the following are included in the nutrition management of peptic ulcer?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The nutritional management of Peptic Ulcer Disease (PUD) heavily focuses on reducing foods and beverages that stimulate the overproduction of stomach acid, cause direct irritation to the gastric mucosa, or delay the healing process:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The nutritional management of Peptic Ulcer Disease (PUD) heavily focuses on reducing foods and beverages that stimulate the overproduction of stomach acid, cause direct irritation to the gastric mucosa, or delay the healing process:\n\n• Decrease consumption of coffee and caffeine: Both caffeinated and decaffeinated coffee trigger gastric acid secretion and can worsen upper gastrointestinal discomfort.\n• Decrease consumption of spices: Irritating spices—especially black pepper, chili powder, and red pepper—can induce severe dyspemia (indigestion) and irritate the open lining of an existing gastric or duodenal ulcer.\n• Decrease consumption of alcohol: Alcohol can directly damage the protective mucosal barrier of the stomach, exacerbate inflammation, and increase susceptibility to acid damage.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Decrease consumption of alcohol, Decrease consumption of spices, Decrease consumption of coffee and caffeine"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a patient is receiving treatment for crohn''s disease. which food found on the patient''s food tray should the patient avoid?:::fresh salad', 'a patient is receiving treatment for crohn''s disease. which food found on the patient''s food tray should the patient avoid?', 'Fresh salad', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Crohn''s disease is a chronic inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) that can affect any part of the gastrointestinal tract. During an acute flare, patients often experience abdominal pain, diarrhea, weight loss, fatigue, and malabsorption. Nutrition therapy focuses on reducing gastrointestinal irritation, maintaining nutritional status, and preventing deficiencies. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Fresh salad\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Crohn''s disease is a chronic inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) that can affect any part of the gastrointestinal tract. During an acute flare, patients often experience abdominal pain, diarrhea, weight loss, fatigue, and malabsorption. Nutrition therapy focuses on reducing gastrointestinal irritation, maintaining nutritional status, and preventing deficiencies. \n\nFresh salad contains raw vegetables that are generally high in insoluble fiber, which can increase stool volume and intestinal motility. In patients with active Crohn''s disease—especially those with intestinal inflammation, strictures, or obstruction risk—raw vegetables may worsen abdominal pain, bloating, diarrhea, or cramping.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: White rice, Baked chicken, Cooked skinless apples"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a patient is receiving treatment for crohn''s disease. which food found on the patient''s food tray should the patient avoid?:::', 'a patient is receiving treatment for crohn''s disease. which food found on the patient''s food tray should the patient avoid?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Crohn''s disease is a chronic inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) that can affect any part of the gastrointestinal tract. During an acute flare, patients often experience abdominal pain, diarrhea, weight loss, fatigue, and malabsorption. Nutrition therapy focuses on reducing gastrointestinal irritation, maintaining nutritional status, and preventing deficiencies. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Fresh salad\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Crohn''s disease is a chronic inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) that can affect any part of the gastrointestinal tract. During an acute flare, patients often experience abdominal pain, diarrhea, weight loss, fatigue, and malabsorption. Nutrition therapy focuses on reducing gastrointestinal irritation, maintaining nutritional status, and preventing deficiencies. \n\nFresh salad contains raw vegetables that are generally high in insoluble fiber, which can increase stool volume and intestinal motility. In patients with active Crohn''s disease—especially those with intestinal inflammation, strictures, or obstruction risk—raw vegetables may worsen abdominal pain, bloating, diarrhea, or cramping.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: White rice, Baked chicken, Cooked skinless apples"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:it refers to the accumulation of fluids in the abdominal cavity::::ascites', 'it refers to the accumulation of fluids in the abdominal cavity:', 'Ascites', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Ascites specifically refers to the abnormal accumulation of excess fluid within the peritoneal (abdominal) cavity. It is most commonly driven by severe liver diseases like cirrhosis, where high blood pressure in the liver''s veins (portal hypertension) forces fluid to leak into the abdomen.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Ascites\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Ascites specifically refers to the abnormal accumulation of excess fluid within the peritoneal (abdominal) cavity. It is most commonly driven by severe liver diseases like cirrhosis, where high blood pressure in the liver''s veins (portal hypertension) forces fluid to leak into the abdomen.\n\n\t• Esophageal varices: These are enlarged, abnormal veins in the lower part of the esophagus that develop due to obstructed blood flow through the liver. While they share a common cause with ascites (portal hypertension), they are blood vessels, not fluid accumulation.\n\t• Collateral formation: This is the biological process where the body grows alternative blood pathways (collateral vessels) to detour around a blocked or high-pressure vein.\n\t• Edema: This is a broad, generic medical term for swelling caused by fluid trapped in any body tissue. While ascites is technically a localized type of edema, the term edema on its own usually refers to generalized swelling in the limbs (like ankle or leg edema) rather than the abdominal space.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Esophageal varices, Edema, Collateral formation"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:it refers to the accumulation of fluids in the abdominal cavity::::', 'it refers to the accumulation of fluids in the abdominal cavity:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Ascites specifically refers to the abnormal accumulation of excess fluid within the peritoneal (abdominal) cavity. It is most commonly driven by severe liver diseases like cirrhosis, where high blood pressure in the liver''s veins (portal hypertension) forces fluid to leak into the abdomen.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Ascites\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Ascites specifically refers to the abnormal accumulation of excess fluid within the peritoneal (abdominal) cavity. It is most commonly driven by severe liver diseases like cirrhosis, where high blood pressure in the liver''s veins (portal hypertension) forces fluid to leak into the abdomen.\n\n\t• Esophageal varices: These are enlarged, abnormal veins in the lower part of the esophagus that develop due to obstructed blood flow through the liver. While they share a common cause with ascites (portal hypertension), they are blood vessels, not fluid accumulation.\n\t• Collateral formation: This is the biological process where the body grows alternative blood pathways (collateral vessels) to detour around a blocked or high-pressure vein.\n\t• Edema: This is a broad, generic medical term for swelling caused by fluid trapped in any body tissue. While ascites is technically a localized type of edema, the term edema on its own usually refers to generalized swelling in the limbs (like ankle or leg edema) rather than the abdominal space.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Esophageal varices, Edema, Collateral formation"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:non-digestible food ingredients that encourage the growth of beneficial bacteria::::prebiotic', 'non-digestible food ingredients that encourage the growth of beneficial bacteria:', 'Prebiotic', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Prebiotics are substrates that are selectively utilized by host microorganisms and provide a health benefit. Many are non-digestible carbohydrates, such as certain oligosaccharides and inulin-type fructans, that reach the colon and are fermented by beneficial microorganisms. This fermentation can produce short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs), which contribute to intestinal and metabolic health. Thus, the description in the question corresponds to a prebiotic, not a probiotic. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Prebiotic\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Prebiotics are substrates that are selectively utilized by host microorganisms and provide a health benefit. Many are non-digestible carbohydrates, such as certain oligosaccharides and inulin-type fructans, that reach the colon and are fermented by beneficial microorganisms. This fermentation can produce short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs), which contribute to intestinal and metabolic health. Thus, the description in the question corresponds to a prebiotic, not a probiotic. \n\nFor NDLE purposes, distinguish the three terms clearly: \n\nPrebiotics are substrates that beneficial microorganisms use; \nProbiotics are live microorganisms that provide a health benefit when administered in adequate amounts; and \nMicrobiota refers to the community of microorganisms living in a particular environment, such as the gastrointestinal tract.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Probiotic, Microbiota, None of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:drug that may cause nausea and vomiting:::digitalis', 'drug that may cause nausea and vomiting', 'Digitalis', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Nausea and vomiting are the classic, earliest gastrointestinal hallmarks of digitalis toxicity (e.g., from digoxin). Digitalis works by inhibiting the Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase pump to treat heart failure and arrhythmias, but it has a very narrow therapeutic window.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Digitalis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Nausea and vomiting are the classic, earliest gastrointestinal hallmarks of digitalis toxicity (e.g., from digoxin). Digitalis works by inhibiting the Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase pump to treat heart failure and arrhythmias, but it has a very narrow therapeutic window.\n\nWhen digitalis levels become elevated in the blood, the drug directly stimulates the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) in the medulla oblongata of the brainstem, which triggers a powerful urge to vomit. It is also frequently accompanied by neurological symptoms like anorexia, confusion, and classic visual disturbances (such as a yellow-green halo or blurred vision).\n\n\t• Antacids: Are primarily used to neutralize gastric acid and help alleviate nausea associated with indigestion or GERD rather than causing it.\n\t• Laxatives: Typically cause abdominal cramping, bloating, and diarrhea rather than upper GI symptoms like vomiting.\n\t• Amphetamines: While stimulants can occasionally cause nausea due to sympathetic nervous system activation, it is not a hallmark warning sign of toxicity or a key diagnostic indicator in the way it is uniquely tied to digitalis management in medical examinations.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Antacid, Laxative, Amphetamines"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:drug that may cause nausea and vomiting:::', 'drug that may cause nausea and vomiting', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Nausea and vomiting are the classic, earliest gastrointestinal hallmarks of digitalis toxicity (e.g., from digoxin). Digitalis works by inhibiting the Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase pump to treat heart failure and arrhythmias, but it has a very narrow therapeutic window.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Digitalis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Nausea and vomiting are the classic, earliest gastrointestinal hallmarks of digitalis toxicity (e.g., from digoxin). Digitalis works by inhibiting the Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase pump to treat heart failure and arrhythmias, but it has a very narrow therapeutic window.\n\nWhen digitalis levels become elevated in the blood, the drug directly stimulates the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) in the medulla oblongata of the brainstem, which triggers a powerful urge to vomit. It is also frequently accompanied by neurological symptoms like anorexia, confusion, and classic visual disturbances (such as a yellow-green halo or blurred vision).\n\n\t• Antacids: Are primarily used to neutralize gastric acid and help alleviate nausea associated with indigestion or GERD rather than causing it.\n\t• Laxatives: Typically cause abdominal cramping, bloating, and diarrhea rather than upper GI symptoms like vomiting.\n\t• Amphetamines: While stimulants can occasionally cause nausea due to sympathetic nervous system activation, it is not a hallmark warning sign of toxicity or a key diagnostic indicator in the way it is uniquely tied to digitalis management in medical examinations.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Antacid, Laxative, Amphetamines"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:for cancer patients in chemotherapy treatment, the following medical nutrition therapy helps in the management of dysgeusia, except::::avoid drinking fluids with meals', 'for cancer patients in chemotherapy treatment, the following medical nutrition therapy helps in the management of dysgeusia, except:', 'Avoid drinking fluids with meals', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Patients with dysgeusia benefit from good oral hygiene, mouth rinses, and sugar-free gum to stimulate saliva. Avoiding fluids during meals is generally recommended for early satiety or dumping syndrome, not for taste alterations.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Avoid drinking fluids with meals\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Patients with dysgeusia benefit from good oral hygiene, mouth rinses, and sugar-free gum to stimulate saliva. Avoiding fluids during meals is generally recommended for early satiety or dumping syndrome, not for taste alterations.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Use of mouth rinse, Chewing sugar-free gums and mints, SFF"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:for cancer patients in chemotherapy treatment, the following medical nutrition therapy helps in the management of dysgeusia, except::::', 'for cancer patients in chemotherapy treatment, the following medical nutrition therapy helps in the management of dysgeusia, except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Patients with dysgeusia benefit from good oral hygiene, mouth rinses, and sugar-free gum to stimulate saliva. Avoiding fluids during meals is generally recommended for early satiety or dumping syndrome, not for taste alterations.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Avoid drinking fluids with meals\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Patients with dysgeusia benefit from good oral hygiene, mouth rinses, and sugar-free gum to stimulate saliva. Avoiding fluids during meals is generally recommended for early satiety or dumping syndrome, not for taste alterations.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Use of mouth rinse, Chewing sugar-free gums and mints, SFF"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:common side effect of gastric resection::::dumping syndrome', 'common side effect of gastric resection:', 'Dumping syndrome', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dumping syndrome is one of the most common complications following partial or total gastric resection, particularly after procedures such as Billroth I, Billroth II, Roux-en-Y reconstruction, and sleeve gastrectomy. It occurs because the stomach loses part of its reservoir function, allowing hyperosmolar food—especially those rich in simple carbohydrates—to empty rapidly into the small intestine. There are two recognized forms: ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Dumping syndrome\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dumping syndrome is one of the most common complications following partial or total gastric resection, particularly after procedures such as Billroth I, Billroth II, Roux-en-Y reconstruction, and sleeve gastrectomy. It occurs because the stomach loses part of its reservoir function, allowing hyperosmolar food—especially those rich in simple carbohydrates—to empty rapidly into the small intestine. There are two recognized forms: \n\n• Early dumping syndrome (10–30 minutes after eating): Characterized by abdominal cramps, nausea, diarrhea, bloating, flushing, tachycardia, dizziness, and hypotension due to rapid fluid shifts from the bloodstream into the intestinal lumen. \n• Late dumping syndrome (1–3 hours after eating): Results from an exaggerated insulin response following rapid glucose absorption, leading to reactive hypoglycemia with symptoms such as sweating, weakness, tremors, hunger, and confusion. \n\nMedical nutrition therapy (MNT) is the primary treatment and includes: \n• Eating small, frequent meals \n• Limiting foods high in simple sugars \n• Increasing protein and moderate fat intake \n• Consuming soluble fiber when appropriate \n• Separating liquids from meals by about 30 minutes \n• Resting in a semi-recumbent position after meals in some patients \n\nPersistent symptoms that do not respond to dietary modification may require pharmacologic therapy (e.g., acarbose for late dumping syndrome or octreotide for severe cases).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Blind loop syndrome, Dysphagia, Fistula formation"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:common side effect of gastric resection::::', 'common side effect of gastric resection:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dumping syndrome is one of the most common complications following partial or total gastric resection, particularly after procedures such as Billroth I, Billroth II, Roux-en-Y reconstruction, and sleeve gastrectomy. It occurs because the stomach loses part of its reservoir function, allowing hyperosmolar food—especially those rich in simple carbohydrates—to empty rapidly into the small intestine. There are two recognized forms: ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Dumping syndrome\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dumping syndrome is one of the most common complications following partial or total gastric resection, particularly after procedures such as Billroth I, Billroth II, Roux-en-Y reconstruction, and sleeve gastrectomy. It occurs because the stomach loses part of its reservoir function, allowing hyperosmolar food—especially those rich in simple carbohydrates—to empty rapidly into the small intestine. There are two recognized forms: \n\n• Early dumping syndrome (10–30 minutes after eating): Characterized by abdominal cramps, nausea, diarrhea, bloating, flushing, tachycardia, dizziness, and hypotension due to rapid fluid shifts from the bloodstream into the intestinal lumen. \n• Late dumping syndrome (1–3 hours after eating): Results from an exaggerated insulin response following rapid glucose absorption, leading to reactive hypoglycemia with symptoms such as sweating, weakness, tremors, hunger, and confusion. \n\nMedical nutrition therapy (MNT) is the primary treatment and includes: \n• Eating small, frequent meals \n• Limiting foods high in simple sugars \n• Increasing protein and moderate fat intake \n• Consuming soluble fiber when appropriate \n• Separating liquids from meals by about 30 minutes \n• Resting in a semi-recumbent position after meals in some patients \n\nPersistent symptoms that do not respond to dietary modification may require pharmacologic therapy (e.g., acarbose for late dumping syndrome or octreotide for severe cases).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Blind loop syndrome, Dysphagia, Fistula formation"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:dietary management of gastric secretory disturbances includes the following except::::high fat diet', 'dietary management of gastric secretory disturbances includes the following except:', 'High fat diet', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Gastric secretory disturbances refer to conditions involving abnormal gastric acid secretion, such as gastritis, peptic ulcer disease (PUD), hyperchlorhydria, and gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD). The primary goals of nutrition therapy are to minimize gastric irritation, reduce symptoms, promote healing, and maintain adequate nutritional status. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"High fat diet\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Gastric secretory disturbances refer to conditions involving abnormal gastric acid secretion, such as gastritis, peptic ulcer disease (PUD), hyperchlorhydria, and gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD). The primary goals of nutrition therapy are to minimize gastric irritation, reduce symptoms, promote healing, and maintain adequate nutritional status. \n\nA high-fat diet is generally not recommended because fat delays gastric emptying and may stimulate gastric acid secretion, prolong gastric distention, and worsen symptoms such as epigastric discomfort, reflux, and indigestion. Therefore, meals are usually moderate to low in fat, depending on the patient''s tolerance.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Complex carbohydrate, Low fat diet, Low protein"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:dietary management of gastric secretory disturbances includes the following except::::', 'dietary management of gastric secretory disturbances includes the following except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Gastric secretory disturbances refer to conditions involving abnormal gastric acid secretion, such as gastritis, peptic ulcer disease (PUD), hyperchlorhydria, and gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD). The primary goals of nutrition therapy are to minimize gastric irritation, reduce symptoms, promote healing, and maintain adequate nutritional status. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"High fat diet\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Gastric secretory disturbances refer to conditions involving abnormal gastric acid secretion, such as gastritis, peptic ulcer disease (PUD), hyperchlorhydria, and gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD). The primary goals of nutrition therapy are to minimize gastric irritation, reduce symptoms, promote healing, and maintain adequate nutritional status. \n\nA high-fat diet is generally not recommended because fat delays gastric emptying and may stimulate gastric acid secretion, prolong gastric distention, and worsen symptoms such as epigastric discomfort, reflux, and indigestion. Therefore, meals are usually moderate to low in fat, depending on the patient''s tolerance.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Complex carbohydrate, Low fat diet, Low protein"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what should be done when a known diabetic patient is undergoing hypoglycemia?:::give 1 tbsp sugar, then wait for 15 minutes', 'what should be done when a known diabetic patient is undergoing hypoglycemia?', 'Give 1 tbsp sugar, then wait for 15 minutes', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"For a conscious patient with hypoglycemia who can safely swallow, the recommended immediate treatment is the 15-15 rule: provide approximately 15 g of fast-acting carbohydrate, then recheck blood glucose after 15 minutes. If blood glucose remains low, repeat the treatment. One tablespoon of table sugar provides approximately 12–15 g of carbohydrate, making it the best answer among the choices.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Give 1 tbsp sugar, then wait for 15 minutes\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"For a conscious patient with hypoglycemia who can safely swallow, the recommended immediate treatment is the 15-15 rule: provide approximately 15 g of fast-acting carbohydrate, then recheck blood glucose after 15 minutes. If blood glucose remains low, repeat the treatment. One tablespoon of table sugar provides approximately 12–15 g of carbohydrate, making it the best answer among the choices.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Give a cup of water to rehydrate, Inject an insulin short, Provide 1 pc candy"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what should be done when a known diabetic patient is undergoing hypoglycemia?:::', 'what should be done when a known diabetic patient is undergoing hypoglycemia?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"For a conscious patient with hypoglycemia who can safely swallow, the recommended immediate treatment is the 15-15 rule: provide approximately 15 g of fast-acting carbohydrate, then recheck blood glucose after 15 minutes. If blood glucose remains low, repeat the treatment. One tablespoon of table sugar provides approximately 12–15 g of carbohydrate, making it the best answer among the choices.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Give 1 tbsp sugar, then wait for 15 minutes\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"For a conscious patient with hypoglycemia who can safely swallow, the recommended immediate treatment is the 15-15 rule: provide approximately 15 g of fast-acting carbohydrate, then recheck blood glucose after 15 minutes. If blood glucose remains low, repeat the treatment. One tablespoon of table sugar provides approximately 12–15 g of carbohydrate, making it the best answer among the choices.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Give a cup of water to rehydrate, Inject an insulin short, Provide 1 pc candy"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:how many mg of potassium is contained in 40 meq?:::1560 mg k', 'how many mg of potassium is contained in 40 meq?', '1560 mg K', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"For potassium (K⁺), the conversion between mEq and mg is: mg = mEq * (atomic weight ÷ valence) ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"1560 mg K\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"For potassium (K⁺), the conversion between mEq and mg is: mg = mEq * (atomic weight ÷ valence) \n\nPotassium has an atomic weight of approximately 39 mg/mmol and a valence of +1. \n\nTherefore: 40 mEq * (39mg ÷ 1) = 1560 mg K \n\nThus, 40 mEq of potassium = 1,560 mg K. This conversion is clinically important because electrolytes may be prescribed or reported in mEq, while nutrition labels, laboratory references, and calculations may use mg.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 1500 mg K, 2000 mg K, 1864 mg K"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:how many mg of potassium is contained in 40 meq?:::', 'how many mg of potassium is contained in 40 meq?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"For potassium (K⁺), the conversion between mEq and mg is: mg = mEq * (atomic weight ÷ valence) ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"1560 mg K\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"For potassium (K⁺), the conversion between mEq and mg is: mg = mEq * (atomic weight ÷ valence) \n\nPotassium has an atomic weight of approximately 39 mg/mmol and a valence of +1. \n\nTherefore: 40 mEq * (39mg ÷ 1) = 1560 mg K \n\nThus, 40 mEq of potassium = 1,560 mg K. This conversion is clinically important because electrolytes may be prescribed or reported in mEq, while nutrition labels, laboratory references, and calculations may use mg.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 1500 mg K, 2000 mg K, 1864 mg K"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:inherent na in the diet is 950 mg. the diet rx states that your patient is allowed 90 meq/day. how much is the salt is to be added to meet the diet rx?:::2.8 grams', 'inherent na in the diet is 950 mg. the diet rx states that your patient is allowed 90 meq/day. how much is the salt is to be added to meet the diet rx?', '2.8 grams', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Step 1: Convert the Diet Rx from mEq to milligrams (mg) of Sodium","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"2.8 grams\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Step 1: Convert the Diet Rx from mEq to milligrams (mg) of Sodium\n\t• Rule: 1 mEq of Sodium (Na) = 23 mg\n\t• Calculation: 90 mEq x 23 mg/mEq = 2,070 mg of total allowed Sodium\n\nStep 2: Calculate the additional Sodium needed\n\t• Subtract the inherent sodium already in the food from the total allowed sodium.\n\t• Calculation: 2,070 mg (Total Allowed) - 950 mg (Inherent) = 1,120 mg of additional Sodium needed\n\nStep 3: Convert the additional Sodium to grams of Table Salt (NaCl)\n\t• Rule: Table salt is 40% sodium (0.40). To find the amount of salt, divide the sodium milligrams by 0.40.\n\t• Calculation: 1,120 mg / 0.40 = 2,800 mg of salt\n\t• Convert to grams: 2,800 mg / 1,000 = 2.8 grams of salt","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 2.0 grams, 4.3 grams, 5.0 grams"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:inherent na in the diet is 950 mg. the diet rx states that your patient is allowed 90 meq/day. how much is the salt is to be added to meet the diet rx?:::', 'inherent na in the diet is 950 mg. the diet rx states that your patient is allowed 90 meq/day. how much is the salt is to be added to meet the diet rx?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Step 1: Convert the Diet Rx from mEq to milligrams (mg) of Sodium","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"2.8 grams\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Step 1: Convert the Diet Rx from mEq to milligrams (mg) of Sodium\n\t• Rule: 1 mEq of Sodium (Na) = 23 mg\n\t• Calculation: 90 mEq x 23 mg/mEq = 2,070 mg of total allowed Sodium\n\nStep 2: Calculate the additional Sodium needed\n\t• Subtract the inherent sodium already in the food from the total allowed sodium.\n\t• Calculation: 2,070 mg (Total Allowed) - 950 mg (Inherent) = 1,120 mg of additional Sodium needed\n\nStep 3: Convert the additional Sodium to grams of Table Salt (NaCl)\n\t• Rule: Table salt is 40% sodium (0.40). To find the amount of salt, divide the sodium milligrams by 0.40.\n\t• Calculation: 1,120 mg / 0.40 = 2,800 mg of salt\n\t• Convert to grams: 2,800 mg / 1,000 = 2.8 grams of salt","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 2.0 grams, 4.3 grams, 5.0 grams"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is a free food?:::tea', 'which of the following is a free food?', 'Tea', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In clinical dietetics and food exchange systems, a free food is defined as any food or beverage that contains negligible amounts of macronutrients—specifically less than 20 calories and 5 grams or less of carbohydrates per serving.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Tea\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In clinical dietetics and food exchange systems, a free food is defined as any food or beverage that contains negligible amounts of macronutrients—specifically less than 20 calories and 5 grams or less of carbohydrates per serving.\n\nUnsweetened black, green, or herbal tea is virtually calorie-free and carbohydrate-free. Because it does not cause any significant spike in blood glucose or daily caloric intake, it can be consumed freely by patients (such as those managing diabetes or weight) without needing to be calculated or swapped out in a meal plan.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Peanut brittle, Soft drinks, All of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is a free food?:::', 'which of the following is a free food?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In clinical dietetics and food exchange systems, a free food is defined as any food or beverage that contains negligible amounts of macronutrients—specifically less than 20 calories and 5 grams or less of carbohydrates per serving.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Tea\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In clinical dietetics and food exchange systems, a free food is defined as any food or beverage that contains negligible amounts of macronutrients—specifically less than 20 calories and 5 grams or less of carbohydrates per serving.\n\nUnsweetened black, green, or herbal tea is virtually calorie-free and carbohydrate-free. Because it does not cause any significant spike in blood glucose or daily caloric intake, it can be consumed freely by patients (such as those managing diabetes or weight) without needing to be calculated or swapped out in a meal plan.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Peanut brittle, Soft drinks, All of the above"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the bmi of a 5''4" individual with an actual weight of 60 kg?:::22.7 kg/m²', 'what is the bmi of a 5''4" individual with an actual weight of 60 kg?', '22.7  kg/m²', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"BMI is calculated using: Weight in kg ÷ Height in m² ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"22.7  kg/m²\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"BMI is calculated using: Weight in kg ÷ Height in m² \n\nFirst, convert 5''4\" to meters: 5’4” = 64 inches = 1.6256 meters \n\nThen calculate BMI: 60 kg ÷ (1.6256)² = 60 ÷ 2.6426 = ~22.7 kg/m²","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 22  kg/m², 23  kg/m², 25  kg/m²"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the bmi of a 5''4" individual with an actual weight of 60 kg?:::', 'what is the bmi of a 5''4" individual with an actual weight of 60 kg?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"BMI is calculated using: Weight in kg ÷ Height in m² ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"22.7  kg/m²\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"BMI is calculated using: Weight in kg ÷ Height in m² \n\nFirst, convert 5''4\" to meters: 5’4” = 64 inches = 1.6256 meters \n\nThen calculate BMI: 60 kg ÷ (1.6256)² = 60 ÷ 2.6426 = ~22.7 kg/m²","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 22  kg/m², 23  kg/m², 25  kg/m²"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following statements is false?:::none of the above', 'which of the following statements is false?', 'None of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"All three statements are considered true based on commonly used Philippine food-composition references for purine content. Purines are naturally occurring compounds found in many foods. They are metabolized to uric acid, making dietary purine intake particularly relevant in the nutritional management of gout and hyperuricemia. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"None of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"All three statements are considered true based on commonly used Philippine food-composition references for purine content. Purines are naturally occurring compounds found in many foods. They are metabolized to uric acid, making dietary purine intake particularly relevant in the nutritional management of gout and hyperuricemia. \n\n• Tamban (sardines) is a fish with relatively high purine content. \n• Dried beans also contain purines, but their purine content may be lower than that of certain high-purine fish such as sardines. \n• Chicken organ meats, including gizzard and intestine, contain substantial amounts of purines. \n• Pork liver is a high-purine organ meat and has a higher purine content than pork blood in the referenced food data.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Tamban has higher purine content as compared to dried beans., Chicken gizzard has about the same purine content of that of the chicken intestine., Pork liver has higher purine content as compared to pork blood"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb)
