('mc:vitamin c (ascorbic acid) is an essential cofactor for which enzymatic reaction during collagen biosynthesis?:::hydroxylation of proline and lysine residues', 'vitamin c (ascorbic acid) is an essential cofactor for which enzymatic reaction during collagen biosynthesis?', 'Hydroxylation of Proline and Lysine residues', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Ascorbic acid maintains iron in its ferrous (Fe2+) state, serving as a reducing agent for prolyl hydroxylase and lysyl hydroxylase. Without it, defective triple-helix collagen fibers fail to crosslink, leading to Scurvy (fragile capillaries, bleeding gums, impaired wound healing).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Hydroxylation of Proline and Lysine residues\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Ascorbic acid maintains iron in its ferrous (Fe2+) state, serving as a reducing agent for prolyl hydroxylase and lysyl hydroxylase. Without it, defective triple-helix collagen fibers fail to crosslink, leading to Scurvy (fragile capillaries, bleeding gums, impaired wound healing).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Decarboxylation of Glutamate to GABA, Methylation of Homocysteine to Methionine, Transamination of Alanine to Pyruvate"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:vitamin c (ascorbic acid) is an essential cofactor for which enzymatic reaction during collagen biosynthesis?:::', 'vitamin c (ascorbic acid) is an essential cofactor for which enzymatic reaction during collagen biosynthesis?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Ascorbic acid maintains iron in its ferrous (Fe2+) state, serving as a reducing agent for prolyl hydroxylase and lysyl hydroxylase. Without it, defective triple-helix collagen fibers fail to crosslink, leading to Scurvy (fragile capillaries, bleeding gums, impaired wound healing).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Hydroxylation of Proline and Lysine residues\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Ascorbic acid maintains iron in its ferrous (Fe2+) state, serving as a reducing agent for prolyl hydroxylase and lysyl hydroxylase. Without it, defective triple-helix collagen fibers fail to crosslink, leading to Scurvy (fragile capillaries, bleeding gums, impaired wound healing).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Decarboxylation of Glutamate to GABA, Methylation of Homocysteine to Methionine, Transamination of Alanine to Pyruvate"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:where is the vitamin b12 (cobalamin) – intrinsic factor complex primarily absorbed in the human gastrointestinal tract?:::terminal ileum', 'where is the vitamin b12 (cobalamin) – intrinsic factor complex primarily absorbed in the human gastrointestinal tract?', 'Terminal Ileum', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dietary B12 binds haptocorrin in saliva/gastric juice, is transferred to gastric parietal cell-secreted Intrinsic Factor (IF) in the duodenum, and the intact B12-IF complex is absorbed via cubam receptors in the Terminal Ileum.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Terminal Ileum\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dietary B12 binds haptocorrin in saliva/gastric juice, is transferred to gastric parietal cell-secreted Intrinsic Factor (IF) in the duodenum, and the intact B12-IF complex is absorbed via cubam receptors in the Terminal Ileum.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Duodenum, Proximal Jejunum, Ascending Colon"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:where is the vitamin b12 (cobalamin) – intrinsic factor complex primarily absorbed in the human gastrointestinal tract?:::', 'where is the vitamin b12 (cobalamin) – intrinsic factor complex primarily absorbed in the human gastrointestinal tract?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dietary B12 binds haptocorrin in saliva/gastric juice, is transferred to gastric parietal cell-secreted Intrinsic Factor (IF) in the duodenum, and the intact B12-IF complex is absorbed via cubam receptors in the Terminal Ileum.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Terminal Ileum\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dietary B12 binds haptocorrin in saliva/gastric juice, is transferred to gastric parietal cell-secreted Intrinsic Factor (IF) in the duodenum, and the intact B12-IF complex is absorbed via cubam receptors in the Terminal Ileum.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Duodenum, Proximal Jejunum, Ascending Colon"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which amino acid serves as the biological precursor for endogenous synthesis of niacin (nad/nadp)?:::tryptophan', 'which amino acid serves as the biological precursor for endogenous synthesis of niacin (nad/nadp)?', 'Tryptophan', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Tryptophan is converted to nicotinic acid mononucleotide via the kynurenine pathway (requiring Vitamin B6, B2, and iron as cofactors). The conversion ratio is approximately 60 mg of dietary tryptophan = 1 mg of Niacin Equivalent (NE).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Tryptophan\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Tryptophan is converted to nicotinic acid mononucleotide via the kynurenine pathway (requiring Vitamin B6, B2, and iron as cofactors). The conversion ratio is approximately 60 mg of dietary tryptophan = 1 mg of Niacin Equivalent (NE).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Tyrosine, Phenylalanine, Methionine"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which amino acid serves as the biological precursor for endogenous synthesis of niacin (nad/nadp)?:::', 'which amino acid serves as the biological precursor for endogenous synthesis of niacin (nad/nadp)?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Tryptophan is converted to nicotinic acid mononucleotide via the kynurenine pathway (requiring Vitamin B6, B2, and iron as cofactors). The conversion ratio is approximately 60 mg of dietary tryptophan = 1 mg of Niacin Equivalent (NE).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Tryptophan\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Tryptophan is converted to nicotinic acid mononucleotide via the kynurenine pathway (requiring Vitamin B6, B2, and iron as cofactors). The conversion ratio is approximately 60 mg of dietary tryptophan = 1 mg of Niacin Equivalent (NE).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Tyrosine, Phenylalanine, Methionine"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the classic dietary fat to combined carbohydrate + protein ratio used in the ketogenic diet for refractory pediatric epilepsy?:::', 'what is the classic dietary fat to combined carbohydrate + protein ratio used in the ketogenic diet for refractory pediatric epilepsy?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The classic ketogenic diet uses a 4:1 ratio of fat (in grams) to combined carbohydrate and protein (in grams), providing ~90% of calories from fat to induce and sustain ketosis, providing ketone bodies (beta-hydroxybutyrate) as the primary cerebral fuel.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"4:1 (or 3:1) ratio\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The classic ketogenic diet uses a 4:1 ratio of fat (in grams) to combined carbohydrate and protein (in grams), providing ~90% of calories from fat to induce and sustain ketosis, providing ketone bodies (beta-hydroxybutyrate) as the primary cerebral fuel.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 1:1 ratio, 2:1 ratio, 5:2 ratio"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the recommended protein intake for a non-dialysis chronic kidney disease (ckd) patient in stage 4 (gfr 15–29 ml/min)?:::0.6 to 0.8 g/kg body weight/day', 'what is the recommended protein intake for a non-dialysis chronic kidney disease (ckd) patient in stage 4 (gfr 15–29 ml/min)?', '0.6 to 0.8 g/kg body weight/day', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"For CKD Stages 3–5 (non-dialysis), protein is restricted to 0.6–0.8 g/kg/day to slow renal disease progression and decrease uremic toxin accumulation. Once on hemodialysis, protein requirement increases to 1.2–1.4 g/kg/day due to dialysate losses.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"0.6 to 0.8 g/kg body weight/day\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"For CKD Stages 3–5 (non-dialysis), protein is restricted to 0.6–0.8 g/kg/day to slow renal disease progression and decrease uremic toxin accumulation. Once on hemodialysis, protein requirement increases to 1.2–1.4 g/kg/day due to dialysate losses.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 1.2 to 1.4 g/kg body weight/day, 1.5 to 2.0 g/kg body weight/day, 0.3 to 0.5 g/kg body weight/day"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
