('mc:what is the maximum osmolarity cutoff for peripheral parenteral nutrition (ppn) infusion to avoid phlebitis?:::900 mosm/l', 'what is the maximum osmolarity cutoff for peripheral parenteral nutrition (ppn) infusion to avoid phlebitis?', '900 mOsm/L', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Peripheral veins can tolerate solutions up to ~900 mOsm/L. Solutions exceeding 900 mOsm/L are hypertonic and must be infused via Central Parenteral Nutrition (CPN / TPN) through a large central vein (e.g., subclavian/jugular) where high blood flow rapidly dilutes the solution.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"900 mOsm/L\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Peripheral veins can tolerate solutions up to ~900 mOsm/L. Solutions exceeding 900 mOsm/L are hypertonic and must be infused via Central Parenteral Nutrition (CPN / TPN) through a large central vein (e.g., subclavian/jugular) where high blood flow rapidly dilutes the solution.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 500 mOsm/L, 1500 mOsm/L, 300 mOsm/L"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the maximum osmolarity cutoff for peripheral parenteral nutrition (ppn) infusion to avoid phlebitis?:::', 'what is the maximum osmolarity cutoff for peripheral parenteral nutrition (ppn) infusion to avoid phlebitis?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Peripheral veins can tolerate solutions up to ~900 mOsm/L. Solutions exceeding 900 mOsm/L are hypertonic and must be infused via Central Parenteral Nutrition (CPN / TPN) through a large central vein (e.g., subclavian/jugular) where high blood flow rapidly dilutes the solution.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"900 mOsm/L\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Peripheral veins can tolerate solutions up to ~900 mOsm/L. Solutions exceeding 900 mOsm/L are hypertonic and must be infused via Central Parenteral Nutrition (CPN / TPN) through a large central vein (e.g., subclavian/jugular) where high blood flow rapidly dilutes the solution.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 500 mOsm/L, 1500 mOsm/L, 300 mOsm/L"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the classic dietary fat to combined carbohydrate + protein ratio used in the ketogenic diet for refractory pediatric epilepsy?:::4:1 (or 3:1) ratio', 'what is the classic dietary fat to combined carbohydrate + protein ratio used in the ketogenic diet for refractory pediatric epilepsy?', '4:1 (or 3:1) ratio', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The classic ketogenic diet uses a 4:1 ratio of fat (in grams) to combined carbohydrate and protein (in grams), providing ~90% of calories from fat to induce and sustain ketosis, providing ketone bodies (beta-hydroxybutyrate) as the primary cerebral fuel.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"4:1 (or 3:1) ratio\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The classic ketogenic diet uses a 4:1 ratio of fat (in grams) to combined carbohydrate and protein (in grams), providing ~90% of calories from fat to induce and sustain ketosis, providing ketone bodies (beta-hydroxybutyrate) as the primary cerebral fuel.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 1:1 ratio, 2:1 ratio, 5:2 ratio"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what sodium limit is recommended in the strict dash (dietary approaches to stop hypertension) diet trial?:::1,500 mg/day (lower limit) or 2,300 mg/day (standard)', 'what sodium limit is recommended in the strict dash (dietary approaches to stop hypertension) diet trial?', '1,500 mg/day (Lower limit) or 2,300 mg/day (Standard)', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The standard DASH diet limits sodium to 2,300 mg/day, while the lower-sodium DASH plan caps sodium at 1,500 mg/day. It emphasizes potassium, calcium, magnesium, fiber, and lean proteins.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"1,500 mg/day (Lower limit) or 2,300 mg/day (Standard)\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The standard DASH diet limits sodium to 2,300 mg/day, while the lower-sodium DASH plan caps sodium at 1,500 mg/day. It emphasizes potassium, calcium, magnesium, fiber, and lean proteins.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 500 mg/day, 3,500 mg/day, 1,000 mg/day max"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what sodium limit is recommended in the strict dash (dietary approaches to stop hypertension) diet trial?:::', 'what sodium limit is recommended in the strict dash (dietary approaches to stop hypertension) diet trial?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The standard DASH diet limits sodium to 2,300 mg/day, while the lower-sodium DASH plan caps sodium at 1,500 mg/day. It emphasizes potassium, calcium, magnesium, fiber, and lean proteins.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"1,500 mg/day (Lower limit) or 2,300 mg/day (Standard)\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The standard DASH diet limits sodium to 2,300 mg/day, while the lower-sodium DASH plan caps sodium at 1,500 mg/day. It emphasizes potassium, calcium, magnesium, fiber, and lean proteins.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 500 mg/day, 3,500 mg/day, 1,000 mg/day max"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:development which starts in the central axis and progresses towards the extremities:::proximodistal', 'development which starts in the central axis and progresses towards the extremities', 'Proximodistal', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In human growth and development, the proximodistal pattern dictates that physical development and motor control progress from the center of the body outward toward the extremities (periphery). For example, a developing fetus grows its torso and shoulder buds before its arms, hands, and fingers. Similarly, an infant gains muscle control over their trunk and arms before they master the fine motor control of their fingers.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Proximodistal\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In human growth and development, the proximodistal pattern dictates that physical development and motor control progress from the center of the body outward toward the extremities (periphery). For example, a developing fetus grows its torso and shoulder buds before its arms, hands, and fingers. Similarly, an infant gains muscle control over their trunk and arms before they master the fine motor control of their fingers.\n\n• Cephalocaudal: This describes development that moves from head-to-toe. A fetus''s head develops much faster than its lower body, and an infant learns to hold their head up before they learn to sit up or walk.\n• Hypertrophy and Hyperplasia: These are specific cellular growth mechanisms (increase in cell size and cell number, respectively) rather than directional paths of physical development.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Cephalocaudal, Hyperplasia, Hypertropy"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:development which starts in the central axis and progresses towards the extremities:::', 'development which starts in the central axis and progresses towards the extremities', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In human growth and development, the proximodistal pattern dictates that physical development and motor control progress from the center of the body outward toward the extremities (periphery). For example, a developing fetus grows its torso and shoulder buds before its arms, hands, and fingers. Similarly, an infant gains muscle control over their trunk and arms before they master the fine motor control of their fingers.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Proximodistal\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In human growth and development, the proximodistal pattern dictates that physical development and motor control progress from the center of the body outward toward the extremities (periphery). For example, a developing fetus grows its torso and shoulder buds before its arms, hands, and fingers. Similarly, an infant gains muscle control over their trunk and arms before they master the fine motor control of their fingers.\n\n• Cephalocaudal: This describes development that moves from head-to-toe. A fetus''s head develops much faster than its lower body, and an infant learns to hold their head up before they learn to sit up or walk.\n• Hypertrophy and Hyperplasia: These are specific cellular growth mechanisms (increase in cell size and cell number, respectively) rather than directional paths of physical development.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Cephalocaudal, Hyperplasia, Hypertropy"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the estimated weight of the amniotic fluid of a pregnant woman?:::2 lbs', 'what is the estimated weight of the amniotic fluid of a pregnant woman?', '2 lbs', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When breaking down the component weight gain of a full-term pregnancy using clinical reference values (totaling 12.5 kg or ~27.5 lbs), the components weigh out as follows:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"2 lbs\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When breaking down the component weight gain of a full-term pregnancy using clinical reference values (totaling 12.5 kg or ~27.5 lbs), the components weigh out as follows:\n\n• Amniotic Fluid: ~1.8 lbs (0.80 kg) — which rounds to 2 lbs\n• Baby (Fetus): ~7.5 lbs (3.40 kg) — note: this is likely where the answer key confused \"7 lbs\"\n• Maternal Fat Stores: ~7.4 lbs (3.35 kg)\n• Extracellular Extravascular Fluid: ~3.3 lbs (1.48 kg)\n• Blood Volume: ~3.2 lbs (1.45 kg)\n• Maternal Tissue: ~3.0 lbs (1.38 kg)\n• Placenta: ~1.4 lbs (0.65 kg)","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 5 lbs, 10.5 lbs, 7 lbs"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a specialized organ formed during pregnancy which functions in removing fetal waste products:::placenta', 'a specialized organ formed during pregnancy which functions in removing fetal waste products', 'Placenta', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The placenta is a highly specialized, temporary organ that forms inside the uterus exclusively during pregnancy. It establishes an interface between the maternal and fetal circulatory systems without letting their blood mix directly.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Placenta\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The placenta is a highly specialized, temporary organ that forms inside the uterus exclusively during pregnancy. It establishes an interface between the maternal and fetal circulatory systems without letting their blood mix directly.\n\nDeoxygenated blood carrying metabolic waste products from the fetus (such as carbon dioxide, urea, and creatinine) travels to the placenta. The placenta filters these waste materials out of the fetal bloodstream and transfers them into the mother''s blood, where her kidneys and lungs excrete them. Essentially, the placenta acts as the temporary lungs, liver, and kidneys for the baby until birth.\n\n• Umbilical cord: This functions purely as a transport passageway or biological conduit. It contains blood vessels that physically carry waste to the placenta, but the cord itself does not process, filter, or remove the waste.\n• Amniotic sac: This is the fluid-filled \"bag of waters\" that surrounds, cushions, and thermally protects the fetus, but it plays no role in chemical waste extraction.\n• Uterus: This is the permanent muscular maternal organ that houses and protects the growing embryo and placenta, but it is not the actual specialized organ managing nutrient and waste exchange.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Amniotic sac, Umbilical cord, Uterus"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a specialized organ formed during pregnancy which functions in removing fetal waste products:::', 'a specialized organ formed during pregnancy which functions in removing fetal waste products', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The placenta is a highly specialized, temporary organ that forms inside the uterus exclusively during pregnancy. It establishes an interface between the maternal and fetal circulatory systems without letting their blood mix directly.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Placenta\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The placenta is a highly specialized, temporary organ that forms inside the uterus exclusively during pregnancy. It establishes an interface between the maternal and fetal circulatory systems without letting their blood mix directly.\n\nDeoxygenated blood carrying metabolic waste products from the fetus (such as carbon dioxide, urea, and creatinine) travels to the placenta. The placenta filters these waste materials out of the fetal bloodstream and transfers them into the mother''s blood, where her kidneys and lungs excrete them. Essentially, the placenta acts as the temporary lungs, liver, and kidneys for the baby until birth.\n\n• Umbilical cord: This functions purely as a transport passageway or biological conduit. It contains blood vessels that physically carry waste to the placenta, but the cord itself does not process, filter, or remove the waste.\n• Amniotic sac: This is the fluid-filled \"bag of waters\" that surrounds, cushions, and thermally protects the fetus, but it plays no role in chemical waste extraction.\n• Uterus: This is the permanent muscular maternal organ that houses and protects the growing embryo and placenta, but it is not the actual specialized organ managing nutrient and waste exchange.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Amniotic sac, Umbilical cord, Uterus"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:normally, an infant is able to meet its iron needs for the first 3 months from::::its own liver stores of iron', 'normally, an infant is able to meet its iron needs for the first 3 months from:', 'its own liver stores of iron', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"During the third trimester of pregnancy, there is a massive transfer of iron from the mother across the placenta to the fetus. This allows a healthy, full-term newborn to be born with a rich endowment of iron, which is primarily accumulated and stored in the fetal liver as ferritin and hemosiderin.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"its own liver stores of iron\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"During the third trimester of pregnancy, there is a massive transfer of iron from the mother across the placenta to the fetus. This allows a healthy, full-term newborn to be born with a rich endowment of iron, which is primarily accumulated and stored in the fetal liver as ferritin and hemosiderin.\n\nDuring the first two months of life, a newborn''s hemoglobin concentration drops naturally because they are transitioning from the low-oxygen intrauterine environment to normal air breathing. As these extra fetal red blood cells break down, the iron inside them is recycled and pushed directly into the infant''s hepatic (liver) stores. These accumulated stores safely meet 100% of the infant''s growth demands for the first 3 to 6 months of life.\n\n• The iron content in milk: Human breast milk contains exceptionally small, trace amounts of iron. While this minor amount is highly bioavailable, it is mathematically insufficient on its own to support rapid infant growth without the presence of the built-in hepatic reserves.\n• Supplements: Healthy, full-term infants do not routinely require iron supplements during the first 3 months of life. Standard guidelines (like those from the American Academy of Pediatrics) only advise starting daily supplemental iron drops at 4 months of age for exclusively breastfed infants as their liver stores begin to deplete.\n• Weaning food: Weaning foods (complementary solid foods) are strictly introduced at 6 months of age. An infant''s digestive and motor systems are completely unready for solid food within the first 3 months of life.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: the iron content in milk, supplements, weaning food"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:normally, an infant is able to meet its iron needs for the first 3 months from::::', 'normally, an infant is able to meet its iron needs for the first 3 months from:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"During the third trimester of pregnancy, there is a massive transfer of iron from the mother across the placenta to the fetus. This allows a healthy, full-term newborn to be born with a rich endowment of iron, which is primarily accumulated and stored in the fetal liver as ferritin and hemosiderin.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"its own liver stores of iron\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"During the third trimester of pregnancy, there is a massive transfer of iron from the mother across the placenta to the fetus. This allows a healthy, full-term newborn to be born with a rich endowment of iron, which is primarily accumulated and stored in the fetal liver as ferritin and hemosiderin.\n\nDuring the first two months of life, a newborn''s hemoglobin concentration drops naturally because they are transitioning from the low-oxygen intrauterine environment to normal air breathing. As these extra fetal red blood cells break down, the iron inside them is recycled and pushed directly into the infant''s hepatic (liver) stores. These accumulated stores safely meet 100% of the infant''s growth demands for the first 3 to 6 months of life.\n\n• The iron content in milk: Human breast milk contains exceptionally small, trace amounts of iron. While this minor amount is highly bioavailable, it is mathematically insufficient on its own to support rapid infant growth without the presence of the built-in hepatic reserves.\n• Supplements: Healthy, full-term infants do not routinely require iron supplements during the first 3 months of life. Standard guidelines (like those from the American Academy of Pediatrics) only advise starting daily supplemental iron drops at 4 months of age for exclusively breastfed infants as their liver stores begin to deplete.\n• Weaning food: Weaning foods (complementary solid foods) are strictly introduced at 6 months of age. An infant''s digestive and motor systems are completely unready for solid food within the first 3 months of life.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: the iron content in milk, supplements, weaning food"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:cooking an egg primarily causes its proteins to undergo::::', 'cooking an egg primarily causes its proteins to undergo:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Egg proteins denature and then coagulate during cooking, changing the egg from a fluid to a firmer structure.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Denaturation and coagulation\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Egg proteins denature and then coagulate during cooking, changing the egg from a fluid to a firmer structure.\n\n• Gelatinization is a starch phenomenon involving starch and water under heat.\n• Caramelization is a nonenzymatic browning reaction involving sugars.\n• Retrogradation refers primarily to reassociation of starch molecules after gelatinization.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Gelatinization, Retrogadation, Caramelization"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the estimated weight of the amniotic fluid of a pregnant woman?:::', 'what is the estimated weight of the amniotic fluid of a pregnant woman?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When breaking down the component weight gain of a full-term pregnancy using clinical reference values (totaling 12.5 kg or ~27.5 lbs), the components weigh out as follows:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"2 lbs\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When breaking down the component weight gain of a full-term pregnancy using clinical reference values (totaling 12.5 kg or ~27.5 lbs), the components weigh out as follows:\n\n• Amniotic Fluid: ~1.8 lbs (0.80 kg) — which rounds to 2 lbs\n• Baby (Fetus): ~7.5 lbs (3.40 kg) — note: this is likely where the answer key confused \"7 lbs\"\n• Maternal Fat Stores: ~7.4 lbs (3.35 kg)\n• Extracellular Extravascular Fluid: ~3.3 lbs (1.48 kg)\n• Blood Volume: ~3.2 lbs (1.45 kg)\n• Maternal Tissue: ~3.0 lbs (1.38 kg)\n• Placenta: ~1.4 lbs (0.65 kg)","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 5 lbs, 10.5 lbs, 7 lbs"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are dietary management for morning sickness during pregnancy except::::citrus juice, water, milk, coffee, or tea if experiencing nausea', 'the following are dietary management for morning sickness during pregnancy except:', 'Citrus juice, water, milk, coffee, or tea if experiencing nausea', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When actively experiencing nausea, drinking standard beverages like citrus juices, plain water, milk, coffee, or tea is heavily discouraged because they can stimulate the stomach lining or trigger the gag reflex. Citrus juice is highly acidic, milk is heavy, and coffee/tea contain caffeine, which acts as a gastric irritant.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Citrus juice, water, milk, coffee, or tea if experiencing nausea\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When actively experiencing nausea, drinking standard beverages like citrus juices, plain water, milk, coffee, or tea is heavily discouraged because they can stimulate the stomach lining or trigger the gag reflex. Citrus juice is highly acidic, milk is heavy, and coffee/tea contain caffeine, which acts as a gastric irritant.\n\nClinical guidelines state that when a woman is actively nauseated, she should instead sip carbonated beverages (like ginger ale), suck on ice chips, or use clear broths. Furthermore, liquids should always be consumed between meals rather than with solid foods to prevent over-distending the stomach.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Frequent small meals, Dry toast or crackers, Avoidance of foods with offensive odors"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a characteristic of complementary foods wherein its nutritional value is comparable with breast milk:::adequate', 'a characteristic of complementary foods wherein its nutritional value is comparable with breast milk', 'Adequate', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The World Health Organization (WHO) outlines four essential characteristics for complementary feeding (often referred to as the T.A.S.A. guidelines). Each has a distinct definition:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Adequate\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The World Health Organization (WHO) outlines four essential characteristics for complementary feeding (often referred to as the T.A.S.A. guidelines). Each has a distinct definition:\n\n• Adequate: This means the food is nutritionally balanced and sufficient. It provides the exact amount of energy, protein, macronutrients, and micronutrients necessary to bridge the gap and match or complement the high nutritional value of breast milk for a growing infant.\n• Timely: This means the foods are introduced at the correct developmental age—specifically when the infant''s nutritional demands exceed what exclusive breast milk can provide (at 6 months of age).\n• Safe: This means the foods are hygienically prepared, stored, and fed using clean hands and utensils to minimize the risk of contamination or diarrheal illness.\n• Appropriate: This means the foods are given in a texture, consistency, and frequency that match the child''s developmental and motor abilities (e.g., transitioning from pureed to mashed to finger foods).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Timely, Safe, Appropriate"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are hormones of lactation except::::', 'the following are hormones of lactation except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Direct Lactation Hormones:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Insulin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Direct Lactation Hormones:\n\t• Prolactin: The absolute primary hormone responsible for the synthesis and production of milk within the mammary alveoli.\n\t• Oxytocin: The hormone responsible for the milk ejection (let-down) reflex, causing the myoepithelial cells surrounding the alveoli to contract.\n\t• Estrogen: Crucial during pregnancy for mammogenesis (the structural development and branching of the milk ducts) to prepare the breasts for lactation.\n\nInsulin is a general metabolic hormone produced by the pancreas to regulate blood glucose homeostasis. While it exhibits minor cellular synergy to maintain healthy maternal tissue, it is an endocrine system baseline hormone rather than a primary, targeted hormone of lactation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Prolactin, Estrogen, Oxytocin"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a characteristic of complementary foods wherein its nutritional value is comparable with breast milk:::', 'a characteristic of complementary foods wherein its nutritional value is comparable with breast milk', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The World Health Organization (WHO) outlines four essential characteristics for complementary feeding (often referred to as the T.A.S.A. guidelines). Each has a distinct definition:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Adequate\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The World Health Organization (WHO) outlines four essential characteristics for complementary feeding (often referred to as the T.A.S.A. guidelines). Each has a distinct definition:\n\n• Adequate: This means the food is nutritionally balanced and sufficient. It provides the exact amount of energy, protein, macronutrients, and micronutrients necessary to bridge the gap and match or complement the high nutritional value of breast milk for a growing infant.\n• Timely: This means the foods are introduced at the correct developmental age—specifically when the infant''s nutritional demands exceed what exclusive breast milk can provide (at 6 months of age).\n• Safe: This means the foods are hygienically prepared, stored, and fed using clean hands and utensils to minimize the risk of contamination or diarrheal illness.\n• Appropriate: This means the foods are given in a texture, consistency, and frequency that match the child''s developmental and motor abilities (e.g., transitioning from pureed to mashed to finger foods).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Timely, Safe, Appropriate"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a drug that should be given to nursing with nursing mothers because of it may cause metabolic acidosis and can cause rash.:::aspirin', 'a drug that should be given to nursing with nursing mothers because of it may cause metabolic acidosis and can cause rash.', 'Aspirin', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid) is rapidly broken down into salicylic acid. When taken by a breastfeeding mother in high doses, significant amounts can pass into breast milk. Salicylates disrupt cellular metabolism by uncoupling oxidative phosphorylation. In young nursing infants with immature metabolic pathways, this accumulation directly leads to high anion gap metabolic acidosis.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Aspirin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid) is rapidly broken down into salicylic acid. When taken by a breastfeeding mother in high doses, significant amounts can pass into breast milk. Salicylates disrupt cellular metabolism by uncoupling oxidative phosphorylation. In young nursing infants with immature metabolic pathways, this accumulation directly leads to high anion gap metabolic acidosis.\n\nIn addition to metabolic imbalances, aspirin exposure through breast milk can trigger hypersensitivity reactions in the infant, causing a rash or skin abnormalities. It can also interfere with platelet aggregation, causing an increased risk of bruising or petechiae.\n\nBeyond the immediate side effects of a rash and acidosis, maternal aspirin use is heavily discouraged because pediatric salicylate exposure is closely linked to Reye''s syndrome, a rare but life-threatening condition causing severe liver and brain swelling.\n\n• Phenobarbital & Primidone: These are anticonvulsant medications. Their primary adverse effects on a nursing infant are severe drowsiness, sedation, poor feeding, and potential withdrawal symptoms upon weaning, rather than metabolic acidosis.\n• Clemastine: This is an antihistamine. Its primary reported side effects in a breastfed baby include extreme drowsiness, irritability, and a high-pitched cry.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Clemastine, Phenobarital, Primidone"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a drug that should be given to nursing with nursing mothers because of it may cause metabolic acidosis and can cause rash.:::', 'a drug that should be given to nursing with nursing mothers because of it may cause metabolic acidosis and can cause rash.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid) is rapidly broken down into salicylic acid. When taken by a breastfeeding mother in high doses, significant amounts can pass into breast milk. Salicylates disrupt cellular metabolism by uncoupling oxidative phosphorylation. In young nursing infants with immature metabolic pathways, this accumulation directly leads to high anion gap metabolic acidosis.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Aspirin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid) is rapidly broken down into salicylic acid. When taken by a breastfeeding mother in high doses, significant amounts can pass into breast milk. Salicylates disrupt cellular metabolism by uncoupling oxidative phosphorylation. In young nursing infants with immature metabolic pathways, this accumulation directly leads to high anion gap metabolic acidosis.\n\nIn addition to metabolic imbalances, aspirin exposure through breast milk can trigger hypersensitivity reactions in the infant, causing a rash or skin abnormalities. It can also interfere with platelet aggregation, causing an increased risk of bruising or petechiae.\n\nBeyond the immediate side effects of a rash and acidosis, maternal aspirin use is heavily discouraged because pediatric salicylate exposure is closely linked to Reye''s syndrome, a rare but life-threatening condition causing severe liver and brain swelling.\n\n• Phenobarbital & Primidone: These are anticonvulsant medications. Their primary adverse effects on a nursing infant are severe drowsiness, sedation, poor feeding, and potential withdrawal symptoms upon weaning, rather than metabolic acidosis.\n• Clemastine: This is an antihistamine. Its primary reported side effects in a breastfed baby include extreme drowsiness, irritability, and a high-pitched cry.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Clemastine, Phenobarital, Primidone"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are hormones of lactation except::::insulin', 'the following are hormones of lactation except:', 'Insulin', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Direct Lactation Hormones:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Insulin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Direct Lactation Hormones:\n\t• Prolactin: The absolute primary hormone responsible for the synthesis and production of milk within the mammary alveoli.\n\t• Oxytocin: The hormone responsible for the milk ejection (let-down) reflex, causing the myoepithelial cells surrounding the alveoli to contract.\n\t• Estrogen: Crucial during pregnancy for mammogenesis (the structural development and branching of the milk ducts) to prepare the breasts for lactation.\n\nInsulin is a general metabolic hormone produced by the pancreas to regulate blood glucose homeostasis. While it exhibits minor cellular synergy to maintain healthy maternal tissue, it is an endocrine system baseline hormone rather than a primary, targeted hormone of lactation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Prolactin, Estrogen, Oxytocin"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a premature infant is born fewer than days of gestation.:::270', 'a premature infant is born fewer than days of gestation.', '270', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In many classic nutrition and nursing reviewers (such as local dietetics or NCM/obstetric notes), a premature infant is traditionally defined as one born fewer than 270 days of gestation (or weighing less than 2,500 grams).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"270\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In many classic nutrition and nursing reviewers (such as local dietetics or NCM/obstetric notes), a premature infant is traditionally defined as one born fewer than 270 days of gestation (or weighing less than 2,500 grams).\n\nGlobally, the World Health Organization (WHO) and modern obstetrics define preterm birth strictly as a baby born before 37 completed weeks (which equals fewer than 259 days). However, among the standard multiple-choice options provided (270, 300, 170, 200), 270 days aligns with the classic threshold taught in older local curricula.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 300, 170, 200"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a premature infant is born fewer than days of gestation.:::', 'a premature infant is born fewer than days of gestation.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In many classic nutrition and nursing reviewers (such as local dietetics or NCM/obstetric notes), a premature infant is traditionally defined as one born fewer than 270 days of gestation (or weighing less than 2,500 grams).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"270\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In many classic nutrition and nursing reviewers (such as local dietetics or NCM/obstetric notes), a premature infant is traditionally defined as one born fewer than 270 days of gestation (or weighing less than 2,500 grams).\n\nGlobally, the World Health Organization (WHO) and modern obstetrics define preterm birth strictly as a baby born before 37 completed weeks (which equals fewer than 259 days). However, among the standard multiple-choice options provided (270, 300, 170, 200), 270 days aligns with the classic threshold taught in older local curricula.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 300, 170, 200"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what is true about the purpose of the menstrual cycle?:::a and b are both true', 'what is true about the purpose of the menstrual cycle?', 'A and B are both TRUE', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The overarching biological purpose of the entire menstrual cycle is to prepare the female reproductive system for a potential pregnancy. To achieve this goal, the cycle carries out two separate but deeply connected processes simultaneously:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"A and B are both TRUE\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The overarching biological purpose of the entire menstrual cycle is to prepare the female reproductive system for a potential pregnancy. To achieve this goal, the cycle carries out two separate but deeply connected processes simultaneously:\n\n• Statement A is TRUE: Driven by Follicle-Stimulating Hormone (FSH) and Luteinizing Hormone (LH), the ovarian cycle works to develop and release a single mature ovum (egg cell) from the ovaries during the ovulation phase.\n• Statement D is TRUE: Driven by estrogen and progesterone, the uterine cycle concurrently works to prepare, thicken, and enrich the uterus lining (endometrium), creating a plush environment ready to sustain a fertilized egg if implantation takes place.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: To develop and release a mature ovum, To prepare the uterus lining, A and B are both FALSE"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what is true about the purpose of the menstrual cycle?:::', 'what is true about the purpose of the menstrual cycle?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The overarching biological purpose of the entire menstrual cycle is to prepare the female reproductive system for a potential pregnancy. To achieve this goal, the cycle carries out two separate but deeply connected processes simultaneously:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"A and B are both TRUE\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The overarching biological purpose of the entire menstrual cycle is to prepare the female reproductive system for a potential pregnancy. To achieve this goal, the cycle carries out two separate but deeply connected processes simultaneously:\n\n• Statement A is TRUE: Driven by Follicle-Stimulating Hormone (FSH) and Luteinizing Hormone (LH), the ovarian cycle works to develop and release a single mature ovum (egg cell) from the ovaries during the ovulation phase.\n• Statement D is TRUE: Driven by estrogen and progesterone, the uterine cycle concurrently works to prepare, thicken, and enrich the uterus lining (endometrium), creating a plush environment ready to sustain a fertilized egg if implantation takes place.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: To develop and release a mature ovum, To prepare the uterus lining, A and B are both FALSE"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the mesoderm gives rise to::::', 'the mesoderm gives rise to:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"During early embryonic development, the mesoderm forms the intermediate layer sandwiched between the ectoderm and endoderm. It gives rise to structural, circulatory, and excretory systems.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Urogenital system\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"During early embryonic development, the mesoderm forms the intermediate layer sandwiched between the ectoderm and endoderm. It gives rise to structural, circulatory, and excretory systems.\n\nThe intermediate mesoderm specifically condenses into the urogenital ridges, which directly differentiate into the urinary system (kidneys and ducts) and the reproductive/genital system (gonads, internal sex organs, and uterus).\n\n• Nervous system: Arises from the ectoderm (the outermost layer), specifically through the differentiation of the neuroectoderm into the neural tube and neural crest.\n• Pancreas and Thyroid: Develop from the endoderm (the innermost layer), which buds outward to form the linings and organs of the digestive and respiratory tracts.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Nervous system, Pancreas, Thyroid"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the estimated weight of the placenta of a pregnant woman?:::2 lbs', 'what is the estimated weight of the placenta of a pregnant woman?', '2 lbs', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"At full term, a normal, healthy human placenta typically weighs around 500 to 650 grams (roughly 1.4 to 1.5 lbs, or up to ~2 lbs when grouped in standard prenatal weight-gain breakdowns).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"2 lbs\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"At full term, a normal, healthy human placenta typically weighs around 500 to 650 grams (roughly 1.4 to 1.5 lbs, or up to ~2 lbs when grouped in standard prenatal weight-gain breakdowns).\n\nWhen clinical references (such as the Mayo Clinic or standard obstetrics texts) break down the typical 25–35 lb pregnancy weight gain, the placenta is consistently categorized at 1.5 to 2 lbs.\n\nWhen breaking down the component weight gain of a full-term pregnancy using clinical reference values (totaling 12.5 kg or ~27.5 lbs), the components weigh out as follows:\n\n• Amniotic Fluid: ~1.8 lbs (0.80 kg) — which rounds to 2 lbs\n• Baby (Fetus): ~7.5 lbs (3.40 kg) — note: this is likely where the answer key confused \"7 lbs\"\n• Maternal Fat Stores: ~7.4 lbs (3.35 kg)\n• Extracellular Extravascular Fluid: ~3.3 lbs (1.48 kg)\n• Blood Volume: ~3.2 lbs (1.45 kg)\n• Maternal Tissue: ~3.0 lbs (1.38 kg)\n• Placenta: ~1.4 lbs (0.65 kg)","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 5 lbs, 10.5 lbs, 7 lbs"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:age period characterized by rapid differentiation and establishment of systems and orgarns::::embryonic', 'age period characterized by rapid differentiation and establishment of systems and orgarns:', 'Embryonic', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The embryonic period spans from the third week through the eighth week of pregnancy. This critical phase is defined by organogenesis, which is the rapid differentiation of cells into distinct germ cell layers (ectoderm, mesoderm, and endoderm) and the foundation of all major body systems and rudimentary organs. By the end of the eighth week, the basic structural form of the human body and its systems are fully established.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Embryonic\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The embryonic period spans from the third week through the eighth week of pregnancy. This critical phase is defined by organogenesis, which is the rapid differentiation of cells into distinct germ cell layers (ectoderm, mesoderm, and endoderm) and the foundation of all major body systems and rudimentary organs. By the end of the eighth week, the basic structural form of the human body and its systems are fully established.\n\nThe fetal periods (both early and late) span from the ninth week until birth. This phase is primarily characterized by the growth, refinement, and functional maturation of those already established systems rather than their initial structural establishment or differentiation.\n\nThe neonatal period refers to the first 28 days of life after birth, where the newborn adapts physiologically to extrauterine life.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Early fetal, Late fetal, Neonatal"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the estimated weight of the placenta of a pregnant woman?:::', 'what is the estimated weight of the placenta of a pregnant woman?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"At full term, a normal, healthy human placenta typically weighs around 500 to 650 grams (roughly 1.4 to 1.5 lbs, or up to ~2 lbs when grouped in standard prenatal weight-gain breakdowns).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"2 lbs\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"At full term, a normal, healthy human placenta typically weighs around 500 to 650 grams (roughly 1.4 to 1.5 lbs, or up to ~2 lbs when grouped in standard prenatal weight-gain breakdowns).\n\nWhen clinical references (such as the Mayo Clinic or standard obstetrics texts) break down the typical 25–35 lb pregnancy weight gain, the placenta is consistently categorized at 1.5 to 2 lbs.\n\nWhen breaking down the component weight gain of a full-term pregnancy using clinical reference values (totaling 12.5 kg or ~27.5 lbs), the components weigh out as follows:\n\n• Amniotic Fluid: ~1.8 lbs (0.80 kg) — which rounds to 2 lbs\n• Baby (Fetus): ~7.5 lbs (3.40 kg) — note: this is likely where the answer key confused \"7 lbs\"\n• Maternal Fat Stores: ~7.4 lbs (3.35 kg)\n• Extracellular Extravascular Fluid: ~3.3 lbs (1.48 kg)\n• Blood Volume: ~3.2 lbs (1.45 kg)\n• Maternal Tissue: ~3.0 lbs (1.38 kg)\n• Placenta: ~1.4 lbs (0.65 kg)","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 5 lbs, 10.5 lbs, 7 lbs"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the mesoderm gives rise to::::urogenital system', 'the mesoderm gives rise to:', 'Urogenital system', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"During early embryonic development, the mesoderm forms the intermediate layer sandwiched between the ectoderm and endoderm. It gives rise to structural, circulatory, and excretory systems.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Urogenital system\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"During early embryonic development, the mesoderm forms the intermediate layer sandwiched between the ectoderm and endoderm. It gives rise to structural, circulatory, and excretory systems.\n\nThe intermediate mesoderm specifically condenses into the urogenital ridges, which directly differentiate into the urinary system (kidneys and ducts) and the reproductive/genital system (gonads, internal sex organs, and uterus).\n\n• Nervous system: Arises from the ectoderm (the outermost layer), specifically through the differentiation of the neuroectoderm into the neural tube and neural crest.\n• Pancreas and Thyroid: Develop from the endoderm (the innermost layer), which buds outward to form the linings and organs of the digestive and respiratory tracts.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Nervous system, Pancreas, Thyroid"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:cut-off weight for low birth weight::::', 'cut-off weight for low birth weight:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The World Health Organization (WHO) defines Low Birth Weight (LBW) as an absolute birth weight of less than 2,500 grams (up to and including 2,499 grams).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"5.50 lbs or less\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The World Health Organization (WHO) defines Low Birth Weight (LBW) as an absolute birth weight of less than 2,500 grams (up to and including 2,499 grams).\n\nConverting 2,500 grams into the imperial system commonly used in board exams:\n2,500 grams ÷ 453.59 = 5.511 lbs\n\nTherefore, a threshold of 5.50 lbs represents the direct equivalent cutoff used to diagnose low birth weight in medical and public health data.\n\n• Low Birth Weight (LBW): Less than 2,500 grams (~5.5 lbs)\n• Very Low Birth Weight (VLBW): Less than 1,500 grams (~3.3 lbs)\n• Extremely Low Birth Weight (ELBW): Less than 1,000 grams (~2.2 lbs)","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 5 lbs or less, 5 kg or less, 25 kg or less"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:physiologic anemia of pregnancy is due to::::increased blood volume', 'physiologic anemia of pregnancy is due to:', 'Increased blood volume', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"During pregnancy, a woman''s total blood volume expands dramatically to support the growing fetus. However, the plasma volume increases by roughly 50%, while the actual mass of red blood cells only increases by about 20% to 30%.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Increased blood volume\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"During pregnancy, a woman''s total blood volume expands dramatically to support the growing fetus. However, the plasma volume increases by roughly 50%, while the actual mass of red blood cells only increases by about 20% to 30%.\n\nBecause the liquid portion of the blood (plasma) expands much faster and more significantly than the red blood cells, it creates a dilutional effect. This lowers the overall concentration of hemoglobin and hematocrit, leading to what is safely classified as physiologic (or normal) anemia of pregnancy.\n\nNote on other options: While iron deficiency and Vitamin B12 deficiency are common causes of pathological anemia during pregnancy, they do not cause the normal physiologic adaptation described above.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Iron deficiency, Vitamin B12 deficiency, Decreased blood volume"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:acrolein is associated with::::overheated fats', 'acrolein is associated with:', 'Overheated fats', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Acrolein can form when fats are overheated, particularly when glycerol components break down.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Overheated fats\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Acrolein can form when fats are overheated, particularly when glycerol components break down.\n\n• Fermented milk is associated with lactic acid fermentation rather than acrolein formation.\n• Gelatinized starch results from starch absorbing water and swelling during heating.\n• Fresh fruits are not the food phenomenon described by acrolein formation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Fresh fruits, Fermented milk, Gelatinized starch"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:physiologic anemia of pregnancy is due to::::', 'physiologic anemia of pregnancy is due to:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"During pregnancy, a woman''s total blood volume expands dramatically to support the growing fetus. However, the plasma volume increases by roughly 50%, while the actual mass of red blood cells only increases by about 20% to 30%.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Increased blood volume\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"During pregnancy, a woman''s total blood volume expands dramatically to support the growing fetus. However, the plasma volume increases by roughly 50%, while the actual mass of red blood cells only increases by about 20% to 30%.\n\nBecause the liquid portion of the blood (plasma) expands much faster and more significantly than the red blood cells, it creates a dilutional effect. This lowers the overall concentration of hemoglobin and hematocrit, leading to what is safely classified as physiologic (or normal) anemia of pregnancy.\n\nNote on other options: While iron deficiency and Vitamin B12 deficiency are common causes of pathological anemia during pregnancy, they do not cause the normal physiologic adaptation described above.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Iron deficiency, Vitamin B12 deficiency, Decreased blood volume"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the calcium recommendation in the pdri for a pregnant woman on her first month versus a six-month pregnant woman is::::the same', 'the calcium recommendation in the pdri for a pregnant woman on her first month versus a six-month pregnant woman is:', 'the same', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to the Philippine Dietary Reference Intakes (PDRI), the baseline calcium requirement for a woman of childbearing age (e.g., 750–800 mg/day depending on age) does not change during the first two trimesters of pregnancy.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"the same\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to the Philippine Dietary Reference Intakes (PDRI), the baseline calcium requirement for a woman of childbearing age (e.g., 750–800 mg/day depending on age) does not change during the first two trimesters of pregnancy.\n\nA woman in her first month is in her 1st trimester, and a woman in her sixth month is in her 2nd trimester. Because neither phase requires an increase, their daily recommended intakes are identical.\n\nThe PDRI explicitly applies the +50 mg/day calcium addition only during the 3rd trimester. This is when rapid fetal skeletal mineralization occurs, requiring extra maternal dietary support. In the earlier months (1st and 6th), the mother''s body naturally compensates by increasing its own intestinal calcium absorption efficiency, keeping the dietary recommendation unchanged.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: lower, higher, not comparable"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb)
