('mc:a triglyceride is composed primarily of::::', 'a triglyceride is composed primarily of:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"One glycerol plus three fatty acids describes the basic structure of a triglyceride.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"One glycerol molecule and three fatty acids\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"One glycerol plus three fatty acids describes the basic structure of a triglyceride.\n\n• A triglyceride has a glycerol backbone esterified with three fatty acids.\n• Triglycerides are lipids, not compounds made from amino acids and glycerol.\n• Glucose is not the backbone of a triglyceride.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Three glycerol molecules and one fatty acid, One glucose molecule and three fatty acids, Three amino acids and one glycerol molecule"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:during dry heating of starch, the formation of shorter-chain products is known as formation of::::dextrins', 'during dry heating of starch, the formation of shorter-chain products is known as formation of:', 'Dextrins', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dextrins are shorter-chain products formed when starch is subjected to dry heat.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Dextrins\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dextrins are shorter-chain products formed when starch is subjected to dry heat.\n\n• Gluten is a protein complex associated with wheat dough, not a starch breakdown product.\n• Casein is the principal protein of milk.\n• Pectin is a structural polysaccharide found in plant tissues.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Pectin, Gluten, Casein"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:during dry heating of starch, the formation of shorter-chain products is known as formation of::::', 'during dry heating of starch, the formation of shorter-chain products is known as formation of:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dextrins are shorter-chain products formed when starch is subjected to dry heat.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Dextrins\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dextrins are shorter-chain products formed when starch is subjected to dry heat.\n\n• Gluten is a protein complex associated with wheat dough, not a starch breakdown product.\n• Casein is the principal protein of milk.\n• Pectin is a structural polysaccharide found in plant tissues.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Pectin, Gluten, Casein"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which enzyme is associated with the tenderizing activity of figs?:::ficain', 'which enzyme is associated with the tenderizing activity of figs?', 'Ficain', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Ficain is a proteolytic enzyme found in figs and can contribute to meat tenderization.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Ficain\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Ficain is a proteolytic enzyme found in figs and can contribute to meat tenderization.\n\n• Papain is associated with papaya.\n• Bromelain is associated with pineapple.\n• Lactase hydrolyzes lactose and is not the fig enzyme used for meat tenderizing.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Papain, Bromelain, Lactase"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which type of cooking method is generally most appropriate for a tender cut of meat?:::dry-heat cooking', 'which type of cooking method is generally most appropriate for a tender cut of meat?', 'Dry-heat cooking', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dry-heat methods are generally appropriate for tender cuts because they do not require prolonged moist cooking.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Dry-heat cooking\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dry-heat methods are generally appropriate for tender cuts because they do not require prolonged moist cooking.\n\nTender cuts of meat come from muscles that do the least work (such as the loin or rib areas) and contain very little tough connective tissue (collagen).\n\nBecause these cuts are already naturally tender, they do not require prolonged cooking to break down tough fibers. Dry-heat cooking methods—such as grilling, broiling, roasting, sautéing, or pan-frying—cook the meat quickly at high temperatures, preserving its natural juices and preventing it from becoming overcooked and dry.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Stewing, Braising, Moist cooking"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which type of cooking method is generally most appropriate for a tender cut of meat?:::', 'which type of cooking method is generally most appropriate for a tender cut of meat?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dry-heat methods are generally appropriate for tender cuts because they do not require prolonged moist cooking.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Dry-heat cooking\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dry-heat methods are generally appropriate for tender cuts because they do not require prolonged moist cooking.\n\nTender cuts of meat come from muscles that do the least work (such as the loin or rib areas) and contain very little tough connective tissue (collagen).\n\nBecause these cuts are already naturally tender, they do not require prolonged cooking to break down tough fibers. Dry-heat cooking methods—such as grilling, broiling, roasting, sautéing, or pan-frying—cook the meat quickly at high temperatures, preserving its natural juices and preventing it from becoming overcooked and dry.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Stewing, Braising, Moist cooking"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a nonenzymatic browning reaction associated with sugar undergoing heating; prolonged heating above one hundred sixty (>160) degrees celsius (three hundred twenty (32) degrees fahrenheit) resulting in dark brown, highly viscous syrup with distinctive taste and odor.:::caramelization', 'a nonenzymatic browning reaction associated with sugar undergoing heating; prolonged heating above one hundred sixty (>160) degrees celsius (three hundred twenty (32) degrees fahrenheit) resulting in dark brown, highly viscous syrup with distinctive taste and odor.', 'Caramelization', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Caramelization is a type of non-enzymatic browning that involves the thermal decomposition and pyrolysis of sugars alone, completely independent of proteins or amino acids.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Caramelization\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Caramelization is a type of non-enzymatic browning that involves the thermal decomposition and pyrolysis of sugars alone, completely independent of proteins or amino acids.\n\nCommon disaccharides like sucrose (table sugar) and monosaccharides like glucose melt and begin to caramelize rapidly at around 160°C (320°F). Prolonged heating creates complex polymers (caramelans, caramelens, and caramelins) and volatile flavor compounds, transforming clear sugar crystals into a dark, thick, highly aromatic syrup.\n\n• Oxidation is a reaction with oxygen. \n• Maillard Browning is sugar + amino acids, not pure sugar.\n• Reduction is the process of simmering or boiling a liquid uncovered so that water evaporates","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Oxidation, Maillard browning, Reduction"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a nonenzymatic browning reaction associated with sugar undergoing heating; prolonged heating above one hundred sixty (>160) degrees celsius (three hundred twenty (32) degrees fahrenheit) resulting in dark brown, highly viscous syrup with distinctive taste and odor.:::', 'a nonenzymatic browning reaction associated with sugar undergoing heating; prolonged heating above one hundred sixty (>160) degrees celsius (three hundred twenty (32) degrees fahrenheit) resulting in dark brown, highly viscous syrup with distinctive taste and odor.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Caramelization is a type of non-enzymatic browning that involves the thermal decomposition and pyrolysis of sugars alone, completely independent of proteins or amino acids.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Caramelization\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Caramelization is a type of non-enzymatic browning that involves the thermal decomposition and pyrolysis of sugars alone, completely independent of proteins or amino acids.\n\nCommon disaccharides like sucrose (table sugar) and monosaccharides like glucose melt and begin to caramelize rapidly at around 160°C (320°F). Prolonged heating creates complex polymers (caramelans, caramelens, and caramelins) and volatile flavor compounds, transforming clear sugar crystals into a dark, thick, highly aromatic syrup.\n\n• Oxidation is a reaction with oxygen. \n• Maillard Browning is sugar + amino acids, not pure sugar.\n• Reduction is the process of simmering or boiling a liquid uncovered so that water evaporates","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Oxidation, Maillard browning, Reduction"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:cooking an egg primarily causes its proteins to undergo::::denaturation and coagulation', 'cooking an egg primarily causes its proteins to undergo:', 'Denaturation and coagulation', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Egg proteins denature and then coagulate during cooking, changing the egg from a fluid to a firmer structure.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Denaturation and coagulation\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Egg proteins denature and then coagulate during cooking, changing the egg from a fluid to a firmer structure.\n\n• Gelatinization is a starch phenomenon involving starch and water under heat.\n• Caramelization is a nonenzymatic browning reaction involving sugars.\n• Retrogradation refers primarily to reassociation of starch molecules after gelatinization.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Gelatinization, Retrogadation, Caramelization"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a menu that repeats according to a planned sequence over a specified period is called a::::cycle menu', 'a menu that repeats according to a planned sequence over a specified period is called a:', 'Cycle menu', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A cycle menu features a series of menus that repeat in a set pattern (such as weekly, bi-weekly, or monthly) over a designated period.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Cycle menu\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A cycle menu features a series of menus that repeat in a set pattern (such as weekly, bi-weekly, or monthly) over a designated period.\n\n This is widely used in institutional settings like schools, hospitals, long-term care facilities, and corporate cafeterias to simplify purchasing, control costs, and maintain dietary variety.\n\n• Du jour menu: A menu \"of the day\" that changes daily depending on what is fresh or left over.\n• Single-use menu: A menu planned and used only once, typically for a special event, holiday banquet, or one-time catered function.\n• Static menu: A fixed menu that remains the same every day, commonly found in commercial restaurants.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Static menu, Du jour menu, Single-use menu"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a menu that repeats according to a planned sequence over a specified period is called a::::', 'a menu that repeats according to a planned sequence over a specified period is called a:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A cycle menu features a series of menus that repeat in a set pattern (such as weekly, bi-weekly, or monthly) over a designated period.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Cycle menu\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A cycle menu features a series of menus that repeat in a set pattern (such as weekly, bi-weekly, or monthly) over a designated period.\n\n This is widely used in institutional settings like schools, hospitals, long-term care facilities, and corporate cafeterias to simplify purchasing, control costs, and maintain dietary variety.\n\n• Du jour menu: A menu \"of the day\" that changes daily depending on what is fresh or left over.\n• Single-use menu: A menu planned and used only once, typically for a special event, holiday banquet, or one-time catered function.\n• Static menu: A fixed menu that remains the same every day, commonly found in commercial restaurants.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Static menu, Du jour menu, Single-use menu"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which statement best describes dietary fiber?:::it consists largely of nondigestible carbohydrate components and related substances', 'which statement best describes dietary fiber?', 'It consists largely of nondigestible carbohydrate components and related substances', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dietary fiber is a broad term for the structural components of plant foods—including polysaccharides like cellulose, hemicellulose, pectins, and non-carbohydrate polymers like lignin.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"It consists largely of nondigestible carbohydrate components and related substances\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dietary fiber is a broad term for the structural components of plant foods—including polysaccharides like cellulose, hemicellulose, pectins, and non-carbohydrate polymers like lignin.\n\nUnlike sugars and starches, the human digestive system lacks the specific enzymes needed to break down the chemical bonds found in dietary fiber. As a result, it passes through the stomach and small intestine relatively intact.\n\n• It is a form of animal protein: Fiber is exclusively derived from plant foods (fruits, vegetables, grains, legumes, nuts); animal products contain zero dietary fiber.\n• It is composed entirely of dietary fat: Fiber belongs to the carbohydrate family of macronutrients, not fats or lipids.\n• It is completely digested in the small intestine: Fiber resists enzymatic breakdown in the small intestine completely. Instead, it moves into the large intestine (colon), where it is either partially fermented by beneficial gut bacteria (soluble fiber) or excreted unchanged (insoluble fiber).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: It is completely digested in the small intestine, It is composed entirely of dietary fat, It is a form of animal protein"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which statement best describes dietary fiber?:::', 'which statement best describes dietary fiber?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dietary fiber is a broad term for the structural components of plant foods—including polysaccharides like cellulose, hemicellulose, pectins, and non-carbohydrate polymers like lignin.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"It consists largely of nondigestible carbohydrate components and related substances\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dietary fiber is a broad term for the structural components of plant foods—including polysaccharides like cellulose, hemicellulose, pectins, and non-carbohydrate polymers like lignin.\n\nUnlike sugars and starches, the human digestive system lacks the specific enzymes needed to break down the chemical bonds found in dietary fiber. As a result, it passes through the stomach and small intestine relatively intact.\n\n• It is a form of animal protein: Fiber is exclusively derived from plant foods (fruits, vegetables, grains, legumes, nuts); animal products contain zero dietary fiber.\n• It is composed entirely of dietary fat: Fiber belongs to the carbohydrate family of macronutrients, not fats or lipids.\n• It is completely digested in the small intestine: Fiber resists enzymatic breakdown in the small intestine completely. Instead, it moves into the large intestine (colon), where it is either partially fermented by beneficial gut bacteria (soluble fiber) or excreted unchanged (insoluble fiber).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: It is completely digested in the small intestine, It is composed entirely of dietary fat, It is a form of animal protein"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which food is a well-known source of lycopene?:::', 'which food is a well-known source of lycopene?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Lycopene is a powerful antioxidant and a bright red carotenoid hydrocarbon.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Tomato\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Lycopene is a powerful antioxidant and a bright red carotenoid hydrocarbon.\n\nTomatoes and tomato-based products (like tomato paste, ketchup, and sauces) are the most abundant dietary sources of lycopene. Interestingly, cooking tomatoes and combining them with a little fat (like olive oil) actually increases the bioavailability of lycopene, making it easier for the body to absorb.\n\n• Cauliflower - glucosinolates because cruciferous (sulfur + nitrogen)\n• Cabbage - glucosinolates because cruciferous (sulfur + nitrogen)\n• Potato - carbohydrates","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Potato, Cauliflower, Cabbage"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the desired product of jelly making is generally a::::clear, firm gel', 'the desired product of jelly making is generally a:', 'Clear, firm gel', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"By culinary and food science standards, a perfect jelly must be a clear, sparkling liquid that has been trapped in a firm, shimmering gel network. It should hold its shape when turned out of a container but remain tender enough to cut or spoon easily.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Clear, firm gel\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"By culinary and food science standards, a perfect jelly must be a clear, sparkling liquid that has been trapped in a firm, shimmering gel network. It should hold its shape when turned out of a container but remain tender enough to cut or spoon easily.\n\nThis specific texture is achieved through the precise chemical balance of three structural elements: pectin (the gelling agent), acid (which prompts the pectin to form a network), and sugar (which bonds with water to assist the pectin).\n\n• Thick emulsion: This describes a mixture of two unmixable liquids, like oil and water (e.g., mayonnaise or hollandaise sauce).\n• Foamy suspension: This is a mixture of gas bubbles trapped in a liquid or solid (e.g., whipped cream or meringue). In fact, foam is an undesirable byproduct in jelly making that must be skimmed off during boiling.\n• Crystalline sugar mass: This occurs when a jelly fails, and the sugar recrystallizes, resulting in a gritty, grainy texture instead of a smooth gel.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Thick emulsion, Foamy suspension, Crystalline sugar mass"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the desired product of jelly making is generally a::::', 'the desired product of jelly making is generally a:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"By culinary and food science standards, a perfect jelly must be a clear, sparkling liquid that has been trapped in a firm, shimmering gel network. It should hold its shape when turned out of a container but remain tender enough to cut or spoon easily.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Clear, firm gel\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"By culinary and food science standards, a perfect jelly must be a clear, sparkling liquid that has been trapped in a firm, shimmering gel network. It should hold its shape when turned out of a container but remain tender enough to cut or spoon easily.\n\nThis specific texture is achieved through the precise chemical balance of three structural elements: pectin (the gelling agent), acid (which prompts the pectin to form a network), and sugar (which bonds with water to assist the pectin).\n\n• Thick emulsion: This describes a mixture of two unmixable liquids, like oil and water (e.g., mayonnaise or hollandaise sauce).\n• Foamy suspension: This is a mixture of gas bubbles trapped in a liquid or solid (e.g., whipped cream or meringue). In fact, foam is an undesirable byproduct in jelly making that must be skimmed off during boiling.\n• Crystalline sugar mass: This occurs when a jelly fails, and the sugar recrystallizes, resulting in a gritty, grainy texture instead of a smooth gel.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Thick emulsion, Foamy suspension, Crystalline sugar mass"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:carotenoid pigments are generally::::relatively stable to mild acid compared with chlorophyll', 'carotenoid pigments are generally:', 'Relatively stable to mild acid compared with chlorophyll', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Compared with chlorophyll, carotenoids are relatively stable to mild acid conditions. They contain around 40 carbon backbone, atleast 8 isoprene units.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Relatively stable to mild acid compared with chlorophyll\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Compared with chlorophyll, carotenoids are relatively stable to mild acid conditions. They contain around 40 carbon backbone, atleast 8 isoprene units.\n\n• Carotenoids are not generally destroyed immediately by mild acid.\n• Acid does not convert carotenoids into chlorophyll. These are two completely different structures.\n• Chlorophyll, not carotenoids, is primarily responsible for green vegetable color.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Highly destroyed immediately by mild acid, Responsible for green vegetable color, Converted into chlorophyll by acid"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:carotenoid pigments are generally::::', 'carotenoid pigments are generally:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Compared with chlorophyll, carotenoids are relatively stable to mild acid conditions. They contain around 40 carbon backbone, atleast 8 isoprene units.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Relatively stable to mild acid compared with chlorophyll\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Compared with chlorophyll, carotenoids are relatively stable to mild acid conditions. They contain around 40 carbon backbone, atleast 8 isoprene units.\n\n• Carotenoids are not generally destroyed immediately by mild acid.\n• Acid does not convert carotenoids into chlorophyll. These are two completely different structures.\n• Chlorophyll, not carotenoids, is primarily responsible for green vegetable color.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Highly destroyed immediately by mild acid, Responsible for green vegetable color, Converted into chlorophyll by acid"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which food is a well-known source of lycopene?:::tomato', 'which food is a well-known source of lycopene?', 'Tomato', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Lycopene is a powerful antioxidant and a bright red carotenoid hydrocarbon.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Tomato\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Lycopene is a powerful antioxidant and a bright red carotenoid hydrocarbon.\n\nTomatoes and tomato-based products (like tomato paste, ketchup, and sauces) are the most abundant dietary sources of lycopene. Interestingly, cooking tomatoes and combining them with a little fat (like olive oil) actually increases the bioavailability of lycopene, making it easier for the body to absorb.\n\n• Cauliflower - glucosinolates because cruciferous (sulfur + nitrogen)\n• Cabbage - glucosinolates because cruciferous (sulfur + nitrogen)\n• Potato - carbohydrates","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Potato, Cauliflower, Cabbage"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which vitamin is particularly susceptible to losses during food processing?:::vitamin c', 'which vitamin is particularly susceptible to losses during food processing?', 'Vitamin C', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Vitamin C is highly sensitive to heat, oxygen, and water and is readily lost during some processing methods.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vitamin C\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Vitamin C is highly sensitive to heat, oxygen, and water and is readily lost during some processing methods.\n\n• Vitamin D is generally more stable under ordinary food-processing conditions.\n• Vitamin K is not typically identified as the vitamin most susceptible to processing losses in this context.\n• Vitamin B12 can also be lost during cooking but is more heat-tolerant","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vitamin B12, Vitamin D, Vitamin K"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which vitamin is particularly susceptible to losses during food processing?:::', 'which vitamin is particularly susceptible to losses during food processing?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Vitamin C is highly sensitive to heat, oxygen, and water and is readily lost during some processing methods.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vitamin C\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Vitamin C is highly sensitive to heat, oxygen, and water and is readily lost during some processing methods.\n\n• Vitamin D is generally more stable under ordinary food-processing conditions.\n• Vitamin K is not typically identified as the vitamin most susceptible to processing losses in this context.\n• Vitamin B12 can also be lost during cooking but is more heat-tolerant","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vitamin B12, Vitamin D, Vitamin K"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:chevon refers to meat from::::goat', 'chevon refers to meat from:', 'Goat', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Chevon is the official culinary term for the meat of an adult goat. It comes from the French word for goat, chèvre.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Goat\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Chevon is the official culinary term for the meat of an adult goat. It comes from the French word for goat, chèvre.\n\nYou may also see young goat meat referred to as cabrito or simply called mutton in certain regions (like India and parts of Asia), though in formal culinary terms, mutton belongs to another animal.\n\n• Sheep meat is commonly called mutton (2-3yo) or lamb \n• Deer meat is commonly called venison.\n• Rabbit meat is usually referred to simply as rabbit or lapan (in some culinary contexts).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sheep, Rabbit, Deer"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:chevon refers to meat from::::', 'chevon refers to meat from:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Chevon is the official culinary term for the meat of an adult goat. It comes from the French word for goat, chèvre.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Goat\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Chevon is the official culinary term for the meat of an adult goat. It comes from the French word for goat, chèvre.\n\nYou may also see young goat meat referred to as cabrito or simply called mutton in certain regions (like India and parts of Asia), though in formal culinary terms, mutton belongs to another animal.\n\n• Sheep meat is commonly called mutton (2-3yo) or lamb \n• Deer meat is commonly called venison.\n• Rabbit meat is usually referred to simply as rabbit or lapan (in some culinary contexts).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sheep, Rabbit, Deer"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is a recommended minimum internal temperature for cooked seafood?:::145°f', 'which of the following is a recommended minimum internal temperature for cooked seafood?', '145°F', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to USDA Food Safety and Inspection Service","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"145°F\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to USDA Food Safety and Inspection Service\n\n• All Poultry: 165°F (74°C) for whole birds, breasts, legs, thighs, wings, and ground poultry.\n• Stuffing & Casseroles: 165°F (74°C).\n• Leftovers: 165°F (74°C) to reheat thoroughly.\n• Ground Meats: 160°F (71°C) for beef, pork, veal, and lamb. \n• Egg Dishes: 160°F (71°C) for frittatas and quiches.\n• Beef, Pork, Veal, and Lamb: 145°F (63°C) for steaks, chops, and roasts, followed by a 3-minute rest time.\n• Fish and Shellfish: 145°F (63°C) or until the flesh is opaque and flakes easily.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 125°F, 135°F, 165°F"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is a recommended minimum internal temperature for cooked seafood?:::', 'which of the following is a recommended minimum internal temperature for cooked seafood?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to USDA Food Safety and Inspection Service","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"145°F\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to USDA Food Safety and Inspection Service\n\n• All Poultry: 165°F (74°C) for whole birds, breasts, legs, thighs, wings, and ground poultry.\n• Stuffing & Casseroles: 165°F (74°C).\n• Leftovers: 165°F (74°C) to reheat thoroughly.\n• Ground Meats: 160°F (71°C) for beef, pork, veal, and lamb. \n• Egg Dishes: 160°F (71°C) for frittatas and quiches.\n• Beef, Pork, Veal, and Lamb: 145°F (63°C) for steaks, chops, and roasts, followed by a 3-minute rest time.\n• Fish and Shellfish: 145°F (63°C) or until the flesh is opaque and flakes easily.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 125°F, 135°F, 165°F"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a fish with its viscera removed but its head, fins, and tail retained is described as::::drawn', 'a fish with its viscera removed but its head, fins, and tail retained is described as:', 'Drawn', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A drawn fish is a whole fish that has had only its internal organs (viscera or entrails) removed. The head, fins, scales, and tail all remain completely intact.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Drawn\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A drawn fish is a whole fish that has had only its internal organs (viscera or entrails) removed. The head, fins, scales, and tail all remain completely intact.\n\n• A whole fish has not had the viscera removed.\n• A dressed fish has undergone additional preparation beyond simply drawing. • A fish that has been drawn, but has also had its head, tail, fins, and scales completely removed.\n• A filleted fish has the flesh separated from the bones, usually into fillets. The boneless side of a fish cut lengthwise away from the backbone.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Whole, Fillet, Dressed"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a fish with its viscera removed but its head, fins, and tail retained is described as::::', 'a fish with its viscera removed but its head, fins, and tail retained is described as:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A drawn fish is a whole fish that has had only its internal organs (viscera or entrails) removed. The head, fins, scales, and tail all remain completely intact.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Drawn\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A drawn fish is a whole fish that has had only its internal organs (viscera or entrails) removed. The head, fins, scales, and tail all remain completely intact.\n\n• A whole fish has not had the viscera removed.\n• A dressed fish has undergone additional preparation beyond simply drawing. • A fish that has been drawn, but has also had its head, tail, fins, and scales completely removed.\n• A filleted fish has the flesh separated from the bones, usually into fillets. The boneless side of a fish cut lengthwise away from the backbone.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Whole, Fillet, Dressed"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the statements is correct::::balut is a fertilized duck egg that has been incubated before cooking', 'which of the statements is correct:', 'Balut is a fertilized duck egg that has been incubated before cooking', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Balut is a famous Filipino street food consisting of a fertilized duck egg. It is traditionally incubated in heated rice husks for roughly 14 to 21 days (usually 18 days) to allow a semi-developed duck embryo to form inside before it is hard-boiled or steamed.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Balut is a fertilized duck egg that has been incubated before cooking\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Balut is a famous Filipino street food consisting of a fertilized duck egg. It is traditionally incubated in heated rice husks for roughly 14 to 21 days (usually 18 days) to allow a semi-developed duck embryo to form inside before it is hard-boiled or steamed.\n\nPenoy: is an unfertilized duck egg that is boiled and consumed. It does not contain a developing duck embryo.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Balut is an unfertilized duck egg, Penoy is a fertilized duck egg that has been incubated before cooking, Penoy is a fertilized chicken egg"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the statements is correct::::', 'which of the statements is correct:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Balut is a famous Filipino street food consisting of a fertilized duck egg. It is traditionally incubated in heated rice husks for roughly 14 to 21 days (usually 18 days) to allow a semi-developed duck embryo to form inside before it is hard-boiled or steamed.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Balut is a fertilized duck egg that has been incubated before cooking\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Balut is a famous Filipino street food consisting of a fertilized duck egg. It is traditionally incubated in heated rice husks for roughly 14 to 21 days (usually 18 days) to allow a semi-developed duck embryo to form inside before it is hard-boiled or steamed.\n\nPenoy: is an unfertilized duck egg that is boiled and consumed. It does not contain a developing duck embryo.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Balut is an unfertilized duck egg, Penoy is a fertilized duck egg that has been incubated before cooking, Penoy is a fertilized chicken egg"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:true or false: mutton (meat of an older sheep) is lighter in color and flavor than the lamb.:::false', 'true or false: mutton (meat of an older sheep) is lighter in color and flavor than the lamb.', 'FALSE', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Why Mutton is Darker","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"FALSE\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Why Mutton is Darker\n• Myoglobin buildup: Older animals have muscles that work for years. This increases myoglobin, the protein that stores oxygen in muscle tissue and turns red meat dark.\n• Diet accumulation: Years of grazing on grass and forage deposit rich nutrients and minerals into the animal''s muscle and fat tissues.\n\nWhy Mutton is More Flavorful\n• Fat and fatty acids: Mutton develops a higher concentration of species-specific fatty acids and heavier fat deposits that carry a robust, earthy, and gamey taste.\n• Mature muscle chemistry: As the sheep ages, the chemical compounds responsible for deep savory flavors multiply and concentrate, moving past the mild and milky taste profile of young lamb.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: TRUE, Depends, Maybe"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:true or false: mutton (meat of an older sheep) is lighter in color and flavor than the lamb.:::', 'true or false: mutton (meat of an older sheep) is lighter in color and flavor than the lamb.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Why Mutton is Darker","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"FALSE\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Why Mutton is Darker\n• Myoglobin buildup: Older animals have muscles that work for years. This increases myoglobin, the protein that stores oxygen in muscle tissue and turns red meat dark.\n• Diet accumulation: Years of grazing on grass and forage deposit rich nutrients and minerals into the animal''s muscle and fat tissues.\n\nWhy Mutton is More Flavorful\n• Fat and fatty acids: Mutton develops a higher concentration of species-specific fatty acids and heavier fat deposits that carry a robust, earthy, and gamey taste.\n• Mature muscle chemistry: As the sheep ages, the chemical compounds responsible for deep savory flavors multiply and concentrate, moving past the mild and milky taste profile of young lamb.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: TRUE, Depends, Maybe"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which condition can cause milk proteins to coagulate?:::acidification', 'which condition can cause milk proteins to coagulate?', 'Acidification', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Lowering milk pH through acidification can cause milk proteins, particularly casein, to coagulate.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Acidification\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Lowering milk pH through acidification can cause milk proteins, particularly casein, to coagulate.\n\n• Freezing alone does not describe the usual mechanism of milk-protein coagulation. \n• Homogenization reduces fat globule size and does not itself cause the described coagulation.\n• Refrigeration alone does not normally coagulate milk proteins.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Refrigeration, Freezing alone, Homogenization"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which condition can cause milk proteins to coagulate?:::', 'which condition can cause milk proteins to coagulate?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Lowering milk pH through acidification can cause milk proteins, particularly casein, to coagulate.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Acidification\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Lowering milk pH through acidification can cause milk proteins, particularly casein, to coagulate.\n\n• Freezing alone does not describe the usual mechanism of milk-protein coagulation. \n• Homogenization reduces fat globule size and does not itself cause the described coagulation.\n• Refrigeration alone does not normally coagulate milk proteins.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Refrigeration, Freezing alone, Homogenization"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which artificial sweetener is associated with phenylketonuria?:::aspartame', 'which artificial sweetener is associated with phenylketonuria?', 'Aspartame', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Aspartame is associated with phenylketonuria (PKU) because it contains phenylalanine. Individuals with PKU cannot properly metabolize phenylalanine, so they need to restrict their intake. This is why products containing aspartame carry a warning for people with PKU.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Aspartame\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Aspartame is associated with phenylketonuria (PKU) because it contains phenylalanine. Individuals with PKU cannot properly metabolize phenylalanine, so they need to restrict their intake. This is why products containing aspartame carry a warning for people with PKU.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Saccharin, Acesulfame-K, Sucralose"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which artificial sweetener is associated with phenylketonuria?:::', 'which artificial sweetener is associated with phenylketonuria?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Aspartame is associated with phenylketonuria (PKU) because it contains phenylalanine. Individuals with PKU cannot properly metabolize phenylalanine, so they need to restrict their intake. This is why products containing aspartame carry a warning for people with PKU.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Aspartame\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Aspartame is associated with phenylketonuria (PKU) because it contains phenylalanine. Individuals with PKU cannot properly metabolize phenylalanine, so they need to restrict their intake. This is why products containing aspartame carry a warning for people with PKU.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Saccharin, Acesulfame-K, Sucralose"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which noodle is traditionally used in lomi?:::miki', 'which noodle is traditionally used in lomi?', 'Miki', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Miki is the thick fresh egg noodle commonly associated with lomi.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Miki\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Miki is the thick fresh egg noodle commonly associated with lomi.\n\n• Sotanghon is a glass or cellophane noodle made from mungbean.\n• Bihon is a thin rice vermicelli noodle.\n• Miswa is a thin wheat noodle and is not the traditional noodle identified for lomi.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Miswa, Sotanghon, Bihon"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is a microorganism commonly used in bread production?:::yeast', 'which of the following is a microorganism commonly used in bread production?', 'Yeast', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Yeast, particularly Saccharomyces cerevisiae, is a microorganism commonly used in bread production. It ferments sugars in the dough and produces carbon dioxide (CO₂). The CO₂ becomes trapped within the gluten network, causing the dough to rise and giving bread its characteristic volume and texture.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Yeast\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Yeast, particularly Saccharomyces cerevisiae, is a microorganism commonly used in bread production. It ferments sugars in the dough and produces carbon dioxide (CO₂). The CO₂ becomes trapped within the gluten network, causing the dough to rise and giving bread its characteristic volume and texture.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Bacteria, Mold, Protozoa"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is a microorganism commonly used in bread production?:::', 'which of the following is a microorganism commonly used in bread production?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Yeast, particularly Saccharomyces cerevisiae, is a microorganism commonly used in bread production. It ferments sugars in the dough and produces carbon dioxide (CO₂). The CO₂ becomes trapped within the gluten network, causing the dough to rise and giving bread its characteristic volume and texture.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Yeast\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Yeast, particularly Saccharomyces cerevisiae, is a microorganism commonly used in bread production. It ferments sugars in the dough and produces carbon dioxide (CO₂). The CO₂ becomes trapped within the gluten network, causing the dough to rise and giving bread its characteristic volume and texture.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Bacteria, Mold, Protozoa"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is a chemical leavening agent?:::baking soda', 'which of the following is a chemical leavening agent?', 'Baking soda', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Baking soda is a fine white chemical salt called sodium bicarbonate (NaHCO3). It acts as a base that reacts with acids to create carbon dioxide gas. This reaction makes baked goods rise.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Baking soda\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Baking soda is a fine white chemical salt called sodium bicarbonate (NaHCO3). It acts as a base that reacts with acids to create carbon dioxide gas. This reaction makes baked goods rise.\n\n• Yeast is a biological leavening agent.\n• Whipped egg white can provide physical aeration rather than chemical leavening.\n• Gluten provides structure and gas retention but is not itself a leavening agent.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Gluten, Yeast, Egg white"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is a chemical leavening agent?:::', 'which of the following is a chemical leavening agent?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Baking soda is a fine white chemical salt called sodium bicarbonate (NaHCO3). It acts as a base that reacts with acids to create carbon dioxide gas. This reaction makes baked goods rise.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Baking soda\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Baking soda is a fine white chemical salt called sodium bicarbonate (NaHCO3). It acts as a base that reacts with acids to create carbon dioxide gas. This reaction makes baked goods rise.\n\n• Yeast is a biological leavening agent.\n• Whipped egg white can provide physical aeration rather than chemical leavening.\n• Gluten provides structure and gas retention but is not itself a leavening agent.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Gluten, Yeast, Egg white"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is a non-perishable food?:::dried beans', 'which of the following is a non-perishable food?', 'Dried beans', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dried beans are considered non-perishable foods because their low moisture content allows them to be stored for long periods at room temperature without rapid spoilage, provided they are kept in proper dry-storage conditions.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Dried beans\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dried beans are considered non-perishable foods because their low moisture content allows them to be stored for long periods at room temperature without rapid spoilage, provided they are kept in proper dry-storage conditions.\n\n• Potatoes: semi-perishable; \n• Fresh milk and fresh meat: perishable","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Potatoes, Fresh milk, Fresh meat"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is a non-perishable food?:::', 'which of the following is a non-perishable food?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dried beans are considered non-perishable foods because their low moisture content allows them to be stored for long periods at room temperature without rapid spoilage, provided they are kept in proper dry-storage conditions.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Dried beans\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dried beans are considered non-perishable foods because their low moisture content allows them to be stored for long periods at room temperature without rapid spoilage, provided they are kept in proper dry-storage conditions.\n\n• Potatoes: semi-perishable; \n• Fresh milk and fresh meat: perishable","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Potatoes, Fresh milk, Fresh meat"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a cooked mixture of about equal weights of flour and fat used to thicken soups, sauces, and gravies.:::roux', 'a cooked mixture of about equal weights of flour and fat used to thicken soups, sauces, and gravies.', 'Roux', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A roux is the culinary term for a thickener made by cooking equal parts by weight of fat (usually butter) and flour.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Roux\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A roux is the culinary term for a thickener made by cooking equal parts by weight of fat (usually butter) and flour.\n\nIt serves as the foundational thickening agent for classic French mother sauces (like Béchamel and Velouté) as well as various soups, stews, and gravies.\n\n• Sherry is a wine.\n• White wash is flour + water, not flour + fat.\n• Custard is simple base of milk/cream, eggs or egg yolks and sugar.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Custard, Sherry, White wash"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a cooked mixture of about equal weights of flour and fat used to thicken soups, sauces, and gravies.:::', 'a cooked mixture of about equal weights of flour and fat used to thicken soups, sauces, and gravies.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A roux is the culinary term for a thickener made by cooking equal parts by weight of fat (usually butter) and flour.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Roux\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A roux is the culinary term for a thickener made by cooking equal parts by weight of fat (usually butter) and flour.\n\nIt serves as the foundational thickening agent for classic French mother sauces (like Béchamel and Velouté) as well as various soups, stews, and gravies.\n\n• Sherry is a wine.\n• White wash is flour + water, not flour + fat.\n• Custard is simple base of milk/cream, eggs or egg yolks and sugar.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Custard, Sherry, White wash"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the most suitable wall material for institutional kitchen is::::ceramic tile', 'the most suitable wall material for institutional kitchen is:', 'Ceramic Tile', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Ceramic Tiles are Durable, smooth, and easy to clean.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Ceramic Tile\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Ceramic Tiles are Durable, smooth, and easy to clean.\n\n• Quarry tile is for floors. \n• Fiberglass is not durable.\n• Stainless steel is for counters.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Stainless steel, Quarry Tile, Fiberglass"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the most suitable wall material for institutional kitchen is::::', 'the most suitable wall material for institutional kitchen is:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Ceramic Tiles are Durable, smooth, and easy to clean.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Ceramic Tile\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Ceramic Tiles are Durable, smooth, and easy to clean.\n\n• Quarry tile is for floors. \n• Fiberglass is not durable.\n• Stainless steel is for counters.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Stainless steel, Quarry Tile, Fiberglass"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:at higher altitudes, the boiling point of water::::decreases', 'at higher altitudes, the boiling point of water:', 'Decreases', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"At higher altitudes, atmospheric pressure is lower because there is less air pressing down on the Earth’s surface. A liquid boils when its vapor pressure becomes equal to the surrounding atmospheric pressure.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Decreases\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"At higher altitudes, atmospheric pressure is lower because there is less air pressing down on the Earth’s surface. A liquid boils when its vapor pressure becomes equal to the surrounding atmospheric pressure.\n\nTherefore: Higher altitude → lower atmospheric pressure → liquid reaches its boiling point at a lower temperature → lower boiling point","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Increases, Cannot be determined, Remains exactly the same"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:at higher altitudes, the boiling point of water::::', 'at higher altitudes, the boiling point of water:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"At higher altitudes, atmospheric pressure is lower because there is less air pressing down on the Earth’s surface. A liquid boils when its vapor pressure becomes equal to the surrounding atmospheric pressure.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Decreases\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"At higher altitudes, atmospheric pressure is lower because there is less air pressing down on the Earth’s surface. A liquid boils when its vapor pressure becomes equal to the surrounding atmospheric pressure.\n\nTherefore: Higher altitude → lower atmospheric pressure → liquid reaches its boiling point at a lower temperature → lower boiling point","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Increases, Cannot be determined, Remains exactly the same"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a common mixing method used to incorporate fat into flour for pie crust is::::cutting in', 'a common mixing method used to incorporate fat into flour for pie crust is:', 'Cutting in', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Cutting in distributes solid fat through flour to create the desired pastry texture.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Cutting in\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Cutting in distributes solid fat through flour to create the desired pastry texture.\n\n• Creaming is commonly used to incorporate fat and sugar, especially in cakes and cookies.\n• Folding is used to gently incorporate ingredients while preserving air or delicate structure.\n• Kneading develops gluten and is not the standard method for incorporating fat into pie-crust flour.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Creaming, Folding, Kneading"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a common mixing method used to incorporate fat into flour for pie crust is::::', 'a common mixing method used to incorporate fat into flour for pie crust is:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Cutting in distributes solid fat through flour to create the desired pastry texture.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Cutting in\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Cutting in distributes solid fat through flour to create the desired pastry texture.\n\n• Creaming is commonly used to incorporate fat and sugar, especially in cakes and cookies.\n• Folding is used to gently incorporate ingredients while preserving air or delicate structure.\n• Kneading develops gluten and is not the standard method for incorporating fat into pie-crust flour.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Creaming, Folding, Kneading"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb)
