('mc:in pickling, sugar primarily contributes::::sweetness and flavor balance', 'in pickling, sugar primarily contributes:', 'Sweetness and flavor balance', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sugar contributes sweetness and helps balance the sourness and other flavors of pickled foods","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sweetness and flavor balance\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sugar contributes sweetness and helps balance the sourness and other flavors of pickled foods\n\n• Sugar alone does not guarantee crispness.\n• The primary preservation effects in many pickles are associated with acidity and other controlled conditions rather than sugar alone.\n• Sugar does not by itself cause fermentation; fermentation depends on microorganisms and suitable conditions.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Fermentation, Crispness by itself, The main antimicrobial action"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:in pickling, sugar primarily contributes::::', 'in pickling, sugar primarily contributes:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sugar contributes sweetness and helps balance the sourness and other flavors of pickled foods","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sweetness and flavor balance\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sugar contributes sweetness and helps balance the sourness and other flavors of pickled foods\n\n• Sugar alone does not guarantee crispness.\n• The primary preservation effects in many pickles are associated with acidity and other controlled conditions rather than sugar alone.\n• Sugar does not by itself cause fermentation; fermentation depends on microorganisms and suitable conditions.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Fermentation, Crispness by itself, The main antimicrobial action"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:soft pickles may result from::::weak brine or vinegar and exposure to air', 'soft pickles may result from:', 'Weak brine or vinegar and exposure to air', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Pickles may become soft when the brine or vinegar solution is too weak and/or when the pickles are exposed to air. Exposure to air can contribute to undesirable microbial activity and softening.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Weak brine or vinegar and exposure to air\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Pickles may become soft when the brine or vinegar solution is too weak and/or when the pickles are exposed to air. Exposure to air can contribute to undesirable microbial activity and softening.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Too much vinegar, Strong vinegar concentration alone, Excessive sugar concentration alone"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:soft pickles may result from::::', 'soft pickles may result from:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Pickles may become soft when the brine or vinegar solution is too weak and/or when the pickles are exposed to air. Exposure to air can contribute to undesirable microbial activity and softening.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Weak brine or vinegar and exposure to air\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Pickles may become soft when the brine or vinegar solution is too weak and/or when the pickles are exposed to air. Exposure to air can contribute to undesirable microbial activity and softening.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Too much vinegar, Strong vinegar concentration alone, Excessive sugar concentration alone"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which microorganism is associated with lactic acid fermentation?:::leuconostoc mesenteroides', 'which microorganism is associated with lactic acid fermentation?', 'Leuconostoc mesenteroides', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Leuconostoc mesenteroides is a lactic acid bacterium associated with vegetable fermentations.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Leuconostoc mesenteroides\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Leuconostoc mesenteroides is a lactic acid bacterium associated with vegetable fermentations.\n\n• Acetobacter aceti: associated with acetic acid production from ethanol.\n• Saccharomyces cerevisiae: yeast associated with alcoholic fermentation.\n• Saccharomyces carlsbergensis: yeast associated with lager brewing.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Saccharomyces carlsbergensis, Acetobacter aceti, Saccharomyces cerevisiae"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which microorganism is associated with lactic acid fermentation?:::', 'which microorganism is associated with lactic acid fermentation?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Leuconostoc mesenteroides is a lactic acid bacterium associated with vegetable fermentations.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Leuconostoc mesenteroides\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Leuconostoc mesenteroides is a lactic acid bacterium associated with vegetable fermentations.\n\n• Acetobacter aceti: associated with acetic acid production from ethanol.\n• Saccharomyces cerevisiae: yeast associated with alcoholic fermentation.\n• Saccharomyces carlsbergensis: yeast associated with lager brewing.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Saccharomyces carlsbergensis, Acetobacter aceti, Saccharomyces cerevisiae"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the recommended temperature for dry storage is approximately::::60-70°f', 'the recommended temperature for dry storage is approximately:', '60-70°F', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"About 60–70°F is an appropriate general range for dry storage under controlled conditions.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"60-70°F\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"About 60–70°F is an appropriate general range for dry storage under controlled conditions.\n\n• 29–33°F is within the range of freezing or near-freezing storage rather than typical dry storage.\n• 34–40°F is a refrigerated-storage range.\n• 0–10°F is a freezing-storage range.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 29-33°F, 34-40°F, 0-10°F"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:the recommended temperature for dry storage is approximately::::', 'the recommended temperature for dry storage is approximately:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"About 60–70°F is an appropriate general range for dry storage under controlled conditions.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"60-70°F\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"About 60–70°F is an appropriate general range for dry storage under controlled conditions.\n\n• 29–33°F is within the range of freezing or near-freezing storage rather than typical dry storage.\n• 34–40°F is a refrigerated-storage range.\n• 0–10°F is a freezing-storage range.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 29-33°F, 34-40°F, 0-10°F"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which sensory test would be most appropriate when the objective is to determine whether panelists can distinguish between two samples based on a specified sensory attribute?:::paired comparison', 'which sensory test would be most appropriate when the objective is to determine whether panelists can distinguish between two samples based on a specified sensory attribute?', 'Paired comparison', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Panelists are presented with two samples and asked to compare them with respect to a particular characteristic, such as sweetness, saltiness, tenderness, or intensity of flavor.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Paired comparison\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Panelists are presented with two samples and asked to compare them with respect to a particular characteristic, such as sweetness, saltiness, tenderness, or intensity of flavor.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Option B, Hedonic scaling, Rank-order"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
