('mc:a central facility prepares meals in large quantities and distributes the prepared meals to several remote service locations. which system best fits this arrangement?:::commissary system', 'a central facility prepares meals in large quantities and distributes the prepared meals to several remote service locations. which system best fits this arrangement?', 'Commissary system', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The commissary foodservice system (also referred to as a central kitchen or central production facility) concentrates food procurement and production in a single, large-scale facility.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Commissary system\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The commissary foodservice system (also referred to as a central kitchen or central production facility) concentrates food procurement and production in a single, large-scale facility.\n\nOnce the meals are prepared in bulk, they are packaged and transported to distinct, remote service locations (often called satellite units or receiving kitchens) for final finishing and service.\n\n• À la carte system: A style of menu where items are priced and ordered separately, rather than a production and distribution system.\n• Conventional system: The traditional model where food is purchased, prepared from scratch, and served on the same premises.\n• Individualized system: A specialized approach focused on custom dietary tailoring (often used in clinical healthcare), not a bulk production and remote distribution network.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Conventional system, À la carte system, Individualized system"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:a central facility prepares meals in large quantities and distributes the prepared meals to several remote service locations. which system best fits this arrangement?:::', 'a central facility prepares meals in large quantities and distributes the prepared meals to several remote service locations. which system best fits this arrangement?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The commissary foodservice system (also referred to as a central kitchen or central production facility) concentrates food procurement and production in a single, large-scale facility.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Commissary system\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The commissary foodservice system (also referred to as a central kitchen or central production facility) concentrates food procurement and production in a single, large-scale facility.\n\nOnce the meals are prepared in bulk, they are packaged and transported to distinct, remote service locations (often called satellite units or receiving kitchens) for final finishing and service.\n\n• À la carte system: A style of menu where items are priced and ordered separately, rather than a production and distribution system.\n• Conventional system: The traditional model where food is purchased, prepared from scratch, and served on the same premises.\n• Individualized system: A specialized approach focused on custom dietary tailoring (often used in clinical healthcare), not a bulk production and remote distribution network.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Conventional system, À la carte system, Individualized system"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:under pd 856, the minimum kitchen size for a 40 m² dining area is .:::10 m²', 'under pd 856, the minimum kitchen size for a 40 m² dining area is .', '10 m²', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to the Implementing Rules and Regulations (IRR) of Chapter III (Food Establishments) of the Code on Sanitation of the Philippines (Presidential Decree No. 856), the kitchen area must be at least ¼ (25%) of the total dining area, provided it is not less than a base minimum of 9.30 m² (100 sq. ft.).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"10 m²\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to the Implementing Rules and Regulations (IRR) of Chapter III (Food Establishments) of the Code on Sanitation of the Philippines (Presidential Decree No. 856), the kitchen area must be at least ¼ (25%) of the total dining area, provided it is not less than a base minimum of 9.30 m² (100 sq. ft.).\n\nCalculation: Required Kitchen Size = 40 m² x ¼ = 10 m²\n\nSince 10 m² meets the legal ratio and exceeds the minimum threshold of 9.30 m², it serves as the absolute statutory minimum required floor area for that setup.\n\n• 12 m² – Not prescribed.\n• 8 m² – Too small.\n• 15 m² – Above requirement.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 12 m², 8 m², 15 m²"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:under pd 856, the minimum kitchen size for a 40 m² dining area is .:::', 'under pd 856, the minimum kitchen size for a 40 m² dining area is .', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to the Implementing Rules and Regulations (IRR) of Chapter III (Food Establishments) of the Code on Sanitation of the Philippines (Presidential Decree No. 856), the kitchen area must be at least ¼ (25%) of the total dining area, provided it is not less than a base minimum of 9.30 m² (100 sq. ft.).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"10 m²\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to the Implementing Rules and Regulations (IRR) of Chapter III (Food Establishments) of the Code on Sanitation of the Philippines (Presidential Decree No. 856), the kitchen area must be at least ¼ (25%) of the total dining area, provided it is not less than a base minimum of 9.30 m² (100 sq. ft.).\n\nCalculation: Required Kitchen Size = 40 m² x ¼ = 10 m²\n\nSince 10 m² meets the legal ratio and exceeds the minimum threshold of 9.30 m², it serves as the absolute statutory minimum required floor area for that setup.\n\n• 12 m² – Not prescribed.\n• 8 m² – Too small.\n• 15 m² – Above requirement.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 12 m², 8 m², 15 m²"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which menu term means "of the day"?:::du jour', 'which menu term means "of the day"?', 'Du jour', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Du jour is a French phrase that literally translates to \"of the day\".","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Du jour\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Du jour is a French phrase that literally translates to \"of the day\".\n\nIn restaurants, a du jour menu or item changes daily depending on what fresh ingredients are available or what specials the chef has created (e.g., \"Soup du jour\" means soup of the day).\n\n• À la cart: Means \"by the menu\" or \"from the menu\"; items are listed, ordered, and priced individually.\n• Table d''hôte: Means \"the host''s table\"; it offers a complete, multi-course meal with limited choices at a single fixed price.\n• Prix fixe: Means \"fixed price\"; a set menu where customers pay a predetermined total price for a specific sequence of courses.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: À la carte, Table d''hôte, Prix fixe"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which menu term means "of the day"?:::', 'which menu term means "of the day"?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Du jour is a French phrase that literally translates to \"of the day\".","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Du jour\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Du jour is a French phrase that literally translates to \"of the day\".\n\nIn restaurants, a du jour menu or item changes daily depending on what fresh ingredients are available or what specials the chef has created (e.g., \"Soup du jour\" means soup of the day).\n\n• À la cart: Means \"by the menu\" or \"from the menu\"; items are listed, ordered, and priced individually.\n• Table d''hôte: Means \"the host''s table\"; it offers a complete, multi-course meal with limited choices at a single fixed price.\n• Prix fixe: Means \"fixed price\"; a set menu where customers pay a predetermined total price for a specific sequence of courses.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: À la carte, Table d''hôte, Prix fixe"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
('mc:which should be a healthier option of market forms of milk for people who monitor their blood profiles?:::toned milk', 'which should be a healthier option of market forms of milk for people who monitor their blood profiles?', 'Toned milk', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Toned milk – Lower fat but retains protein and calcium, better for heart health.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Toned milk\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Toned milk – Lower fat but retains protein and calcium, better for heart health.\n\n• Whole milk – High in saturated fat, raises cholesterol.\n• Condensed milk – Very high in sugar and fat.\n• Evaporated milk – Concentrated, higher fat though no added sugar.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Whole milk, Condensed milk, Evaporated milk"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
