('mc:a foodservice manager wants to reduce the risk of microbial growth in cooked food during preparation, holding, and service. which practice best demonstrates proper time-temperature control?:::monitoring food temperatures and minimizing the time food remains at unsafe temperatures', 'a foodservice manager wants to reduce the risk of microbial growth in cooked food during preparation, holding, and service. which practice best demonstrates proper time-temperature control?', 'Monitoring food temperatures and minimizing the time food remains at unsafe temperatures', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Foodborne pathogens multiply rapidly in the Temperature Danger Zone, which is between 41°F and 135°F (5°C to 57°C).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Monitoring food temperatures and minimizing the time food remains at unsafe temperatures\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Foodborne pathogens multiply rapidly in the Temperature Danger Zone, which is between 41°F and 135°F (5°C to 57°C).\n\nProper time-temperature control for safety (TCS) requires food managers to constantly measure temperatures using food thermometers and ensure that foods move through or stay out of this danger zone as quickly as possible to prevent dangerous microbial growth.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Checking on appearance and odor to determine whether food is safe, Storing cooked food to remain at room temperature until needed, Keeping all foods at the same temperature regardless of whether they are served hot or cold to save storage space"}'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb),
